window.MASTERY_SEARCH = [{"title": "01.01 · Scarcity, choice and opportunity cost", "url": "v01-c01.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "The problem economics begins with You have one evening. You can work for pay, study, spend time with family, or rest. Each option uses the same limited hours. The problem is not necessarily a shortage of money; it is that you cannot simultaneously do everything you value. Economics studies choices made under constraints, the interactions among those choices, and their consequences. A constraint describes what is feasible. A household faces time, income, borrowing and information constraints. A firm faces technology, capacity, financing and contractual constraints. A government faces resources, administrative capabilities, political commitments and legal constraints. Start an analysis by identifying the decision maker, the available actions, the constraints, and the objective. An objective is what the decision maker values. It might be consumption, profit, health, learning, reliability, or a combination. A financial return is one possible component. It is not a universal measure of wellbeing. When important outcomes cannot be expressed credibly in money, keep them explicit rather than assigning invented dollar values. Opportunity cost is an alternative The opportunity cost of a choice is the value of the best feasible alternative forgone. It is not necessarily an invoice or cash payment. Using a building you already own can have an opportunity cost because you could rent it out. Studying for three hours can have an opportunity cost because you could work or rest. The best alternative must be realistic and available. Suppose you can choose only one of three activities with net benefits of 100, 70 and 40 measured in the same units. If you choose the first, its opportunity cost is 70. Adding 70 and 40 would pretend you could undertake both forgone alternatives even though the constraint permits only one. The advantage of the first choice over the best alternative is 30. Be clear about the accounting convention. If you have already subtracted the forgone alternative's value when calculating an economic net benefit, subtracting it again would double count it. A useful approach is to calculate the future benefits and costs of each feasible action consistently, then compare their net values. Worked example 1 — Work, study, or rest You have four hours. Paid work earns $25 an hour but requires a $10 transportation expense. You estimate the value of studying during the same four hours at $130. Rest has an estimated value of $80. Assume the three values are comparable monetary equivalents, and the actions are mutually exclusive. Work's net value is 4 × $25 − $10 = $90. Study's net value is $130; rest's is $80. The best alternative to study is work, worth $90. Study's opportunity cost is $90. Study has a $130 − $90 = $40 advantage over the best alternative. The recommendation is conditional on those valuations. If the work is unavailable, it cannot be the opportunity cost. If studying causes exhaustion that was omitted from its valuation, the comparison needs correction. If rest and paid work can be combined in a different feasible schedule, that schedule must also be evaluated. Relevant costs and sunk costs A cost is sunk at the time of a decision when it has already been incurred and cannot be recovered through any currently available action. A past payment does not automatically qualify: if you can obtain a refund or sell what you purchased, the recoverable value affects the decision. Compare consequences that differ among the actions from the decision time forward. Suppose a business spent $40,000 developing a product. Launching now would generate $30,000 of revenue and require another $20,000 of cost. Abandoning would generate no future revenue or expense. With no other consequences, launching has a $10,000 advantage. The unrecoverable $40,000 affects the historical project's total return, but it does not reverse the current comparison. Historical accountability and current decision quality answer different questions. Managers should learn why the development money was spent and whether the original decision was justified. They should also choose the best remaining action. Continuing a bad project merely to make the past expenditure feel worthwhile can destroy more value. Past choices can still matter through their present consequences. A signed contract can impose a future cancellation charge. An acquired skill can improve a future benefit. A reputation can influence demand. Count these consequences if they differ between the actions; do not label all history irrelevant. Think at the margin An average describes a total divided by a quantity. A marginal quantity describes the change caused by an additional unit. A firm deciding whether to make one more item needs the additional revenue and additional cost, rather than the average historical profit of all items. For discrete actions, write the comparison explicitly: Incremental net benefit = incremental benefit − incremental cost An additional unit is worth taking when this quantity is positive, given the constraints and the consequences already included. When benefits decrease and costs increase with quantity, this often means expanding until the next unit is no longer worthwhile. Without those conditions, a local comparison need not find the best overall decision; compare the feasible alternatives directly. Later, calculus will formalize marginal changes with derivatives. The familiar condition “marginal benefit equals marginal cost” applies to particular interior, differentiable optimization problems. It is not a rule that every optimum must satisfy. Capacity constraints, indivisible choices and corner solutions often produce an optimum where equality fails. Worked example 2 — A partially completed workshop You already paid a nonrefundable $60 registration fee for a workshop. Attending requires another $10 transportation payment. You value the remaining workshop at $45. Staying home and working earns $40 net. Assume no additional benefits or costs. Current action Future benefit Future cash cost Net value Attend $45 $10 $35 Work instead $40 $0 $40 Working is better by $5. The $60 already paid is common to both actions and cancels from their comparison. Equivalently, attending provides $45 and costs $10 plus a $40 forgone alternative, for an incremental advantage of −$5. These are two consistent calculations of the same choice. Now suppose a refund of $20 is available only if you do not attend. The alternative becomes worth $60, and attending is worse by $25. The registration payment has a recoverable component and is no longer entirely sunk. A decision procedure you can reuse First specify the choice and the decision time. List realistic alternatives, including doing nothing when it is feasible. Then describe the consequences that differ among them. Put money on a common date when timing matters; Chapter 5 develops that calculation. Identify uncertain outcomes rather than silently treating them as certain; Chapter 8 develops scenarios. Check for double counting. A lost wage can appear as the alternative's benefit or as an opportunity cost of the chosen action, but it should not appear twice in the same comparison. An expense already deducted from profit should not be subtracted again. Nonmonetary outcomes should remain visible when monetary equivalents are unreliable. Finally state a conditional conclusion: which action is best under the assumptions, which assumptions matter most, and what new information could change the result. A disciplined decision is transparent about its limits. It need not pretend that uncertain personal values or future outcomes have been measured precisely. Common errors Counting only cash: overlooks the best use of time, owned assets, or scarce capacity. Adding incompatible alternatives: inflates opportunity cost beyond what was feasible. Counting the same loss twice: mixes a net-value comparison with an opportunity-cost adjustment inconsistently. Letting a sunk payment decide: confuses historical performance with the best current action. Using averages for additional decisions: ignores the consequences of the next unit. Inventing certainty: converts uncertain or nonmonetary values into precise-looking numbers without justification. Exercises Attempt each problem before opening the solutions. State your assumptions and units. You can work for five hours at $18 per hour with a $15 transportation cost, study for an estimated value of $95, or rest for an estimated value of $65. The choices are exclusive. Which is best, what is its opportunity cost, and what is its advantage? An owned office can be rented out for $900 per month after rental expenses. Using it for your own business requires $150 per month in additional cleaning costs. What monthly cost should the business compare with the benefits of occupying it? Explain why the historical purchase price is not enough information. A nonrefundable $120 ticket has already been purchased. Attending provides a benefit of $90 and costs $20 in transportation. An alternative activity provides $80 net. Compare the current choices. How does the result change if the ticket can be resold for $30 after fees? A project already cost $8,000. Completing it requires $2,000 and produces $2,700 of future revenue. Abandonment has no future consequence. Calculate the best current decision and the total project profit if completed. Explain why these can point to different judgments. The net benefits of three mutually exclusive actions are 160, 120 and 90. Someone says the first action's opportunity cost is 210. Identify the error and calculate the relevant comparison. A seller can produce up to four units. Additional-unit benefits are 30, 25, 18 and 10; additional-unit costs are 12, 15, 17 and 20. Assuming these are the complete consequences, how many units should be produced? Calculate the total net benefit. A student says, “I must finish this course because I have already spent 100 hours on it.” Give a case where continuing is rational and one where stopping is rational. Identify which future consequences would decide the comparison. Write a decision memo of no more than 150 words about a real choice. Identify the decision time, two feasible alternatives, the opportunity cost, a potentially sunk cost, uncertainty, and a fact that could change your decision. Do not invent monetary values for outcomes you cannot credibly price. Solutions and evaluation criteria Work is worth 5 × 18 − 15 = $75. Study is best at $95; its opportunity cost is $75, and its advantage is $20. Rest is not added to work because the forgone alternatives are exclusive. The relevant monthly cost is $900 forgone net rent plus $150 additional cleaning, or $1,050, assuming those consequences are distinct and complete. Historical purchase price does not describe the current best alternative. If selling is also feasible, compare its future consequences on a consistent horizon. Attendance is worth 90 − 20 = $70; the alternative is $80. Choose the alternative by $10. With resale available only if you do not attend, the alternative is worth $110 and is better by $40. The resale opportunity changes the current comparison. Complete: 2,700 − 2,000 = $700 more future value than abandonment. Total project profit is 2,700 − 8,000 − 2,000 = −$7,300. The original project lost money, yet completion minimizes the remaining loss under these assumptions. The analyst added alternatives that cannot both be taken. The best forgone alternative is 120; the first action's advantage is 160 − 120 = 40. Marginal net benefits are 18, 10, 1 and −10. Produce three units for a total net benefit of 29. The complete quantity comparison gives totals 0, 18, 28, 29 and 19, confirming the choice. Continuing is rational if its expected future benefits exceed those of the best alternative after remaining costs. Stopping is rational if an alternative has higher future net value. The 100 unrecoverable hours are sunk, but skills already acquired, remaining fees, completion requirements and future opportunities can affect the comparison. A strong memo compares genuinely feasible choices, uses a consistent time horizon, separates recoverable from sunk costs, avoids double counting, preserves important nonmonetary outcomes, and gives a conditional recommendation. There is no universal correct personal choice; the quality of the reasoning is assessable. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain opportunity cost to someone without economic training using an owned asset and a time allocation. Explain why a disappointing past investment can still rationally receive more spending. Delayed check: a month later, revisit an actual choice. Reconstruct the alternatives without reading this chapter. Can you distinguish the best forgone alternative, sunk costs, and future consequences that genuinely differ? Where this leads Later chapters formalize constraints and objectives with algebra, optimization, probability and dynamic models. Welfare economics asks whose benefits and costs are counted; information economics asks how knowledge changes choice; institutional analysis asks which choices are actually available. The discipline begins with a correct comparison."}, {"title": "01.02 · Numbers, units and percentage changes", "url": "v01-c02.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Numbers need a meaning A number without a unit, time period or denominator can mislead. Revenue of “50” could mean $50, $50 million, fifty units sold, or an index reading. Growth of “5” could mean five dollars, five percent or five percentage points. Before calculating, identify what each quantity measures and the population or period to which it applies. Write quantities in forms such as “$50 million per year,” “12 unemployed people per 100 labor-force participants,” or “$30 per item.” Units discipline the calculation. Dividing dollars by dollars yields a ratio. Dividing dollars by units yields dollars per unit. Adding a monthly rate to a yearly rate without conversion is a mistake even if a calculator accepts it. Fractions, ratios and decimals A fraction expresses a part relative to a whole: 3/4 means three parts when the whole is divided into four equal parts. A ratio compares quantities. Debt of $60 and equity of $40 produce a debt-to-equity ratio of 60/40 = 1.5, while debt's share of total financing is 60/(60 + 40) = 0.6. These answer different questions. A percentage is a ratio multiplied by 100. The decimal 0.06 equals 6%; 1.06 equals 106%; 0.006 equals 0.6%. When using a formula, translate percentages into decimal rates unless the formula explicitly expects percentage units. Multiplying $1,000 by 6 yields $6,000, whereas multiplying it by a 6% rate gives $60. Financial quantities can be negative. A loss of $20 relative to a $100 initial investment is a −20% return. However, percentage changes from zero are undefined, and changes from a negative base require careful interpretation. A business's profit moving from −$10 to +$10 is a $20 improvement; describing it as an ordinary −200% growth rate would obscure the economic meaning. Levels, changes and growth rates The absolute change is the new level minus the old level. The ordinary percentage change uses the old level as the denominator: Absolute change = new − old Percentage change = 100 × (new − old) / old This definition requires a nonzero base and is most straightforward for positive levels. An increase from 80 to 100 is 20 units and 25%. Reversing the move from 100 to 80 is −20 units and −20%. Percentage changes are asymmetric because their denominators differ. The fraction remaining after a loss matters for recovery. A 50% loss leaves half the original value. Doubling the remaining half requires a 100% gain. More generally, after a fractional loss L with 0 ≤ L < 1, recovering to the original level requires a gain of L/(1 − L). A complete loss cannot be recovered by applying a finite percentage return to the zero remaining balance. Worked example 1 — Growth, losses and changing denominators A business's monthly sales rise from $80,000 to $100,000, then fall back to $80,000. The increase is $20,000/$80,000 = 0.25, or 25%. The decrease is −$20,000/$100,000 = −0.20, or −20%. The combined growth factor is 1.25 × 0.80 = 1.00. Sales return to the initial level. Adding the rates gives 25% − 20% = 5%, which is not the combined percentage change. For consecutive changes, multiply growth factors. If a quantity rises 10% and then 20%, its factor is 1.10 × 1.20 = 1.32, a 32% increase. The extra two percentage points arise because the second change applies to a larger base. Percentages and percentage points When the measured quantity is itself a percentage, distinguish an absolute difference in percentage units from a relative percentage change. If an interest rate rises from 4% to 6%, it rises by two percentage points . Relative to the original 4% rate, it rises by (6 − 4)/4 = 50%. A basis point is one hundredth of a percentage point. One percentage point is 100 basis points. A rate increase from 4.00% to 4.25% is 0.25 percentage points, 25 basis points, and a 6.25% relative increase in the rate. The phrases are not interchangeable. Suppose a quoted unemployment rate falls from 10% to 9%. That is a decline of one percentage point or 10% relative to its previous rate. It does not establish that the number of unemployed people fell by 10%, because the labor-force denominator may also have changed. Reconstruct the counts when that is the question. Weighted averages An ordinary average gives each observation equal weight. A weighted average assigns specified weights that sum to one: Weighted average = w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + wₙxₙ with w₁ + w₂ + … + wₙ = 1 To combine a rate across groups, the relevant weights typically correspond to the rate's denominator. Combining employment shares uses group populations in the applicable population measure. Combining profit margins uses revenues. A large and a small division should not receive equal weights merely because each is one division. For a portfolio measured over one common period with no intermediate flows, the simple portfolio return is the initial-value-weighted average of component simple returns. This statement does not imply that averaging yearly returns gives compound growth. Chapter 5 develops the distinction between growth over time and aggregation across assets. Worked example 2 — Combining profit margins correctly Division A has revenue of $100 and profit of $20. Division B has revenue of $900 and profit of $45. Assume the revenues and profits are measured over the same period and can be added without interdivision double counting. A's margin is 20/100 = 20%. B's is 45/900 = 5%. The equal-weight average is (20% + 5%)/2 = 12.5%. It gives the small division the same importance as the large one. The business's actual combined margin is (20 + 45)/(100 + 900) = 65/1,000 = 6.5%. Revenue weights are 0.1 and 0.9. The weighted margin is 0.1 × 20% + 0.9 × 5% = 6.5%. The method follows the denominator. If the objective were to describe a typical division's margin rather than the combined business's margin, the equal-weight average might answer that different question. Good analysis states which question it is answering. Rates across time and measurement conventions A rate needs its period. A monthly 1% return and an annual 1% return do not describe the same growth. Multiplying a monthly rate by 12 gives a simple annualized convention, not the exact effective annual growth when monthly gains compound. At 1% each month, that growth is (1.01) 12 − 1, approximately 12.68%. When reading a number, ask whether it is a level, an annual total, a monthly flow, a seasonally adjusted rate, or an index. A flow observed for one period cannot automatically be treated as a forecast for the next. A quarter's sales multiplied by four is an annualized representation, not necessarily the year's realized sales. Unit conversions must preserve the quantity. One million dollars equals 1,000 thousand dollars. If a dataset reports money in thousands and you treat it as dollars, the result will be off by a factor of 1,000. When dividing two monetary quantities, check both scales before forming a ratio. Precision, rounding and plausibility Do not round every intermediate calculation unless the contract or measurement convention requires it. Preserve sufficient precision, then round the displayed answer to a level justified by the inputs and the purpose. A forecast based on rough assumptions does not become more reliable when shown to eight decimal places. Use plausibility checks. A margin combining positive revenues should fall between the individual margins if all relevant profits and revenues are aggregated consistently. A 10% increase followed by a 10% decrease should leave slightly less than the initial amount: 1.10 × 0.90 = 0.99. If a conversion makes an ordinary household budget equal to national income, inspect the scale before advancing to a complicated model. Numerical competence is largely disciplined interpretation. The formula is often simple; the challenge is identifying the correct base, denominator, period, population and unit. Exercises A wage rises from $20 to $23 per hour. Calculate its absolute and percentage change. Then calculate the percentage reduction required to return from $23 to $20. A rate rises from 3.50% to 4.10%. Express the change in percentage points, basis points and relative percentage terms. An asset worth $200 loses 30% and then gains 30%. Find the final value and total percentage change. What gain after the initial loss would restore the original value? Division X has revenue of $400 and profit of $40. Division Y has revenue of $600 and profit of $120. Find the divisions' margins, the equal-weight mean margin and the combined margin. A portfolio begins with $300 in asset A and $700 in asset B. During one common period, A returns 10% and B returns −5%, with no external cash flows. Calculate the portfolio's ending value and simple return. A dataset lists debt as 2,400 in thousands of dollars and GDP as 3 in millions of dollars. Calculate debt in dollars and the debt-to-GDP ratio. Explain the unit correction. At time 0, a labor force of 1,000 includes 100 unemployed people. At time 1, a labor force of 1,200 includes 108 unemployed people. Calculate both unemployment rates, their percentage-point change, and the percentage change in the unemployed count. A report states, “Profit increased from −$5 million to $10 million, so profit grew −300%.” Explain the arithmetic behind that expression, why it misleads, and how you would report the result. Add a rule for reporting changes when the original level is zero. Solutions and evaluation criteria The wage rises $3 per hour, or 3/20 = 15%. The reverse change is −3/23, approximately −13.04%. The denominators differ. The increase is 0.60 percentage points or 60 basis points. Relative to 3.50%, it is 0.60/3.50, approximately 17.14%. The ending value is 200 × 0.70 × 1.30 = $182, a −9% total change. Recovery from the $140 remaining value requires 60/140 = 42.857…%, approximately 42.86%. X's margin is 10%; Y's is 20%. The equal-weight mean is 15%. Combined profit is $160 on $1,000 revenue, giving a 16% margin. Revenue weighting produces 0.4 × 10% + 0.6 × 20% = 16%. A ends at $330 and B at $665. Total is $995, a −0.5% return on $1,000. Initial-value weighting gives 0.3 × 10% + 0.7 × (−5%) = −0.5%. Debt is 2,400 × $1,000 = $2.4 million. GDP is $3 million. The ratio is 2.4/3 = 0.8 or 80%. Dividing the unconverted displayed values would mix scales. The initial rate is 100/1,000 = 10%; the later rate is 108/1,200 = 9%. The rate falls one percentage point even though the unemployed count rises by 8%. A changing denominator explains the difference. The ordinary formula gives (10 − (−5))/(−5) = −3. Reporting −300% conceals the improvement because the base is negative. State that the business moved from a $5 million loss to a $10 million profit, a $15 million improvement. With a zero initial level, ordinary percentage growth is undefined; report the absolute change and relevant context. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why “the rate rose 50%” and “the rate rose two percentage points” can both describe the same event. Give your own numbers and identify the denominator. Delayed check: reconstruct the return after a 20% loss followed by a 20% gain, and explain the result without a memorized formula. Where this leads Algebra will express these relationships symbolically. Accounting will make denominator choices more consequential. Statistics will formalize sampling weights and uncertainty. Asset pricing will distinguish simple returns, logarithmic returns, compound growth and cash-flow-sensitive performance measures. Accurate units and bases remain essential at every level."}, {"title": "01.03 · Algebra, functions and graphs", "url": "v01-c03.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Turn a verbal claim into a relationship A model becomes easier to examine when its quantities and relationships are explicit. Suppose a taxi fare consists of a $4 starting charge plus $2 per kilometer. Let x be kilometers traveled and y be dollars charged. The rule is y = 4 + 2x. It makes a testable prediction, specifies units, and exposes an assumption: the per-kilometer charge is constant over the trip. A variable can take different values. A parameter is treated as fixed for a particular exercise. Here x varies; the numbers 4 and 2 are parameters. A parameter can change between scenarios: a new tariff could change the starting charge. Always distinguish a quantity chosen by an actor, a quantity determined within the model, and an input taken as given. An equation says two expressions are equal. An identity holds by the definitions involved, such as total spending equaling the sum of spending categories. A behavioral equation describes how an actor responds, such as a proposed relationship between price and quantity demanded. The mathematical equality sign does not make a behavioral relationship an accounting truth. Solve equations while preserving equality Perform the same legitimate operation on both sides. To solve 4 + 2x = 18, subtract 4 from both sides to obtain 2x = 14, then divide both sides by 2 to obtain x = 7. Substitute 7 into the original expression: 4 + 2 × 7 = 18. Substitution checks the answer and can detect algebraic mistakes. Division requires a nonzero divisor. Multiplying both sides by zero destroys information. Squaring both sides can introduce solutions that did not satisfy the original equation, so check candidates afterward. If an equation represents an economic problem, mathematical solutions must also satisfy its domain and constraints. A negative number of purchased books may solve a rearranged expression but still be infeasible. An inequality describes a feasible range. A budget of $20 with books priced at $5 and meals at $2 permits nonnegative quantities x and y satisfying: 5x + 2y ≤ 20 x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0 Equality identifies bundles that spend the entire budget. Inequality also permits unspent money. Multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number reverses its direction: −2x ≤ 6 implies x ≥ −3. The reversal follows from the ordering of negative numbers, not from an arbitrary convention. Functions, domains and slopes A function assigns one output to each input in its domain. The notation y = f(x) means that y is the output associated with x. For f(x) = 4 + 2x, f(3) = 10. Domain restrictions describe allowed inputs: distance is nonnegative, and a fare rule might apply only within a service area. A linear function has the form y = a + bx. The intercept a is the output when x = 0. The slope b is the change in output per additional unit of input. More generally, the slope between two points is: Slope = (y₂ − y₁) / (x₂ − x₁), for x₂ ≠ x₁ The slope carries units. A fare slope of 2 means $2 per kilometer. A budget slope describing meals against books means meals forgone per additional book. Changing units changes the numerical slope without changing the underlying relationship. A distance measured in meters rather than kilometers gives a fare slope of $0.002 per meter. A positive slope means the modeled quantities move in the same direction; a negative slope means they move in opposite directions. Neither sign establishes causation in observed data. A model specifying a causal mechanism and a graph of an observed association are different objects. Worked example 1 — Read a budget line With a $20 budget, books at $5 each and meals at $2 each, the full-spending boundary is 5x + 2y = 20. Rearrange: 2y = 20 − 5x y = 10 − 2.5x If x = 0, then y = 10: all money goes to meals. If y = 0, then x = 4: all money goes to books. If x = 2, then y = 5. Spending is 5 × 2 + 2 × 5 = $20. The slope is −2.5 meals per book. Another book costs as much as 2.5 meals. Budget line for books and meals The feasible boundary slopes from zero books and ten meals to four books and zero meals. Two books and five meals lies on the line. Bundles below the line are feasible under the budget. 0 2 4 10 5 Books (x) Meals (y) (2, 5) Feasible bundles The line is a constraint, not a prediction of what the consumer chooses. The pictured line permits fractional goods as a mathematical representation. If only whole books and meals are available, restrict x and y to nonnegative integers. The line still describes the cost trade-off, but some points are unavailable. A preference model is needed to predict which feasible point the consumer prefers. Graphs and inverse relationships A graph labels an input-output relationship on axes. Always inspect the labels, units and ranges before interpreting it. The same relationship can look very different when an axis is truncated or its scale is changed. A vertical line can describe an equation in two variables, but it is not an ordinary function y = f(x) because one input would correspond to many outputs. An invertible relationship can be rewritten with the other variable as the output. If quantity demanded is Q = 120 − 2P, solving for price gives P = 60 − Q/2. The slope of quantity with respect to price is −2 units per dollar; the slope of price with respect to quantity is −0.5 dollars per unit. These are different units and correspond to different axis arrangements. Economics often places price on the vertical axis and quantity on the horizontal axis even when a demand equation is written as Q(P). State the inverse form when interpreting that graph. Do not assume that the displayed graph's slope is the coefficient in the original equation. Worked example 2 — Solve a simple market model Suppose buyers demand Q d = 120 − 2P and sellers supply Q s = 20 + 3P, measured in units per day and dollars per unit. The proposed equilibrium has equal quantities demanded and supplied. Set 120 − 2P = 20 + 3P. Subtract 20 and add 2P on both sides: 100 = 5P. Divide by 5: P = $20 per unit. Substitute in each equation: Q d = 120 − 40 = 80 and Q s = 20 + 60 = 80. At P = $10, demand is 100 while supply is 50. The model has excess demand of 50 units at that price. Explaining how price actually adjusts requires assumptions about bargaining, contracts, inventories and institutions. Solving an equality does not itself establish the adjustment process or prove that the equations fit a real market. Nonlinear relationships Not every relationship has a constant slope. If total cost is C(q) = 10 + q 2 , increasing output from 1 to 2 raises cost from 11 to 14, an additional 3. Increasing output from 2 to 3 raises cost from 14 to 19, an additional 5. The same output increment has a different cost consequence at a different starting point. Compounding uses powers. An amount growing at rate r for n periods becomes its initial amount multiplied by (1 + r) n . A logarithm reverses exponentiation: if a x = b with appropriate positive values and a ≠ 1, then x = log a (b). Logarithms will help solve growth and timing problems. They do not permit the real logarithm of a negative quantity. Piecewise functions describe rules that change across ranges. A cost can be $5 per unit up to capacity and $8 per extra unit afterward. Write the ranges explicitly. Ignoring a threshold can produce an incorrect optimum even when the arithmetic within each range is correct. Check the economic interpretation After solving, verify equality or inequality in the original expressions. Then check the units, domain, constraints and meaning. Ask which parameters were fixed and whether their values are plausible. A mathematical result is conditional on the specification. The equation is a language for reasoning, not a substitute for it. A precise model can expose a mistaken claim, but it can also produce a precise answer to the wrong question. Tie each symbol back to a measured or conceptual economic quantity. Exercises A service charges $6 plus $3 per hour. Write its charge as a function of hours, calculate the charge for four hours, and solve for the hours corresponding to a $27 charge. State the units of its slope. A $60 budget purchases goods x at $10 each and y at $5 each. Write the feasible set and full-spending boundary. Find both intercepts and the slope of y against x. Is (4, 5) feasible? Solve 7 + 4x = 31 and verify your answer by substitution. Then solve −3x ≤ 12 and explain why the inequality direction changes. Demand is Q d = 150 − 3P and supply is Q s = 30 + 2P. Find equilibrium price and quantity. Calculate excess demand at P = 20. Rewrite Q = 80 − 4P with P as a function of Q. Give the slope and units for each representation. State a nonnegative-quantity domain for P. For C(q) = 20 + 2q + q 2 , calculate costs at q = 0, 1, 2 and 3 and the additional cost of each unit. Does a constant additional cost describe this rule? A $100 amount grows by 10% per period. Write the amount after n periods and calculate it after two periods. Solve 100 × 1.1 n = 121 by recognizing the power. Explain why n is a period count. A graph shows sales and advertising moving together. Explain what its slope could describe and why that relationship alone does not show that more advertising causes the observed sales increase. Propose one alternative explanation. Solutions and evaluation criteria Charge f(h) = 6 + 3h dollars. Four hours costs $18. Set 6 + 3h = 27 to obtain h = 7 hours. The slope is $3 per hour. The set is 10x + 5y ≤ 60 with x,y ≥ 0. The boundary is y = 12 − 2x, with intercepts (0,12) and (6,0). The slope is −2 y-units per x-unit. Bundle (4,5) costs $65 and is infeasible. 4x = 24, so x = 6; 7 + 4 × 6 = 31. Dividing the inequality by −3 gives x ≥ −4. Values at or above −4 satisfy the original inequality. 150 − 3P = 30 + 2P implies 120 = 5P, hence P = 24 and Q = 78. At P = 20, demand is 90 and supply is 70, giving excess demand of 20. P = 20 − Q/4. The Q-on-P slope is −4 quantity units per dollar; the P-on-Q slope is −0.25 dollars per quantity unit. For nonnegative prices and quantities, 0 ≤ P ≤ 20. Costs are 20, 23, 28 and 35. Additional costs are 3, 5 and 7. The function is nonlinear and its additional cost increases over these quantities. The amount is 100 × 1.1 n . After two periods it is $121, so n = 2 solves the equation. The base is the per-period growth factor; the exponent counts applications of that factor. The slope describes an association given the graph's measurement and scales. Firms may advertise more when they expect unusually high demand, or seasonal factors may increase both advertising and sales. A causal claim needs a credible counterfactual and a design that handles confounding. Several explanations are acceptable if their mechanisms are clear. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the budget diagram without using the word “slope” until you have described what one more book requires giving up. Then connect that description to the equation. Delayed check: invent a new two-good budget and market-clearing problem, solve each from scratch, and verify your results in the original equations. Where this leads Calculus studies nonlinear marginal changes and optimization. Linear algebra handles many equations simultaneously. Econometrics estimates relationships from data and examines their interpretation. Throughout these developments, the essential habit is to state the variables, units, domain, assumptions and question before solving."}, {"title": "01.04 · Stocks, flows and balance sheets", "url": "v01-c04.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A snapshot is different from a rate of activity A stock is measured at a point in time. Examples include cash held at the end of a day, outstanding debt on a reporting date, inventory in a warehouse, and the population living in a country. A flow is measured over an interval. Examples include wages earned during a month, purchases made during a quarter, interest charged during a year, and births during a period. Units often expose the distinction. A stock might be $10,000 at 31 December. A flow might be $1,000 per month or $12,000 earned over a year. Comparing a debt stock with annual income is meaningful if the ratio is described as debt relative to one year's income. It is not a statement that debt and income are the same kind of quantity. A frequent mistake is to treat income as wealth. A household can have a high income flow and little net wealth because it spends heavily or owes large debts. Another can have modest current income and substantial wealth from prior savings or asset ownership. Similarly, a government's annual deficit and its outstanding debt measure different objects. Reconcile the opening and closing stock A stock changes through transactions and other changes during the period. A general reconciliation is: Closing stock = opening stock + additions − reductions + other changes For physical inventory, additions can be purchases or production; reductions can be sales, use or spoilage. For cash, additions and reductions are cash receipts and payments. For the measured value of an asset, other changes can include price movements even when no transaction occurs. The categories depend on what the stock measures. Interest capitalized into a loan increases outstanding debt. A payment reduces the obligation according to the contract's allocation rules. When all interest and borrowing are added to a modeled balance and all payments are subtracted from that same balance, the reconciliation must preserve those conventions consistently. If a stock does not reconcile, possibilities include a missing transaction, a valuation change, inconsistent reporting dates, mismatched units, or an error. Identify the difference rather than hiding it in an unexplained “other” category. A reconciliation is an analytical check and often the start of an investigation. Worked example 1 — Reconcile a loan balance A simplified credit balance is $1,000 at the start of a month. During the month, $300 of new borrowing and $20 of interest are added. The borrower makes a $150 payment. There are no fees, write-offs or other changes. Closing debt = 1,000 + 300 + 20 − 150 = $1,170 Debt rises by $170 despite the payment because additions exceed the reduction. The $150 payment is a flow; the $1,170 closing balance is a stock. The payment cannot be equated with a $150 improvement in the household's net wealth without examining the cash used, the interest expense and the other transactions. If the closing statement instead shows $1,190, there is a $20 unexplained difference. Check for a fee, an omitted purchase, timing differences or an incorrect record. A correct analyst does not silently force the reported balance to match by inventing a transaction. Assets, liabilities and equity An asset is a resource or claim held by an entity. A liability is an obligation of that entity. Equity , or net worth in a simplified household balance sheet, is the residual after liabilities are subtracted from assets. The core identity is: Assets = liabilities + equity Equity = assets − liabilities The identity does not say that equity can always be realized at its recorded amount. Asset values may be uncertain; selling can incur costs; liabilities may have contractual and legal complexities. Later accounting and valuation chapters examine measurement. For now, use a consistent specified basis and reporting date. Borrowing money increases cash and liabilities by the same amount at origination in a simple transaction. It does not by itself create income or equity. Spending cash on an asset exchanges one asset for another. Paying down loan principal reduces cash and debt equally. Earning profit can increase equity, while an owner's capital contribution increases equity without being revenue. These distinctions prevent serious errors in business and policy reasoning. Cash inflow is not synonymous with revenue. Cash outflow is not synonymous with expense. A transaction's classification depends on what happened economically and on the specified accounting rules. Profit and cash flow Profit summarizes revenues minus recognized expenses for a period. Cash flow summarizes receipts and payments. Under accrual accounting, revenue can be recorded before a customer pays, and expenses can reflect resources consumed at a different time from the related cash payment. A company that sells $100 of goods on credit can record revenue while receiving no immediate cash. It holds a receivable instead. Purchasing a durable machine is an immediate cash outflow, but its cost is generally allocated across periods through depreciation under the applicable accounting method. A business can therefore be profitable while facing a cash shortage. Likewise, raising a loan can improve cash liquidity while leaving profit unchanged at origination. Interest affects later earnings and cash according to timing; repaying principal reduces the obligation. Keep the income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow statement distinct, then reconcile their connections. Worked example 2 — A simple business from transactions to statements An owner contributes $1,000 cash. The business borrows $600, purchases equipment for $500 cash, purchases inventory for $300 cash, and sells all that inventory for $450 cash. Assume no depreciation, taxes, interest, fees or other transactions during this short illustrative period. Transaction Cash change Other effect Owner contribution +$1,000 Equity +$1,000 Loan received +$600 Debt +$600 Equipment bought −$500 Equipment +$500 Inventory bought −$300 Inventory +$300 Inventory sold +$450 Inventory −$300; profit +$150 Ending cash is 1,000 + 600 − 500 − 300 + 450 = $1,250. Profit is sales revenue $450 minus inventory cost consumed $300 = $150. Assets are cash $1,250 plus equipment $500 = $1,750. Liabilities are $600. Equity is the contribution $1,000 plus retained profit $150 = $1,150. The balance sheet reconciles: 1,750 = 600 + 1,150. Cash-flow categories also reconcile: operating cash flow is 450 − 300 = $150; investing cash flow is −$500; financing cash flow is 1,000 + 600 = $1,600. Their sum is $1,250. The same final cash appears through a different organized view of the transactions. The example's excluded expenses are explicit assumptions, not a claim that real companies avoid depreciation, interest or tax. Including those consequences would change the statements and must be done consistently. The same claim has two sides A financial claim can be an asset for one party and a liability for another. A bank deposit is an asset of the depositor and an obligation of the bank. A loan is an asset of the lender and a liability of the borrower. When combining entities' balance sheets, do not count an internal claim as an external net asset of the combined group. Consider two entities where A lends B $100. A records a $100 claim; B records a $100 liability. Consolidating them eliminates the internal claim and obligation. This does not mean the transaction has no significance: contracts affect incentives, liquidity and risk within the group. It means the consolidated group's external net financial position must not count both sides as new net wealth. For an economy, the interpretation becomes more complex because domestic financial claims, real assets and claims on foreign parties differ. National accounting formalizes those boundaries. The general habit remains valuable: specify the entity or group, the date, the valuation convention and whether the claim is internal or external. Build an auditable reconciliation Begin with an opening statement whose identity balances. Record each transaction with effects on both assets and financing claims. Distinguish a transaction from a valuation change and from an estimate. Recompute the closing statement and explain any difference. Keep the original records so another analyst can follow the steps. A spreadsheet that balances only because an unexplained financing item absorbs every mistake is unreliable. Some forecasting models intentionally use a specified funding policy to close a cash gap; that is acceptable when the policy is explicit. An unexplained balancing plug is not equivalent to a realistic financing decision. Exercises Classify each quantity as a stock or flow and give a suitable time description: outstanding mortgage debt, annual wages, cash on hand, quarterly equipment purchases, inventory, and interest paid during a month. Inventory begins at 80 units. The business buys 50 units, sells 90 and loses 5 to spoilage. Find closing inventory. If the count instead shows 40, calculate the unexplained difference. A household has cash of $2,000, investments valued at $8,000 and debt of $3,000. Find equity. Then show the balance-sheet effects of borrowing another $1,000 and keeping the cash. Using the household after the borrowing in Exercise 3, repay $500 of loan principal with cash. Show closing assets, debt and equity. Explain why the principal payment is not by itself a $500 loss of net worth. A business earns $1,200 of revenue from a sale on credit but receives no cash yet. The sold inventory cost $700 and was already paid for. Ignoring all other expenses, what happens to receivables, inventory, profit, equity and current cash? A business's opening cash is $100. During the period it collects $900 from customers, pays $600 of operating costs, buys $200 of equipment, borrows $300 and repays $150 of principal. Calculate operating, investing and financing cash flows and closing cash. A shareholding is valued at $5,000 at the beginning of a period and $5,400 at the end. There are no purchases, sales or dividends. Explain the $400 stock change and why it is not a transaction inflow to cash. Entity A has $500 cash and a $200 loan claim on B. Entity B has $300 cash and owes A $200. There are no other assets or liabilities. Calculate each entity's net worth and the consolidated group's cash, external liabilities and net worth. Explain the elimination. Solutions and evaluation criteria Mortgage debt, cash and inventory are stocks at a date. Wages, purchases and interest payments are flows over intervals. The units and reporting dates must match the classification. Closing inventory is 80 + 50 − 90 − 5 = 35 units. A count of 40 exceeds the reconciliation by 5. Investigate records, timing and counting rather than assuming an unrecorded purchase. Opening equity is 2,000 + 8,000 − 3,000 = $7,000. Borrowing increases cash to $3,000 and debt to $4,000, leaving assets of $11,000 and equity of $7,000. Cash becomes $2,500, investments remain $8,000 and debt becomes $3,500. Assets are $10,500 and equity remains $7,000. Cash and principal owed decline together. Interest, fees or other consequences would require additional entries. Receivables rise $1,200; inventory falls $700. Profit and equity rise $500. Current cash does not change on this credit sale. Future collection converts the receivable into cash without recording the sale a second time. Operating flow is 900 − 600 = $300. Investing flow is −$200. Financing flow is 300 − 150 = $150. Net cash change is $250; closing cash is $350. Borrowing is financing, not operating revenue. The holding has a $400 valuation increase. It changes measured asset value and, under an appropriately specified measurement basis, net worth; it does not add cash. Accounting recognition of the gain depends on the applicable classification and rules. A's net worth is $700. B's is $300 − $200 = $100. Combined cash is $800; there is no external liability in the stated case. Consolidated net worth is $800. Remove A's internal $200 claim and B's matching $200 obligation; summing the individual net worths also gives $800. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why receiving loan proceeds can increase cash without increasing profit or net worth, and why earning revenue can increase profit without immediately increasing cash. Delayed check: record five original business transactions and reconstruct their effects on cash, other assets, liabilities and equity. The closing identity must balance without an unexplained plug. Where this leads Financial accounting develops formal recognition and measurement. Corporate finance converts forecasts into value. Macroeconomics connects income flows, capital stocks and sector balances. Banking and crises require balance-sheet reasoning because financing claims transmit obligations, liquidity demands and losses across institutions."}, {"title": "01.05 · Compounding and present value", "url": "v01-c05.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Put cash flows on the same date A dollar available today and a dollar promised a year from now differ in timing, risk and purchasing power. To compare choices, specify when each payment occurs and bring the payments to one valuation date. Start with a simple setting: certain cash flows, a constant per-period rate, and a feasible opportunity to borrow or invest at that rate without fees or restrictions. These assumptions make the timing arithmetic transparent. Real decisions may require different rates, credit adjustments, taxes, currency conventions or borrowing constraints. Later finance chapters develop those issues. A correct present-value calculation still needs an economically justified discount rate. Let V 0 be an amount at time 0 and r the rate per period expressed as a decimal. If interest stays invested, the amount after one period is V 0 (1 + r). After two periods, interest also accrues on the previous period's interest. Repeating the rule gives: Future value after n periods = V₀(1 + r)ⁿ Specify what a period means. If r is a monthly rate, n counts months. If r is an annual rate, n counts years. A rate of 5% enters as 0.05. For standard positive discount factors, require 1 + r > 0. Negative rates above −100% are mathematically possible, although their economic interpretation and feasibility must be examined. Compounding and effective rates Simple interest on the original principal alone yields V 0 (1 + nr). Compound interest yields V 0 (1 + r) n . At $100 and 10% per year for two years, simple interest produces $120; annual compounding produces $121. The additional dollar reflects interest earned on the first year's interest. If a nominal annual rate j is compounded m times per year, the specified periodic rate is j/m and its effective annual rate is (1 + j/m) m − 1. That is a rate convention, not a universal interpretation of any quoted number. A 12% nominal rate compounded monthly gives a 1% monthly rate and approximately 12.68% effective annual growth. Fees, interest-calculation conventions and cash-flow timing can make a quoted rate differ from an all-in borrowing cost. Read the specified terms and calculate using the actual payments. Do not assume an illustrative mathematical rate equals a jurisdiction's legally defined disclosure measure. Discounting reverses compounding If an amount C is available n periods from now, the present value is the amount that would grow to C under the specified rate: Present value = C / (1 + r)ⁿ At a 10% annual rate, $1,000 due in two years has a present value of 1,000/1.1 2 , approximately $826.45. Check by compounding the unrounded result forward two years. A higher positive discount rate gives a lower present value for the same positive future payment because less initial money is needed to grow to that payment. For several dated cash flows, discount each to the same date and add: Present value = Σ Cₜ / (1 + r)ᵗ Net present value = −initial cost + present value of later net cash flows The symbol Σ means “sum over the listed periods.” Use net cash flows that include the relevant benefits and costs. Do not subtract an expense again if it was already deducted in constructing a net cash flow. Cash at time 0 has no period of discounting: (1 + r) 0 = 1. Worked example 1 — An investment with two payments A project requires $1,000 now and pays $600 at the end of each of the next two years. Assume these are certain net cash flows, the opportunity rate is 10% annually, and there are no other consequences. Time-0 cash flow is −$1,000. The first payment's present value is 600/1.1 = $545.4545…. The second payment's present value is 600/1.1 2 = $495.8678…. NPV is −1,000 + 545.4545… + 495.8678… = $41.3223…, displayed as $41.32. The positive NPV means the project exceeds the specified feasible opportunity in this setting. Adding $600 + $600 − $1,000 gives $200, but ignores timing. Using the same unrounded amounts, the project's advantage at year 2 is 600 × 1.1 + 600 − 1,000 × 1.1 2 = $50. Discounting $50 back two years gives the same $41.32 NPV. If the opportunity rate is 20%, NPV becomes −1,000 + 600/1.2 + 600/1.2 2 = −$83.33 approximately. A decision can change when an important assumption changes. Uncertain project payments would require a risk analysis rather than being labeled certain for convenience. Annuities and repeated payments An ordinary annuity pays the same amount A at the end of each period for n periods. Let d = 1/(1 + r). Its present value is A(d + d 2 + … + d n ). Multiplying the sum by d shifts every term one period. Subtracting the shifted sum from the original cancels the middle terms: S = d + d² + … + dⁿ dS = d² + d³ + … + dⁿ⁺¹ (1 − d)S = d − dⁿ⁺¹ For r ≠ 0, rearranging yields the annuity formula: PV = A × [1 − (1 + r)⁻ⁿ] / r If r = 0, use PV = nA directly. An annuity paid at the beginning of each period is an annuity due; with identical rates and n payments, it has the ordinary annuity's value multiplied by (1 + r), because each payment arrives one period earlier. Draw the timeline before choosing a formula. A perpetual constant end-of-period payment A has PV = A/r if r > 0 and the payments truly continue indefinitely. This mathematical limit is useful, but a finite business, contract or investment need not actually pay forever. Terminal-value models must justify their assumptions. Worked example 2 — A level-payment loan A loan advances $1,200 now. It has a 1% monthly rate and is repaid through 12 equal end-of-month payments, with no fees or additional charges. The present value of those payments must equal the advance. 1,200 = A × [1 − 1.01⁻¹²] / 0.01 A = 1,200 × 0.01 / [1 − 1.01⁻¹²] A ≈ $106.62 per month Using the unrounded payment, total payments are approximately $1,279.42. The difference from the $1,200 advance is approximately $79.42. Contractual rounding can make the final payment slightly different. The first month's interest is $1,200 × 0.01 = $12. The principal reduction is approximately 106.6185 − 12 = $94.6185, leaving approximately $1,105.3815. As principal declines, the interest portion declines and the principal portion rises if the payment and rate remain constant. The loan recurrence is B t = B t−1 (1 + r) − A. Applying it for 12 periods with the unrounded payment produces a final balance of zero up to numerical precision. This independent check tests the formula against the actual payment schedule. Nominal and real cash flows A nominal amount is expressed in the money units of its date. A real amount is adjusted to a specified purchasing-power basis. If a nominal investment return is r and the applicable price index grows at rate π over the same period, the purchasing-power growth factor is (1 + r)/(1 + π). The exact real return is that factor minus one. A 10% nominal gain with 5% inflation gives 1.10/1.05 − 1, approximately 4.76% real growth, rather than exactly 5%. Subtracting inflation from the nominal return is an approximation that can be useful for small rates. It is not the exact relation. Chapter 7 examines the measurement and interpretation of price indices. Use nominal cash flows with nominal rates and real cash flows with corresponding real rates. Consistency also applies to currency, tax treatment and compounding frequency. A technically correct formula with incompatible inputs produces an economically incorrect comparison. What present value does not decide by itself The calculation does not identify the correct cash-flow forecast, measure every nonmonetary consequence, or determine which risk-adjusted rate fits a real investment. A borrower who cannot finance an attractive project faces a constraint absent from the simple example. Liquidity, default risk and the distribution of outcomes can matter even when expected NPV is positive. Use the timeline and formulas to make assumptions visible. Then ask what could change: payment dates, inflation, reinvestment opportunities, borrowing costs, risk, fees and feasible alternatives. The value of discounting is that it disciplines comparison rather than making uncertainty disappear. Solving for an unknown rate can require a nonlinear equation. For the two-payment loan in Exercise 8, substituting x = 1 + r produces a quadratic. For ax 2 + bx + c = 0 with a ≠ 0, the quadratic formula is x = [−b ± √(b 2 − 4ac)]/(2a). The expression under the square root must be nonnegative for real roots. Check candidates in the original equation and retain only values satisfying the model's domain, including x > 0 for a positive discount factor. Exercises Calculate the future value of $500 after three annual periods at 8% compounded annually. Compare it with simple interest at 8% over the same horizon. Find the present value of $2,000 due three years from now at a 5% annual rate. Verify the answer by compounding it forward. A project costs $900 now and pays $500 at the end of each of the next two years. At a 10% rate, calculate NPV and state the conditional decision. Calculate the present value of three end-of-year payments of $200 at 10% both by summing the payments and by the annuity formula. What is the value if the rate is zero? A contract promises three $100 payments: one immediately, one in a year and one in two years. Calculate its value at a 10% annual rate. Explain why the ordinary three-period annuity formula alone has the wrong timing. A nominal 6% annual rate is compounded monthly. Find the monthly rate and effective annual rate. State why this calculation alone does not measure the cost of a loan that also has fees. An investment gains 8% nominally while the applicable price index rises 3%. Calculate the exact real return and compare it with the subtraction approximation. A loan advances $1,000 now and requires two end-of-year payments of $600. Derive the equation for the effective annual financing rate, express it as a quadratic in x = 1 + r, and solve for the economically admissible positive x. Ignore fees and default. Solutions and evaluation criteria Compound value is 500 × 1.08 3 = $629.856, or $629.86. Simple-interest value is 500 × (1 + 3 × 0.08) = $620. The $9.856 difference comes from compounding. PV = 2,000/1.05 3 = $1,727.675… approximately $1,727.68. Multiplying the unrounded value by 1.05 3 restores $2,000. NPV = −900 + 500/1.1 + 500/1.1 2 = −$32.2314… approximately −$32.23. Under the stated opportunity rate, certain payments and complete consequences, reject the project in favor of the specified alternative. The sum is 200/1.1 + 200/1.1 2 + 200/1.1 3 = $497.370… approximately $497.37. The annuity expression gives the same answer. At zero interest, PV is 3 × 200 = $600. PV = 100 + 100/1.1 + 100/1.1 2 = $273.5537… approximately $273.55. The payments occur at times 0,1,2 rather than 1,2,3. It is a three-payment annuity due. The monthly rate is 0.06/12 = 0.005, or 0.5%. Effective annual growth is 1.005 12 − 1, approximately 6.17%. Fees alter the borrower's actual advance or payments, so calculate from the full cash-flow schedule. The exact return is 1.08/1.03 − 1, approximately 4.85%. Subtraction gives 5%; it is an approximation. 1,000 = 600/x + 600/x 2 , hence 1,000x 2 − 600x − 600 = 0. The positive root is x = (600 + √2,760,000)/2,000 ≈ 1.130662386. Thus r ≈ 13.0662%. The negative root is inadmissible for the positive discount-factor convention. Substitution verifies the two payments' present value equals the advance. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: draw a three-payment timeline and explain why moving a payment one period earlier changes its present value. Derive the ordinary annuity formula by showing which terms cancel. Delayed check: construct a loan balance schedule using the recurrence, verify its final balance, and explain the difference between a quoted periodic rate and an all-in cost based on actual payments. Where this leads Corporate finance evaluates project and business cash flows. Bond valuation discounts contractual payments using a term structure. Asset pricing determines how uncertain payments are valued across states. The simple formulas here establish timing discipline; advanced valuation adds the economics of risk, information and market constraints."}, {"title": "01.06 · Incentives, marginal decisions and equilibrium", "url": "v01-c06.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Actions respond to consequences An incentive changes the consequences of an action. A higher wage can make working an additional hour more attractive. A price increase can make conserving a good more attractive. A penalty for late delivery can encourage timeliness. Predicting the response requires knowing the alternatives, constraints, information and objectives; the existence of an incentive does not determine its magnitude automatically. A person can respond on several margins. A consumer facing a higher fuel price may drive less, buy a more efficient vehicle, move closer to work, or reduce other spending. Those responses take different amounts of time and may be constrained. A short-run response need not equal a long-run response. Incentives can also change reporting instead of the underlying activity. Paying employees for a measured target may encourage real improvement, manipulation of the measurement, or neglect of unmeasured tasks. Good analysis asks what actions are rewarded and what the metric fails to capture. Private choice and social consequences A privately attractive action may impose costs on others. A firm may gain by producing more while its emissions affect neighboring residents. Conversely, a person who improves public health can create benefits beyond their own. These consequences are called external effects when they are not fully accounted for through the relevant decision's prices or arrangements. Separate the decision maker's calculation from the evaluation of everyone's consequences. To predict behavior, ask what the actor faces. To evaluate a policy, specify whose outcomes count, the feasible alternatives and any distributional concerns. A statement that an actor optimized does not prove that the outcome is socially desirable. Rules and institutions shape the feasible choices. Property rights, contracts, enforcement, professional norms and information systems can change the incentive structure. When a policy fails, examine the mechanism and implementation rather than assuming that a stated intention became the actual incentive. Optimization and equilibrium are different ideas Optimization describes an actor choosing the best feasible action under a specified objective. Equilibrium describes a set of choices or prices that are mutually consistent under a model. A competitive market model often requires each buyer and seller to choose appropriately given prices and the quantities supplied and demanded to balance. A point where supply equals demand is a market-clearing condition. It does not show by itself that choices are optimal, that adjustment reaches the point, or that the outcome is fair. A game-theoretic equilibrium uses a different consistency condition: strategies must be best responses to one another. Later chapters formalize both concepts. A model can have multiple equilibria or none under its assumptions. Equilibrium is an analytical concept; observed markets can contain rationing, queues, inventories, delayed contracts or price rules. Use a model's conditions to examine reality rather than assuming reality must satisfy every condition. Worked example 1 — A marginal production decision A producer can sell additional units for $20 each and make up to four additional units. Their incremental costs are $8, $12, $18 and $25. Assume there are no other consequences, each unit can be chosen sequentially, and these costs include all relevant opportunities. The incremental net benefits are 12, 8, 2 and −5 dollars. Producing three units adds $22 of value. Producing a fourth reduces total value to $17. If price falls to $15, the incremental net benefits become 7, 3, −3 and −10. Producing two units is best, adding $10. The response follows a margin: the third unit becomes unattractive when its $18 cost exceeds the new $15 benefit. A historical fixed cost already incurred does not change this additional-unit comparison unless it implies a distinct future consequence. Capacity also matters: even a higher price cannot create a fifth unit if the technology permits only four. Movements along a curve and shifts of a curve A demand relationship specifies quantities buyers would choose at different prices while other specified influences are held fixed. Changing price under that fixed relationship produces a movement along the curve. Changing income, preferences, expectations or another relevant input can change the relationship itself, producing a shift. A supply relationship similarly connects price and offered quantity while fixing technology, input costs and other relevant conditions. Cheaper inputs can shift supply even if the final product's price has not changed. To analyze a new equilibrium, identify which relationship changed, write the new rule and solve the consistency condition. These are conditional statements, not universal signs. A simple demand curve often slopes downward; exceptions and aggregation complications require more careful models. Holding other variables fixed is especially important when observed price and quantity both change because their equilibrium responds to several forces. Worked example 2 — A per-unit tax creates a wedge Buyers demand Q d = 120 − 2P b ; sellers supply Q s = 20 + 3P s . P b is the buyer's price and P s is the seller's receipt. Without tax, both equal $20 and quantity is 80. A $4 per-unit tax creates P b = P s + 4. 120 − 2(Pₛ + 4) = 20 + 3Pₛ 112 − 2Pₛ = 20 + 3Pₛ Pₛ = 92 / 5 = $18.40 Pᵦ = $22.40 Q = 20 + 3(18.40) = 75.20 Relative to the original price, buyers pay $2.40 more and sellers receive $1.60 less. Buyers bear 60% and sellers 40% of the $4 price wedge in this model. Statutory remittance—the party legally sending the tax payment—does not alone determine economic incidence. Tax receipts are 4 × 75.2 = $300.80 per modeled period. That revenue is not itself a complete welfare calculation; some effects are transfers, while changed quantities and external effects require additional analysis. The model excludes evasion, collection costs, market power and effects in other markets. Its fractional quantity is a continuous approximation. Adjustment and institutions If price is below a model's market-clearing price, quantity demanded can exceed quantity supplied. Calling this excess demand is a statement about the model's planned quantities. It does not tell you which buyer receives the available good. Queues, priority rules, lotteries, contracts or other mechanisms can allocate the supply. If price is above the clearing value, planned supply can exceed demand. Sellers might accumulate inventory or revise future production. Whether they cut prices depends on contracts, coordination, information and the relevant market structure. An explicit adjustment rule is needed to make a prediction about speed or stability. A price floor or ceiling changes feasible prices under a policy. If a ceiling lies below the unconstrained clearing price and the simple curves remain applicable, excess demand arises. The policy's actual effect depends on enforcement, quality changes, rationing and other responses. Describe these margins rather than stopping after naming a shortage. Elasticity measures responsiveness A slope measures change in units; an elasticity measures a proportional response. For a finite comparison from a positive base, a simple elasticity calculation divides the percentage change in quantity by the percentage change in price. The base convention must be stated. Later calculus gives point elasticity, and midpoint formulas offer a symmetric finite-change convention. Elasticity is useful because units such as dollars versus cents no longer change its value when the ratio is constructed consistently. It still depends on the starting point, range, time horizon and which other influences are held fixed. “Demand is inelastic” is incomplete without explaining where and over what horizon. In the tax model, the relatively more responsive supply side adjusts quantity more readily and bears less of the price wedge. This is a mechanism to analyze under stated competitive conditions. Incidence in settings with market power, incomplete contracts or dynamic responses can require a different model. Evaluate claims by tracing a mechanism For an incentive claim, identify the affected action, the changed consequence and the constraint. For an equilibrium claim, identify the consistency conditions. For a welfare claim, identify whose consequences are included. Then ask what evidence would support the proposed mechanism or distinguish it from alternatives. A policy can achieve one goal while creating another problem. That is a reason to compare the full consequences and feasible alternatives, not a reason to assume every intervention fails. Transparent analysis states trade-offs, uncertainty and the assumptions on which a recommendation rests. Exercises A producer sells at $12 per unit. The first four incremental-unit costs are $4, $8, $11 and $15. Find the best additional output and total incremental net benefit. Repeat at a $9 price. Demand is Q d = 100 − 2P and supply is Q s = 10 + P. Find the equilibrium. Then income changes demand to 130 − 2P while supply stays fixed. Find the new equilibrium and distinguish the shift from the movement along the unchanged supply curve. Using the original demand and supply from Exercise 2, impose a $6 per-unit tax with P b = P s + 6. Find buyer price, seller price, quantity, buyer and seller shares of the price wedge, and tax receipts. Using the original untaxed model in Exercise 2, set a binding price ceiling of $20. Find planned demand, planned supply and excess demand. Explain why the model does not identify who gets the goods. Price rises from $10 to $12 while quantity demanded falls from 50 to 45 along a fixed relationship. Calculate the percentage changes using the original bases and their ratio. Why would a different finite-change convention need to be stated? A school rewards teachers for a single test score. Describe a response that improves learning and a response that improves the score while harming another educational objective. Identify the relevant measurement limitation. A firm chooses output optimally given prices, but its emissions harm neighbors and are unpriced. Explain why private optimization alone does not establish social efficiency. Identify what a policy comparison would need to include. A commentator observes price and quantity increasing together and concludes that demand curves slope upward. Give a demand-shift explanation consistent with downward-sloping demand. State what additional information is needed to estimate a demand response. Solutions and evaluation criteria At $12, net increments are 8,4,1,−3; produce three for $13. At $9, increments are 5,1,−2,−6; produce two for $6. Compare the cumulative alternatives as a check. 100 − 2P = 10 + P gives P = 30 and Q = 40. After the demand shift, 130 − 2P = 10 + P gives P = 40 and Q = 50. Demand's rule changed; suppliers move along their unchanged relationship as price rises. 100 − 2(P s + 6) = 10 + P s gives P s = 26, P b = 32 and Q = 36. Buyers pay $2 more than 30 and sellers receive $4 less, giving one-third and two-thirds of the $6 wedge. Tax receipts are 6 × 36 = $216. At 20, planned demand is 60 and supply is 30, leaving excess demand of 30. Allocation requires a rationing or trading institution; the two equations alone do not specify it. Price rises 20%; quantity falls 10%; the ratio is −0.5. Original-base and midpoint conventions can differ for finite changes, so an analyst must identify the convention. The ratio describes the stipulated fixed relationship, not an arbitrary observational association. Teachers may improve comprehension through better instruction. They may also narrow instruction to tested items or manipulate reporting. Evaluate actual learning across relevant objectives, not only the rewarded measure. Several coherent mechanisms are acceptable. The firm compares private benefits and costs while omitting the neighbors' harm. A policy analysis must consider that harm, changes in production and consumption, implementation costs, distribution, and feasible alternatives. Private optimality under incomplete prices is insufficient to establish efficiency. An outward demand shift can raise both clearing price and quantity while each demand curve slopes downward. Estimating movement along demand requires separating supply-driven price variation from demand shifts and addressing confounding. Observation of equilibrium changes alone cannot identify the curve. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the difference between an actor's optimum, market clearing, and a socially desirable outcome. Use a tax wedge to explain why the remitting party need not bear the whole economic burden. Delayed check: invent a supply-demand model with a price rule or tax, solve its conditions, and describe an institutional detail that could change the real-world response. Where this leads Microeconomics formalizes preferences, technology, competition and strategic behavior. Public economics analyzes incidence, externalities and distribution. Institutional analysis examines implementation. Econometrics asks whether a mechanism is supported by credible evidence. Incentive and equilibrium reasoning connects those fields."}, {"title": "01.07 · Prices, inflation and purchasing power", "url": "v01-c07.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Separate money amounts from purchasing power A higher salary does not necessarily buy more goods and services. If money income rises by 5% while the prices relevant to a household rise by 8%, the household's purchasing power falls. To analyze this, distinguish a nominal amount, a price level or index, and a real amount expressed on a specified purchasing-power basis. The price of a good is the money required per unit of that good. A relative price compares its price with another price. An economy contains many prices, so a general price index summarizes selected movements using specified items and weights. It is a measurement construction, not a price that every household literally pays. Inflation is the percentage increase in a specified price index over a specified interval. State which index, which population or activity it represents, the interval, and whether the comparison uses adjusted or unadjusted values. A statement that “prices rose 4%” leaves important measurement choices unstated. Construct a simple fixed-basket index Choose a basket of quantities q i,0 in a base period, and price that same basket in each comparison period. Let p i,t be the price of item i in period t. An illustrative fixed-basket index is: Basket cost at time t = Σ pᵢ,ₜ qᵢ,₀ Price index Iₜ = 100 × basket cost at t / basket cost in the base period The base period's index is 100 by construction. An index of 120 means the specified basket costs 20% more than in the base period. It does not mean that every item rose 20%, that every household's expenditures rose 20%, or that inflation during the latest period was 20%. Inflation between two dates uses their ratio: Inflation from s to t = Iₜ / Iₛ − 1 If an index moves from 120 to 126, inflation is 126/120 − 1 = 5%, not 6%. The six-index-point increase is a difference in index levels. The preceding chapter's denominator discipline applies directly. Worked example 1 — Price a basket and deflate income A hypothetical basket contains two loaves of bread and three transit trips. In the base period, bread costs $2 and a trip costs $10. Later, bread costs $3 and a trip costs $11. These are illustrative prices, not observations about October 2026. Base basket cost is 2 × 2 + 3 × 10 = $34. Later basket cost is 2 × 3 + 3 × 11 = $39. The later index is 100 × 39/34 ≈ 114.71. Basket inflation is 39/34 − 1 ≈ 14.71%. If nominal income rises from $3,400 to $3,900, it buys 100 baskets in each period. Purchasing power relative to this basket is unchanged. Bread rises 50%, while trips rise 10%. The basket inflation is neither the unweighted mean of those rates nor a statement that every person experiences the same change. Base expenditure weights are 4/34 for bread and 30/34 for trips. Their weighted price changes reproduce the basket's 5/34 increase. Deflate a nominal amount To express a nominal amount N t in the base period's purchasing power when the index is based at 100, divide by I t /100: Real amount in base-period money = Nₜ / (Iₜ / 100) More generally, to express an amount at time t in the price basis of period b, multiply by I b /I t . An income of $63,000 when the index is 126 equals $50,000 in the price basis of an index at 100. The calculation is conditional on the chosen index being appropriate for the question. For growth between two dates, use nominal growth factor divided by the price growth factor. A nominal wage increase of 6% with 8% inflation produces a real growth factor of 1.06/1.08, or approximately −1.85% real growth. Simply subtracting the rates gives a −2% approximation. Comparisons also need consistent populations and hours. An increase in average annual earnings can reflect more hours or a changing worker composition. A real hourly wage for comparable work answers a different question from average annual nominal earnings across a changing population. Worked example 2 — A wage gain with a purchasing-power loss A worker earns $50,000 at time 0 and $53,000 at time 1. The relevant illustrative price index moves from 100 to 108. Nominal wage growth is 53,000/50,000 − 1 = 6%. Price growth is 108/100 − 1 = 8%. The later wage in time-0 purchasing power is 53,000/1.08 ≈ $49,074.07. Real wage growth is 1.06/1.08 − 1 ≈ −1.85%. The conclusion concerns purchasing power under the chosen basket and assumes comparable work. If the worker's expenditure basket differs substantially, another index could be more informative. Changes in taxes, household size or working hours require separate analysis rather than being silently attributed to inflation. Inflation, disinflation and deflation Inflation describes a rising price index. Disinflation describes a reduction in its positive rate of increase: for example, annual inflation dropping from 8% to 3%. The index can continue rising. Deflation describes a fall in the index, meaning a negative inflation rate over the relevant interval. If an index starts at 100, rises 8%, then rises 3%, it becomes 100 × 1.08 × 1.03 = 111.24. Inflation slowed, but the final price level is 11.24% above the starting level. A slowing increase is different from a reversal. A single good becoming cheaper is not necessarily general deflation. Likewise, an increase in one highly visible price does not establish the inflation rate of a broad basket. Relative price changes can be large even when the general price level changes little. Measurement limits and appropriate indices An index must define the population, items, weights, measurement timing and treatment of changing products. A fixed basket is transparent, but people can substitute between goods when relative prices change. Quality can improve or deteriorate; new goods appear; the relevant basket can evolve. A complete cost-of-living comparison asks how much spending would maintain a specified level of wellbeing, which is more demanding than repricing an unchanged basket. The U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics explains that CPI averages need not match an individual household's experience and that its index is not a complete measure of every factor affecting living standards. Use its documentation for the actual index's scope and methodology. Our two-item calculations are teaching models, not replicas of its full statistical process. BLS CPI documentation . Consumer-price, producer-price and output-price indices answer different questions. A measure designed for household consumption is not automatically the best deflator for a firm's output or a national production aggregate. Choose the index for the conceptual quantity being analyzed and document the match. Index bases and comparisons Rebasing an index changes its scale without changing proportional movements. If the current series is 100, 110 and 121, rebasing the middle date to 100 produces 90.909…, 100 and 110. Both series imply a 10% increase in each interval. Differences in displayed index levels across series do not establish differences in their current purchasing power. Comparing places is another question. An inflation index tracks changes through time within its defined setting; it does not by itself provide a cross-country price-level comparison. Exchange rates convert currencies but can differ from purchasing-power comparisons. Later international economics develops those distinctions. When comparing data releases, distinguish the reference period from the release date. Some series are revised or seasonally adjusted. Record the exact version used in an analysis. This chapter makes no claim about the latest inflation reading; it teaches how to interpret a documented series. Why this matters for decisions Real and nominal consistency affects wages, budgets, investment returns and contract values. A fixed nominal payment can lose purchasing power when prices rise. A nominal debt obligation and a variable-rate debt contract respond differently to inflation and rate changes. The effects also depend on expectations and contractual terms, so a general statement that “inflation helps borrowers” needs qualifications. For analysis, state the price basis of every real number, keep the time period consistent, and preserve the distinction between money gains and purchasing-power gains. The arithmetic is simple enough to audit independently. The harder step is choosing the appropriate economic measure. Exercises A fixed basket contains four units of A and one unit of B. Base prices are $5 and $20; later prices are $6 and $22. Calculate both basket costs, the later base-100 index and inflation. An index rises from 125 to 130. Calculate the index-point increase and the percentage change. Explain why they differ. A salary is $60,000 when the price index is 120. Express it in the purchasing-power basis where the index is 100. If nominal salary rises to $63,000 while the index rises to 126, calculate real salary growth. An asset gains 12% nominally over a period in which the relevant price index rises 7%. Calculate its exact real return. A price index starts at 100 and rises 10%, then 2%. Find the final level. Explain whether the second period has inflation, disinflation or deflation. Rebase the index series 80, 100, 120 so that the first period equals 100. Show that the final-to-middle percentage change is unchanged. A household spends 80% of its base budget on category X and 20% on Y. X prices rise 5% and Y prices rise 20%, with the fixed basket otherwise unchanged. Calculate its basket inflation. Compare with a household whose weights are reversed. A report concludes that every household became 4% worse off because a consumer-price index rose 4%. Identify at least four missing pieces of information or conceptual problems. Give a more defensible statement. Solutions and evaluation criteria Base cost is 4 × 5 + 20 = $40. Later cost is 4 × 6 + 22 = $46. The index is 115 and inflation is 15%. The increase is 5 index points. Percentage growth is 5/125 = 4%. The index-point difference uses the scale; the percentage comparison uses the earlier level as denominator. The first salary is 60,000/1.20 = $50,000 in base purchasing power. The later one is 63,000/1.26 = $50,000. Real growth is zero: nominal salary and the index both rise 5%. Real return is 1.12/1.07 − 1 ≈ 4.67%. The five-percentage-point subtraction is only an approximation. The final index is 100 × 1.10 × 1.02 = 112.2. The second period has 2% inflation and disinflation relative to the earlier 10% rate. It does not have deflation. Multiply all values by 100/80 = 1.25 to obtain 100,125,150. The final-to-middle change is 120/100 − 1 = 20% in the original series and 150/125 − 1 = 20% in the rebased series. First basket inflation is 0.8 × 5% + 0.2 × 20% = 8%. Reversed weights produce 17%. This uses fixed quantities and base expenditure weights; the two households have different exposure. Needed information includes nominal income changes, household-specific expenditure patterns, taxes, household composition, relevant nonmarket outcomes and what “worse off” means. A defensible statement is that the specified index's measured price level rose 4% over the identified interval. Individual purchasing power and wellbeing require further analysis. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why inflation falling does not mean prices have fallen. Demonstrate with three index levels and distinguish a relative price from a general price index. Delayed check: construct a new fixed basket, compute its inflation, rebase its index, and deflate a nominal income without changing the underlying economic comparison. Where this leads Macroeconomics explains inflation mechanisms and policy. Statistics develops index measurement. Finance requires real-nominal consistency in valuation and returns. Distributional analysis asks how different households experience the same set of price changes. Accurate interpretation precedes any policy conclusion."}, {"title": "01.08 · Risk, uncertainty and evidence", "url": "v01-c08.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A decision can be good before its outcome is known Some choices have uncertain consequences. A business expansion can succeed or fail; a forecast can be wrong; an apparently safe receipt can arrive late. Evaluating a decision requires distinguishing the information available when the choice was made from the outcome that eventually occurred. A sound decision can lead to a disappointing realization, and a poorly reasoned gamble can happen to succeed. Evaluate the process using the probabilities, alternatives, constraints and information that were reasonably available at the decision time. Then use the realized outcome to improve future beliefs where it genuinely contains information. Define the outcomes before assigning probabilities. A scenario set should identify distinct possibilities and avoid overlap or omitted cases when it is presented as complete. “Revenue rises,” “revenue doubles,” and “revenue falls” are not three exclusive cases if doubling is included in rising. Make their definitions precise. Probabilities and expectations A probability lies between zero and one. Probabilities assigned to a complete set of mutually exclusive outcomes sum to one. Probabilities can arise from a specified chance mechanism, repeated observations under suitable conditions, or informed judgments. State their basis. Historical frequencies do not automatically apply to a changed environment. The expected value of a quantity is its probability-weighted average across outcomes: Expected value = Σ probability of outcome i × value in outcome i An expectation is not a guaranteed outcome and may not itself be one of the possible outcomes. If a project pays either $100 or −$50, an expected value of $55 does not mean the project literally pays $55 in a third scenario. Use expectations carefully. Expected revenue minus expected expense equals expected profit when the quantities are defined consistently, but a nonlinear function of an expectation generally differs from the expectation of that function. Later probability and decision theory formalize these distinctions. For now, compute each scenario's consequence first when the rule is nonlinear. Worked example 1 — Expected profit does not settle every decision Project A has net profit of $100 with probability 0.7 and a net loss of $50 with probability 0.3. Project B yields a certain net profit of $40. The choices are exclusive and occur over the same horizon. A's expected net profit is 0.7 × 100 + 0.3 × (−50) = $55. B's expected net profit is $40. A exceeds B by $15 in expected monetary profit. A still has a 30% probability of losing $50, while B has no loss in the specified model. An actor who maximizes expected monetary profit and can absorb the outcomes chooses A. An actor with a severe liquidity constraint or a strong preference for avoiding the loss may choose B. Neither preference can be inferred from the expectation alone. Specify the objective and financial capacity before making a recommendation. If A's success probability is p rather than a fixed 0.7, expected profit is 100p − 50(1 − p) = 150p − 50. It exceeds B's $40 when p > 0.6. The threshold shows which belief matters for the choice. Risk, uncertainty and ambiguity Usage differs across fields, but a useful distinction is between outcomes modeled with a specified probability distribution and uncertainty about the distribution itself. The latter is often called ambiguity. Knowing that profits can be +100 or −50 does not establish their probabilities. Where probabilities are poorly supported, present scenarios and sensitivity rather than assigning precision without evidence. For the project above, examine p across a plausible range. If all plausible values imply the same decision, the comparison is robust to that uncertainty. If the threshold lies inside the plausible range, additional information may be valuable. Different risks can interact. A household may lose income precisely when its investment performs badly. Treating the outcomes as independent can understate the bad combined scenario. Later covariance and dependence models quantify such relationships. At this stage, explicitly list which joint outcomes are feasible and why their probabilities are assigned. Downside and capacity to absorb loss Expected value averages across outcomes. It does not describe the worst case, the probability of a cash shortage, the size of losses when they occur, or whether an actor survives to benefit from favorable outcomes. A project with a high expected payoff can be infeasible if a required payment cannot be financed in a bad state. Describe the distribution and constraints relevant to the decision. Useful questions include: How much could be lost? When would cash be needed? Which obligations still fall due? What outcomes would violate a constraint? Can the actor delay, hedge, insure, reduce the position or obtain committed financing? The value of an outcome need not equal its money amount. Losing a small sum from a large buffer may have a different consequence from losing the same sum when it prevents essential spending. Expected-utility models later formalize preferences over uncertain outcomes. They do not eliminate the need to state whose objective and circumstances are being analyzed. Worked example 2 — Interpret a screening signal In an illustrative population of 10,000 applicants, 1% will default. A screening rule flags 90% of future defaulters and also flags 10% of applicants who will not default. Assume these rates apply to this population and that every applicant belongs to one of the two outcome groups. Actual outcome Applicants Flagged Not flagged Default 100 90 10 No default 9,900 990 8,910 Total 10,000 1,080 8,920 Among flagged applicants, 90/1,080 = 1/12 ≈ 8.33% default. The rule catches 90% of all defaults, but that does not mean 90% of flagged applicants default. The low original default frequency and the false-positive rate both matter. The example teaches conditional reasoning, not an actual credit decision. A lending policy would also need the costs of errors, repayment economics, legal constraints, fairness, measurement quality and whether the model transports to a new population. A signal's accuracy statistic alone cannot determine a responsible policy. Information can change a decision Information is valuable when it can improve the choice under the specified objective. Perfect information tells the relevant state before acting. Imperfect information changes beliefs without resolving all uncertainty. More information does not automatically justify a cost: it may not change the best action, or it may arrive after the decision. For an expected-money objective, compute expected payoff when decisions can respond to the signal, then subtract the best expected payoff without the signal. This is the gross value of information under the model. Its net value subtracts the cost of obtaining and using it. Later Bayesian decision theory develops the general calculation. Be careful about hindsight. Information acquired after a loss may explain what happened, but it could not guide a prior decision unless it was available then. Retrospective evaluations should reconstruct the information set at the decision time. Evidence and confidence Separate a claim from the observation offered to support it. An isolated success, an average from selected survivors, and a controlled comparison have different evidential value. Ask who was observed, who was omitted, how quantities were measured, and which alternative explanations remain possible. A large dataset can measure an association precisely while leaving its causal interpretation unresolved. A complicated forecast can be overfit. An impressive backtest can use information that was unavailable at the supposed decision time. Evidence needs a credible design and a transparent workflow, not only many observations or advanced terminology. Use uncertainty statements that identify what is uncertain: the outcome, the probability, the measured data, the causal effect, or the model itself. “We are uncertain” is less useful than explaining which assumption changes the conclusion and what evidence would resolve it. Exercises A project returns net profit of $200 with probability 0.4 and a net loss of $100 with probability 0.6. Calculate its expectation. Compare it with a certain $10 profit under an expected-money objective, and state a reason another objective could change the decision. A payoff is +$200 with probability p and −$100 otherwise. Find the success-probability threshold for positive expected profit. Evaluate the expectation at p = 0.3 and p = 0.5. Scenarios labeled “growth,” “growth above 10%,” and “decline” receive probabilities 0.5, 0.3 and 0.2. Explain why summing to one does not make them a valid exclusive scenario set. Rewrite categories that could be valid. In a population of 1,000, 10% have a specified condition. A signal detects 80% of those cases and falsely flags 10% of those without it. Find the probability of the condition among flagged cases using a count table. A loss of $100 occurs with probability 0.05; otherwise the loss is zero. Full insurance costs $6 and pays the $100 loss if it occurs. Calculate expected net monetary outcomes without and with insurance, ignoring all other consequences. Explain why the expected-money comparison does not decide every person's preference. A project yields +$80 in a favorable state with probability 0.6 and −$40 otherwise. Not undertaking it yields zero. Compute the best expected payoff without information, the expected payoff with perfect information before choosing, and the gross value of perfect information. You have only $30 available, no ability to borrow, and a project may require a $50 payment in a bad state. It has a positive expected profit. Explain why feasibility must be examined separately from expected profitability. A fund reports the performance of ten surviving strategies but excludes fifty discontinued strategies. Write a short critique identifying what evidence is missing, which inference is threatened, and what a better evaluation would require. Do not assume that all discontinued strategies lost money. Solutions and evaluation criteria The expectation is 0.4 × 200 − 0.6 × 100 = $20. It exceeds the certain $10 by $10 for the stated objective. A downside constraint or preference over risk can favor the certain alternative; identify the particular condition instead of asserting a universal preference. Expectation is 200p − 100(1 − p) = 300p − 100. It is positive for p > 1/3 and zero at p = 1/3. At 0.3 it is −$10; at 0.5 it is $50. “Growth above 10%” is included within “growth,” so the events overlap; unchanged output is also omitted unless assigned somewhere. A valid partition could be growth above 10%, growth from 0% through 10%, and negative growth, with zero explicitly in the middle category. Assign justified probabilities to those revised events. There are 100 cases, of which 80 are flagged, and 900 noncases, of which 90 are flagged. Of 170 flagged observations, 80 are cases: 80/170 ≈ 47.06%. Detection rate and the probability conditional on a flag are different. Without insurance, the expected net outcome is −$5. With full insurance and a certain premium, it is −$6 in either state. A risk-neutral expected-money objective favors being uninsured by $1. Loss aversion, risk aversion or a cash constraint can create a preference for insurance. Actual policies can also have exclusions and counterparty risk absent here. Expected project payoff is 0.6 × 80 − 0.4 × 40 = $32, exceeding zero. With perfect information, undertake it only in the favorable state: 0.6 × 80 + 0.4 × 0 = $48. Gross information value is $16 under the objective and assumptions. The bad-state obligation exceeds available cash by $20. Unless the contract or another feasible arrangement avoids or finances it, the project violates the stated constraint. Averaging across states does not produce cash in the bad state. The omitted strategies create selection and survivorship concerns. Ask for the full defined population, launch/closure dates, returns, costs, benchmarks and closure reasons. Reconstruct evaluation using the information available at each date. The critique should identify the threat without inventing the missing performance. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why a high detection rate does not imply a high probability of the condition after a positive signal. Use counts to make the denominator visible. Delayed check: reconstruct a two-state decision, vary the success probability, and identify the threshold at which the recommendation changes. Then add a cash constraint and explain its separate role. Where this leads Probability formalizes distributions and conditioning. Statistics quantifies sampling uncertainty. Econometrics evaluates identification. Finance analyzes risk-bearing, hedging and asset prices. Decision theory connects evidence to actions through objectives and losses. The foundational habit is to keep outcomes, beliefs, evidence and constraints distinct."}, {"title": "01.09 · Money, credit and financial institutions", "url": "v01-c09.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Trace the claims before telling the story Money and credit become easier to understand when you identify who holds an asset and who owes the corresponding obligation. A household's deposit is its claim on a bank. A bank's loan is its claim on a borrower. These relationships do not become clearer when described only as money “moving around.” Write the parties' balance-sheet changes. Money commonly serves as a means of payment, a unit in which prices and obligations are stated, and a way to carry purchasing power across time. These functions need not be perfectly fulfilled by every instrument. Inflation changes purchasing power; a payment arrangement can impose access or settlement constraints; an asset may be valuable without being routinely accepted for payment. A loan is a credit contract. It specifies an advance or borrowing right, repayment obligations, timing, interest and other terms. The borrowing is an asset acquisition and obligation at origination, not earned income. The lender's expected economic value depends on repayment, default, costs and discounting, not only the face amount promised. Deposits and reserves are different claims In a simplified two-tier banking model, customers hold commercial-bank deposits, while banks hold reserve balances used to settle with one another through the central bank's accounts. A customer's balance and the bank's reserve balance are different instruments held by different entities. A deposit liability is not automatically matched by an equal quantity of cash held aside for that specific depositor. A bank holds a portfolio of assets and finances it with deposits, other liabilities and equity. Its ability to meet payments depends on available liquidity and funding, while its solvency depends on the value of its assets relative to obligations. The Bank of England's 2014 account explains that commercial-bank lending can create deposits and that a simple story of banks mechanically multiplying a fixed quantity of central-bank money is misleading. The discussion is a conceptual source, not an assertion about every jurisdiction's current operating rules. Bank of England, Money creation in the modern economy . A loan can create a deposit Consider a modeled bank granting a loan and crediting the borrower's deposit at that same bank. The bank records a loan asset and a matching deposit liability. The borrower records a deposit asset and a loan liability. Neither party gains equity merely from recording equal new claims at the same initial amount. This accounting possibility does not imply unlimited profitable lending. The bank must assess repayment risk, expected income and costs, capital, liquidity, funding, contractual obligations and applicable rules. Borrowers must want and qualify for credit. Subsequent payments can require settlement resources. An accounting entry is not the complete economics of a sustainable banking business. Paying loan principal using a deposit at the same bank can reduce both the loan asset and the deposit liability. Interest has a different effect because it is a payment for financing under the contract and can affect income and equity. Record the allocation between principal, interest and fees explicitly. Worked example 1 — Origination and a payment to another bank Bank A begins with reserves of $100 and loans of $400. It owes customer deposits of $450 and has equity of $50. Its identity is 100 + 400 = 450 + 50. It grants a new $50 loan and credits the borrower's deposit. Loans rise to $450 and deposits to $500. Reserves remain $100 and equity $50. Assets and liabilities plus equity both become $550. The borrower pays $40 to a seller at Bank B. Assume settlement transfers $40 of reserves from A to B. Bank A's reserves fall to $60 and deposits to $460. Its loans remain $450. Its balance sheet is 60 + 450 = 460 + 50. Bank B gains $40 reserves and records a $40 deposit for the seller. Its equity does not change from that payment alone. The cross-bank payment changes the location of deposits and reserves. It does not mean that the borrower's loan vanished. If the borrower later repays $10 of principal using a deposit at A, A's loans and deposits both fall $10: 60 + 440 = 450 + 50. Every step identifies the entity and the paired entries. The example assumes available settlement resources and no fees or default. Different settlement arrangements require corresponding additional entries; the accounting must match the specified institution. Payments, settlement and liquidity A payment instruction, the customer's balance change, and final settlement are conceptually distinct steps. An instruction might be authorized before the receiving party has an unconditional settled claim. A payment arrangement defines how obligations are transferred or discharged and what happens if a participant cannot meet them. Liquidity is the ability to obtain the resources needed to meet obligations when due at acceptable cost. An institution can own valuable assets and still have difficulty obtaining cash quickly. Selling a long-horizon asset immediately may require a discount; borrowing can require collateral and willing counterparties. Solvency concerns whether assets adequately cover obligations under an appropriate economic valuation. A shortfall of immediate cash is not identical to negative net worth. They can interact: distress sales can reduce asset values, and doubts about solvency can make funding harder to obtain. Later banking and crisis chapters analyze the feedback. Worked example 2 — A withdrawal and a credit loss Start again with a bank holding $100 of immediately usable settlement resources and $400 of loans, financed by $450 deposits and $50 equity. Assume no other claims or valuation differences. Depositors withdraw $80 using those resources. Cash/reserves fall to $20 and deposits to $370. Assets are now $420 and liabilities $370. Equity remains $50: withdrawals reduce an asset and a liability together. A further immediate $30 withdrawal exceeds the $20 available resources. The bank needs another feasible funding or asset-conversion arrangement. Separately, suppose the loans lose $60 of economic value and the loss is recognized. Loan value becomes $340; total assets become $360. With liabilities still $370, economic equity becomes −$10. The cash shortfall and value shortfall are different problems. Borrowing another $10 can cover a payment only if financing is available; it adds both cash and an obligation and does not automatically repair negative equity. New equity, asset recovery or a restructuring can affect solvency in different ways. A real response requires contractual, institutional and legal analysis. Other financial institutions and securities A bond represents a specified debt claim. Equity represents an ownership claim whose payoff depends on the entity and the rights attached to the instrument. Funds pool investments under a mandate. Insurers combine underwriting, investment and contractual payout obligations. Exchanges and intermediaries support trading and transfer of claims. The labels do not remove risk. A debt promise can default. An ownership claim can lose value. An investment vehicle can have redemption constraints and fees. To understand an instrument, identify cash flows, claim priority, contingencies, timing, liquidity and governance. Later volumes develop valuation and specific structures. Intermediation can provide useful services: collecting information, monitoring borrowers, matching financial needs, pooling risks and facilitating payments. It can also create incentive conflicts and fragility. Evaluate the actual mechanism rather than assuming that every intermediary performs the same function. Central-bank roles and limits of a simple story A central bank can occupy roles involving currency, settlement, monetary policy and financial stability, with responsibilities depending on its legal and institutional setting. Commercial banks and a central bank have different balance sheets, objectives and powers. A central-bank asset purchase exchanges claims; understanding its effects requires tracing the counterparties and payment mechanism. Do not infer that expanding one monetary quantity guarantees a fixed increase in lending, prices or output. The consequences depend on behavior, constraints, expectations and the policy framework. Monetary economics later connects these mechanisms to equilibrium and evidence. For any current institutional claim, identify the jurisdiction and date and use the relevant official source. This introductory model does not state current reserve requirements, deposit-insurance limits, policy rates or emergency lending terms. Those are dated facts, while the paired-entry reasoning is a reusable analytical tool. Exercises A bank has $80 reserves, $320 loans, $350 deposits and $50 equity. It grants a $40 loan by crediting a deposit. Write its new balance sheet and the borrower's paired entries. What happens to equity at origination? Following Exercise 1, the borrower pays $25 to a customer at another bank, settled by transferring reserves. Show the first bank's new reserves, loans, deposits and equity. Show the receiving bank's transaction changes. Following Exercise 2, $15 of loan principal is repaid from a deposit at the first bank. Show the balance-sheet changes and closing identity, excluding interest and fees. A bank has assets of $600, liabilities of $540 and equity of $60. It recognizes a $75 asset-value loss. Find closing assets and equity with liabilities unchanged. Explain why borrowing $20 at face value does not fix the net-worth shortfall by itself. An institution has valuable assets of $200 due next year, $10 cash, and a $25 payment due today. Assume it has positive net worth. Identify the immediate liquidity problem and two possible responses whose feasibility would need checking. A household borrows $500 and immediately buys equipment for $500. Ignoring fees and valuation differences, trace its cash, equipment, debt and net-worth changes. Explain why the borrowing is not $500 of income. Someone says, “If banks can create deposits when lending, they can lend profitably without limits.” Identify at least five omitted economic or institutional constraints. Compare a deposit, a bond and an equity claim using holder, issuer obligation, contingent payoff, timing and liquidity. Explain why neither the name nor the promised face value proves safety. Solutions and evaluation criteria The bank has reserves 80, loans 360, deposits 390 and equity 50: 440 = 390 + 50. The borrower gains a $40 deposit asset and a $40 loan obligation. Equity is unchanged at initial equal values. The first bank has reserves 55, loans 360, deposits 365 and equity 50: 415 = 365 + 50. The receiving bank gains $25 reserves and a matching $25 customer deposit liability, with no equity change from the transfer alone. Loans become 345 and deposits 350; reserves remain 55 and equity 50. Assets are 400 = 350 + 50. Principal repayment reduces both loan asset and deposit liability. Assets fall to 525, so equity is 525 − 540 = −$15. Borrowing $20 raises both assets and liabilities by $20, leaving equity −$15 absent other consequences. The institution lacks $15 of immediately usable resources. It might sell or pledge an asset, obtain a loan, secure a capital contribution or renegotiate timing. Each option has costs and conditions; positive net worth does not guarantee immediate access. Loan origination raises cash and debt by $500. The purchase exchanges the $500 cash for $500 equipment. Net worth remains unchanged in the specified initial-value accounting. Income arises from earning or relevant subsequent gains, not the equal borrowing entries. Relevant constraints include borrower demand, creditworthiness, expected losses, funding costs, capital, liquidity and settlement, regulation, collateral, competition and risk appetite. The answer should explain how several affect feasible or profitable lending. Identify the actual contract and parties. A deposit is a claim on a bank, a bond has specified debt terms, and equity has ownership rights and contingent residual outcomes. Liquidity and repayment depend on the instrument and setting. Do not invent universal insurance or legal guarantees. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: trace a loan, an interbank payment, and a principal repayment on both sides of the transactions. Explain the distinction between reserve resources and a customer's deposit. Delayed check: construct a balance-sheet case with a payment shortage but positive net worth, then one with enough immediate cash but negative economic net worth. Explain why the remedies differ. Where this leads Accounting formalizes measurement. Corporate finance studies financing claims. Banking develops intermediation, capital and liquidity. Monetary economics examines policy transmission. Crisis analysis studies how claims, funding and asset values interact under stress. All depend on tracing who owes what to whom."}, {"title": "01.10 · Models, causation and economic arguments", "url": "v01-c10.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Four kinds of statement An economic argument can contain definitions, accounting identities, behavioral models, and empirical claims. Confusing them allows weak reasoning to appear stronger than it is. Ask what kind of statement is being made and what would establish it. A definition specifies how a concept is used. An identity follows from the defined components, such as assets equaling liabilities plus equity. A behavioral model describes how choices or outcomes depend on assumptions. An empirical claim concerns the world and requires evidence from an appropriate measurement and research design. A policy recommendation adds values and a choice among alternatives. Evidence that a policy raises total output does not alone establish that it should be adopted: distribution, costs, rights, feasibility and other objectives may matter. Separate the positive claim about consequences from the normative judgment about which consequences are desirable. Models isolate mechanisms A model deliberately abstracts from some features to examine others. It should define the actors, objectives, constraints, information, sequence of events and consistency conditions. Its conclusion follows conditionally from those choices. The question is whether the abstraction clarifies the mechanism relevant to the problem. “Holding other things fixed” makes a conditional comparison. A demand response to price with income fixed differs from a historical change in which price, income, product quality and expectations all moved. A model can help interpret that historical change, but the data do not automatically isolate the model's particular response. Assess a model by its logic, its relevance and its confrontation with evidence. An unrealistic assumption can still help isolate a useful mechanism, but a conclusion that depends critically on a false institutional feature can mislead. State which assumptions are essential and test how the conclusion changes when they are relaxed. Causation requires a counterfactual A causal effect compares what happens under an intervention with what would happen without that intervention for the relevant units and setting. The unobserved alternative is the counterfactual . Observing an outcome after an action does not reveal the counterfactual by itself. If a company trains workers and productivity later rises, possible explanations include training, improving demand, new equipment, worker selection or measurement changes. A causal claim needs a way to distinguish those mechanisms. Naming plausible alternatives is a useful start, but not an estimate of their magnitudes. Identification concerns whether the target causal or structural quantity can be recovered under the stated information and assumptions. Estimation then calculates it from finite data. A precisely estimated association can remain causally unidentified. Large sample size addresses some sampling uncertainty; it does not automatically repair confounding. Worked example 1 — Why a before-and-after change is incomplete A shop's weekly sales rise from $1,000 to $1,300 after it begins advertising. The manager attributes the entire $300 increase to the advertisement. During the same period, a comparable untreated shop's sales rise from $1,000 to $1,200. The treated shop's before-after increase is $300. The comparison shop's increase is $200. The difference in those changes is $100. Under an assumption that the comparison's $200 trend describes the treated shop's counterfactual change without advertising, the implied advertising effect is $100. The fourth step is the crucial assumption. The shops may face different customers, demand shocks or spillovers. They may not be comparable even if their original sales levels match. The $100 calculation is transparent, but its causal interpretation remains conditional on the design. This introduces the intuition of a difference-in-differences comparison. The advanced econometrics volume examines its assumptions, treatment timing, heterogeneous effects and inference in detail. A single illustrative pair of shops cannot validate those conditions. Randomization and comparisons Random assignment can make treatment and control groups comparable in expectation before the intervention, helping distinguish its effect from systematic selection. The intervention must be implemented and measured consistently. Attrition, noncompliance, interference across participants and chance imbalance can still complicate interpretation. Random assignment is different from random sampling. Assignment concerns who receives an intervention and can support internal causal comparisons. Sampling concerns who enters a study and influences how results generalize to a larger population. A randomized intervention among volunteers does not automatically identify effects for everyone elsewhere. Observational studies can also support causal reasoning when their design and assumptions are credible. Potential strategies include using a documented assignment threshold, an external source of variation or a carefully justified comparison. Each has conditions and limitations. The label attached to a method cannot substitute for examining them. Worked example 2 — Describe an experiment's result honestly In an illustrative randomized study, 200 participants are assigned to a program and 200 to control. At the specified follow-up, 40 program participants and 60 control participants have the adverse outcome. Assume outcomes are observed for everyone, assignment is implemented as intended and there are no spillovers. The observed program rate is 40/200 = 20%. The observed control rate is 60/200 = 30%. The observed difference is −10 percentage points. The relative reduction compared with the control rate is (30% − 20%)/30% = 1/3, approximately 33.33%. State the population, intervention, outcome and horizon. These figures are sample results, not proof that the exact population effect is ten percentage points or that the same effect applies in a different setting. Statistical inference quantifies sampling or assignment uncertainty under appropriate assumptions. For a policy decision, also ask about costs, implementation, other outcomes and distribution. A causally interpretable benefit is important evidence, but it is not the entire decision criterion. Prediction answers a different question A predictor aims to forecast an outcome using information available before the forecast target is realized. A causal analysis asks what would happen if an action were changed. A variable can be highly predictive without being a useful intervention. An umbrella can predict rain because people carry one when they expect rain. Giving everyone an umbrella does not create rainfall. In an economic setting, a company's expansion announcement may predict future sales because management already knows demand is improving. Changing the announcement alone need not generate the sales increase. Predictive evaluation must respect timing. If a backtest uses next month's information in a supposed decision today, it benefits from leakage. If a model is repeatedly modified until it fits the test period, that period is no longer an independent test. Later computation and econometrics provide disciplined evaluation procedures. Falsifiability and competing explanations A useful claim states what observations would be consistent with it and what would count against it. “The policy always works, except whenever it does not” provides no meaningful test. A model can specify conditional predictions whose validity depends on measurable conditions. Do not treat failure to reject a claim as proof that it is true. Evidence can be insufficient, measurement weak, or the study underpowered. Likewise, a statistically detectable association can be economically small or irrelevant to the decision. Distinguish correctness, precision, magnitude and practical consequence. Compare explanations through different predicted observations or designs that separate them. If both a supply shock and a demand shock could explain a price increase, examine the quantity change, input costs, institutional details and credible sources of variation. Several mechanisms can operate together; a forced single-cause story can be wrong. Make the argument auditable A strong argument names the question, defines the target quantity, states the model or design, presents the evidence and explains uncertainty. It separates observed facts from inference and gives the alternatives that remain plausible. A reader should be able to follow why each step supports the next. Sources should support the associated claim, not merely discuss the same topic. Dates and units matter. An opinion article, a dataset release, a theoretical paper and a causal study have different roles. Use each for what it actually establishes. Economic reasoning remains useful even before advanced methods are learned. The essential discipline is to avoid moving from identity to behavior, from correlation to causation, from prediction to intervention, or from one valued outcome to an entire policy conclusion without explaining the additional assumptions. Exercises Classify these statements as definition, identity, behavioral hypothesis, empirical claim or recommendation: “Equity equals assets minus liabilities”; “Consumers buy less when price rises, other relevant influences fixed”; “The observed program group earned 12% more”; “The government should adopt the program”; “A stock is measured at a point in time.” A firm reports sales rising from $500 to $650 after a pricing change. An untreated comparison rises from $400 to $500. Calculate both changes and their difference. State the assumption required for the difference to represent the pricing change's causal effect. In a randomized illustrative study, 100 of 500 treated participants and 150 of 500 controls experience an adverse outcome. Calculate both rates, their percentage-point difference and the relative reduction. Explain why uncertainty still matters. A survey finds that people with more schooling have higher income. Identify three possible explanations for the association and explain what a causal effect of additional schooling would compare. Explain the difference between random sampling and random assignment. Give a setting with assignment but a limited sampled population. A prediction model for defaults uses a field recorded only after a borrower defaults. Explain the evaluation error and how an honest forecasting dataset should be constructed. A researcher finds no statistically significant effect and concludes that the intervention has no effect. Identify missing information and give a more careful interpretation. Write a causal claim about an economic choice. State its counterfactual, one confounder, a possible design, an assumption that design needs, and one limit to generalizing the result. Solutions and evaluation criteria Respectively: identity; behavioral hypothesis; empirical descriptive claim; normative recommendation; definition. The reported group difference alone is not necessarily causal. The treated change is $150 and comparison change $100, leaving $50. Causal interpretation requires the comparison's change to represent the treated firm's counterfactual trend without its pricing change, with relevant spillovers or differential shocks addressed. The rates are 20% and 30%, a −10 percentage-point treated-minus-control difference and approximately 33.33% relative reduction. These are realized sample rates; inference is needed for uncertainty about the target effect, and external validity is separate. Schooling may increase skills; family resources may affect both schooling and income; ability, location or selection may contribute. A causal effect compares the same relevant person's or population's income under differing schooling interventions, with other design conditions specified. Sampling determines study inclusion; assignment determines treatment among included units. Randomized assignment within a volunteer group can support an internal comparison without establishing that the group represents all possible recipients. The model uses post-outcome information unavailable at forecast time, causing leakage. Freeze features according to their actual availability at each decision date, preserve a genuinely held-out evaluation, and document timing. Needed information includes the effect estimate, interval, study size, power, design, measurement and relevant effect magnitude. A nonsignificant result can be consistent with small, zero or sizable effects depending on the uncertainty. State what the evidence can and cannot exclude. A strong answer defines an intervention and outcome, identifies the counterfactual, explains the confounding mechanism, gives a feasible design and its identifying assumptions, and recognizes a population or institutional limit. Several well-supported designs are possible. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain how an identity can be true without identifying the response to an intervention. Then explain why a highly predictive variable might be a poor policy lever. Delayed check: find an economic causal claim in a public discussion. Reconstruct its counterfactual, evidence, assumptions and feasible competing explanation without appealing to the speaker's status. Where this leads Economic theory clarifies mechanisms and consistency. Econometrics formalizes identification and inference. Research practice evaluates contribution and reproducibility. Teaching and leadership require communicating those distinctions clearly when decisions must be made despite incomplete evidence."}, {"title": "01.11 · Reading, writing and explaining analysis", "url": "v01-c11.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Make reasoning visible to another person Analysis has limited value if another person cannot determine what was asked, what was measured, how the conclusion follows, and what could change it. Good communication is part of technical competence. It forces the analyst to identify hidden assumptions and separate a defensible inference from an attractive story. Begin with the question. “Describe unemployment” is broad; “Did the unemployment rate fall because employment rose or because participation changed?” directs attention to particular counts and mechanisms. “Should we expand?” requires a comparison among feasible actions, not just a forecast of revenue. Write the conclusion early, with the condition that matters most. Then present the evidence and reasoning needed to assess it. A reader should not have to search through computations to discover whether the project was recommended, which alternative was considered, or which assumption makes the difference. A reusable analytical memo A concise memo can follow five elements: the question and decision; the recommendation or finding; the mechanism and calculation; uncertainty and alternatives; and the action or evidence needed next. The arrangement can change with the audience, but the chain of reasoning must remain clear. For an executive, a central risk and its consequence can matter more than the derivation's intermediate lines. For a technical reviewer, the derivation and identification assumptions must be accessible. Keep supporting detail in a table or appendix rather than replacing it with unexplained authority. State who benefits, who bears costs and which objective guides the recommendation. “This is optimal” is incomplete without specifying the objective and constraints. An economically positive project may conflict with another institutional priority, face financing constraints, or distribute costs unequally. Distinguish facts, assumptions and inference A measured fact might be the observed sale count in a supplied dataset. An assumption might be that its reporting coverage is consistent through time. An inference might be that demand changed. A recommendation might be to increase capacity. Label the transitions rather than presenting all four as equally established facts. Use language that matches the evidence. “The supplied records show,” “under the stated assumption,” “this is consistent with,” and “the design identifies” have different meanings. Avoid changing “associated with” to “caused by” during a summary. Concision should preserve the epistemic status of the claim. Quantify an assumption's relevance when possible. “Results are sensitive” is vague. “The recommendation switches when the success probability falls below 60%” identifies a decision threshold. “The effect's plausible range includes outcomes that would reverse the decision” explains why uncertainty matters. Worked example 1 — Rewrite a revenue-only recommendation An analyst writes: “Buy the equipment. It will bring in $600 next year and $600 the year after. The total is $1,200, so we make $200.” The equipment costs $1,000 now, and the specified opportunity rate is 10% annually. A more complete version is: Under the supplied certain net-cash-flow assumptions, purchase is better than the specified 10% opportunity by $41.32 in present value: −1,000 + 600/1.1 + 600/1.1². The recommendation requires the forecast to include all relevant operating costs and the purchase to be financeable. At a 20% discount rate, NPV is −$83.33. Verify the cash-flow forecast and rate before committing. The revised memo names the objective, timing, alternative and assumptions. It does not convert unverified forecasts into facts. It also distinguishes net receipts from revenue, a difference that could materially change the calculation. Tables and graphs are arguments A table should make comparisons easy to inspect. Put like quantities in the same column, give units in headers, identify the time period, and include denominators when rates matter. Totals and subtotals should reconcile. A note should explain missing values, rounding and relevant measurement choices. A graph must label axes and scales and identify the data or model it represents. A bar chart with a truncated axis can exaggerate differences; a time-series plot can use a narrower range appropriately if its purpose and scale are explicit. The issue is whether the presentation makes the comparison understandable rather than manipulating its impression. A plotted line connecting observations can suggest continuous measurement or a trend model. A diagram of a theoretical relationship is different from a graph of observed data. Label it as a model or illustrative example when that is what it is. Do not add fabricated empirical observations for visual polish. Worked example 2 — Show counts as well as rates A report says, “Unemployment improved from 10% to 9%.” The underlying illustrative labor-force counts are: Period Labor force Unemployed Unemployment rate 0 1,000 people 100 people 10% 1 1,200 people 108 people 9% A careful summary is: “The unemployment rate fell one percentage point, while the unemployed count rose 8%. The labor force increased 20%, changing the denominator. These figures alone do not establish the cause or the change in every person's labor-market experience.” The table preserves the facts needed to assess the claim. Its purpose is not to declare whether every aspect improved or worsened; it separates the measured quantities before evaluating them. Source provenance and reproducibility A citation should let a reader locate the material supporting a claim. Record the source, dataset or series identifier, date or version, measurement units, and transformations. For a hypothetical example, say that its numbers are constructed. A reader should never have to guess whether a figure is observed or invented for teaching. Reproducibility means another person can follow the documented inputs and procedure to obtain the stated output, within explained numerical differences. For an early manual calculation, supply the original values and steps. For later computational work, provide the code, data access instructions, environment and relevant settings. Reproducing a result is important but does not prove that its interpretation is correct. A biased analysis can reproduce perfectly. Keep computational reliability, measurement validity and causal interpretation as distinct review questions. Explain a mechanism before its vocabulary Technical vocabulary is efficient once a concept is understood. Introduce the idea first: an extra book requires giving up some meals under a budget. Then name the opportunity cost and slope. If the learner can recite the term but cannot reason through a new example, the instruction has not achieved transfer. Use multiple representations carefully. An intuitive story, a balance-sheet table, an equation and a graph can describe the same mechanism. Check that they agree. An analogy helps when its relevant mapping is explicit; it misleads when an irrelevant feature of the analogy becomes part of the argument. Ask learners to reconstruct a result rather than merely recognize it. “Explain why principal repayment leaves initial-value net worth unchanged” is more diagnostic than “Is principal repayment an expense?” Follow with a case containing interest or fees to see whether the distinction survives a changed problem. Read actively and fairly When reading an argument, reconstruct its strongest defensible version before criticizing it. Identify its question, definitions, assumptions, evidence and conclusion. Determine whether the objection concerns mathematical correctness, measurement, identification, generalization or the objective being optimized. A disagreement about values should not be disguised as a false arithmetic claim. A disagreement about causal identification should not be dismissed as a difference in taste. Precise criticism helps the author revise the part that actually needs work. Keep a record of questions rather than copying every sentence into notes. For each important result, write its mechanism, conditions and one counterexample or boundary case. Return later and derive it without looking. This practice creates a testable record of understanding. Editing for clarity Remove repetition, unsupported adjectives and claims of importance that do not explain a consequence. Replace “it is obvious” with the reasoning. Replace an unexplained acronym with a definition where it first appears. Use units and concrete verbs. A sentence should contribute a fact, a logical step, a condition or a decision-relevant implication. Keep essential qualifications near the claim they limit. Hiding all assumptions at the end of a long document can allow the main text to overstate its conclusion. The aim is readable rigor: enough detail to examine the reasoning, organized so the reader can find it. Exercises Rewrite: “Profit grew 200%, proving the strategy worked.” Specify the missing base, period, population, costs and causal evidence. Give a version that reports only what the supplied statement could establish. A project costs $500 now and pays a certain $600 in one year. At a 10% opportunity rate, calculate NPV and write a 75-word memo including timing, assumptions and the financing question. Build a table for two divisions: A has $200 revenue and $50 profit; B has $800 revenue and $40 profit. Include revenues, profits and margins and calculate the consolidated margin. Write one sentence explaining why an equal-weight average answers a different question. Rewrite: “Inflation fell from 6% to 2%, so prices became cheaper.” State the correct inference and give an illustrative two-period index path starting at 100. Read an economic claim and classify each supporting item as measured evidence, assumption, calculation, inference or recommendation. Identify a transition that would need additional justification. Design a provenance note for a hypothetical chart of annual real wages. Specify the information a reader needs, including how “real” is defined. Explain what changes if the numbers are constructed for teaching. Prepare a two-minute explanation of why a loan can increase cash without increasing equity at origination. Use one table and one everyday explanation, then ask a question that tests transfer. Give a fair critique of this claim: “Our randomized study among 100 volunteers identifies the effect of the program everywhere.” Separate the possible internal validity from external validity and list further evidence needed. Solutions and evaluation criteria A 200% increase requires a defined positive base and comparable period. Even if measured correctly, it is a descriptive result, not proof of causation. A careful rewrite states the specific observed profit change, its accounting basis and period, then says the strategy's causal contribution remains to be established. NPV is −500 + 600/1.1 = $45.4545… approximately $45.45. A strong memo gives a conditional recommendation, identifies the assumed complete certain net payment, names the 10% alternative and checks whether financing is feasible. Its word count should not force omission of the decisive assumptions. A's margin is 25%; B's is 5%. Total profit is $90 on $1,000 revenue, so the consolidated margin is 9%. Equal weighting gives 15% for a typical two-division average, while consolidated performance requires revenue weights. The positive inflation rate slowed. An index rises from 100 to 106, then to 108.12. Prices in that index continue rising in the second period; they do not fall. Assess whether categories are separated accurately and whether the chosen transition needs an additional measurement, identifying assumption or normative criterion. The content of the selected claim determines the answer. Record the wage source/series, population, hours basis, period, currency, nominal measure, price index, base year, vintage and transformation. If constructed, label the chart illustrative and supply the invented values without presenting them as observations. The table should add equal cash and debt at initial equal values, leaving equity unchanged. A good transfer question adds a fee, interest or asset purchase and asks which entries differ. Evaluate the learner's reconstruction, not their recognition of a slogan. Random assignment can support a causal comparison among participants under implementation, measurement and interference assumptions. Generalization to other populations and institutions needs evidence about representativeness, mechanisms, treatment differences and context. The sample size also affects precision; it does not alone invalidate randomization. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: present a finding in 30 seconds, then defend it for five minutes. The short version must preserve the principal assumption and uncertainty; the long version must expose the reasoning. Delayed check: revisit an old memo and reconstruct its calculations from the documented inputs alone. Identify one claim whose wording is stronger than its evidence, if any, and revise it. Where this leads Research papers require an explicit contribution and auditable methods. Teaching requires diagnosing understanding. Institutional leadership requires decisions and accountability. Clear communication is the bridge between analysis and those responsibilities."}, {"title": "01.12 · Foundation integration and diagnostic", "url": "v01-c12.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Integrate the foundations before moving ahead The earlier chapters taught separate habits: compare feasible alternatives, preserve units and denominators, express relationships, reconcile stocks and flows, discount dated cash flows, trace incentives, deflate money amounts, reason under uncertainty, identify financial claims, evaluate causal arguments and communicate clearly. This chapter puts those habits into one case. Integration is a test of whether you can decide which tool applies. A formula named in a question is easier to recognize than a method hidden inside a realistic narrative. Begin by organizing the case into entities, dates, units, decisions and assumptions. Then perform calculations in a sequence that allows independent checks. Every number below is constructed for instruction. The case is deliberately simplified: it excludes taxes, depreciation and many contract details so the relevant relationships can be seen. Those exclusions must remain visible. Later volumes add the omitted economics and accounting rather than pretending the simplified case is a complete business model. The business case An owner contributes $1,800 to a newly formed business. The business borrows $1,200, purchases equipment for $1,000 cash and inventory for $800 cash, and sells the entire inventory for $1,200 cash. It pays $60 interest during the illustrative period. No principal is repaid. There are no other transactions, expenses or valuation changes. The questions are: What happened to cash and equity? Did the business earn its borrowing as revenue? Can its future investment proposal be judged from revenue alone? Which conclusions follow by accounting, and which need behavioral or empirical evidence? Worked example 1 — Statements that reconcile Cash changes are +1,800 +1,200 −1,000 −800 +1,200 −60 = +$2,340 from a zero opening balance. Revenue is $1,200. Inventory expense is $800, and interest expense is $60. Profit is $340. Closing assets are cash $2,340 plus equipment $1,000 = $3,340. Closing liabilities are $1,200. Equity is the $1,800 contribution plus $340 retained profit = $2,140. The identity holds: 3,340 = 1,200 + 2,140. The cash inflow from borrowing is financing, not sales revenue. The equipment purchase converts cash into another asset. This simplified case explicitly omits depreciation; introducing it would affect equipment's carrying amount and profit consistently, while not creating an additional cash payment at recognition. For cash-flow presentation, classification can depend on the applicable rules. An internal analysis can separately show operating receipts and inventory payments of $400 net, an interest payment of $60, equipment investment of −$1,000, and initial financing of $3,000. Their sum remains $2,340 regardless of how the stated interest payment is grouped. A future investment proposal For the next one-year period, the business can spend $800 immediately on a separate project. Its end-of-year net receipt is $1,200 in a favorable state and $700 in an unfavorable state. The assigned favorable-state probability is 0.6. Assume these receipts already include all subsequent project costs; the initial $800 is not included in them. The expected receipt is 0.6 × 1,200 + 0.4 × 700 = $1,000. At a specified 10% discount benchmark, the discounted expectation minus the initial expenditure is 1,000/1.1 − 800 ≈ $109.09. This is a transparent benchmark calculation, not a proof that 10% properly prices the project's risk. At the same benchmark, the favorable-state NPV is 1,200/1.1 − 800 ≈ $290.91. The unfavorable-state NPV is 700/1.1 − 800 ≈ −$163.64. The expected calculation does not remove the 40% unfavorable-state probability or tell us whether the owner can absorb it. Let p be the favorable-state probability. The expected receipt is 700 + 500p. The benchmark NPV is positive when (700 + 500p)/1.1 > 800, or p > 0.36. A probability estimate near that threshold is decision-relevant; an unexamined point estimate is insufficient evidence. The household financing case A different prospective owner has only $500 of cash available for the same $800 project. A loan of $300 is offered with a $330 end-of-year repayment. Keeping and investing the $500 instead produces $550 in one year under a specified certain 10% opportunity. The project receipts are as above, and the new loan repayment must be deducted separately. Worked example 2 — Expected advantage with a cash constraint At initiation, $500 existing cash plus $300 loan proceeds funds the $800 cost. Remaining cash is zero. In the favorable state, final cash after repayment is 1,200 − 330 = $870. In the unfavorable state, final cash is 700 − 330 = $370. Expected final cash is 0.6 × 870 + 0.4 × 370 = $670. The alternative produces $550, so the project exceeds it by $120 in expected year-end cash. Relative to that alternative, the project gives +$320 in the favorable state and −$180 in the unfavorable state. The expected advantage is not a recommendation for every household. Starting with zero remaining cash can violate the person's needs for interim spending or buffers. If an additional $100 payment can arise before project receipts and no funding is available, the proposal may be infeasible even though its expectation is favorable. Do not subtract the original $500 again from only one alternative. Both use the same starting cash. Comparing year-end wealth under consistent actions already accounts for it. If expressing each action's NPV instead, apply the same valuation date and complete cash flows to both. Add purchasing power and evidence If the applicable price index rises 5% over the year, final cash amounts expressed in initial purchasing power are divided by 1.05. The project expectation becomes 670/1.05 ≈ $638.10 and the alternative 550/1.05 ≈ $523.81. This common deflation preserves their comparison but clarifies the units. The favorable-state probability needs support. Was it estimated from comparable projects? Were failed projects omitted? Did the economic environment change? Is the receipt forecast genuinely net of all future costs? These questions concern measurement and evidence, not the arithmetic of a supplied probability. Suppose the business's sales rose after buying the equipment. The rise is not by itself a causal estimate of the equipment's effect. Demand, staffing, prices and seasonality can also change. A comparison design needs an explicit counterfactual. Accounting can document sales; it cannot independently establish the causal contribution of the equipment. Use an integration checklist Entity: distinguish business, owner, borrower, bank and economy. Time: identify the valuation date and every payment date. Units: separate money, rates, percentages, percentage points and real price bases. Accounting: reconcile all stocks and flows. Decision: name the best feasible alternative and incremental consequences. Uncertainty: specify outcomes, probabilities and constraints. Mechanism: identify incentives and consistency conditions. Evidence: separate constructed inputs, measured facts and causal inference. Communication: state a conclusion with the assumptions that could change it. A mistake found during integration often points to a specific prerequisite. Incorrect rate bases lead back to Chapter 2. Unreconciled financing leads to Chapter 4 or 9. Discounting inconsistency leads to Chapter 5. Unsupported probabilities lead to Chapter 8. Causal overstatement leads to Chapter 10. Use this map for targeted remediation. Exercises Reconstruct the business's balance sheet and profit from the case without looking at Worked Example 1. Then suppose it pays another $100 of principal from cash. Show the new cash, assets, liabilities and equity. For the business case, add a $100 depreciation expense and a corresponding $100 reduction in equipment carrying value. Keep the actual cash transactions unchanged. Recompute profit, cash, assets and equity and reconcile the identity. Calculate the project's expected receipt and 10%-benchmark NPV when the favorable-state probability is 0.3 and when it is 0.5. Verify the probability threshold for positive benchmark NPV. For the household financing case, change the year-end loan repayment from $330 to $360. Recompute both final-cash outcomes, expected final cash and the expected advantage over the $550 alternative. In the original household financing case, vary the favorable-state probability p. Derive expected final cash and the threshold at which it exceeds the $550 alternative. With a 5% price-index increase, express the original project's favorable and unfavorable household final cash and the alternative in initial purchasing power. Explain why a common deflator does not settle a preference over risk. A promoter reports a 60% favorable-state rate based only on businesses still operating after five years. Write a critique identifying selection, comparability, timing and the data needed to assess the estimate. Write a 200-word investment memo using the household financing case. Include a conditional decision, figures that reconcile, the alternative, probability sensitivity, risk and interim liquidity, a price basis, and a fact that could change the decision. Present no guaranteed outcome. Solutions and evaluation criteria Before repayment, cash is 2,340, equipment 1,000, assets 3,340, debt 1,200 and equity 2,140; profit is 340. Principal repayment reduces cash to 2,240 and debt to 1,100. Assets become 3,240 and equity remains 2,140: 3,240 = 1,100 + 2,140. Profit becomes 240; cash remains 2,340 because this entry is not another cash payment. Equipment becomes 900, assets 3,240, liabilities 1,200 and equity 2,040: 3,240 = 1,200 + 2,040. This assumes the specified depreciation treatment and no tax effect. Expected receipt is 700 + 500p. At 0.3 it is 850, giving NPV 850/1.1 − 800 ≈ −$27.27. At 0.5 it is 950, giving approximately $63.64. Solving 700 + 500p = 880 gives p = 0.36. A risk-appropriate valuation still requires more than this benchmark. Final cash is 840 or 340. Expectation is 0.6 × 840 + 0.4 × 340 = 640. Expected advantage is 640 − 550 = $90, reduced by the additional $30 repayment. Expected final cash is 870p + 370(1 − p) = 370 + 500p. It exceeds 550 when p > 0.36. At the threshold it matches the alternative in expectation, while their outcome distributions remain different. Real amounts are 870/1.05 ≈ 828.57, 370/1.05 ≈ 352.38 and 550/1.05 ≈ 523.81. The common price basis does not remove uncertain states or establish the owner's utility, constraints or risk preference. Surviving businesses can be a selected subset, and the data may omit failures or differ from the proposed project. Seek a defined original cohort, all outcomes, start dates, industry and conditions, costs, reasons for exit and the information available at the forecast date. Do not assume the survivor rate equals the proposal's success probability. Evaluate consistency and conditional reasoning. The memo should distinguish expectation from a guarantee, give the relevant comparison, expose the liquidity constraint and data weakness, use a stated price basis and avoid double counting. A justified decision to proceed, reduce scale, seek more information or decline can be defensible under explicit additional assumptions. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the complete case to a beginner, then defend its accounting and uncertainty treatment to a technical reader. Both explanations must describe the same mechanism and assumptions. Delayed check: change the case's prices, loan terms and probabilities and reconstruct the analysis from the transaction list and timelines. Diagnose the first point of failure if your statements or comparisons do not reconcile. Readiness for the next volume You are ready for the mathematical toolkit when you can translate relationships into equations, preserve units, solve basic systems, compare dated amounts, distinguish probabilities from outcomes, and explain assumptions. Use the volume diagnostic and cumulative assessments to identify gaps. Advancing with an unresolved error in these foundations makes later sophisticated calculations less reliable. The next volume develops proof, calculus, linear algebra and optimization. Those tools will strengthen the same reasoning rather than replace it. The aim is to derive and evaluate unfamiliar results with confidence grounded in explicit steps."}, {"title": "02.01 · Mathematical language and proof", "url": "v02-c01.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Why an economist needs proof A calculation answers a particular question with particular inputs. A proof establishes what follows throughout a stated domain. If you claim that every affordable plan remains affordable after income rises, three successful numerical trials are insufficient. The claim concerns all plans and all eligible income changes. Conversely, one valid counterexample defeats a universal claim that every positive-profit business has enough cash to pay its bills. Mathematical discipline begins before advanced formulas. Define the objects, specify the domain, state what is assumed, and distinguish the conclusion from what you would like it to mean economically. A logically valid theorem about a model does not establish that the model describes observed behavior. Theoretical validity and empirical relevance are separate questions, both necessary for good research. This chapter supplies the language used throughout the library. You should be able to read an unfamiliar proposition, identify its exact logical burden, construct an argument, and explain which step fails when an assumption changes. The examples are original simplified economic models; none is an empirical claim. Objects, sets and feasible alternatives A set is a collection of distinct objects called elements. Write x ∈ A when x belongs to A, and x ∉ A otherwise. The empty set ∅ has no elements. Here ℕ = {1, 2, 3, …}, ℕ₀ = {0, 1, 2, …}, ℤ denotes the integers, ℚ the rational numbers, and ℝ the real numbers. A rational number is a ratio of integers with a nonzero denominator. Real numbers also include irrational values such as √2. A ⊆ B means every element of A also belongs to B. Equality A = B requires both inclusions. A proper subset is written A ⊊ B. Distinguish an element from a subset: 2 ∈ {1, 2}, while {2} ⊆ {1, 2}. Repetition and order do not change a set, although they matter in a sequence or an ordered pair. A ∪ B contains elements in at least one of the two sets; A ∩ B contains elements in both. The difference A \\ B contains elements in A but outside B. A complement Aᶜ means U \\ A relative to a specified universe U. Without U, “everyone outside the eligible group” is ambiguous: everyone in the applicant pool, population, or world? Interval notation records boundaries. [a, b] includes both endpoints, (a, b) excludes both, and [a, b) includes only a. Thus [0, ∞) is the set of nonnegative real numbers; infinity is not an included real endpoint. Set-builder notation {x ∈ ℝ : x ≥ 0} describes that same set through a condition. The Cartesian product A × B contains ordered pairs (a, b) with a ∈ A and b ∈ B. Consequently ℝ² represents two real coordinates, and ℝⁿ represents n coordinates. Coordinates have an interpretation and order: a bundle (food, housing) cannot be swapped silently. A feasible set collects all choices satisfying the constraints, not merely the choice somebody eventually makes. For nonnegative quantities x and y, positive prices p and q, and income m ≥ 0, define B(m) = {(x, y) ∈ [0, ∞)² : px + qy ≤ m}. This one expression specifies dimensions, signs, and the budget restriction. It includes unspent income. Replacing ≤ by = would define a different set, excluding bundles that leave money unused. Statements, implication and quantifiers A proposition is a statement with a truth value. “x is affordable” becomes a proposition after x, prices and income are specified; before that it is a condition depending on inputs. P ∧ Q means both statements hold, P ∨ Q means at least one holds, and ¬P means P is false. The “or” used here is inclusive. P ⇒ Q means there is no case in which P is true and Q is false. P is sufficient for Q; Q is necessary for P. The converse Q ⇒ P is a separate assertion. The contrapositive ¬Q ⇒ ¬P is equivalent to the original implication, whereas the inverse ¬P ⇒ ¬Q generally is not. To establish P ⇔ Q, or “if and only if,” prove both directions. For example, with a real number r, r > 2 implies r > 0. Exceeding 2 is sufficient for positivity; positivity is necessary for exceeding 2. Positivity alone is insufficient: r = 1 is a counterexample to the converse. A necessary condition can narrow the candidates without selecting the answer. Later, a zero derivative will often be a necessary condition for an interior optimum, and it will need additional conditions to be sufficient. The symbol ∀ means “for every”; ∃ means “there exists”; ∃! means “there exists exactly one.” State their domains. “∀x ∈ B, P(x)” requires P for every feasible x. Its negation is “∃x ∈ B such that ¬P(x).” Negating “∃x ∈ B, P(x)” gives “∀x ∈ B, ¬P(x).” Negation exchanges the quantifier as well as the condition. Order changes meaning. “For every positive tolerance ε, there exists a sufficiently large sample size n” permits n to depend on ε. “There exists n that works for every positive ε” demands one n for arbitrarily severe tolerances. The first may be true when the second is false. Limits in the next chapter depend on precisely this distinction. Universal statements over ∅ are true in the logical sense because there is no violating element. An existence claim over ∅ is false. Thus “every optimal plan is affordable” provides no evidence that an optimal plan exists. A proof of existence must supply an object or use a theorem whose hypotheses ensure one. Relations, functions and identification A binary relation on A specifies which ordered pairs in A × A satisfy a condition. It is reflexive if x relates to itself for every x, symmetric if xRy implies yRx, and transitive if xRy and yRz imply xRz. An equivalence relation has all three properties. For example, “has the same account balance as” is an equivalence relation on a fixed collection of accounts at a fixed date. The accounts need not be identical. A weak ordering can instead be reflexive, transitive and complete: every pair is comparable in at least one direction. It need not be symmetric. A partial order is reflexive, transitive and antisymmetric, meaning xRy and yRx require x = y. Coordinatewise ≤ on bundles is a partial order: (1, 3) and (2, 2) are incomparable. Do not assume that every mathematically defined comparison yields a complete ranking. A function f: A → B assigns exactly one output in B to each input in its domain A. B is the codomain; the image f(A) consists of outputs actually reached. A rule and its domain jointly define the function. f(x) = x² on ℝ and the same rule on [0, ∞) have different invertibility properties. Injectivity means distinct inputs have distinct outputs: f(x) = f(y) ⇒ x = y. Surjectivity means every element of the codomain is reached. A bijection has both properties and admits an inverse f⁻¹: B → A. x²: ℝ → [0, ∞) is surjective but not injective because 2 and −2 both give 4. Restricting the domain to [0, ∞) makes it a bijection with inverse √x. This is an early form of identification reasoning. If several parameter values produce the same observed prediction, the prediction alone cannot determine which parameter generated it. More data may help only if the enlarged mapping distinguishes the relevant alternatives. A numerical solver returning one answer is not a proof of uniqueness. Worked example 1 — An income increase expands a feasible set Claim: for fixed positive prices and 0 ≤ m₁ ≤ m₂, B(m₁) ⊆ B(m₂). Begin with an arbitrary bundle (x, y) ∈ B(m₁). By definition x, y ≥ 0 and px + qy ≤ m₁. Since m₁ ≤ m₂, transitivity of ≤ gives px + qy ≤ m₂. The signs of x and y have not changed. Hence (x, y) ∈ B(m₂), proving the inclusion for every original bundle. If m₂ > m₁ and p > 0, the inclusion is strict: (m₂/p, 0) is in B(m₂), spends exactly m₂ and is outside B(m₁). The construction supplies a witness to strictness rather than merely drawing a larger triangle. The conclusion concerns opportunity, not behavior. It does not prove the household buys more of each good. It also requires fixed prices. If income rises from 10 to 12 while p rises from 1 to 2, the old bundle (10, 0) becomes unaffordable. That counterexample violates the fixed-price assumption rather than refuting the proved theorem. Choosing and writing a proof A direct proof starts from the hypotheses and established definitions and reaches the conclusion. To prove a set inclusion, take an arbitrary element of the first set. To prove equality of sets, prove both inclusions. To prove existence, construct a witness when possible. To prove uniqueness, suppose two objects meet the conditions and show they must coincide. A contrapositive proof is useful when failure of the conclusion reveals failure of a hypothesis. If a product ab is nonzero, both a and b are nonzero: prove the contrapositive by observing that a zero factor makes the product zero. A contradiction proof assumes the hypotheses and the negation of the desired conclusion, then derives an impossibility. The contradiction must come from those assumptions, not from an unsupported claim. For a classical example, suppose √2 = a/b for relatively prime positive integers a and b. Squaring gives a² = 2b², so a² is even. An odd integer has form 2k + 1 and an odd square, so a must be even. Write a = 2c. Substitution gives b² = 2c², so b is also even. Both being even contradicts their being relatively prime. Therefore √2 is irrational. The proof covers every rational representation, unlike decimal inspection. A proof by cases partitions the domain into exhaustive possibilities. If you divide an equation by x, separate x = 0 from x ≠ 0 first. Squaring can introduce extraneous candidates; applying a logarithm requires a positive argument; multiplying an inequality by a negative value reverses it. Every transformation should preserve the asserted implication or explicitly narrow the remaining candidates. A counterexample must satisfy the hypotheses and violate the conclusion. If a theorem assumes positive prices, setting a price to zero may illustrate why that assumption matters but cannot disprove the theorem as stated. Counterexamples to overbroad claims are often more informative than additional confirming cases. Induction and repeated financial relationships Induction proves statements indexed by integers. First prove the base case P(0), or whichever first index applies. Then for arbitrary k in the domain assume P(k) and prove P(k + 1). The assumption is local to that step; it is not permission to assume the desired result for all k. Together the base and step reach every subsequent integer. Why does this work? If any index failed, the nonempty set of failing nonnegative integers would have a least element. It could not be the proved base. Its predecessor would therefore satisfy the statement, and the induction step would imply that the supposedly failing index satisfies it too—a contradiction. This uses the well-ordering property of nonnegative integers. An argument of the same form does not automatically establish a statement for all real times. Worked example 2 — A recurring payment formula with its exceptional case Let V₀ be a balance, let g be a fixed gross growth factor, and let d be the same addition at the end of each period. Define Vₙ₊₁ = gVₙ + d for n ∈ ℕ₀. For g ≠ 1 the claim is Vₙ = gⁿV₀ + d(gⁿ − 1)/(g − 1). At n = 0 the expression is V₀ + 0, so the base case holds. Suppose it holds at k. Then Vₖ₊₁ = g[gᵏV₀ + d(gᵏ − 1)/(g − 1)] + d = gᵏ⁺¹V₀ + d[g(gᵏ − 1) + (g − 1)]/(g − 1) = gᵏ⁺¹V₀ + d(gᵏ⁺¹ − 1)/(g − 1). That is the claimed formula at k + 1. Induction completes the proof. At g = 1, division by g − 1 is invalid. The recurrence instead directly gives Vₙ = V₀ + nd, also proved by the base and one-step addition. With V₀ = 100, g = 1.1 and d = 20, direct balances are 130, 163 and 199.3. The closed form at n = 3 gives 133.1 + 66.2 = 199.3. The formula remains an algebraic identity for real g ≠ 1, but a negative gross factor generally lacks the intended ordinary account interpretation. Beginning-of-period deposits would instead obey Vₙ₊₁ = g(Vₙ + d). Correct algebra cannot compensate for a mistaken timing equation. A practical proof audit Before accepting an argument, read its conclusion without the prose surrounding it. Ask what is quantified, what is fixed, and what may depend on what. Then annotate each substantive step with a definition, a hypothesis, or an already established result. A step that depends on the conclusion is circular. A string of equalities that assumes nonzero denominators without checking them is incomplete. Existence, uniqueness and construction are different achievements. Showing at most one solution does not show there is a solution. Finding one solution does not show there are no others. Deriving a candidate from an equation requires checking that candidate in the original equation and domain. Good explanations make these burdens visible and use the least machinery that settles them. Exercises Let U = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6}, A = {1, 2, 4} and B = {2, 3, 4}. Find A ∪ B, A ∩ B, A \\ B and (A ∪ B)ᶜ. Prove, for arbitrary subsets of a fixed universe, (A ∪ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ. Compare the statements ∀ε > 0, ∃n ∈ ℕ such that 1/n < ε and ∃n ∈ ℕ, ∀ε > 0 such that 1/n < ε. Explain which is true, using the fact that integers can exceed any given real number. Write the exact negation of the first statement. On ℝ define xRy when |x − y| ≤ 1. Test reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity. Supply a counterexample for each property that fails. For f: ℝ → [0, ∞) defined by f(x) = (x − 3)², test injectivity and surjectivity. Restrict the domain to [3, ∞), obtain a bijection, and derive its inverse including the inverse's domain. Prove that under fixed positive prices, B(m₁) = B(m₂) if and only if m₁ = m₂, for nonnegative incomes. Identify which implication needs a constructed bundle. A loan balance obeys Lₙ₊₁ = 1.05Lₙ − 30 with L₀ = 200. Derive and prove by induction a closed form while this recurrence is applied without truncation. Compute L₁ and L₂. Explain why an actual payoff rule would eventually require a changed recurrence. For a ≥ −1 and integer n ≥ 1, prove (1 + a)ⁿ ≥ 1 + na by induction. Identify why the sign restriction is needed in the induction step. Find an a outside the restriction and an n for which the inequality fails. A researcher claims that a model has a unique parameter because f(θ) = θ² = 9 has the solution θ = 3. Audit the claim on ℝ and on [0, ∞). Give separate proofs of existence and uniqueness on the restricted domain. Explain the logical gap in treating one solver output as uniqueness. Solutions and proof guidance The sets are {1, 2, 3, 4}, {2, 4}, {1}, and {5, 6}. For any x ∈ U, x ∈ (A ∪ B)ᶜ exactly when x is in neither A nor B, exactly when x ∈ Aᶜ and x ∈ Bᶜ. Thus membership in the two sides is equivalent, proving both inclusions. The shared universe is essential to the complement notation. The first is true: for each chosen ε > 0 choose an integer n > 1/ε. The second is false: after any proposed n choose ε = 1/(2n), so 1/n is not less than ε. The first statement's negation is ∃ε > 0, ∀n ∈ ℕ, 1/n ≥ ε. The example illustrates dependence on tolerance, not a single universal n. Reflexive because |x − x| = 0 ≤ 1; symmetric because |x − y| = |y − x|. Not transitive: 0R1 and 1R2 but not 0R2. Approximate equality under a fixed tolerance therefore need not form equivalence classes. Not injective: f(2) = f(4) = 1. Surjective: for every z ≥ 0, x = 3 + √z is a real input with f(x) = z. On [3, ∞), x − 3 is nonnegative; equality of squares forces equality of those differences and hence of inputs. The inverse is f⁻¹(z) = 3 + √z for z ∈ [0, ∞), with values in [3, ∞). If incomes equal, the definitions coincide. For the reverse implication, suppose they differ and, after relabeling, m₂ > m₁. The bundle (m₂/p, 0) is feasible only at m₂, so the sets differ. This contradicts equality. The constructed bundle establishes the reverse direction; fixed prices prevent changing the definition between comparisons. The form is Lₙ = 200(1.05)ⁿ − 30[(1.05)ⁿ − 1]/0.05 = 600 − 400(1.05)ⁿ. Base n = 0 gives 200. If true at k, then 1.05[600 − 400(1.05)ᵏ] − 30 = 600 − 400(1.05)ᵏ⁺¹, proving the step. L₁ = 180 and L₂ = 159. Eventually the expression becomes negative; an actual debt paid off with a smaller final payment would cease following this unrestricted recurrence. No negative debt balance is being asserted. At n = 1 equality holds. Given the bound at k, multiply by 1 + a ≥ 0 to obtain (1 + a)ᵏ⁺¹ ≥ (1 + ka)(1 + a) = 1 + (k + 1)a + ka² ≥ 1 + (k + 1)a. A negative factor would reverse the first inequality. For a = −4 and n = 3, the left side is −27 and the right side is −11, so the claimed inequality fails outside the restriction. On ℝ, both 3 and −3 solve the equation, so uniqueness is false. On [0, ∞), 3 is a permitted solution, proving existence. If θ ≥ 0 and θ² = 9, (θ − 3)(θ + 3) = 0 and θ + 3 > 0, so θ = 3. This proves uniqueness. A solver reports a found solution; it does not eliminate other permissible solutions or establish the parameter domain. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain necessity, sufficiency, existence and uniqueness using a single budget or parameter example. Then defend the induction proof of recurring balances without skipping its base case or timing assumption. Delayed check: after a week, negate a nested quantified statement, prove a new feasible-set inclusion, and construct a valid counterexample to its overbroad economic interpretation. Reconstruct the recurring-balance proof with g = 1 treated separately. Further study and scope For independent mathematical study, MIT's 18.100A lecture-note collection covers proof, sets and functions before developing analysis. Use Lectures 1–2 for additional practice. This chapter's economic constructions and exercises are original; the reference is a further-study route, not an empirical justification for the models. These are working foundations rather than an axiomatic construction of all mathematics. Later chapters add precise convergence, continuity and optimization conditions. Maintain a proof notebook: statement, domain, hypotheses, argument, counterexample when a hypothesis is dropped, and economic interpretation."}, {"title": "02.02 · Functions, limits and continuity", "url": "v02-c02.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "What does “arbitrarily close” mean? A finite decimal display cannot tell you whether a sequence converges. A graph that looks smooth cannot establish continuity, and two price observations with opposite excess-demand signs do not guarantee a clearing price unless the behavior between them satisfies the appropriate condition. Limits turn such visual impressions into statements that can be defended. The essential idea is control: specify an acceptable output error, then show how an input restriction or sufficiently late sequence index guarantees that error. The order of these choices matters. This chapter develops that language and uses it to prove existence, diagnose jumps, and bound a numerical search. Its functions and market quantities are synthetic instructional examples. Domains, composition and basic function shapes A function needs a domain, a rule, and a codomain. For f(x) = 1/(x − 2), the natural real domain excludes 2. For √x it is [0, ∞), and for ln x it is (0, ∞). A formula for a mathematical function can allow inputs that the model does not: a demand equation defined algebraically for negative prices may have an economic domain restricted to nonnegative prices and quantities. If f: A → B and g: B → C, their composition g ∘ f maps x to g(f(x)). More generally, the composition is defined only for x where the intermediate value f(x) is in g's domain. For ln(1 − x²), this requires 1 − x² > 0, hence −1 < x < 1. Evaluating the outer function without checking this condition gives an undefined real expression. A function is increasing if x < y implies f(x) ≤ f(y), and strictly increasing if the output inequality is strict. Decreasing has the opposite output inequality. Monotonicity is a statement over the whole stated interval; checking two endpoints is insufficient. For a ≥ 0, powers xᵃ on positive x, exponentials and logarithms provide useful shapes, while negative powers decrease. The natural logarithm is the inverse of eˣ on positive outputs; ln(eˣ) = x and e^(ln x) = x for x > 0. Ordinary real exponentials and logarithms are continuous on their domains. We use their established real-number definitions here; a later integration chapter connects ln x with the accumulated integral of 1/x. For limits, the relevant fact is domain-aware continuity, not a decimal approximation to e. Piecewise rules require special attention where their formulas change. Sequences and their limits A sequence is a function from integer indices to values; write (aₙ) with n ∈ ℕ or ℕ₀ as specified. The notation aₙ → L means: for every ε > 0, there exists an integer N such that for every n ≥ N, |aₙ − L| < ε. Absolute value measures distance. N may depend on ε, but cannot then depend on which n ≥ N you inspect. For aₙ = 1/n, choose an integer N > 1/ε. Then n ≥ N gives |aₙ − 0| = 1/n ≤ 1/N < ε. This proves convergence to zero. It does not say a term ever equals zero. For 3 + 2/n the same reasoning with N > 2/ε proves convergence to 3. A limit is unique. If a sequence converged to distinct L and M, choose ε = |L − M|/3. Beyond the larger of the two required indices, both distances would be less than ε. The triangle inequality would give |L − M| ≤ |L − aₙ| + |aₙ − M| < 2|L − M|/3, an impossibility. This proof uses one index that satisfies both bounds. Convergent sequences are bounded: after some index their terms lie within 1 of the limit, and a finite number of earlier terms has a finite maximum absolute value. Boundedness alone is insufficient. The bounded sequence (−1)ⁿ alternates between −1 and 1 and has no limit, since its even and odd terms approach different values. If aₙ → A and bₙ → B, then their sum approaches A + B and their product approaches AB. A quotient approaches A/B if B ≠ 0. For the sum, bound the error by |aₙ − A| + |bₙ − B| and give each term half the tolerance. Products additionally use boundedness of one factor. The nonzero denominator condition keeps bₙ eventually away from zero; it cannot be omitted. The squeeze principle says that if lₙ ≤ aₙ ≤ uₙ eventually, and both bounds converge to L, then aₙ also converges to L. For example, −1/n ≤ sin(n)/n ≤ 1/n proves convergence to zero without requiring sin(n) to settle down. A statement need only hold from some sufficiently late index; finitely many initial terms cannot change a limit. A nondecreasing sequence bounded above converges to its least upper bound in ℝ. Briefly, if L is that bound, every ε > 0 admits a term greater than L − ε; otherwise L − ε would itself be an upper bound. All later terms remain between that term and L. Thus their distance from L is less than ε. The real numbers' least-upper-bound property is called completeness and is examined more deeply in Chapter 10. Infinite sums are limits of partial sums, not an instruction to add endlessly without a criterion. For |g| < 1, gⁿ → 0: if 0 < |g| < 1, put c = 1/|g| − 1 > 0 and use the preceding chapter's bound (1 + c)ⁿ ≥ 1 + nc, giving |g|ⁿ ≤ 1/(1 + nc) → 0; g = 0 is immediate. Multiplying the finite sum S = 1 + g + … + gⁿ by 1 − g cancels its interior terms, yielding S = (1 − gⁿ⁺¹)/(1 − g). Its limit is therefore 1/(1 − g). For g ≥ 1 this infinite sum does not have that finite limit. A perpetuity formula needs its convergence condition as well as its payment timing. Limits of functions Write lim x→a f(x) = L when: for every ε > 0 there exists δ > 0 such that x in the domain and 0 < |x − a| < δ imply |f(x) − L| < ε. The point a must be approachable by distinct domain points. The exclusion of x = a means the limit does not require f(a) to be defined or equal to L. The input tolerance δ may depend on ε and the point a. It must work for every domain input in that punctured neighborhood. Testing one trajectory or a handful of small distances is evidence about those inputs, not proof of the universal bound. Limits from the right restrict x > a; limits from the left restrict x < a. At an interior point approachable from both sides, a two-sided finite limit exists exactly when both one-sided limits exist and coincide. At a boundary of the domain, continuity is interpreted using only domain inputs. For example, √x is continuous at 0 relative to its nonnegative domain. The same algebraic rules for sums, products and quotients apply to function limits, with a nonzero limiting denominator for division. If x² − 4 is divided by x − 2, canceling gives x + 2 only for x ≠ 2. That is enough to compute the limit at 2, but it does not fill the missing function value unless a new definition explicitly does so. Worked example 1 — Prove a removable limit with a usable tolerance For x ≠ 2, let f(x) = (x² − 4)/(x − 2). We claim its limit at 2 is 4. Factor the numerator to obtain f(x) = x + 2 on the permitted domain. Therefore |f(x) − 4| = |x − 2|. Given any ε > 0, choose δ = ε. Every permitted input with 0 < |x − 2| < δ has output error less than ε. This is a complete limit proof. Suppose instead we extend the function by defining f(2) = 9. The limit remains 4 because its definition ignores the value exactly at 2. The extension is discontinuous there. The continuous extension must assign f(2) = 4. Thus assigning a value and proving a limit are separate tasks. For an uncanceled quadratic, lim x→2 x² = 4, the bound requires another step: |x² − 4| = |x − 2||x + 2|. If |x − 2| < 1, then 1 < x < 3 and |x + 2| < 5. Choose δ = min{1, ε/5}. The product is then less than ε. The preliminary neighborhood bound makes the second factor controllable. Continuity and what it buys you A function is continuous at a point a in its domain if input closeness guarantees output closeness including the value at a. At an approachable point this says lim x→a f(x) = f(a). Polynomials are continuous on ℝ; rational functions are continuous where their denominators are nonzero. Sums and products of continuous functions are continuous, and their quotients are continuous at points with nonzero denominators. Compositions are continuous wherever the component functions are continuous at the relevant inputs. Continuity does not mean an output change is small under any specified input perturbation. A continuous function can be steep, and its required δ can be very small. Nor does continuity imply a finite slope everywhere: |x| is continuous at zero but has left slope −1 and right slope 1. A derivative requires those local slopes to agree, as the next chapter shows. The intermediate value theorem says a real-valued continuous function on [a, b] takes every value between f(a) and f(b). In particular, if f(a) < 0 < f(b), it has at least one zero inside the interval. Continuity rules out jumping over the desired value. The theorem gives existence, not uniqueness or a closed formula. One proof outline uses repeated halving: retain a closed half-interval whose endpoint values bracket the target. Its lengths tend to zero and its nested endpoints approach a common real point. Continuity at that point forces its value to be the target. Completeness of ℝ and closed endpoints ensure the limiting point remains in the interval. Chapter 10 supplies the more general convergence machinery. The extreme value theorem says a continuous function on a closed bounded interval attains a maximum and a minimum. “Attains” means there is an actual input realizing each extreme, not only an upper or lower bound. Domain conditions matter: f(x) = x on (0, 1) has supremum 1 but no maximum; 1/x on (0, 1] is unbounded above. An optimization routine should not be asked to discover an optimum whose existence has not been established. A discontinuity may be removable, a finite jump, or an unbounded singularity. For a fixed participation fee, C(q) = 0 at q = 0 and C(q) = 10 + 2q at q > 0 jumps at zero. The limit from the right is 10, not C(0). Treating that cost as continuous would erase a discrete decision: whether to participate at all. Worked example 2 — Existence, uniqueness and a price bracket Let synthetic demand be D(p) = 40/(1 + p) and supply S(p) = 4 + 2p on [0, 10], in common quantity units. Excess demand z(p) = D(p) − S(p) is continuous because 1 + p is positive there. At p = 0 it is 36 > 0; at p = 10 it is 40/11 − 24 < 0. The intermediate value theorem establishes a clearing price. For p₂ > p₁ ≥ 0, 1/(1 + p₂) < 1/(1 + p₁), while 4 + 2p₂ > 4 + 2p₁. Hence z(p₂) < z(p₁). Strict decrease establishes at most one zero. Combined with existence, the zero is unique. Solving 40 = (4 + 2p)(1 + p) gives p² + 3p − 18 = 0, or (p − 3)(p + 6) = 0. Only p = 3 lies in the domain, and D(3) = S(3) = 10. Algebra provides a location; the preceding argument explains existence and uniqueness. To locate it without a closed formula, use bisection on [0, 10]. The successive retained brackets are [0, 5], [2.5, 5], [2.5, 3.75], [2.5, 3.125] and [2.8125, 3.125]. After five halvings the bracket length is 10/2⁵ = 0.3125. Its midpoint 2.96875 is guaranteed within 0.15625 of the true root; its actual error here is 0.03125. The guaranteed bound comes from bracketing, not from knowing the solution in advance. If supply or demand jumps, opposite endpoint signs alone need not imply equality at any price. If excess demand is continuous but not strictly monotone, multiple clearing prices may exist. These are mathematical conditions on this model; no real market's adjustment process is established by the example. Limits and modeling decisions A small numerical residual does not always imply a small error in the underlying parameter. If a function is almost flat near its zero, a substantial input error can produce little output error. Bisection's bracket gives an input error bound; evaluating |z(p)| alone gives an output mismatch. Keep the criterion aligned with what the decision requires. At discontinuities, a limit from one side may represent a meaningful scenario but should be named explicitly. When a rule changes at a tax threshold or transaction size, specify whether the threshold itself uses the old or new rule. An algebraic equality established for x ≠ a cannot justify the value at a without an additional argument. Exercises Find the real domains of ln(4 − x²), √(x − 1)/(x − 3), and h(x) = ln(√x). State each as a set or interval and explain the excluded endpoints. Prove that aₙ = 5 − 3/n converges to 5. Give an explicit N ensuring an error strictly less than 0.01. Explain why convergence does not require a term equal to 5. Determine whether (−1)ⁿ + 1/n converges. Then prove sin(n)/n² → 0 using a bound. Distinguish a bounded sequence from a convergent one. For f(x) = (x² − 9)/(x − 3), x ≠ 3, compute the limit at 3 and choose f(3) for continuity. Prove directly that x² → 9 as x → 3 using a δ depending on ε. Define T(x) = 2x for x < 1 and T(x) = x + c for x ≥ 1. Find c making T continuous at 1. Determine whether the function with that c has a derivative at 1 using its left and right slopes. Let z(p) = 30/(1 + p) − 3p on [0, 10]. Establish existence and uniqueness of a zero without relying on the quadratic formula. Then compute the permissible root. How many bisection halvings from [0, 10] suffice to guarantee midpoint error at most 0.01? Construct a function on [0, 2] with a negative value at 0, a positive value at 2, and no zero. Explain exactly which hypothesis of the intermediate value theorem fails. Contrast this with f(x) = x on (0, 1), which has no maximum. A sequence of truncated discounted receipts is Pₙ = Σ t=1 n 12/(1.2)ᵗ. Derive Pₙ, its limit, and the exact omitted tail. Find the smallest positive integer n making the tail less than 1. Explain what fails if the gross discount factor is 1. Solutions and proof guidance The logarithm requires 4 − x² > 0, so (−2, 2). The square root requires x ≥ 1 and the denominator excludes 3, giving [1, 3) ∪ (3, ∞). For ln(√x), the intermediate square root must be strictly positive, so x > 0. Zero is allowed for a square root alone but excluded by the outer logarithm. The error is 3/n. For any ε > 0 choose an integer N > 3/ε; then n ≥ N implies 3/n < ε. For 0.01 take N = 301, since at 300 the error equals rather than falls below 0.01. Each finite term is below 5, consistently with convergence to it. The even subsequence approaches 1 and the odd subsequence approaches −1, so no common limit exists. Every subsequence of a convergent sequence must share its limit, directly from the “all sufficiently late indices” condition. For the second sequence, |sin(n)/n²| ≤ 1/n²; for each ε > 0 choose N > 1/√ε. Boundedness of the oscillating numerator supplies a bound, not its own convergence. For x ≠ 3 cancel to x + 3; the limit is 6 and the continuous extension sets f(3) = 6. To prove the quadratic limit, if |x − 3| < 1 then |x + 3| < 7. Choose δ = min{1, ε/7}; |x² − 9| = |x − 3||x + 3| < ε. The proof does not divide by an expression that could be zero. The left limit is 2; the right limit and value are 1 + c, so c = 1. With that choice the left slope is 2 and the right slope is 1. Continuity holds but a common two-sided derivative does not. Continuity holds on the domain. z(0) = 30 > 0 and z(10) = 30/11 − 30 < 0, so a zero exists. Strictly decreasing demand minus strictly increasing supply gives uniqueness. The equation p² + p − 10 = 0 gives p = (−1 + √41)/2 ≈ 2.701562; the other root is negative. After k halvings the midpoint bound is 10/2ᵏ⁺¹. Requiring it ≤ 0.01 gives 2ᵏ ≥ 500, hence k = 9 suffices; k = 8 does not. Let f(x) = −1 for x < 1 and f(x) = 1 for x ≥ 1. It jumps at 1 and never equals zero. Continuity on the whole closed interval fails. For x on (0, 1), continuity holds, but the domain is not closed: every candidate value can be exceeded by moving toward 1, and 1 is absent. The missing maximum is not a failure of the extreme value theorem. With v = 1/1.2 = 5/6, Pₙ = 12v(1 − vⁿ)/(1 − v) = 60(1 − (5/6)ⁿ). The limit is 60 and the omitted tail is 60(5/6)ⁿ. At n = 22 the tail is about 1.08684; at 23 it is about 0.90570, so 23 is smallest. With gross factor 1, each term is 12 and Pₙ = 12n diverges rather than approaching a finite value. The question fixes a discount benchmark and constant receipts; it does not price uncertain risk. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the order of ε and N, then the order of ε and δ, using a concrete error requirement. Explain why opposite endpoint signs need continuity for existence and why strict monotonicity supplies a different conclusion. Delayed check: prove a new polynomial limit with an explicit neighborhood bound, classify a piecewise threshold, and rebuild a bisection error guarantee. Derive a discounted tail rather than relying on the rounded infinite-sum value. Further study and scope MIT 18.100A's lecture collection provides independent study of sequences, function limits and continuity; Lectures 6–10 and 13–16 are relevant. More general compactness and interchange-of-limits issues return in Chapter 10. The examples here require only single-variable real functions, not yet multidimensional equilibrium theory. The immediate successor is differentiation. A derivative adds a precise first-order response to the continuity framework. Keep existence of a function value, existence of its limit, continuity and differentiability as separate questions."}, {"title": "02.03 · Derivatives and economic change", "url": "v02-c03.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "From changes over intervals to local responses A marginal cost measures how total cost responds locally to output. A price elasticity measures a proportional response rather than an absolute one. A bond's rate sensitivity measures how its value changes when a discount-rate input changes under specified assumptions. These quantities share one mathematical tool: the derivative. A derivative is not merely a symbol produced by rules. It is a limit of changes divided by input changes, and it describes a local approximation. Keep the domain, units, held-fixed inputs and size of the contemplated change visible. A derivative in a model is not automatically a causal effect in data; the model's relationship must first be justified. The definition and its approximation meaning For an interior domain point a, define f′(a) = lim h→0 [f(a + h) − f(a)]/h when the limit exists and is finite. The numerator is the output change; h is the input change. A two-sided derivative requires the same limiting ratio for positive and negative h. At a boundary, specify a one-sided derivative rather than implying movement beyond the feasible domain. The derivative of f(x) = x² follows directly: [(a + h)² − a²]/h = 2a + h for h ≠ 0, which approaches 2a. For f(x) = c, the numerator is zero, so the derivative is zero. For f(x) = mx + b, it is mh, giving derivative m. These computations connect constant and linear functions to the familiar slope. Writing the limit as an approximation gives f(a + h) = f(a) + f′(a)h + r(h), where r(h)/h → 0 as h → 0. The remainder is of smaller order than the input change. The notation r(h) = o(h) records that ratio condition; it does not mean the remainder is zero. For x² the remainder is exactly h². If a derivative exists, continuity follows. The output difference equals h times a difference quotient that approaches a finite value, so it approaches zero. The converse fails: f(x) = |x| is continuous at zero, but its difference quotient is 1 for h > 0 and −1 for h < 0. A participation fee can cause discontinuity, while a change in marginal pricing can leave continuity with a kink. Neither should be smoothed away merely to use differentiation. If output is dollars and input is units of goods, f′ has dollars-per-unit dimensions. If output is a price and input is a rate expressed as a decimal, the derivative measures dollars per unit change in that decimal. A 1 percentage point rate increase is Δr = 0.01. Multiplying by 1 would instead analyze a 100 percentage point change. Rules, their derivation and their domains For differentiable functions, linearity gives (af + bg)′ = af′ + bg′ for constants a and b. The product rule is (fg)′ = f′g + fg′. To see why, insert and subtract f(a + h)g(a) in the output difference. Dividing by h yields g(a)[f(a + h) − f(a)]/h + f(a + h)[g(a + h) − g(a)]/h. Taking limits uses continuity of f and produces the two terms. Differentiating a product by multiplying derivatives would omit both correct contributions. The chain rule says (f ∘ g)′(x) = f′(g(x))g′(x), provided g is differentiable at x and f is differentiable at g(x). The inner input first changes by g′(x)h + o(h); the outer output then changes by f′(g(x)) times that change plus a smaller remainder. This derivation works even when the inner change is zero on some inputs, unlike an informal cancellation that divides by it. For example, d[(3x + 1)²]/dx = 2(3x + 1) × 3. The outer derivative is evaluated at the inner value. When a composite function has several layers, work from the outside inward and multiply the layer responses. Write intermediate variables if this helps preserve the dependence structure. Integer powers satisfy d(xⁿ)/dx = nxⁿ⁻¹, obtained by repeatedly using the product rule. Differentiating 1 = xⁿx⁻ⁿ where x ≠ 0 extends the rule to negative integer powers. For general real powers on x > 0, the same formula follows from xᵃ = e^(a ln x). Square roots require separate boundary care: d√x/dx = 1/(2√x) for x > 0, with no finite right derivative at zero. The standard exponential satisfies d(eˣ)/dx = eˣ; equivalently its defining local normalization is lim h→0 (eʰ − 1)/h = 1. The inverse-function relationship then gives d(ln x)/dx = 1/x for x > 0. For a positive constant b, d(b x )/dx = b x ln b. The sine and cosine rules are d(sin x)/dx = cos x and d(cos x)/dx = −sin x, with angles in radians. These standard elementary-function results can be justified from their constructions; the present economic applications chiefly use powers, exponentials and logarithms. The reciprocal and product rules give the quotient rule: (f/g)′ = (f′g − fg′)/g² where g ≠ 0. One can derive the reciprocal rule directly by subtracting 1/g(a + h) and 1/g(a), putting them over a common denominator, and taking the limit. A valid symbolic derivative is usable only where both the original function and the required differentiability conditions hold. Higher derivatives apply differentiation again: f″ is the second derivative and f‴ the third. A positive f″ means the first derivative is increasing on an interval under the usual continuity/differentiability conditions; it does not imply the function's level is positive or increasing. For f(x) = x² − 10x, f″ = 2 but f′ = 2x − 10 is negative for x < 5. The mean value theorem and slope claims If f is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), with a < b, the mean value theorem ensures some c ∈ (a, b) has f′(c) = [f(b) − f(a)]/(b − a). The average slope over the interval equals a local slope somewhere inside. It does not identify c or assert that the slope is constant. The result follows by subtracting the straight line through the two endpoint values. The resulting function has equal endpoint values. If it is nonconstant, its attained maximum or minimum is interior, where its derivative is zero; if constant, its derivative is zero everywhere. This is Rolle's theorem. Adding the straight-line slope back yields the stated result. The interior zero-slope condition itself follows from the opposing signs required of right and left difference quotients at a differentiable local extremum. Consequently f′ > 0 throughout an interval implies strict increase there. If |f′| ≤ M, then |f(b) − f(a)| ≤ M|b − a|. The derivative at just one point cannot establish either assertion over an interval. Also, a zero derivative is not a sufficient optimality condition: x³ has f′(0) = 0 and no local extremum there, while x² has a minimum rather than a maximum. Constrained and global optimization are developed in Chapters 8–9. Worked example 1 — Marginal, average and proportional cost responses Suppose total cost is C(q) = 40 + 6q + q² dollars for continuous quantity q ≥ 0. For interior q, C′(q) = 6 + 2q dollars per unit and C″(q) = 2 dollars per squared unit. At q = 4, total cost is 80, marginal cost is 14, and average cost is C(q)/q = 20 dollars per unit. They answer different questions. A move from q = 4 to 4.1 gives the first-order estimate ΔC ≈ 14 × 0.1 = 1.4. The exact change is C(4.1) − C(4) = 1.41. The omitted quadratic term is (0.1)² = 0.01, so the linear approximation understates the cost increase. For q > 0, average cost is A(q) = 40/q + 6 + q, with A′(q) = −40/q² + 1. At 4 the derivative is −1.5: average cost is falling locally while total cost is rising. Different denominators and functions eliminate the apparent contradiction. The cost elasticity is ε C,q = C′(q)q/C(q), defined here for q > 0 and C(q) > 0. At 4 it is 14 × 4/80 = 0.7. A 1% quantity increase predicts approximately a 0.7% total-cost increase near that point. It is neither the marginal cost of 14 nor the average cost of 20. Elasticity and logarithmic change For a positive differentiable y = f(x) with x > 0, elasticity is ε y,x = (dy/dx)(x/y). It is dimensionless and equals d ln y/d ln x through the chain rule. This makes proportional comparisons independent of a change from dollars to thousands of dollars or units to thousands of units. For y = Axᵏ with A > 0, elasticity is k everywhere on x > 0. Taking logs gives ln y = ln A + k ln x, so log changes satisfy Δ ln y = k Δ ln x exactly. Ordinary percentage changes satisfy y₂/y₁ = (x₂/x₁)ᵏ; replacing them by k times the input percentage change is usually only a local approximation. Demand elasticity is often negative; some economic discussions report its absolute magnitude. State the convention. A zero or negative level does not permit a real natural logarithm, and elasticity at a zero denominator is undefined. Near zero, large calculated elasticities may reflect the base as much as the economically relevant absolute response. Implicit differentiation Sometimes x and y are related by an equation F(x, y) = 0 rather than an explicit y = f(x). If there is a differentiable solution branch y(x), differentiate the equation while remembering that y depends on x. This produces F x + F y y′ = 0 and hence y′ = −F x /F y when F y ≠ 0. The subscripts mean derivatives with respect to one coordinate holding the other fixed; Chapter 7 formalizes these partial derivatives. The calculation alone does not prove that the branch exists. A continuously differentiable F with nonzero F y at a solution has a local differentiable branch under the implicit-function theorem. At a zero denominator, inspect the original relationship rather than treating an infinite symbolic ratio as an ordinary derivative. For x² + y² = 25, differentiating gives 2x + 2yy′ = 0, so y′ = −x/y where y ≠ 0. At (3, 4), the upper branch has slope −3/4. The lower branch y = −√(25 − x²) has the opposite sign at x = 3. The equation defines more than one branch; the chosen point and domain determine the local response. Worked example 2 — Rate sensitivity with a second-order correction A certain payment of 121 at date 2 is discounted at a constant per-period decimal rate r > −1: P(r) = 121(1 + r)⁻². This is a stipulated discount model, with payment and date held fixed. Differentiation gives P′(r) = −242(1 + r)⁻³ and P″(r) = 726(1 + r)⁻⁴. At r₀ = 0.10, P = 100, P′ ≈ −181.818182 and P″ ≈ 495.867769. Raising the rate by h = 0.01 gives a linear estimate P ≈ 100 − 1.818182 = 98.181818. Including the quadratic term gives P ≈ 100 − 1.818182 + 0.5 × 495.867769 × 0.01² = 98.206612. The exact price at 0.11 is 121/1.11² ≈ 98.206314. The first-order price error is about −0.024496; the second-order error is about +0.000298. Positive curvature explains why the tangent-line estimate lies below the actual price for this increase. For a bound, P‴(r) = −2904(1 + r)⁻⁵. On [0.10, 0.11] its absolute value is at most 2904/1.1⁵ ≈ 1803.155522. Taylor's second-order remainder is therefore bounded by M|h|³/6 ≈ 0.000301. This bound is meaningful because the interval and third-derivative bound are specified. The local proportional sensitivity −P′/P is 2/(1 + r), equal to about 1.818182 at 0.10. Later fixed-income chapters distinguish duration definitions and multiple yield-curve changes. This example varies a single rate and says nothing about credit-risk changes or which discount rate is appropriate. Taylor approximation and disciplined error claims If f has continuous second derivative near a, the quadratic expansion is f(a + h) = f(a) + f′(a)h + ½f″(a)h² + o(h²). Under stronger regularity, Taylor's theorem supplies an explicit bound: with continuous third derivative on the interval between a and a + h and |f‴| ≤ M there, the second-order remainder has absolute value at most M|h|³/6. A first-order expansion similarly has error at most K|h|²/2 if |f″| ≤ K on that interval. A higher-order approximation may improve accuracy without settling model error. A bond formula evaluated to ten decimals can still be economically unsuitable if the payment is uncertain or the discount assumptions are wrong. Separate truncation error, arithmetic rounding and uncertainty in the relationship itself. For ln(1 + h) around zero, the quadratic approximation is h − h²/2. It requires h > −1 and is locally accurate; for h = 0.1 it gives 0.095 versus ln(1.1) ≈ 0.09531018. Saying “log growth equals percentage growth” without the approximation qualifier would be false. Exercises Derive the derivative of f(x) = 3x² + 2x − 7 directly from its difference quotient. At x = 2 and h = 0.05, compare the exact output change with the first-order estimate and state the exact remainder. Differentiate (x² + 1)e −x , ln(2x + 3), and (3x + 1)/(x − 2). Give the relevant real domains and identify which rules are used. For C(q) = 25 + 4q + 2q² with q ≥ 0, compute total, marginal and average cost at q = 5. Compute the total-cost elasticity there and the derivative of average cost. Interpret their units and signs. Synthetic demand is Q(p) = 80p −1.5 for p > 0. Find the signed elasticity. For a price increase of 10%, compare the elasticity-based local prediction for quantity change with its exact proportional change. For x² + xy + y² = 7, calculate the local y′ at (1, 2). State the denominator condition. Find the two solutions for y at x = 1 and explain why specifying the branch matters. Use a quadratic Taylor expansion at zero to approximate e 0.2 . Bound its error using |f‴| ≤ e 0.2 < 1.222 on [0, 0.2]. Compare with the exact exponential and explain whether the estimate is above or below it. Explain why f′(a) = 0 does not establish a maximum, using x², −x² and x³ at zero. Explain why |x| at zero has a minimum without a two-sided derivative, and why an endpoint optimum need not satisfy an interior condition. A payment of 110 at date 1 has P(r) = 110/(1 + r), r > −1. At r = 0.10, compute P, P′ and P″. Estimate the price at 0.12 using first- and second-order Taylor expansions and compare with the exact price. State the decimal rate change and the assumptions held fixed. Solutions and derivations The quotient is 3[(x + h)² − x²]/h + 2 = 6x + 3h + 2, approaching 6x + 2. At 2 the slope is 14; the linear change is 0.7. The exact change is 0.7 + 3(0.05)² = 0.7075, and the remainder is 0.0075. The constant cancels. The derivatives are e −x [2x − (x² + 1)] on ℝ, 2/(2x + 3) for x > −1.5, and [3(x − 2) − (3x + 1)]/(x − 2)² = −7/(x − 2)² for x ≠ 2. They use product plus chain, logarithm plus chain, and quotient respectively. None acquires a value at an excluded domain point by differentiation. C(5) = 95 dollars, C′(5) = 24 dollars per unit, and C(5)/5 = 19 dollars per unit. Elasticity is 24 × 5/95 = 24/19 ≈ 1.263158, dimensionless. Average cost is 25/q + 4 + 2q; its derivative at 5 is −25/25 + 2 = 1 dollar per squared unit. Both total cost and average cost increase locally there. Q′ = −120p −2.5 and pQ′/Q = −1.5. A 10% price rise predicts approximately −15% quantity change. Exactly Q₂/Q₁ = 1.1 −1.5 ≈ 0.866784, so quantity falls about 13.3216%. Constant elasticity makes the relation between log changes exact, not the finite ordinary percentage approximation. Differentiate to get 2x + y + (x + 2y)y′ = 0, hence y′ = −(2x + y)/(x + 2y), requiring x + 2y ≠ 0. At (1, 2), slope is −4/5. At x = 1, y² + y − 6 = 0 gives y = 2 or −3; the latter has slope −1/5. Each solution is on a different local branch, so the relation alone does not specify which response is intended. The quadratic value is 1 + 0.2 + 0.2²/2 = 1.22. The error bound is 1.222 × 0.2³/6 ≈ 0.001629333. The exact value is about 1.221402758, giving error about −0.001402758, within the bound. All higher exponential derivatives are positive, so the quadratic expansion is below the exact value for this positive increment; Taylor's third-order remainder has positive sign. x² has a minimum, −x² has a maximum, and x³ has neither at zero, despite all three derivatives being zero. |x| has a minimum at its nondifferentiable kink. On [0, 1], f(x) = x has a maximum at 1 with slope 1; the interior first-order condition does not apply at that boundary. A condition can be necessary under particular hypotheses without being sufficient or universally necessary. At 0.10, P = 100, P′ = −110/1.1² ≈ −90.909091, and P″ = 220/1.1³ ≈ 165.289256. h = 0.02, a 2 percentage point increase. Linear price is 98.181818; quadratic price is 98.214876. The exact price 110/1.12 is about 98.214286, so respective errors are approximately −0.032468 and +0.000590. Payment certainty, date, amount and the single-rate discount model are fixed. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: derive a product rule rather than merely naming it. Explain why marginal and average cost differ, translate an elasticity into a local proportional response, and state the assumptions behind a Taylor error bound. Delayed check: differentiate a new composite function, compute a response with correct units, diagnose a kink, and construct a stationary point that is not an optimum. Recompute the rate-sensitivity example with a decrease and compare predicted and exact prices. Further study and scope MIT's Single Variable Calculus course supplies further derivative instruction and independent practice. This chapter is an original economic application of standard calculus, not a reproduction of its problems. For proof-oriented extensions, MIT 18.100A Lectures 17–20 cover derivative definitions, differentiation rules, the mean value theorem and Taylor's theorem. Multivariable responses and existence of implicit branches receive fuller treatment in Chapter 7; optimization conditions follow in Chapters 8–9."}, {"title": "02.04 · Integrals and accumulated quantities", "url": "v02-c04.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Recovering totals from rates of change A marginal cost curve tells you how total cost changes with output, but it does not reveal the initial fixed cost. A flow of dollars per year does not equal a dollar stock without accumulation over time. A stream of discounted receipts requires both a timing model and a rule for adding contributions. Integration makes these relationships precise. The simplest picture is many narrow rectangles: each has height equal to a rate and width equal to a small amount of the input. Their summed area approximates accumulated output. The integral is the limiting total when the partition becomes arbitrarily fine. Its units are the rate's units multiplied by the input's units. This chapter focuses on real single-variable Riemann integration, sufficient for the continuous models here; probability and more general integration come later. Definite integrals and signed accumulation Partition [a, b] into ordered endpoints a = x₀ < x₁ < … < xₙ = b. In each interval choose a tag ξᵢ and width Δxᵢ = xᵢ − xᵢ₋₁. A Riemann sum is Σᵢ f(ξᵢ)Δxᵢ. The largest interval width is the partition's mesh. A bounded function is Riemann integrable if these sums approach the same finite number as the mesh approaches zero, regardless of the tags and partitions. Write that number ∫ a b f(x) dx. The “regardless” clause matters: one selected sequence of rectangles is not the whole definition. Continuous functions on closed bounded intervals are integrable. Many piecewise continuous functions with finitely many jumps are also integrable: narrow intervals around the jumps contribute arbitrarily little area, while continuity controls the rest. A point's value can affect continuity without changing the integral. An integral is signed accumulation. Where f is negative, its contribution is negative. The geometric area between a curve and the horizontal axis instead uses ∫|f|. For example ∫ −1 1 x dx = 0, while ∫ −1 1 |x| dx = 1. Positive and negative contributions cancel in the first, not in the second. Integration is linear: ∫(αf + βg) = α∫f + β∫g over the same interval for constants α and β and integrable functions. It is additive over adjacent intervals: ∫ a c f = ∫ a b f + ∫ b c f. Reversing endpoints changes the sign, and equal endpoints give zero. If f ≥ g, then ∫f ≥ ∫g for an interval oriented from smaller to larger input. If |f| ≤ M on [a, b], the integral's absolute value is at most M(b − a). This inequality explains how a small input interval limits accumulation for a bounded rate, and it supports error analysis. If the rate is unbounded near an endpoint, this bound is unavailable; an improper integral needs a separate limit. Worked example 1 — Recover total cost and reconcile rectangle bounds Suppose marginal cost is m(q) = 6 + 2q dollars per unit for q ≥ 0, and initial cost C(0) = 40 dollars. Accumulated variable cost to q = 4 is ∫ 0 4 (6 + 2q) dq = [6q + q²] 0 4 = 40 dollars. Therefore C(4) = 40 + 40 = 80, matching the total-cost function in the preceding chapter. For a direct sum check, divide [0, 4] into n equal widths 4/n. The rate is increasing, so left endpoint rectangles underestimate and right endpoint rectangles overestimate. The left sum is 24 + (32/n²)Σ i=0 n−1 i = 24 + 16(n − 1)/n = 40 − 16/n. The right sum is 40 + 16/n. The finite sum Σ i=0 n−1 i = n(n − 1)/2 follows by pairing first and last terms; both bounds converge to 40. For n = 4 the bounds are 36 and 44, and for n = 100 they are 39.84 and 40.16. The midpoint rectangles give exactly 40 for this linear rate because their average heights equal the average of the endpoint heights. That special exactness does not hold for every curved rate. Integrating marginal cost identifies changes in cost, not the initial level. If C(0) were 70, the same marginal curve would imply C(4) = 110. Also, the continuous quantity model is an approximation when goods must be purchased in indivisible units. A discrete cost difference may then be the relevant object. Antiderivatives and the fundamental theorem An antiderivative F of f on an interval satisfies F′ = f there. The indefinite integral notation ∫f(x) dx = F(x) + K describes a family differing by a constant K. If F and G have the same derivative on an interval, their difference has zero derivative and is constant by the mean value theorem. Disconnected domains can require different constants on separate components. The fundamental theorem of calculus links the limiting sum definition to derivatives. First, if f is continuous on [a, b] and A(x) = ∫ a x f(t) dt, then A′(x) = f(x) for interior x. Its difference quotient is the average value of f over the small interval between x and x + h. Continuity makes that average approach f(x). More precisely, A(x + h) − A(x) = ∫ x x+h f(t) dt. Subtract f(x)h and divide by h. For sufficiently small nonzero h, continuity makes |f(t) − f(x)| less than ε throughout the relevant interval, so the resulting average error has absolute value less than ε. The reasoning works for either sign of h through oriented integrals. Second, if f is continuous on [a, b], F is continuous on [a, b], and F′ = f on (a, b), then ∫ a b f(x) dx = F(b) − F(a). To justify it, A and F have the same derivative in the interior, hence differ by a constant; compare their endpoint values using continuity. This evaluation rule is often written [F(x)] a b . Thus a differentiable total with continuous marginal rate obeys C(q) = C(0) + ∫ 0 q C′(s) ds. An initial condition sets the constant. If a total has a jump, integrating its ordinary derivative alone omits that jump; fixed participation costs require a separate term. Calculus must reflect the actual piecewise relationship. A small antiderivative toolkit For n ≠ −1, ∫xⁿ dx = xⁿ⁺¹/(n + 1) + K where the real power is defined and differentiable. On x > 0 this holds for any real n except −1. The exceptional case is ∫1/x dx = ln|x| + K on intervals excluding zero. The absolute value accommodates the negative component without making a logarithm of a negative number. For constant k ≠ 0, ∫e kx dx = e kx /k + K; when k = 0, the integrand is 1 and its antiderivative is x. Also ∫cos x dx = sin x + K and ∫sin x dx = −cos x + K. Verify each candidate by differentiation. Integration generally has no product rule that simply multiplies antiderivatives. The logarithm can also be constructed by ln x = ∫ 1 x (1/t) dt for x > 0. The fundamental theorem then gives its derivative 1/x. A substitution t = au establishes ln(ab) = ln a + ln b for positive a and b, connecting its accumulated-rate definition to multiplicative growth. Its inverse is the exponential. Substitution: undo a chain rule If u = g(x), the differential notation du = g′(x) dx records the change-of-variable rule. For a continuously differentiable g and continuous f on the relevant image, ∫ a b f(g(x))g′(x) dx = ∫ g(a) g(b) f(u) du. This follows by composing an antiderivative of f with g and applying the chain rule and fundamental theorem. For ∫ 0 2 2x/(1 + x²) dx, set u = 1 + x² and du = 2x dx. The endpoints become 1 and 5, giving ∫ 1 5 du/u = ln 5. Either evaluate entirely in u with transformed endpoints or convert the antiderivative back to x before using the original endpoints. Mixing these conventions produces wrong values. Substitution does not require g to be increasing for this antiderivative identity. Its endpoint order handles a decreasing map. More general changes of variables for domains and probability densities require additional attention to one-to-one mappings and absolute Jacobians; those are taught where needed rather than imported silently here. Integration by parts: undo a product rule The product rule (uv)′ = u′v + uv′ yields ∫ a b u(x)v′(x) dx = [u(x)v(x)] a b − ∫ a b u′(x)v(x) dx, for continuously differentiable functions on the interval. The abbreviated notation is ∫u dv = uv − ∫v du. Choose u so differentiating it simplifies the remaining integral, and choose dv so it has a known antiderivative. For ∫ 0 T t e −rt dt with r > 0, choose u = t and dv = e −rt dt, giving v = −e −rt /r. The result is [−te −rt /r] 0 T + (1/r)∫ 0 T e −rt dt = [1 − (1 + rT)e −rT ]/r². Differentiate the indefinite version −te −rt /r − e −rt /r² to check the cancellation. For ∫ln x dx on x > 0, take u = ln x and dv = dx. This gives x ln x − ∫1 dx = x ln x − x + K. A rule choice should reduce the unresolved problem. If it makes the new integral harder, reconsider the decomposition. Improper integrals and convergence An improper integral replaces an infinite endpoint or an unbounded endpoint integrand by a limit of ordinary integrals. Define ∫ a ∞ f(x) dx = lim T→∞ ∫ a T f(x) dx when that limit is finite. A lower-endpoint singularity uses lim ε↓0 ∫ ε b f(x) dx. If there are multiple problematic endpoints, require each separately to converge. Cancellation under one specially coupled truncation is not ordinary improper convergence. For p ≠ 1, ∫ 1 T x⁻ᵖ dx = (T¹⁻ᵖ − 1)/(1 − p). As T → ∞ this is finite exactly when p > 1, then equal to 1/(p − 1). At p = 1 it is ln T and diverges. Near zero, ∫ ε 1 x⁻ᵖ dx is finite exactly when p < 1, then equal to 1/(1 − p). The same exponent can therefore be harmless at one boundary and problematic at another. For nonnegative integrands, comparison provides a useful convergence argument: if 0 ≤ f ≤ g eventually and ∫g converges, then ∫f converges; if f ≥ g ≥ 0 and ∫g diverges, ∫f diverges. The first conclusion uses monotone bounded accumulation. Sign-changing integrands need more care, and absolute convergence is stronger than convergence with cancellations. Worked example 2 — Discount a growing continuous receipt stream Suppose receipts arrive continuously at flow rate c(t) = 120e 0.02t dollars per year, for t measured in years. A stipulated continuously compounded annual discount rate is r = 0.05. The present-value integrand is c(t)e −rt = 120e −0.03t dollars per year. Over five years, present value is ∫ 0 5 120e −0.03t dt = 120(1 − e −0.15 )/0.03 ≈ 557.168094 dollars. The discount is continuous because the model's receipts are continuous; a year-end payment sequence would require a discrete sum with its own dates. Over an infinite horizon, the improper integral converges to 120/0.03 = 4,000 dollars. The remaining present value after year 5 is 4,000e −0.15 ≈ 3,442.831906, so finite-horizon value plus tail equals 4,000. A large tail here is a consequence of indefinitely persistent growing receipts and a small net discount rate. More generally, with flow c₀e gt and discount r, convergence requires r > g, giving c₀/(r − g). If r = g, discounted flow is constant and accumulation grows linearly without bound. If r < g, discounted flow grows and the integral also diverges. Calling an infinite-horizon expression a valuation formula without checking r − g would be invalid. Rates r and g have inverse-year units, so dividing dollars per year by r − g gives dollars. Continuous compounding and an annual effective rate are different conventions: an effective yearly rate i corresponds to r = ln(1 + i) under this constant-rate convention. The example specifies a benchmark discount rate, not an assessment that it adequately prices uncertain receipts. Accumulation, surplus and limits of interpretation If an inverse-demand curve p(q) is interpreted as marginal willingness to pay, ∫ 0 Q p(q) dq gives the corresponding gross willingness-to-pay area, and subtracting price times Q gives a surplus area. This economic interpretation needs assumptions about preferences, money and whose curve is represented. The integral itself is a mathematical total, not proof of a complete social-welfare measure. For p(q) = 12 − q on [0, 12] and a price of 4, demand is Q = 8 and the area above price is ∫ 0 8 (8 − q) dq = 32. If demand was inferred from selected observations or money has materially changing marginal value, stronger welfare claims require additional economics and evidence. Later microeconomics develops those distinctions. When an antiderivative is unavailable, approximate the definite integral numerically with a specified error criterion. Chapter 12 develops quadrature methods. Never treat an arbitrary sum of samples as an integral without including interval widths, endpoint conventions and enough control of the approximation. Exercises Recover C(q) from C′(q) = 4 + 3q² and C(0) = 15. Compute C(2) and the cost change from q = 1 to q = 2. Explain what would remain unidentified without the initial condition. Evaluate ∫ −2 2 x dx and ∫ −2 2 |x| dx. Interpret the difference. Explain whether changing the integrand's value only at zero alters either Riemann integral. Use substitution to evaluate ∫ 0 1 3x²e x³ dx. Show the transformed endpoints and independently differentiate the resulting antiderivative. Use integration by parts to evaluate ∫ 1 e ln x dx and ∫ 0 1 x e x dx. State u, dv and the endpoint terms for each. Determine convergence and values, when finite, of ∫ 1 ∞ x⁻² dx, ∫ 0 1 x −1/2 dx, and ∫ 0 1 x⁻¹ dx. Express each as an endpoint limit rather than substituting infinity or zero into an undefined expression. A constant continuous receipt flow is 80 dollars per year, discounted continuously at 0.04 per year. Compute its present value over ten years and over an infinite horizon, and its remaining present value after year ten. Find the horizon T making the remaining value less than 100. For marginal willingness to pay p(q) = 20 − 2q on [0, 10] and a posted price of 8, compute demanded quantity, expenditure, gross area and the surplus area. State an assumption needed to interpret the last area economically. Let C(0) = 0 and C(q) = 30 + 5q for q > 0. A student integrates the ordinary marginal rate 5 from zero and reports C(4) = 20. Diagnose the omission. Also evaluate ∫ 0 2 (6 + 2q) dq using n equal left and right rectangles, and give a guaranteed error bound for each. Solutions and endpoint checks C(q) = 15 + 4q + q³ for q ≥ 0. C(2) = 31, C(1) = 20, so the increase is 11. Without C(0), all functions K + 4q + q³ have the same marginal rate; the level K is unidentified. A difference between two quantities remains identified. The signed integral is [x²/2] −2 2 = 0. The absolute-area integral is twice ∫ 0 2 x dx = 4. Positive and negative accumulation cancels only in the signed case. A finite changed value at a single point affects neither integral, since that point can be enclosed in intervals of arbitrarily small width. Set u = x³ and du = 3x²dx; endpoints are 0 and 1. The integral is ∫ 0 1 e u du = e − 1. Differentiating e x³ gives 3x²e x³ , confirming the antiderivative on the stated domain. For ln x, take u = ln x, dv = dx, v = x. The value is [x ln x − x] 1 e = 0 − (−1) = 1. For xe x , take u = x, dv = e x dx, v = e x . The antiderivative is xe x − e x and its endpoint difference is 0 − (−1) = 1. The first is lim T→∞ (1 − 1/T) = 1. The second is lim ε↓0 2(1 − √ε) = 2. The third is lim ε↓0 (−ln ε), which grows without bound, so it has no finite improper value. A finite truncated answer is not evidence of convergence. PV₁₀ = 80(1 − e −0.4 )/0.04 = 2,000(1 − e −0.4 ) ≈ 659.359908. PV∞ = 2,000. The tail is 2,000e −0.4 ≈ 1,340.640092. Require 2,000e −0.04T < 100, hence T > ln(20)/0.04 ≈ 74.893307 years; equality at that horizon gives exactly 100, not less. This long horizon follows from the stipulated persistent flow and rate. 20 − 2Q = 8 gives Q = 6. Expenditure is 48; gross area ∫ 0 6 (20 − 2q) dq = 120 − 36 = 84; surplus area is 84 − 48 = 36. One sufficient interpretation assumes utility equals unspent money plus a benefit u(q), with u′(q) = p(q), so each dollar has a constant utility contribution. This is called a quasi-linear preference model. The geometric area alone does not establish distributional or complete social-welfare conclusions. For positive q there is an initial participation jump of 30, so C(4) = 50. The ordinary derivative on q > 0 is 5 and its integral recovers only the variable increment, not the discontinuity at zero. For the separate linear rate on [0, 2], the integral is 16. With width 2/n, the left sum is 16 − 4/n and right sum is 16 + 4/n, each with exact absolute error 4/n in this case. Increasing-rate ordering establishes which is below and above. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain an integral through units and narrow rectangles, then derive the evaluation rule from accumulation and differentiation. Show how an initial condition, a participation jump and a tail-convergence condition answer three different questions. Delayed check: recover a new total from a marginal curve, evaluate one substitution and one integration-by-parts problem, and assess an infinite horizon before computing its value. Include the exact truncated-tail expression in the answer. Further study and scope MIT's Single Variable Calculus course offers independent integration practice, including definite integrals and standard techniques. The receipt streams and cost cases here are original constructions with explicitly stated conventions. For a proof-oriented treatment, MIT 18.100A Lectures 20–22 develop Riemann sums, integrability, the fundamental theorem, change of variables and integration by parts. Later probability instruction adds expectations and densities; advanced analysis distinguishes broader integration concepts when the applications require them."}, {"title": "02.05 · Vectors, matrices and linear systems", "url": "v02-c05.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Several equations do not necessarily provide several answers A firm may face several resource constraints, an analyst may observe several financial outcomes, and a researcher may estimate several coefficients. Counting equations is insufficient. Some equations can duplicate others, some can contradict them, and some can leave economically important directions unresolved. Linear algebra identifies these possibilities before a solver's output is mistaken for a uniquely determined decision. This chapter develops vectors, matrices, elimination, rank and the spaces that describe solutions. The examples are synthetic and exact. Statistical noise, inference and numerical error are separate issues developed later, but the underlying algebra already reveals what information a system can and cannot supply. Vectors, coordinates and units A vector x ∈ ℝⁿ is an ordered collection of n real coordinates. Unless stated otherwise, vectors are columns; xᵀ is the corresponding row, and T denotes transpose. Coordinatewise addition and scalar multiplication give x + y and cx. A linear combination c₁v₁ + … + cₖvₖ adds specified multiples of vectors of the same dimension. The dot product xᵀy = Σᵢ xᵢyᵢ is a scalar. For a price vector p and quantity vector q in matching order, pᵀq is total expenditure. With p = (3, 5)ᵀ and q = (4, 2)ᵀ, expenditure is 3 × 4 + 5 × 2 = 22. Each product must have a common output unit. Swapping one vector's order without swapping the other changes the meaning. The Euclidean norm ‖x‖₂ = √(xᵀx) measures geometric length. It is useful for errors measured in comparable coordinates. Squaring and adding quantities in dollars and percentages without scaling is not a neutral measurement choice. A norm can be mathematically well defined while economically inappropriate for the task. The standard coordinate vectors e₁, …, eₙ each have a single coordinate equal to 1 and the rest zero. Every x is Σᵢ xᵢeᵢ. This representation is unique because each coefficient determines one separate coordinate. Alternative coordinate systems can be useful, but their meaning and units must also be stated. Matrices are linear mappings An m × n matrix A has m rows and n columns, with entry aᵢⱼ in row i and column j. It maps an n-coordinate input x to an m-coordinate output Ax. The ith output is (Ax)ᵢ = Σⱼ aᵢⱼxⱼ. Equivalently, Ax is the linear combination of A's columns using x's coordinates as coefficients. The row view describes equations; the column view describes attainable outputs. Matrix addition requires matching dimensions. If A is m × n and B is n × k, their product AB is m × k with (AB)ᵢⱼ = Σₗ aᵢₗbₗⱼ. B acts first on an input, then A acts on the result. The shared inner dimension makes composition possible. For mismatched dimensions the product is undefined, not zero. Multiplication is associative and distributes over addition, but is generally not commutative. Even when both AB and BA exist and have the same size, their entries may differ. For A = [[1, 2], [0, 1]] and B = [[2, 0], [1, 3]], AB = [[4, 6], [1, 3]], while BA = [[2, 4], [1, 5]]. Here double brackets list rows. Always preserve the order of transformations. The identity matrix Iₙ has ones on its diagonal and zero elsewhere, giving Iₙx = x. The zero matrix maps every input to zero. Transpose exchanges rows and columns, and (AB)ᵀ = BᵀAᵀ: the entry formula gives the same sum on both sides. This reversed order will matter when deriving least-squares equations. A mapping is linear when it preserves addition and scalar multiplication: A(x + y) = Ax + Ay and A(cx) = cAx. A rule Ax + b with b ≠ 0 is affine, rather than linear, because it does not map the zero vector to zero. Intercepts can be represented through an extra constant coordinate, but that is a modeling representation, not a change in the underlying definition. Solving Ax = b by elimination Write the coefficients and outcomes as an augmented matrix [A | b]. Elementary row operations swap two equations, multiply one by a nonzero constant, or add a multiple of one equation to another. Each is reversible, so it preserves the solution set when applied to both coefficients and outcomes. Gaussian elimination successively creates zeros below chosen nonzero entries called pivots. If a proposed pivot is zero, swap in a row with a nonzero entry in that column if possible; otherwise move to a later column. A zero pivot encountered in one ordering does not itself establish singularity. Back substitution then recovers pivot variables from an upper-triangular system. In reduced row-echelon form, each pivot is 1 and the only nonzero entry in its column, pivots move rightward down the rows, and zero rows are last. A row [0, …, 0 | c] with c ≠ 0 asserts 0 = c and proves inconsistency. A row of zeros on both sides supplies no additional restriction. Columns without pivots correspond to free variables, provided the system is consistent. For a consistent system, choose values for the free variables and solve the pivot variables from the reduced equations. The choices can range over real numbers in pure algebra. Economic constraints such as nonnegative quantities must then be intersected with this solution family. An algebraically unique solution can be economically infeasible, and an algebraically nonunique family can become unique under additional restrictions: x + y = 0 has infinitely many real solutions but only (0, 0) when both coordinates must be nonnegative. Worked example 1 — A uniquely determined resource allocation Two production activities use resource amounts summarized by A = [[2, 1], [1, 3]]. Let activity quantities be x and y. Required resource use b = (14, 17)ᵀ gives 2x + y = 14 and x + 3y = 17. The coefficients record resource units per activity unit; these equations specify exact use, not upper limits or profit maximization. Subtract half the first equation from the second to obtain 2.5y = 10, hence y = 4. Substitution gives x = 5. Check both original equations: 2 × 5 + 4 = 14 and 5 + 3 × 4 = 17. Both quantities are nonnegative, so this solution also respects the stated sign restrictions. There are two pivot columns and no free variable. Uniqueness can also be proved directly: if two solutions differed by (u, v), that difference would satisfy 2u + v = 0 and u + 3v = 0. The first gives v = −2u; the second then gives −5u = 0. Thus u = v = 0, so the original solutions coincide. Changing the outcome vector changes the required activities. For b = (1, 10)ᵀ, the same matrix gives x = −1.4 and y = 3.8. The algebraic solution is unique but violates nonnegativity. Resource equalities, output requirements, feasible inequality sets and optimal choices are distinct objects; interpreting them as interchangeable would invent an economic conclusion. Subspaces, span and independence A subspace of ℝⁿ contains zero and is closed under addition and scalar multiplication. The span of vectors v₁, …, vₖ is the set of all their real linear combinations and is a subspace. A nonnegative orthant is not a subspace because multiplying a nonzero nonnegative vector by −1 leaves it. An affordable set with a finite budget is generally not a subspace either. Vectors are linearly independent if c₁v₁ + … + cₖvₖ = 0 implies every cᵢ = 0. Otherwise a nonzero coefficient combination produces zero, revealing redundancy. This is stronger than merely requiring the vectors to be different. For example (1, 0)ᵀ, (0, 1)ᵀ and (1, 1)ᵀ are different but dependent: the third is the sum of the first two. A basis of a subspace is an independent spanning set. It supplies every vector in the space with a unique coordinate representation. Spanning establishes existence of a representation; independence establishes uniqueness, since subtracting two representations would give a nonzero dependence if their coefficients differed. The number of basis vectors is the subspace's dimension. In finite dimensions, all bases of the same subspace have the same size. One way to see the restriction is that independent vectors expressed using a spanning set cannot outnumber that set: elimination of their coordinate columns would otherwise leave a free coefficient in a zero combination. Applying this argument in both directions gives equal basis sizes. A vector can be independent of a particular list while still belonging to a larger space. Specify the list and ambient space. Independence is an exact algebraic property; vectors very close to dependence can remain independent and still create unstable numerical calculations. The spaces describing solvability The column space Col(A) is the span of A's columns in ℝᵐ. By the column interpretation of Ax, Ax = b has a solution exactly when b ∈ Col(A). The null space Null(A) = {z ∈ ℝⁿ : Az = 0} is a subspace containing input directions invisible to the mapping. The row space is the span of A's rows, viewed as vectors in ℝⁿ. The rank of A is the dimension of its column space and also of its row space, equal to the number of pivots in elimination. Row operations preserve dependencies among columns and the row span; in reduced form the independent pivot columns and nonzero rows each count the same pivots. To obtain a column-space basis from elimination, use the corresponding pivot columns of the original A. Row operations can change the actual column space as a subset of ℝᵐ. The rank-nullity relationship is rank(A) + dim Null(A) = n, where n is the number of input coordinates. In reduced form each nonpivot variable can be freely chosen; its associated special solution forms one null-space basis vector. There are n minus rank(A) such variables. Output dimension m does not replace n in this relationship. If xₚ solves Ax = b, every solution is xₚ + z for some z ∈ Null(A). Indeed A(xₚ + z) = b, and any other solution x gives A(x − xₚ) = 0. Thus consistency and uniqueness can be diagnosed separately: existence needs b in the column space; a consistent system is unique exactly when the null space contains only zero. Full column rank means rank(A) = n, so a solution, when it exists, is unique. Full row rank means rank(A) = m, so every b ∈ ℝᵐ is attainable. A tall matrix with more rows than columns may have unique solutions for attainable outcomes but fail to fit other outcomes exactly. A wide matrix with more columns than rows cannot have full column rank and therefore cannot uniquely determine unrestricted inputs. Worked example 2 — Redundant observations and an invisible direction Consider A = [[1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 6]] and b = (12, 24)ᵀ. The second equation is twice the first. Subtracting twice row 1 from row 2 produces a zero row, leaving x₁ + 2x₂ + 3x₃ = 12. The rank is 1, not 2. Choose x₂ = s and x₃ = t. Then x = (12 − 2s − 3t, s, t)ᵀ = (12, 0, 0)ᵀ + s(−2, 1, 0)ᵀ + t(−3, 0, 1)ᵀ. The two latter vectors form a null-space basis: each maps to zero and they are independent because their second and third coordinates isolate the coefficients. Nullity is 2, consistent with 1 + 2 = 3. A column-space basis is (1, 2)ᵀ; a row-space basis is (1, 2, 3). If activities must be nonnegative, impose s ≥ 0, t ≥ 0 and 2s + 3t ≤ 12. There are still many feasible inputs, including (12, 0, 0)ᵀ and (0, 6, 0)ᵀ. If the recorded outcome instead is (12, 25)ᵀ, elimination gives 0 = 1 in the second row, so there is no exact solution. The new outcome is outside the column space. This could arise from noise or a wrong model, but algebra alone cannot decide which. The next chapter introduces a closest-fit criterion rather than pretending an inconsistent system is exactly solved. Observing the same combination twice does not identify three underlying inputs. Collecting an additional observation helps only if it supplies a genuinely different restriction on the unresolved directions. More rows are not automatically more independent information. Inverse matrices and when to use them A square n × n matrix is invertible if there is an A⁻¹ satisfying A⁻¹A = AA⁻¹ = Iₙ. Then Ax = b has the unique solution x = A⁻¹b for every b. Conversely, a square matrix with rank n maps every outcome uniquely and has an inverse obtained by solving for the inputs corresponding to each identity column. To compute that inverse by elimination, reduce [A | Iₙ] to [Iₙ | A⁻¹]. If the left side cannot be reduced to the identity because rank is deficient, no ordinary inverse exists. For A = [[a, b], [c, d]], determinant ad − bc is nonzero exactly when it is invertible, and A⁻¹ = (ad − bc)⁻¹[[d, −b], [−c, a]]. Multiplying verifies both identity products. The determinant of a square matrix more generally is a scalar detecting whether its columns span the full space; higher-dimensional computation is better handled by elimination than expansion by hand. For the resource matrix above, det A = 5 and A⁻¹ = (1/5)[[3, −1], [−1, 2]], which reproduces (5, 4)ᵀ from (14, 17)ᵀ. An inverse is a useful theoretical expression, but solving a system numerically does not usually require explicitly forming one. Factorization and substitution can be more efficient and more stable. Exact rank and numerical reliability differ. With equations x + y = 2 and x + (1 + ε)y = 2 + ε, ε ≠ 0 determines x = y = 1. A perturbation δ to the second outcome changes y by δ/ε and x by −δ/ε. When ε is tiny, a small data discrepancy is amplified. An invertible matrix can therefore leave practical estimates poorly determined even though their exact mathematical solution is unique. Exercises For prices p = (2, 7, 4)ᵀ and quantities q = (3, 2, 5)ᵀ, compute expenditure, q + (1, 0, −2)ᵀ and ‖q‖₂. Explain why the expenditure has economic units while a norm over goods of different units needs a scaling convention. For A = [[1, 2], [0, 1]] and B = [[2, 0], [1, 3]], compute AB and BA and verify (AB)ᵀ = BᵀAᵀ. Explain the order of operations represented by AB. Solve x + y + z = 6, 2x + y + 3z = 13, and x − y + z = 2 by row elimination. Display the intermediate equations and check the result in all original equations. For A = [[1, 2, −1], [2, 4, −2]], find rank and bases for the column, row and null spaces. Describe all solutions for b = (6, 12)ᵀ. Determine whether b = (6, 13)ᵀ has a solution. Let u = (1, 1, 0)ᵀ, v = (0, 1, 1)ᵀ and w = (1, 2, 1)ᵀ. Determine independence of the three-vector list, give a basis for its span, and express (2, 3, 1)ᵀ in that basis. Prove uniqueness of the two basis coefficients. Find the inverse of A = [[3, 1], [2, 1]] and verify it by multiplication. Solve Ax = (11, 8)ᵀ. State the rank and nullity. A parameter mapping gives observations (α + β, 2α + 2β)ᵀ = (4, 8)ᵀ. Describe all real parameter pairs that fit. If a new observation α − β = 2 is added, determine the pair and explain why the new equation identifies the previous invisible direction. For x + y = 2 and x + 1.001y = 2.001, solve the system. Then change only the second outcome to 2.011 and solve again. Derive the response for arbitrary ε ≠ 0 and perturbation δ. Explain how setting ε = 0 changes the rank and why negative solutions might be disallowed by an economic model. Solutions and space calculations Expenditure is 6 + 14 + 20 = 40. The changed quantity vector is (4, 2, 3)ᵀ, and the norm is √(9 + 4 + 25) = √38. Each price-times-quantity term contributes currency. A raw norm mixes squared goods coordinates; a meaningful distance should specify comparable units or explicit weights/scales. AB = [[4, 6], [1, 3]] and BA = [[2, 4], [1, 5]]. (AB)ᵀ = [[4, 1], [6, 3]]. BᵀAᵀ = [[2, 1], [0, 3]][[1, 0], [2, 1]] gives that same matrix. AB applies B first and A second; the differing BA demonstrates that reversing the transformations changes the result. Subtract twice equation 1 from equation 2 to obtain −y + z = 1. Subtract equation 1 from equation 3 to obtain −2y = −4. Thus y = 2, z = 3, and x = 1. Checks: 1 + 2 + 3 = 6; 2 + 2 + 9 = 13; 1 − 2 + 3 = 2. All three columns are pivots, giving rank 3 and a unique solution. Rank is 1. A column basis is {(1, 2)ᵀ}, a row basis is {(1, 2, −1)}, and a null basis is {(−2, 1, 0)ᵀ, (1, 0, 1)ᵀ}. All consistent solutions are (6 − 2s + t, s, t)ᵀ with s, t ∈ ℝ. For (6, 13)ᵀ the second equation minus twice the first gives 0 = 1, so no exact solution exists. w = u + v, so the list is dependent. u and v are independent: au + bv = 0 forces a = 0 from coordinate 1 and b = 0 from coordinate 3. They span the list, so form a basis. The target is 2u + v; any representation must have coefficient 2 in coordinate 1 and coefficient 1 in coordinate 3, proving uniqueness. det A = 3 − 2 = 1, giving A⁻¹ = [[1, −1], [−2, 3]]. Both identity products can be checked directly. x = (11 − 8, −22 + 24)ᵀ = (3, 2)ᵀ, and A(3, 2)ᵀ = (11, 8)ᵀ. Rank is 2 and nullity is 0. All pairs are (α, β) = (4 − t, t) for real t. The null direction of the original mapping is (1, −1)ᵀ. Combining α + β = 4 and α − β = 2 gives α = 3, β = 1. The new measurement changes along that null direction, so it removes the previous ambiguity; the augmented coefficient matrix has rank 2. Initially subtracting the first equation gives 0.001y = 0.001, so y = x = 1. With the perturbation it gives 0.001y = 0.011, so y = 11 and x = −9. Generally y = 1 + δ/ε and x = 1 − δ/ε. At ε = 0, the two coefficient rows coincide: rank is 1, giving many solutions if their outcomes match and none if δ ≠ 0. If x and y represent nonnegative activities, the perturbed real solution is economically infeasible. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain existence using the column space and uniqueness using the null space. Demonstrate why a repeated observation does not supply new identification and why an invertible system can still amplify measurement error. Delayed check: reduce a new rectangular matrix, report the correct spaces and dimensions, and construct one attainable and one unattainable outcome when rank permits. Verify a general solution family in the original equations, not only in the reduced system. Further study and scope MIT's Column Space and Nullspace materials provide independent study of the geometry of exact systems. This chapter's resource and parameter cases are original and use no observed data. MIT's Independence, Basis and Dimension materials offer further practice. The next chapter adds orthogonal projections, eigenvalues, quadratic forms and singular values; later empirical instruction supplies the statistical assumptions needed to interpret fitted parameters."}, {"title": "02.06 · Eigenvalues, projections and quadratic forms", "url": "v02-c06.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Geometry reveals what equations conceal An inconsistent system can still have a well-defined closest fit. A matrix can stretch one direction strongly and another barely at all. A collection of risks can contain both a common movement and an offsetting movement. Orthogonal projections, eigenvalues and singular values describe these relationships in coordinates suited to the problem. This chapter builds on exact systems and Euclidean norms. It develops least squares as a geometric calculation, symmetric quadratic forms as measures of curvature and dispersion, and eigenvectors as directions preserved by a mapping. The examples are original and synthetic. A mathematical fit does not establish causality, and a covariance calculation does not supply a complete investment decision. Orthogonality and projection onto a line Vectors u and v are orthogonal when uᵀv = 0. For orthogonal vectors, ‖u + v‖₂² = ‖u‖₂² + ‖v‖₂² because the cross term vanishes. This is the vector form of Pythagoras. An orthonormal list consists of mutually orthogonal vectors each of norm 1. For a nonzero vector a, find the multiple ca closest to b. Expand ‖b − ca‖₂² = bᵀb − 2c aᵀb + c²aᵀa. Completing the square gives a unique minimizing coefficient c = (aᵀb)/(aᵀa). The projection is p = a(aᵀb)/(aᵀa), and the residual r = b − p is orthogonal to a. The same conclusion follows without a derivative: for any other multiple p + ta, b − (p + ta) = r − ta, whose squared norm is ‖r‖₂² + t²‖a‖₂². Every nonzero t increases the error. The best approximation is therefore characterized by residual orthogonality, not by an exact equation b = ca. If the columns of Q are orthonormal, the projection onto their span is QQᵀb. Its coordinates in that basis are Qᵀb. For a square orthogonal matrix Q, QᵀQ = QQᵀ = I; it preserves Euclidean lengths. For a rectangular matrix with orthonormal columns, QᵀQ = I still holds, but QQᵀ is generally a projection rather than the identity on the larger output space. Least squares and the normal equations For an m × n matrix A and observed b ∈ ℝᵐ, least squares minimizes ‖b − Ax‖₂² over real x. The fitted output Ax is constrained to lie in Col(A). A closest output exists because this finite-dimensional subspace admits an orthonormal basis and hence a projection. The fitted output is unique even when the coefficient x is not. At a minimizing x̂, the residual r = b − Ax̂ must be orthogonal to each column of A. Otherwise moving along that column with a sufficiently small coefficient of the appropriate sign would reduce squared error. Algebraically, the change for a candidate x̂ + th is −2t rᵀAh + t²‖Ah‖₂²; if the linear coefficient were nonzero, one sign of small t would reduce it. Thus Aᵀr = 0, giving the normal equations AᵀA x̂ = Aᵀb. Conversely, if these equations hold, then for any h, ‖b − A(x̂ + h)‖₂² = ‖r‖₂² + ‖Ah‖₂² ≥ ‖r‖₂². This proves global optimality, rather than merely identifying a stationary candidate. If A has independent columns, AᵀA is invertible: hᵀAᵀAh = ‖Ah‖₂² > 0 for nonzero h, so its null space is trivial. Then x̂ = (AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀb is unique, and the projection matrix P = A(AᵀA)⁻¹Aᵀ gives the fitted output Pb. P is symmetric, Pᵀ = P, and idempotent, P² = P. The first follows from the symmetry of AᵀA and its inverse; the second cancels the middle AᵀA. Projecting a fitted output again leaves it unchanged. Residuals lie in the orthogonal complement of the column space, meaning the set of vectors orthogonal to every member of that space. If columns are dependent, several coefficients can generate the same fitted output; adding a null-space vector preserves it. A chosen minimum-length coefficient or a regularized coefficient is then an additional selection rule, not evidence that the observations identified the original parameters. Worked example 1 — Fit a line and check residual orthogonality Suppose three synthetic observations have inputs t = −1, 0, 1 and outputs b = (1, 2, 4)ᵀ. Fit bᵢ ≈ α + βtᵢ with equal squared-error weights. The design matrix is A = [[1, −1], [1, 0], [1, 1]]. Its columns represent an intercept and an input slope. AᵀA = [[3, 0], [0, 2]] and Aᵀb = (7, 3)ᵀ. Hence α̂ = 7/3 and β̂ = 3/2. Fitted values are (5/6, 7/3, 23/6)ᵀ, giving residuals (1/6, −1/3, 1/6)ᵀ. Their sum is zero and their dot product with the input column is −1/6 + 1/6 = 0, as the normal equations require. Residual squared norm is 1/36 + 1/9 + 1/36 = 1/6. For any alternative coefficient pair x̂ + h, the error increases by ‖Ah‖₂²; since A has rank 2, this increase is strictly positive for nonzero h. Thus the coefficient pair is uniquely optimal under the stated criterion. The projection matrix is P = (1/6)[[5, 2, −1], [2, 2, 2], [−1, 2, 5]]. Multiplication gives the same fitted values and P² = P. Negative entries do not invalidate a projection; its role is to preserve the subspace and enforce orthogonality, not to serve as a probability matrix. The fit summarizes these three points. Predicting at t = 2 gives 16/3 under the same line, but extrapolation quality needs evidence. Interpreting β̂ as a causal effect additionally requires a valid research design, not only a small residual or independent columns. Constructing orthonormal coordinates Gram–Schmidt converts an independent list v₁, …, vₖ into an orthonormal basis of the same span. Normalize v₁ to obtain q₁. Subtract from v₂ its projection q₁(q₁ᵀv₂), then normalize the nonzero remainder to obtain q₂. At step j subtract every previous component Σ i<j qᵢ(qᵢᵀvⱼ), where the sum runs over i < j, and normalize what remains. Independence ensures the remainder is nonzero in exact arithmetic; a zero remainder identifies dependence. Every new remainder is orthogonal to the preceding basis vectors because their inner products cancel. The span is preserved since the original vector equals the remainder plus previously represented components. For a full-column-rank A this construction gives A = QR, with orthonormal columns in Q and an upper-triangular square R. Least squares can then be solved by R x̂ = Qᵀb. This avoids forming AᵀA, which can worsen sensitivity to rounding. Practical algorithms use numerically stable orthogonal transformations or modified procedures; the conceptual construction here is not a claim that naive Gram–Schmidt is always a robust implementation. Eigenvalues and directions preserved by a mapping For a square matrix M, an eigenvector v ≠ 0 and eigenvalue λ satisfy Mv = λv. The zero vector is excluded because it would satisfy the equation for every λ. Multiplying v by a nonzero scalar yields another vector in the same eigendirection. For a given λ, the eigenspace is Null(M − λI). Eigenvalues satisfy det(M − λI) = 0. For a 2 × 2 matrix, the characteristic equation is λ² − tr(M)λ + det(M) = 0, where trace tr(M) is the sum of diagonal entries. Its roots sum to the trace and multiply to the determinant. After finding a root, solve the homogeneous system to obtain its eigenvectors and normalize when useful. Eigenvectors associated with distinct eigenvalues are independent. For two, if v₂ were a multiple of v₁, applying M would require its nonzero direction to have both eigenvalues, impossible. The general result follows by applying M − λI to a dependence and reducing the number of distinct eigenvalues inductively. If M has n independent eigenvectors, put them into the invertible matrix V and their eigenvalues on diagonal D. Then MV = VD, hence M = VDV⁻¹ and Mᵗ = VDᵗV⁻¹ for nonnegative integer t. The mapping becomes coordinatewise scaling in its eigenbasis. A real matrix need not have real eigenvalues or a complete eigenbasis. [[0, −1], [1, 0]] rotates vectors and has roots ±i, with i² = −1. [[1, 1], [0, 1]] has repeated eigenvalue 1 but only one independent eigenvector; its tth power is [[1, t], [0, 1]], not the identity. The real symmetric case has substantially stronger structure. Symmetry and quadratic forms A real symmetric matrix S satisfies Sᵀ = S. Its eigenvalues are real and it has an orthonormal eigenbasis: S = QΛQᵀ with Q orthogonal and Λ real diagonal. This is the spectral theorem for real symmetric matrices. We use that standard theorem here; Chapter 10 supplies its full existence proof through compactness, extrema on the unit sphere and induction on an invariant orthogonal complement. Orthogonality of distinct-eigenvalue directions follows directly. If Su = λu and Sv = μv, then uᵀSv = μuᵀv but also (Su)ᵀv = λuᵀv because S is symmetric. Thus (λ − μ)uᵀv = 0, so uᵀv = 0 when λ ≠ μ. Within a repeated-eigenvalue eigenspace, an orthonormal basis can be chosen using Gram–Schmidt. A quadratic form is xᵀSx. In eigen-coordinates z = Qᵀx it becomes Σᵢ λᵢzᵢ². S is positive definite if this is positive for every nonzero x, positive semidefinite if nonnegative for every x, negative definite if negative for every nonzero x, and indefinite if it takes both positive and negative values. These conditions correspond respectively to all eigenvalues positive, all nonnegative, all negative, or a mixture of positive and negative eigenvalues. For a nonsymmetric matrix M, xᵀMx equals xᵀ[(M + Mᵀ)/2]x, since its antisymmetric part contributes zero. Use the symmetric part for quadratic-form curvature. Positive diagonal entries alone do not prove positive definiteness: S = [[1, 2], [2, 1]] gives (1, −1)S(1, −1)ᵀ = −2 while its value on (1, 1)ᵀ is positive. For symmetric S = [[a, b], [b, c]], positive definiteness is equivalent to a > 0 and ac − b² > 0. Complete the square: xᵀSx = a(x₁ + (b/a)x₂)² + (c − b²/a)x₂² when a > 0. Both coefficients must be positive. Positive semidefiniteness instead requires a ≥ 0, c ≥ 0 and ac − b² ≥ 0, including boundary cases such as a = 0, which then forces b = 0. Worked example 2 — Common and offsetting risk directions Use the symmetric matrix Σ = [[4, 2], [2, 4]] as a synthetic return covariance matrix, measured in squared percentage points. One explicit zero-mean distribution producing it has four equally likely return pairs: (√6, √6), (−√6, −√6), (√2, −√2), and (−√2, √2), in percentage points. Expected squared returns are 4 and the expected product is 2. Σ(1, 1)ᵀ = 6(1, 1)ᵀ and Σ(1, −1)ᵀ = 2(1, −1)ᵀ. Normalize to q₊ = (1, 1)ᵀ/√2 and q₋ = (1, −1)ᵀ/√2. These orthonormal directions reveal a stronger common movement and a weaker offsetting movement. The decomposition is Σ = 6q₊q₊ᵀ + 2q₋q₋ᵀ. For any exposure vector w, wᵀΣw = 6(q₊ᵀw)² + 2(q₋ᵀw)². Both eigenvalues are positive, so no nonzero exposure has zero variance in this model. Equal fully invested weights w = (1/2, 1/2)ᵀ have variance 3 and standard deviation √3 percentage points. Holding only either asset has variance 4. Why is variance a quadratic form? For returns Rᵢ with means μᵢ, covariance Σᵢⱼ is E[(Rᵢ − μᵢ)(Rⱼ − μⱼ)]. Squaring the centered weighted return and using expectation's linearity gives Var(ΣᵢwᵢRᵢ) = ΣᵢΣⱼwᵢwⱼΣᵢⱼ = wᵀΣw, provided the expected squares are finite. Nonnegative variance establishes positive semidefiniteness of any valid covariance matrix. A small-eigenvalue direction can involve offsetting positions whose weights do not sum to 1. Eigen-directions therefore do not automatically solve a constrained portfolio choice. Expected returns, liability needs, permitted positions, estimation error and preferences enter later investment analysis. Dynamic interpretation in an eigenbasis For a linear recurrence xₜ₊₁ = Mxₜ, xₜ = Mᵗx₀. If M is diagonalizable, each eigen-coordinate evolves as its initial coefficient times λᵗ. An absolute eigenvalue less than 1 makes that component decay; greater than 1 amplifies it; equal to 1 requires more careful analysis. Negative eigenvalues alternate sign, and complex ones can represent rotations. For M = [[0.8, 0.1], [0.1, 0.8]], the common direction (1, 1)ᵀ has eigenvalue 0.9 and the difference direction (1, −1)ᵀ has eigenvalue 0.7. Both components decay, though at different rates. This describes the stipulated linear recurrence; nonlinear systems require a local linearization and additional conditions, developed in Chapter 11. Singular values and weakly measured directions Eigenvectors require a square mapping. A singular value decomposition works for any real m × n A of rank r: A = UᵣDᵣVᵣᵀ, where Uᵣ and Vᵣ have r orthonormal columns and Dᵣ has positive diagonal entries σ₁ ≥ … ≥ σᵣ > 0. These are the thin factors; full orthogonal bases can add zero directions when needed. To derive the construction, AᵀA is symmetric positive semidefinite, since xᵀAᵀAx = ‖Ax‖₂². Choose orthonormal eigenvectors vᵢ for its positive eigenvalues σᵢ² and set uᵢ = Avᵢ/σᵢ. Their pairwise inner products are vᵢᵀAᵀAvⱼ/(σᵢσⱼ), equal to 1 for i = j and 0 otherwise. Thus A maps a right singular direction vᵢ into σᵢ times the left direction uᵢ; vectors in its null space map to zero. A small σ means large input movement in that direction produces little output movement. For square invertible A, its Euclidean condition number is κ₂(A) = σ₁/σₙ. It is a worst-direction amplification ratio, not the error realized under every perturbation. Forming AᵀA squares this ratio, explaining why normal-equation calculations can lose precision compared with orthogonal-factor methods. The pseudoinverse A⁺ = VᵣDᵣ⁻¹Uᵣᵀ provides the least-squares coefficient with smallest Euclidean norm. It reciprocates positive singular values while assigning zero to unseen directions. Other coefficients can have the same fitted output by adding a null-space vector; choosing the smallest norm is a convention about inputs, not identification from outcomes. For A = [[3, 0], [0, 1], [0, 0]], singular values are 3 and 1, right directions are the two coordinate vectors, and left directions are the first two coordinates of ℝ³. With b = (6, 2, 5)ᵀ, the minimum-norm fit has x = (2, 2)ᵀ, fitted output (6, 2, 0)ᵀ and residual (0, 0, 5)ᵀ. The third output direction is unattainable; no inverse manipulation can eliminate that mismatch. Exercises Project b = (3, 0)ᵀ onto the span of a = (1, 2)ᵀ. Compute the coefficient, projection, residual and residual squared norm. Verify orthogonality and the decomposition of ‖b‖₂². Fit y ≈ α + βt by equal-weight least squares to t = 0, 1, 2 and y = 1, 2, 2. Compute AᵀA, Aᵀy, coefficients, fitted values and residuals. Check both orthogonality conditions and the residual sum of squares. Find eigenvalues and orthonormal eigenvectors of S = [[3, 1], [1, 3]]. Reconstruct S from its spectral factors and evaluate xᵀSx at x = (1, −1)ᵀ in both original and eigen-coordinates. Classify [[2, 1], [1, 2]], [[1, 1], [1, 1]], [[1, 2], [2, 1]], and [[−2, 0], [0, −1]] by definiteness. For the semidefinite matrix give a nonzero zero-value vector; for the indefinite matrix give positive- and negative-value vectors. Using Σ = [[4, 2], [2, 4]], compute variance for weights (3/4, 1/4)ᵀ and (1, −1)ᵀ. Compare with equal fully invested weights and explain why the offsetting exposure is not another fully invested long-only allocation. For xₜ₊₁ = [[0.8, 0.1], [0.1, 0.8]]xₜ and x₀ = (3, 1)ᵀ, derive xₜ in common and difference directions. Compute x₁ and x₂ and determine the limit. Explain why a particular coordinate can initially rise despite decay in every eigen-coordinate. For A = [[3, 0], [0, 0.01]], give its singular values and condition number. Solve Ax = (3, 0.01)ᵀ and then Ax = (3, 0.02)ᵀ. Describe the weakly measured direction and why exact invertibility does not guarantee small input errors. Apply Gram–Schmidt to v₁ = (1, 1, 0)ᵀ and v₂ = (0, 1, 1)ᵀ. Give the two orthonormal vectors, check their inner products, and write a thin QR factorization for A = [v₁ v₂]. Solutions and geometric checks aᵀb = 3 and aᵀa = 5, so c = 3/5. Projection is (3/5, 6/5)ᵀ and residual is (12/5, −6/5)ᵀ. Their dot product with a is 12/5 − 12/5 = 0. Residual squared norm is 180/25 = 36/5; projection squared norm is 45/25 = 9/5. Their sum is 9 = ‖b‖₂². A = [[1, 0], [1, 1], [1, 2]], AᵀA = [[3, 3], [3, 5]], Aᵀy = (5, 6)ᵀ. The inverse Gram matrix is (1/6)[[5, −3], [−3, 3]], giving α̂ = 7/6 and β̂ = 1/2. Fitted values are (7/6, 5/3, 13/6)ᵀ; residuals are (−1/6, 1/3, −1/6)ᵀ. They sum to zero and their weighted sum with t is 1/3 − 2/6 = 0. Squared residual norm is 1/6. These are fitting conditions, not causal identification. Eigenvalues are 4 and 2, with q₊ = (1, 1)ᵀ/√2 and q₋ = (1, −1)ᵀ/√2. S = 4q₊q₊ᵀ + 2q₋q₋ᵀ. For x = (1, −1)ᵀ, q₊ᵀx = 0 and q₋ᵀx = √2, so the quadratic value is 2 × 2 = 4. Directly Sx = (2, −2)ᵀ and xᵀSx = 4. The first is positive definite with eigenvalues 3 and 1. The second is positive semidefinite with eigenvalues 2 and 0; x = (1, −1)ᵀ gives zero. The third is indefinite with eigenvalues 3 and −1; (1, 1)ᵀ gives 6 and (1, −1)ᵀ gives −2. The fourth is negative definite with eigenvalues −2 and −1. Zero eigenvalues distinguish semidefinite from definite behavior. Variance for (3/4, 1/4)ᵀ is 4(9/16) + 4(3/16) + 4(1/16) = 13/4 = 3.25, versus 3 for equal weights. For (1, −1)ᵀ it is 4 − 4 + 4 = 4, in squared percentage points. The latter weights sum to zero and include a negative exposure, so they are not a fully invested long-only portfolio. Comparing the numbers without matching exposure constraints would misstate the decision. x₀ = 2(1, 1)ᵀ + (1, −1)ᵀ, giving xₜ = 2(0.9)ᵗ(1, 1)ᵀ + (0.7)ᵗ(1, −1)ᵀ. x₁ = (2.5, 1.1)ᵀ and x₂ = (2.11, 1.13)ᵀ. Both eigen-coefficients tend to zero, so the vector limit is zero. The second original coordinate subtracts the faster-decaying difference component; that subtraction initially shrinks fast enough to let the coordinate rise. The singular values are 3 and 0.01 with coordinate directions on each side; κ₂ = 300. Initial x is (1, 1)ᵀ and perturbed x is (1, 2)ᵀ. The second output changes by only 0.01 while its inferred input changes by 1. The second direction is weakly measured because the mapping scales it down by 0.01. q₁ = (1, 1, 0)ᵀ/√2. Subtract q₁(q₁ᵀv₂) = (1/2, 1/2, 0)ᵀ from v₂, leaving (−1/2, 1/2, 1)ᵀ of norm √(3/2). Thus q₂ = (−1, 1, 2)ᵀ/√6. Both have unit norm and their dot product is zero. With Q = [q₁ q₂], R = [[√2, 1/√2], [0, √(3/2)]], the product QR reconstructs both original columns. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: derive the normal equations through residual geometry and prove their sufficiency. Explain the distinct roles of eigenvalues, singular values and rank; demonstrate why fitting, identification and causal interpretation require different evidence. Delayed check: solve a fresh projection and least-squares problem, classify a symmetric quadratic form with an explicit witness for each failed sign condition, and describe one weak singular direction. Reconstruct an eigenbasis recurrence and verify its first two steps directly. Further study and scope MIT's Projection Matrices and Least Squares materials provide further geometric study. The fitted datasets here are original, and the exercise solutions do not reuse that course's problem bank. MIT's Symmetric Matrices and Positive Definiteness materials offer further study of the spectral theorem and quadratic geometry. MIT's Singular Value Decomposition materials provide deeper matrix-factorization instruction. The next chapters combine this geometry with multivariable derivatives and constrained decisions; statistical and financial applications later add their own empirical assumptions."}, {"title": "02.07 · Multivariable calculus", "url": "v02-c07.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A marginal response must say what is held fixed Output can depend on labor, capital and technology at once. Cost can depend on quantity, capacity and input prices. An equilibrium price and quantity can change together when a parameter changes. A single-variable derivative cannot represent all these dependencies without first specifying which inputs move and which remain fixed. Multivariable calculus turns local changes into linear mappings and curvature into quadratic forms. The linear algebra already developed tells us when those mappings are invertible and which directions they measure. This chapter develops the derivative objects needed for optimization and comparative statics; its economic examples are synthetic rather than estimates from observed data. Domains, contours and multivariable limits A scalar-valued function f: D ⊆ ℝⁿ → ℝ assigns one value to each permitted input vector. A vector-valued function F: D → ℝᵐ assigns m outputs. A domain point is interior if a small open ball around it remains in D; an open ball of radius δ around a contains inputs whose Euclidean distance from a is less than δ. A level set {x ∈ D : f(x) = c} collects inputs producing a fixed output c. In two dimensions it may look like a contour curve. A graph displays input and output together, whereas a level set remains in input space. Distinguishing these objects helps interpret a tangent direction: movement along a level curve changes the inputs while preserving the level locally. The limit f(x) → L as x → a requires every permitted input sufficiently near a, except possibly a itself, to have output within a chosen tolerance of L. In symbols, for every ε > 0 there exists δ > 0 such that 0 < ‖x − a‖₂ < δ implies |f(x) − L| < ε. Checking only coordinate axes misses approaches along diagonals or curved paths. For example, f(x, y) = xy/(x² + y²) away from the origin, with f(0, 0) = 0, is zero along both axes but equals 1/2 along y = x ≠ 0. It is not continuous at the origin. Partial derivatives there will still exist: holding the other coordinate at zero gives a constant zero function. Their existence alone cannot establish continuity or a valid total approximation. Partial derivatives and the gradient The partial derivative with respect to input i is f i (a) = lim h→0 [f(a + heᵢ) − f(a)]/h, where eᵢ is the ith coordinate vector. It varies that coordinate alone. Treat other coordinates as constants while using the ordinary differentiation rules. Both the domain and the held-fixed inputs belong in the interpretation. For f(x, y) = x²y + eʸ, f x = 2xy and f y = x² + eʸ. The first response holds y fixed; the second holds x fixed. If observed y changes with x, neither partial by itself describes the total observed response. A behavioral or equilibrium relationship must supply that other change. The gradient ∇f(a) is the column vector of all partial derivatives. For a differentiable scalar function it packages the local response as df = ∇f(a)ᵀdx. Each derivative has output-per-corresponding-input units, so multiplying it by that input's change yields a common output unit. The coordinates of a gradient do not necessarily share a physical unit. Total differentiability is stronger than a list of partials f is differentiable at an interior point a if there is a linear approximation f(a + h) = f(a) + ∇f(a)ᵀh + r(h), with r(h)/‖h‖₂ → 0 as h → 0. The condition controls all small vector perturbations at once. Partial derivatives, when differentiability holds, determine the coefficients uniquely by choosing coordinate directions. Continuous partial derivatives throughout a neighborhood are a sufficient condition for differentiability. To understand why, move from a to a + h one coordinate at a time. The single-variable mean value theorem expresses each increment as that coordinate change times a partial derivative at an intermediate point. Continuity makes each intermediate partial approach its value at a. The total error is then bounded by a small coefficient times Σᵢ|hᵢ| ≤ √n‖h‖₂. Even existence of every directional derivative is insufficient without the linear structure and remainder control. Let g(x, y) = x³/(x² + y²) away from zero and g(0, 0) = 0. Since |g| ≤ |x|, it is continuous at zero. Its directional derivative along a nonzero vector (u, v) is u³/(u² + v²), obtained by substituting (tu, tv) and dividing by t. This is not linear in the direction. Its coordinate partials are (1, 0), which would predict derivative 1 along direction (1, 1); the actual directional derivative is 1/2. Thus the function is not totally differentiable there. A vector of existing partials is only a candidate approximation until the stronger condition is justified. Directional derivatives and local geometry For differentiable f, the derivative along direction d is D d f(a) = ∇f(a)ᵀd. If ‖d‖₂ = 1, this measures output response per unit movement in the chosen Euclidean coordinates. The Cauchy–Schwarz inequality |uᵀv| ≤ ‖u‖₂‖v‖₂ shows the largest such response is ‖∇f‖₂, attained along the normalized gradient when it is nonzero. The inequality follows by expanding ‖u − tv‖₂² ≥ 0 and minimizing the quadratic over t; its minimum is ‖u‖₂² − (uᵀv)²/‖v‖₂² for nonzero v. This also identifies when equality holds: the vectors lie in the same line. The zero-vector case is immediate. The gradient is orthogonal to tangent directions of a differentiable level curve. If x(t) remains on f(x(t)) = c, differentiation gives ∇f(x(t))ᵀx′(t) = 0. This does not make every orthogonal direction a globally feasible path; it describes the local tangent relationship under regularity. “Steepest” depends on the distance convention. Measuring a coordinate in cents instead of dollars rescales its gradient component and changes the Euclidean unit sphere. When inputs have unlike units, specify scaling or a metric before turning the mathematical steepest direction into a resource-allocation recommendation. The Jacobian and the multivariable chain rule For a differentiable F: ℝⁿ → ℝᵐ, its Jacobian J F is m × n, with entry (i, j) equal to ∂Fᵢ/∂xⱼ. Each row contains one output's partial responses. The total first-order approximation is F(a + h) ≈ F(a) + J F (a)h, with a vector remainder small relative to ‖h‖₂. If x = x(t) is a differentiable path, dF(x(t))/dt = J F (x(t))x′(t). If an outer mapping G has m inputs and k outputs, J G∘F (x) = J G (F(x))J F (x). The product is k × n and applies the inner response first. Multiplying in reverse order usually has wrong dimensions or meaning. For a scalar cost C(q, k, t) whose inputs and explicit time argument all change, dC/dt = C q q′(t) + C k k′(t) + C t . Omitting the last term assumes no direct time dependence; omitting another term assumes that input is fixed. Drawing a dependency diagram or naming intermediate variables can make such assumptions visible. Hessians and second-order responses The Hessian H f collects second partial derivatives: entry (i, j) is ∂(∂f/∂xᵢ)/∂xⱼ. If second partials are continuous in a neighborhood, the mixed partials agree and H is symmetric. Existence without the continuity conditions is not enough to assume this interchange. For f with continuous second partials near a, the second-order expansion is f(a + h) = f(a) + ∇f(a)ᵀh + ½hᵀH f (a)h + o(‖h‖₂²). Apply ordinary Taylor expansion to the path φ(t) = f(a + th): φ′(0) = ∇f(a)ᵀh and φ″(0) = hᵀH f (a)h. This reduces the vector approximation to the single-variable result. More explicitly, the remaining error is ∫ 0 1 (1 − t)hᵀ[H f (a + th) − H f (a)]h dt. The largest entrywise Hessian difference on the small segment tends to zero by continuity. Bounding the quadratic form by that difference times (Σᵢ|hᵢ|)² ≤ n‖h‖₂² proves the required small remainder for all vector approaches, not merely one fixed direction. Diagonal Hessian terms describe each input's own curvature, while mixed terms describe how one input changes another input's marginal response. For two inputs the quadratic term is ½f xx hₓ² + f xy hₓhᵧ + ½f yy hᵧ². The cross term has no extra 1/2 because it appears twice in the symmetric matrix product. Worked example 1 — Several cost changes with an exact second-order check Let C(q, k) = 10 + 4q² + qk + 2k² dollars, with q and k nonnegative scaled input coordinates. At the interior point (3, 2), C = 60 and ∇C = (8q + k, q + 4k)ᵀ = (26, 11)ᵀ. The Hessian is [[8, 1], [1, 4]]. For h = (0.1, −0.2)ᵀ, the linear predicted change is 26 × 0.1 + 11 × (−0.2) = 0.4. The second-order correction is 4(0.1)² + (0.1)(−0.2) + 2(−0.2)² = 0.1. Since the function is quadratic, there are no higher terms: the exact new cost is 60.5. The Hessian's eigenvalues are 6 ± √5, both positive, so every nonzero displacement has positive quadratic curvature. This does not imply every local cost change is positive: the linear term can be negative for a move toward lower-cost inputs. Curvature and the gradient answer different questions. A first-order level-preserving direction is (11, −26)ᵀ because its dot product with the gradient is zero. A finite straight move in that direction generally changes cost through the quadratic term; exact contour preservation may require curving the path. The gradient's steepest unit direction is (26, 11)ᵀ/√797 under the stated coordinate scaling. The implicit-function theorem Suppose F(a, y) = 0 is a system of n equations, with parameter vector a ∈ ℝᵏ and unknown vector y ∈ ℝⁿ. Assume F is continuously differentiable on an open neighborhood of a solution (a₀, y₀), and the n × n unknown Jacobian F y (a₀, y₀) is invertible. The implicit-function theorem ensures a unique continuously differentiable local branch y = φ(a) near that point. Differentiating F(a, φ(a)) = 0 gives F a + F y Dφ = 0. Therefore Dφ = −F y ⁻¹F a . The derivative is n × k, and all matrices must be evaluated on the chosen solution branch. The theorem supplies the local branch; the chain rule supplies its derivative. Formal differentiation alone cannot replace the existence hypotheses. For one equation and one unknown, this reduces to dy/da = −F a /F y . For a square mapping G, apply the theorem to F(z, y) = G(y) − z. If J G is invertible at the point, G has a local differentiable inverse with derivative J G ⁻¹ there. Neither conclusion establishes a global inverse or a unique equilibrium across the entire domain. At F(a, y) = y² − a and (0, 0), F y = 0. For positive a there are two real branches, for negative a none, and the positive-branch slope 1/(2√a) grows without bound as a approaches zero. The theorem's failed condition is informative; it should not be bypassed by dividing by zero. Worked example 2 — An equilibrium response is a system response Let synthetic demand and supply satisfy q = 25 − p² and q = 2p + a, with p and q nonnegative and a a supply-shift parameter. At a = 1, the solution p = 4, q = 9 satisfies both equations. Write F₁ = q + p² − 25 and F₂ = q − 2p − a. With unknown order y = (p, q)ᵀ, F y = [[2p, 1], [−2, 1]], which at the solution is [[8, 1], [−2, 1]] with determinant 10. F a = (0, −1)ᵀ. Thus dy/da = −F y ⁻¹F a = (−0.1, 0.8)ᵀ. Increasing a by 0.1 predicts Δp ≈ −0.01 and Δq ≈ 0.08. Solving the branch explicitly gives p(a) = −1 + √(26 − a), q(a) = 2p(a) + a. At a = 1.1, p ≈ 3.98998998 and q ≈ 9.07997996. The small differences from the linear prediction reflect nonlinear curvature. Supply's partial response to a at fixed p is 1, while equilibrium quantity's total response is 0.8 because price also falls. The held-fixed and equilibrium responses are different quantities. The other algebraic root at a = 1 is p = −6, q = −11, outside the economic domain; local branch uniqueness from the theorem and domain-based elimination of another root are separate arguments. These comparative statics describe solved states of a specified model. They do not establish a dynamic adjustment path or a causal estimate of a real intervention. Near a singular unknown Jacobian, inferred responses may be unstable and branch structure may change. Exercises For f(x, y) = 3x² + 2xy + 4y² − 5x, compute its value, gradient and Hessian at (1, 2). Compute the derivative in unit direction (3/5, 4/5)ᵀ. For h = (0.1, −0.1)ᵀ, compare first- and second-order predicted changes with the exact change. For f(x, y) = xy/(x² + y²) away from zero and f(0, 0) = 0, compute both coordinate partials at zero. Evaluate the function along y = x and explain why it is neither continuous nor totally differentiable at zero. For production Y(K, L) = 2√(KL), with K, L > 0, compute both partial derivatives at (4, 9). Use the total differential to predict the output change for ΔK = 0.2, ΔL = −0.3 and compare with the exact new output. For G(x, y) = (xy, x² + y²)ᵀ, calculate the Jacobian at (2, 1). Is a local inverse justified? Find the first-order input change corresponding to desired output change (0.03, 0)ᵀ. Check the actual output change at that predicted input. For f(x, y) = x²y + eʸ, x(t) = 1 + t and y(t) = t², calculate d[f(x(t), y(t))]/dt at t = 1 using the chain rule. Check it by differentiating the fully substituted single-variable expression. A model has q = 16 − p² and q = p + a. At a = 4, verify the positive solution and use the implicit-function system to calculate dp/da and dq/da. Confirm the derivatives from its explicit positive-price branch. For f(x, y) = ln x + 2 ln y with positive inputs, compute the gradient at (2, 4). Change the first coordinate convention to x̃ = 100x and express the gradient in (x̃, y). Explain why a fixed physical input change has the same predicted output change even though the Euclidean steepest direction changes. For F(a, y) = y² − a, check the implicit-function theorem's unknown derivative at (a, y) = (4, 2) and at (0, 0). Find the positive-branch derivative where it exists. Explain why neither a finite slope at zero nor a single real branch across negative and positive a follows. Solutions and derivative checks Value is 18; gradient is (6x + 2y − 5, 2x + 8y)ᵀ = (5, 18)ᵀ, and Hessian is [[6, 2], [2, 8]]. Directional derivative is 5(3/5) + 18(4/5) = 87/5 = 17.4. Linear change is −1.3; quadratic correction is 3(0.1)² + 2(0.1)(−0.1) + 4(−0.1)² = 0.05. The exact change is −1.25 and the new value is 16.75. Both coordinate difference quotients are zero because the function vanishes on each axis. Along y = x ≠ 0, its value is 1/2, so approaching zero along that line does not give f(0, 0) = 0. Differentiability would imply continuity through the linear approximation and vanishing remainder, so it also fails. Y K = √(L/K) = 3/2 and Y L = √(K/L) = 2/3. Initial output is 12. Predicted change is (3/2)(0.2) + (2/3)(−0.3) = 0.1. Exact output is 2√(4.2 × 8.7) = 2√36.54 ≈ 12.089665, so the actual increase is about 0.089665. Both perturbed inputs remain positive. Jacobian is [[y, x], [2x, 2y]], giving [[1, 2], [4, 2]], determinant −6. G is continuously differentiable, so the inverse-function condition holds locally. Its inverse Jacobian is [[−1/3, 1/3], [2/3, −1/6]], giving h = (−0.01, 0.02)ᵀ. At (1.99, 1.02), outputs are (2.0298, 5.0005)ᵀ, changes (0.0298, 0.0005)ᵀ rather than exactly the desired values. The difference is second order. At t = 1, x = 2, y = 1, x′ = 1 and y′ = 2. Partials are f x = 4 and f y = 4 + e, giving 4 + 2(4 + e) = 12 + 2e. Substitution yields (1 + t)²t² + eᵗ²; its derivative is 2(1 + t)t² + 2t(1 + t)² + 2teᵗ², with the same value at 1. p = 3, q = 7 satisfies 16 − 9 = 3 + 4. Unknown Jacobian in (p, q) order is [[6, 1], [−1, 1]], determinant 7; parameter derivative is (0, −1)ᵀ. Multiplication gives (dp/da, dq/da) = (−1/7, 6/7). The positive branch is p(a) = [−1 + √(65 − 4a)]/2, q(a) = p(a) + a. Its derivatives at 4 are −1/7 and 1 − 1/7 = 6/7. Original gradient is (1/2, 1/2)ᵀ. In new coordinates f = ln(x̃/100) + 2 ln y, and the point is (200, 4), giving gradient (1/200, 1/2)ᵀ. Since dx̃ = 100dx, the first-coordinate contribution is (1/200)(100dx) = (1/2)dx, unchanged. Unit Euclidean movements in the two coordinate systems represent different physical perturbations, so the steepest-coordinate direction changes. F y = 2y is 4 at (4, 2), hence nonzero, and the local derivative is −(−1)/4 = 1/4. For a > 0 the positive branch y = √a has derivative 1/(2√a). At zero the unknown derivative is zero, the positive-branch slope is unbounded from the right, and negative a has no real solution. Positive a also permits the distinct negative branch; a global uniqueness claim would be false. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why partial derivatives need not produce a valid total approximation. Build a Jacobian with explicit input/output order, derive a chain rule, and distinguish a partial supply response from an equilibrium response. Delayed check: compute a fresh gradient and Hessian, check a mixed term in a quadratic expansion, and reconstruct an implicit-system response with its regularity conditions. Include a function whose axis behavior conceals its multivariable limit failure. Further study and scope MIT's Multivariable Calculus course supplies independent study of tangent approximations, gradients, directional derivatives and constrained differentials. The examples and exercise sets in this chapter are original. MIT's Calculus of Several Variables lecture on implicit functions provides a method-specific further-study route. The full local-existence theorem is stated and used here; formal differentiation is derived. Chapter 10 supplies the general local C¹ existence proof using contractions, uniform neighborhood bounds and parameter dependence. The next chapter uses these derivatives to justify optimization conditions. A stationary point, a feasible point and a global optimum will remain distinct until the required curvature and constraint arguments are supplied."}, {"title": "02.08 · Unconstrained and constrained optimization", "url": "v02-c08.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A candidate is not yet a best choice Setting derivatives to zero finds candidates under particular conditions. It does not by itself prove feasibility, existence, local optimality, global optimality or uniqueness. Constrained decisions add boundaries and trade-offs: the best choice can sit at a corner, a resource can remain unused, and a binding restriction can have zero marginal value. A complete optimization argument states the objective and domain, identifies feasible candidates, checks the conditions behind its method, and supplies a comparison or a curvature certificate. This chapter develops that workflow for smooth finite-dimensional problems. All allocations and benefits below are synthetic mathematical examples. Define the problem before choosing a method A maximization problem chooses x from a feasible set X to attain the highest f(x). A minimization chooses the lowest. A global optimum compares with every feasible alternative; a local optimum compares only with feasible alternatives in some neighborhood. A strict optimum requires a strict improvement over every different alternative in the comparison set. Write X explicitly, including signs, equality versus inequality, and whether quantities are continuous or discrete. An equality budget requires exact expenditure; an inequality permits saving resources. A stationary calculation on real coordinates cannot settle an integer-choice problem without an additional discrete comparison. Existence deserves its own argument. A continuous objective on a nonempty closed bounded subset of finite-dimensional Euclidean space attains a maximum and a minimum; this is the compact-set extreme value theorem, with compactness developed in Chapter 10. On an unbounded domain, a continuous coercive minimization objective—one tending to +∞ as ‖x‖₂ → ∞—also attains a minimum on a nonempty closed feasible set, because an appropriate sublevel set is closed and bounded. Neither bounded values nor a numerical solver's termination proves attainment. Maximizing 1 − e⁻ˣ over x ≥ 0 has supremum 1 but never reaches it. Maximizing x on ℝ has no finite upper bound. Conversely, constructing a feasible point and proving it dominates every other feasible point establishes existence without needing a separate compactness argument. Unconstrained first- and second-order conditions At a differentiable interior local optimum, ∇f(x*) = 0. Each coordinate can move in either direction, so its directional derivative must be both nonpositive and nonnegative. At a boundary or nondifferentiable point that argument does not apply as stated. For twice continuously differentiable f, an interior local minimum has positive semidefinite Hessian; a local maximum has negative semidefinite Hessian, meaning every directional quadratic form is nonpositive. A positive definite Hessian at a stationary point is sufficient for a strict local minimum; a negative definite Hessian is sufficient for a strict local maximum. The Taylor quadratic dominates the smaller-order remainder in a sufficiently small neighborhood. An indefinite Hessian gives a saddle: nearby directions increase and decrease the objective. A semidefinite or zero Hessian can be inconclusive. At zero, x⁴ has a strict minimum, −x⁴ a strict maximum, and x³ neither, despite identical zero first and second derivatives there. Higher-order behavior or a direct comparison settles such cases. Concavity gives a global argument A set is convex if every segment between two of its points lies in the set. A function f is concave on a convex domain if f(θx + (1 − θ)y) ≥ θf(x) + (1 − θ)f(y) for 0 ≤ θ ≤ 1. Convexity of a function reverses that inequality. Strict concavity requires a strict inequality for distinct points and 0 < θ < 1. For differentiable concave f, f(z) ≤ f(x) + ∇f(x)ᵀ(z − x). One justification restricts f to the segment: concavity makes its secant slope from x no greater than its derivative at x. A differentiable convex function instead lies above its tangent approximation. Consequently a zero gradient of a concave function is a global maximum on its domain; a zero gradient of a convex function is a global minimum. Strict concavity on a convex feasible set allows at most one maximizer: two distinct maximizers would have a midpoint with a strictly higher value. For a twice continuously differentiable function on an open convex domain, negative semidefinite Hessians everywhere imply concavity, since the second derivative along every segment is nonpositive. Negative definiteness everywhere is sufficient for strict concavity. A sign check at a single candidate provides a local result, not concavity over an entire domain. Nor is negative definiteness necessary for strict concavity; −x⁴ is strictly concave despite its zero second derivative at zero. Equality constraints and Lagrange multipliers For a smooth equality h(x) = 0, a regular point has ∇h(x*) ≠ 0. Feasible tangent directions satisfy ∇h(x*)ᵀd = 0. At a constrained local optimum, the objective's derivative vanishes along every such tangent direction. Its gradient therefore lies in the span of the constraint gradient: ∇f(x*) = λ∇h(x*). With several equality constraints, the Jacobian must have independent rows for this regularity argument. The implicit-function theorem then supplies local feasible paths for tangent directions. The stationarity equation expresses the objective gradient as a combination of constraint gradients. Equality multipliers are unrestricted in sign; their economic meaning depends on how each equation is written. For maximizing benefit B under a resource equality aᵀx = m, use ℒ = B(x) + λ(m − aᵀx). Stationarity is ∇B = λa. The multiplier equates marginal benefit per resource unit across interior activities. Multiplying a constraint equation by a constant rescales its multiplier, so its units and normalization must be reported. Worked example 1 — An equality allocation with a global certificate Maximize U(x, y) = ln x + 2 ln y over positive x and y subject to 2x + y = m, for m > 0. The objective is a specified benefit index, not a monetary welfare estimate. ℒ = ln x + 2 ln y + λ(m − 2x − y). Stationarity gives 1/x = 2λ and 2/y = λ. Hence x = 1/(2λ), y = 2/λ, and the resource equation gives 3/λ = m. Therefore λ = 3/m, x* = m/6 and y* = 2m/3. Both choices are positive and satisfy the equality. The Hessian of U is diagonal with entries −1/x² and −2/y², both negative. U is strictly concave on the positive domain and the feasible segment is convex. For any other feasible (x, y), the tangent bound at the candidate gives U(x, y) ≤ U(x*, y*) + λ[2(x − x*) + (y − y*)] = U(x*, y*). Strict concavity establishes uniqueness. This comparison supplies a global certificate even though the positive feasible segment excludes its endpoints and logarithms are undefined there. The candidate exists and dominates every permitted alternative; blindly applying a closed-set existence theorem to the open log domain would be unnecessary and invalid. At m = 12, choices are x = 2 and y = 8, λ = 0.25 benefit-index units per resource unit, and U = ln 128 ≈ 4.852030. The value function is V(m) = ln(m/6) + 2 ln(2m/3), so V′(m) = 3/m = λ. A resource increase of 0.6 predicts a benefit increase of 0.15; the exact increase is 3 ln(1.05) ≈ 0.146370. Inequality constraints and the KKT system Use one consistent convention. For minimizing f(x) subject to gᵢ(x) ≤ 0 and hⱼ(x) = 0, define L = f + Σᵢλᵢgᵢ + Σⱼνⱼhⱼ, with λᵢ ≥ 0 and unrestricted νⱼ. The Karush–Kuhn–Tucker, or KKT, conditions comprise four requirements: Primal feasibility: every gᵢ ≤ 0 and every hⱼ = 0. Dual feasibility: every inequality multiplier λᵢ ≥ 0. Complementary slackness: λᵢgᵢ = 0 for each inequality. Stationarity: ∇f + Σᵢλᵢ∇gᵢ + Σⱼνⱼ∇hⱼ = 0. An active inequality has gᵢ = 0; an inactive one has gᵢ < 0. Complementary slackness forces the multiplier of an inactive constraint to zero. A positive multiplier therefore implies an active constraint, but an active constraint can have a zero multiplier. Equality constraints are always imposed; they do not use inequality sign restrictions or complementary slackness. For maximization, minimize −B under the same constraints or equivalently use ℒ = B − Σᵢλᵢgᵢ − Σⱼνⱼhⱼ with λᵢ ≥ 0. Write the actual constraint functions before choosing signs. A memorized multiplier sign detached from minimization/maximization and ≤/≥ conventions is unreliable. When KKT is necessary and when it is sufficient For continuously differentiable constraints and objective near a local optimum, linear independence of all equality gradients and active inequality gradients is a sufficient constraint qualification for KKT necessity. It is called LICQ. This condition is sufficient rather than necessary for the theorem to work; other qualifications can apply, and a point can satisfy KKT even when LICQ fails. Without a qualification, a genuine optimum may have no KKT multipliers. Minimize f(x) = x subject to g(x) = x² ≤ 0. The only feasible point is zero, hence it is the optimum. But stationarity would require 1 + λ × 0 = 0, which is impossible. The active constraint's gradient is zero, so LICQ fails. For a convex minimization problem—convex f and gᵢ on a convex domain, affine equality constraints—any feasible KKT point is a global optimum. Proof: its Lagrangian is convex in x, so stationarity makes x* a global minimizer of L. Complementary slackness gives L(x*) = f(x*). For any feasible z, λ ≥ 0 and g(z) ≤ 0 give f(z) ≥ L(z) ≥ L(x*) = f(x*). This sufficiency proof does not require a constraint qualification. A qualification is needed when arguing an optimum must admit multipliers. For differentiable convex objectives and inequalities on an open convex domain, a point satisfying every inequality strictly and all affine equalities supplies the familiar Slater condition. If a finite optimal value is attained, this condition ensures that optimal points admit KKT multipliers. General statements use relative interiors and refined conditions, developed with duality in the next chapter. For a nonconvex problem, satisfying KKT generally identifies a candidate rather than a global optimum. Equality-constrained second-order tests inspect the Hessian of the Lagrangian on feasible tangent directions, not just the objective Hessian. Under equality-gradient independence, positive curvature on every nonzero tangent direction is sufficient for a strict local minimum; negative curvature gives the analogous maximum in a maximization formulation. Inequality versions need carefully defined critical directions, so the problems here use global convex certificates or direct feasible comparisons. Worked example 2 — Corners, a scarce resource and an unused resource Maximize B(x, y) = 8x + 6y − x² − y² subject to x ≥ 0, y ≥ 0 and x + y ≤ m, with m > 0. Express inequalities as x + y − m ≤ 0, −x ≤ 0 and −y ≤ 0. The maximizing Lagrangian is ℒ = B + λ(m − x − y) + μₓx + μᵧy, with all three multipliers nonnegative. Stationarity gives 8 − 2x − λ + μₓ = 0 and 6 − 2y − λ + μᵧ = 0. Complementarity gives λ(m − x − y) = 0, μₓx = 0 and μᵧy = 0. The objective Hessian is −2I, so it is strictly concave, and all constraints are affine. A feasible KKT point is the unique global maximum. If both activities are positive and resource is scarce, μₓ = μᵧ = 0 and x + y = m. The equations imply x − y = 1, giving x = (m + 1)/2, y = (m − 1)/2 and λ = 7 − m. This regime requires m > 1 for positive y and m < 7 for positive λ. For 0 < m ≤ 1, use x = m and y = 0. Stationarity gives λ = 8 − 2m, μₓ = 0 and μᵧ = 2 − 2m ≥ 0. The lower-bound multiplier accounts for why transferring scarce resource from x into y would not improve benefit. For m ≥ 7, the unconstrained benefit maximizer x = 4, y = 3 is feasible, and every multiplier is zero. Optimal regimes for the synthetic allocation Resource m x* y* λ Value V(m) 0 < m ≤ 1 m 0 8 − 2m 8m − m² 1 < m < 7 (m + 1)/2 (m − 1)/2 7 − m 7m − m²/2 + 1/2 m ≥ 7 4 3 0 25 At m = 0.5, the corner is (0.5, 0), value 3.75, λ = 7 and μᵧ = 1. At m = 4, choices are (2.5, 1.5), value 20.5 and λ = 3. At m = 8, choices remain (4, 3), value 25 and one resource unit is unused. At m = 7 the resource constraint is exactly binding but its multiplier is zero: equality of use and availability does not itself establish scarcity value. At m = 1 the entering activity is still at zero and its lower-bound multiplier is also zero. That is a regime transition, not an inconsistency. At m = 0 only (0, 0) is feasible; handle it directly as a parameter boundary rather than assuming the interior regularity of the m > 0 analysis. Envelope reasoning and shadow values A value function reports the optimized objective as a parameter changes. When a regular optimum and its multipliers vary differentiably within a fixed constraint regime, the envelope relation is V′(m) = ∂ℒ/∂m in the chosen maximizing convention. The derivative includes direct parameter effects in the objective and constraint functions; it does not generally equal the derivative of the objective holding everything else fixed. For the equality example, dU(x*(m), y*(m))/dm = ∇Uᵀ(x*′, y*′)ᵀ = λ(2x*′ + y*′) = λ because differentiated feasibility gives 2x*′ + y*′ = 1. The optimal-input responses enter the chain rule but cancel into the constraint's resource change. In the quadratic allocation, differentiating each value regime gives 8 − 2m, 7 − m or 0, matching λ. The value slope happens to agree across the two transitions, while the allocation slopes change. Other problems can have nondifferentiable values, multiple optima or nonunique multipliers; use appropriate one-sided responses or explicit comparisons rather than assuming a smooth envelope everywhere. A multiplier's units are objective units per constraint unit. Multiplying a benefit index by 10 multiplies its shadow value by 10 without changing the optimal choice. Rescaling a resource equation also rescales the corresponding multiplier. A modeled shadow value is a conditional local comparison, not a guaranteed market price or causal effect. A reliable solution procedure First establish the domain and feasibility. Then use derivatives to obtain candidates, including plausible active sets and boundary cases. Reject any candidate with a sign, domain or complementary-slackness violation. Supply a global concavity/convexity certificate when available; otherwise compare all relevant candidates and explain the scope of any local second-order test. Finally, verify the original equations and evaluate the objective. Report parameter ranges for each regime and how results change at transitions. A solver can help find candidates, but an optimization claim must expose which feasibility, curvature and regularity conditions support it. Integer restrictions, nonsmooth costs and uncertain objectives require methods beyond simply reusing these smooth formulas. Exercises Classify the stationary point at (0, 0) for x² + y², −x² − y², x² − y² and (x² + y²)². Give the Hessian and a direct comparison where the second-order test is inconclusive. Maximize B = 10x + 4y − x² − y² under nonnegativity and x + y ≤ m. Solve at m = 2 and at m = 5, including all KKT multipliers under the maximizing convention. Give a global uniqueness argument. Minimize f(x, y) = x² + 3y² subject to x + y = m, where x and y are unrestricted real variables. Solve at m = 8 using L = f + ν(x + y − m), prove global uniqueness, and compute the derivative of optimized cost with respect to m. Maximize ln x + ln y over positive inputs with x + 3y = 12. Solve the equality Lagrangian, prove global uniqueness and give the local value of an additional unit of the right-hand-side resource. Minimize (x − 2)² subject to 0 ≤ x ≤ 1. Write the constraints as ≤ 0 functions, solve for both inequality multipliers, and explain how removing the upper bound changes the solution. Minimize f(x, y) = x subject to x² + y² = 1. Find both equality-Lagrangian stationary points and multipliers, classify them using feasible tangent curvature, and verify their global values by a direct bound. Minimize x subject to x² ≤ 0. Identify the optimum, check LICQ and Slater feasibility, and show why no multiplier solves KKT stationarity. Explain why this does not contradict convex KKT sufficiency. In Worked example 2, resource rises from 0.9 to 1.1. Compute the exact optimized-value gain using the correct regimes, then compare with the local prediction at 0.9. Explain why the allocation's active set changes even though the value slope agrees at the transition. Solutions and optimality certificates The first has Hessian 2I and a unique global minimum; the second has −2I and a unique global maximum. The third has diagonal (2, −2) and a saddle, with opposing changes along the axes. The fourth has zero Hessian at zero, but (x² + y²)² > 0 at every nonzero point, proving a unique global minimum directly. All four gradients vanish there. Stationarity is 10 − 2x − λ + μₓ = 0 and 4 − 2y − λ + μᵧ = 0. At m = 2, x = 2, y = 0, λ = 6, μₓ = 0 and μᵧ = 2; benefit is 16. At m = 5, both activities are positive and x − y = 3, giving x = 4, y = 1, λ = 2 and both lower-bound multipliers zero; benefit is 27. Feasibility, multiplier signs and complementary slackness all hold. Hessian −2I and convex affine constraints establish global uniqueness. Stationarity gives 2x + ν = 0 and 6y + ν = 0, so x = 3y. With m = 8, x = 6, y = 2, ν = −12 and cost is 48. The objective is strictly convex and the feasible line is convex, so this feasible stationary point is the unique global minimum. Generally x = 3m/4, y = m/4 and V(m) = 3m²/4, so V′(8) = 12. Here L m = −ν; a negative equality multiplier in this normalization corresponds to a positive cost of increasing the required total. Use ℒ = ln x + ln y + λ(12 − x − 3y). Conditions 1/x = λ and 1/y = 3λ give x = 6, y = 2 and λ = 1/6. Value is ln 12. Strict concavity on the positive domain and the affine equality certify global uniqueness. The local optimized-value derivative with respect to the resource right-hand side is 1/6 in the specified benefit-index units. Use g₁ = −x and g₂ = x − 1. At x = 1, the lower bound is inactive, so λ₁ = 0. Stationarity is 2(1 − 2) − λ₁ + λ₂ = 0, giving λ₂ = 2. All conditions hold; convexity gives the unique global minimum with value 1. Without the upper bound, x = 2 is feasible and achieves zero with the remaining multiplier zero. L = x + ν(x² + y² − 1). Stationarity gives 1 + 2νx = 0 and 2νy = 0, so y = 0 and x = ±1. At (−1, 0), ν = 1/2 and H L = I, positive on the tangent directions (0, d); it is a strict local minimum. At (1, 0), ν = −1/2 and H L = −I, giving a maximum. Every feasible point has −1 ≤ x ≤ 1, so these are also the global extremes, with values −1 and 1. The objective Hessian alone is zero and misses the constrained curvature. Only x = 0 is feasible, hence optimal. The active gradient g′(0) = 0 is not independent, so LICQ fails; no real x has x² < 0, so Slater fails. Stationarity 1 + 2λx = 0 becomes 1 = 0 at zero. Convex KKT sufficiency states that a feasible KKT point is optimal; it does not assert that every optimum has multipliers without the conditions for necessity. V(0.9) = 8(0.9) − 0.9² = 6.39. V(1.1) = 7(1.1) − 1.1²/2 + 1/2 = 7.595, so the exact gain is 1.205. At 0.9, λ = 8 − 1.8 = 6.2 and the local prediction is 6.2 × 0.2 = 1.24, larger by 0.035. The second activity enters at m = 1 and its lower-bound multiplier reaches zero. The value derivatives from both regimes equal 6 there, although the optimal allocation's derivatives switch. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: distinguish a stationary candidate, a local optimum and a unique global optimum. Derive multiplier signs from the chosen Lagrangian convention, explain complementary slackness, and give an optimum at which KKT necessity fails because regularity is absent. Delayed check: solve a fresh allocation across a corner and interior regime, verify every KKT condition, and compute a value derivative with correctly normalized units. Diagnose a binding constraint with zero multiplier and a case in which the objective Hessian alone is inadequate. Further study and scope MIT's Lagrange Multipliers and Constrained Differentials materials offer further equality-constrained practice. The allocations and solutions above are original. Stanford's optimization review notes provide further study of constraint qualification and local optimality. The full inequality second-order theory is beyond the present examples and is not replaced by an unconstrained Hessian test. The authors' Convex Optimization duality lecture supplies deeper study of KKT, Slater conditions and sensitivity. The next chapter develops convex sets, separating arguments, dual bounds and comparative statics more systematically."}, {"title": "02.09 · Convexity, duality and comparative statics", "url": "v02-c09.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Two routes to a defensible optimum An optimization calculation can approach a decision from two sides. A feasible choice supplies an attainable objective. A dual calculation supplies a bound on what any feasible choice can achieve. When these agree, the decision is certified globally. When they differ, their difference measures the remaining uncertainty about optimality; it does not necessarily measure an algorithm's error. This chapter develops convex geometry, Lagrange duality and parameter comparisons using the derivatives and KKT conditions already established. Distinguish three questions: how the best objective changes, how the chosen quantities change, and whether the model's predictions describe a causal effect in the world. Mathematical sensitivity answers the first two conditionally on the model. All numerical decisions here are original synthetic examples. Convex geometry: combinations, domains and boundaries A convex combination of points x₁, …, xₖ is Σθᵢxᵢ with θᵢ ≥ 0 and Σθᵢ = 1. A set is convex when it contains every such combination of its points. The two-point segment definition implies this finite version by induction: combine the first k − 1 points after normalizing their total weight, then combine their average with the last point. Zero weights can be omitted. The convex hull of a set contains all its finite convex combinations. Intersections of convex sets are convex: a segment between two points in the intersection belongs to every component set. Affine maps preserve convex combinations, so their images and inverse images preserve convexity. Consequently affine equalities, halfspaces and their intersections give convex feasible regions. Nonnegative quantities with an upper resource budget form a convex region. Requiring each quantity to be an integer usually destroys this property. A function f on a convex domain D is convex when f(θx + (1 − θ)y) ≤ θf(x) + (1 − θ)f(y). Its epigraph is the set of pairs (x, t) with x ∈ D and t ≥ f(x). The epigraph is convex exactly when f is convex: apply the definition to two points above the graph, and conversely use the graph heights as the two t coordinates. A convex function's sublevel set {x ∈ D: f(x) ≤ c} is convex by the same inequality. Its superlevel sets need not be convex. Nonnegative sums and pointwise maxima of convex functions are convex. For a maximum, each component's value at the combination is bounded by the same combination of the two maximum values. Composition needs conditions: a convex nondecreasing outer function composed with a convex inner function is convex, by applying monotonicity and then convexity. A nonlinear equality f(x) = c is generally not a convex constraint, even when f itself is convex. For example, a circle is the boundary of a convex disk but is not a convex set. The affine hull of D is the smallest affine set containing it. The relative interior, written relint D, consists of points with a neighborhood inside D when neighborhoods are measured within that affine hull. A line segment in the plane has empty ordinary interior but a nonempty relative interior consisting of the segment without its endpoints. Ordinary interior is therefore the wrong test for a domain intrinsically confined to a lower-dimensional affine space. Separation: a price vector can certify exclusion A hyperplane has equation aᵀx = b for nonzero a; a halfspace has aᵀx ≤ b. The vector a assigns linear weights to coordinates. A separation argument finds weights that place a point or set on one side and the feasible set on the other. In economics these weights can resemble prices, but their economic interpretation still depends on the model. Here is a useful result with a proof. If C is a nonempty closed convex subset of finite-dimensional Euclidean space and z lies outside C, choose y ∈ C minimizing ‖z − y‖₂. Such a point exists: restrict the search to the closed bounded intersection with a ball about z large enough to contain an initial candidate; points outside that ball cannot improve its distance. The compact-set existence result used in Chapter 8 applies. Its full analysis foundation is supplied in Chapter 10. For any x ∈ C, the segment y + t(x − y), 0 ≤ t ≤ 1, is feasible. Its squared distance to z is minimized at t = 0, so its right derivative there is nonnegative: −2(z − y)ᵀ(x − y) ≥ 0. Set a = z − y ≠ 0. Then aᵀx ≤ aᵀy for every x ∈ C, while aᵀz = aᵀy + ‖a‖₂² > aᵀy. The hyperplane with b = aᵀy strictly excludes z and supports C at y. Closedness and the type of separation matter. If z is only a boundary point of an open set, positive distance and this strict exclusion proof may fail. More general supporting-hyperplane results are standard tools of convex analysis; this proof establishes the stated point-versus-closed-set result, not every version of separation. Construct a dual problem with one sign convention Consider the primal minimization p* = inf f(x), with x ∈ D, inequalities gᵢ(x) ≤ 0 and equalities hⱼ(x) = 0. The symbol inf allows a best lower value even when no choice attains it. Use L(x, λ, ν) = f(x) + Σλᵢgᵢ(x) + Σνⱼhⱼ(x), with λᵢ ≥ 0 and unrestricted equality multipliers νⱼ. Define the dual function d(λ, ν) = inf over x ∈ D of L(x, λ, ν). The dual problem maximizes d over the permitted multipliers. The infimum defining d removes the explicit g and h restrictions. Restrictions retained in D remain in the minimization. Forgetting which restrictions were relaxed changes the dual function. A finite stationary point of L is enough to compute its infimum only when a global argument makes it a minimizer. Otherwise d can equal −∞ despite the existence of stationary points. Weak duality: for every primal-feasible x and permitted multipliers, d(λ, ν) ≤ L(x, λ, ν) ≤ f(x). The second inequality follows from nonnegative λ times nonpositive g and from h = 0. Thus the largest dual lower bound d* satisfies d* ≤ p*. No convexity assumption was used. Maximization requires the corresponding reversed-bound convention or conversion to minimization of the negative objective. The dual function is concave in its multipliers even if the primal problem is nonconvex. For each fixed x, L is affine in (λ, ν). At a combination of two multiplier vectors, every L value is at least the same combination of their separate infima. Taking the infimum again proves the concavity inequality. Some multipliers yield −∞ and are uninformative; the effective dual domain contains those with finite d. If a feasible choice x and a finite dual value d are available, 0 ≤ f(x) − p* ≤ f(x) − d. Their difference is an optimality certificate in objective units. Equality certifies that x is optimal and that the multipliers are dual optimal. A small gap provides a bound on objective error, not automatically on the distance between quantities. With additional strong curvature, a distance bound can be derived, as an exercise below shows. Worked example 1 — A target with an upper capacity constraint Minimize f(x) = (x − 3)² for a real-valued x with x ≤ b. The target and the capacity use the same quantity units; the loss is in squared quantity units. L = (x − 3)² + λ(x − b), λ ≥ 0. Completing the square gives L = (x − 3 + λ/2)² + λ(3 − b) − λ²/4. Therefore d(λ; b) = λ(3 − b) − λ²/4, attained over x at 3 − λ/2. The dual maximizer is λ* = 2 max(3 − b, 0). Direct primal comparison gives x* = min(3, b) and p(b) = [max(3 − b, 0)]². Substitution gives d(λ*; b) = p(b) for every real b. When b < 3, the restriction binds and λ* is positive. When b > 3 it is slack. At b = 3 it binds with zero multiplier, so binding does not imply a positive shadow value. At b = 1, x* = 1, p = 4 and λ* = 4. The unoptimized but valid multiplier λ = 3 gives d = 3.75. A feasible candidate x = 0.8 gives loss 4.84; the resulting certificate bounds its excess loss by 4.84 − 3.75 = 1.09. Using λ* sharpens that bound to 0.84, which is its actual excess loss. Each bound concerns the original constrained problem. Increase b from 1 to 1.2. Exact optimized loss falls from 4 to 3.24. The initial derivative p′(1) = −4 predicts a change of −0.8, while the actual change is −0.76. The initial dual multiplier gives the global lower bound p(1.2) ≥ 4 − 4(0.2) = 3.2. This inequality remains valid beyond an infinitesimal change; claiming the linear prediction is exact would be incorrect. Strong duality, Slater and saddle certificates Strong duality means d* = p*. It does not by itself say that either optimum is attained. A useful finite-dimensional Slater theorem is the following: let f and all gᵢ be convex on their common nonempty convex domain D, with affine equalities Ax = b. If some x̄ ∈ relint D satisfies Ax̄ = b and gᵢ(x̄) < 0 for every inequality, and the primal value is finite, then strong duality holds and a dual optimum is attained. This is a sufficient qualification; it is not a necessary condition in every problem. The theorem also admits refinements for affine inequalities, which are not needed for our applications. When D is open and full dimensional, relative interior reduces to ordinary interior. For a lower-dimensional D, work in affine coordinates on its hull. Slater's domain requirement concerns D, not the interior of the equality-feasible region in the full ambient space. In the target example, D = ℝ and x̄ = b − 1 is strictly feasible for any b, including b = 3. The geometry behind the theorem considers attainable upper bounds on constraint violations and objective value. Convexity makes this upper-bound region convex: combine two choices and use the convex-function inequalities. A supporting hyperplane at the best feasible value yields coefficients interpretable as multipliers. Strict feasibility prevents the objective coefficient from vanishing, allowing normalization into a dual certificate. This explains the theorem's role; a proof for general domains requires a general separation theorem beyond the point-separation result above. We use Slater as an explicitly stated theorem, rather than presenting this geometric explanation as its full proof. For differentiable convex f and g on an open convex domain, affine h, a feasible stationary KKT tuple supplies an elementary certificate independently of Slater. The Lagrangian is convex in x, so ∇ₓL = 0 makes x* its global minimizer. Complementary slackness then gives d(λ*, ν*) = L(x*, λ*, ν*) = f(x*). Slater helps guarantee multipliers exist; it is unnecessary for checking a certificate already in hand. At a retained domain boundary, replace unconstrained stationarity by actual minimization over D or an appropriate boundary condition. A primal-dual optimum with equality of values has a saddle property: L(x*, λ, ν) ≤ L(x*, λ*, ν*) ≤ L(x, λ*, ν*) for all x ∈ D and all permitted multipliers. Feasibility establishes the first side; minimizing L establishes the second. Conversely this property forces feasibility, since a violated inequality or equality could make the left side arbitrarily large. It also establishes global primal optimality by comparison with any feasible x. Qualification failures can produce several different outcomes. In min x subject to x² ≤ 0, only x = 0 is feasible, so p* = 0. For λ > 0, minimizing x + λx² gives d(λ) = −1/(4λ); λ = 0 gives −∞. The dual supremum is zero but is never attained at finite λ. Strong duality holds without an optimal multiplier; KKT stationarity fails. Failure of Slater therefore does not logically imply a positive duality gap. A positive gap is possible outside convexity. Minimize x with x ∈ D = {0, 1} and 1/2 − x ≤ 0. Only x = 1 is feasible, so p* = 1. Retaining the discrete domain, the dual function is d(λ) = min(λ/2, 1 − λ/2) for λ ≥ 0. Its maximum is 1/2 at λ = 1. Both optima are attained, but the gap is 1/2. This is a property of the relaxation, not a calculation error. Replacing D by its convex hull [0, 1] changes the primal problem and allows x = 1/2, exactly attaining that lower bound. Value functions and global sensitivity bounds Relax constraints to gᵢ(x) ≤ uᵢ and hⱼ(x) = vⱼ, holding the objective and D fixed. Let p(u, v) be the optimized cost. The dual function for this perturbation is d(λ, ν) − λᵀu − νᵀv. If the original problem has strong duality and optimal multipliers, weak duality at the new parameters gives p(u, v) ≥ p(0, 0) − λ*ᵀu − ν*ᵀv. It is a global supporting bound, even if the new problem's optimum or multipliers change. For convex f and g and affine h, p is convex in these right-hand-side parameters wherever finite. To prove this without assuming attainment, take choices whose costs lie within ε of each parameter problem's infimum. Their convex combination is feasible at the combined parameters and its cost is at most the combined costs. Let ε decrease to zero. Infeasible parameters can be assigned value +∞. When only an inequality is relaxed, the feasible set expands, so its minimum cost cannot rise. A subgradient s of a convex function p at w satisfies p(z) ≥ p(w) + sᵀ(z − w) throughout its domain. The multiplier bound therefore identifies (−λ*, −ν*) as a subgradient at the original parameters. If p is differentiable at an interior parameter point, applying the bound to positive and negative small changes forces ∇p = (−λ*, −ν*). Without differentiability, use supporting bounds and one-sided slopes rather than assign a single derivative. For example, minimize x over x ≥ 0 and x ≥ −u. With g₁ = −x and g₂ = −x − u, the value is p(u) = max(0, −u). At u = 0, stationarity requires λ₁ + λ₂ = 1, with both nonnegative. All such pairs are optimal multipliers; slopes −λ₂ fill the subgradient interval [−1, 0]. The left derivative is −1 and the right derivative is 0. Nonunique multipliers are compatible with a unique primal choice x = 0 and a kinked value function. Quantity responses need a separate argument For an interior scalar maximum of a smooth benefit π(q, θ), the first-order equation is π q (q*, θ) = 0. If π qq ≠ 0 and the local branch is regular, the implicit-function formula gives dq*/dθ = −π qθ /π qq . Under strict negative curvature, the sign matches the cross partial. With vector quantities the response is −H⁻¹ times the parameter-gradient vector. Negative definite curvature does not make every entry of that vector response positive; interactions among quantities matter. There is also a global comparison requiring no differentiability. Let π(q, θ) = θq − C(q) on a feasible set independent of θ. Take global optimal choices q₁ and q₂ at θ₁ < θ₂. Their two optimality inequalities are θ₁q₁ − C(q₁) ≥ θ₁q₂ − C(q₂) and θ₂q₂ − C(q₂) ≥ θ₂q₁ − C(q₁). Add them to obtain (θ₂ − θ₁)(q₂ − q₁) ≥ 0. Thus q₂ ≥ q₁. This result permits corners, nondifferentiable costs and multiple optima; existence is still required. A changing feasible set or additional θ-dependent costs invalidates this particular cancellation. Worked example 2 — A payoff changes before capacity does Maximize π(q, θ) = θq − q² under 0 ≤ q ≤ k, with fixed k > 0. The parameter θ is a modeled unit payoff; its units make θq commensurate with q². Completing the square or using strict concavity gives q*(θ) = 0 for θ ≤ 0, θ/2 for 0 < θ < 2k, and k for θ ≥ 2k. The value W(θ) is 0, θ²/4, or θk − k² across these three regimes. Its derivative is q*(θ), including both transition points where the adjacent derivatives agree. Inside the positive unconstrained regime, dq*/dθ = 1/2. At capacity it is zero. At θ = 0 and θ = 2k, the quantity's two one-sided slopes differ, so its ordinary derivative does not exist. A differentiable value need not imply a differentiable policy. At θ = 8 and k = 3, choose q = 3 and earn value 15. Under the maximizing Lagrangian ℒ = θq − q² + αq + β(k − q), the lower-bound multiplier is α = 0 and the capacity multiplier is β = θ − 2k = 2. Increasing θ to 9 leaves quantity at 3 and raises value to 18. The derivative of value with respect to θ is 3; the derivative with respect to k at the original regular capacity-constrained point is 2. These are different sensitivities to different parameters. Keeping θ = 8 and raising k to 3.2 raises quantity to 3.2 and value to 15.36. The initial capacity shadow value predicts a gain of 0.4; the exact gain is 0.36. Increasing k beyond 4 adds no value: the unconstrained choice is q = 4 with value 16. Neither the local price sensitivity nor the local capacity sensitivity should be extrapolated through an unchecked regime change. Exercises Determine whether each positive-coordinate region is convex: x + y ≥ 2; xy ≥ 1; xy ≤ 1. Give a proof or two feasible points whose midpoint fails. Explain why convexity of an expression alone does not settle both inequality directions. For the closed disk C = {(x, y): x² + y² ≤ 4} and outside point z = (3, 4), find its nearest feasible point and a strictly separating supporting line. Verify exclusion numerically. Minimize (x − 5)² subject to x ≤ 2. Derive the dual function, both optima and the exact gap from feasible x = 1.5 and dual multiplier λ = 5. Explain the meaning of the gap. Minimize x² + 2y² for unrestricted real x, y with x + y = m. Derive the equality dual, the unique primal solution and the value at m = 6. Find the cost derivative and relate its sign to your equality-multiplier convention. For Worked example 1, move b from 2.5 to 3.5. Give the actual cost change, initial linear prediction and dual supporting bound. Explain what changes at b = 3. For p(u) = max(0, −u), prove directly that every s ∈ [−1, 0] is a subgradient at zero. Find the optimal multipliers at u = −2 and u = 2, and explain why an ordinary derivative cannot be assigned at zero. In Worked example 2 set k = 2. Solve at θ = −1, 3 and 6, including both bound multipliers. Give the value derivative and quantity derivative in each regime, and use the global comparison argument to explain monotonicity. Suppose differentiable f is μ-strongly convex on an open convex domain: f(y) ≥ f(x) + ∇f(x)ᵀ(y − x) + (μ/2)‖y − x‖₂² for μ > 0. An optimum x* on a convex feasible set satisfies ∇f(x*)ᵀ(x − x*) ≥ 0 for feasible x. If a feasible x and dual lower bound d give gap ε = f(x) − d, derive a bound on ‖x − x*‖₂. Check it for f(x) = (x − 3)², capacity b = 1, x = 0.8 and d = 3.75. Distinguish this bound from the actual distance. Solutions and certificates The first is a halfspace intersected with the positive domain, hence convex. For the second, y ≥ 1/x with x > 0; the function 1/x has positive second derivative 2/x³, so its epigraph is convex. The third is not convex: (1, 1) and (3, 1/3) satisfy xy ≤ 1, but their midpoint (2, 2/3) has product 4/3 > 1. Reversing a convex-function sublevel inequality can destroy convexity. The radius is 2 and ‖z‖₂ = 5. The nearest point is y = (6/5, 8/5), distance 3: the triangle inequality gives distance at least 5 − 2 and this radial choice attains it. The supporting line can be normalized as 3x + 4y = 10. Every point in C satisfies 3x + 4y ≤ 5‖(x, y)‖₂ ≤ 10 by Cauchy–Schwarz, while at z the left side is 25. This is strict exclusion. L = (x − 5)² + λ(x − 2). Completing the square yields d(λ) = 3λ − λ²/4 for λ ≥ 0. Its unique maximum is λ* = 6 with d* = 9, attained in the Lagrangian at x = 2. Direct comparison certifies primal x* = 2, p* = 9. At λ = 5 the bound is 15 − 25/4 = 35/4 = 8.75. Feasible x = 1.5 has loss 49/4 = 12.25; the gap is 7/2 = 3.5. Its actual excess loss is 13/4 = 3.25, no greater than the certificate. Use L = x² + 2y² + ν(x + y − m). Unrestricted minimization gives x = −ν/2, y = −ν/4 and d(ν; m) = −3ν²/8 − νm. The dual maximum is ν* = −4m/3. Thus x* = 2m/3, y* = m/3 and p(m) = 2m²/3; strict convexity gives uniqueness. At m = 6 the choice is (4, 2), ν* = −8 and cost is 24, equal to the dual value. Cost derivative p′(6) = 8 equals −ν*, because the constraint is x + y − m = 0. Initial cost is 1/4, final cost zero; actual change is −1/4. The initial multiplier is 1, so the initial derivative predicts change −1. The global bound is p(3.5) ≥ 1/4 − 1 = −3/4, which is valid but weak; nonnegative squared loss gives the sharper bound zero. At b = 3, the bound remains binding with zero multiplier and the choice switches from capacity to target. Its slope changes from 1 to 0, while the cost slope joins continuously at zero. For u ≥ 0, p(u) = 0 ≥ su because s ≤ 0. For u < 0, p(u) = −u ≥ su because s ≥ −1 and multiplication by negative u reverses the comparison. At u = −2, x* = 2: the first lower bound is slack, so λ₁ = 0 and λ₂ = 1. At u = 2, x* = 0: the second bound is slack, so λ₂ = 0 and λ₁ = 1. At zero both bind and λ₂ can be anywhere in [0, 1]. Unequal one-sided slopes rule out an ordinary derivative. Stationarity is θ − 2q + α − β = 0. At θ = −1, q = 0, α = 1, β = 0, W = 0, W′ = 0 and q′ = 0. At θ = 3, q = 3/2, α = β = 0, W = 9/4, W′ = 3/2 and q′ = 1/2. At θ = 6, q = 2, α = 0, β = 2, W = 8, W′ = 2 and q′ = 0. The feasible interval stays fixed and the payoff is θq − C(q) with C(q) = q²; adding the two global optimality inequalities proves choices cannot decrease as θ rises. Strong convexity and the stated feasible directional condition give f(x) − f(x*) ≥ (μ/2)‖x − x*‖₂². Weak duality gives f(x) − f(x*) ≤ ε. Hence ‖x − x*‖₂ ≤ √(2ε/μ). Here μ = 2, ε = 4.84 − 3.75 = 1.09, and the bound is √1.09 ≈ 1.044. The actual distance is 0.2. The bound is conservative because the supporting first-order term is positive and the chosen dual bound is below the optimum. Without positive strong curvature an objective-gap certificate alone supplies no such general distance bound. Teach-back and Delayed check Explain to a colleague why a feasible decision and a dual bound can certify a result without trusting the solver. Include the sign convention, the retained domain, and the difference between a shadow-value bound and a quantity response. Then explain a true optimum with no attained multiplier and a unique optimum whose value has a kink. After several days, change the target and capacity in Worked example 1 and reconstruct both primal and dual without its formulas. Reverse the role of the changing parameter in Worked example 2. State the exact domain and regularity assumptions for every derivative you use. Scope and further study This chapter establishes elementary convex geometry, complete dual calculations, certificates and sensitivity reasoning. General convex separation, nonsmooth optimality, monotone comparative statics on lattices and market decentralization receive deeper treatment in graduate theory. Slater is a sufficient theorem used with its hypotheses, not a claim that every optimization problem is convex or has multipliers. Stanford's authors' convex-optimization slides provide further study of convex sets, epigraphs and supporting geometry. The library's proofs and examples are original. The Stanford duality lecture is a method reference for Slater's relative-domain qualification and perturbation bounds. Match its multiplier convention to the one displayed here before using a formula."}, {"title": "02.10 · Real analysis and convergence", "url": "v02-c10.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "When an approximation has a legitimate destination A sequence of computed quantities is useful only when its limit exists in the intended domain and represents the problem we meant to solve. Small successive changes alone do not prove convergence. Boundedness alone does not imply that a best choice is attained. Convergence at each input separately does not justify moving a limit through an integral or derivative. This chapter supplies proofs and error bounds behind those distinctions. We work mainly in finite-dimensional Euclidean space, then introduce complete metric spaces for fixed-point iteration. The final two proofs close the existence arguments deferred in matrix geometry and multivariable calculus . Economic applications use these results to justify equilibria, optimal choices and numerical approximations; the mathematical conditions still require checking in each model. Completeness and least upper bounds An upper bound for S ⊂ ℝ is a number at least as large as every member of S. A supremum sup S is its least upper bound. An infimum is the greatest lower bound. A maximum is a member of S attaining its supremum; a supremum need not belong to S. For example, (0, 1) has supremum 1 and no maximum. The completeness property of ℝ says every nonempty set bounded above has a real supremum. We take this as a defining property of the real-number system, rather than pretend to deduce it from algebra alone. It implies that a bounded nondecreasing sequence converges: if L is the supremum of its terms, for every ε > 0 some term exceeds L − ε and all later terms lie between that term and L. The decreasing case follows by changing signs. The rational numbers have the usual arithmetic and order but lack this completeness. There is no rational number whose square is 2: if a reduced fraction p/q had that square, p² = 2q² would force p even; substitution then forces q even, contradicting reduction. Rational lower approximations to √2 can approach one another arbitrarily closely while having no rational limit. The distinction is about the domain, not the accuracy of the approximations. Subsequences and bounded-sequence extraction A subsequence x nₖ uses strictly increasing indices n₁ < n₂ < … . If a sequence converges to x, every subsequence converges to x, since its indices eventually exceed any convergence threshold. The reverse implication from just one convergent subsequence is false: (−1)ⁿ has constant positive and negative subsequences but does not converge. Bolzano–Weierstrass in ℝ: every bounded sequence has a convergent subsequence. Put its terms in a closed interval. Bisect the interval and retain a half containing infinitely many terms; repeat, choosing closed halves. The nested intervals have lengths decreasing to zero. Their left endpoints increase to a supremum L; nesting ensures every right endpoint is at least L, so L lies in every interval. Select a term in the kth interval with index greater than the previous selected index. Its distance from L is at most that interval's length, proving convergence. For a bounded sequence in ℝᵈ, first extract convergence of coordinate 1, then a subsequence of that subsequence for coordinate 2, continuing through the finite number of coordinates. The final subsequence converges coordinatewise and therefore in Euclidean norm: the sum of the d squared coordinate errors tends to zero. Finitely many coordinates matter; the same argument alone does not establish compactness in an infinite-dimensional function space. Cauchy sequences and complete spaces A sequence is Cauchy if for every ε > 0 there is N such that ‖xₙ − xₘ‖ < ε whenever m, n ≥ N. Unlike the limit definition, this compares terms without naming a proposed destination. Every convergent sequence is Cauchy by bounding each term's distance to the limit by ε/2. Conversely every Cauchy sequence in ℝᵈ converges. It is bounded: beyond one threshold its terms lie within distance 1 of a particular term, and finitely many earlier terms enlarge the bound only finitely. Bolzano–Weierstrass gives a subsequence converging to x. Given ε, choose a Cauchy threshold with pairwise distance below ε/2, then a subsequence term beyond that threshold within ε/2 of x. Every sufficiently late term is within ε of x by the triangle inequality. A metric d(x, y) is a nonnegative distance, zero exactly when x = y, symmetric and satisfying the triangle inequality. A metric space is complete if every Cauchy sequence converges to a point of that space. Euclidean space is complete by the proof above. An open set contains a ball of positive radius about each of its points; a closed set has an open complement. Closedness is equivalent to containing every limit of a convergent sequence of its points. If a purported limit were in the open complement, a small complementary ball would exclude all sufficiently late terms. Conversely, if the complement is not open at an outside point, each ball of radius 1/n meets the set, supplying a sequence converging to that excluded point. A closed subset of Euclidean space is complete: a Cauchy sequence has an ambient limit, and closedness keeps that limit in the subset. An open interval need not be complete; 1/n lies in (0, 1) for n ≥ 2 but its limit does not. The condition ‖xₙ₊₁ − xₙ‖ → 0 is weaker than Cauchy convergence. Harmonic partial sums have increments 1/(n + 1) tending to zero, but the increase from n to 2n is at least n/(2n) = 1/2. Arbitrarily late terms therefore remain separated by at least 1/2. A reliable stopping rule needs a proved link between the observable change and total remaining error. Compactness in finite dimensions A set K is compact if every collection of open sets covering K has a finite subcollection covering K. In ℝᵈ, this is equivalent to being closed and bounded, and to every sequence in K having a subsequence converging to a point of K. We establish the equivalence rather than use it as an unexplained existence slogan. Closed and bounded implies the sequence property by Bolzano–Weierstrass and closedness. The sequence property implies boundedness, because otherwise choices with norm greater than n would have no convergent subsequence. It implies closedness, because a sequence from K approaching a missing boundary point would have that same limit for every convergent subsequence. To obtain the open-cover property from closedness and boundedness, enclose K in a closed box and suppose some open cover has no finite subcover. Divide the box into finitely many equal smaller boxes. At least one intersection with K still has no finite subcover, or their finite subcovers could be combined. Keep such a box and repeat. The boxes are nested and their diameters tend to zero. Coordinate completeness gives a common limiting point z; choose a point of K in each box to see that those points approach z and hence z ∈ K. One open member of the cover contains a ball about z. Eventually the entire selected box lies in that ball, so its intersection with K is covered by that single member, a contradiction. Finally the open-cover property implies the sequence property. If a sequence had no convergent subsequence to a point of K, every z ∈ K would have a neighborhood containing only finitely many sequence indices; otherwise progressively smaller neighborhoods could supply a subsequence converging to z. These neighborhoods cover K. A finite subcover would contain only finitely many indices in total, contradicting that all infinitely many terms lie in K. This completes the finite-dimensional equivalences. Complete does not mean compact: ℝ is complete but unbounded. Nor does bounded mean compact in all spaces. Infinite-dimensional problems require additional theorems and the correct topology; a finite-dimensional closed-and-bounded argument must not be silently exported to them. Attained extrema and uniform continuity A continuous real function on nonempty compact K is bounded. Otherwise choose points with |f(xₙ)| > n; a convergent subsequence in K would, by continuity, have finite limiting function values, a contradiction. Let M be the supremum of its values and choose xₙ with f(xₙ) > M − 1/n. A convergent subsequence approaches x* ∈ K, and continuity gives f(x*) = M. Apply this argument to −f for a minimum. This proves the extreme value theorem used earlier. Continuity is uniform on compact K: for every ε > 0 a single δ > 0 works for all pairs of points in K. If not, there would be some ε and pairs xₙ, yₙ with ‖xₙ − yₙ‖ < 1/n but |f(xₙ) − f(yₙ)| ≥ ε. Extract a subsequence xₙₖ → x; the corresponding yₙₖ also approaches x, contradicting continuity. The common δ is why a sufficiently fine mesh can control approximation across the entire compact domain. Complete proof: real symmetric spectral decomposition Let A be a real symmetric d × d matrix. The unit sphere is nonempty, closed and bounded when d ≥ 1, so the continuous function R(u) = uᵀAu attains a maximum on it at a unit vector u. For any v perpendicular to u, consider the unit path w(t) = (u + tv)/‖u + tv‖₂. Its derivative at zero is v. At the maximum, differentiating R(w(t)) gives 2vᵀAu = 0, using symmetry of A. Thus Au is perpendicular to every vector perpendicular to u, so Au = λu. Multiplication by uᵀ gives λ = uᵀAu, a real number. This constructs a first real eigenvector without assuming a polynomial's roots already supply one. The orthogonal complement u⊥ is invariant under A: if v ⊥ u, then uᵀAv = (Au)ᵀv = λuᵀv = 0. Choose an orthonormal basis of this (d − 1)-dimensional space using the finite Gram–Schmidt procedure. The matrix of the restricted map in that basis is symmetric, since vᵀAw = (Av)ᵀw. Apply induction on dimension to this smaller symmetric map. Dimension 1 is immediate; adding u to the complement's orthonormal eigenbasis produces an orthonormal eigenbasis for A. Putting those eigenvectors in columns of Q gives QᵀQ = I and A = QΛQᵀ with real diagonal Λ. Repeated eigenvalues are allowed; one may choose different orthonormal bases within their eigenspaces. For any x, xᵀAx = Σλᵢ(Qᵀx)ᵢ². Consequently positive semidefiniteness is equivalent to all λᵢ ≥ 0, and positive definiteness to all λᵢ > 0. This proves the existence and sign characterization used in Chapter 6 for real symmetric matrices. Pointwise and uniform convergence of functions Pointwise convergence fₙ(x) → f(x) permits the threshold N to depend on x: for each x and each ε, sufficiently late n have |fₙ(x) − f(x)| < ε. Uniform convergence on D requires one N for all x ∈ D. Equivalently the supremum error ‖fₙ − f‖∞ = sup over x ∈ D of |fₙ(x) − f(x)| tends to zero. A supremum can be used even when no input attains it. A uniform limit of continuous functions is continuous. At a point x₀, choose one fₙ uniformly within ε/3 of f. Continuity of that fixed fₙ gives a neighborhood where |fₙ(x) − fₙ(x₀)| < ε/3. The two approximation errors and this middle difference sum to less than ε. Choosing n after x instead would fail to provide the common approximation needed for this argument. On a finite closed interval [a, b], uniform convergence of continuous fₙ permits integration of the limit: |∫fₙ − ∫f| ≤ (b − a)‖fₙ − f‖∞ → 0. The limit is continuous and hence Riemann integrable. This bound does not by itself apply to an infinite interval. Pointwise convergence alone is insufficient, as concentrated spikes in the exercises show. Uniform convergence of the functions alone also does not justify differentiating the limit. On [0, 1], fₙ(x) = sin(nx)/n converges uniformly to zero, yet fₙ′(0) = 1 for every n, while the limit's derivative is zero. A useful sufficient result is stronger: if each fₙ is continuously differentiable, fₙ(x₀) converges at one common point, and fₙ′ converges uniformly to g, then the fundamental theorem gives fₙ(x) = fₙ(x₀) + ∫ from x₀ to x of fₙ′. The uniform integral bound shows fₙ converges uniformly to f(x) = c + ∫g, and the continuous uniform limit g gives f′ = g. For a function series Σaₜ(x), suppose |aₜ(x)| ≤ Mₜ for every x and the nonnegative numerical series ΣMₜ converges. Tails of the function series are uniformly bounded by corresponding numerical tails, making the partial sums uniformly Cauchy. Completeness of ℝ supplies the pointwise limits, and the same tail bound proves uniform convergence. This is the M-test. It allows termwise integration on finite intervals when the terms are continuous; termwise differentiation needs its own derivative-series conditions. Worked example 1 — A geometric approximation on an entire input range For 0 ≤ x ≤ 2, let Sₙ(x) = Σ from t = 0 to n of (x/3)ᵗ. The geometric identity gives S(x) = 1/(1 − x/3) and error S(x) − Sₙ(x) = (x/3)ⁿ⁺¹/(1 − x/3). Its maximum is at x = 2, since numerator and reciprocal denominator are nondecreasing and nonnegative. Thus the exact supremum error is 3(2/3)ⁿ⁺¹. To obtain uniform function error below 10⁻⁴, n = 25 suffices: 3(2/3)²⁶ ≈ 0.0000792043. The previous degree n = 24 has error approximately 0.000118806, which exceeds the target. Selecting an input-specific tail without maximizing over the entire range could understate the required degree. Termwise integration gives ∫ from 0 to 2 of Sₙ = Σ from t = 0 to n of 2ᵗ⁺¹/[3ᵗ(t + 1)]. The limit integral is −3 ln(1 − 2/3) = 3 ln 3 ≈ 3.295836866. At n = 25 its error is at most twice the function-error bound, approximately 0.000158409. That certificate is valid but does not establish integral error below 10⁻⁴. To guarantee that tighter integral tolerance with this bound, n = 27 suffices. This approximation is mathematical. Interpreting its terms as discounted payoffs additionally requires a model connecting t, x and the discount factor; uniform convergence alone supplies no evidence for that economic interpretation. Contraction mappings: existence and an error certificate Let X be a nonempty complete metric space and T: X → X satisfy d(Tx, Ty) ≤ k d(x, y) for all x, y ∈ X, with 0 ≤ k < 1. Starting at any x₀, iterate xₙ₊₁ = T(xₙ). Then d(xₙ₊₁, xₙ) ≤ kⁿd(x₁, x₀). For m > n, summing consecutive distances gives d(xₘ, xₙ) ≤ [kⁿ/(1 − k)]d(x₁, x₀). This tends to zero, so completeness gives a limit x* ∈ X. The contraction inequality makes T continuous; taking limits in xₙ₊₁ = T(xₙ) yields x* = T(x*). If y* were another fixed point, its distance from x* would be at most k times itself, forcing equality of the points. This proves the contraction mapping theorem, including convergence from every starting point in X. The same inequalities give an a priori bound d(xₙ, x*) ≤ [kⁿ/(1 − k)]d(x₁, x₀). For n ≥ 1, a bound using the latest step is d(xₙ, x*) ≤ [k/(1 − k)]d(xₙ, xₙ₋₁). More generally any x ∈ X satisfies the residual bound d(x, x*) ≤ d(x, T(x))/(1 − k), by comparing x to T(x) and then T(x) to T(x*). A numerical tolerance can therefore be connected to a proved total error. Every hypothesis matters. T(x) = x/2 maps (0, 1) into itself and contracts distances but has no fixed point there because the space is incomplete. T(x) = x + 1 maps complete ℝ into itself with Lipschitz constant 1 and has no fixed point. Checking a derivative only near one candidate is not a contraction guarantee across an asserted global domain. In a differentiable scalar map on an interval, a uniform bound |T′| ≤ k < 1 supplies the distance inequality by the mean value theorem; mapping the interval into itself needs a separate check. Contractions also control perturbations. If S has a fixed point y*, T has fixed point x*, and d(Tx, Sx) ≤ δ on their common domain, then d(x*, y*) ≤ k d(x*, y*) + δ, so d(x*, y*) ≤ δ/(1 − k). This requires the uniform discrepancy bound and a shared domain; it does not compare arbitrary models merely because their coefficients look close. Worked example 2 — A nonlinear equilibrium with certified iteration Consider the synthetic equilibrium q = 1 + (2/5)q/(1 + q). Use T(q) equal to its right-hand side on X = [1, 7/5]. This closed interval is complete. T is increasing and its endpoint images are 6/5 and 37/30, both in X. Its derivative is (2/5)/(1 + q)² ≤ 1/10 throughout X. The theorem gives one fixed point in X and convergence from any starting q in X. With q₀ = 1, q₁ = 1.2, q₂ ≈ 1.218181818, q₅ ≈ 1.219803034 and q₆ ≈ 1.219803832. The a priori bound after n iterations is (2/9)(1/10)ⁿ. At n = 5 it is approximately 2.2222 × 10⁻⁶; at n = 6 it is approximately 2.2222 × 10⁻⁷, certifying error below 10⁻⁶ by the sixth iterate. Independently multiply the equilibrium by 1 + q to obtain q² − (2/5)q − 1 = 0. The root in X is q* = [(2/5) + √(104/25)]/2 ≈ 1.219803903. The sixth iterate's actual error is approximately 7.05 × 10⁻⁸, smaller than the guarantee. The other root is negative and lies outside the stated equilibrium domain. Now replace the constant 1 by 1.001, keeping X. The new map also sends X into itself and has the same derivative bound. The uniform map discrepancy is δ = 0.001, so the fixed-point change is at most 0.001/(0.9) ≈ 0.00111111. This is a mathematical stability bound under a specified perturbation, not an estimate of a real policy effect. Completeness for uniform function approximation For any nonempty domain D, bounded real functions have the norm ‖f‖∞ = sup |f(x)|. A sequence Cauchy in this norm is Cauchy at each point and hence has a pointwise real limit f. For a uniform Cauchy threshold, take the limit in |fₙ(x) − fₘ(x)| ≤ ε as m grows to obtain a bound holding for every x; using ε/2 in the initial choice gives the desired strict ε uniform error. One bounded fₙ plus this bound makes f bounded. Thus bounded functions form a complete normed space. If D carries a metric and the functions are continuous, their uniform limit is continuous by the earlier proof. The bounded continuous functions therefore form a complete space as well. This supplies the setting for many functional fixed-point arguments. It does not assert that every operator on that space maps it into itself or contracts its norm; those checks remain necessary, including in the later introduction to Bellman equations. Complete proof: local implicit-function existence Let F: U → ℝᵐ be continuously differentiable on open U ⊂ ℝᵖ × ℝᵐ. Write a for the parameter and y for the unknown. Suppose F(a₀, y₀) = 0 and the square unknown Jacobian J = F y (a₀, y₀) is invertible. We will prove that nearby parameters have a unique nearby solution y(a), that this solution is continuously differentiable, and that Dy(a) = −[F y (a, y(a))]⁻¹F a (a, y(a)). 1. Turn roots into fixed points on a complete ball The Euclidean operator norm of a matrix M is sup of ‖Mv‖₂ over unit v. It gives ‖Mv‖₂ ≤ ‖M‖‖v‖₂ and ‖MN‖ ≤ ‖M‖‖N‖. These follow by normalizing a nonzero vector and then applying the bound twice. All matrix estimates in this proof use this norm. Set B = J⁻¹ and Tₐ(y) = y − BF(a, y). Its fixed points are exactly the roots of F(a, ·), since B is invertible. At the base point, the y derivative of T is I − BJ = 0. Continuity of F y lets us choose small closed parameter and unknown balls whose product lies in U and on which ‖I − BF y ‖ ≤ k = 1/2 in Euclidean operator norm. The unknown ball has some radius r > 0 about y₀. For points y, z in that ball, the vector fundamental theorem along their segment gives Tₐ(y) − Tₐ(z) = ∫ from 0 to 1 of [I − BF y (a, z + t(y − z))](y − z) dt. Thus its norm is at most k‖y − z‖. Shrink the parameter neighborhood so continuity of F(a, y₀) ensures ‖Tₐ(y₀) − y₀‖ < (1 − k)r/2. Then for ‖y − y₀‖ ≤ r, ‖Tₐ(y) − y₀‖ ≤ kr + (1 − k)r/2 < r. Each Tₐ is therefore a contraction mapping this same closed, complete unknown ball into itself. The theorem produces exactly one root there. The fixed-point inequality also gives ‖y(a) − y₀‖ < r/2, so the roots lie in the interior. At a = a₀ the root is y₀. This establishes local existence and uniqueness, not merely a formal derivative formula. 2. Control parameter dependence before differentiating On the chosen compact product, continuity bounds the operator norm of F a by some finite M. For parameters a and b in the smaller convex neighborhood, integrating along their segment yields ‖Tₐ(y) − Tᵦ(y)‖ ≤ ‖B‖M‖a − b‖ uniformly over the unknown ball. Comparing the two roots gives ‖y(a) − y(b)‖ ≤ k‖y(a) − y(b)‖ + ‖B‖M‖a − b‖. Hence the branch is Lipschitz, with constant ‖B‖M/(1 − k), and in particular continuous. 3. Preserve invertibility and derive the derivative For a nearby pair (a, y), let E = I − BF y (a, y). Since ‖E‖ ≤ k < 1, the matrix series Σ from j = 0 to infinity of Eʲ converges, with tails bounded by kᴺ⁺¹/(1 − k). Its finite partial sums multiply I − E to give I − Eᴺ⁺¹; taking limits shows the series is the inverse of I − E = BF y . Thus F y is invertible and its inverse equals (ΣEʲ)B. Uniform geometric tails make this inverse continuous in the nearby matrix entries. Fix an interior parameter a and write Δy = y(a + h) − y(a). Continuous differentiability of F gives 0 = F(a + h, y(a) + Δy) − F(a, y(a)) = F a h + F y Δy + o(‖h‖ + ‖Δy‖), with both Jacobians evaluated at (a, y(a)). The proved Lipschitz bound gives ‖Δy‖ = O(‖h‖), turning the remainder into o(‖h‖). Multiplication by the inverse yields Δy = −F y ⁻¹F a h + o(‖h‖), which is total differentiability with the stated derivative. The derivative is continuous because F a , F y , the inverse operation and y(a) are continuous. We have proved the full local C¹ branch theorem. It allows other distant roots, says nothing about global continuation through a singular Jacobian, and does not prove numerical stability merely from a nonzero determinant. Apply it to F(a, y) = G(y) − a for a square C¹ map G with invertible base derivative. If A is the open parameter neighborhood and Bᵣ the open unknown ball, V = Bᵣ ∩ G⁻¹(A) is open, and existence plus local uniqueness makes G: V → A a bijection. Its C¹ inverse is the branch y(a), with derivative DG(y)⁻¹. This establishes the local inverse theorem as well. Exercises Let rₙ = floor(10ⁿ√2)/10ⁿ, where floor rounds down to an integer. Prove this rational sequence is Cauchy. Explain why it has a limit in ℝ but none in ℚ, and identify which definition of completeness fails in ℚ. For harmonic partial sums Hₙ = Σ from j = 1 to n of 1/j, show the successive increments tend to zero while H₂ₙ − Hₙ ≥ 1/2. Use a fixed ε to prove the sequence is not Cauchy. Decide whether (0, 1], ℝ, and the closed unit disk in ℝ² are complete and compact in Euclidean distance. Give a sequence or an open-cover obstruction when a property fails. Explain what the extreme value theorem would require for a real objective on each set. For fₙ(x) = xⁿ on [0, 1], identify the pointwise limit and prove convergence is not uniform. On [0, ρ] for fixed 0 < ρ < 1, give the exact supremum error and prove uniform convergence. For n ≥ 2, let hₙ(x) = n max(1 − |nx − 1|, 0) on [0, 1]. Prove pointwise convergence to zero, calculate each integral, and determine the supremum norm. Explain why moving the limit through the integral fails here. Iterate T(q) = 2 + q/4 on [2, 3] starting at q₀ = 2. Verify the contraction hypotheses, solve the fixed point independently, and find the smallest n for which the displayed a priori bound guarantees error below 10⁻⁴. Give an exact expression for the actual error. For A with rows (3, 1, 0), (1, 3, 0), (0, 0, 2), construct an orthonormal eigenbasis and spectral decomposition. Find the extreme Rayleigh values and explain how the invariant-complement step works despite a repeated eigenvalue. Let F(a, y₁, y₂) = (y₁ + y₂ − a, y₁² + y₂ − 2). At a = 2 and (y₁, y₂) = (1, 1), verify the implicit-function hypotheses and compute the response derivative. Obtain the explicit nearby branch and its value at a = 2.1. Identify another root at a = 2 and the parameter where the two branches collide. Explain the scope of local uniqueness. Solutions and proof details 0 ≤ √2 − rₙ < 10⁻ⁿ. For m, n ≥ N, |rₙ − rₘ| ≤ |rₙ − √2| + |rₘ − √2| < 2 × 10⁻ᴺ. Choose N with that bound below ε to prove Cauchy convergence. In ℝ it converges to √2 by its direct error bound. Uniqueness of limits and irrationality of √2 rule out a rational limit. Thus a Cauchy sequence in ℚ can lack a limit in that space, although it has a perfectly well-defined real limit. Hₙ₊₁ − Hₙ = 1/(n + 1) → 0. The n terms from n + 1 through 2n are each at least 1/(2n), giving H₂ₙ − Hₙ ≥ 1/2. With ε = 1/4, every proposed Cauchy threshold is contradicted by choosing n beyond it and comparing n with 2n. Small local increments have not bounded the accumulated tail. (0, 1] is incomplete: 1/n is Cauchy and tends to excluded zero. It is not compact; those same terms have no subsequence limiting in the set, or the open sets (1/j, 2), j ≥ 2, cover it with no finite subcover. ℝ is complete but not compact; the open intervals (−j, j) cover it without a finite subcover. The closed unit disk is closed and bounded, hence complete and compact. A continuous real objective is guaranteed to attain extremes on the disk. On the other two sets, continuity alone is insufficient; an additional model-specific existence argument is needed. Particular objectives there can still attain extrema. The limit is zero for x < 1 and one for x = 1. At xₙ = (1/2)¹⁄ⁿ < 1, fₙ(xₙ) = 1/2 but the limiting function is zero. A single uniform threshold cannot bound errors below, for example, 1/4. Equivalently the limit is discontinuous and cannot be a uniform limit of the continuous fₙ. On [0, ρ], the limit is zero everywhere and the supremum error is ρⁿ → 0, so convergence is uniform. At zero hₙ = 0. For each fixed x > 0, eventually nx > 2 and hₙ = 0. Its graph is a continuous triangle supported on [0, 2/n] with peak n at 1/n, so its integral is (1/2)(2/n)n = 1 and its supremum norm is n. The integral of the pointwise limit is zero, different from the constant integral sequence. Uniform convergence fails; the finite-interval uniform integral theorem's hypotheses are not met. T sends [2, 3] into [5/2, 11/4], a subset of the same complete interval; its Lipschitz constant is 1/4. The unique fixed point solves 3q/4 = 2, giving q* = 8/3. Since the first step is 1/2, the bound is (2/3)4⁻ⁿ. At n = 6 it is approximately 0.000162760, too large; at n = 7 it is approximately 0.0000406901, below the target. Directly qₙ − q* = (q₀ − q*)4⁻ⁿ, so the actual error equals that bound in this affine example. Use u₁ = (1, 1, 0)/√2 with eigenvalue 4, u₂ = (1, −1, 0)/√2 with eigenvalue 2, and u₃ = (0, 0, 1) with eigenvalue 2. They are orthonormal; Q formed from them gives A = Q diag(4, 2, 2)Qᵀ. For unit x, the quadratic value is 4c₁² + 2c₂² + 2c₃² with Σcᵢ² = 1, hence ranges from 2 to 4. The complement of u₁ is spanned by u₂ and u₃, is invariant under A, and the restriction acts as 2 times the identity. The repeated eigenvalue permits any orthonormal basis of this complement. F = 0 at the specified base point. F y has rows (1, 1) and (2, 1), determinant −1, and F a = (−1, 0)ᵀ. Solving F y y′ = (1, 0)ᵀ gives y₁′ = −1 and y₂′ = 2. Substitution y₂ = a − y₁ gives y₁² − y₁ + a − 2 = 0. The branch through (1, 1) is y₁(a) = [1 + √(9 − 4a)]/2, y₂(a) = a − y₁(a). At 2.1 it is approximately (0.887298335, 1.212701665). At a = 2 the other root is (0, 2), outside a sufficiently small neighborhood of (1, 1). At a = 9/4 the branches meet at (1/2, 7/4), where determinant 1 − 2y₁ is zero. For larger a no real roots exist. Local uniqueness near the regular base point neither excludes the distant branch nor extends through this singular collision. Teach-back and Delayed check Explain why completeness, compactness and uniform convergence answer different questions. Prove one attained-maximum result and derive a contraction stopping rule from the triangle inequality. Then reconstruct the two long proofs: why symmetry leaves an invariant complement, and why implicit differentiation must follow existence and parameter-continuity arguments. After several days, rebuild a nested-interval subsequence proof, a spike counterexample and a nonlinear contraction with changed coefficients. Check its domain before iterating. For an unfamiliar implicit system, identify the invertible Jacobian block and say precisely which roots the local theorem compares. Sources and extensions MIT's completeness lecture provides an independent proof-oriented route for Cauchy sequences and series. MIT's bounded-sequence lecture provides further study of subsequences and Bolzano–Weierstrass. MIT's uniform-convergence lecture provides further practice with common error bounds and the M-test. Jiří Lebl's author-hosted metric-space chapter extends completeness and compactness beyond Euclidean examples. Lebl's author-hosted fixed-point section is further reading for contractions and functional applications. Lebl's inverse/implicit-function section gives an independent theorem route. The proof here constructs the parameterized implicit branch directly; all exercises are original. Measure-theoretic limit theorems, weak topologies and infinite-dimensional optimization require additional instruction. The chapter does not infer those results from its finite-interval Riemann arguments or finite-dimensional compactness proofs."}, {"title": "02.11 · Difference and differential equations", "url": "v02-c11.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A steady state is a candidate path, not a forecast A stock can accumulate, decay, oscillate or explode even when its governing rule has a steady state. To analyze a dynamic model, specify the time convention, initial condition, inputs and admissible state domain. Then solve the evolution equation and distinguish a trajectory from the equilibrium around which it moves. A difference equation links observations at discrete dates, usually t = 0, 1, 2, … . An ordinary differential equation, or ODE, specifies an instantaneous time derivative. The same coefficient has different meanings in these settings: a per-period retention factor is dimensionless, while a continuous decay rate has units of inverse time. We develop both before introducing numerical approximations to them. First-order affine recurrences Consider xₜ₊₁ = axₜ + b, where a and b are constants and x₀ is given. Repeated substitution gives xₜ = aᵗx₀ + bΣ from j = 0 to t − 1 of aʲ. For a ≠ 1, the geometric identity gives xₜ = aᵗx₀ + b(1 − aᵗ)/(1 − a). A steady state x* satisfies x* = ax* + b, so x* = b/(1 − a), and the solution becomes xₜ = x* + aᵗ(x₀ − x*). Check the initial value and substitute this expression into the original rule. At a = 1 the solution is xₜ = x₀ + tb. If b ≠ 0 there is no steady state; if b = 0 every state is constant. Division by 1 − a would conceal this exceptional case. At a = 0 all initial deviations disappear in one update. For a ≠ 1, deviations decay when |a| < 1, grow when |a| > 1, and alternate signs when a < 0. At a = −1, deviations oscillate without decaying. A negative coefficient can describe corrective adjustment around a target; it need not be a valid retention factor for a physical stock. Check whether trajectories remain in the economic model's admissible domain. Time-varying inputs xₜ₊₁ = axₜ + bₜ give xₜ = aᵗx₀ + Σ from j = 0 to t − 1 of aᵗ⁻¹⁻ʲbⱼ. An added input δ at date j first changes xⱼ₊₁ by δ and changes xₜ for t ≥ j + 1 by aᵗ⁻¹⁻ʲδ. This is an impulse response conditional on a fixed rule. It is not automatically an empirical causal estimate. Define stability precisely An equilibrium is stable in the Lyapunov sense if for every tolerance ε > 0, a sufficiently small initial deviation δ > 0 keeps the entire forward path within ε. It is asymptotically stable if it is stable and all sufficiently nearby paths converge to it. Global asymptotic stability adds convergence from every admissible initial state, with trajectories defined for all forward dates. An equilibrium can be stable without being attracting. The affine scalar model with |a| < 1 is globally asymptotically stable on ℝ because |xₜ − x*| ≤ |x₀ − x*| and the geometric factor tends to zero. At a = −1 it is stable but not asymptotically stable: initial error never grows, but generally never vanishes. When |a| > 1, an arbitrarily small nonzero initial error eventually exceeds a fixed tolerance. Existence of x* has not established stability. Worked example 1 — Retention and a constant inflow A synthetic stock follows xₜ₊₁ = (3/4)xₜ + 6, starting at x₀ = 8. The coefficient is the fraction retained per period and 6 is an inflow in stock units per update. Nonnegative starting stocks remain nonnegative. The steady state is x* = 6/(1 − 3/4) = 24 and the exact trajectory is xₜ = 24 − 16(3/4)ᵗ. The first four updates produce 12, 15, 17.25 and 18.9375. Starting below the target, the stock rises monotonically because its positive deviation multiplier is less than one. A different starting stock above 24 would fall monotonically toward it. The same equilibrium does not imply identical short-run paths. To be within 0.1 unit of the steady state, require 16(3/4)ᵗ < 0.1. Taking logarithms and reversing the inequality when dividing by the negative ln(3/4) gives t > ln(0.1/16)/ln(3/4) ≈ 17.6416. The first integer date meeting the strict tolerance is t = 18. A new one-time inflow of 2 at date 3 instead changes the stock first at date 4 by 2 and at date 7 by 2(3/4)³ = 0.84375. The long-run stock response to a permanent inflow change db is dx*/db = 1/(1 − a) = 4. Its units are stock units per inflow-per-update unit. It depends on the specified retention rule remaining fixed. Linear systems, repeated eigenvalues and transient growth For xₜ₊₁ = Axₜ + b, a steady state solves (I − A)x* = b. An invertible I − A gives one steady state; a singular matrix can give none or many, depending on b. Deviations zₜ = xₜ − x* satisfy zₜ = Aᵗz₀, where the superscript t is a power, not a transpose. We write transpose as ᵀ. When A = PΛP⁻¹ is diagonalizable, zₜ = PΛᵗP⁻¹z₀. Each eigen-coordinate is multiplied by its eigenvalue each period. Complex conjugate eigenvalues re^{±iω} yield real oscillating components with envelope rᵗ. Here i² = −1, and e^{iω} = cos ω + i sin ω. Modulus r controls decay; angle ω controls rotation per update. The general finite-dimensional linear stability criterion is that all eigenvalues have modulus below one for asymptotic stability. Repeated eigenvalues require more than simply counting eigenvectors. A generalized chain obeys (A − λI)v₁ = 0 and (A − λI)v₂ = v₁; it creates polynomial factors in t as well as λᵗ. In standard Jordan-block coordinates, a block λI + N with nilpotent N has powers Σ from j = 0 to m − 1 of binomial(t, j)λᵗ⁻ʲNʲ, with terms j > t omitted. For nonzero |λ| < 1 the polynomial factors still decay with the geometric term; a zero-eigenvalue block vanishes after finitely many powers. A unit-modulus eigenvalue needs special care. The matrix with rows (1, 1) and (0, 1) has powers with rows (1, t) and (0, 1): an initial second-coordinate deviation causes unbounded first-coordinate drift. By contrast, a diagonal unit-modulus block can remain bounded without converging. For a linear discrete system, stability requires eigenvalues of modulus at most one and no nontrivial generalized chains at unit-modulus eigenvalues. The Jordan reduction used to express the general criterion is a standard linear-algebra theorem; the explicit cases here can be checked directly without computing a general reduction. Asymptotic stability does not require every measured deviation to shrink at each date. For A with rows (1/2, 4) and (0, 1/2), start z₀ = (0, 1). Then z₁ = (4, 1/2), much larger in Euclidean norm, but zₜ = (4t(1/2)ᵗ⁻¹, (1/2)ᵗ) tends to zero. This nonnormal matrix has AAᵀ ≠ AᵀA, so orthogonal eigen-coordinate intuition is unavailable. Temporary amplification and eventual decay are compatible; both matter for short-run constraints. Nonlinear recurrences and the scope of linearization For xₜ₊₁ = G(xₜ), a fixed point satisfies G(x*) = x*. If G is continuously differentiable, G(x* + z) − x* = G′(x*)z + o(|z|). In one dimension, |G′(x*)| < 1 proves local asymptotic stability: continuity of the derivative supplies a small interval with |G′| ≤ k < 1, and the mean value theorem gives |G(x) − x*| ≤ k|x − x*|. The interval maps into itself and the geometric bound applies. If |G′(x*)| > 1, choose a nearby interval on which the distance from the fixed point is expanded by a factor c > 1. The derivative definition or mean value theorem supplies this lower bound. Every small nonzero deviation eventually leaves that interval, proving instability. At |G′| = 1 the test is inconclusive. Near zero, G(x) = x − x³ makes absolute deviations strictly smaller for 0 < |x| < 1; they decrease to zero. G(x) = x + x³ expands every nonzero deviation and is unstable. Both have derivative 1 at zero. For vector recurrences, the Jacobian replaces the scalar derivative. A uniform nearby operator-norm bound below one directly provides the same contraction argument. More general local linearization results use the spectrum: all eigenvalues strictly inside the unit circle give local asymptotic stability, and an eigenvalue outside gives instability. Unit-circle cases require further nonlinear analysis. These are local results for C¹ rules in a neighborhood; a derivative at one equilibrium does not settle a distant initial condition or a constrained boundary model. ODEs and initial-value problems An initial-value problem specifies x′(t) = f(t, x(t)) and x(t₀) = x₀. A solution is a differentiable path satisfying both, on a stated interval. An autonomous equation has no explicit t in f. Its equilibria satisfy f(x*) = 0, rather than G(x*) = x*. Write the units of x′ as state units per unit time. Continuity of the right-hand side alone need not give uniqueness. For x′ = 3|x|^{2/3}, x(0) = 0, the zero path is a solution. So is the path that stays zero through any chosen c ≥ 0 and then follows (t − c)³. Both sides of the equation and the derivative agree at the joining time. The right-hand side is not locally Lipschitz at zero. A local existence-and-uniqueness argument uses the contraction theorem from Chapter 10. Suppose f is continuous on a closed time-and-state neighborhood of (t₀, x₀), bounded there in norm by M, and uniformly Lipschitz in the state with constant L. Choose a closed time interval of half-length h within that neighborhood and a state radius r such that Mh ≤ r and hL < 1. Continuous paths on the interval staying in the closed radius-r state ball form a nonempty closed subset of a complete uniform-norm space. Define the path operator (Pu)(t) = x₀ + ∫ from t₀ to t of f(s, u(s)) ds. The bound Mh keeps Pu in the state ball, and ‖Pu − Pv‖∞ ≤ hL‖u − v‖∞ makes it a contraction. Its unique fixed path solves the integral equation; continuity and the fundamental theorem make that path differentiable with derivative f(t, x(t)). Any sufficiently local solution staying in this ball is a fixed point, so uniqueness follows. Continuous state derivatives of f on a slightly larger neighborhood are a sufficient way to obtain the local Lipschitz bound. Local existence is not global existence. The smooth equation x′ = x² with x(0) = 2 has solution x(t) = 2/(1 − 2t), which becomes unbounded at t = 1/2. A theorem guaranteeing a short path cannot be reported as a forever-defined solution. Global boundedness or appropriate growth conditions require their own argument. Solving scalar differential equations For x′ = ax + b with constant a ≠ 0, the equilibrium is x* = −b/a and x(t) = x* + e^{a(t − t₀)}(x₀ − x*). Negative a gives exponential decay and positive a gives growth. At a = 0 the path is x₀ + b(t − t₀). Unlike the discrete multiplier criterion |a| < 1, the continuous rate criterion is a < 0. A time-varying linear equation x′ + p(t)x = b(t), with continuous coefficients, uses the integrating factor μ(t) = exp(∫ from t₀ to t of p(s) ds). The product rule gives (μx)′ = μb. Integrating yields x(t) = μ(t)⁻¹[x₀ + ∫ from t₀ to t of μ(s)b(s) ds]. This formula handles the initial condition and supplies a direct verification. It is not restricted to constant coefficients. For a separable autonomous equation x′ = g(x), on a path region where g(x) ≠ 0, integrate dx/g(x) = dt to obtain an implicit path. Check the domain and restore equilibrium solutions lost by division by g. For logistic adjustment x′ = r x(1 − x/K), with r, K > 0 and positive x₀, separation gives x(t) = K/[1 + (K/x₀ − 1)e^{-r(t − t₀)}]. The constant zero path is an additional solution; the constant K path is included when x₀ = K. Positive paths approach K, while zero is unstable against small positive perturbations. A phase line records the sign of g between its zeros. Positive g moves x to the right and negative g to the left. In a scalar C¹ equation, g′(x*) < 0 gives local attraction and stability; g′(x*) > 0 gives repulsion. The proof uses the sign of g(x)/(x − x*) near the root to bound the squared deviation's derivative. When the derivative is zero, higher-order terms or direct solution are needed. Matrix exponentials and continuous stability For a constant matrix A, define e^{At} = Σ from j = 0 to infinity of Aʲtʲ/j!, with the j = 0 term I. On any bounded time interval the terms are bounded by (‖A‖|t|)ʲ/j!, a convergent exponential series. The derivative series has a corresponding uniform bound. The uniform-convergence results of Chapter 10 therefore justify termwise differentiation, giving (e^{At})′ = Ae^{At} and e^{A0} = I. Thus z(t) = e^{A(t − t₀)}z₀ solves z′ = Az. For a diagonalizable matrix A = PΛP⁻¹, this gives e^{At} = P diag(e^{λᵢt})P⁻¹. Generalized eigenvector blocks can contribute powers of t multiplying the exponentials. All eigenvalues with strictly negative real parts imply asymptotic stability; any with positive real part imply instability. Imaginary-axis eigenvalues can give bounded persistent oscillations or polynomial growth, depending on their generalized chains. In linear systems, bounded stability requires nonpositive real parts and no nontrivial chains for eigenvalues with zero real part. For a real 2 × 2 matrix, eigenvalues solve λ² − Tλ + D = 0, where T is the trace and D the determinant. D < 0 gives opposing real signs, a saddle. D > 0 and T < 0 give a sink; D > 0 and T > 0 give a source. A negative discriminant T² − 4D produces a conjugate pair and spiral behavior; a positive one gives real directions. Zero determinant or zero real parts need separate boundary analysis. A forcing path z′ = Az + b(t) has solution z(t) = e^{A(t − t₀)}z₀ + ∫ from t₀ to t of e^{A(t − s)}b(s) ds. Differentiating verifies the original equation and the initial value. This is the continuous analogue of the discrete weighted-input sum. Products e^{(A+B)t} = e^{At}e^{Bt} require commuting A and B; they cannot be assumed for arbitrary matrices. Worked example 2 — A decaying coupled oscillation Let z denote two scaled deviations from a steady state, with z′ = Az, A having rows (−1, 2) and (−1/2, −1), and z(0) = (2, 0). The deviations may be negative even when the underlying levels are positive. The trace is −2, determinant 2, and eigenvalues are −1 ± i: the steady state is an asymptotically stable spiral. Write A = −I + Ω, where Ω has rows (0, 2) and (−1/2, 0). Since Ω² = −I, the even and odd terms of its exponential series give e^{Ωt} = I cos t + Ω sin t. The commuting decomposition yields e^{At} = e⁻ᵗ(I cos t + Ω sin t). Hence z(t) = (2e⁻ᵗ cos t, −e⁻ᵗ sin t). At t = π/2, z = (0, −e^{-π/2}) ≈ (0, −0.207879576). At t = π, z = (−2e⁻π, 0), showing a sign reversal before decay. Direct differentiation checks both equations. The weighted squared deviation E = z₁² + 4z₂² equals 4e⁻²ᵗ and satisfies E′ = −2E; this supplies an exact decay certificate rather than relying only on a plot. The oscillation period is 2π in the chosen time units and the envelope halves after ln 2. Rescaling the time unit rescales both rates and angular frequency. The coefficients describe a specified synthetic adjustment mechanism, without asserting that any actual market follows it. Nonlinear ODE linearization and numerical time steps For a C¹ autonomous system x′ = f(x), near f(x*) = 0 write z′ = Jz + o(‖z‖), with J = Df(x*). The standard local linearization stability theorem says strictly negative real parts of all eigenvalues imply local asymptotic stability; an eigenvalue with positive real part implies instability. A hyperbolic equilibrium has no eigenvalue with zero real part. Nonhyperbolic cases are not settled by the linearization alone. Full proofs of the general nonlinear-system theorem require additional dynamical-systems tools; the scalar proof and explicit systems here do not pretend to establish that general theorem. For instance, x′ = −x³ and x′ = x³ both linearize to x′ = 0 at zero. The first attracts from both sides; the second repels and has finite-time blow-up for nonzero initial values. A vanishing linear term has discarded the very term that determines the behavior. The analogous discrete boundary is an eigenvalue on the unit circle, not an eigenvalue with zero real part. An Euler approximation to x′ = −a x, a > 0, uses xₙ₊₁ = (1 − ah)xₙ for time step h > 0. Its discrete path decays only if |1 − ah| < 1, or 0 < h < 2/a. At h = 2/a it oscillates without decay; at larger h it explodes. The exact sampled solution instead has multiplier e^{-ah}, always between zero and one. A numerically unstable path can therefore be a property of the algorithm rather than of the differential equation. Stability of an algorithm still does not certify accuracy at the chosen step. The next chapter connects discretization, residuals, conditioning and tolerances. Keep separate the economic adjustment rule, its continuous-time idealization and the numerical method used to calculate it. Exercises Solve xₜ₊₁ = 10 − xₜ/4 with x₀ = 4. Give the steady state, the first three updates, the exact initial-deviation path and the response at date t to an initial stock increase of one. Classify stability and oscillation. A simplified debt rule is Bₜ₊₁ = 1.05Bₜ − 150, with fixed payment at each period end and no new borrowing. Starting at B₀ = 3,200, solve the path and its steady state. Explain why that steady state is unstable and why this rule must be adjusted when a below-target balance would reach repayment. For A with rows (1/2, 4), (0, 1/2), derive Aᵗ directly by induction and solve from z₀ = (0, 1). Calculate the first three updates and explain why repeated eigenvalues and initial amplification do not contradict asymptotic stability. Solve x′ + 2x = 6 + 2t with x(0) = 1 using an integrating factor. Verify the initial value and derivative equation. Does this forced path converge to a constant steady state? Solve x′ = (2/5)x(1 − x/20) with x(0) = 5. Find when it first reaches 10, classify both nonnegative equilibria and explain which solution would be lost by dividing by x during separation. Solve x′ = −x³ and x′ = x³ from a nonzero x₀, and classify zero. Give the forward domain for each path and explain why a zero Jacobian is inconclusive. Solve z′ = Az for A with rows (−2, 1), (1, −2) and z(0) = (2, 0). Derive the solution from eigenvectors, classify stability, and verify both component equations. For x′ = −4x and x(0) = 1, classify Euler iterations with h = 0.2, 0.5 and 0.6. Compare the Euler value at t = 1 using h = 0.2 with the exact value. Explain both the stability and accuracy conclusions. Solutions and stability arguments The steady state is 8, and xₜ = 8 − 4(−1/4)ᵗ. Updates are 9, 7.75 and 8.0625. The initial-deviation multiplier is (−1/4)ᵗ; an extra initial unit changes xₜ by exactly that amount. The sign alternates and its absolute magnitude tends to zero. On the real-state domain the fixed point is globally asymptotically stable; a restricted economic state domain requires its own invariance check. B* = 150/0.05 = 3,000, and Bₜ = 3,000 + 200(1.05)ᵗ. Starting above the target, the initial payment is less than interest of 160, so the balance rises; the first next balance is 3,210. Perturbations about 3,000 grow as (1.05)ᵗ, proving instability. Starting below it gives a decreasing formula that eventually becomes negative if extended without modification. An actual outstanding-loan model must stop at repayment or reduce the last payment; allowing a negative debt under the unchanged rule would change its economic meaning. For integer t ≥ 1, Aᵗ has rows ((1/2)ᵗ, 4t(1/2)ᵗ⁻¹), (0, (1/2)ᵗ). Multiplying by A advances the diagonal powers and adds the two upper-right terms into 4(t + 1)(1/2)ᵗ, proving induction; A⁰ = I handles the start. The path is zₜ = (4t(1/2)ᵗ⁻¹, (1/2)ᵗ). Its first three updates are (4, 1/2), (4, 1/4) and (3, 1/8). The polynomial factor t decays when multiplied by a geometric factor below one, so every initial deviation tends to zero. The repeated eigenvalue has a nontrivial generalized chain, explaining the t term and the temporary amplification. Multiplying by e²ᵗ gives (e²ᵗx)′ = e²ᵗ(6 + 2t). Integration yields x(t) = t + 5/2 − (3/2)e⁻²ᵗ. At zero it equals 1. Its derivative is 1 + 3e⁻²ᵗ, while 6 + 2t − 2x is the same expression. The growing input means the solution does not converge to a constant: it approaches the moving path t + 5/2. A time-varying forced equation need not have an autonomous steady state. The path is 20/[1 + 3e^{-2t/5}]. Setting x = 10 gives 3e^{-2t/5} = 1, so t = (5/2)ln 3 ≈ 2.746530722. Equilibria are 0 and 20. The derivative of the right-hand side is (2/5)(1 − x/10), positive at zero and negative at 20; zero is unstable to small positive states, and 20 is attracting and stable. The identically zero path is lost by division by x. Dividing by 1 − x/20 also excludes the constant 20 path, which must be restored. For the negative cubic, x(t) = x₀/√(1 + 2x₀²t) exists for all t ≥ 0, preserves the sign, has nonincreasing absolute value and approaches zero. This proves global asymptotic stability on ℝ. For the positive cubic, x(t) = x₀/√(1 − 2x₀²t) exists forward only for 0 ≤ t < 1/(2x₀²); its magnitude grows and becomes unbounded at that boundary. Arbitrarily small nonzero initial values eventually exceed any fixed small tolerance, proving instability. Both derivatives at zero vanish, so their shared linearization cannot distinguish them. Eigenvectors (1, 1) and (1, −1) have eigenvalues −1 and −3. The initial vector is their sum, giving z₁ = e⁻ᵗ + e⁻³ᵗ and z₂ = e⁻ᵗ − e⁻³ᵗ. Both initial coordinates match (2, 0), and both tend to zero. Differentiation gives z₁′ = −e⁻ᵗ − 3e⁻³ᵗ = −2z₁ + z₂ and z₂′ = −e⁻ᵗ + 3e⁻³ᵗ = z₁ − 2z₂. The linear equilibrium is globally asymptotically stable. The respective Euler multipliers are 0.2, −1 and −1.4. The first decays without sign reversal, the second is stable but nondecaying and alternates, and the third is unstable with alternating growth. At t = 1, h = 0.2 uses five steps and gives 0.2⁵ = 0.00032. The exact value is e⁻⁴ ≈ 0.018315639, giving absolute error approximately 0.017995639. The small-step recurrence is stable but inaccurate by roughly 98.25% relative to that exact value. All three exact sampled multipliers e^{-4h} are stable; instability of the latter Euler recurrence is computational. Teach-back and Delayed check Explain the difference between a fixed point, a stable equilibrium and an attracting equilibrium. Use one persistent oscillation and one unstable debt path. Derive an affine recurrence and an integrating-factor solution, then explain why eigenvalue tests have different boundaries in discrete and continuous time. After several days, change the stock's retention rate and inflow and recalculate its convergence date. Solve a different two-coordinate system and verify its path in the original equations. Give a nonlinear example where the linearization is inconclusive and an Euler step where a computational path misrepresents the exact equation. Sources and extensions MIT's first-order linear ODE materials provide independent practice with integrating factors and initial conditions. MIT's exponential-matrix notes provide further study of matrix-series solutions and commuting decompositions. MIT's nonlinear-system linearization materials explain the limits of borderline classifications. The general nonlinear stability theorem is stated here with its conditions; its full dynamical-systems proof is later study. Lebl's author-hosted Picard section is a method reference for local Lipschitz conditions and the path-space existence argument. The chapter's stock, debt, systems and exercises are original. Higher-order ODEs, boundary-value problems, bifurcations, delay equations and stochastic dynamics require additional methods. The later macroeconomic and research volumes develop the particular dynamic models needed there; an initial-value solution is not a complete theory of forward-looking equilibrium selection."}, {"title": "02.12 · Numerical methods and dynamic foundations", "url": "v02-c12.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Compute an answer together with its accuracy A displayed decimal is not an accuracy statement. A useful numerical result identifies the mathematical problem, domain, approximation rule, stopping criterion and sources of error. Separate inaccurate input data from approximation error, incomplete iteration and rounding. Solving an approximate problem very precisely does not recover an accurately specified original problem. This chapter develops root finding, interpolation and quadrature, then combines dynamic reasoning with a finite deterministic Bellman problem. Programming syntax is not a prerequisite: first reconstruct the algorithms on paper. A small optional Python benchmark uses only the standard library and supplies implementations you can inspect and modify. Bisection: preserve a bracket and halve uncertainty Suppose f is continuous on [a, b] and its endpoint values have opposing signs. The intermediate value theorem guarantees at least one root. Evaluate the midpoint m. If its value is zero, it is a root in exact arithmetic. Otherwise retain the half interval whose endpoints have opposing signs. Continuity preserves existence inside the new interval; uniqueness requires an additional argument, such as strict monotonicity. After N completed halvings, interval width is (b − a)/2ᴺ. Returning the midpoint of that final interval gives absolute root error at most (b − a)/2ᴺ⁺¹ relative to some bracketed root. To require this bound below ε, choose N with that inequality. Specify whether N counts function evaluations, updates or halvings; mixing those conventions causes off-by-one stopping errors. A sign change without continuity is insufficient: 1/x has opposing signs at −1 and 1 but is undefined between them and has no zero. A repeated root can also have no sign change, as (x − 1/2)² shows. Bisection's sufficient conditions do not describe every possible root. Floating-point implementations must also detect a midpoint rounding to an endpoint; continued iteration then cannot shrink the represented interval. Newton steps and why they can fail At xₙ, solve the tangent equation f(xₙ) + f′(xₙ)(xₙ₊₁ − xₙ) = 0 to obtain xₙ₊₁ = xₙ − f(xₙ)/f′(xₙ). The derivative must be nonzero and the proposal must remain in the function's domain. Newton does not automatically preserve a sign bracket or approach the desired root from arbitrary starting points. Its fast local convergence follows from Taylor's theorem. Let x* be a simple root, f twice continuously differentiable nearby, |f′| ≥ m > 0 and |f″| ≤ M there. Expanding f(x*) about xₙ and substituting the Newton step gives |xₙ₊₁ − x*| ≤ [M/(2m)]|xₙ − x*|² as long as both points lie in the stated neighborhood. A sufficiently small starting error makes the bound preserve that neighborhood and decrease the error. This is local quadratic convergence; it is not a global promise. For f(x) = x³ − 2x + 2, the start x₀ = 0 produces x₁ = 1 and x₂ = 0, repeating forever. For f(x) = x², nonzero starts halve at each step rather than converge quadratically, because the root has zero derivative. Small steps can also reflect poor scaling or a flat objective. Verify the original residual and a valid error relationship. A bracketed hybrid can use Newton only when its derivative is usable and its proposal lies safely inside the current bracket, otherwise bisect. Merely accepting every interior Newton proposal does not guarantee a uniform halving rate. Requiring the proposal to lie in the middle half of the bracket ensures each retained interval has at most three quarters of the previous width. It combines a global bracket argument with potentially faster local steps. For a system F(x) = 0, solve DF(xₙ)Δx = −F(xₙ) and set xₙ₊₁ = xₙ + Δx. Solve the linear system rather than explicitly form an inverse in routine computation. Check Jacobian rank, domain and residuals; damping or a trust region can control proposals. The scalar bisection argument does not generalize into an automatic multi-dimensional bracket guarantee. Worked example 1 — A normalized nonlinear equilibrium Let a normalized quantity q ≥ 0 satisfy q³ + q = 5. Define f(q) = q³ + q − 5. At q = 1 it is −3 and at q = 2 it is 5. On [1, 2], f′ = 3q² + 1 ≥ 4, so there is exactly one root there; strict increase on all nonnegative q rules out another admissible equilibrium. Starting from [1, 2], 19 completed bisection halvings give midpoint error at most 2⁻²⁰ ≈ 0.000000953674, below 10⁻⁶. A residual gives another conditional bound: the mean value theorem implies |q − q*| ≤ |f(q)|/4 for a candidate q in the same interval. At q = 1.516, the exact residual is 0.000156096, so the root error is at most 0.000039024. A residual is not itself a quantity error; the derivative bound supplies the conversion. Newton starting at 1.5 gives approximately 1.516129032, 1.515980240 and 1.515980228. Their residuals are approximately 0.001174851, 0.000000100693 and a rounding-sized value. On [1, 2], |f″| ≤ 12 gives the local error factor M/(2m) = 3/2. These iterates stay in the domain and can be checked against an independent bisection bracket. If the right-hand side is θ instead of 5, implicit differentiation gives dq*/dθ = 1/(3q*² + 1), approximately 0.12667 at this equilibrium. This is model sensitivity to changed input, separate from algorithm error at fixed θ. The variables were normalized before writing q³ + q, so adding powers has a consistent mathematical interpretation. Conditioning and stable arithmetic Conditioning asks how much the solution changes when the problem's input changes. For a differentiable scalar solution x = g(θ), |g′(θ)| measures local absolute sensitivity. When both θ and g(θ) are nonzero, |θg′(θ)/g(θ)| measures local relative sensitivity. These depend on scaling and units. A large absolute sensitivity need not mean a large relative sensitivity. For Ax = b with invertible A, a right-hand-side perturbation gives Δx = A⁻¹Δb. Combining ‖Δx‖ ≤ ‖A⁻¹‖‖Δb‖ with ‖b‖ ≤ ‖A‖‖x‖ yields ‖Δx‖/‖x‖ ≤ κ(A)‖Δb‖/‖b‖, where κ(A) = ‖A‖‖A⁻¹‖ in the same norm, for nonzero b. In Euclidean norm it is the largest divided by the smallest singular value. Near-singular systems can magnify tiny data or residual errors. A small residual alone does not prove a small solution error. Algorithmic stability asks whether computation introduces small errors relative to the intended input problem. A good algorithm cannot remove the intrinsic sensitivity of an ill-conditioned problem. Arithmetic rearrangement can nevertheless avoid unnecessary cancellation. For small x, computing √(1 + x) − 1 subtracts nearly equal numbers. The identical expression x/[√(1 + x) + 1] avoids that subtraction, for x ≥ −1. If 1 + x rounds to 1, the first expression can return zero even though the true result is positive. Choose a tolerance commensurate with meaningful input precision and required decisions. Keep a record of units, derivative or curvature bounds and the norm used. Reporting ten decimal places cannot compensate for input quantities known only to the nearest tenth. Interpolation: values between known nodes Linear interpolation between distinct nodes a and b uses L(x) = [(b − x)/(b − a)]f(a) + [(x − a)/(b − a)]f(b). Inside the interval the weights are nonnegative and sum to one, so the interpolated value stays between the endpoint values. It is exact for affine functions; exact agreement at the nodes does not make the interior exact for a curved function. For twice continuously differentiable f on [a, b], f(x) − L(x) = [f″(ξ)/2](x − a)(x − b) for some ξ in the interval when x is interior. To prove it, subtract from f − L a multiple of (t − a)(t − b) chosen to make the difference vanish at t = x. The difference has three distinct zeros, so applying Rolle's theorem twice gives a zero second derivative and identifies that multiple. At the nodes the error is directly zero. If |f″| ≤ M₂, the error is at most M₂(b − a)²/8 because the product's absolute value is maximized at the midpoint. Piecewise linear interpolation on a grid inherits this bound using its largest cell width. Outside the node range, the weights can be negative; the same inside-interval certificate no longer applies without an extended smoothness domain and a suitable bound. For distinct nodes x₀, …, xₙ, the Lagrange polynomial is P(x) = Σf(xⱼ)ℓⱼ(x), with ℓⱼ(x) = product over k ≠ j of (x − xₖ)/(xⱼ − xₖ). It has degree at most n and matches every node. It is unique: the difference of two such polynomials would have n + 1 distinct zeros while having degree at most n. Under Cⁿ⁺¹ smoothness over the nodes and x, the repeated-Rolle argument gives error f⁽ⁿ⁺¹⁾(ξ) product of (x − xⱼ)/(n + 1)!. High degree alone does not establish accuracy: derivative sizes, node placement and amplification of noisy values all matter. Piecewise low-degree approximations can control error locally. When approximating a value function, also check shape restrictions and whether interpolation preserves feasibility; a mathematical fit is not a substitute for the decision model. Quadrature: integrate the approximation with an error bound Divide [a, b] into n equal cells of width h = (b − a)/n and let xⱼ = a + jh. The composite trapezoid rule is Tₕ = h[f(a)/2 + Σ from j = 1 to n − 1 of f(xⱼ) + f(b)/2]. It integrates the piecewise linear interpolant. Applying the interpolation error on each cell and integrating |(x − xⱼ)(x − xⱼ₊₁)| gives cell error at most M₂h³/12. Summing proves |∫f − Tₕ| ≤ M₂(b − a)h²/12 for f ∈ C². The midpoint rule takes h times the sum of cell-midpoint values. Taylor expansion about each midpoint cancels the integrated odd linear term and bounds the cell error by M₂h³/24, giving composite error at most M₂(b − a)h²/24. These are global bounds over a fixed interval, not merely errors of individual samples. For even n, Simpson's rule is Sₕ = (h/3)[f(a) + f(b) + 4Σ over odd j of f(xⱼ) + 2Σ over interior even j of f(xⱼ)]. It integrates quadratic interpolants over pairs of cells and is also exact for cubics because the odd cubic contribution cancels about each pair's midpoint. For f ∈ C⁴ and |f⁽⁴⁾| ≤ M₄, |∫f − Sₕ| ≤ M₄(b − a)h⁴/180. Derive the Simpson error constant Shift one pair to [−h, h]. Its error functional E(f) is the integral minus (h/3)[f(−h) + 4f(0) + f(h)]. It annihilates polynomials of degree at most three. The integral Taylor remainder about −h writes f(x) as such a polynomial plus ∫ from −h to x of f⁽⁴⁾(t)(x − t)³/6 dt. Integrating this remainder and the three weighted evaluations gives E(f) = ∫ from −h to h of f⁽⁴⁾(t)K(t) dt. Direct evaluation of the kernel, or expansion separately on each half, gives K(t) = −(h − |t|)³(h + 3|t|)/72. For example, on 0 ≤ t ≤ h, it is (h − t)⁴/24 − h(h − t)³/18. Its absolute integral is 2h⁵/72 times ∫ from 0 to 1 of (1 − u)³(1 + 3u) du = h⁵/90. The pair error is at most M₄h⁵/90. There are n/2 pairs; using nh = b − a produces the displayed composite bound. Halving h reduces these bounds by a factor of four for midpoint/trapezoid and sixteen for Simpson when the smoothness bounds remain valid. A comparison of coarse and fine answers is useful evidence, but it is not automatically a rigorous bound: nonsmoothness, singularities or shared bias can defeat the expected rate. Split known discontinuities and handle improper endpoints separately. Discretization error over a time horizon Euler uses xₙ₊₁ = xₙ + hf(tₙ, xₙ). Along a sufficiently smooth exact path, Taylor's theorem bounds the one-step truncation error by Ch². If f is Lipschitz in the state with constant L throughout a region containing both paths, the total error eₙ obeys eₙ₊₁ ≤ (1 + Lh)eₙ + Ch², with e₀ = 0 for an exact initial value. Summing this recurrence gives eₙ ≤ (Ch/L)[(1 + Lh)ⁿ − 1] ≤ (Ch/L)(e^{LT} − 1) for L > 0 and nh ≤ T. When L = 0 the bound is ChT. Thus the fixed-horizon global error is order h under the stated uniform conditions. An order-h² one-step error has accumulated over roughly 1/h steps. This accuracy argument complements, rather than replaces, the step-stability analysis in Chapter 11. The Bellman equation for a finite deterministic decision Let S be a finite set of states. At state s choose an action a from a finite nonempty feasible set A(s), obtain bounded immediate reward r(s, a), and move deterministically to g(s, a) ∈ S. Rewards use a consistent objective unit. For discount factor 0 ≤ β < 1, evaluate a path by Σ from t = 0 to infinity of βᵗr(sₜ, aₜ). Bounded rewards make this sum absolutely convergent. A stationary policy selects one action for each state. For a candidate value vector v, define (Tv)(s) = max over a ∈ A(s) of [r(s, a) + βv(g(s, a))]. This is the Bellman operator. For each action the continuation-value difference under v and w is at most β‖v − w‖∞. The absolute difference between two maxima is no larger than the maximum absolute difference of their components, so ‖Tv − Tw‖∞ ≤ β‖v − w‖∞. Finite-dimensional value space with the maximum norm is complete; the contraction theorem gives a unique fixed point v*. Why is that fixed point the actual optimum? For any feasible path, the Bellman inequality gives r(sₜ, aₜ) + βv*(sₜ₊₁) ≤ v*(sₜ). Multiplying by βᵗ and summing through N − 1 yields Σβᵗr ≤ v*(s₀) − βᴺv*(sᴺ). The final term tends to zero since v* is bounded. Thus no path beats v*. Choose a maximizing action at every state, possible because the action sets are finite. Along that stationary greedy policy each inequality is equality, so its infinite-horizon value attains v*. This establishes both optimality and policy existence. Iterate vₙ₊₁ = Tvₙ. Starting from v₀ = 0, vₙ is also the optimal n-period value with zero terminal payoff. For any current approximation v, its Bellman residual η = ‖Tv − v‖∞ supplies the value-error bound ‖v − v*‖∞ ≤ η/(1 − β). A small residual becomes less informative when β is close to one. An approximation to T itself introduces an additional operator-error term divided by 1 − β. Value accuracy and action accuracy differ. If ‖v − v*‖∞ ≤ ε, each action score has error at most βε. Therefore a computed score gap greater than 2βε certifies the winning action also maximizes the true score. Smaller gaps allow ties or uncertain rankings even when values are accurate. An unchanged policy across several iterations does not alone certify value convergence. Worked example 2 — Operating versus servicing a machine A synthetic machine has states good (G) and worn (W), with β = 4/5. In G the sole action operates: net reward 4 and next state W. In W, operating gives reward 1 and leaves it worn; servicing gives net reward −1 and next state G. Servicing sacrifices current operation and incurs its stated net cost; there is no additional unrecorded reward in that period. The equations are v G = 4 + (4/5)v W and v W = max[1 + (4/5)v W , −1 + (4/5)v G ]. If servicing is chosen in W, solving the two affine equations gives v G = 80/9 and v W = 55/9. Verify the alternative: operating's score in W is 53/9, below servicing's 55/9 by 2/9. The candidate satisfies the Bellman equation and is therefore the unique optimal value vector. A policy that never services has values v W = 5 and v G = 8. It collects the larger immediate reward in W but loses the option of restoring higher future output. The optimal plan is conditional on the specified transition and reward model, not a universal maintenance rule. Value iteration from zero produces (4, 1), (4.8, 2.2), (5.76, 2.84) and (6.272, 3.608) after the first four updates. After 30 updates, it gives approximately (8.880979828, 6.103545922). Its current residual is approximately 0.001856910, yielding value-error bound 0.009284551. This certifies error below 0.01 without knowing the exact solution in advance. At that approximation, servicing's computed advantage in W is about 0.221947124. The possible score-difference error is at most 2βε ≈ 0.014855281, so servicing is also certified as the true optimal action. The value bound and action bound answer different questions. With β = 1 the contraction and finite discounted-value argument would fail; it must not be silently inserted into the same algorithm. An auditable numerical workflow Write the original equation and admissible domain, choose a method whose conditions hold, and maintain the invariant behind its guarantee: a root bracket, a derivative bound, a smoothness bound or a contraction modulus. Record the stopping criterion in solution units. Recompute residuals in the original equations and compare with an independent method or benchmark when available. Download the standard-library numerical benchmark . It implements bisection, quadrature and deterministic value iteration for the original examples. Read the returned error estimate and iteration count alongside the answer. Its floating-point calculations do not include an outward-rounded interval-arithmetic proof; mathematical bounds assume reliable evaluations and signs. The separate editorial verifier also uses exact fractions where the examples permit them. For bisection, reconstruct the implementation as follows: validate an ordered finite bracket and positive tolerance; evaluate endpoints; return an endpoint if it is a root; otherwise require opposite signs. While the bracket midpoint error bound exceeds tolerance, evaluate its midpoint and retain the sign-changing half. Return the final midpoint, bracket and half-width. Enforce an iteration limit and detect a midpoint indistinguishable from an endpoint. A status of “iteration limit” is unfinished computation, not successful convergence. Exercises For Worked example 1, find the minimum number of completed bisection halvings guaranteeing midpoint error below 10⁻⁵. At q = 1.516 compute the exact residual and the derivative-based error bound. Explain what would change without monotonicity. Apply Newton to x² − 3 starting at x₀ = 1, computing the first three updates exactly. Explain why the root is simple and why the start for x³ − 2x + 2 discussed above cycles instead of benefiting from local convergence. For x(θ) = √θ, θ > 0, derive absolute and relative sensitivity. Evaluate them at θ = 10⁻⁸. Explain why a perturbation that could make θ negative cannot be treated by the same real-valued local approximation. Linearly interpolate e⁻ˣ between nodes 0 and 1/2 at x = 1/4. Calculate the prediction and actual error, then give a rigorous error bound using the second derivative on this cell. Approximate ∫ from 0 to 1 of x⁴ dx with two-cell trapezoid and Simpson rules. Compute exact errors and compare with the respective derivative bounds. Explain why Simpson requires an even number of cells. For x′ = −x, x(0) = 1, calculate Euler values at t = 1 using n = 10 and 20 equal steps. Compare with e⁻¹ and discuss the observed error ratio. Supply a fixed-horizon global error bound using L = 1 and C = 1/2; identify its assumptions. In the machine problem, take approximate values (8.88, 6.10). Compute its Bellman residual, a value-error bound, and a score-gap certificate for the action in W. Distinguish the bound from the actual error using the exact values. Change servicing's net reward to −c while keeping the other machine inputs fixed. Determine the optimal stationary policy at c = 2 and find the threshold c ≥ 0 where servicing and operating tie. Check candidate values against both actions. Solutions, error bounds and policy checks The midpoint bound after N halvings is 2⁻ᴺ⁻¹. At N = 15 it is approximately 0.0000152588, too large; at N = 16 it is approximately 0.00000762939, below the target. The residual at 1.516 is 0.000156096 and dividing by 4 gives 0.000039024. Without monotonicity a sign bracket can contain multiple roots; the interval still guarantees one, but not a uniquely identified equilibrium. A lower absolute derivative bound throughout the bracket would itself exclude such multiplicity for a continuous derivative. The update is (x + 3/x)/2. From 1 it gives 2, 7/4 and 97/56 ≈ 1.732142857. The root √3 has derivative 2√3 ≠ 0. Near it, the smoothness and nonzero-derivative hypotheses give quadratic convergence; the first jump is not justified by merely calling the method local. For the cubic cycle, f(0) = 2 and f′(0) = −2 give 1, while f(1) = 1 and f′(1) = 1 give 0. These iterates never enter the local neighborhood of a simple root. x′(θ) = 1/(2√θ); the relative sensitivity is |θx′/x| = 1/2. At 10⁻⁸, x = 10⁻⁴ and the absolute sensitivity is 5,000 while the relative sensitivity remains 1/2. The real-valued domain is θ > 0; an input change comparable with θ can cross its boundary and invalidates a local derivative extrapolation. Small absolute θ does not alone imply large relative conditioning. The prediction is (1 + e⁻¹ᐟ²)/2 ≈ 0.803265330. The actual value is e⁻¹ᐟ⁴ ≈ 0.778800783, so the overestimate is approximately 0.024464547. On [0, 1/2], |f″| = e⁻ˣ ≤ 1, so the bound is (1/2)²/8 = 1/32 = 0.03125. Convexity explains the sign of the interpolation error. The integral is 1/5. With h = 1/2, T = (1/2)[0/2 + 1/16 + 1/2] = 9/32, error 13/160. Simpson gives (1/6)[0 + 4/16 + 1] = 5/24, error 1/120. Since max |f″| = 12, the trapezoid bound is 12h²/12 = 1/4, valid but conservative. Since |f⁽⁴⁾| = 24, the Simpson bound is 24h⁴/180 = 1/120, attained here. Simpson groups adjacent cells in pairs around a midpoint; an odd number cannot use that composite formula throughout without a separate final-cell treatment. Euler gives (9/10)¹⁰ ≈ 0.348678440 and (19/20)²⁰ ≈ 0.358485922. The exact value is e⁻¹ ≈ 0.367879441, so absolute errors are approximately 0.019201001 and 0.009393519, with ratio about 2.04. This is consistent with first-order global accuracy, not a proof from two data points. The derived bound is (h/2)(e − 1), approximately 0.085914091 and 0.042957046. It uses the Lipschitz constant of f = −x, the exact path's second derivative bounded by 1 on [0, 1], an exact initial value and a region containing both paths. Both Euler steps also satisfy the stability condition h < 2. T(8.88, 6.10) = (8.88, max(5.88, 6.104)) = (8.88, 6.104). The residual is 0.004; dividing by 1/5 gives value-error bound 0.02. Servicing's computed advantage is 6.104 − 5.88 = 0.224, greater than 2(4/5)(0.02) = 0.032, certifying that action. Actual value errors are 2/225 ≈ 0.008888889 in G and 1/90 ≈ 0.011111111 in W, so their maximum is below the conservative 0.02 certificate. At c = 2, operating in W gives v W = 5 and v G = 8. Servicing's score is −2 + (4/5)8 = 4.4, below 5, so the no-service policy is optimal. A servicing candidate solves v W = (16/5 − c)/(9/25) and v G = 4 + (4/5)v W . Its Bellman consistency requires v W ≥ 1 + (4/5)v W , or v W ≥ 5, equivalent to c ≤ 7/5. At c = 7/5 the values are (8, 5) and both actions tie in W. For 0 ≤ c < 7/5 servicing is uniquely optimal there; above it operating is uniquely optimal. This threshold uses net reward, not an additional cost subtracted twice. Teach-back and Delayed check Explain why root residual, solution error, input sensitivity and policy-score error require different bounds. Derive one bisection stopping rule, one quadrature bound and the Bellman contraction. Describe what evidence would support an accuracy claim when the exact answer is unavailable. After several days, change the equilibrium's right-hand side, the interpolation cell and the machine's servicing cost. Reconstruct the algorithms, evaluate their bounds and check whether the action changes. Deliberately try a Newton start that fails and explain the violated guarantee rather than merely changing the starting point until it works. Sources and next steps MIT's numerical-analysis nonlinear-equation notes provide further study of root iterations and their local limits. MIT's interpolation notes provide an independent route to polynomial interpolation and quadrature. MIT's numerical-integration notes provide further practice with grid spacing, Taylor bounds and numerical differentiation. Sargent and Stachurski's author-led QuantEcon lecture extends dynamic programming to stochastic finite-state problems. This chapter's deterministic machine model, proofs and benchmark are original and require no external numerical package. This completes the volume's chapter sequence, not its mastery assessment. The cumulative examinations and constrained-allocation project test whether these mathematical tools can be selected, combined and defended. Probability, data work and later economic theory extend the deterministic foundations."}, {"title": "03.01 · Probability spaces and counting", "url": "v03-c01.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Specify what can happen before assigning probabilities A risk calculation is only as clear as its experiment. Are you selecting a transaction from a fixed archive, forecasting one future period, or repeatedly drawing from a population? Are repeated selections allowed? Which distinctions does an outcome preserve? A statement such as “three of the four possibilities are unfavorable” is not a probability calculation until you explain why those four possibilities deserve equal weight. Probability supplies rules for coherent uncertainty. It does not by itself choose a realistic economic model or estimate its inputs. This chapter builds the language of outcomes and events, derives useful consequences of the probability axioms, and shows how counting and independence enter a specified sampling design. All numerical business examples are synthetic. Outcomes, events and event operations The sample space Ω is a set of possible outcomes for the experiment. A particular outcome is ω ∈ Ω. An event A is a collection of outcomes: it occurs precisely when the realized ω belongs to A. An outcome can record several attributes, such as activity and funding conditions. Those attributes must not be mistaken for separate mutually exclusive outcomes. For events A and B, their intersection A ∩ B means both occur; their union A ∪ B means at least one occurs, including both. The complement Aᶜ = Ω \\ A means A does not occur. The difference A \\ B means A occurs and B does not. Events are disjoint when their intersection is empty, ∅. A partition divides Ω into disjoint events whose union is Ω. Membership logic gives De Morgan's rules: (A ∪ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ and (A ∩ B)ᶜ = Aᶜ ∪ Bᶜ. For example, “neither a delay nor a funding disruption” is the complement of their union, not the complement of their intersection. Prove such an identity by showing that an arbitrary outcome belongs to the left side if and only if it belongs to the right side. A probability space is a triple (Ω, ℱ, P). Here ℱ is the collection of events on which probabilities are defined, and P assigns each event a number. On a finite sample space we may use every subset, called the power set. In general ℱ is a sigma-algebra: it contains Ω and is closed under complements and countable unions. This ensures that countably combined questions remain events. For continuous spaces, not every conceivable subset must be in ℱ; the measure foundations chapter develops this distinction rather than assuming it away. The probability axioms and their consequences The axioms are nonnegativity, P(A) ≥ 0; normalization, P(Ω) = 1; and countable additivity, P(∪Aⱼ) = ΣP(Aⱼ) for any countable sequence of pairwise disjoint events. Probability is dimensionless. Finite additivity is a special case. The disjointness condition matters: the same outcome cannot be counted twice merely because two descriptions include it. First derive P(∅) = 0 by applying additivity to Ω and ∅. Since A and Aᶜ partition Ω, P(Aᶜ) = 1 − P(A). If A ⊆ B, then B is the disjoint union of A and B \\ A, proving monotonicity P(A) ≤ P(B). In particular every event probability lies between zero and one. Splitting A ∪ B into A \\ B, B \\ A and A ∩ B gives P(A ∪ B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A ∩ B). For three events, subtract all three pair intersections and add the triple intersection: P(A ∪ B ∪ C) = P(A) + P(B) + P(C) − P(A ∩ B) − P(A ∩ C) − P(B ∩ C) + P(A ∩ B ∩ C). An outcome in one event is counted once; in two events, twice minus once; in all three, three times minus three plus once. This proves the formula by disjoint outcome categories. The union bound is P(∪ from j = 1 to n of Aⱼ) ≤ ΣP(Aⱼ). One proof assigns each outcome to the first event in the list containing it. These assigned subsets are disjoint, each lies inside its associated Aⱼ, and together they form the union. Monotonicity and additivity give the inequality. It needs no independence assumption; when overlap is large, it can be conservative. A bound larger than one is valid but can be tightened to one. Two marginal probabilities also restrict their possible intersection. Write p = P(A), q = P(B), r = P(A ∩ B). The four disjoint cells have probabilities r, p − r, q − r and 1 − p − q + r. Requiring all to be nonnegative gives max(0, p + q − 1) ≤ r ≤ min(p, q). Conversely every r in this interval gives a valid four-cell probability model with those marginals. Thus marginals alone generally do not identify joint risk. Worked example 1 — A weighted economic-state table A hypothetical one-period model has four outcomes. Activity is strong or weak; funding is stable or stressed. These labels define mutually exclusive, exhaustive states, with the specified weights below. They are model inputs, not October market observations. Outcome Probability Strong activity, stable funding 0.42 Strong activity, stressed funding 0.18 Weak activity, stable funding 0.10 Weak activity, stressed funding 0.30 Let A mean weak activity and B mean stressed funding. Then P(A) = 0.10 + 0.30 = 0.40, P(B) = 0.18 + 0.30 = 0.48, and P(A ∩ B) = 0.30. At least one adverse condition has probability 0.40 + 0.48 − 0.30 = 0.58, while neither has probability 0.42. The table sums to one and every weight is nonnegative. Counting three adverse states out of four would give 0.75, incorrectly replacing the supplied weights with a uniform law. Assuming independence would instead give joint probability 0.40 × 0.48 = 0.192 and union probability 0.688. Both disagree with this specified model. With fixed marginals, a larger intersection increases the probability of both conditions but decreases the probability of at least one, because more adverse mass overlaps. “Dependence raises every risk probability” is not a valid general rule. The feasible range for the intersection is [0, 0.40]. Our 0.30 is coherent, but a proposed joint probability of 0.45 would exceed the weak-activity marginal and could not be part of any probability model. A mathematical audit detects this inconsistency before any monetary loss calculation. Finite weights and the special case of equal likelihood For finite Ω = {ω₁, …, ωₙ}, assign weights pᵢ ≥ 0 summing to one. Define P(A) = Σ over ωᵢ ∈ A of pᵢ. This construction satisfies the axioms. If and only if the outcomes have equal weights in this construction, each weight is 1/n and P(A) = |A|/|Ω|, where vertical bars denote the number of elements. Counting becomes probability only after the weighting assumption is justified. Changing how outcomes are recorded changes their weights. Two independent fair binary observations have four equally likely ordered records, but the three possible totals 0, 1 and 2 have probabilities 1/4, 1/2 and 1/4. A coarse label's probability is the sum of the probabilities of the detailed outcomes mapping to it. Fewer labels do not imply equally likely labels. A nonempty event can have probability zero. In a finite model this means all its outcome weights are zero; the empty event is logically impossible, whereas a nonempty zero-weight event is excluded by the chosen probability law. In a continuous uniform length model on [0, 1], any particular point lies in intervals of arbitrarily small length, so monotonicity makes its probability zero. Yet some point is realized. Countable additivity cannot be replaced by additivity over an uncountable collection of singleton points. Counting without double counting The addition principle counts alternatives that are disjoint. The product principle counts a sequence of choices: if the first step has n possibilities and every first choice leaves m possibilities for the second, there are nm complete records. The available second choices may depend on the first as long as their number is always m. If counts vary by branch, sum the branch counts instead of multiplying by an unqualified common number. For an ordered list of k distinct objects selected from n, the count is n(n − 1)…(n − k + 1) = n!/(n − k)!, for integers 0 ≤ k ≤ n. The factorial n! is the product of integers 1 through n, with 0! = 1. If order does not matter, every k-object subset was counted k! times. Divide to obtain the binomial coefficient C(n, k), read “n choose k,” equal to n!/[k!(n − k)!]. Choosing none or all gives one subset. Set C(n, k) = 0 for integer k outside 0 through n when using a counting sum. A particular distinguished object either belongs or does not belong to a k-subset. This partition proves C(n, k) = C(n − 1, k − 1) + C(n − 1, k). Counting every subset by size gives Σ from k = 0 to n of C(n, k) = 2ⁿ, since each object has two membership choices. Expanding (u + v)ⁿ by choosing a u or v term from each factor similarly gives ΣC(n, k)uᵏvⁿ⁻ᵏ. Replacement changes the count. An ordered k-draw record from n labels, allowing repetition, has nᵏ possibilities. An unordered multiset of k selections has C(n + k − 1, k) possibilities for n ≥ 1: record the k selections as stars separated into n labeled categories by n − 1 bars, then choose the star positions among n + k − 1 positions. This counts records, not their probabilities. Under independent uniform draws, a multiset with category counts m₁, …, mₙ has k!/(m₁!…mₙ!) ordered versions, so different multisets need not be equally likely. For two independent uniform draws from labels a and b, the ordered records aa, ab, ba, bb each have probability 1/4. The unordered records {a, a}, {a, b}, {b, b} have probabilities 1/4, 1/2, 1/4. Using a uniform law over these three multisets would describe a different experiment. The physical draw mechanism, rather than a convenient description, determines the law. Independence is a property of a joint law Events A and B are independent when P(A ∩ B) = P(A)P(B). This symmetric definition makes sense even when an event has probability zero. It is not the same as disjointness: two disjoint positive-probability events cannot be independent, because their intersection is zero while their product is positive. For n events, mutual independence requires P(∩ over j ∈ J of Aⱼ) = product over j ∈ J of P(Aⱼ) for every nonempty subset J of their indices. Pairwise independence checks only subsets of size two; it can miss higher-order dependence. For two independent fair bits X and Y, take A = {X = 1}, B = {Y = 1}, C = {X ≠ Y}. Each event has probability 1/2 and each pair intersection has probability 1/4, but the triple intersection is empty. Thus these three events are pairwise independent without being mutually independent. Independence extends to complements. From P(A ∩ Bᶜ) = P(A) − P(A ∩ B), independence gives P(A)[1 − P(B)]. Applying the same argument to Aᶜ proves all four combinations factor. Under mutual independence, the same subtraction argument replaces any selected event with its complement; repeated replacement yields the product law for complete success/failure patterns. Independent repeated draws are a model assumption about how joint outcomes are generated. Drawing with replacement permits the same object to recur, but replacement alone does not establish independence: a sampler could deliberately repeat the previous selection or vary weights based on earlier choices. The familiar independent-with-replacement model also specifies fresh independent randomization and fixed selection probabilities. Without replacement from a uniformly randomized finite population, ordered records of distinct labels are equally likely, but draws are dependent: removing one flagged item changes the remaining composition. Unordered subsets of a fixed size remain equally likely because each has the same number of possible orders. Both descriptions can give the same probability if their counts are used consistently. Worked example 2 — Sampling flagged files An archive contains twelve distinct files, three flagged and nine unflagged. Select four distinct files uniformly without replacement. The unordered sample space has C(12, 4) = 495 subsets. Exactly one flag requires choosing one of three flagged files and three of nine unflagged files: C(3, 1)C(9, 3) = 252 samples. The probability is 252/495 = 28/55 ≈ 0.509091. No flags has probability C(9, 4)/495 = 126/495 = 14/55. At least one therefore has probability 41/55 ≈ 0.745455. Exactly two flags has count C(3, 2)C(9, 2) = 108, and exactly three has count C(3, 3)C(9, 1) = 9. The counts 126 + 252 + 108 + 9 equal 495, an independent normalization check. Now change the experiment to four independently randomized uniform selections with replacement, keeping all selections even when the same file repeats. A specified one-flag pattern has probability (1/4)(3/4)³; there are C(4, 1) = 4 positions for it, giving 27/64 = 0.421875. No flags has probability (3/4)⁴ = 81/256, so at least one has probability 175/256 ≈ 0.683594. The two experiments have the same first-draw flag proportion but different joint laws. Reusing the without-replacement denominator or silently treating four selections as four distinct reviewed files would answer the wrong question. A randomly selected file's status is fixed in the archive; randomness comes from the selection mechanism. Two general sampling formulas, with their conditions For a population of N distinct objects containing M flagged ones, a uniform sample of n distinct objects has exactly k flags with probability C(M, k)C(N − M, n − k)/C(N, n), for 0 ≤ n ≤ N. The numerator independently chooses the flagged and unflagged subsets; it counts every eligible sample once. The support is max(0, n − (N − M)) ≤ k ≤ min(n, M). Outside that range the event is empty. This formula will later be named the hypergeometric law. For n mutually independent binary trials with the same success probability p, the probability of exactly k successes is C(n, k)pᵏ(1 − p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ. Choose the k successful positions; every such pattern has the same product weight. The binomial expansion verifies that the probabilities sum to one. At p = 0 or 1 the entire mass is at zero or n, respectively; use that direct interpretation instead of allowing ambiguous 0⁰ notation to distract from the experiment. If independent trials have different success probabilities, patterns of the same size need not have equal weights, so the common-p formula no longer applies. If they are dependent, even pattern products require a different joint law. An accurate marginal estimate does not license an independent-trial calculation. Countably many outcomes and continuity of probability For countable Ω = {1, 2, …}, nonnegative weights summing to one again define P(A) by summation. There is no uniform probability law assigning every positive integer the same mass: a positive common mass makes the total unbounded, and zero common mass sums to zero. “Uniformly choose an integer from all positive integers” needs a different, actually defined procedure. A valid example assigns outcome n probability 2⁻ⁿ. The geometric sum is one. Outcome n could record the index of the first success under an independently repeated fair binary trial; the product of n − 1 failures and one success gives 2⁻ⁿ. In a first-success record space one may include “never succeeds” as an extra outcome with probability zero. This is a conclusion under the specified independent law, not a logical prohibition of an infinite all-failure path. Countable additivity also implies continuity from below: if A₁ ⊆ A₂ ⊆ …, then P(∪Aₙ) = lim P(Aₙ). Define disjoint increments B₁ = A₁ and Bₙ = Aₙ \\ Aₙ₋₁; their finite partial sums equal P(Aₙ), and their full sum equals the union probability. Taking complements gives continuity from above for decreasing events: P(∩Aₙ) = lim P(Aₙ). These principles link the earlier sequence mathematics to probability and will justify later limiting arguments. A finite model can establish internal coherence without establishing empirical adequacy. Keep three questions separate: is the law mathematically valid, is its selection/forecasting mechanism correctly described, and are its weights credibly learned or defended? Later statistics addresses the third question using data and sampling uncertainty. Exercises On Ω = {a, b, c, d}, weights are proportional to 1, 2, 3, 4. Let A = {a, c} and B = {b, c}. Normalize the weights and calculate P(A), P(B), P(A ∩ B), P(A ∪ B), P(Aᶜ) and P(A \\ B). Check independence. A proposed model says P(A) = 0.7, P(B) = 0.6 and P(A ∩ B) = 0.2. Show why it is impossible by both a bound and a four-cell table. Replace the intersection by 0.4 and give a coherent table. Eight analysts include four from each of two specialties. Count three-person committees and ordered appointments to three distinct roles. Under uniform committee selection, find the probability that both specialties are represented. Ten files contain four flags. For a uniform three-file sample without replacement, calculate exactly two flags and at least one flag. Compare with three independent uniform draws with replacement. State what is held fixed and what changes. For independent fair bits X and Y, verify every pairwise independence relation among A = {X = 1}, B = {Y = 1}, C = {X ≠ Y}, then demonstrate the failure of mutual independence. Three modeled faults have marginal probabilities 0.20, 0.30, 0.25; pair intersections 0.08, 0.05, 0.10 for AB, AC, BC; and triple intersection 0.02. Compute at least one, exactly one, exactly two and none. Check all eight atom probabilities are nonnegative. Prove that independence of A and B implies independence of their complements. Explain why positive-probability disjoint events cannot be independent. For independent events with probabilities 0.3 and 0.4, compute the probability that neither occurs. For outcome n ∈ {1, 2, …} with weight 2⁻ⁿ, calculate the probability that n ≥ 4 and that n is odd. Explain why equal positive weights cannot define a uniform law on this sample space and why zero weights do not solve the problem. Solutions and assumption checks The total unnormalized weight is 10, giving (0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.4). Probabilities are 0.4, 0.5, 0.3, 0.6, 0.6 and 0.1, in the requested order. Since 0.3 ≠ 0.4 × 0.5 = 0.2, A and B are dependent. Adding event weights, not counting labels, is essential. The intersection must be at least 0.7 + 0.6 − 1 = 0.3. At 0.2 the four cells (both, A only, B only, neither) would be (0.2, 0.5, 0.4, −0.1), violating nonnegativity. At 0.4 they become (0.4, 0.3, 0.2, 0.1), all nonnegative and summing to one. This example repairs coherence; it does not empirically estimate dependence. Committees: C(8, 3) = 56. Ordered roles: 8 × 7 × 6 = 336, with no repeated analyst. Uniform committee selection gives eight single-specialty committees, C(4, 3) from each specialty. Thus both represented has probability (56 − 8)/56 = 6/7. This probability assumes equal selection weights across committees. Without replacement, exactly two flags has probability C(4, 2)C(6, 1)/C(10, 3) = 36/120 = 0.3. At least one is 1 − C(6, 3)/C(10, 3) = 5/6. Independent replacement draws retain p = 2/5 per draw: exactly two has probability 3(2/5)²(3/5) = 36/125 = 0.288; at least one is 1 − (3/5)³ = 98/125 = 0.784. The first-draw marginal is fixed; dependence and the possibility of repeated files change. The equally likely outcomes 00, 01, 10, 11 give each event mass 1/2. A ∩ B is {11}, A ∩ C is {10}, and B ∩ C is {01}, each with mass 1/4, equal to the corresponding product. The triple intersection is empty, with mass zero instead of (1/2)³ = 1/8. Pairwise checks do not establish the required triple factorization. The union is 0.75 − 0.23 + 0.02 = 0.54. Exactly two is the sum of pair intersections minus three times the triple: 0.23 − 0.06 = 0.17. Exactly one is 0.75 − 2(0.23) + 3(0.02) = 0.35; none is 0.46. The atoms are A only 0.09, B only 0.14, C only 0.12, AB only 0.06, AC only 0.03, BC only 0.08, ABC 0.02, and none 0.46. They sum to one. Pair intersections include the triple and cannot be treated as “exactly two” directly. P(Aᶜ ∩ Bᶜ) = 1 − P(A) − P(B) + P(A ∩ B) = [1 − P(A)][1 − P(B)] under independence. The analogous one-complement identity follows by subtraction from a marginal. Disjointness gives intersection probability zero, inconsistent with a positive product. An event of probability zero is a degenerate exception. For 0.3 and 0.4, neither has probability 0.7 × 0.6 = 0.42. The tail is Σ from n = 4 to infinity of 2⁻ⁿ = (1/16)/(1 − 1/2) = 1/8. Odd outcomes have mass (1/2)Σ from j = 0 to infinity of (1/4)ʲ = 2/3. Any common positive mass makes finite partial totals grow without bound; common zero mass gives total zero by countable additivity. Neither is normalized. Uniform finite truncations are valid experiments, but there is no normalized equal-mass limit law on all positive integers. Teach-back and Delayed check Optional exact-fraction Python laboratory enumerates the small archive experiments and displays the following chapter's Bayes updates. It needs only the standard library; the chapter can be completed without programming. Enumeration checks finite examples rather than proving general probability theorems. Explain why the same archive can yield different sample probabilities under replacement and nonreplacement. Reconstruct the subset count, probability weighting and independence assumptions separately. Give an example where disjointness implies dependence and one where pairwise independence fails to establish mutual independence. After several days, change the archive size, flag count and sample size. Solve the event by both ordered records and unordered subsets, checking that they agree for the same experiment. Audit a proposed three-event table for negative atoms and explain whether the issue is mathematical inconsistency or uncertain empirical inputs. Sources and next steps MIT's counting-and-sets reading offers independent practice with event operations and combinatorial counts. MIT's probability-terminology reading develops discrete weighting and the importance of specifying the sample space. Harvard Statistics 110 provides a broad further-study route in probability. The state model, audit archive and exercises here are original. The next chapter conditions on information and derives Bayes' rule. Conditioning must preserve the event's meaning and the observation mechanism; it cannot repair a sample space or weighting law that was never specified."}, {"title": "03.02 · Conditional probability and Bayes", "url": "v03-c02.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Information changes the reference population Suppose an automated review flags an invoice. The probability of a flag among genuinely misclassified invoices is not the probability of misclassification among flagged invoices. The first conditions on the underlying status; the second conditions on the observed signal. Their denominators describe different groups, often of very different sizes. This chapter derives conditional probability, total probability, Bayes' rule and odds updates. It also explains why evidence from two sources can count as one observation, why conditioning can create dependence, and why a reporting or stopping rule belongs inside the probability model. All numerical rates are synthetic; they illustrate reasoning rather than recommend actual screening or financial decisions. Conditional probability and its domain For events A and B with P(B) > 0, define P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B)/P(B), read “the probability of A given B.” Restrict attention to outcomes in B, then renormalize their original probability weights to sum to one. Uniform weights within B arise only when the underlying weights warrant them; conditioning does not automatically make all surviving outcomes equally likely. The conditional law P B (A) = P(A | B) satisfies the probability axioms: nonnegativity follows from the numerator; P B (Ω) = P(B)/P(B) = 1; and disjoint Aⱼ have disjoint intersections Aⱼ ∩ B, so countable additivity survives division by the same positive denominator. In particular P(Aᶜ | B) = 1 − P(A | B). The complement here belongs to the event being evaluated, while the conditioning event stays fixed. When P(B) = 0 this definition cannot be used: it would divide zero by zero. Conditioning on an exact continuous observation requires tools beyond this event-ratio definition, developed later through densities and conditional expectation. “The conditioning event is improbable” is different from “its probability is zero.” A tiny positive probability remains a mathematically valid denominator, though estimated inputs may then be fragile. Rearranging the definition gives the multiplication rule P(A ∩ B) = P(A | B)P(B). With positive relevant denominators, reversing the order gives P(B | A)P(A). The two products equal the same joint probability; the two conditional probabilities generally do not equal each other. Independence from Chapter 1 is equivalent to P(A | B) = P(A) when P(B) > 0. For a sequence of events E₁, …, Eₙ whose preceding intersections have positive probability, repeated multiplication gives P(E₁ ∩ … ∩ Eₙ) = P(E₁)P(E₂ | E₁)…P(Eₙ | E₁ ∩ … ∩ Eₙ₋₁). This chain rule requires no independence assumption. If a preceding history has probability zero, the complete path probability is zero; omit that path instead of evaluating an undefined conditional and multiplying it by zero. Total probability: sum disjoint routes to the observation Let H₁, …, Hₘ partition Ω, with positive probabilities. Since E is the disjoint union of E ∩ Hⱼ, additivity and the multiplication rule give P(E) = ΣP(E | Hⱼ)P(Hⱼ). This is the law of total probability. For countably many partition cells, countable additivity gives the same sum; zero-probability cells can be omitted rather than assigned undefined ratios. A tree represents successive conditional branches. Multiply probabilities along one path to obtain its joint weight, then add weights of disjoint paths ending in the requested event. Branches leaving a node must sum to one under that node's condition. The likelihoods P(E | Hⱼ) across different hypotheses need not sum to one: they are not alternatives within a single conditioning group. A table provides an equivalent audit. For each hypothesis, multiply its prior probability by the conditional probability of each possible observation. Every joint cell must be nonnegative; its row and column totals must agree with the marginals. Bayes' rule then reads a conditional column instead of a conditional row. Drawing the tree and filling the table should produce the same result. Bayes' rule is a normalization identity For P(H) > 0 and P(E) > 0, the joint probability has two descriptions: P(H ∩ E) = P(E | H)P(H) = P(H | E)P(E). Division gives P(H | E) = P(E | H)P(H)/P(E). Combining it with total probability over the hypothesis partition yields P(Hⱼ | E) = πⱼLⱼ/ΣπₖLₖ, where πⱼ = P(Hⱼ) and Lⱼ = P(E | Hⱼ). The prior πⱼ is the probability before the new evidence; the likelihood Lⱼ is the probability of that evidence under the hypothesis; the posterior is the updated probability after the evidence. The denominator is the prior-weighted probability of the evidence, sometimes called its marginal probability. The likelihood is not the posterior, and summing likelihoods without their prior weights does not supply the denominator. In the finite-hypothesis case the update can be reconstructed in three steps: compute each nonnegative weight πⱼLⱼ; sum them; divide each by that positive sum. The posterior weights then sum to one. Normalization checks coherence within the stated model; it does not prove that the hypotheses are exhaustive or their likelihoods realistic. Excluding a plausible “other” state can make a polished posterior calculation misleading. A zero-prior event has zero joint probability with any evidence and therefore remains zero after conditioning on positive-probability evidence. Its elementary conditional P(E | H) is itself undefined when P(H) = 0. A statistical model may separately specify a hypothetical data law for such a hypothesis, but multiplying its model likelihood by zero still cannot revive it. Distinguish genuine impossibility from a prior that merely encodes very strong skepticism; the latter should not be made zero casually. Worked example 1 — A rare invoice error and a positive flag Let H mean an invoice is genuinely misclassified. Its prior probability is π = 0.02. A review system flags 85% of misclassified invoices and 4% of correctly classified invoices: P(+ | H) = 0.85 and P(+ | Hᶜ) = 0.04. The plus sign denotes the flag event, not a numerical addition. These hypothetical rates are assumed applicable to the same reference population. Total flag probability is 0.85(0.02) + 0.04(0.98) = 0.0562. Bayes gives P(H | +) = 0.017/0.0562 = 85/281 ≈ 0.302491. A flag raises the probability from 2% to about 30.25%; it does not make it 85%. The flag's sensitivity conditions on genuine errors, while the posterior conditions on all flags. Synthetic reference count Misclassified H Correct Hᶜ Total Flag + 170 392 562 No flag − 30 9,408 9,438 Total 200 9,800 10,000 The counts scale the model probabilities by 10,000; they are not observed sample evidence or an estimate of sampling error. Among flags, 170/562 are genuinely misclassified. Among nonflags, 30/9,438 = 5/1,573 ≈ 0.003179 are misclassified. Overall correct classification is (170 + 9,408)/10,000 = 95.78%, but overall accuracy is not the positive flag's posterior reliability. The 4% false-positive rate means flags among correct invoices. It differs from P(Hᶜ | +) = 196/281 ≈ 69.75%, the share of correct invoices among flags. Confusing the two conditionings is a base-rate error. With the same assumed sensitivity and false-positive rate but π = 0.10, the positive posterior is 85/121 ≈ 70.25%. At π = 0.001, it is 85/4,081 ≈ 2.08%. Changes in the reference population change the result. Writing sensitivity s and false-positive rate α, the positive posterior is sπ/[sπ + α(1 − π)]. With positive s and α, its derivative with respect to π is sα/[α + (s − α)π]² > 0. It exceeds one half precisely when π > α/(s + α), here 4/89 ≈ 4.49%. This is a posterior threshold, not an optimal-action rule. Choosing whether to investigate also needs the consequences and costs of the alternatives. Odds and likelihood ratios For an event H with probability p strictly between zero and one, its odds against Hᶜ are O = p/(1 − p). Conversely p = O/(1 + O). Odds of 3 to 1 mean probability 3/4, not 3. Probability is bounded by one; finite odds are nonnegative and unbounded. Endpoints p = 0 and 1 require the corresponding zero or infinite-odds interpretation. For evidence E with positive likelihood under both H and Hᶜ, divide the two Bayes formulas. Their common denominator cancels: posterior odds = prior odds × [P(E | H)/P(E | Hᶜ)]. The ratio in brackets is the likelihood ratio or, for these specified competing models, Bayes factor. A ratio above one favors H relative to Hᶜ; below one favors the alternative. Its magnitude measures relative evidence within the model, not the posterior probability itself. In the invoice example, prior odds are 1/49. A positive flag has ratio 0.85/0.04 = 85/4 = 21.25. Posterior odds are 85/196, which convert to 85/281. A nonflag has ratio 0.15/0.96 = 5/32; its posterior odds are 5/1,568, converting to 5/1,573. A negative signal has its own likelihood ratio; it is not obtained by subtracting the positive ratio from one. If evidence has positive likelihood under H and zero under Hᶜ, with both prior probabilities positive, it rules out Hᶜ within that model and yields posterior one. If both likelihoods are zero, the evidence has zero marginal probability and this update is undefined. That observation contradicts the specified model, rather than demonstrating certainty. Check zero denominators before treating an infinite or indeterminate ratio as a usable answer. Odds comparisons also work between two members of a larger exhaustive hypothesis partition: posterior ratio P(Hᵢ | E)/P(Hⱼ | E) = (πᵢ/πⱼ)(Lᵢ/Lⱼ), when the denominators are positive. Other hypotheses affect normalization into probabilities, even though they cancel from this pairwise ratio. When a hypothesis combines several latent states, its evidence likelihood depends on their specified mixture; it is not automatically the likelihood of one convenient representative. Sequential evidence and conditional independence For two observations E₁ and E₂, update first on E₁, then on E₂ using P(E₂ | H, E₁) and P(E₂ | Hᶜ, E₁). Here commas abbreviate intersection conditions. The total evidence ratio factors into P(E₁ | H)/P(E₁ | Hᶜ) times P(E₂ | H, E₁)/P(E₂ | Hᶜ, E₁). This factorization follows from the chain rule, without independence. Only if E₂ and E₁ are independent conditional on each hypothesis can the second ratio use the original marginal likelihoods P(E₂ | H) and P(E₂ | Hᶜ). Conditional independence means P(E₁ ∩ E₂ | H) = P(E₁ | H)P(E₂ | H), with the analogous equality under Hᶜ. It must hold under both competing conditions for that simple odds multiplication. Repeating the same report is not a new independent observation. For many conditionally independent observations, odds multiply by their likelihood ratios. With positive finite ratios, log odds equal prior log odds plus the sum of log ratios. This additive form makes evidence accumulation transparent and can reduce numerical overflow or underflow. Correlated signals require their joint or history-dependent likelihoods, not an arbitrary discount to a mistaken independent calculation. Worked example 2 — Shared lot quality and two flags A supplier lot is poor with prior probability 1/4 and good with probability 3/4. Two separate tests produce flag events E₁ and E₂. Given lot quality, the tests are independent, each flagging with probability 0.6 in a poor lot and 0.2 in a good lot. The status persists across both tests; it is not redrawn between them. Two flags have conditional likelihoods 0.6² = 0.36 and 0.2² = 0.04. Their marginal probability is (1/4)(0.36) + (3/4)(0.04) = 0.12. Posterior poor-lot probability is 0.09/0.12 = 3/4. The same calculation in odds gives prior 1/3 times Bayes factor 9, producing odds 3 and probability 3/4. A single flag has marginal probability (1/4)(0.6) + (3/4)(0.2) = 0.30 and poor-lot posterior 1/2. After that first flag, multiply odds 1 by another conditional ratio 3 to obtain the two-flag posterior. Conditional independence allows this step; unconditional independence would incorrectly predict P(E₁ ∩ E₂) = 0.30² = 0.09 rather than 0.12. Shared uncertain quality makes the observed flags dependent in the population. The next independent test's predictive flag probability, after both flags, is (3/4)(0.6) + (1/4)(0.2) = 0.50 by total probability under the posterior weights. This predicts an observable signal; it differs from the posterior probability 0.75 that the underlying lot is poor. Now suppose the second “flag” is a copied report of the first test, so E₂ = E₁. The joint likelihoods are then 0.6 and 0.2, not their squares. Once E₁ is known, P(E₂ | H, E₁) = P(E₂ | Hᶜ, E₁) = 1. The second report's incremental evidence ratio is one; the posterior stays at 1/2. Identical text from two outlets does not supply two independent measurements of the same underlying state. Conditioning can remove or create dependence The lot example shows conditional independence need not imply unconditional independence. The reverse also fails. Let two independent fair binary events A and B represent separate checks, and let C mean at least one check passes. Conditional on C, the possible records are 10, 01 and 11, equally weighted. P(A | C) = P(B | C) = 2/3, while P(A ∩ B | C) = 1/3, not 4/9. The events became dependent through selection. The reason is logical: among records selected for having at least one pass, a failed first check guarantees the second passed. Neither check changed the other's physical mechanism. The conditioning event selected a group in which their relationship differs. Later causal inference will study such selection effects formally; here the joint table already exposes the error in assuming independence survives all conditioning. Likewise P(Y | X) is a conditional association, not automatically a causal effect of setting X. A shared cause, reverse direction or selected observation group can produce the association. Bayes' rule is a correct identity inside a joint probability model; it does not convert an observational conditional into an intervention model. The evidence includes how it was observed “A test was positive,” “the first test was positive,” “at least one of two tests was positive,” and “testing continued until a positive result” are different events. Their likelihoods can differ. Define the actual evidence before calculating. If a reporting rule selects among outcomes, include that rule in the experiment rather than treating the reported observation as a uniformly chosen record. For example, with independent tests having flag probability p, the event of at least one flag in two tests has probability 1 − (1 − p)². Its likelihood is not p. If only that aggregate event is reported, use its aggregate likelihood. If the full ordered pattern is observed, use the probability of that pattern. Recording less information can change an update even though the underlying tests are unchanged. A fixed stopping rule provides another distinction. Repeating independent tests until the first flag occurs at test n has likelihood (1 − p)ⁿ⁻¹p. Reporting only that a flag eventually appeared has probability one for any fixed p > 0: the probability of no flag through n tests is (1 − p)ⁿ, tending to zero. The stopping time carries information; the bare eventual-positive report carries none for comparing two such positive-p models. This elementary observation does not settle every statistical stopping-rule debate; it identifies which event is being conditioned on. A report-generating rule can sometimes have the same conditional behavior under all hypotheses, given the recorded data; that common factor then cancels from posterior odds. It does not always cancel. Work with the actual joint likelihood to establish cancellation instead of deciding in advance that observation selection is irrelevant. Assumptions, sensitivity and decisions Every update should state its reference population, hypothesis partition, prior, evidence definition and likelihood assumptions. Sensitivity analysis changes questionable priors or likelihoods and recomputes the result. It asks how conclusions depend on inputs; it is not automatically a probability interval describing uncertainty about a parameter. Later Bayesian inference will distinguish those objects. Rates learned in one population need not transfer unchanged to another. A higher prevalence can coincide with different case severity, measurement quality or selection, so holding sensitivity and false-positive rates fixed is an explicit transport assumption. The arithmetic can be exact while that assumption is wrong. Estimated rates also have sampling error; this chapter's stipulated-rate examples postpone estimation until the sampling chapters. Finally a posterior probability does not choose an action by itself. An investigation, investment or policy decision needs a loss or utility model, constraints and alternatives. First update the probability coherently; then connect it to consequences with the later decision chapters. Reporting a probability as a certainty or an automatic recommendation bypasses an essential step. Exercises Select one integer uniformly from 1 through 12. Let A mean even and B mean divisible by 3. Compute P(A | B), P(B | A) and the joint probability; check independence. Explain why the two conditional values need not equal each other even when the events are independent. A synthetic review has prior error rate 0.03, sensitivity 0.90 and false-positive rate 0.06. Construct a joint table scaled to 10,000 records. Compute error probability after a flag and after a nonflag, then confirm the positive update in odds form. Distinguish overall accuracy from flag reliability. Three exhaustive hypotheses have priors 1/2, 1/3 and 1/6. Evidence likelihoods are 1/5, 1/2 and 4/5. Calculate the evidence probability and all posterior probabilities. Explain why the likelihoods are not required to sum to one. In Worked example 2, observe a flag on the first test and a nonflag on the second. Find the poor-lot posterior and the next flag's predictive probability. Would reporting only “exactly one flag in the two tests” change this posterior under the specified model? Compare two positive reports in Worked example 2 when they arise from separate conditionally independent tests versus an exact duplicate of the first test. Write the joint likelihood ratio and the second report's incremental likelihood ratio in each case. For independent fair binary events A and B, condition on C = A ∪ B. Compute both conditional marginals and their joint, then determine whether A and B remain independent conditional on C. Explain the selection mechanism in words. A model has prior error rate 0.02 and sensitivity 0.80, but its false-positive rate α is only defended within [0.01, 0.05]. Find the range of the positive posterior and the α threshold for that posterior to exceed one half. Explain why this sensitivity range is not automatically a credible interval. Two hypotheses have equal priors and independently repeated tests with flag probabilities 0.4 and 0.2, respectively. Testing stops at the first flag. Update on the full observation that stopping occurred at test 3. Compare with observing a flag on a prespecified first test and with being told only that a flag eventually occurred. Solutions, normalization and evidence mechanisms A has six elements, B four, and their intersection {6, 12} two. Thus P(A | B) = 2/4 = 1/2, P(B | A) = 2/6 = 1/3 and P(A ∩ B) = 1/6 = (1/2)(1/3). They are independent. Each conditional equals the corresponding event's marginal, not the other event's marginal. Different base group sizes explain the unequal reversed conditionals. The table has 300 errors and 9,700 correct records. Flags comprise 270 errors and 582 correct records, total 852; nonflags comprise 30 errors and 9,118 correct records, total 9,148. Positive posterior is 270/852 = 45/142 ≈ 0.316901; negative posterior is 30/9,148 = 15/4,574 ≈ 0.003279. Prior odds 3/97 times likelihood ratio 0.90/0.06 = 15 gives odds 45/97 and probability 45/142. Overall accuracy is (270 + 9,118)/10,000 = 0.9388, a different denominator and question. Unnormalized weights are 1/10, 1/6 and 2/15. Their sum is 2/5. Dividing gives posteriors 1/4, 5/12 and 1/3, summing to one. The likelihoods describe the same evidence under different conditions; they are not disjoint alternatives under one common condition. Within each hypothesis, probabilities of a complete set of possible evidence outcomes must sum to one. Ordered likelihoods are 0.6(0.4) = 0.24 under poor quality and 0.2(0.8) = 0.16 under good quality. Prior-weighted masses are 0.06 and 0.12, so posterior poor is 1/3. The next flag probability is (1/3)(0.6) + (2/3)(0.2) = 1/3. For exactly one flag, either order is possible and doubles both likelihoods; the common factor cancels, leaving the same posterior. This invariance relies on the specified identical conditional test rates and conditional independence; it need not hold for different tests. Separate tests have joint likelihood ratio 0.36/0.04 = 9 and incremental second ratio 0.6/0.2 = 3, yielding posterior 3/4. A duplicate has joint ratio 0.6/0.2 = 3 and incremental ratio 1/1 = 1, yielding posterior 1/2. Under the duplicate mechanism, the second report is certain once the first flag is known; counting it as a new test invents evidence. P(C) = 3/4. P(A | C) = P(B | C) = (1/2)/(3/4) = 2/3, and P(A ∩ B | C) = (1/4)/(3/4) = 1/3. Since 1/3 ≠ 4/9, conditional independence fails. Removing outcome 00 means a failed first check forces a passed second check among retained observations, even though the original checks were independent. The posterior is 0.016/[0.016 + 0.98α], decreasing in α. At α = 0.05 it is 16/65 ≈ 0.246154; at α = 0.01 it is 80/129 ≈ 0.620155. Therefore the defended input range yields posterior range [16/65, 80/129]. It exceeds one half when α < 0.016/0.98 = 4/245 ≈ 0.016327; equality gives one half. The endpoints come from varying a stipulated likelihood input, with no probability distribution assigned over α. They are sensitivity bounds, not a posterior credible interval for α. Stopping at test 3 means two nonflags followed by a flag. Likelihoods are (0.6)²(0.4) = 0.144 and (0.8)²(0.2) = 0.128. Equal priors give high-flag hypothesis posterior 0.144/(0.144 + 0.128) = 9/17 ≈ 0.529412. A flag on a prespecified first test instead gives 0.4/(0.4 + 0.2) = 2/3. Eventual appearance has probability one under both positive-p models, so it leaves the equal priors at 1/2. The denominator and likelihood must match the observation actually reported. Teach-back and Delayed check The optional exact-fraction laboratory reproduces the invoice and separate-versus-duplicate updates from specified priors and likelihoods. It reports rational probabilities rather than simulated frequencies. Input validation enforces normalization and rejects zero-probability evidence; it cannot determine whether the chosen model fits actual observations. Explain Bayes' rule using both a joint table and posterior odds. Identify the reference group for a false-positive rate and for the share of false positives among flags. Then explain why two conditionally independent signals can be dependent overall and why a copied report adds no evidence. After several days, change the invoice base rate and one likelihood. Reconstruct the joint table and odds update without notes. Compare a fully recorded test sequence with a selected report of it, and state which additional likelihood information is needed before updating. Include a zero-probability conditioning case where the elementary formula is undefined. Sources and next steps MIT's conditional-probability reading supplies independent practice in total probability, Bayes' rule and base-rate interpretation. MIT's odds-update reading provides a further route to likelihood-ratio calculations. Harvard Statistics 110 links to further probability instruction and practice. This chapter's invoice, lot-quality, sensitivity and stopping-rule problems are original. Next we represent numerical outcomes as random variables and specify their distributions. Later chapters estimate uncertain likelihood inputs, evaluate sampling evidence and combine posterior distributions with explicit loss functions."}, {"title": "03.03 · Random variables and distributions", "url": "v03-c03.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "From uncertain states to numerical outcomes An experiment may produce a long record, but a decision often uses a number extracted from that record: how many files were flagged, how long settlement took, or what a position paid. A random variable is the rule that extracts that number. Its distribution describes how probability is allocated across the resulting values. The rule and its distribution are different objects: several state descriptions can produce the same distribution. This chapter develops probability mass functions, densities and cumulative distribution functions, then derives five common distribution families. The models are stipulated mathematical examples. Choosing a familiar distribution does not establish that it fits an actual economic process. State the experiment, support, parameters and dependence assumptions before calculating. Random variables, events and support A real random variable X is a function from outcomes Ω to real numbers. For each real x, the set {ω: X(ω) ≤ x}, abbreviated {X ≤ x}, must be an event whose probability is defined. This requirement is measurability; Chapter 12 gives its general sigma-algebra formulation. On a finite outcome space where every subset is an event, every real-valued function satisfies it. “Random” describes uncertainty about the realized outcome, not uncertainty about how the function is defined. For example, if an outcome is an ordered pair of reviewed files, X can count flagged files. Distinct pairs can yield the same count. P(X = 1) adds the probabilities of all pairs yielding one flag. It does not assign equal weights to the three possible counts merely because there are three of them. The probability law on outcomes must be carried through the function. The law or distribution of X assigns probabilities to events determined by its values. X and Y have the same law if those probabilities agree for every interval, but they may behave differently together. Two independent draws can share a law; two copies of the same draw also share it. Their joint behavior is different and cannot be recovered from the separate laws. The support identifies where the law lives. In a discrete model, the possible values with positive mass give the effective range, and their closure gives the topological support. More generally a real value is in that support when every open neighborhood around it has positive probability. Thus endpoints of a continuous interval can belong to the support despite each having probability zero. Writing a density as zero outside a specified range is a useful practical description; do not infer that every point inside has positive point probability. Mass functions and cumulative distributions A discrete law is concentrated on a finite or countable set S. Its probability mass function, or PMF, is p X (x) = P(X = x). It satisfies p X (x) ≥ 0 and Σ x∈S p X (x) = 1. Conversely such weights define a discrete law. For a set A of values, P(X ∈ A) is the sum of the masses at values in A. A PMF value is a probability, and is dimensionless. The cumulative distribution function, or CDF, works for every real random variable: F X (x) = P(X ≤ x). It is nondecreasing, lies in [0, 1], tends to zero as x tends to −∞ and to one as x tends to +∞, and is right-continuous. In a discrete model it is Σ t≤x p X (t), including the mass at the threshold. A flat segment means no probability falls between its endpoints; a jump records an atom of the law. These properties follow from the event rules in Chapter 1. If a ≤ b, {X ≤ a} is contained in {X ≤ b}. The events {X ≤ n} increase to Ω, whereas {X ≤ −n} decrease to the empty set because X takes finite real values. Probability continuity gives the two limits. For right-continuity, {X ≤ x + 1/n} decreases to {X ≤ x}; the probabilities therefore tend to F(x). Monotonicity extends that result to arbitrary thresholds approaching x from above. Define the left limit F(x−) = lim t↑x F(t). The events {X ≤ x − 1/n} increase to {X < x}, so F(x−) = P(X < x). Consequently the jump F(x) − F(x−) equals P(X = x). For a ≤ b, P(a < X ≤ b) = F(b) − F(a), P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = F(b) − F(a−), P(X > b) = 1 − F(b). The different endpoint conventions matter whenever the law has atoms. An interval probability cannot be negative. Checking the ordering of its endpoints and the corresponding CDF subtraction is a quick way to catch a reversed calculation. Densities are probability per unit of the variable An absolutely continuous law has a probability density function, or PDF, f, with f(x) ≥ 0 and ∫ −∞ ∞ f(x) dx = 1, such that interval probabilities equal density integrals. Then F(x) = ∫ −∞ x f(t) dt. At points where f is continuous, the fundamental theorem of calculus gives F′(x) = f(x). The value assigned to a density at one isolated point does not change any probability. A density value is not P(X = x). Every exact point has probability zero under an absolutely continuous law because an integral over a singleton is zero. For small widths h, f(x)h approximates the probability of a short interval when the density is locally smooth. If X is measured in days, its density has units per day. The product of height and interval width is dimensionless, as probability must be. A density may exceed one. Uniform X on [0, 0.2] has f(x) = 5 on that interval and zero elsewhere; its integral is 5(0.2) = 1. Interpreting f(0.1) = 5 as a 500% point probability is an error. The probability of [0.10, 0.12] is 5(0.02) = 0.10. “No atoms” is weaker than “has a density”: there are singular continuous laws, studied in the formal foundations. A law may also mix atoms with a density. Its CDF still describes all its probabilities even when no single ordinary PDF or countable PMF does so. This is one reason the CDF is the most general of these three representations. Bernoulli and binomial: indicators and repeated trials A Bernoulli random variable takes value 1 with probability p and 0 with probability 1 − p, for 0 ≤ p ≤ 1. The indicator I A of any event A is Bernoulli with p = P(A). One indicator requires no independence assumption. Its CDF is zero below 0, 1 − p from 0 up to but excluding 1, and one from 1 onward. At p = 0 or 1 the variable is constant. Let X count successes in n independent Bernoulli trials with the same probability p, where n is a nonnegative integer. For 0 < p < 1, an ordered sequence with k successes has probability p k (1 − p) n−k . There are C(n, k) such sequences. Thus X has the binomial law P(X = k) = C(n, k)p k (1 − p) n−k , k = 0, …, n. The binomial theorem gives total mass [p + (1 − p)] n = 1. At p = 0 all mass is at 0; at p = 1 all mass is at n. These direct descriptions avoid relying on an ambiguous written 0⁰. With n = 0 the empty count is 0 with certainty. A negative or fractional number of trials does not define this model. Independence and a common success probability are substantive requirements. Sampling without replacement from a finite population generally violates independence. Independent trials with changing success probabilities also generally have a different count law. A success label, such as “error” or “default,” does not by itself make its count binomial. Worked example 1 — Two files without replacement Eight distinct files contain three flagged files and five clean files. Choose an unordered pair uniformly from the C(8, 2) = 28 distinct pairs. X is the number of flagged files in the pair. For k = 0, 1, 2, choose k of the three flagged files and 2 − k of the five clean files. The respective counts are 10, 15 and 3, summing to 28. k Pair count P(X = k) 0 10 5/14 1 15 15/28 2 3 3/28 The CDF is zero for x < 0, 5/14 for 0 ≤ x < 1, 25/28 for 1 ≤ x < 2, and one for x ≥ 2. Therefore P(X ≥ 1) = 1 − F(0) = 9/14. At x = 1, F(1) includes both zero and one flag; the point probability is F(1) − F(1−) = 15/28. If instead we draw twice with replacement using fresh independent uniform randomization, the count is binomial with n = 2 and p = 3/8. Its masses are 25/64, 30/64 and 9/64. They differ from the without-replacement masses. In the original experiment a flagged first draw leaves only two flagged files among seven remaining files. In the replacement experiment its flag probability stays 3/8. The sampling mechanism changes the law even though the original population is the same. Poisson: a nonnegative integer count For λ > 0, the Poisson law assigns P(X = k) = e −λ λ k /k! to k = 0, 1, 2, …. The exponential series Σλ k /k! = e λ proves normalization. At λ = 0 define X = 0 with certainty. For λ > 0, adjacent masses satisfy p(k + 1) = p(k)λ/(k + 1), useful for calculating a table without repeatedly evaluating factorials. The parameter λ is dimensionless. In a model with a constant arrival rate r per day observed for t days, λ = rt is an integrated intensity. A rate is not a probability and can exceed one per day. A Poisson count model for one interval does not by itself specify independent counts across intervals or exponential waiting times. Those require a stochastic process model with additional assumptions. The law can arise as a rare-event limit. Take independent trials with success probability p = λ/n, for n large enough that p ≤ 1, and keep λ fixed. For each fixed nonnegative integer k, C(n, k)(λ/n) k (1 − λ/n) n−k = (λ k /k!) ∏ j=0 k−1 (1 − j/n) · (1 − λ/n) n−k → e −λ λ k /k!. The finite product tends to one. For λ > 0, use log(1 − u) = −u + O(u²) near zero: (n − k)log(1 − λ/n) tends to −λ. Exponentiating gives the other factor. The λ = 0 case is already degenerate. This derivation gives a limiting probability at each fixed k, not an exact identity for a finite n or a certified approximation error for arbitrary dependent events. Exponential: a constant-hazard waiting model An exponential waiting time T with rate r > 0 has density re −rt for t ≥ 0 and zero for t < 0. Integration gives total mass one and CDF F(t) = 0 for t < 0, and 1 − e −rt for t ≥ 0. Its survival probability is P(T > t) = e −rt for nonnegative t. The rate has units inverse to time; a quoted rate must include those units. For s, t ≥ 0 the event {T > s + t} is contained in {T > s}. Conditioning gives P(T > s + t | T > s) = e −r(s+t) /e −rs = e −rt = P(T > t). This is memorylessness: conditional on still waiting at age s, the remaining waiting time has the original law. It follows from this model, not from the mere fact that a variable is a waiting time. The instantaneous hazard is f(t)/[1 − F(t)] = r: conditional arrival probability over a short additional interval h is approximately rh. Over a full interval of length h it is exactly 1 − e −rh , which differs from rh. Worked example 2 — A stipulated processing-time law Suppose T, measured in days, has an exponential rate of 0.25 per day. The model implies P(T ≤ 4) = 1 − e −1 ≈ 0.632121. It implies P(2 < T ≤ 6) = F(6) − F(2) = e −0.5 − e −1.5 ≈ 0.383400. This interval probability is an area, rather than the density at its midpoint. After six days without completion, the probability of still waiting beyond day ten is P(T > 10 | T > 6) = e −2.5 /e −1.5 = e −1 ≈ 0.367879. It equals the original probability of waiting more than four days. It is not the unconditional probability of exceeding ten days. The median m solves 1 − e −0.25m = 1/2, so m = 4 log 2 ≈ 2.772589 days. Half the probability lies below this threshold, although P(T = m) = 0. A real queue with changing workload or scheduled processing may have a different hazard. The calculation describes the assumed law; it supplies no evidence that this law fits a particular queue. The normal family and standardization A normal random variable, written X ∼ N(μ, σ²), has location μ ∈ ℝ, scale σ > 0 and density f(x) = [1/(σ√(2π))] exp[−(x − μ)²/(2σ²)], −∞ < x < ∞. The notation uses σ² as its second parameter. The next chapter verifies that μ is its mean and σ² its variance. The exponent is dimensionless because x, μ and σ have the same units. The density's units are the inverse of those units. This nondegenerate formula excludes σ = 0; a point mass at μ is a separate limiting law. Why the normal density integrates to one Let J R = ∫ −R R e −x²/2 dx. Over the square [−R, R]², the integral of e −(x²+y²)/2 is J R ². To justify this bounded-domain product, choose a rectangular mesh: its double Riemann sum factors into the product of the two one-dimensional sums. Continuity makes their limits the respective integrals. No exchange of unbounded signed integrals is being assumed. On a disk of radius a, the same integrand depends only on radius. Partition the disk into thin annuli with radii r j and r j+1 . Its decreasing radial value on an annulus lies between its endpoint values, and that annulus has area π(r j+1 ² − r j ²). Upper and lower sums squeeze its integral to ∫ 0 a 2πr e −r²/2 dr = 2π(1 − e −a²/2 ). Continuity and a mesh tending to zero justify the squeeze. This supplies the needed radial area calculation directly. The disk of radius R lies inside the square, which lies inside the disk of radius √2R. The integrand is nonnegative, so 2π(1 − e −R²/2 ) ≤ J R ² ≤ 2π(1 − e −R² ). Both bounds tend to 2π. Since J R is positive, its limit is √(2π). Thus φ(z) = e −z²/2 /√(2π) integrates to one on ℝ. The substitution z = (x − μ)/σ, dx = σ dz, proves normalization for every σ > 0. The argument uses bounded continuous integrals, elementary circle areas and limits from Volume 2; more general integration theorems come later. For Z = (X − μ)/σ, the change of scale gives the standard normal law N(0, 1). Define Φ(z) = ∫ −∞ z φ(t) dt. There is no elementary antiderivative for φ; the integral definition is nevertheless an exact formula, and numerical quadrature can approximate it. Evenness of φ gives Φ(−z) = 1 − Φ(z). For a ≤ b, P(a ≤ X ≤ b) = Φ((b − μ)/σ) − Φ((a − μ)/σ). Endpoint inclusion makes no difference here because the law has no atoms. Its support is all ℝ, so it allocates some probability to negative values regardless of a positive location. For a quantity that must be nonnegative, this may be an unacceptable model or an approximation requiring a demonstrated small negative tail. Normality of a variable, normality of a transformed variable and a later normal approximation for a statistic are separate claims. Quantiles, transformations and mixed laws For 0 < u < 1, define a quantile Q(u) = inf{x: F(x) ≥ u}. The CDF limits ensure that this set is nonempty and bounded below, so the quantile is finite. Monotonicity and right-continuity imply F(Q(u)) ≥ u: points in the defining set can be chosen approaching its infimum from above, and right-continuity preserves the inequality. At an atom, F(Q(u)) can exceed u. A quantile is therefore not always a solution of F(x) = u. For a continuous strictly increasing CDF on its support, ordinary inversion is available. If Y = g(X), start from P(Y ≤ y) = P(g(X) ≤ y). In a discrete model, add the masses of all x mapped to each y. If g is strictly increasing, its inverse transforms thresholds in the expected direction; if it is decreasing, the direction reverses and atoms require attention to strict inequalities. For a continuous density and a differentiable one-to-one transformation with nonzero derivative, the density is f Y (y) = f X (g −1 (y)) |(g −1 )′(y)|. This follows by differentiating the transformed CDF; the absolute value handles reversed orientation. Non-one-to-one transformations require separate branches or the direct event calculation. Consider a mixed model that sets X = 0 with probability 0.2 and otherwise draws X uniformly on [1, 2], with probability 0.8. Its CDF is zero below 0; 0.2 for 0 ≤ x < 1; 0.2 + 0.8(x − 1) for 1 ≤ x ≤ 2; and one above 2. There is a jump of 0.2 at zero and continuous probability on [1, 2]. Integrating the density 0.8 on [1, 2] captures only 0.8 of the probability. An ordinary PDF cannot represent the atom. Keep its discrete and continuous components explicitly, or use the CDF. A model specification should name its units and parameterization, check total probability, and identify which outcomes are possible. Distribution parameters are assumed known here. Estimating them, diagnosing model fit and assessing approximation error are later tasks. A distribution alone also does not define the consequences of actions or the dependence among different economic quantities. Exercises X takes values −1, 0 and 2 with probabilities 0.2, 0.5 and 0.3. Write its PMF and piecewise CDF. Compute P(−1 < X ≤ 2), P(X < 0) and P(X ≤ 0). Explain the jump at zero. Four independent checks each flag with probability 0.12. Give the count law, prove its normalization and compute probabilities of exactly one flag and at least one flag. Explain why n = 4.5 is not a permissible alternative binomial trial count. A count has a Poisson law with λ = 1.5. Find probabilities of zero, exactly two and at least two. Give the limiting degenerate law at λ = 0. Does this marginal count law alone establish independent counts in successive intervals? Let f(x) = cx on [0, 2] and zero elsewhere. Find c, the CDF, P(0.5 < X ≤ 1.5), the median and P(X = 1). Distinguish density height from point probability. T has exponential rate 0.4 per day. Find P(T > 3), P(T > 5 | T > 2) and its 90th-percentile time. Compare the two survival probabilities when T instead is uniform on [0, 10] days; is that law memoryless? X ∼ N(100, 15²), in specified units. Use Φ(1) = 0.841344746, Φ(−1) = 0.158655254 and Φ(2) = 0.977249868 to compute P(85 ≤ X ≤ 115) and P(X > 130). State its second parameter and explain why exact endpoint probabilities are zero. For the mixed law with mass 0.2 at zero and weight 0.8 on a uniform [1, 2] draw, write the CDF. Find P(1 < X ≤ 1.5), P(X ≤ 1), P(X ≥ 1) and P(X = 1). Explain why a single ordinary PDF fails to describe the complete law. Let f(x) = 1/x² for x ≥ 1 and zero elsewhere. Prove normalization, derive its CDF, find its 99th percentile and P(X > 10). Does the weighted integral ∫ 1 ∞ x f(x) dx converge? Explain what normalization does and does not guarantee. Solutions and model boundaries The PMF has masses 1/5, 1/2 and 3/10 at −1, 0 and 2, and zero elsewhere. F(x) is 0 below −1, 1/5 on [−1, 0), 7/10 on [0, 2), and 1 at or above 2. The interval probability is 1 − 1/5 = 4/5; the strict negative probability is 1/5; and the nonpositive probability is 7/10. The jump at zero has size 7/10 − 1/5 = 1/2, exactly its point mass. Omitting the left-limit distinction would confuse the last two answers. The law is binomial (4, 3/25). Sum C(4, k)(3/25) k (22/25) 4−k over k = 0, …, 4; the binomial theorem gives [(3 + 22)/25]⁴ = 1. Exactly one has probability 4(3/25)(22/25)³ = 127,776/390,625 = 0.32710656. At least one has probability 1 − (22/25)⁴ = 156,369/390,625 = 0.40030464. A trial count is an integer count of events; 4.5 does not give this experiment or the stated support. Independence and identical flag probabilities are both assumed. P(X = 0) = e −1.5 ≈ 0.223130. P(X = 2) = e −1.5 (1.5)²/2 = (9/8)e −1.5 ≈ 0.251021. P(X ≥ 2) = 1 − P(X = 0) − P(X = 1) = 1 − (5/2)e −1.5 ≈ 0.442175. At λ = 0 the count is zero almost surely. Specifying one count's law does not supply a joint law for successive intervals, so independence has not been established. Normalization requires c∫ 0 2 x dx = 2c = 1, hence c = 1/2. F(x) = 0 for x < 0, x²/4 for 0 ≤ x ≤ 2, and 1 for x > 2. The interval probability is (1.5² − 0.5²)/4 = 1/2. The median solves m²/4 = 1/2 with m in [0, 2], giving √2. P(X = 1) = 0, although the density there is 1/2 per unit of X. Density describes local interval probability per width. Both exponential survival probabilities equal e −1.2 ≈ 0.301194. The 90th percentile satisfies e −0.4t = 0.1, so t = log(10)/0.4 ≈ 5.756463 days. For the uniform law, P(T > 3) = 7/10, but P(T > 5 | T > 2) = (5/10)/(8/10) = 5/8. They differ: knowing that some of the bounded waiting interval has elapsed changes the remaining-time law. The standardized thresholds are −1 and 1, so the central interval probability is 0.841344746 − 0.158655254 = 0.682689492. The upper threshold 130 standardizes to 2, yielding tail probability 1 − 0.977249868 = 0.022750132. The second parameter is 225 in squared units, while the scale is 15 in original units. A finite density integrated over an exact point has zero area. Including either endpoint leaves these interval probabilities unchanged. F(x) = 0 below 0; 0.2 on [0, 1); 0.2 + 0.8(x − 1) on [1, 2]; and 1 above 2. The requested probabilities are 0.4, 0.2, 0.8 and 0 respectively. Although 1 is a support endpoint, the continuous component places no point mass there. Any ordinary density integrates to zero at {0}, so it cannot capture the positive atom 0.2; retaining only its continuous part loses probability. ∫ 1 R x −2 dx = 1 − 1/R tends to 1. F(x) is 0 below 1 and 1 − 1/x at or above 1. Solving F(q) = 0.99 gives q = 100; P(X > 10) = 1/10. But ∫ 1 R x·x −2 dx = log R grows without bound. A valid probability law need not have a finite weighted average of its numerical outcomes. The next chapter makes the relevant moment conditions explicit. Teach-back and Delayed check Explain a PMF, a PDF and a CDF using the same interval-probability question. Include a density greater than one and an atom at zero. Explain why neither a density height nor a median is a point probability. Then describe the assumptions needed to turn repeated success indicators into a binomial count. After several days, reconstruct the file-pair count law and its CDF from the experiment rather than from memorized masses. Change the population composition and compare sampling with and without replacement. Derive the exponential survival ratio and standardize a new normal interval. Finish by giving a normalized law whose weighted first-moment integral is infinite. Sources and next steps MIT's discrete-random-variable reading is a further route to PMFs, CDFs and Bernoulli/binomial models. MIT's continuous-random-variable reading supports further practice with densities, CDFs and probability areas. MIT's distribution gallery supplies exponential and normal parameterization practice. The normalization proof and all problems here are original presentations. MIT's probability terminology reading also gives the Poisson mass law and its exponential-series normalization. Next we compute moments, specify when they exist and use joint probabilities to explain covariance and diversification. Later we study joint continuous densities, parameter estimation, convergence and formal integration."}, {"title": "03.04 · Expectation, variance and covariance", "url": "v03-c04.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A distribution's summaries need conditions A payoff's average, dispersion and relationship to other payoffs help compare uncertain alternatives. They are summaries of a probability law, not substitutes for it. Different laws can share the same mean and variance while assigning very different probabilities to severe losses. Some valid laws have no finite mean or variance at all. We first define moments and their existence conditions, then derive the algebra of expectation, variance and covariance. Joint state probabilities make diversification visible. The final section conditions on a finite partition of states to separate uncertainty within groups from differences between groups. All numerical models here are synthetic and stipulated; empirical estimation and actual portfolio choice require later methods. Expectation and functions of outcomes For a discrete random variable X, its expectation is the probability-weighted sum E[X] = Σx p X (x). For a variable with density f, it is E[X] = ∫x f(x) dx. The integrals run over the entire support. A finite signed expectation requires absolute integrability: E[|X|] < ∞. In a finite discrete model this is automatic. With countable or unbounded continuous support it must be checked. For any measurable real function g for which E[|g(X)|] is finite, the same weighting gives E[g(X)] = Σg(x)p X (x), or ∫g(x)f(x) dx. In a finite model, the justification is direct. Sum the weights of all original states that give a common X value, then attach g(x) to that total. Grouping instead by common g(x) values produces the same sum. Thus we do not need to find the transformed variable's entire distribution before calculating its expectation. For densities the analogous integration rule is the expectation change-of-variable principle; its full measure-theoretic justification is supplied in Chapter 12. The present continuous examples use elementary nonnegative or absolutely convergent integrals. The expectation has the same units as X. E[X²] has squared units; E[g(X)] has the units of g(X). Expectation need not be a possible realized value. A weighted mean can lie between isolated outcomes. It also need not equal the median or the most probable outcome. Explaining it as a long-run average requires a repetition model and a law of large numbers, whose assumptions will be studied in Chapter 6. The definition itself requires neither repeated observations nor independence. When an expected value is infinite or undefined Write X⁺ = max(X, 0) and X⁻ = max(−X, 0). Then X = X⁺ − X⁻ and |X| = X⁺ + X⁻. Each nonnegative part has an expectation in [0, ∞]. If both are finite, their difference is a finite expectation. If exactly one is infinite, an extended expectation is respectively +∞ or −∞. If both are infinite, E[X] is undefined: ∞ − ∞ has no assigned value. Algebra involving finite expectations must not be applied blindly to these cases. The preceding chapter's density x −2 on x ≥ 1 has E[X] = +∞. For a different failure, consider the Cauchy density f(x) = 1/[π(1 + x²)] on ℝ. It is normalized because the integral is [arctan x]/π from −∞ to +∞. Its positive first-moment integral to R is log(1 + R²)/(2π), tending to infinity; symmetry gives the same divergence for the negative part. Therefore E[X] is undefined. The symmetric truncated integral ∫ −R R x f(x) dx = 0 does not make its expectation zero. That symmetric principal value uses a particular cancellation rule rather than the expectation definition. Linearity, indicators and averages For integrable X and Y on the same probability space and finite constants a, b and c, E[aX + bY + c] = aE[X] + bE[Y] + c. In a finite state table, distribute the probability weight through each state's linear combination and sum. For general integrable variables the same linear integral rule holds. Independence is unnecessary: every state can couple X and Y in an arbitrary way, and the identity still follows. If X ≤ Y with probability one and both are integrable, then E[X] ≤ E[Y] because Y − X is nonnegative. In particular a bounded payoff in [a, b] has mean in [a, b]. “With probability one,” also called almost surely , permits a zero-probability exceptional set; it is a statement inside the specified law. For an event A, E[I A ] = 1·P(A) + 0·P(Aᶜ) = P(A). A count X = ΣI Aⱼ therefore has mean ΣP(Aⱼ), whether or not its indicators are independent. An average of n integrable variables with the same mean μ has expectation μ by linearity. This expectation result does not establish that the realized average is near μ; dependence and dispersion govern that separate question. Nonlinear transformations generally do not commute with expectation. E[X²] need not equal E[X]², and E[g(X)] need not equal g(E[X]). Use the weighted transformation formula rather than substituting a mean into a nonlinear function. Convexity gives useful inequalities later; it does not turn those inequalities into universal equalities. Variance, second moments and squared loss Assume E[X²] < ∞. Since |x| ≤ 1 + x², this ensures E[|X|] < ∞ and a finite mean μ. Define Var(X) = E[(X − μ)²]. Expansion and linearity give Var(X) = E[X²] − μ². Variance is nonnegative and has squared units. Its square root, the standard deviation σ X , has the original units of X. E[aX + b] = aμ + b, Var(aX + b) = a²Var(X), SD(aX + b) = |a| SD(X). The centered transformed variable is a(X − μ), which proves the variance rule. Adding a constant changes the location but not dispersion. Multiplication by −1 reverses payoff signs without changing their squared deviations. For a = 0 the transformed variable is constant and its variance is zero. Zero variance is equivalent to X = μ almost surely. One direction follows immediately. For the other, set W = (X − μ)² ≥ 0 with E[W] = 0. For each positive integer n, E[W] ≥ (1/n)P(W ≥ 1/n), so this event has probability zero. The union of these events is {W > 0}, and countable additivity bounds its probability by zero. Thus squared deviation vanishes almost surely. The mean also minimizes expected squared prediction error. For any constant c, expansion around μ gives E[(X − c)²] = Var(X) + (μ − c)². The cross term disappears because E[X − μ] = 0. The unique minimizing constant is c = μ. This is a result for squared loss and finite second moments. Other loss functions can choose different summaries. A squared-dollar loss is not itself a cash expenditure unless a decision model supplies a coefficient with appropriate units. Worked example 1 — Mean, spread and a changed exposure A stipulated profit X, in dollars, is −100 with probability 1/5, 40 with probability 1/2, and 160 with probability 3/10. Its mean is (−100)/5 + 40/2 + 160(3/10) = 48 dollars. Its raw second moment is 10,000/5 + 1,600/2 + 25,600(3/10) = 10,480 dollars squared. Variance is 10,480 − 48² = 8,176 dollars squared; standard deviation is √8,176 ≈ 90.421236 dollars. A direct centered calculation gives (1/5)(−148)² + (1/2)(−8)² + (3/10)(112)² = 8,176, independently confirming the subtraction formula. For Y = 2X − 30, the state payoffs become −230, 50 and 290 dollars. Their weighted mean is 66 dollars. The variance is 4(8,176) = 32,704 dollars squared, and the standard deviation doubles. The fixed subtraction of 30 affects the mean while the doubled exposure changes dispersion. If a constant forecast is 50 dollars, expected squared error is 8,176 + (48 − 50)² = 8,180 dollars squared. Directly weighting squared errors gives 4,500 + 50 + 3,630 = 8,180. Forecasting the mean 48 reduces that squared error to 8,176. But 48 is not one of the possible profits; the median and modal outcome are both 40. Nor does the mean alone settle whether an investor prefers this payoff to another. Derive common moments from the model Moment formulas become safer to use when their assumptions and derivations remain visible. The following all concern population-law moments, with parameters treated as known. Law Mean Variance Conditions and route Bernoulli(p) p p(1 − p) 0 ≤ p ≤ 1; X² = X. Binomial(n, p) np np(1 − p) Independent equal-p indicators; integer n ≥ 0. Poisson(λ) λ λ λ ≥ 0; factorial moments from the exponential series. Exponential rate r 1/r 1/r² r > 0; integration by parts. Normal N(μ, σ²) μ σ² σ > 0; standard-normal symmetry and integration by parts. Uniform [a, b] (a + b)/2 (b − a)²/12 a < b; integrate the constant density 1/(b − a). For Bernoulli, E[X²] = E[X] = p, so variance is p − p². For the independent binomial indicators the covariance terms vanish, as shown below, and their variances add. The mean formula also follows without independence, but the displayed variance and entire binomial law do require it. For Poisson with λ > 0, cancel k against k! in Σ k≥1 k e −λ λ k /k!: it becomes λe −λ Σ j≥0 λ j /j! = λ. Similarly E[X(X − 1)] = λ². Since X² = X(X − 1) + X, E[X²] = λ² + λ and Var(X) = λ. All series are nonnegative, so shifting their indices does not require a cancellation of divergent signed terms. The λ = 0 case has both moments zero. For exponential rate r, integration by parts gives ∫ 0 ∞ t r e −rt dt = 1/r and ∫ 0 ∞ t²r e −rt dt = (2/r)∫ 0 ∞ t r e −rt dt = 2/r². The boundary terms t e −rt and t²e −rt vanish at infinity; exponential decay dominates these powers, as follows from its power series. Subtracting the mean square yields 1/r². For a standard normal Z, ∫|z|φ(z) dz is finite since on z ≥ 0, zφ(z) = −φ′(z) integrates to φ(0); double this value by symmetry. Oddness then gives E[Z] = 0. Integration by parts on [−R, R] yields ∫z²φ(z) dz = [−zφ(z)] −R R + ∫φ(z) dz. Letting R tend to infinity gives E[Z²] = 1 because the boundary term vanishes and φ is normalized. For X = μ + σZ, linearity and scaling give the displayed normal moments. For uniform [a, b], integration gives E[X] = (b² − a²)/[2(b − a)] = (a + b)/2 and E[X²] = (a² + ab + b²)/3. Subtracting the mean square gives (b − a)²/12. A uniform variable on a singleton is instead constant, with variance zero, and is not represented by division by b − a. Covariance requires a joint law For finite-valued X and Y, a joint PMF p(x, y) = P(X = x, Y = y) gives nonnegative cell weights summing to one. Row sums and column sums give their marginal PMFs. The cross-moment E[XY] = Σ x,y xy p(x, y) depends on the joint cells, not just the two marginals. The next chapter develops joint continuous laws in detail. Suppose both second moments are finite. Write U = X − E[X] and V = Y − E[Y]. Their product is integrable because 2|UV| ≤ U² + V². Define Cov(X, Y) = E[UV]. Expanding gives Cov(X, Y) = E[XY] − E[X]E[Y]. Covariance has units equal to the product of the two variables' units. It is symmetric, and Cov(X, X) = Var(X). Covariance is linear in each argument and unaffected by adding constants: Cov(aX + b, cY + d) = ac Cov(X, Y). Positive covariance means centered values have a positive weighted cross-product on average, while negative covariance means a negative one. It does not mean every state moves in the same direction, and neither sign establishes a causal effect. For independent X and Y, the finite joint law factors as p(x)p(y), making E[XY] = (Σx p(x))(Σy p(y)); hence covariance is zero. The same factorization result extends to independent integrable variables through general integration. The converse fails: dependence can remain in a nonlinear relationship that centered cross-products do not detect. Exercise 5 gives a complete example. For event indicators, I A I B = I A∩B , so Cov(I A , I B ) = P(A ∩ B) − P(A)P(B). This identity makes the dependence correction to a count's variance explicit. Correlation, its bound and its failure cases If both variances are finite and strictly positive, correlation is ρ XY = Cov(X, Y)/(σ X σ Y ). It is dimensionless. Rescaling either variable by a positive factor preserves correlation; a negative rescaling flips its sign. If either variance is zero, this expression is undefined. Assigning a constant variable correlation zero would hide division by zero. To prove |ρ| ≤ 1, use the nonnegative quantity E[(U − tV)²] = Var(X) − 2tC + t²Var(Y), where C = Cov(X, Y). For Var(Y) > 0, its minimizing t is C/Var(Y). Substituting shows Var(X) − C²/Var(Y) ≥ 0, or C² ≤ Var(X)Var(Y). Equality occurs precisely when U = [C/Var(Y)]V almost surely, by the zero-variance argument. With both variances positive, perfect correlation therefore means an exact affine relationship almost surely, with its slope determining the sign. Zero correlation records a zero centered cross-moment. It does not rule out nonlinear dependence, shared tails or conditional association. Correlation also requires the stated finite moment conditions. A sample correlation computed from finitely many observations can be a number even if the underlying population correlation is undefined. Variance of sums and the geometry of diversification Var(aX + bY) = a²Var(X) + b²Var(Y) + 2ab Cov(X, Y). Expand the square of aU + bV to prove the identity. For a sum of many variables, add their variances and twice every pairwise covariance. Independence is sufficient for the covariance terms to vanish; their actual values must otherwise be included. Negative covariance can reduce the variance of a positively weighted combination. It is the joint behavior of payoffs that provides this reduction. For a vector of variables with finite second moments, its covariance matrix Σ has entry Σ ij = Cov(Xᵢ, Xⱼ). For any real exposure vector w, Var(wᵀX) = wᵀΣw ≥ 0, so Σ is symmetric positive semidefinite. This links probability to Volume 2's matrix geometry. A proposed covariance matrix producing a negative variance for any exposure is inconsistent with a probability law. Nonnegative diagonal entries alone are insufficient. Worked example 2 — A joint payoff table Two equal-reference-scale cash payoffs A and B have four equally probable states. They are measured in dollars per the same stipulated reference exposure, so the weighted combination wA + (1 − w)B is meaningful without a further price conversion. State Probability A B (A + B)/2 1 1/4 −20 40 10 2 1/4 0 20 10 3 1/4 20 0 10 4 1/4 40 20 30 The means are E[A] = 10 and E[B] = 20. Centered A values are −30, −10, 10 and 30; centered B values are 20, 0, −20 and 0. Thus Var(A) = 500, Var(B) = 200 and Cov(A, B) = (−600 + 0 − 200 + 0)/4 = −200, in squared dollars. Correlation is −200/√(500·200) = −2/√10 ≈ −0.632456. With weight w on A and 1 − w on B, mean payoff is 20 − 10w and variance is 500w² + 200(1 − w)² − 400w(1 − w) = 1,100w² − 800w + 200. At w = 1/2 it is 75, with mean 15. The table independently gives centered equal-weight payoffs −5, −5, −5 and 15, whose mean square is 75. Both components have greater variance individually. On the stipulated long-only range 0 ≤ w ≤ 1, differentiation gives the unique minimum at w = 4/11, weight 7/11 on B. Its variance is 600/11 ≈ 54.545455 and mean is 180/11 ≈ 16.363636. The second derivative 2,200 is positive, and the candidate lies inside the permitted interval, so it is the global minimum there. Generally, if D = Var(A) + Var(B) − 2Cov(A, B) = Var(A − B) > 0, the unconstrained minimum-variance weight is [Var(B) − Cov(A, B)]/D. A long-only constraint clips this candidate to [0, 1]. If D = 0, the centered payoffs are equal almost surely and every weight has the same variance, so this formula must not be divided by zero. This minimizes variance under one known joint law and exposure constraint. It does not optimize every investor's objective: mean payoff changes with w, and prices, liabilities, costs, estimation uncertainty and tail preferences have been omitted. A favorable model variance is a conditional result requiring a model and decision audit. Sampling without replacement: a covariance correction Draw n distinct items uniformly without replacement from N items, M of which are flagged, with N > 1 and 0 ≤ n ≤ N. For the count X, each position's indicator has flag probability p = M/N. For two different positions, joint flag probability is M(M − 1)/[N(N − 1)]. Subtract p² and simplify: Cov(Iᵢ, Iⱼ) = −p(1 − p)/(N − 1), E[X] = np, Var(X) = np(1 − p) + n(n − 1)Cov(Iᵢ, Iⱼ) = np(1 − p)(N − n)/(N − 1). There are n(n − 1)/2 unordered position pairs; the variance expansion doubles each pair, explaining its coefficient. The factor (N − n)/(N − 1) is a finite-population correction. At n = N the count is exactly M and variance is zero. For n = 1 it reduces to the Bernoulli variance. If N = 1, allowable draws give deterministic counts and should be handled directly rather than using the denominator N − 1. This calculation also derives the hypergeometric count's moments without memorizing its PMF. Total expectation and variance across groups Let H₁, …, Hₘ partition the states with probabilities qⱼ > 0. Conditional expectations within a cell use its probability weights divided by qⱼ, as in Chapter 2. Denote the conditional mean μⱼ = E[X | Hⱼ] and conditional variance vⱼ = Var(X | Hⱼ). In a finite model, regrouping the weighted state sum proves μ = E[X] = Σqⱼμⱼ. This is total expectation for a finite partition. Conditional means are weighted by group probabilities, not averaged equally unless those probabilities are equal. A group indicator's information changes the relevant distribution, and an overall average must recombine the groups in the right proportions. With finite second moment, within Hⱼ write X − μ = (X − μⱼ) + (μⱼ − μ). Conditional expectation of the cross term is zero because E[X − μⱼ | Hⱼ] = 0. Averaging the remaining squares yields Var(X) = Σqⱼvⱼ + Σqⱼ(μⱼ − μ)². The first term is average within-group variance; the second is dispersion of group means. Dropping the second understates overall variance whenever group means differ. Zero-probability cells can be omitted; an elementary conditional average in such a cell is undefined. Chapter 5 develops conditioning on random variables and continuous observations, while Chapter 12 provides the general conditional-expectation formulation. Boundary checks before interpreting a moment Check the law's normalization, moment existence, units and joint assumptions. Raw-minus-mean-square variance can suffer cancellation in floating-point arithmetic when dispersion is tiny relative to location; direct centered sums provide a useful independent check. A materially negative computed variance is inconsistent and needs investigation, rather than a risk interpretation. Keep model moments separate from sample estimates. Unknown population parameters and finite samples create uncertainty that is absent from these stipulated calculations. Variance also weights upside and downside squared deviations alike; a liability-constrained decision may care about a specific shortfall event. Correlation records linear co-movement within a law and may differ across regimes. The later finance and inference chapters extend these summaries rather than treating them as a complete decision system. Exercises X takes values −2, 1 and 4 with probabilities 1/4, 1/2 and 1/4. Find its mean, raw second moment, variance and standard deviation. For Y = 3X + 5, find the mean and variance, and confirm the mean directly from transformed outcomes. X has density x/2 on [0, 2]. Calculate its mean, second moment and variance by integration. Find the mean and variance of 2X − 1. Why is the mean of X² different from the square of its mean? Twenty distinct files contain five flags. Draw four uniformly without replacement. Derive the count's mean and variance from position indicators and compute the covariance of two positions' flag indicators. Compare its variance with four independent draws with replacement. A joint law has P(X = 0, Y = 0) = 0.4, P(X = 0, Y = 2) = 0.1, P(X = 1, Y = 0) = 0.2 and P(X = 1, Y = 2) = 0.3. Compute both means, both variances, E[XY], covariance and correlation. Find the mean and variance of X + Y using both its state payoffs and the sum formula. X is uniform on {−1, 0, 1} and Y = X². Compute means, variances, covariance and correlation. Test independence using the event X = 0, Y = 0. Explain how the two conclusions can coexist. A stress group has probability 0.2, conditional mean −50 and conditional variance 400. A normal group has probability 0.8, conditional mean 25 and conditional variance 100. Calculate overall mean and variance, separating within-group and between-group components. Is averaging the two variances enough? Someone proposes covariance matrix [[9, 8], [8, 4]]. Reject or accept it using both the correlation bound and one exposure's variance. If covariance is changed to 3, find the long-only minimum-variance weight on the first variable and the resulting variance. Also calculate equal-weight variance under the repaired matrix. Compare densities f(x) = (3/2)x −5/2 and h(x) = 3x −4 , each on x ≥ 1 and zero elsewhere. Normalize each, compute its mean and second moment where finite, and assess its variance. Can a finite observed sample variance establish that the population second moment is finite? Solutions, joint-law checks and existence conditions E[X] = −2/4 + 1/2 + 4/4 = 1. E[X²] = 4/4 + 1/2 + 16/4 = 11/2. Variance is 11/2 − 1 = 9/2 and standard deviation is 3/√2. For Y the mean is 8 and variance is 9(9/2) = 81/2. Its outcomes −1, 8 and 17 have weighted mean −1/4 + 8/2 + 17/4 = 8. The translation changes location; the scale multiplies variance by nine. E[X] = (1/2)∫ 0 2 x² dx = 4/3. E[X²] = (1/2)∫ 0 2 x³ dx = 2. Variance is 2 − 16/9 = 2/9. The transformed mean is 2(4/3) − 1 = 5/3 and variance is 4(2/9) = 8/9. Here E[X²] − E[X]² equals positive variance; replacing a nonlinear transformation's expectation by the transformation of the mean would erase that dispersion. Each position has p = 1/4, so the mean count is 4/4 = 1. Two positions are both flagged with probability (5/20)(4/19) = 1/19. Their covariance is 1/19 − 1/16 = −3/304. The count variance is 4(3/16) + 12(−3/304) = 12/19 ≈ 0.631579. Equivalently apply the correction (20 − 4)/(20 − 1) to the independent-binomial variance 3/4. Replacement removes depletion and gives variance 3/4; it leaves the mean at one under fresh independent randomization. P(X = 1) = 1/2 and P(Y = 2) = 2/5. Hence E[X] = 1/2, E[Y] = 4/5, Var(X) = 1/4 and Var(Y) = 4(2/5)(3/5) = 24/25. Only cell (1, 2) contributes to E[XY], giving 2(3/10) = 3/5. Covariance is 3/5 − (1/2)(4/5) = 1/5, and correlation is (1/5)/√[(1/4)(24/25)] = 1/√6. X + Y has values 0, 2, 1 and 3 with respective probabilities 2/5, 1/10, 1/5 and 3/10. Its mean is 13/10 and second moment 33/10. Variance is 33/10 − 169/100 = 161/100, agreeing with 1/4 + 24/25 + 2/5. Marginals alone would not determine the cross-moment. E[X] = 0, Var(X) = 2/3, E[Y] = 2/3 and E[Y²] = 2/3, giving Var(Y) = 2/9. E[XY] = E[X³] = 0, so covariance and correlation are zero. Yet P(X = 0, Y = 0) = 1/3 while P(X = 0)P(Y = 0) = 1/9. They are dependent: Y is determined by X. Symmetric positive and negative cross-products cancel in covariance; that cancellation does not remove the nonlinear relationship. The mean is (1/5)(−50) + (4/5)(25) = 10. Average within-group variance is (1/5)(400) + (4/5)(100) = 160. The between-group component is (1/5)(−60)² + (4/5)(15)² = 900. Total variance is 1,060. A concrete compatible law assigns probabilities 0.1, 0.1, 0.4 and 0.4 to outcomes −70, −30, 15 and 35. Its direct centered variance around 10 confirms 1,060. Merely averaging conditional variances misses variation in conditional means. The proposed covariance squared is 64, exceeding the product of variances 36. It would give correlation 8/6 > 1. Exposure (1, −2) has alleged variance 9 − 32 + 16 = −7, impossible. With covariance 3, the repaired symmetric matrix has positive first diagonal and determinant 36 − 9 = 27, so it is positive definite. The long-only first weight is (4 − 3)/(9 + 4 − 6) = 1/7, with second weight 6/7. The variance is 27/7 ≈ 3.857143. Equal weighting gives (9 + 4 + 2·3)/4 = 19/4. A positive covariance does not forbid variance reduction through an appropriate mixture. For f, normalization is (3/2)∫ 1 ∞ x −5/2 dx = 1. Its mean is (3/2)∫x −3/2 dx = 3, but its second moment is (3/2)∫x −1/2 dx = ∞. Since its mean is finite, its variance is infinite; equivalently for sufficiently large x, (x − 3)² is bounded below by a positive multiple of x². For h, normalization is 3∫x −4 dx = 1, mean is 3∫x −3 dx = 3/2, second moment is 3∫x −2 dx = 3, and variance is 3 − 9/4 = 3/4. A finite dataset contains finitely many finite values and hence can have a finite sample variance under either law. That observation cannot by itself establish a finite population second moment. Teach-back and Delayed check Use a finite joint table to explain why expectation is linear without independence while a sum's variance needs covariance terms. Prove the correlation bound by minimizing an expected square, then give the dependent but uncorrelated example. State the conditions under which each displayed moment is finite. After several days, recompute the two worked examples from their state weights using centered sums, without first applying the summary formulas. Change the group probability in Exercise 6 and separate within- and between-group variance again. Explain why a Cauchy symmetric truncated integral is not a mean and why a finite sample cannot certify a population moment's existence. Sources and next steps MIT's expectation reading provides independent further practice with weighted values and linearity. MIT's variance reading provides additional practice with second moments and affine transformations. MIT's continuous-expectation reading gives further routes to density integrals and common moments. MIT's joint-distribution reading supports practice translating state tables into marginals and cross-moments. MIT's covariance reading provides additional covariance and correlation examples. This chapter's payoff tables, proofs and exercises are original. Next we develop joint laws, conditional distributions and conditional expectation more fully, including continuous conditioning where point-event ratios cannot be used. Later chapters connect finite moments to sample behavior, forecasting, inference and explicit decisions."}, {"title": "03.05 · Joint distributions and conditional expectation", "url": "v03-c05.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Information is part of the probability model A forecast of cash flow can change after a signal arrives. To calculate the revision, we need the signal and cash flow's joint law. Separate distributions describe each variable in isolation; they do not say which outcomes occur together. Conditional expectation turns this joint law into a forecast based on the information actually available. This chapter develops finite joint tables and continuous joint densities, distinguishes a conditional mean at an observed value from a conditional-mean random variable, and proves the total-expectation, total-variance and nested-information rules. It also explains what information can improve under squared loss, and why an exact continuous observation cannot be handled by dividing two point probabilities. Joint laws and their marginals For real random variables X and Y on the same space, the joint CDF is F(x, y) = P(X ≤ x, Y ≤ y), where the comma denotes intersection. Inclusion-exclusion gives the probability of a half-open rectangle: P(a < X ≤ b, c < Y ≤ d) = F(b, d) − F(a, d) − F(b, c) + F(a, c). This difference must be nonnegative. A proposed joint CDF needs that rectangle property in addition to increasing in each argument. The marginal CDF F X (x) is lim y→∞ F(x, y); similarly interchange the variables to obtain F Y . The limit sums all possible values of the other coordinate. In a finite or countable joint PMF, p(x, y) = P(X = x, Y = y), all cells are nonnegative and their total is one. Summing over y gives p X (x); summing over x gives p Y (y). For any function g with integrable absolute value, E[g(X, Y)] = Σg(x, y)p(x, y). The support includes the pairs that can occur. It need not be the Cartesian product of the two marginal supports. Two fair binary variables illustrate the missing information. Their marginals each put probability 1/2 at zero and one. Independence assigns probability 1/4 to all four pairs. Perfect agreement assigns 1/2 to 00 and 11, while perfect disagreement assigns 1/2 to 01 and 10. All three joint laws share the same marginals. Their joint events, covariance and conditional forecasts differ. Independence means P(X ∈ A, Y ∈ B) = P(X ∈ A)P(Y ∈ B) for all measurable sets A and B. In a discrete law this is equivalent to factorization of every cell p(x, y) = p X (x)p Y (y). Equivalently the joint CDF factors at every threshold. One matching cell or one zero covariance does not establish independence. Conditioning in a joint table For p Y (y) > 0, the conditional PMF of X given Y = y is p X|Y (x | y) = p(x, y)/p Y (y). Fix the observed y, then renormalize its column. The conditional masses sum to one. Conditioning in the other direction uses the other marginal denominator and generally gives a different function. Define m(y) = E[X | Y = y] = Σx p X|Y (x | y) when the absolute sum is finite. This is a number for a specified y. Before Y is observed, m(Y) is a random variable: it takes the value m(y) when Y takes y. We denote it E[X | Y]. The notation describes a function of the available information, not a new draw of X. Similarly v(y) = Var(X | Y = y) is a number, while v(Y) = Var(X | Y) is a random variable. An overall positive variance does not require every conditional variance to be positive. Some signals can reveal X exactly. Values at zero-probability conditioning cells may be assigned for convenience when defining a version of m(Y), but their elementary conditional ratios are undefined and those assignments cannot change any unconditional expectation. Worked example 1 — Cash-flow forecasts from a signal Let C be a one-period cash flow in dollars, and S a recorded low or high signal. The following stipulated joint table gives probabilities, rather than counts from an observed sample. Signal C = −40 C = 20 C = 80 Signal probability Low 0.30 0.15 0.05 0.50 High 0.05 0.20 0.25 0.50 Cash-flow probability 0.35 0.35 0.30 1 The unconditional mean is −40(0.35) + 20(0.35) + 80(0.30) = 17 dollars. Its second moment is 2,620 dollars squared, giving variance 2,620 − 17² = 2,331 dollars squared. Dividing the low row by 0.5 gives conditional probabilities 0.6, 0.3 and 0.1. Its conditional mean is −10 and its second moment 1,720, so conditional variance is 1,620. The high row gives probabilities 0.1, 0.4 and 0.5, conditional mean 44, second moment 3,520 and variance 1,584. Thus E[C | S] takes values −10 and 44, each with probability 1/2. Its expectation is 17, the original cash-flow mean; its variance is 729. Average conditional cash-flow variance is (1,620 + 1,584)/2 = 1,602. The decomposition 2,331 = 1,602 + 729 separates uncertainty left after the signal from variation in the signal-based forecasts. The probability of receiving 80 dollars rises from its marginal 0.30 to 0.50 after a high signal and falls to 0.10 after a low one. Neither conditional mean is a certain payoff. The signal changes the forecast and the conditional law while leaving substantial residual uncertainty. Under squared forecasting loss, using the constant unconditional mean incurs expected loss 2,331. Using the signal-specific conditional mean incurs 1,602. The reduction 729 is the variance of the conditional mean. This is a forecast-error comparison under the stated law; it is not automatically a dollar value to pay for acquiring the signal. Joint densities: integrate over the actual region A jointly absolutely continuous law has a nonnegative joint density f(x, y) integrating to one over the plane. Probability of a region A is the double integral of f over A. Its density units are inverse X-units times inverse Y-units. The marginals are f X (x) = ∫f(x, y) dy, f Y (y) = ∫f(x, y) dx. Extend f by zero outside its support before integrating. If support is triangular, one coordinate's limits may depend on the other. Integrating over an entire rectangle when the law occupies only a triangle adds outcomes that the model excludes. On bounded continuous pieces these are the iterated area integrals from multivariable calculus; for general nonnegative densities, the order-of-integration theorem is formalized in Chapter 12. Signed expectation integrals require absolute integrability before exchanging their order. Independence corresponds to f(x, y) = f X (x)f Y (y) for almost every pair, meaning outside a set of area zero. Changing density values on such a set does not change the law. A support restriction can make independence impossible even though both marginal supports are large. Compare integrated probabilities or density factorization on positive-area regions, rather than treating a single density value as a point-event probability. Continuous marginals do not ensure a joint density. For example, let U be uniform on [−1, 1], X = U and Y = U². Both marginals have densities, but their joint law is entirely on the curve y = x². That curve has area zero: partition x into intervals of width h and cover each graph segment by a rectangle whose height is at most 2h, using |x₁² − x₂²| ≤ 2|x₁ − x₂|. Total covering area tends to zero with h. An ordinary two-dimensional density would assign this curve probability zero, whereas the specified joint law assigns it probability one. Use the generating variable U or the joint CDF rather than inventing a joint PDF. Conditioning on a continuous observation Where a joint density is given and 0 < f Y (y) < ∞, a density-based conditional version is f X|Y (x | y) = f(x, y)/f Y (y), m(y) = ∫x f X|Y (x | y) dx. The normalization is immediate by integrating over x. This is a density ratio, not P(X ∈ A, Y = y)/P(Y = y): both point-event terms involving Y = y are zero. Conditional densities are versions determined for almost every y under the marginal law. Changing a version at a particular null-probability y does not alter the joint law. A chosen continuous model formula can provide a useful specified extension, but probability alone does not uniquely fix every such pointwise value. The formula can also be motivated by observing a narrow interval rather than a point. For an X-region A, the elementary probability given y < Y ≤ y + h is [∫ y y+h ∫ A f(x, t) dx dt] / [∫ y y+h f Y (t) dt]. If both integrated numerator density and marginal density are continuous at y and the latter is positive, divide both by h and let h decrease to zero. The limit is ∫ A f(x, y) dx/f Y (y). Those regularity conditions explain when the shrinking-interval interpretation justifies the displayed version. At a boundary or a zero marginal density, do not divide by zero or assume every limiting observation scheme gives a unique answer. Worked example 2 — Ordered timestamps Two independent timestamps are uniform on the interval [0, 1], measured as a fraction of a fixed observation window. Let X be the earlier timestamp and Y the later. Their joint density is 2 on 0 < x < y < 1 and zero elsewhere. To see the factor two, an interior ordered pair can arise from either assignment of the two original timestamps; the two unit-density regions map to the same ordered region. Ties have probability zero. The triangle has area 1/2, confirming normalization. Integrating over x gives f Y (y) = 2y on (0, 1). Integrating over y gives f X (x) = 2(1 − x). The joint CDF for thresholds 0 ≤ a, b ≤ 1 is b² when b ≤ a, and 2ab − a² when a ≤ b. In the second case integrate 2 over x ∈ [0, a] and y ∈ [x, b]; in the first, the event is simply Y ≤ b. Negative thresholds give probability zero, and thresholds above one can be clipped to one. For 0 < y < 1, the conditional density f X|Y (x | y) = 2/(2y) = 1/y on (0, y). Thus X given Y = y is uniform on [0, y], with conditional mean y/2 and variance y²/12. E[X | Y] = Y/2 is a random variable. It is not the fixed value obtained after a particular later timestamp has been observed. E[Y] = 2/3 and E[Y²] = 1/2, so E[X] = E[Y/2] = 1/3. Average conditional variance is E[Y²]/12 = 1/24. Since Var(Y) = 1/18, variance of the conditional mean is 1/72. Their sum is Var(X) = 1/18, agreeing with the marginal integral E[X²] = 1/6 minus (1/3)². The variables are dependent: P(X > 1/2) = 1/4, P(Y > 1/2) = 3/4, but their intersection has probability 1/4 because X ≤ Y. The product 3/16 is different. Also E[XY] = E[Y·E[X | Y]] = E[Y²]/2 = 1/4, giving covariance 1/36 and correlation 1/2. Ordering created a restricted joint support even though the original two timestamps were independent. Iterated expectation and taking out known quantities For integrable X, average the conditional means over the distribution of Y: E[E[X | Y]] = E[X]. In a discrete law, Σ y p Y (y)Σ x x p(x, y)/p Y (y) = Σ x,y x p(x, y). In a density law, ∫m(y)f Y (y)dy = ∫∫x f(x, y)dx dy. Absolute integrability licenses these signed rearrangements; zero-probability discrete cells are omitted. The conditional expectation is itself integrable because |m(y)| ≤ E[|X| | Y = y] and the latter's average equals E[|X|]. If h(Y) is known once Y is known, then E[h(Y)X | Y] = h(Y)E[X | Y], assuming the relevant products are integrable. At a fixed y, h(y) is a constant and can be taken outside the conditional weighted sum or integral. Averaging gives E[h(Y)X] = E[h(Y)E[X | Y]]. This identity is especially useful for cross-moments and forecast errors. Conditional expectations are linear on a fixed information set. Also E[h(Y) | Y] = h(Y) and, under independence with integrable X, E[X | Y] = E[X] almost surely. A constant conditional mean is weaker than independence: a signal can change conditional dispersion while leaving the conditional mean unchanged. Nested information and the tower rule Suppose Z records detailed information and Y = h(Z) records a coarser version. Every Y-cell is a union of Z-cells. Conditioning first on Z and then averaging over those cells within a fixed Y-cell gives E[E[X | Z] | Y] = E[X | Y]. This tower rule follows by regrouping the joint weighted sum within each coarse cell. The fine-cell probabilities conditional on the coarse cell supply the weights; each fine conditional mean is multiplied by its corresponding conditional probability. For continuous or general information the same nested sigma-algebra principle holds, with its formal construction in Chapter 12. One can use Z = (Y, W) to represent information that includes Y plus an additional observation W. The reverse ordering under this nesting gives E[E[X | Y] | Z] = E[X | Y], since a function of the coarse information is already known from the fine information. It generally does not equal E[X | Z]. Without nesting, the first displayed tower formula cannot simply be asserted. Exercise 5 demonstrates why keeping track of the information sets matters. Total variance and optimal conditional forecasts With E[X²] < ∞, define m(Y) = E[X | Y] and v(Y) = E[(X − m(Y))² | Y]. Applying total expectation to the conditional second moment and subtracting the mean square yields Var(X) = E[Var(X | Y)] + Var(E[X | Y]). Equivalently write X − E[X] = [X − m(Y)] + [m(Y) − E[X]]. The expected cross term vanishes because, conditional on Y, the first bracket has mean zero and the second is known. The conditional mean has a finite second moment: m(y)² ≤ E[X² | Y = y], as follows by expanding a nonnegative conditional variance. Therefore every term in this decomposition is finite. For any forecast g(Y) with finite second moment, expand X − g(Y) around m(Y). The same zero cross-term calculation proves E[(X − g(Y))²] = E[Var(X | Y)] + E[(m(Y) − g(Y))²]. The conditional mean uniquely minimizes expected squared error up to changes on probability-zero sets. This is the orthogonal-projection property: the residual R = X − m(Y) satisfies E[R h(Y)] = 0 for bounded h, and for other h when the product is integrable. It has zero average association with every admissible function of the observed information, not merely with Y itself. It need not be independent of Y, since its conditional variance or higher distributional features may depend on Y. With nested Y = h(Z), apply the same decomposition to the two forecasts and use the tower rule. It gives E[Var(X | Y)] − E[Var(X | Z)] = E[(E[X | Z] − E[X | Y])²] ≥ 0. More information cannot worsen the best achievable expected squared error under a fixed law and no information cost. This does not say every observed signal lowers conditional variance. A signal can reveal that a high-uncertainty group was encountered. Nor does better squared-error prediction establish better welfare under another loss function, or justify a particular information-acquisition cost. Independent sums and a random number of outcomes For independent variables with densities, the sum T = X + Y has density f T (t) = ∫f X (x)f Y (t − x)dx. Fix t and express y = t − x; the linear transformation from (x, y) to (x, t) has determinant one. Integrating out x gives the formula. For integer-valued variables, replace the integral with Σp X (k)p Y (t − k). These are convolution formulas; support restrictions still apply. Let N be a nonnegative integer independent of an entire sequence of independent identically distributed payoffs Xᵢ, with mean μ and variance σ². Assume E[N²] < ∞ and E[X₁²] < ∞, and define T = Σ i=1 N Xᵢ, with an empty sum of zero. Given N = n, its mean is nμ and variance nσ². Consequently E[T] = μE[N], Var(T) = σ²E[N] + μ²Var(N). The extra term comes from uncertainty about how many payoffs occur. Independence from N ensures that learning the count does not change the summands' laws. A count chosen by stopping after seeing payoffs generally lacks that independence; the argument cannot be imported without new conditions. Optional stopping and other valid extensions require separate theorems later. Exercises A joint PMF assigns probabilities 0.1, 0.3, 0.4 and 0.2 to (X, Y) = (0, −1), (0, 1), (2, −1) and (2, 1), respectively. Find both marginals, the conditional law of X at each Y, both conditional means and variances, and the total-variance decomposition of X. Are X and Y independent? A joint density is 3y on 0 < x < y < 1 and zero elsewhere. Prove normalization, find both marginal densities and the conditional law of X given Y = y for 0 < y < 1. Compute E[X], Var(X), P(X > 1/2) and the within/between components of total variance. U is uniform on [−1, 1], X = U and Y = U². Derive Y's CDF and density. Explain why a two-dimensional joint density does not exist, despite both marginal densities existing. Find E[Y | X], a version of E[X | Y], and covariance; assess independence. In Worked example 1, compare squared forecast loss for the constant zero, the constant 17, the forecast g(low) = 0 and g(high) = 40, and the optimal conditional mean. Verify the decomposition into irreducible conditional variance plus forecast mismatch. Explain how Z = (S, C) provides a limiting fully revealing information set and verify the tower rule back to S. A and B are independent fair bits taking 0 or 1, and W = A. Compute E[E[W | B] | A] and compare it with E[W | A]. Why does a tower simplification fail here? Give a properly nested coarse/fine pair of information sets for which it holds. A latent poor-quality state has probability 0.2. Two flags are conditionally independent given quality, each with probability 0.7 in the poor state and 0.1 in the good state. Find their marginal flag probability, joint flag probability and covariance. After two flags, find poor-state probability and the predictive probability of a third conditionally independent flag. An independent count N equals 0, 2 or 5 with probabilities 0.2, 0.5 and 0.3. Independent identically distributed cash payoffs have mean 8 dollars and variance 9 dollars squared, and the whole sequence is independent of N. Derive the mean and variance of their sum through N. Explain why choosing N after examining payoffs invalidates this particular derivation. X equals −10, 0 or 10 with probabilities 0.1, 0.8 and 0.1. A signal reveals whether |X| = 10. Find conditional means and variances, the unconditional variance and optimal expected squared error before and after the signal. Does this signal reduce variance for every observation? Does it improve the optimal squared-error mean forecast? Solutions and information-set checks The X marginal is 0.4 at 0 and 0.6 at 2; the Y marginal is 1/2 at each sign. Given Y = −1, X probabilities are 0.2 and 0.8, giving mean 8/5 and variance 4(0.8)(0.2) = 16/25. Given Y = 1, they are 0.6 and 0.4, giving mean 4/5 and variance 24/25. The overall mean is 6/5 and variance 4(0.6)(0.4) = 24/25. Average conditional variance is 4/5; variance of the two equally weighted conditional means around 6/5 is 4/25. Their sum is 24/25. Independence fails: for example p(0, −1) = 0.1 differs from the marginal product 0.4(0.5) = 0.2. ∫ 0 1 ∫ 0 y 3y dx dy = ∫3y²dy = 1. Hence f Y (y) = 3y² and f X (x) = ∫ x 1 3y dy = (3/2)(1 − x²), each on (0, 1). The conditional density is 1/y on (0, y). E[Y] = 3/4 and E[Y²] = 3/5, so E[X] = 3/8 and E[X²] = E[Y²]/3 = 1/5. Var(X) = 1/5 − 9/64 = 19/320. P(X > 1/2) = ∫ 1/2 1 (3/2)(1 − x²)dx = 5/16. Average conditional variance is (3/5)/12 = 1/20; conditional-mean variance is Var(Y)/4 = (3/80)/4 = 3/320. These add to 19/320. For 0 ≤ y ≤ 1, P(U² ≤ y) = P(−√y ≤ U ≤ √y) = √y. The CDF is zero below zero and one above one; its density is 1/(2√y) on (0, 1). All joint probability lies on a zero-area curve, incompatible with a joint ordinary density. E[Y | X] = X². By sign symmetry a conditional version of X given Y = y places equal weights on −√y and √y for 0 < y < 1, hence E[X | Y] = 0 almost surely. E[X] = 0, E[Y] = 1/3 and E[XY] = E[U³] = 0, so covariance is zero. Yet Y is a nonconstant function of X: P(|X| ≤ 1/2, Y ≤ 1/4) = 1/2 differs from the product 1/4. Zero covariance and a constant conditional mean do not imply independence. The zero forecast has loss E[C²] = 2,620. The constant 17 has loss Var(C) = 2,331. For g the mismatch term is (1/2)(−10 − 0)² + (1/2)(44 − 40)² = 58, so loss is 1,602 + 58 = 1,660. The optimal conditional mean's mismatch is zero and loss 1,602. For the constant 17 the mismatch is 729, reproducing 2,331; for zero it is (100 + 1,936)/2 = 1,018, reproducing 2,620. With Z = (S, C), E[C | Z] = C and residual variance is zero. Since S is part of Z, E[E[C | Z] | S] = E[C | S]. This hypothetical full revelation is an information benchmark, not a claim that actual forecasts can observe future cash flow. Independence gives E[W | B] = 1/2, so conditioning that constant on A still gives 1/2. But E[W | A] = A. They differ whenever A is 0 or 1. The information B does not contain A, and A is not a coarsening of B. For a valid nesting, use fine information Z = (A, B) and coarse information Y = B. Then E[E[W | Z] | Y] = E[A | B] = 1/2 = E[W | B]. Each marginal flag probability is 0.2(0.7) + 0.8(0.1) = 0.22 = 11/50. Joint flags have probability 0.2(0.7²) + 0.8(0.1²) = 0.106 = 53/500. Indicator covariance is 0.106 − 0.22² = 0.0576 = 36/625. Posterior poor quality after two flags is 0.098/0.106 = 49/53. A third flag's predictive probability is (49/53)(0.7) + (4/53)(0.1) = 347/530 ≈ 0.654717. Conditional independence removes dependence within each latent-quality group; mixing uncertain group quality creates positive marginal association. E[N] = 2(0.5) + 5(0.3) = 5/2. E[N²] = 4(0.5) + 25(0.3) = 19/2, so Var(N) = 13/4. Given N = n the sum's mean is 8n and variance 9n. Total expectation gives 20 dollars; total variance gives 9(5/2) + 64(13/4) = 461/2 dollars squared. A stopping count based on realized payoffs can select particular summand outcomes, so conditioning on N = n need not leave n independent draws with the original mean and variance. Additional stopping-time conditions and a different argument are required. The mean is zero and variance is 20. The |X| = 10 signal cell has probability 0.2, conditional mean zero and conditional variance 100; the other cell is the certain zero outcome with variance zero. Average conditional variance is 0.2(100) = 20 and conditional-mean variance is zero. The best squared-error forecast remains zero under either signal, so expected loss is unchanged at 20. An observed tail-group signal raises conditional variance above the overall variance. It conveys information about dispersion without improving a squared-error mean forecast. Teach-back and Delayed check Explain the difference between m(y) and m(Y) using the signal table. Derive total variance by centering once around the conditional mean and once around the overall mean. Then explain why a continuous conditional density uses density normalization rather than a ratio of zero point-event probabilities. After several days, reconstruct the triangle's marginals and conditional law from its support. Verify the forecast-error decomposition for a new signal rule. Give a nonnested conditioning example where a proposed tower identity fails, and an informative signal that changes conditional spread without changing the mean forecast. Sources and next steps MIT's joint-distribution reading provides further practice with joint PMFs/PDFs, marginalization and independence. MIT's conditional-expectation lecture supplies a further route to conditional moments as random variables, iterated expectation, total variance and independent random sums. MIT's covariance reading supports the distinction between cross-moment summaries and independence. The signal, timestamp and exercise models here are original. Next we ask when sample averages concentrate around these population expectations and how their scaled errors behave. Conditional expectations will return in inference, forecasting, econometrics and stochastic finance."}, {"title": "03.06 · Large samples and convergence", "url": "v03-c06.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Three different claims about many observations “An average has the correct expectation,” “an average becomes close to its target,” and “an average's scaled error is approximately normal” are different statements. Linearity gives the first without independence. Laws of large numbers establish the second under stated conditions. Central limit theorems describe the third, with a different scaling and additional moment conditions. We develop convergence modes, finite-sample probability bounds, weak and strong laws, and the classical independent identically distributed central limit theorem. The proofs identify what averaging removes and what it can leave behind. The examples use known synthetic laws, so the probabilities are model calculations rather than confidence statements about estimated unknown parameters. Convergence modes and what each controls Let Xₙ and X be real random variables. For convergence in probability, they are defined on a common probability space. Write Xₙ → P X when for every ε > 0, P(|Xₙ − X| > ε) tends to zero. This controls the probability of an error at a particular large n; it does not state that errors never occur later along a realized infinite sequence. Almost sure convergence, Xₙ → a.s. X, means P({ω: Xₙ(ω) tends to X(ω)}) = 1. It controls eventual behavior along almost every entire path. It implies convergence in probability. To see this, for fixed ε consider the decreasing events B N = ∪ n≥N {|Xₙ − X| > ε}. Under almost sure convergence their intersection has probability zero. Probability continuity gives P(B N ) → 0, and each later single-error event has no greater probability. Convergence in distribution, Xₙ → d X, means F Xₙ (x) → F X (x) at every continuity point of the limiting CDF. It concerns laws, not a particular coupling of outcomes. Variables need not be on the same space for this definition. The restriction to continuity points matters at jumps: deterministic Xₙ = 1/n converges in distribution to zero even though F Xₙ (0) = 0 while the limiting F(0) = 1. Convergence in probability implies convergence in distribution. For any ε > 0, event containment gives F X (x − ε) − P(|Xₙ − X| > ε) ≤ F Xₙ (x) ≤ F X (x + ε) + P(|Xₙ − X| > ε). First let n increase and then let ε decrease to zero at a continuity point of F X . Conversely convergence in distribution to a constant c implies convergence in probability to c: the CDF values at c − ε and c + ε bound the probability of being outside that neighborhood and converge to zero and one. For a nonconstant limit, distributional convergence does not imply closeness under a particular coupling. Mean absolute convergence, or L¹ convergence, means E[|Xₙ − X|] → 0. Mean square convergence, or L² convergence, means E[(Xₙ − X)²] → 0. They imply convergence in probability through the bounds below. L² also implies L¹ by E[|W|]² ≤ E[W²], obtained by the nonnegative variance of |W|. Neither distributional nor probability convergence alone licenses interchange of a limit and an expectation. A useful counterexample is U uniform on (0, 1) and Xₙ = n I {U≤1/n} . For every U > 0, Xₙ eventually equals zero, so it converges almost surely and in probability to zero. Yet E[Xₙ] = 1 for every n and E[Xₙ²] = n. A shrinking event carries a growing magnitude. Without adequate tail control, convergence of typical outcomes can coexist with nonconvergence of their moments. Chapter 12 treats dominated convergence and uniform integrability formally. Markov and Chebyshev: actual probability bounds For a nonnegative variable W with finite expectation and a > 0, W ≥ a I {W≥a} . Taking expectations gives Markov's inequality P(W ≥ a) ≤ E[W]/a. No independence or distribution family is needed. The bound can be replaced by one when its right side exceeds one. For X with mean μ and finite variance σ², apply Markov to W = (X − μ)². Chebyshev's inequality is P(|X − μ| ≥ ε) ≤ σ²/ε², ε > 0. The same argument applied to |Xₙ − X| or its square proves the L¹/L² implications. These are finite-sample upper bounds under their moment assumptions. A normal approximation estimates a probability; Chebyshev bounds it. A loose bound is still valid, and a close-looking approximation is still not a bound unless an error theorem accompanies it. The weak law: variance shrinks under independent sampling Let X₁, X₂, … be independent identically distributed, abbreviated iid, with finite mean μ and variance σ². Put Sₙ = Σ i=1 n Xᵢ and X̄ₙ = Sₙ/n. Then E[X̄ₙ] = μ and Var(X̄ₙ) = σ²/n, because the cross-covariances vanish. Chebyshev gives P(|X̄ₙ − μ| ≥ ε) ≤ σ²/(nε²) → 0. This proves the finite-variance weak law of large numbers: X̄ₙ → P μ. It also gives L² convergence and an explicit conservative sample-size bound. It does not ensure that every finite sample is close, that error decreases monotonically on a path, or that a larger sample corrects systematic measurement or selection bias. Identical distributions are sufficient but not necessary for this variance proof. For uncorrelated variables with a common mean, Var(X̄ₙ) = n −2 ΣVar(Xᵢ); if this tends to zero, the same argument works. For dependent observations, include every covariance. A sufficient mean-square condition is Var(X̄ₙ) → 0 plus convergence of E[X̄ₙ] to the target. Merely having the right expectation does not control the remaining spread. A finite mean is enough for the iid weak law The weak law extends to iid Xᵢ with E[|X₁|] < ∞ even when variance is infinite. Here is a truncation proof. For M > 0 set Xᵢ M = Xᵢ I {|Xᵢ|≤M} , with mean μ M , and Dᵢ M = Xᵢ − Xᵢ M . The truncated variables remain iid and bounded, so their average obeys the finite-variance weak law. Let δ M = E[|X₁|I {|X₁|>M} ]. Absolute integrability makes δ M tend to zero: these are tails of a convergent nonnegative expectation sum or integral. Write X̄ₙ − μ = (X̄ₙ M − μ M ) + (D̄ₙ M − E[D₁ M ]). The expected absolute value of the second bracket is at most 2δ M , by the triangle inequality and linearity. A union bound and Markov therefore give P(|X̄ₙ − μ| > ε) ≤ 4Var(X₁ M )/(nε²) + 4δ M /ε. Keep M fixed and let n tend to infinity, then let M tend to infinity. Both terms can be made arbitrarily small in that order. This proves the weak law without a finite variance. It provides no distribution-free numerical rate when only integrability is known: the tail δ M may shrink very slowly. Worked example 1 — A known flag rate, bound and approximation Suppose independent records each flag with known probability p = 0.2. With n = 625, the average flag indicator has mean 0.2 and variance 0.16/625 = 0.000256. Its standard deviation is 0.016. Chebyshev bounds the probability of an absolute deviation at least 0.04 by 0.000256/0.04² = 0.16. To make that same bound at most 0.05, it suffices to use n ≥ 0.16/[0.05(0.04²)] = 2,000. This is a sufficient conservative sample size under a known iid model. It is not a claim that 2,000 observations are necessary, nor a confidence interval calculation for an unknown p. The flag count K is binomial (625, 0.2), with mean 125 and standard deviation 10. The deviation event is K ≤ 100 or K ≥ 150. Summing its exact binomial masses gives probability approximately 0.014206311. A continuity-corrected normal approximation uses thresholds 100.5 and 149.5, giving Φ(−2.45) + 1 − Φ(2.45) ≈ 0.014285621. The approximation is close here; Chebyshev's 0.16 is much looser but has a different guarantee. The half-unit correction allocates each integer count an interval of width one. It improves this calculation, but its observed accuracy does not prove that it works equally well for every count model, tail or dependence structure. Exact finite-law calculations remain preferable when readily available. The strong law with finite variance: control entire paths For iid variables with finite variance, the strong law says X̄ₙ → a.s. μ. A direct attempt to sum the Chebyshev bounds σ²/(nε²) fails because Σ1/n diverges. That failure concerns this proof attempt, not the truth of the strong law. We need a bound controlling many partial sums at once. Maximal inequality and a complete finite-variance strong-law proof First consider independent mean-zero W₁, …, W N with finite variances, and partial sums Tₖ. For a > 0 let Aₖ be the event that k is the first index with |Tₖ| ≥ a. These events are disjoint and their union is {max k≤N |Tₖ| ≥ a}. Aₖ and Tₖ depend only on the first k variables; the future sum T N − Tₖ is independent of that history and has mean zero. Expand T N ² on Aₖ. The expected cross term 2Tₖ(T N − Tₖ)I Aₖ is zero by independence and its future factor's zero mean. The remaining future square is nonnegative. Hence E[T N ² I Aₖ ] ≥ E[Tₖ² I Aₖ ] ≥ a²P(Aₖ). Summing over the disjoint events and retaining the nonnegative contribution from their complement gives P(max k≤N |Tₖ| ≥ a) ≤ E[T N ²]/a² = [Σ i≤N Var(Wᵢ)]/a². This is Kolmogorov's maximal inequality. The independence requirement concerns full history and future variables, not just pairwise zero covariance; it justified multiplying the future sum by the history-dependent first-crossing indicator. We also need the first Borel–Cantelli implication. If ΣP(Bₘ) < ∞, a union bound gives P(∪ m≥M Bₘ) ≤ Σ m≥M P(Bₘ), tending to zero. Continuity for these decreasing union events shows that infinitely many Bₘ occur with probability zero. This implication needs no independence. Now set Wᵢ = Xᵢ − μ, N = 2 m and a = ε2 m . The maximal bound is σ²/(ε²2 m ), summable over m. Therefore almost surely max k≤2ᵐ |Tₖ| < ε2 m eventually. For 2 m−1 < n ≤ 2 m , this implies |Tₙ|/n < 2ε. Apply the argument simultaneously to the countably many ε = 1/j: the intersection of their probability-one events still has probability one. On that intersection, |Tₙ|/n tends to zero. Thus X̄ₙ tends to μ almost surely. The standard iid strong law in fact needs only E[|X₁|] < ∞. Its full variable-level truncation, independent-series and averaging proof is supplied in Chapter 12 ; the proof just given establishes the finite-variance version. The distinction matters: infinite variance does not automatically defeat a law of large numbers, though it defeats the preceding mean-square bound and the classical finite-variance CLT. The central limit theorem: the error needs a scale For iid Xᵢ with mean μ and variance 0 < σ² < ∞, the classical central limit theorem, or CLT, states Zₙ = (Sₙ − nμ)/(σ√n) = √n(X̄ₙ − μ)/σ → d N(0, 1). Equivalently, P(Zₙ ≤ z) tends to Φ(z) for each fixed real z. The unscaled mean converges to a constant under the law of large numbers; its scaled centered error retains a nondegenerate limiting distribution. The population law of each Xᵢ need not be normal. If σ² = 0, all Xᵢ are constant almost surely, so the CLT normalization divides by zero and should be replaced by that deterministic description. For finite n, the CLT motivates Sₙ ≈ N(nμ, nσ²) or X̄ₙ ≈ N(μ, σ²/n). These are approximations to sampling laws, not statements that individual observations become normal. The theorem alone gives no universal “n ≥ 30” guarantee or certified finite-n error. A complete finite-variance CLT proof using smooth replacement The key idea is to replace one normalized summand at a time by a normal variable with the same first two moments. A smooth function's Taylor expansion then cancels those matching terms. Tail control makes the total remainder vanish without assuming a finite third moment. This is the classical Lindeberg replacement method; the presentation below spells out its finite-variance remainder and the final CDF squeeze. First verify the normal comparison law. For independent centered normals of positive variances a and b, convolution and the identity x²/a + (t − x)²/b = [(a + b)/(ab)](x − at/(a + b))² + t²/(a + b) give the density exp[−t²/(2(a + b))]/√(2π(a + b)), using the normalized Gaussian integral from Chapter 3. Thus variances add within the normal family. Induction shows that a sum of n independent standard normals divided by √n is exactly standard normal. Normalize the original summands to Yᵢ = (Xᵢ − μ)/σ, of mean zero and second moment one. For each n introduce standard normals G₁, …, Gₙ independent of one another and of every Yᵢ. Let h be bounded and twice continuously differentiable, with bounded first and second derivatives and uniformly continuous second derivative. Define ω(r) = sup u, |v|≤r |h″(u + v) − h″(u)|. Then ω(r) → 0 as r → 0 and ω(r) ≤ 2M where M = sup|h″|. The integral Taylor formula from calculus gives h(s + u) = h(s) + h′(s)u + ½h″(s)u² + R(s, u), |R(s, u)| ≤ ½u²ω(|u|). Indeed the second-order term before subtraction is u²∫ 0 1 (1 − t)h″(s + tu)dt; subtracting ½h″(s)u² gives the bound. It holds uniformly in s and for either sign of u. In the i-th replacement, let Qᵢ be the sum of the other n − 1 terms divided by √n, with some already replaced by G's. It is independent of Yᵢ and Gᵢ. Expand h(Qᵢ + Yᵢ/√n) and h(Qᵢ + Gᵢ/√n). Independence cancels their expected first-order terms because both inserted variables have mean zero. Their expected second-order terms also agree because both have second moment one. Bounded derivatives ensure the products used in this cancellation are integrable. The absolute difference in expectations is at most [E(Y₁²ω(|Y₁|/√n)) + E(G₁²ω(|G₁|/√n))]/(2n). Telescoping n replacements bounds |E[h(Zₙ)] − E[h(G)]| by half the sum of these two expectations, where G is standard normal. Each tends to zero. To prove this without assuming a third moment, fix K: on |W| ≤ K the expectation is at most E[W²]ω(K/√n); on |W| > K it is at most 2M E[W²I {|W|>K} ]. For W = Y₁ or G₁, keep K fixed and send n to infinity, then send K to infinity. Finite second moments make their tail expectations vanish. We have proved convergence for every such smooth h. Finally approximate the event {t ≤ z} from below and above. Use the transition q(v) = 1 − 10v³ + 15v⁴ − 6v⁵ for 0 ≤ v ≤ 1, extended as one for v ≤ 0 and zero for v ≥ 1. It is twice continuously differentiable, has bounded derivatives and uniformly continuous second derivative; q′(v) = −30v²(1 − v)² shows it stays between zero and one and decreases. For δ > 0 put h₋(t) = q((t − z + δ)/δ) and h₊(t) = q((t − z)/δ). Then h₋ ≤ I {t≤z} ≤ h₊, with h₋ equal to one below z − δ and h₊ equal to zero above z + δ. The already proved smooth convergence gives Φ(z − δ) ≤ liminf P(Zₙ ≤ z) ≤ limsup P(Zₙ ≤ z) ≤ Φ(z + δ). Let δ decrease to zero. The normal CDF is continuous, so both outer bounds approach Φ(z). This proves the stated CLT at every real z, using only matching first two moments, independence, finite second-moment tail control, calculus and the density/CDF tools already taught. No moment-generating function or finite-third-moment assumption was introduced. Finite-sample error and tails If β₃ = E[|X₁ − μ|³] is finite as well, the Berry–Esseen theorem gives a universal-constant bound on CDF error: sup z∈ℝ |P(Zₙ ≤ z) − Φ(z)| ≤ C β₃/(σ³√n). This is a stated quantitative theorem, not proved by the preceding qualitative argument. The conservative choice C = 1 is valid; sharper constants are available in the further-reading source. For an interval, subtracting two CDFs can require twice the one-CDF bound. If the displayed bound exceeds one, it is uninformative. A small absolute error can still be large relative to a very rare event probability. For Bernoulli p, β₃ = p(1 − p)[(1 − p)² + p²], so β₃/σ³ = [(1 − p)² + p²]/√[p(1 − p)]. It grows as p approaches zero or one. In Worked example 1 the ratio is 1.7 and the C = 1 bound is 1.7/25 = 0.068 for one CDF. The two-tail bound can be 0.136, far larger than the observed approximation error. A valid general bound need not be tight for a particular law. The classical CLT applies when variance is finite even if the third absolute moment is infinite, but that Berry–Esseen rate cannot then be asserted. Extreme thresholds moving with n also need care: the qualitative theorem is about fixed standardized thresholds. Large deviations, stable limits and dependent-process CLTs require additional theorems, not a change of labels on the iid result. Dependence and effective information Worked example 2 — A shared shock survives averaging Let Xᵢ = μ + B + εᵢ, where B equals −2 or 2 with equal probability, and εᵢ are independent fair values −1 or 1, independent of B. B is drawn once and shared by all observations. Each Xᵢ has mean μ and variance 5, and two different observations have covariance Var(B) = 4. The average is μ + B + ε̄ₙ. Its variance is 4 + 1/n, either by this representation or by [5n + 4n(n − 1)]/n². At n = 100 it is 4.01. Incorrectly treating all observations as independent would report 5/100 = 0.05. More repetitions reduce the idiosyncratic ε noise while leaving the shared B uncertainty. The finite-variance strong law for εᵢ gives ε̄ₙ → 0 almost surely, hence X̄ₙ → μ + B, a nonconstant limit. It does not converge in probability to μ. In fact |B + ε̄ₙ| ≥ 1 for every n because |ε̄ₙ| ≤ 1, so P(|X̄ₙ − μ| > 1/2) = 1. The average is unbiased for μ yet never close within this tolerance. If instead m independent groups each have a fresh Bⱼ and k independent ε observations, the grand mean across mk observations has variance 4/m + 1/(mk). More observations within a fixed group cannot substitute for more independent groups. Actual clustered or time-series data require a dependence model and design appropriate to the target; counting rows is not the same as counting independent information. For a stationary covariance pattern with common variance γ₀ and lag covariances γₕ, direct variance expansion gives Var(X̄ₙ) = [nγ₀ + 2Σ h=1 n−1 (n − h)γₕ]/n². If Σ h≥1 |γₕ| is finite, this is at most [γ₀ + 2Σ|γₕ|]/n and hence tends to zero. That proves a weak-law conclusion with a common finite mean, but covariance summability alone does not establish a normal limit. A dependent CLT needs conditions controlling fuller dependence. The shared-shock model has nondecaying covariance and fails this summability condition. Heavy tails, transformations and estimated scales The Pareto density (3/2)x −5/2 on x ≥ 1 has finite mean 3 and infinite variance, as computed in Chapter 4. Independent averages obey the integrable weak law, but the finite-variance CLT and variance-based Chebyshev sample-size calculation are unavailable. In contrast the density (5/2)x −7/2 on x ≥ 1 has mean 5/3 and second moment 5, giving variance 20/9, but an infinite third absolute central moment. Its iid averages satisfy the classical CLT, while the displayed Berry–Esseen bound is unavailable. The density x −2 has infinite mean and lacks a finite mean target for that law. A Cauchy law has no defined expectation. Being able to compute a finite sample average does not repair these population moment failures. If Xₙ → P c and g is continuous at c, then g(Xₙ) → P g(c): continuity supplies a δ neighborhood of c whose image is within any chosen ε neighborhood of g(c), and the probability of leaving that δ neighborhood tends to zero. The same argument extends to finitely many probability-convergent quantities by a union bound. A related result permits an estimated scale in a distributional limit. If Zₙ → d Z with continuous CDF, Aₙ → P 1 and Bₙ → P 0, then AₙZₙ + Bₙ → d Z; no independence between these quantities is needed. Here is the argument. Distributional convergence makes Zₙ bounded in probability: choose large continuity thresholds ±M so its outside probability is eventually small. Except on that tail event, |(Aₙ − 1)Zₙ + Bₙ| ≤ M|Aₙ − 1| + |Bₙ|, which tends to zero in probability. Choose M large and then n large. The perturbation therefore vanishes in probability. The earlier CDF sandwich, with Zₙ as the reference and a vanishing perturbation, preserves the continuous limiting CDF. This is the needed form of Slutsky's theorem. Applied to a consistently estimated positive standard deviation, it justifies replacing σ in a CLT statistic by its estimate. It does not guarantee that the estimate is consistent under heavy tails, dependence or changing distributions. Those assumptions must be checked before invoking the replacement. Exercises For U uniform on (0, 1), let Xₙ = n I {U≤1/n} . Determine almost sure, probability, L¹ and L² convergence to zero, showing the relevant probabilities and moments. Separately, if R is a fair ±1 variable and Yₙ = (−1)ⁿR, does Yₙ converge in distribution to R, in probability to R, or almost surely? Independent Bernoulli observations have known p = 0.3. With n = 400, bound P(|X̄ₙ − p| ≥ 0.05) by Chebyshev. Find a sufficient n to make that bound at most 0.05. What sufficient n would use the worst-case Bernoulli variance when p is not specified? K is binomial (100, 0.1). Compute its mean and standard deviation. Write the exact expression for P(K ≥ 15), then give normal approximations with and without a half-unit correction. Use Φ(1.5) = 0.933192799 and Φ(5/3) = 0.952209648. Compare with the exact value 0.072572965 and distinguish approximation from a bound. For the shared-shock model in Worked example 2, calculate the variance of the average at n = 25 and at n = 1,000. With ten independent groups of 100 observations each, calculate grand-mean variance. Compare with one group of 1,000. What target does the one-group mean converge to? For U uniform on [0, 1), set X 2ᵐ+k = I {k/2ᵐ≤U<(k+1)/2ᵐ} for m ≥ 0 and k = 0, …, 2ᵐ − 1. Prove convergence in probability to zero but failure of almost sure convergence. Explain why the single-n and entire-path claims differ. For iid observations with density (3/2)x −5/2 on x ≥ 1, which of the stated weak-law, finite-variance strong-law, classical CLT and Berry–Esseen conclusions can be established with the proofs or theorem hypotheses in this chapter? Distinguish a theorem that remains true under weaker conditions from an inapplicable proof. Contrast the density x −2 . Let iid Wᵢ take ±1 with equal probabilities, and Tₖ = Σ i≤k Wᵢ. Use the maximal inequality to bound P(max k≤64 |Tₖ| ≥ 16). Bound the probability that max k≤2ᵐ |Tₖ| ≥ ε2ᵐ occurs for at least one m ≥ M, and explain how this bound yields almost sure average convergence. For iid Xᵢ with finite positive variance σ², set Vₙ = n −1 ΣXᵢ² − X̄ₙ². Prove Vₙ → P σ² using the integrable weak law and continuous transformations. Explain how this gives asymptotic standard normality for √n(X̄ₙ − μ)/√Vₙ on events where Vₙ > 0. Does it require X̄ₙ and Vₙ to be independent? Solutions and theorem-condition audits For each U > 0, the indicator eventually vanishes, giving almost sure convergence. For any fixed ε > 0 and n > ε, P(|Xₙ| > ε) = 1/n, giving probability convergence. But E[|Xₙ|] = 1 and E[Xₙ²] = n, so neither L¹ nor L² convergence to zero holds. Yₙ has the same fair ±1 law for every n and thus converges in distribution to R. At odd n, P(|Yₙ − R| > 1) = 1; at even n it is zero, so there is no probability convergence to R. Each path alternates forever between opposite nonzero values, hence no almost sure limit. Distributional convergence describes the unchanged law, not pathwise proximity. The Bernoulli variance is 0.21. The bound is 0.21/[400(0.05²)] = 0.21. Requiring 0.21/(n·0.0025) ≤ 0.05 gives n ≥ 1,680. Since p(1 − p) = 1/4 − (p − 1/2)² ≤ 1/4, the unspecified-p bound requires n ≥ 2,000. These are sufficient model-based deviation bounds, not exact tail probabilities or necessary sample sizes. The mean is 10, variance 9 and standard deviation 3. Exact probability is Σ k=15 100 C(100, k)(0.1) k (0.9) 100−k . Using the threshold 15 directly gives 1 − Φ((15 − 10)/3) = 0.047790352. The half-unit threshold 14.5 gives 1 − Φ(1.5) = 0.066807201. Both understate the exact 0.072572965; the corrected one is closer here. Neither is a one-sided bound by virtue of being a normal approximation, and these observed errors cannot be transferred to a different model without justification. One-group variances are 4 + 1/25 = 4.04 and 4 + 1/1,000 = 4.001. Ten independent groups of 100 have variance 4/10 + 1/1,000 = 0.401. One group of 1,000 remains at 4.001. The one-group mean tends almost surely to μ + B, not μ. More groups average independent shared shocks, while more observations inside one group do not. Every variable in block m has probability 2 −m of being one, which tends to zero as n tends to infinity. For 0 < ε < 1, this is its probability of exceeding ε, proving convergence in probability. For every U in [0, 1), exactly one variable in each block equals one. In every block with m ≥ 1, others equal zero. Thus every path contains infinitely many ones and zeros and has no limit. The probability of an error at a particular late position shrinks, while each whole block still contains one error for every path. The law has mean 3 and infinite variance. The truncation proof establishes the integrable iid weak law. The finite-variance strong-law proof does not apply; the stated general integrable iid strong-law theorem still does, with its full proof now supplied in Chapter 12 . Classical CLT normalization requires a finite σ², and Berry–Esseen also requires a finite third absolute central moment, so neither displayed conclusion is licensed. For x −2 the mean is infinite; there is no finite μ target for either stated iid mean law. Do not conclude that all other forms of convergence are impossible: different normalizations and limit theorems require their own hypotheses. Var(T₆₄) = 64, so the maximal bound is 64/16² = 1/4. At dyadic index m the bound is 1/(ε²2ᵐ). A union bound over m ≥ M is at most Σ m≥M 1/(ε²2ᵐ) = 2 1−M /ε², capped at one. This tends to zero, so almost surely only finitely many such dyadic failures occur. For n between successive dyadic endpoints, |Tₙ|/n is eventually below 2ε. Intersecting the probability-one conclusions for ε = 1/j establishes convergence to zero. Finite variance ensures finite E[X²]. The integrable weak law applied to Xᵢ² gives n −1 ΣXᵢ² → P E[X²], even if Xᵢ² itself has infinite variance. Applied to Xᵢ it gives X̄ₙ → P μ. Continuous squaring and subtraction yield Vₙ → P E[X²] − μ² = σ². Since σ² > 0, the probability of Vₙ ≤ σ²/2 tends to zero, so zero-denominator events become negligible. Define the normalized statistic arbitrarily on those negligible exceptional events. Then σ/√Vₙ → P 1 on the valid events, and Slutsky applied to the classical CLT gives a standard normal limit. Independence between the average and scale estimate is unnecessary. The usual n − 1 sample-variance correction multiplies Vₙ by n/(n − 1), tending to one and preserving consistency. Teach-back and Delayed check Explain in ordinary language what weak convergence of averages and a normal limit of scaled errors each claim. Prove the Chebyshev weak law, identify the extra device used for the strong law, and explain why matching two moments cancels Taylor terms in the CLT proof. Give one failure caused by shared shocks and one caused by nonexistent moments. After several days, derive the sample-size bound for a different tolerance and failure probability. Reconstruct the covariance formula for a grand mean under grouped shocks. Give a probability-convergent sequence whose expectations do not converge, and verify the assumptions needed to replace a known scale by an estimate. Sources and next steps MIT's introductory limit-theorem reading provides further practice standardizing sums and averages and comparing normal approximations with exact count laws. Terence Tao's author-hosted probability notes provide deeper study of convergence, truncation, maximal inequalities and the general integrable strong law. Tao's central-limit notes provide further proof routes, including Lindeberg replacement and characteristic functions. This chapter uses an original exposition of classical proofs and original problems. MIT's mathematical-statistics lecture states quantitative Berry–Esseen bounds and limit transformations for further study. Its sharper constant also justifies the conservative C = 1 used here. Next we estimate unknown quantities from samples, separating bias, variance, consistency and standard errors. The present limit results supply tools; the sampling design and the target population still determine whether those tools answer the intended question."}, {"title": "03.07 · Sampling and estimation", "url": "v03-c07.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Start with the quantity and the sampling mechanism A bank surveys customers who opened its mobile application this week and reports an extremely precise estimate of financial stress. That precision does not establish the average stress of all its customers: application users may differ systematically from nonusers. Estimation requires three explicit objects: the estimand , the quantity sought; the sampling mechanism, which determines what can be observed; and the estimator, a rule for turning observations into a number. More observations can reduce dispersion around the wrong target. Write the unknown target as θ. An estimator T = T(X₁, …, Xₙ) is a random variable before the data are collected; an estimate t = T(x₁, …, xₙ) is its realized value. A statistic uses observed data and known design information, rather than unknown parameters. Its sampling distribution describes how it varies under the specified repeated-sampling mechanism. This is different from the distribution of a single observation and from a probability distribution over θ. There are two useful frameworks. In a finite-population, design-based analysis, values y₁, …, y N are fixed and selection is random. The target might be their mean θ = Σyᵢ/N. In a model-based analysis, observations have a stipulated probability law P θ ; the target might be that law's mean. The finite population and the modeled population are not automatically interchangeable. State the population, time period, unit of observation, measurement definition and target before selecting a formula. A customer-weighted mean and a dollar-weighted mean answer different questions. Probability sampling and design-based uncertainty In simple random sampling without replacement, each subset of n distinct units from N units has equal probability. Every unit has inclusion probability n/N. An ordered draw Y has mean θ and variance V pop = Σ(yᵢ − θ)²/N. Two different positions in the draw have covariance −V pop /(N − 1): their expected product averages yᵢyⱼ over i ≠ j, and Σ i≠j yᵢyⱼ = (Σyᵢ)² − Σyᵢ². Subtracting θ² gives the covariance. Consequently, for N > 1, S² = Σ(yᵢ − θ)²/(N − 1), E[X̄] = θ, Var(X̄) = [nV pop − n(n − 1)V pop /(N − 1)]/n² = (1 − n/N)S²/n. The factor 1 − n/N is the finite-population correction. Sampling a whole finite population removes selection uncertainty about its measured mean. It does not remove measurement error, uncertainty about future customers or model uncertainty. If N = 1, the census is deterministic and this variance formula with N − 1 in its denominator is unnecessary. For n ≥ 2, the sample variance s² = Σ(Xᵢ − X̄)²/(n − 1) is design-unbiased for S². To see this without assuming independent draws, use the identity s² = Σ a<b (Xₐ − Xᵦ)²/[n(n − 1)]. Each pair of positions is uniform over distinct population pairs. The corresponding population identity is S² = Σ i<j (yᵢ − yⱼ)²/[N(N − 1)]. Averaging the sample identity therefore gives S². An unbiased variance estimate for the sample mean is (1 − n/N)s²/n; its square root is an estimated standard error. The square root itself need not be unbiased. Stratified sampling separates the population into known, nonoverlapping groups. In group h, sample n h units without replacement from N h , independently across groups. Let W h = N h /N and S² h be its finite-population variance. Then θ̂ = Σ h W h X̄ h , E[θ̂] = θ, Var(θ̂) = Σ h W² h (1 − n h /N h )S² h /n h . The population shares determine weights. Equal weights on group means estimate an equally weighted group target unless population shares happen to be equal. Stratification can reduce uncertainty when known groups explain substantial variation; its benefit depends on allocation and the values within groups. Estimating each within-group variance requires at least two sampled units in a noncensus group. Unequal probabilities also admit unbiased estimation when the design is known. Let Iᵢ indicate whether unit i is included and let πᵢ = P(Iᵢ = 1) > 0. The Horvitz–Thompson total is T̂ = Σ Iᵢyᵢ/πᵢ. Linearity gives E[T̂] = Σyᵢ because E[Iᵢ] = πᵢ; independence of inclusion indicators is unnecessary. Divide by known N for an unbiased population-mean estimator. With joint inclusion probabilities πᵢⱼ, and πᵢᵢ = πᵢ, its variance is ΣᵢΣⱼ(πᵢⱼ − πᵢπⱼ)yᵢyⱼ/(πᵢπⱼ). Thus first-order weights alone do not specify uncertainty. If a relevant unit has πᵢ = 0, its unobserved value cannot be recovered by this design-based argument. Nonresponse creates another selection stage. Weighting respondents by initial selection probabilities alone does not establish unbiasedness; justified response probabilities or additional modeling assumptions are needed. Very small probabilities can produce unstable weights. Replacing the known N by a random estimated total generally gives a ratio estimator whose unbiasedness does not follow from the unbiasedness of its numerator and denominator. Source route: Kosuke Imai's survey-sampling notes provide design-based and unequal-inclusion-probability background. The derivations and synthetic designs here are independently developed. Worked example 1: changing the design at a fixed sample budget A synthetic population has six measured balances, in hundreds of dollars: 0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10. Its mean is 5 and S² = (25 + 9 + 1 + 1 + 9 + 25)/5 = 14. A simple random sample of two has mean variance (1 − 2/6)14/2 = 14/3. There are 15 equally likely pairs. Directly averaging their pair means gives 5; averaging squared deviations from 5 gives 14/3, independently checking the formula. If the observed pair is 2 and 8, its mean is 5, sample variance is 18, estimated variance of its mean is (2/3)18/2 = 6, and estimated standard error is √6 ≈ 2.449 hundreds of dollars. The realized estimate happens to equal the population mean. That coincidence does not make its design variance zero. Now divide the same six units into the groups {0, 2, 4} and {6, 8, 10}, whose means are 2 and 8 and whose S² values are both 4. Independently select one unit from each group. The weighted mean uses weights 1/2 and has variance 2(1/2)²(1 − 1/3)4 = 4/3. The nine equally likely cross-group pairs verify this result. Both designs observe two units; stratification uses the known grouping to obtain lower true variance here. With only one observation per group, however, the usual within-group sample-variance estimator is unavailable. The displayed stratified variance uses the stipulated full population, not information a two-unit survey would reveal. Bias, variance, mean squared error and consistency For an estimator with finite second moment, bias θ (T) = E θ [T] − θ and mean squared error MSE θ (T) = E θ [(T − θ)²]. Writing T − θ = (T − E[T]) + (E[T] − θ), the cross term has expectation zero. Therefore MSE θ (T) = Var θ (T) + bias² θ (T). Bias is a repeated-sampling property, not the observed error t − θ. An unbiased estimate can be far from θ in a particular sample. An estimator with a little bias can have lower MSE than an unbiased competitor. The ranking depends on the target, model, parameter value and loss criterion; no universal ranking follows from the word “unbiased.” A sequence Tₙ is consistent for θ when Tₙ → P θ under the specified model as n increases. For iid observations of mean μ and finite variance σ², the sample mean is unbiased with variance σ²/n and is consistent by Chebyshev. The estimator using only X₁ remains unbiased but, for a nondegenerate law, is not consistent: its error distribution never changes with n. Conversely X̄ₙ + 1/n is biased for each n and consistent. Consistency describes a sequence and asymptotic experiment. For a fixed finite population, sample size cannot increase beyond N; do not casually import an iid asymptotic claim. A simple shrinkage rule is T a = aX̄ + (1 − a)μ₀, with fixed a and reference μ₀. Under the iid finite-variance model its bias is (1 − a)(μ₀ − μ), variance is a²σ²/n and MSE is their sum. It reduces variance by pulling estimates toward a reference, at the cost of error when the reference is wrong. Fixed a ≠ 1 generally leaves a limiting bias. If aₙ → 1, consistency is restored under the weak law. Choosing a from the data changes the calculation; it cannot be treated as fixed afterward. Observation dispersion versus estimator precision A standard error is the sampling standard deviation of an estimator. For the iid mean it is σ/√n, in the same units as the mean. The observation standard deviation σ describes dispersion of individual observations. Dividing by √n is justified here by independence and a common variance; Chapter 6's shared-shock model shows why the same division can be badly wrong with dependence. For iid data and n ≥ 2, s² = Σ(Xᵢ − X̄)²/(n − 1) is unbiased for σ². The identity Σ(Xᵢ − X̄)² = Σ(Xᵢ − μ)² − n(X̄ − μ)² has expectation nσ² − σ² = (n − 1)σ². Its square root estimates observation dispersion; s/√n estimates the mean's standard error. Under finite second moments, s² is also consistent: write it as [n/(n − 1)][n⁻¹ΣXᵢ² − X̄²], apply the integrable weak law to Xᵢ² and to Xᵢ, and use continuity. No independence between X̄ and s² is required for that argument. A plug-in standard error replaces unknown population quantities by estimates. For an iid Bernoulli sample, p̂ = K/n and the plug-in standard error is √[p̂(1 − p̂)/n]. When K = 0 or n, this is zero even though many population probabilities remain compatible with the data. Boundary samples make the plug-in estimate unreliable; the next chapter constructs intervals that retain uncertainty. Heavy tails, cluster dependence, changing populations and missingness require their own variance arguments. Reporting a standard error without its design or model omits the justification for it. Constructing estimators from moments and likelihood The method of moments equates population moments implied by a model to sample moments. For Bernoulli data E[X] = p suggests p̂ = X̄. For an exponential waiting time with rate r > 0, E[X] = 1/r suggests r̂ = 1/X̄ when X̄ > 0. A moment equation may have several solutions, no solution in the parameter space, or no identifying information. Population moments must exist, and a consistent sample moment must be available before continuity can justify a consistent transformed estimate. Likelihood instead fixes the observed data and varies the parameter. If their joint mass or density is f θ (x), the likelihood is L(θ; x) = f θ (x). Under independent observations it factors as ∏f θ (xᵢ). Maximizing L over the stated parameter space defines a maximum-likelihood estimator (MLE), when a maximizer exists. Where L is positive, maximizing ℓ = log L is equivalent and converts products into sums. Factors independent of θ can be omitted. Parameter-dependent support restrictions cannot. A density value is not the probability of the exact continuous observation. Likelihood is a function of θ, not automatically a normalized probability density over θ. Producing a posterior requires a prior and normalization, covered in Chapter 9. Maximization also does not by itself prove unbiasedness, consistency or a normal limit. Those are distinct claims with additional conditions. Bernoulli maximum likelihood, including endpoints For n iid Bernoulli observations with K = k, L(p) = pᵏ(1 − p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ, p ∈ [0, 1], using the continuous extension at endpoints. For 0 < k < n, ℓ′(p) = k/p − (n − k)/(1 − p) vanishes at p = k/n. The second derivative −k/p² − (n − k)/(1 − p)² is negative, and endpoint likelihoods are zero, proving the unique global maximum. If k = 0, (1 − p)ⁿ decreases and the maximum is p = 0; if k = n, pⁿ increases and the maximum is p = 1. On the open space (0, 1), those endpoint cases have a supremum but no maximizer. The parameter space matters. Exponential rate and its units For positive waiting times x₁, …, xₙ, L(r) = rⁿ exp(−rΣxᵢ), r > 0. If T = Σxᵢ > 0, ℓ′(r) = n/r − T, ℓ″(r) = −n/r², and r̂ = n/T = 1/X̄ is the unique maximum. As r approaches zero or infinity, the log likelihood tends to −∞, confirming global optimality. A time measured in days produces a rate in events per day. The iid weak law and continuity at 1/r > 0 prove consistency, but the reciprocal's expectation is not automatically r. If every recorded time is zero, the formula has no finite solution: under the ideal continuous model this sample has probability zero, and rounded measurements require a measurement model. Observation rules enter likelihood. For independently, noninformatively right-censored exponential observations with known censoring limits, a completed event contributes r exp(−rt) and a censored event contributes exp(−rt). With d completed events and total observed exposure T, the likelihood is proportional to rᵈ exp(−rT), giving d/T for d > 0. Dropping censored observations changes the experiment. Informative censoring requires a joint model; the survival contribution alone is not sufficient. A support-dependent maximum For iid uniform observations on [0, θ], θ > 0, let M = max xᵢ. For data with M > 0, L(θ) = θ⁻ⁿ I {θ≥M} . It decreases over its feasible region, so θ̂ = M is the maximum at a support boundary. Ignoring the indicator and searching for an interior derivative root would miss it. For 0 ≤ m ≤ θ, P(M ≤ m) = (m/θ)ⁿ; differentiating and integrating m against this density gives E[M] = nθ/(n + 1). Thus M is biased downward, while (n + 1)M/n is unbiased. For 0 < ε < θ, P(|M − θ| > ε) = (1 − ε/θ)ⁿ → 0, proving consistency. The endpoint convention for a density is taken as specified here. Claims about regular, interior MLE behavior cannot be transferred to this model merely because it has an MLE. Source route: MIT 18.05's maximum-likelihood reading supplies introductory likelihood background. The parameter-space audit and original waiting-time/support examples make the assumptions explicit. The bootstrap: estimate a sampling law by resampling The ordinary nonparametric iid bootstrap replaces the unknown observation distribution F by the empirical distribution Fₙ: each observed index has probability 1/n. Independently draw n indices with replacement , obtain X₁*, …, Xₙ*, and recompute T* using the same estimator rule. Repeat B times to approximate its conditional distribution, given the actual data. If values repeat, their masses add. The stars denote simulated resamples, not newly observed units. The bootstrap principle is that, for suitable statistics and data-generating laws, the distribution of T* − T given the observations approximates the distribution of T − θ in the original sampling experiment. This is a conditional approximation, not a theorem for every estimator. For the iid mean, finite positive variance underlies the familiar large-sample use. Smooth statistics of sample moments have related validity conditions. Dependence, extreme-value estimators, infinite moments, boundary parameters and selection can invalidate the ordinary procedure. A full general bootstrap convergence theorem is further study rather than a result proved here. For the resampled mean, the conditional moments are exact and elementary. E*[Xᵢ*] = X̄ and Var*[Xᵢ*] = vₙ = n⁻¹Σ(xᵢ − X̄)². Independence within a resample therefore gives E*[X̄*] = X̄ and Var*[X̄*] = vₙ/n = (n − 1)s²/n². This is a property of the empirical sampling law. It does not assert that this finite-sample variance equals the original, unknown σ²/n. Worked example 2: exactly enumerating a small bootstrap Four synthetic iid observations are 2, 4, 4, 10 dollars. Their mean is 5, squared deviations sum to 36, and s² = 12 dollars squared. The iid plug-in standard error of the mean is √(12/4) = √3 ≈ 1.732 dollars. The empirical distribution gives masses 1/4, 1/2, 1/4 to 2, 4, 10. Its variance is 36/4 = 9, so the conditional bootstrap mean variance is 9/4 and bootstrap standard deviation is 1.5 dollars. There are 4⁴ = 256 equally likely ordered index resamples. Enumerating their means gives mean 5 and variance 9/4, confirming the conditional calculation. Treating the three distinct values as equally likely would use a different distribution. Drawing all four observations without replacement would merely permute them, leave every mean equal to 5 and give variance zero. A large B approximates the empirical-law dispersion more accurately. It cannot turn four actual observations into thousands of independent observations. The difference between √3 and 1.5 here is the finite-sample factor √(3/4), not a new finding about population risk. Real applications must also justify that the observations are sampled from the intended population and can be resampled independently. Preserve the sampling unit. For iid paired measurements, resample complete pairs so their joint relationship survives. For independent clusters, a cluster bootstrap resamples clusters, with a variance interpretation tied to that design. Time series require methods that preserve relevant dependence, such as appropriately justified block procedures; shuffling individual dates does not achieve that. No bootstrap can learn an entirely unobserved population segment or unseen tail behavior without additional assumptions. The uniform endpoint provides a concrete failure. For continuous observations, the sample maximum is unique almost surely. An n-observation empirical resample includes its index with probability 1 − (1 − 1/n)ⁿ, so its maximum M* equals M with this positive probability, approaching 1 − e⁻¹. The true normalized endpoint gap n(θ − M)/θ has CDF 1 − (1 − t/n)ⁿ for 0 ≤ t ≤ n and tends to the continuous CDF 1 − e⁻ᵗ. The bootstrap normalized gap n(M − M*)/M has a persistent atom at zero, incompatible with that continuous limit. Merely increasing B cannot repair this discrepancy. Source route: MIT 18.05's bootstrap reading introduces empirical resampling and its approximation principle. The conditional moment calculation and endpoint counterexample here state the limits of that principle. Exercises Let Xᵢ be iid with mean μ and variance 9. Compare Aₙ = X₁ and Bₙ = X̄ₙ + 2/n for bias, variance, MSE and consistency. Give a proof or a nondegenerate example for each consistency conclusion. The finite population is {0, 2, 6, 8}, measured in dollars. List all six equally likely sample means from simple random samples of two without replacement. Compute their mean and variance, and verify the finite-population formula. For the observed pair {2, 8}, calculate the estimate and estimated standard error. A design selects exactly one of three units with values 10, 20, 30 dollars and probabilities 1/2, 1/3, 1/6. Find the Horvitz–Thompson total's three possible estimates, expectation and variance. Compare the expected unweighted selected value with the population mean. Obtain an unbiased mean estimator and its variance. Two strata contain 80 and 20 units. Independent simple random samples without replacement have sizes 8 and 4, sample means 12 and 35 dollars. Compute the weighted mean and compare it with the equally weighted average. If the true finite-population variances are 16 and 36 dollars squared, compute the estimator's true variance and standard error. Why is this not an estimate of variance based only on the reported sample means? Twenty iid Bernoulli observations contain three successes. Derive the MLE and compute its plug-in standard error. If instead there were no successes, describe the likelihood maximum on [0, 1] and on (0, 1). Explain why zero plug-in standard error does not establish that p is zero. Four independent exponential waiting times are 1, 2, 4, 5 days. Derive and calculate the rate MLE. Separately, two completed events are observed after 1 and 2 days and two other events are independently right-censored after 4 and 5 days. Under the noninformative exponential observation model, calculate the rate MLE and explain the change. Four iid uniform [0, θ] observations are 0.2, 0.4, 0.7, 0.8 dollars. Find the MLE and its unbiased correction; derive E[M]. Compute P(M ≤ 0.8θ). What is the conditional probability that a four-observation empirical-bootstrap maximum equals the observed maximum? Explain the bootstrap failure at the endpoint. The data are 0, 3, 6 dollars. Find the empirical variance, sample variance, iid plug-in standard error and exact conditional variance of the bootstrap mean. How many ordered index resamples are there? What happens to the variance if resampling is without replacement? State two additional checks required before using this bootstrap on a customer survey. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria Aₙ has zero bias, variance and MSE 9. Bₙ has bias 2/n, variance 9/n and MSE 9/n + 4/n². The weak law and 2/n → 0 give Bₙ → P μ. Aₙ's law does not change; since its variance is 9, it is not almost surely equal to μ, and there exists an ε > 0 with P(|X₁ − μ| > ε) > 0. That probability stays fixed, ruling out consistency. For example X₁ = μ ± 3 with equal probabilities is outside an error tolerance 1 with probability one. Bias and consistency give different rankings. The means are 1, 3, 4, 4, 5, 7. Their expectation is 4 and variance is (9 + 1 + 0 + 0 + 1 + 9)/6 = 10/3 dollars squared. The population S² = (16 + 4 + 4 + 16)/3 = 40/3; (1 − 2/4)S²/2 = 10/3. The observed mean is 5, s² = [(2 − 5)² + (8 − 5)²]/1 = 18, estimated mean variance is (1/2)18/2 = 9/2, and estimated standard error is 3/√2 ≈ 2.121 dollars. Estimated and true design variances differ. The totals are 20, 60, 180. Their mean is 10 + 20 + 30 = 60. Their second moment is 200 + 1,200 + 5,400 = 6,800, so variance is 6,800 − 60² = 3,200 dollars squared. The unweighted selected value has expectation 5 + 20/3 + 5 = 50/3, below the population mean 20. Divide each total estimate by 3: values 20/3, 20, 60 have mean 20 and variance 3,200/9. The design samples exactly one unit, so inclusion indicators cannot be independent; the unbiasedness argument never needed them to be. Population weights are 0.8 and 0.2, giving 0.8(12) + 0.2(35) = 16.6 dollars. The equally weighted average is 23.5 and addresses a different group-weighted target. True variance is 0.8²(1 − 8/80)16/8 + 0.2²(1 − 4/20)36/4 = 1.152 + 0.288 = 1.44 dollars squared; standard error is 1.2 dollars. The calculation uses stipulated true within-stratum variances. Two sample means alone do not reveal sample within-stratum dispersions; replacing true variances requires the individual data or sample variances. The log-likelihood derivative 3/p − 17/(1 − p) gives p̂ = 3/20 = 0.15; its negative second derivative establishes the interior maximum. Plug-in variance is 0.15(0.85)/20 = 0.006375 and standard error ≈ 0.079844, a probability scale. With k = 0 the likelihood (1 − p)²⁰ decreases: its closed-space maximum is p = 0 and its open-space supremum is approached as p ↓ 0 but not attained. Positive p still assigns positive probability (1 − p)²⁰ to no successes. The zero plug-in dispersion is a boundary defect, not conclusive population evidence. With four completions, total time is 12 days and ℓ = 4 log r − 12r, so r̂ = 1/3 event per day. With two completions and two noninformative censored observations, total observed exposure is still 12 days but ℓ = 2 log r − 12r, giving r̂ = 1/6 event per day. Censored units contribute exposure with no completed event. Dropping them would wrongly use total time 3 and estimate 2/3. Correctness depends on the specified censoring model; informative censoring would require another likelihood. M = 0.8 dollars maximizes θ⁻⁴ on θ ≥ 0.8. The unbiased correction is (5/4)0.8 = 1 dollar. Since f M (m) = 4m³/θ⁴ on [0, θ], integrating mf M gives 4θ/5. The probability is 0.8⁴ = 0.4096. A bootstrap maximum equals 0.8 unless its index is missed on all four draws: probability 1 − (3/4)⁴ = 175/256 ≈ 0.683594. This atom persists asymptotically; the true scaled endpoint-gap limit is continuous. The ordinary empirical maximum bootstrap does not consistently approximate that limit. Mean is 3, empirical variance is (9 + 0 + 9)/3 = 6 dollars squared, and s² = 18/2 = 9. The iid plug-in standard error is √(9/3) = √3 dollars. Conditional bootstrap mean variance is 6/3 = 2 dollars squared. There are 3³ = 27 ordered index resamples. Sampling all three without replacement only permutes the data and gives mean variance zero. An adequate survey audit specifies the intended population and selection/nonresponse probabilities, and checks whether independent persons, households or clusters are the actual resampling unit. Answers that simply propose a larger B do not resolve either problem. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain estimator versus estimate, observation standard deviation versus standard error, and bias versus consistency to someone evaluating a customer survey. Derive one design variance and one likelihood maximum. Require the listener to identify the target and randomness before accepting either formula. Delayed check: after a week, derive MSE = variance + squared bias without notes, recover the finite-population correction from negative draw covariance, and derive the bootstrap mean variance from the empirical distribution. Then explain why a million application-user responses and a million bootstrap replications solve different numerical problems but neither identifies excluded customers' outcomes. Continue to confidence intervals and hypothesis tests , where point estimates become procedures with explicit uncertainty and error probabilities. More general efficient estimation, information bounds and bootstrap asymptotics belong to the later econometrics volumes; the procedures here provide their necessary model, design and parameter-space discipline."}, {"title": "03.08 · Confidence intervals and hypothesis tests", "url": "v03-c08.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "What uncertainty statements promise A precisely estimated improvement can be too small to justify its cost. A large estimated improvement can have too much uncertainty to support a confident conclusion. A confidence interval describes the precision of a specified estimation procedure; a hypothesis test controls a specified kind of mistaken rejection. Neither substitutes for choosing the right economic target, collecting credible data or evaluating the consequences of a decision. Use θ for a fixed unknown parameter, X for random data and C(X) for a random set constructed by a known rule. A confidence procedure with level 1 − α satisfies P θ {θ ∈ C(X)} ≥ 1 − α for every θ in the stated model. An exact procedure has a finite-sample guarantee; equality need not hold because discrete distributions can make it conservative. An asymptotic procedure approaches the stated coverage under a specified sequence of experiments and assumptions. Nominal coverage is the label; actual coverage is the probability under the true law. Once data x have been observed, the frequentist parameter remains fixed and C(x) is fixed. Coverage describes the procedure's behavior over hypothetical repetition, rather than supplying a posterior probability that θ lies in this particular interval. A credible interval answers a posterior question under a prior and likelihood; Chapter 9 develops it. A confidence interval for a population mean is also different from a prediction interval for a new individual observation. Individual dispersion generally persists when uncertainty about the mean shrinks. Derive an interval from a pivot A pivot has a known distribution that does not depend on the unknown parameter. Suppose X₁, …, Xₙ are iid normal with mean μ and known standard deviation σ > 0. The normal-sum result gives Z = √n(X̄ − μ)/σ ∼ N(0, 1) exactly. Let z q = Φ⁻¹(q); in particular z 0.975 ≈ 1.959964. The event −z 1−α/2 ≤ Z ≤ z 1−α/2 has probability 1 − α. Solving its two inequalities for μ gives C(X) = [X̄ − z 1−α/2 σ/√n, X̄ + z 1−α/2 σ/√n]. This is an exact normal-model interval, with the same units as μ. A one-sided lower confidence bound is X̄ − z 1−α σ/√n, because P(Z ≤ z 1−α ) = 1 − α. The corresponding interval is that bound through +∞. A one-sided 95% bound uses z 0.95 ≈ 1.644854; a two-sided 95% interval uses 1.959964. They answer different questions. Without exact normal observations, Chapter 6's iid finite-positive-variance CLT makes the normal pivot an asymptotic approximation. With a consistent s, Slutsky's theorem permits replacing σ by s. Approximate coverage then follows because the limiting normal CDF is continuous at the two cutoffs. It does not follow that n = 30, or any other universal number, guarantees good coverage. Dependence, extreme skewness and heavy tails may demand another argument, another standard error, or another procedure. A zero empirical variance does not establish that the population variance is zero. Unknown normal variance: why Student's t appears For iid normal observations with unknown σ² > 0 and n ≥ 2, the statistic √n(X̄ − μ)/s has a Student t distribution with ν = n − 1 degrees of freedom. Here is the structural proof. Standardize the data to independent N(0, 1) variables Zᵢ = (Xᵢ − μ)/σ. Their joint density is proportional to exp(−ΣZᵢ²/2). Choose an orthonormal coordinate system whose first axis is (1, …, 1)/√n, extending it by the orthogonal-basis construction in Volume 2. The coordinate change preserves squared norm and has absolute Jacobian determinant one, so the transformed density again factors into n independent standard normal densities. The first coordinate is Z₀ = √n(X̄ − μ)/σ. The remaining coordinates describe residuals, whose squared norm is V = Σ(Xᵢ − X̄)²/σ² = (n − 1)s²/σ². Thus Z₀ is independent of V; V is the sum of ν independent squared standard normals, a law called chi-square with ν degrees of freedom. Define t ν as the law of Z₀/√(V/ν). Substitution yields the studentized mean. This proves the relevant pivot without assuming independence between an arbitrary sample mean and its variance: it is the special normal density that supplies the independence. Exact normal-model interval: X̄ ± t ν,1−α/2 s/√n, ν = n − 1. The t law is symmetric and has thicker tails than the standard normal because its denominator fluctuates. At 95% confidence, t 3,0.975 ≈ 3.182446 and t 9,0.975 ≈ 2.262157. Using n instead of n − 1 degrees of freedom changes the procedure. For nonnormal iid data, the rotation factorization generally fails. The studentized mean can still have an asymptotic normal limit with finite positive variance, but the finite-sample t law is no longer guaranteed. A four-observation t interval rests heavily on its normal model. Source route: MIT 18.05's normal-confidence-interval reading supplies interval and test-inversion background. The orthogonal-coordinate proof here uses the library's earlier matrix geometry and normal joint density. Testing, size and power A hypothesis specifies a set of possible data-generating laws. Write H₀: θ ∈ Θ₀ and H₁: θ ∈ Θ₁ for disjoint null and alternative sets. A simple hypothesis specifies a single law; a composite one allows multiple laws or nuisance parameters. For example H₀: μ = 0 with unknown σ is composite as a statement about the full normal law. A rejection rule is a predeclared function of the observations, often “reject when the statistic falls in R.” Its size is sup θ∈Θ₀ P θ (reject). A level-α rule has size at most α. A discrete rule's actual size may be strictly below α. Rejecting a true null is a Type I error. Failing to reject a false null is a Type II error. At a particular alternative θ, power is P θ (reject), and its complement β(θ) is the Type II error probability. Power changes with the alternative, noise, sample size and test design. It is not 1 minus the observed p-value. Failure to reject can reflect a true null, a small effect or insufficient precision. It does not prove the null. Choose the direction before seeing results. For a known-σ normal mean, the two-sided level-α test of μ = μ₀ rejects when |√n(X̄ − μ₀)/σ| > z 1−α/2 . A right-sided test of μ ≤ μ₀ against μ > μ₀ rejects when √n(X̄ − μ₀)/σ > z 1−α . Its worst-case null mean is μ₀: lowering μ shifts the statistic left and decreases rejection probability. A left-sided test reverses the direction. Choosing a favorable one-sided direction after seeing the data changes the error calculation. The null is not privileged as a substantive truth. Its role is to identify the error whose probability the rule controls. Testing whether an investment's expected benefit exceeds its implementation cost can be more relevant than testing whether the benefit is exactly zero. The target, threshold and loss from a mistaken decision must be justified economically. Source route: MIT 18.05's introductory significance-testing reading provides terminology for nulls, alternatives and the two error types. p-values are calibrated tail summaries For a simple null law and a prechosen statistic T whose larger values indicate departures, p(x) = P₀{T(X′) ≥ T(x)}, where X′ denotes hypothetical data under that null. The observed statistic is fixed inside this probability. For a continuous null law, the probability integral transform makes p uniform on (0, 1). With atoms and inclusive tails it is conservative: P₀(p ≤ u) ≤ u. In a finite discrete law, order possible statistics from largest downward. The event p ≤ u consists of a tail whose summed probability is at most u; that sum is exactly its null probability. This proves calibration without assuming a continuous distribution. A valid p-value satisfies this inequality under every null law. Rejecting when p ≤ α therefore yields a level-α test. For composite nulls, using the supremum of the relevant null tail probabilities is one way to obtain validity. In an iid binomial model, coupling successes as I {Uᵢ≤p} using the same uniform Uᵢ shows that the count increases with p. Thus an upper-tail test of p ≤ p₀ is calibrated at the worst-case boundary p₀. The same reasoning fails if independence or a common success probability is absent. For a standard-normal statistic z, the right-sided p-value is 1 − Φ(z), the left-sided one is Φ(z), and the two-sided one based on |Z| is 2[1 − Φ(|z|)]. A two-sided discrete test requires an explicit extremeness rule. Twice the smaller inclusive binomial tail, capped at one, is a valid choice: by a union bound each tail contributes at most α/2 to rejection. Ordering outcomes by null probability is another choice and can produce a different answer. State the rule rather than treating a software label as a definition. A p-value is not P(H₀ | data), the probability that an observed effect was “caused by chance,” or the probability that a published conclusion is false. Those questions require other assumptions. A small p-value can reflect a departure from the target null, an invalid variance model, a selection rule or other misspecification. A large value can coexist with economically important effects when power is low. Report the estimate, units, uncertainty, design and analysis choices so readers can evaluate the evidence. Source route: the American Statistical Association's 2016 statement emphasizes model-conditional interpretation, reporting and the limits of threshold-based conclusions. Our calculations below show those distinctions in explicit experiments. Worked example 1: a detectable benefit need not justify its cost In a stipulated synthetic normal model, n = 64 independent measured monthly benefits have known σ = 4 dollars and observed mean 1.2 dollars. The standard error is 4/8 = 0.5 dollar. The exact two-sided 95% confidence interval is 1.2 ± 1.959964(0.5) = [0.220018, 2.179982] dollars. For H₀: μ = 0 against μ ≠ 0, z = 1.2/0.5 = 2.4, giving p = 2[1 − Φ(2.4)] ≈ 0.016395. The rule rejects at 5%. The interval excludes zero because it is the inversion of that same two-sided rule. Neither result says the null has posterior probability 1.64%. Suppose delivering the benefit costs 2 dollars per person per month. The relevant superiority claim is μ > 2. For H₀: μ ≤ 2, the right-sided statistic is (1.2 − 2)/0.5 = −1.6 and p = 1 − Φ(−1.6) ≈ 0.945201. The one-sided 95% lower bound is 1.2 − 1.644854(0.5) = 0.377573, far below 2. The data do not support the asserted cost-covering benefit. The point estimate of net benefit is −0.8 dollar; uncertainty and decision costs still matter. An observational measurement of an association would also require a causal design before being interpreted as the effect of implementing a program. Power is computed under alternatives, before collecting data For the right-sided known-σ test, let δ = μ − μ₀ > 0 be a planning alternative. Under that law the null-standardized statistic is N(δ√n/σ, 1). Therefore Power(μ₀ + δ) = 1 − Φ(z 1−α − δ√n/σ). For power at least 1 − β: n ≥ [(z 1−α + z 1−β )σ/δ]². Round n up to an integer. The formula assumes independent normal observations, known σ, a fixed final sample size, the stated one-sided rule and no other testing adjustments. Attrition and clustering can change the needed design. With σ = 4 dollars, δ = 1 dollar, α = 0.05 and desired power 0.8, n ≥ [(1.644854 + 0.841621)4]² ≈ 98.921, so 99 observations suffice in this model. For the two-sided test, let d = δ√n/σ. Its power is 1 − Φ(z 1−α/2 − d) + Φ(−z 1−α/2 − d), the sum of probabilities beyond the two rejection cutoffs. In Worked example 1's design, an alternative μ = 1 versus μ₀ = 0 has d = 2 and power ≈ 0.516005. A 5% false-positive rate and about 52% power are quite compatible. Quoting an observed p-value as “power,” or calculating “observed power” by plugging the observed effect into this planning formula, does not supply independent evidence about the unknown effect. Source route: MIT 18.05's normal-testing reading supports the model-specific z and t procedures; the displayed alternative-law power and planning calculations are derived here. Test inversion and discrete intervals For every candidate θ₀, construct a level-α test of H₀: θ = θ₀. Define C(X) as all candidates not rejected by their corresponding test. At the true θ, the event θ ∉ C(X) is precisely rejection of its true null, with probability at most α. This proves the confidence-set guarantee. The resulting set need not always be a single interval. Interval/test agreement requires matching confidence level, tail choices, nuisance treatment and model. Comparing a one-sided test to an unmatched two-sided interval loses that agreement. For a binomial count K ∼ Bin(n, p), the simple Wald interval p̂ ± z√[p̂(1 − p̂)/n] is a large-sample approximation with poor boundary behavior. At k = 0 it degenerates to [0, 0]. Clipping an interval to [0, 1] cannot fix that missing uncertainty. Inverting the approximate score condition (p̂ − p)² ≤ z²p(1 − p)/n instead gives the Wilson interval. Multiplication by n and collection of terms gives (n + z²)p² − (2np̂ + z²)p + np̂² ≤ 0. The leading coefficient is positive, so the admissible interval lies between its quadratic roots: Center = [p̂ + z²/(2n)]/[1 + z²/n], Half-width = z√[p̂(1 − p̂)/n + z²/(4n²)]/[1 + z²/n]. Its endpoints are the center minus and plus the half-width. They lie in [0, 1]. At k = 0 its upper endpoint is z²/(n + z²), which is positive. This useful boundary behavior does not turn an approximate normal-score construction into an exact uniform coverage guarantee. An exact equal-tail binomial interval can instead invert the two inclusive tails. For k > 0, its lower endpoint p L solves P pL (K ≥ k) = α/2; for k = 0 take p L = 0. For k < n, its upper endpoint p U solves P pU (K ≤ k) = α/2; for k = n take p U = 1. These tails are monotone, so bisection can find the roots. Each one-sided exclusion event has probability at most α/2 by valid-tail calibration. Their union has probability at most α, proving coverage at least 1 − α. This is the Clopper–Pearson construction; discreteness generally makes it conservative. For zero successes, the one-sided 1 − α upper bound solves (1 − p U )ⁿ = α, so p U = 1 − α 1/n . With n = 20 and α = 0.05 this is 0.139108. A two-sided equal-tail 95% interval uses α/2 and gives upper bound 0.168433. Both retain uncertainty that the zero plug-in standard error erased. The binomial model still requires iid trials with a common probability and a fixed observation rule. Source route: the NIST/SEMATECH handbook gives Wilson score formulas and exact binomial tail-equation intervals. The library derives the inversion, endpoint cases and coverage argument explicitly. Worked example 2: exact size is not always the nominal level A synthetic experiment makes 12 independent binary observations. Test H₀: p ≤ 1/2 against p > 1/2 at nominal α = 0.05. Suppose ten successes are observed. Under the worst-case null boundary, p-value = P 1/2 (K ≥ 10) = [C(12, 10) + C(12, 11) + C(12, 12)]/2¹² = 79/4096 ≈ 0.019287. The nonrandomized upper-tail rule rejects at K ≥ 10. Its actual size is 79/4096, below 0.05. Including K = 9 would give (220 + 66 + 12 + 1)/4096 = 299/4096 ≈ 0.072998, exceeding the allowed level. Arbitrarily replacing the first rule's actual size by 0.05 would misdescribe the experiment. At the alternative p = 3/4, the rule's power is Σ j=10 12 C(12, j)(3/4)ʲ(1/4)¹²⁻ʲ = 6,554,439/16,777,216 ≈ 0.390675. A small p-value in this observed sample coexists with relatively low power in the design. The point estimate is 10/12 ≈ 0.833333. The one-sided 95% lower confidence bound is approximately 0.561895, solving P pL (K ≥ 10) = 0.05. It exceeds 1/2, matching the right-sided rejection. The two-sided equal-tail 95% interval is approximately [0.515862, 0.979137]. Its corresponding doubled-smaller-tail p-value at 1/2 is 158/4096 ≈ 0.038574, also rejecting. These are different but explicitly matched tail procedures, not interchangeable confidence labels. Multiple testing, selection and error families If five independent valid continuous-null p-values are each compared with 0.05, the probability of at least one false rejection is 1 − 0.95⁵ ≈ 0.226219. Individual 5% calibration does not imply 5% error for a family of findings. Independence is needed for this product calculation; it is unnecessary for the corrections below. Let m be a prespecified number of tested hypotheses, R the number rejected and V the unobserved number of rejected true nulls. The familywise error rate is FWER = P(V ≥ 1). Bonferroni rejects each hypothesis with pᵢ ≤ α/m. If m₀ of the nulls are true, a union bound and each p-value's validity give P(V ≥ 1) ≤ Σ true i P(pᵢ ≤ α/m) ≤ m₀α/m ≤ α. This is strong control: it holds under every configuration of true and false nulls and arbitrary dependence, provided the individual true-null p-values are valid. Holm's step-down rule improves on this simple correction. Sort p-values p (1) ≤ … ≤ p (m) , with their hypotheses. At step i reject if p (i) ≤ α/(m − i + 1) and continue; at the first failure stop and leave that and all later hypotheses unrejected. The stopping step is essential. For a proof of strong control, suppose m₀ > 0 and let i be the position of the first true null in the ordering. Since all m₀ true nulls remain at that position, m − i + 1 ≥ m₀. Any false rejection requires rejecting that first true null and hence min true j pⱼ ≤ α/(m − i + 1) ≤ α/m₀. A union bound over the m₀ true nulls bounds this event by α. When m₀ = 0 no false rejection is possible. No independent-test assumption enters this proof. A different criterion is the false discovery rate, FDR = E[V/max(R, 1)]. It controls an expected fraction among rejections, not the probability of any mistaken rejection. When all nulls are true these coincide; otherwise FDR ≤ FWER because V/max(R, 1) ≤ I {V≥1} . Large exploratory families may justify an FDR target with a procedure and dependence conditions chosen for that purpose. This chapter proves FWER control; advanced FDR procedures and their assumptions are further study, rather than silently treating these two criteria as equivalent. Source route: Xiaodong Li's UC Davis teaching notes cover familywise and false-discovery criteria, Bonferroni and Holm. The library supplies its own explicit proofs and synthetic exercise family. Define the family around the claims being made, including outcomes, subgroups, model variants and analyses tried. Reporting only the smallest p-value changes the reporting experiment. Dividing α by the number of displayed results does not account for omitted searches. Preregistering a primary analysis, documenting all analyses, using appropriately separated confirmation data and choosing justified selective or sequential methods can help. Each requires its own design conditions; the phrase “preregistered” is not a substitute for them. A fixed-sample p-value also does not automatically remain valid if collection stops when it becomes small. Even two independent experiments with individually uniform null p-values, reported as a success whenever either is at most 0.05, have error 1 − 0.95² = 0.0975. Repeated interim looks at overlapping data are dependent, so that particular product is no longer their exact error calculation; calibration still must account for the actual stopping rule. Sequential testing is developed with appropriate stochastic tools later. Evidence for a negligible effect requires an equivalence question Failing to reject μ = 0 does not demonstrate that μ is economically negligible. Define a meaningful margin Δ > 0 in the outcome's units before seeing results. Equivalence asks whether −Δ < μ < Δ. Its null is the union {μ ≤ −Δ} ∪ {μ ≥ Δ}. Under the known-σ normal model, perform two one-sided level-α tests: reject the lower component when (X̄ + Δ)/SE > z 1−α and reject the upper component when (X̄ − Δ)/SE < −z 1−α . Declare equivalence only if both components are rejected. This intersection rule has size at most α: under any value in the union null, at least one component is true, and rejecting both implies rejecting that true component, an event with probability at most α. No independence between the tests is needed. Unlike rejecting whenever any test succeeds, this rule requires every test to succeed. Algebraically it is equivalent to placing the two-sided 1 − 2α interval entirely inside (−Δ, Δ). At α = 0.05 that is a 90% interval, despite the equivalence test itself having 5% size. For X̄ = 0.1 dollar, known SE = 0.2 dollar and Δ = 0.5 dollar, the 90% interval is [−0.228971, 0.428971], strictly inside the chosen equivalence range. Equivalence is supported at 5% in that model, although the usual two-sided zero-effect test has z = 0.5 and does not reject. “Equivalent within the specified margin” is a precise claim; “proved exactly zero” is not. If margins were picked after seeing results, or the sampling model were wrong, the stated calibration would need reassessment. Exercises In the known-σ iid normal model, n = 64, σ = 4 dollars and observed mean is 0.8 dollar. Calculate the two-sided 95% interval and p-value for μ = 0, using Φ(1.6) = 0.945200708. Also calculate the right-sided p-value for μ ≤ 0. Explain why neither p-value is a posterior probability. Assume iid normal observations with unknown variance. The four data are 2, 4, 4, 10 dollars. Calculate the exact normal-model 95% t interval using t 3,0.975 = 3.182446305. Would the matching two-sided test reject μ = 0? Explain why replacing that critical value by 1.959964 would not be exact, and which claim changes if normality is removed. Twenty iid Bernoulli observations have zero successes. Compare the Wald 95% interval, the Wilson upper endpoint at z = 1.959964, the exact one-sided 95% upper bound and the exact two-sided equal-tail 95% upper bound. Derive the exact bounds. State which procedure has the finite-sample coverage guarantee and why the confidence labels matter. Test p ≤ 1/2 against p > 1/2 using ten iid Bernoulli observations, with nine successes observed. Find the inclusive upper-tail p-value. Determine the nonrandomized count rejection threshold at level 0.05 and its actual size; explain why adding one smaller count would be invalid. Calculate its power at p = 0.8. Derive the one-sided known-σ sample-size formula for significance level 0.05, power at least 0.8, σ = 4 dollars and planning improvement δ = 1 dollar. Verify that 98 is insufficient and 99 sufficient, using z 0.95 = 1.644853627 and z 0.8 = 0.841621234. List three design changes that would require revisiting the formula. A predeclared five-hypothesis family has ordered p-values 0.004, 0.011, 0.016, 0.032, 0.20. At family level 0.05, list rejections from individual unadjusted tests, Bonferroni and Holm. Prove Holm's strong error control. If all five p-values were independent and uniform under true nulls, calculate the unadjusted probability of at least one rejection. In the known-normal-standard-error model, X̄ = 0.1 dollar, SE = 0.2 dollar and an advance-specified equivalence margin is Δ = 0.5 dollar. Compute the two one-sided test statistics and use Φ(2) = 0.977249868 and Φ(3) = 0.998650102 for their p-values. State the equivalence conclusion and show it using a matching interval. Explain why the two-sided test of exactly zero addresses another question. A report says: “We tried 30 outcome definitions, displayed the smallest p-value, stopped enrolling when it reached 0.04, and infer a 96% probability that the program generates profitable benefits. The 95% interval for its measured mean is [0.1, 0.6] dollar; delivery costs 1 dollar per person.” Identify each unsupported inference. Propose a concrete analysis and reporting repair, distinguishing causal identification, statistical calibration and the economic decision. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria SE = 0.5 dollar, so the interval is 0.8 ± 0.979982 = [−0.179982, 1.779982] dollars. z = 1.6; the two-sided p-value is 2(1 − 0.945200708) = 0.109598584, and the right-sided value is 0.054799292. Neither rule rejects at 0.05. They are hypothetical null tail probabilities for the specified statistic and direction, not probabilities over hypotheses. A posterior would need a prior, likelihood and normalization. The result also does not demonstrate a negligible effect. Mean is 5, s² = 12 and SE = √3 dollars. The half-width is 3.182446305√3 ≈ 5.512159, giving [−0.512159, 10.512159] dollars. The statistic for μ = 0 is 5/√3 ≈ 2.886751, less than 3.182446305; the matching test does not reject. A normal critical value would use the wrong finite-sample pivot when σ is estimated. With nonnormal iid observations, finite positive variance can justify asymptotic studentization as n grows, but the exact t guarantee for this four-point sample is unavailable. Normality is an assumption here, not established by having four data. Wald gives [0, 0] because its plug-in variance is zero. Wilson gives upper endpoint z²/(20 + z²) ≈ 0.161125. The exact one-sided upper bound solves P p (K = 0) = (1 − p)²⁰ = 0.05, giving 1 − 0.05 1/20 ≈ 0.139108. The two-sided equal-tail upper endpoint uses 0.025 and is 1 − 0.025 1/20 ≈ 0.168433. Valid inclusive-tail inversion gives exact coverage at least the stated level for the appropriate one- or two-sided procedures. Wilson is approximate, while Wald has especially bad boundary coverage. The two exact values differ because the two-sided construction allocates only 0.025 error to this tail. P 1/2 (K ≥ 9) = [10 + 1]/1024 = 11/1024 ≈ 0.0107422. Reject at K ≥ 9; actual size is 11/1024. Adding K = 8 gives (45 + 10 + 1)/1024 = 56/1024 = 0.0546875, exceeding 0.05. At p = 0.8, power is 10(0.8)⁹(0.2) + (0.8)¹⁰ = 0.3758096384. Monotonicity of counts in p makes p = 1/2 the least favorable boundary in the composite null. The alternative statistic has mean δ√n/σ. Requiring 1 − Φ(z 0.95 − √n/4) ≥ 0.8 is equivalent to √n/4 ≥ z 0.95 + z 0.8 ≈ 2.486474861. Squaring gives n ≥ 98.9209157, hence n = 99. At 98, √98/4 ≈ 2.474874 is below this threshold; at 99, √99/4 ≈ 2.487469 is above it. Cluster sampling, replacing known σ by an uncertain estimate, changing to a two-sided or multiple-testing rule, nonrandom attrition and interim stopping are valid examples requiring revised design reasoning. A larger number of bootstrap repetitions is not a substitute for sample-size planning. Unadjusted 0.05 tests reject the first four hypotheses. Bonferroni uses 0.01 and rejects only the first. Holm thresholds are 0.01, 0.0125, 0.0166667, 0.025 and 0.05. The first three pass; the fourth fails and the rule stops, leaving the fourth and fifth unrejected. For proof, if m₀ true nulls exist, the first ordered true null has at least m₀ hypotheses still remaining. Any false rejection implies its p-value, the smallest true-null value, is at most α/m₀. Validity and the union bound over m₀ true nulls give probability at most α. If m₀ = 0 the error probability is zero. Under five independent continuous true nulls, the unadjusted error is 1 − 0.95⁵ = 0.2262190625. Dependence changes this product, not the Bonferroni/Holm guarantee with valid p-values. The lower-null statistic is (0.1 + 0.5)/0.2 = 3, giving upper-tail p = 0.001349898. The upper-null statistic is (0.1 − 0.5)/0.2 = −2, giving lower-tail p = 0.022750132. Both are below 0.05, so declare equivalence within ±0.5 dollar. The two-sided 90% interval is 0.1 ± 1.644854(0.2) = [−0.228971, 0.428971], inside that range. A two-sided zero test has z = 0.5 and p ≈ 0.617075, failing to reject exact zero; this failure alone would not establish equivalence. The equivalence inference comes from rejecting both outside-margin components, with the prespecified economically justified margin and correct model. Searching across 30 definitions and selecting the smallest value invalidates its interpretation as a single prespecified test; the full search and outcome family matter. Stopping at a favorable fixed-sample p-value requires a sequential calibration absent from the report. Subtracting p from one does not produce a posterior probability. A positive measured mean does not establish cost-covering value: even the reported interval's upper endpoint is below the stated cost, though its nominal coverage itself requires revalidation after selection and stopping. A causal effect also requires an identification design; a measured mean alone is insufficient. A strong repair specifies population, measurement and comparison design; chooses a primary economic target such as causal net benefit; reports all 30 analyses and the stopping rule; uses a valid multiplicity/sequential procedure for the actual experiment or a genuinely separated, prospectively planned confirmation sample; and reports effect sizes, correctly calibrated uncertainty and a decision analysis with costs and losses. Credit requires all three dimensions—causality, calibration and economic value—rather than an unsupported recommendation simply to collect more data. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: derive the known-normal mean interval and its matching test on a blank page. Explain “95% confidence,” a p-value, size and power using different repeated experiments. Then use Worked example 1 to explain why evidence of a positive mean and evidence of cost-covering value are different claims. Delayed check: after a week, reconstruct the t pivot's normal-rotation argument, the binomial zero-success upper bound and Holm's first-true-null proof. Explain why failure to reject zero, equivalence within a margin and posterior belief are three separate statements. Audit any proposed procedure for target, sampling law, statistic, tails, multiplicity and stopping before interpreting its numerical output. Continue to Bayesian inference and decisions , which makes prior assumptions and losses explicit. Later empirical volumes develop causal identification, robust uncertainty under dependence, selective inference and general asymptotic estimation. This chapter establishes the error-calibration logic they must preserve."}, {"title": "03.09 · Bayesian inference and decisions", "url": "v03-c09.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "From uncertain parameters to an explicit decision A firm has a small pilot sample and must decide whether to launch a service. The sample success rate is not the whole decision. Prior evidence may be relevant, future customers remain uncertain even if the success probability were known, and the losses from launching a bad service and rejecting a good one may differ. Bayesian analysis makes three inputs explicit: a prior over unknown quantities, a likelihood for the observed experiment, and a loss or utility function for actions. Let θ denote the uncertain parameter and x the observed data. A prior density π(θ) is nonnegative and integrates to one over its stated support; a discrete prior instead sums to one. It describes uncertainty before these data, conditional on the information already used. The likelihood L(θ; x) = f(x | θ) describes how the actual observation mechanism generates data. Their product is a joint density or mass. Integrating out θ gives the marginal likelihood , also called the prior predictive density of the data: m(x) = ∫ f(x | θ)π(θ) dθ, π(θ | x) = f(x | θ)π(θ)/m(x), provided 0 < m(x) < ∞. This follows from the conditional-density construction in Chapter 5; with finite hypotheses replace the integral by a sum. For continuous x, m(x) is a density, not the probability of observing that exact point. The posterior integrates to one and provides probabilities about θ under the chosen model. The symbol ∝ θ means proportional as a function of θ: constants independent of θ may be dropped when identifying the posterior kernel, then normalization must be restored. A zero-prior-probability region retains zero posterior probability when the likelihood is finite there. Data cannot revise an excluded possibility into existence. If m(x) = 0, the observed event is incompatible with the prior/model; the displayed update is undefined, not a license to normalize arbitrarily. Prior support, observation support and measurement rules require inspection before computing a posterior. Sequential updating uses the current posterior as the next prior, but only with the correct conditional likelihood for genuinely additional information. Multiplication by f(x₂ | θ, x₁) reproduces the joint update. The simpler product f(x₂ | θ)f(x₁ | θ) requires conditional independence given θ. Reusing the same observations, or counting dependent sources as independent measurements, artificially concentrates the posterior. Conjugacy: derive a family rather than memorize a recipe A prior family is conjugate to a likelihood when updating preserves that family. Conjugacy simplifies calculation; it does not establish that the prior or model is substantively appropriate. Beta prior with binary observations For a > 0 and b > 0 define B(a, b) = ∫₀¹ p a−1 (1 − p) b−1 dp. The integral is finite because the endpoint powers have exponents greater than −1. The Beta(a, b) density is p a−1 (1 − p) b−1 /B(a, b) on (0, 1). For positive integers, integration by parts gives B(a, b) = [(b − 1)/a]B(a + 1, b − 1) when b > 1, and B(a, 1) = 1/a. Iterating proves B(a, b) = (a − 1)!(b − 1)!/(a + b − 1)!. Suppose, conditional on p, n observations are iid Bernoulli(p), with k successes. Multiplying the beta kernel by pᵏ(1 − p)ⁿ⁻ᵏ gives p | x ∼ Beta(A, D), A = a + k, D = b + n − k. We use D for the second posterior shape to keep it distinct from the beta integral B. The binomial coefficient in the likelihood of the count cancels from this posterior normalization. It still belongs in the probability of observing that count. The evidence for K = k is C(n, k)B(a + k, b + n − k)/B(a, b); an individual ordered sequence with these counts has evidence without C(n, k). The moments can also be derived directly. The identity B(a + 1, b) + B(a, b + 1) = B(a, b) follows by multiplying the integrand by p + (1 − p). Integrating the derivative of pᵃ(1 − p)ᵇ gives aB(a, b + 1) = bB(a + 1, b), since its endpoint terms vanish. Together these yield B(a + 1, b)/B(a, b) = a/(a + b), for all positive shapes. A second iteration gives the second moment. Thus E[p | x] = A/(A + D), Var(p | x) = AD/[(A + D)²(A + D + 1)]. When n > 0, the posterior mean is [n/(a + b + n)](k/n) + [(a + b)/(a + b + n)]a/(a + b): a weighted average of the sample proportion and prior mean. The prior concentration a + b acts as an effective weight in this identity. Calling it “pseudo-observations” is a computational interpretation, not a claim that additional customers were observed. For fixed positive a, b and iid data from a fixed p, the weak law and vanishing prior weight give consistency of this posterior mean. That fact does not guarantee finite-sample coverage of a posterior interval or correct inference under a wrong likelihood. Normal prior with normal observations of known variance Let μ have prior N(m₀, τ₀²), with τ₀² > 0. Conditional on μ, observations are iid N(μ, σ²), with known σ² > 0. The squared-deviation identity gives Σ(xᵢ − μ)² = Σ(xᵢ − x̄)² + n(x̄ − μ)². The first term is independent of μ. Multiplying likelihood and prior produces an exponent proportional to −½[(μ − m₀)²/τ₀² + n(μ − x̄)²/σ²] = −½[Pμ² − 2hμ + constant], P = 1/τ₀² + n/σ², h = m₀/τ₀² + nx̄/σ². Complete the square Pμ² − 2hμ = P(μ − h/P)² − h²/P. Normalization therefore gives μ | x ∼ N(mₙ, vₙ), where vₙ = 1/P and mₙ = h/P. Precision, the reciprocal of variance, adds; the mean is precision-weighted. Prior variance and observation variance have squared-outcome units; their precisions have inverse squared units. Taking a very concentrated prior can dominate a small sample. If σ² is unknown, substituting a sample estimate does not preserve this exact known-variance derivation. Source route: MIT 18.05's beta/normal conjugacy reading provides these families as further study. The integral recurrences and square completion here derive their normalizers and updates. A count-rate model: gamma–Poisson updating To put exposure into the likelihood, let independent counts conditional on λ satisfy Kᵢ ∼ Poisson(λtᵢ), with known positive exposures tᵢ. Their parameter-dependent likelihood is λ Σkᵢ exp(−λΣtᵢ). Define Γ(a) = ∫₀∞ z a−1 e −z dz for a > 0. Integration by parts gives Γ(a + 1) = aΓ(a) and Γ(1) = 1. Substitution z = bλ shows that the gamma density with shape a and rate b is bᵃλ a−1 e −bλ /Γ(a), λ > 0. Its mean is a/b and variance a/b² by the same integral recurrence. Multiplication gives λ | x ∼ Gamma(a + Σkᵢ, rate b + Σtᵢ). If λ is events per day, b has units of days. Some software uses a scale 1/b instead of a rate b; interchanging them reverses the calculation. For future exposure t, the predictive count has mean tE[λ | x] and variance tE[λ | x] + t²Var(λ | x), by total expectation and variance. A plug-in Poisson law discards the second term. Its predictive probability of zero events is E[e −tλ | x] = [b′/(b′ + t)] a′ , by a gamma integral with posterior shapes a′, b′. Source route: NIST's gamma-distribution reference supplies the distribution and gamma-integral background. This chapter explicitly uses shape/rate, rather than its scale convention, and derives the count-rate update. Credible sets describe posterior probability A 1 − α credible set C(x) satisfies ∫ C(x) π(θ | x)dθ = 1 − α, or at least that much mass for a discrete posterior. For a continuous posterior CDF F, the equal-tail interval [F⁻¹(α/2), F⁻¹(1 − α/2)] leaves α/2 in each tail. A highest-posterior-density set instead includes locations whose density exceeds a threshold chosen to obtain the required mass. It can be disconnected for a multimodal distribution. Equal-tail and density-based constructions need not agree. Specify which one is used. The interpretation is conditional: given the data, prior and likelihood, the posterior assigns this probability to the set. It is not automatically a fixed-parameter repeated-sampling confidence guarantee. The distinction can be proved with one binary observation. Start from a uniform prior p ∼ Beta(1, 1). After a success, p | x has density 2p, CDF p², and equal-tail 95% interval [√0.025, √0.975] ≈ [0.158114, 0.987421]. After a failure the interval is [1 − √0.975, 1 − √0.025] ≈ [0.012579, 0.841886]. If the fixed data-generating p is 0.9, only the success interval covers it. Its repeated-sampling coverage is therefore 0.9, not 0.95. Nevertheless, averaging over the stated proper prior and its observation model gives the nominal joint probability: E X [P(θ ∈ C(X) | X)] = 0.95 by the tower law. Prior-averaged calibration and coverage at every fixed θ are different requirements. Neither calculation validates an incorrectly specified measurement model. Prediction integrates parameter uncertainty The posterior predictive law for a future observation Y is f(y | x) = ∫ f(y | θ, x)π(θ | x)dθ. If Y is conditionally independent of the observed data given θ, its inner law simplifies to f(y | θ). That assumption must include the relevant exposure, covariates and sampling design. A future population that changes its mechanism does not automatically share the pilot's parameter. In the beta–Bernoulli model, a future single success has probability E[p | x]. For m conditionally iid future trials, their count J has the beta-binomial predictive mass P(J = j | x) = C(m, j)B(A + j, D + m − j)/B(A, D), j = 0, …, m. Integrating the conditional binomial law proves this expression; summing over j integrates (p + 1 − p)ᵐ and gives one. Conditional independence does not imply posterior predictive independence. Two future indicators have covariance Var(p | x), because their joint success probability is E[p² | x], rather than E[p | x]². With M = E[p | x] and V = Var(p | x), total variance gives Var(J | x) = mM(1 − M) + m(m − 1)V. Substituting beta moments yields mAD(A + D + m)/[(A + D)²(A + D + 1)]. For a normal posterior μ ∼ N(mₙ, vₙ) and a new conditionally independent observation Y = μ + ε, ε ∼ N(0, σ²), convolution gives Y | x ∼ N(mₙ, vₙ + σ²). For the mean of m new conditionally iid observations, the variance is vₙ + σ²/m. The first term is shared parameter uncertainty; the second is fresh observation noise. Distinguish credible intervals for μ from predictive intervals for Y. Source route: MIT 18.05's probabilistic-prediction reading introduces averaging over uncertain hypotheses. The conditional moment and mixture calculations here extend that logic to continuous parameters. Worked example 1: launch decisions and prior sensitivity A synthetic pilot has five conditionally independent binary outcomes, three successes. With prior Beta(2, 3), the posterior is Beta(5, 5). Its density is 630p⁴(1 − p)⁴, mean is 1/2 and variance is 1/44. Integrating its polynomial gives F(p) = 126p⁵ − 420p⁶ + 540p⁷ − 315p⁸ + 70p⁹. Bisection at F = 0.025 and 0.975 gives the equal-tail 95% credible interval approximately [0.212009, 0.787991]. The posterior probability p > 0.6 is 1 − F(0.6) = 0.26656768. If each future success yields 20 dollars and launch costs 9 dollars per customer, risk-neutral expected net benefit is 20E[p | x] − 9 = 1 dollar per customer. This supports launch under that payoff and model. Requiring at least 95% posterior probability that p > 0.6 would be another decision rule, addressing another loss or constraint; the posterior does not choose the rule for the firm. For four future customers, the predictive success-count variance is 14/11 ≈ 1.272727, versus 1 from plugging p = 1/2 into a binomial law. The probability all four succeed is (5·6·7·8)/(10·11·12·13) = 14/143 ≈ 0.097902, versus (1/2)⁴ = 0.0625 from that plug-in. Shared parameter uncertainty changes joint predictions. Using prior Beta(1, 1) instead yields posterior Beta(4, 3), mean 4/7 and expected net benefit 17/7 ≈ 2.428571 dollars. A more pessimistic and more concentrated prior Beta(2, 8) gives Beta(5, 10), mean 1/3 and expected net benefit −7/3 ≈ −2.333333 dollars, reversing the launch decision. These are transparent alternatives, not equally credible priors by decree. Justify each using evidence, population comparability and concentration; inspect disagreement rather than selecting the prior that delivers the desired action. A posterior becomes an action through loss Let d be an available action and L(d, θ) its loss, with units meaningful for the decision. Its posterior expected loss is ρ(d | x) = ∫ L(d, θ)π(θ | x)dθ. A Bayes action minimizes this expression over the feasible actions, if a minimum exists. A Bayes decision rule specifies that action for each possible dataset. For a proper prior and finite expected loss, a measurable rule minimizing posterior loss almost everywhere also minimizes the prior-averaged sampling loss: integrate its conditional loss inequality over the data and use the tower law. This does not claim lowest loss for every fixed parameter value. Under squared estimation loss L(d, θ) = (d − θ)² and finite posterior second moment, expand around the posterior mean: ρ(d | x) = Var(θ | x) + (d − E[θ | x])². The posterior mean uniquely minimizes it when all real actions are available. For absolute loss, a posterior median minimizes expected loss. For a continuous CDF, differentiating E[|d − θ| | x] gives 2F(d | x) − 1, so a median makes the derivative zero; the function is convex. With atoms, its left/right slopes straddle zero at any median, giving the same conclusion. Restricted action sets require minimization over those sets. For a binary action “assert H” versus “do not assert H,” let q = P(H | x). If an incorrect assertion costs C FP and missing a true H costs C FN , with correct choices costing zero, assertion loss is C FP (1 − q) and nonassertion loss is C FN q. Assert when q > C FP /(C FP + C FN ), with indifference at equality. A threshold of 0.95 expresses a particular loss ratio or institutional constraint; it is not a universal Bayesian requirement. The maximum-posterior-density location, or MAP, is another summary. A mode can differ from the mean and median, lie at a boundary or fail to be unique. With a continuous posterior, literal zero-one loss for an exactly correct point gives loss one almost surely for every point, so it does not uniquely justify MAP. Small-neighborhood loss can motivate a mode under suitable conditions, but its definition matters. A density mode also changes under nonlinear reparameterization because densities acquire Jacobian factors. Posterior probabilities of corresponding events transform coherently; coordinate-dependent modes need care. Source route: Berkeley's Stat 210A Bayes-estimation reader develops posterior-loss and prior-averaged-risk reasoning. The loss choices and decision thresholds here are independently derived and tied to explicit units. Worked example 2: parameter uncertainty versus future noise In a synthetic known-variance model, four observed values are 2, 3, 5, 6 dollars, with conditional σ² = 4 dollars squared. Set prior μ ∼ N(0, 4). The observed mean is 4. Posterior precision is 1/4 + 4/4 = 5/4, so vₙ = 4/5 and mₙ = 4/(5/4) = 16/5 = 3.2 dollars. Under squared loss the Bayes estimate is 3.2. The equal-tail 95% credible interval for μ is 3.2 ± 1.959964√0.8 = [1.446955, 4.953045] dollars. The posterior probability μ > 3 is 1 − Φ((3 − 3.2)/√0.8) ≈ 0.588468. If the action's payoff is μ − 3 dollars, the risk-neutral posterior expected payoff is 0.2 dollar, while a binary classification decision about μ > 3 must use its own mistake costs. A new observation is N(3.2, 4.8), rather than N(3.2, 0.8). The future mean of four observations is N(3.2, 1.8). These add new noise to shared parameter uncertainty. In contrast the known-σ frequentist 95% interval for the original population mean is 4 ± 1.959964 = [2.040036, 5.959964]. Its different center reflects that it uses a different procedure and interpretation. Model evidence, prior checks and honest robustness For alternative models M₀ and M₁, each with a proper parameter prior, define mⱼ(x) by integrating its likelihood over that model's prior. Bayes' rule gives posterior model odds = prior model odds × m₁(x)/m₀(x). The ratio is the Bayes factor. It averages likelihood over each model; it is not the ratio of maximized likelihoods. A wide prior can dilute evidence by placing mass where data are unlikely. This is a reason to inspect prior predictive implications, not to tune priors after observing the result. An improper prior has infinite total mass and is not a probability distribution. Sometimes its likelihood product can be normalized to obtain a proper posterior, producing a generalized Bayes procedure. The arbitrary scaling constant cancels in that within-model posterior but does not supply a defined marginal model probability. Ordinary Bayes factors between models cannot generally be obtained from unrelated arbitrary improper-prior constants. Posterior propriety itself must be checked; it is not automatic. A uniform prior is tied to a parameterization. If p is uniform on (0, 1) and q = p², its induced density is 1/(2√q), not uniform on q. A claim of being “prior-free” because a prior is flat is therefore inaccurate. Build sensitivity around plausible support, center, concentration and tail assumptions, plus the likelihood and selection model. A sharp posterior under a misspecified model can be sharply misleading. Check what the prior predicts about observable data before updating and what the fitted model predicts afterward. Unexpected outcomes can reveal an inappropriate prior, likelihood or measurement process. Reusing the observations to fit a prior and then treating that prior as independently established can understate uncertainty; hierarchical or explicitly analyzed empirical-Bayes methods are different procedures. Comparisons with historical evidence require population and measurement comparability. Neither Bayesian updating nor a high posterior probability alone supplies causal identification. Exercises A parameter is either p = 0.2 or p = 0.8, with prior probabilities 3/4 and 1/4. Three conditionally iid Bernoulli observations contain two successes and one failure. Compute the posterior probabilities and next-success probability. For H: p = 0.8, incorrect assertion costs 3 dollars and missed assertion costs 5 dollars. Find the optimal binary action and both expected losses. Starting from Beta(3, 7), observe eight successes in ten conditionally iid trials. Derive the posterior, mean, variance and interior mode. Show the mean's prior/sample weighting. Then update with four successes and one failure from genuinely additional trials; compare sequential and combined updating. Under the Beta(5, 5) posterior in Worked example 1, derive the predictive mass formula for four future trials, their expected count and variance, and the probability of four successes. Find the covariance of two future success indicators. Explain why a binomial plug-in distribution misses a dependence term. A uniform prior and one success yield Beta(2, 1). Derive its CDF, mean, median and equal-tail 95% credible interval. Identify the squared- and absolute-loss optimal point estimates. At a fixed data-generating p = 0.9, calculate coverage of the one-observation equal-tail credible procedure, considering both possible observations. Recompute Worked example 2's normal posterior by square completion, then find the law of one future observation and of the mean of four new observations. Replace the prior by N(−4, 0.25) while preserving the same likelihood/data; find the new posterior mean and variance. Explain the practical implication of the change. A gamma shape/rate prior for λ is Gamma(2, rate 3 days). Independent conditional Poisson counts are 1, 0, 2 with exposures 1, 2, 1 days. Derive the posterior mean/variance. For two future days, compute predictive mean/variance and the probability of zero events. Compare its variance with a plug-in Poisson variance. Compare M₀: p = 1/2 with M₁: p is uniform on (0, 1). Four conditionally iid Bernoulli trials all succeed. Prior model probabilities are 0.9 and 0.1. Compute the Bayes factor and posterior probability of M₁. Repeat with a Beta(1, 9) prior inside M₁. Why is a likelihood maximization, or an arbitrary improper “flat” model prior, inadequate for this calculation? A report selects its prior center from the pilot, reuses the same pilot in updating, excludes p > 0.6 from its prior, and claims its narrow 95% credible interval guarantees 5% error at every true p. It calls its favorable posterior causal evidence for launch. Diagnose the claims and propose a repair that specifies data use, support, likelihood/design, sensitivity and a decision loss. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria For a particular ordered sequence, likelihoods are 0.2²(0.8) = 0.032 and 0.8²(0.2) = 0.128. Weighted numerators are 0.024 and 0.032; posterior probabilities are 3/7 and 4/7. Using the count likelihood multiplies both by 3 and gives the same posterior. The predictive success probability is (3/7)(1/5) + (4/7)(4/5) = 19/35. Assert H because q = 4/7 exceeds 3/(3 + 5) = 3/8. Assertion loss is 3(3/7) = 9/7 dollars; nonassertion loss is 5(4/7) = 20/7 dollars. A higher-probability model is not automatically optimal under every cost ratio. The kernel is p¹⁰(1 − p)⁸, giving Beta(11, 9). Mean is 11/20 = 0.55, variance is 99/(400·21) = 33/2800, and differentiating its log density gives mode (11 − 1)/(11 + 9 − 2) = 5/9. The mean is (1/2)(0.8) + (1/2)(0.3), equally weighting ten data trials and prior concentration ten. Additional updating gives Beta(15, 10), mean 15/25 = 0.6. Combined data have 12 successes and 3 failures out of 15, giving the same shapes 3 + 12 and 7 + 3. Using the pilot twice would not be this additional-data experiment. The mass is C(4, j)B(5 + j, 9 − j)/B(5, 5), j = 0, …, 4. Expected count is 4(1/2) = 2. Its variance is 4(1/2)(1/2) + 4·3(1/44) = 14/11. The all-success probability is E[p⁴ | x] = (5·6·7·8)/(10·11·12·13) = 14/143. Two future indicators have covariance Var(p | x) = 1/44 by conditioning on their shared p. The plug-in binomial has the same mean here but variance 1 and zero covariance. Conditional independence holds given p; integrating out uncertain p creates predictive dependence. Density is 2p and F(p) = p². Mean is ∫₀¹2p²dp = 2/3; median solves p² = 1/2 and is 1/√2. The equal-tail interval is [√0.025, √0.975] ≈ [0.158114, 0.987421]. Squared loss chooses 2/3 and absolute loss chooses 1/√2. For the one-observation procedure, failure yields Beta(1, 2) and interval [0.012579, 0.841886], which excludes 0.9. Success yields the displayed interval, which includes it. Its coverage at fixed p = 0.9 is P(success) = 0.9. Conditional posterior probability 0.95 and prior-averaged probability 0.95 do not imply 0.95 coverage at that fixed parameter. Removing terms constant in μ, the exponent is −½[μ²/4 + (μ − 4)²] = −½[(5/4)(μ − 16/5)² + constant]. Thus the posterior is N(16/5, 4/5). New-observation law is N(16/5, 24/5); the four-new-observation mean is N(16/5, 9/5). With prior N(−4, 1/4), precision is 4 + 1 = 5 and weighted location is −16 + 4 = −12; posterior is N(−12/5, 1/5). A strong, conflicting prior reverses the sign and reduces nominal posterior variance despite contrary data. Audit its provenance, concentration and prior predictive compatibility; narrowness alone is not validation. Counts total 3 and exposures total 4 days, giving Gamma(5, rate 7 days). Posterior mean is 5/7 event per day and variance 5/49 in squared-rate units. For t = 2 days the predictive count mean is 10/7, variance is 10/7 + 4(5/49) = 90/49 ≈ 1.836735, and zero-count probability is (7/9)⁵ ≈ 0.284628. A plug-in Poisson variance is 10/7 and omits 20/49 from parameter uncertainty. Scale 1/7 day⁻¹ is the reciprocal parameterization, not a rate of 1/7 day. For the all-success outcome, m₀ = (1/2)⁴ = 1/16 and m₁ = ∫₀¹p⁴dp = 1/5. Bayes factor favoring M₁ is 16/5. Prior odds M₁:M₀ are 1:9; posterior odds are 16:45, giving P(M₁ | x) = 16/61 ≈ 0.262295. Under Beta(1, 9), m₁ = (1·2·3·4)/(10·11·12·13) = 1/715, factor is 16/715 and posterior probability is 16/6451 ≈ 0.002480. The alternative model's prior predictive support matters greatly. Maximized likelihood ignores the required averaging over parameter uncertainty. An improper prior's arbitrary scale does not define that model's data probability or ordinary model odds. Data-driven prior fitting plus unadjusted reuse can overcount evidence; account for the fitting procedure or use genuinely separate prior information with provenance. Excluding p > 0.6 makes its posterior probability zero regardless of these finite likelihood observations; justify support or use plausible alternatives that admit the possibility. Narrow posterior intervals have conditional/prior-averaged interpretations, not an automatic uniform frequentist guarantee; evaluate fixed-parameter operating properties if those are required. Bayesian probability does not identify a causal program effect without an appropriate comparison/assignment design. A defensible repair records which data informed each input, models the actual selection/measurement process, checks prior predictive compatibility and support, reports plausible prior/likelihood sensitivity, and chooses an action using stated dollar losses or utility and feasible constraints. Credit requires a concrete data-use and decision account, not the assertion that all priors are arbitrary or that more precision alone resolves the issues. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: build a finite prior–likelihood–posterior table, then derive a beta update as the continuous version. Explain why prediction integrates over the posterior and why a posterior probability alone does not choose a business action. Contrast parameter uncertainty, observation noise and loss. Delayed check: after a week, derive beta moments from integral recurrences, complete the normal posterior square and reconstruct the binary-loss threshold. Reproduce the one-observation coverage counterexample. Then choose a decision whose recommendation changes under a plausible prior or loss variation and explain what evidence would reduce that disagreement. Continue to expected utility and risk preferences , which replaces risk-neutral expected-money decisions with explicit preferences over uncertain outcomes. General posterior computation, hierarchical inference and asymptotic Bayesian theory are developed in later empirical and research material."}, {"title": "03.10 · Expected utility and risk preferences", "url": "v03-c10.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Expected money does not fully rank risky outcomes A household with limited wealth can prefer a small certain payment to a larger expected payment that includes a severe loss. An investor may accept a favorable risk while choosing a smaller position than its maximum feasible size. These decisions require preferences over distributions of final outcomes, not only a forecast of their means. Probability describes uncertainty; utility describes how the decision maker values consequences. Confusing them makes either beliefs or preferences impossible to audit. A finite lottery specifies outcomes wᵢ and probabilities pᵢ ≥ 0 with Σpᵢ = 1. Under expected utility its value is Σpᵢu(wᵢ), and the preferred lottery has higher value. For random final wealth W the value is E[u(W)], when well-defined. W is final wealth after gains, losses and costs, measured in a fixed currency unit. An increasing u means more wealth is preferred within this model. Money need not capture all consequences: health, leisure, reputation or other dimensions require an appropriately specified outcome space. Expected utility is a model, not a claim that every person actually follows it in every choice. Why an expectation represents preferences The finite-outcome von Neumann–Morgenstern result gives explicit assumptions. A preference relation is complete if any two lotteries can be compared, and transitive if its comparisons are consistent across three lotteries. Write P ≽ Q for weak preference and P ∼ Q for indifference. Continuity means the sets of lotteries weakly above or below any fixed lottery are closed in the finite probability simplex. It rules out an infinitely overriding preference that cannot be approached through probabilities. The independence axiom requires, for any common lottery R and 0 < t ≤ 1, P ≽ Q ⇔ tP + (1 − t)R ≽ tQ + (1 − t)R. The expression is a probabilistic mixture, with final-outcome probabilities added by total probability. It is not a portfolio that simultaneously holds fractions of two payoffs. Independence says that adding the same chance of a common consequence should preserve the comparison of the remaining alternatives. It is also different from probabilistic independence between random variables. A constructive finite-outcome proof Completeness and transitivity on a finite consequence set give a best consequence b and worst z. Repeated replacement of each lottery consequence by b, using independence, shows b ≽ P for every lottery; replacement by z shows P ≽ z. If b ∼ z, every lottery is indifferent and a constant utility represents preferences. Otherwise b ≻ z. Define B(t) as the lottery giving b with probability t and z otherwise. Independence makes B(t) strictly improve with t. First, for r < 1, b ≻ B(r) follows by mixing b ≻ z with a common b at weight r. For t > r, write B(t) = s b + (1 − s)B(r), with s = (t − r)/(1 − r) > 0. Mixing b ≻ B(r) with a common B(r) gives B(t) ≻ B(r). For any consequence c, the closed sets {t: B(t) ≽ c} and {t: c ≽ B(t)} cover [0, 1] and respectively contain 1 and 0. Connectedness of the interval forces an intersection; strict ordering of B(t) makes that intersection a single probability. Call it u(c), so c ∼ B(u(c)). This use of connectedness is the interval property behind the intermediate-value theorem. In a lottery P, replace each consequence c by its indifferent binary lottery B(u(c)). Independence preserves indifference under each replacement; finite induction allows replacing every consequence. Reducing the resulting compound lottery gives probability Σp(c)u(c) of b. Thus P ∼ B(Σp(c)u(c)). Strict ordering of the binary lotteries proves P ≽ Q exactly when Σp(c)u(c) ≥ Σq(c)u(c). Conversely a linear expectation representation satisfies completeness, transitivity, continuity and independence directly. This establishes the finite theorem with its stated assumptions. Utility numbers are unique up to a positive affine transformation v = au + d, a > 0: E[v] = aE[u] + d preserves every ordering. To see necessity in the nondegenerate finite case, any other expectation representation must preserve c ∼ B(u(c)), hence v(c) = u(c)v(b) + [1 − u(c)]v(z), with v(b) > v(z). A general increasing nonlinear transformation preserves rankings of certain outcomes but can change rankings of lotteries. Therefore one cannot replace u by log u merely because the transformation is increasing. Source route: MIT 14.123's decision-under-risk notes provide the finite-lottery axioms and representation theorem. The construction here states each replacement and endpoint argument explicitly. Concavity, Jensen and the meaning of risk aversion A decision maker is risk averse when a certain E[W] is weakly preferred to the lottery W, for every admissible bounded wealth lottery. In expected utility that means u(E[W]) ≥ E[u(W)]. For a differentiable concave utility, the tangent inequality u(w) ≤ u(m) + u′(m)(w − m) at m = E[W] gives this result immediately on taking expectations. It extends to integrable unbounded W when the expectations exist and the tangent argument applies. For a finite lottery, the same Jensen inequality follows by iterating the definition of concavity over its weights. The converse is equally useful. Apply risk aversion to a two-point lottery giving x with probability t and y otherwise. Then u(tx + (1 − t)y) ≥ tu(x) + (1 − t)u(y), which is precisely concavity. Thus concavity is equivalent to risk aversion over these lotteries, not just a sufficient heuristic. Affine utility is risk neutral; convex utility reverses Jensen and represents risk seeking over the admissible lottery class. Strict concavity makes Jensen strict for nondegenerate lotteries. A person may still accept a risky opportunity with a sufficiently favorable mean: risk aversion does not mean refusal of every risk. Diversification illustrates the distinction between a mixture of lotteries and a mixture of payoffs. If Y and Z have the same marginal wealth distribution, concavity gives u((Y + Z)/2) ≥ [u(Y) + u(Z)]/2 in every state, hence the equally weighted payoff has expected utility at least as high as either original distribution. Independence is unnecessary for this inequality; if Y = Z, there is no strict gain. If they differ with positive probability and u is strictly concave on their values, improvement is strict. Dependence still affects the resulting distribution and the size of that improvement. Certainty equivalents and two different premiums For continuous strictly increasing u, a certainty equivalent c solves u(c) = E[u(W)]. It is unique when expected utility is finite and lies in u's range. For W supported on a compact interval contained in the utility domain, existence follows because its utility expectation lies between the endpoint utilities. Bounded wealth alone need not bound utility near an excluded domain endpoint, as log utility near zero illustrates. The wealth risk premium is π = E[W] − c. A risk-averse decision maker has π ≥ 0. Both c and π are in wealth units; neither is measured in arbitrary utility units. If initial wealth is w and a loss L reduces it to w − L, full insurance at premium P makes wealth certainly w − P. The largest acceptable premium is P max = w − CE(w − L). Since expected uninsured wealth is w − E[L], this equals expected loss plus the risk premium for the uninsured wealth lottery. Distinguish the total insurance price from its loading above expected loss. A certainty equivalent is a person's preference summary under a specified wealth and belief model. It is not automatically a market price. For buying a risky payoff X at cost k, compare E[u(w + X − k)] with u(w). In general the maximal acceptable k is not simply CE(w + X) − w, because subtracting k changes the wealth at which risk is evaluated. Utilities with special translation properties, such as exponential utility, permit that simplification. Budget, borrowing and solvency constraints remain separate feasibility conditions. Worked example 1: a household's full-insurance price Initial wealth is 100 dollars. A synthetic loss of 40 dollars occurs with probability 0.1; otherwise there is no loss. Use utility proportional to √w on w ≥ 0, with wealth expressed in the stated dollar unit. Expected uninsured wealth is 0.9(100) + 0.1(60) = 96 dollars, while expected utility is 0.9(10) + 0.1√60 ≈ 9.774597. The certainty equivalent is [0.9(10) + 0.1√60]² ≈ 95.542740 dollars. The wealth risk premium is 96 − 95.542740 ≈ 0.457260 dollar. Full insurance is acceptable up to premium 100 − 95.542740 ≈ 4.457260 dollars: the expected loss of 4 dollars plus that risk premium. At a 4.3-dollar premium, insured wealth 95.7 exceeds the certainty equivalent, so insurance is preferred. At 4.6 dollars it is not. This is a household preference calculation with stipulated loss probabilities. It does not establish an insurer's competitive premium, which also depends on expenses, correlated losses, capital and market structure. If coverage is incomplete or the loss/wealth model changes, recompute final outcomes rather than carrying forward the same full-insurance formula. Local risk aversion: what curvature actually measures Raw u″ is not a comparable risk measure: multiplying utility by a positive constant changes it without changing preferences. For C² utility with u′(w) > 0, the Arrow–Pratt absolute risk aversion is A(w) = −u″(w)/u′(w). The scale cancels under positive affine transformations. Its units are inverse wealth units. Relative risk aversion R(w) = wA(w) is dimensionless for positive wealth. Let a small centered risk be εZ, where Z is bounded, E[Z] = 0 and Var(Z) = s². Suppose u is C² around w, with u′(w) > 0 and all small-risk outcomes in its domain. Uniform Taylor remainder control on bounded Z gives E[u(w + εZ)] = u(w) + ½u″(w)ε²s² + o(ε²). The inverse utility is differentiable at u(w); applying its first-order expansion to this expected utility gives the certainty equivalent w + [u″(w)/(2u′(w))]ε²s² + o(ε²). Hence π(ε) = ½A(w)ε²Var(Z) + o(ε²). The small-risk approximation is half curvature times variance. It is local, requiring a risk that actually shrinks and a positive derivative. It is not an exact premium for a large, highly skewed or default-threatening loss. For a noncentered payoff, first separate its mean and centered risk, and evaluate curvature at the relevant expected final wealth. Variance alone does not determine expected utility outside special families or a justified local approximation. Risk-aversion comparisons also have a precise scope. If u = g ∘ v for increasing concave g and increasing v, Jensen gives E[u(W)] = E[g(v(W))] ≤ g(E[v(W)]) = u(CE v (W)). Since u increases, CE u (W) ≤ CE v (W). For C² utilities with positive first derivatives on the same interval, g = u ∘ v⁻¹ has g″ = [u″v′ − u′v″]/(v′)³, evaluated at corresponding wealth. The condition A u ≥ A v makes that expression nonpositive, giving the more-concave transformation and the certainty-equivalent ordering over admissible lotteries. Comparing curvature at one wealth point gives only a local comparison, not this ordering over an entire interval. Source route: MIT 14.123's risk-attitudes notes develop certainty equivalents, concavity and Arrow–Pratt comparisons. The local expansion here includes the bounded-risk and derivative conditions needed for its remainder. Useful utility families and their domains Exponential utility u(w) = −exp(−aw), a > 0, is increasing and concave for every real w, with A(w) = a: constant absolute risk aversion, or CARA. The parameter a has inverse-wealth units so the exponential's argument is dimensionless. For a payoff X with finite E[e −aX ], its certainty-equivalent gain is −a⁻¹log E[e −aX ], independent of initial wealth. Indeed E[u(w + X)] = −e −aw E[e −aX ], so solving for the certainty-equivalent wealth gives w plus that gain. A deterministic cost k subtracts k from the gain. If the exponential moment is infinite, the finite calculation is unavailable. For positive wealth, constant relative risk aversion, or CRRA, uses u(w) = (w/w ref ) 1−γ /(1 − γ), γ > 0, γ ≠ 1, with any fixed positive reference wealth w ref ; at γ = 1 use log(w/w ref ). The reference keeps units consistent and does not change preference ordering. Differentiation gives A(w) = γ/w and R(w) = γ. An equal dollar risk is locally less costly at higher wealth; an equal proportional risk has the same relative curvature. For log utility, multiplying all final wealth values by a positive constant shifts utility by a constant, so the certainty equivalent scales by the same factor. Log and CRRA expressions require positive wealth. A normal wealth model has unbounded negative support and is generally incompatible with them, even if its mean is high. Exponential utility permits negative wealth mathematically; actual borrowing/default rules still need their own outcome model. Preferences over bankruptcy cannot be added by silently evaluating a utility outside its domain. When the mean–variance certainty equivalent is exact If X = μ + σZ with Z standard normal, complete the square in the Gaussian integral: −z²/2 − aσz = −(z + aσ)²/2 + a²σ²/2. Integrating its normalized shifted density gives E[e −aX ] = exp(−aμ + a²σ²/2). Thus exponential utility yields the exact certainty-equivalent gain CE(X) = μ − aσ²/2. The formula is exact for this utility and normal payoff law. For general distributions, higher features can change the exponential moment despite identical means and variances. For other utilities it may be an approximation requiring explicit conditions. A mean–variance objective is therefore a model-dependent representation, not a universal consequence of risk aversion. Worked example 2: choosing exposure rather than rejecting risk A synthetic normal payoff X has mean 4 dollars and standard deviation 20 dollars. Exponential utility has a = 0.02 per dollar. An exposure f in [0, 1] yields payoff fX; zero exposure gives zero payoff. Its mean is 4f and variance is 400f², so its exact certainty-equivalent gain is 4f − (0.02/2)400f² = 4f − 4f². The derivative 4 − 8f vanishes at f = 1/2 and the second derivative is −8. The maximum on [0, 1] is therefore f = 1/2, with mean payoff 2 dollars, standard deviation 10 dollars and certainty-equivalent gain 1 dollar. Full exposure has mean 4 dollars but certainty equivalent zero; no exposure also has zero. Risk aversion makes partial exposure optimal, rather than forcing refusal. If a doubles to 0.04 per dollar, the objective becomes 4f − 8f², with optimum f = 1/4 and gain 0.5 dollar. In general the unconstrained optimum is μ/(aσ²) for σ² > 0; projection to [0, 1] solves this strictly concave quadratic under the stated exposure bound. Other costs, distributions, preferences or position constraints require a new optimization problem. Ambiguity is a question about the probability model Risk calculations take a probability law as specified. In many decisions the law itself is poorly known. A Bayesian model can express uncertainty over its parameters and average over the posterior, as in Chapter 9. A decision maker may instead evaluate several plausible laws conservatively, or violate expected-utility assumptions. Distinguish uncertainty about outcomes, uncertainty about parameters, incomplete model knowledge and the rule used to evaluate them. Calling all four “risk” conceals the choice. An Ellsberg-style thought experiment makes the distinction concrete. An urn has 90 balls, 30 red and 60 divided between black and yellow in an unknown way. One ball is uniformly drawn. Bets pay the same positive prize if their named event occurs, zero otherwise. Consider A: red, B: black, C: red or yellow, and D: black or yellow. Consider the strict preference pattern A ≻ B and D ≻ C. For any single subjective probability law with known red probability 1/3, write black probability q and yellow probability 2/3 − q. With the same two payoffs and increasing utility, A ≻ B requires q < 1/3. But D ≻ C requires 2/3 > 1 − q, hence q > 1/3. No single law can represent both strict comparisons. Changing wealth-utility curvature cannot resolve the contradiction because the prize and zero outcomes are the same. A maxmin expected-utility rule evaluates an act by inf P∈𝒫 E P [u(outcome)]. For a stipulated set q ∈ [0, 2/3], normalize prize utility to one and zero utility to zero. The worst-case values of A, B, C and D are respectively 1/3, 0, 1/3 and 2/3, supporting those strict preferences. A single symmetric Bayesian prior over possible black counts instead implies average q = 1/3 and indifference between A/B and C/D. These are different models, not different computations within an identical model. Worst-case evaluation needs a justified set of plausible laws; including implausible extremes can make it excessively conservative. It also needs an updating rule and, in dynamic problems, assumptions sufficient for consistency across time. This introductory example is a static calculation, not a general dynamic robustness theorem. Framing, failure to reduce compound lotteries and other preference models can also matter. A thought experiment demonstrates a representation conflict, not the empirical prevalence of a psychological explanation. Source route: MIT 14.123's alternatives-to-expected-utility notes discuss the Ellsberg conflict and ambiguity models. Our urn, comparison algebra and explicit plausible-law calculations are stated as a thought experiment without a new behavioral-data claim. Exercises With u(w) = w in a fixed dollar unit, compare certain wealth 2.25 with a lottery giving 1 or 4 with equal probabilities. Repeat using v(w) = 3u(w) + 7 and h(w) = log u(w). Which transformations preserve expected-utility preference, and why does increasing h not suffice? Final wealth is 80 or 120 dollars with equal probability and utility log(w/w ref ). Calculate expected wealth, certainty equivalent and risk premium. Compare with the local Arrow–Pratt approximation at wealth 100. Explain why the approximation is close but not exact. Initial wealth is 100 dollars; a 36-dollar loss occurs with probability 0.2. Under utility proportional to √w, calculate expected uninsured wealth, certainty equivalent, risk premium, maximum full-insurance premium and its loading over expected loss. Would premiums of 7.5 or 8 dollars be acceptable? Use exponential utility with a = 0.05 per dollar. A payoff is 0 or 20 dollars with equal probability. Derive its certainty-equivalent gain and risk premium. Is paying 8 dollars for it preferred to no purchase? Explain why an expected-profit calculation alone would give a different recommendation. Reproduce the exposure optimization in Worked example 2 for a = 0.02 and 0.04 per dollar. Derive the unconstrained optimum and then solve with f ∈ [0, 1]. What happens if the payoff mean is negative, or the variance is zero? State the assumptions under which the objective is exact. Payoff A is −1 or +1 dollar, equally likely. Payoff B is −2, 0 or +2 dollars with probabilities 1/8, 3/4, 1/8. Show equal means and variances. Under u(w) = −exp(−w) with dollar units, compare certainty-equivalent gains and prove which payoff is preferred using cosh(2) = 2cosh²(1) − 1. What does this establish about a general mean–variance ranking? For the urn example, derive the conflicting q restrictions from A ≻ B and D ≻ C. Compute maxmin values for q ∈ [0, 2/3], then for the narrower set q ∈ [1/6, 1/2]. Can a single symmetric Bayesian prior over black counts produce both strict preferences? Explain the roles of utility curvature and the plausible-law set. A decision memo uses log utility with normally distributed final wealth, calls a positive expected profit proof that every risk-averse investor should accept, uses μ − aσ²/2 for a highly skewed payoff under unspecified utility, and declares its calculated certainty equivalent a fair market price for all customers. Diagnose each claim and describe what a usable replacement memo would specify. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria Under u, certain utility is 2.25 and lottery expected utility is (1 + 4)/2 = 2.5, so prefer the lottery. Under v these are 13.75 and 14.5, preserving the ordering because the expected value transforms affinely. Under h, certain utility is log 2.25 ≈ 0.810930 and lottery expected utility is [log 1 + log 4]/2 = log 2 ≈ 0.693147, so prefer certainty. Increasing transforms preserve deterministic outcome ordering but generally change lottery comparisons; log introduces concavity and different risk preferences here. Expected wealth is 100. Solving log(c/w ref ) = ½log(80/w ref ) + ½log(120/w ref ) gives c = √(80·120) ≈ 97.979590 dollars. Risk premium is approximately 2.020410 dollars. The centered risk is ±20, with variance 400; A(100) = 1/100, giving local premium ½(1/100)400 = 2 dollars. Taylor remainder terms do not vanish exactly for this finite risk. As its amplitude shrinks the second-order approximation becomes accurate under the stated smoothness/domain conditions. Uninsured wealth is 100 with probability 0.8 and 64 with probability 0.2, giving mean 92.8 dollars. Expected utility is 0.8(10) + 0.2(8) = 9.6, so certainty equivalent is 92.16. Risk premium is 92.8 − 92.16 = 0.64 dollar. Maximum insurance price is 100 − 92.16 = 7.84 dollars. Expected loss is 0.2(36) = 7.2 dollars; its maximum acceptable loading is 0.64. A 7.5-dollar premium gives certain wealth 92.5, above the certainty equivalent, so is accepted; 8 gives 92, below it, so is rejected. Correct separation of expected loss and risk premium is essential. E[e −aX ] = (1 + e −1 )/2, so gain certainty equivalent is −20log[(1 + e −1 )/2] ≈ 7.597710 dollars. Expected payoff is 10 dollars; risk premium is approximately 2.402290 dollars. The 8-dollar cost subtracts 8 from the certainty-equivalent gain under CARA, giving about −0.402290, so no purchase is preferred. Risk-neutral expected net profit is 2 dollars and recommends purchase. The differing recommendations follow from specified preferences, not disagreement about the probability law. For normal X, gain certainty equivalent is fμ − af²σ²/2. With σ² > 0, derivative μ − aσ²f gives unconstrained f* = μ/(aσ²); strict negative second derivative establishes the maximum. Clip to [0, 1]. For μ = 4 and σ² = 400, f* is 1/2 at a = 0.02, yielding gain 1; at a = 0.04 it is 1/4, yielding gain 0.5. With negative mean and positive variance, the constrained optimum is f = 0. At zero variance, the payoff is deterministic and the objective is linear: choose 1 for positive mean, 0 for negative mean, any f for zero mean. Exactness requires exponential utility, the specified normal or deterministic payoff and no omitted costs/constraints. Both means are zero. A has second moment 1; B has (1/8)4 + (1/8)4 = 1, so both variances are 1 dollar squared. A's exponential moment is cosh(1), giving certainty-equivalent gain −log cosh(1) ≈ −0.433781 dollar. B's moment is 3/4 + (1/4)cosh(2). Let c = cosh(1) > 1. This equals (1 + c²)/2, whose excess over c is (c − 1)²/2 > 0. B therefore has more negative expected utility and lower certainty equivalent; prefer A. Mean and variance alone do not determine the general exponential-utility ranking. The normal-family formula is not transferable merely because these discrete payoffs share moments. The same positive prize implies comparisons of event probabilities: A ≻ B gives 1/3 > q, while D ≻ C gives 2/3 > 1 − q and hence q > 1/3. They conflict for any single q. Over [0, 2/3], worst-case values are 1/3, 0, 1/3, 2/3. Over [1/6, 1/2], they are 1/3, 1/6, 1/2, 2/3, still producing A ≻ B and D ≻ C. A symmetric single Bayesian prior implies mean q = 1/3 and ties, not both strict preferences. Concavity over the same two prize outcomes cannot fix the contradictory probabilities. The chosen plausible-law set changes worst-case values and must be justified rather than treated as observed truth. A nondegenerate normal law assigns positive probability to nonpositive wealth where log utility is undefined. Positive expected profit need not dominate a safer option under increasing concave utility, and the relevant costs and final wealth must enter the comparison. The displayed mean–variance expression requires exponential utility and normality for exactness, or a justified small-risk approximation with an appropriate local curvature; skewness and higher features can matter. A preference-dependent certainty equivalent is not a universal market price. A usable memo specifies final outcomes and utility domains, feasible positions/budget/default rules, probability/parameter assumptions and sensitivity, expected utility or explicitly qualified approximations, and the scope of the decision maker's valuation. It distinguishes private valuation from prices and from welfare across people; a leader cannot aggregate arbitrary utility scales into a uniquely justified institutional rule. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: distinguish a probability mixture from a combined payoff. Derive Jensen's risk-aversion implication, a certainty equivalent and an insurance-price decomposition. Explain why concavity can support both insurance purchases and positive risky exposure, and why utility numbers can be affinely rescaled without changing either choice. Delayed check: after a week, reconstruct the finite binary-lottery utility construction and the small-risk Arrow–Pratt expansion. Complete the Gaussian square to derive the exact CARA-normal certainty equivalent. Reproduce the equal-moment counterexample and the urn's contradictory q inequalities. State which arguments concern preferences, which concern probability laws and which depend on a specific parametric model. The next chapter, information, forecasting and scoring , evaluates forecasts and the value of learning before acting. Later theory and finance volumes extend these foundations to portfolio choice, insurance markets, intertemporal utility, stochastic dominance and alternative preference models."}, {"title": "03.11 · Information, forecasting and scoring", "url": "v03-c11.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "The forecast, the information and the decision A risk committee receives three forecasts of a binary loss event: 50%, 20% and 5%. The smallest number is not necessarily the best forecast, and the best forecast need not change the committee's action. To evaluate the reports, specify the event, horizon, information available when the report was issued, outcome measurement and scoring rule. To evaluate a purchase of information, also specify feasible actions and their consequences. Prediction and decision quality share a probability model but answer different questions. Write Y for the future outcome, I for information available at the forecast origin, and q for a forecast of P(Y = 1 | I) when Y is binary. A full predictive distribution describes all possible outcomes; a point forecast describes a particular feature such as a conditional mean or quantile. A forecast of next month's level is different from a forecast of its change, its final revised value or the chance of crossing a threshold. Record the units and the exact event before calculating any score. All numerical laws in this chapter are synthetic. Point forecasts: a loss function selects the target For a real outcome with finite second moment, squared-error loss is (Y − a)². Expanding around μ = E[Y] gives E[(Y − a)²] = Var(Y) + (a − μ)². The unique optimum is the mean. Conditional on information, the same identity selects E[Y | I]. Error is measured in outcome units squared. A large error receives a disproportionately large penalty, which must be appropriate to the use. With E[|Y|] finite, absolute-error loss |Y − a| selects a median. More generally, for 0 < τ < 1 define the pinball loss ρ τ (u) = τu for u ≥ 0 and (τ − 1)u for u < 0, where u = Y − a. Underprediction therefore costs τ per unit and overprediction costs 1 − τ per unit. This loss has the outcome's units and is convex in a. Let F be the CDF. The left derivative of E[ρ τ (Y − a)] is F(a−) − τ and its right derivative is F(a) − τ. To derive them, note that each realized loss has slope 1 − τ when Y < a and −τ when Y > a; at equality its left and right slopes differ. Difference quotients are bounded by 1, so expectation can be interchanged with these one-sided limits. Convexity says a minimizes expected loss exactly when its left slope is nonpositive and its right slope nonnegative: F(a−) ≤ τ ≤ F(a). This is the quantile condition, valid at atoms and across flat parts of F. At τ = 1/2, pinball loss is half absolute loss. Quantiles need not be unique: a CDF flat at τ can make an entire interval optimal. Squared loss, absolute loss and asymmetric loss can therefore give different correct forecasts of the same distribution. Comparing their raw values mixes different objectives and sometimes different units. Proper probability scores Here a score is a loss to minimize . Some references use rewards to maximize; negate their reward to obtain our convention. A scoring loss ℓ(Q, y) is proper on a specified class of distributions when E Y∼P [ℓ(P, Y)] ≤ E Y∼P [ℓ(Q, Y)] for all allowed P and Q. It is strictly proper when equality requires Q = P. Expectations must be well defined. Propriety rewards reporting one's probability assessment accurately in expectation; it does not make a reported belief objectively true or guarantee that it wins on every realized outcome. For Y ∈ {0, 1}, the Brier loss is (q − Y)², with 0 ≤ q ≤ 1. If the true probability is p, direct averaging gives p(1 − q)² + (1 − p)q² = p(1 − p) + (q − p)². The first term is irreducible uncertainty; the second is the cost of an incorrect probability. Thus Brier loss is strictly proper, including at p = 0 or 1. A categorical version sums (q j − 1{Y = j})² across categories. For two categories this sum is twice our binary convention. State the normalization when comparing reports. The binary logarithmic loss is −Y log q − (1 − Y) log(1 − q), using natural logarithms. An impossible forecast for an event that occurs incurs infinite loss. Terms with zero true probability contribute zero to expected loss. For a finite categorical law p, expected excess log loss from reporting q instead of p is the relative entropy D(p || q) = Σ j:p j >0 p j log(p j /q j ). If any such q j is zero the excess is infinite. Otherwise log t ≤ t − 1 gives −D(p || q) ≤ Σ p j >0 (q j − p j ) ≤ 0. Equality requires q j = p j on the positive support and no remaining probability outside it. Hence the log loss is strictly proper. Its units are dimensionless; its penalties become very large near a confidently wrong boundary. Clipping reported probabilities can make computation finite, but changes the permitted forecast class. It must be disclosed and applied consistently. Applying either proof conditional on I shows that the true conditional probability p(I) minimizes conditional expected score; averaging gives the unconditional optimum among I-measurable reports. A forecaster with additional information can reproduce the old report, so its best attainable expected proper loss cannot increase. This statement concerns optimal reports under the stipulated joint law, not a guarantee for a fitted model or for a particular test sample. Do not confuse outcome weighting with a harmless change of scale. If the loss is w 1 Y(1 − q)² + w 0 (1 − Y)q² for positive weights, its expected minimizer is q* = w 1 p/[w 1 p + w 0 (1 − p)]. Except at special cases, unequal weights elicit a transformed probability. A costly false negative can justify a different action threshold without making the underlying event probability larger. Keep the probability forecast and the decision loss explicit. Calibration, resolution and sharpness A binary forecast Q is calibrated under its joint distribution with Y if E[Y | Q] = Q almost surely. Among occasions receiving a report q, the event frequency is q in the population. This is a joint property of reports and outcomes. A forecast always equal to the overall event rate is calibrated even when it ignores informative groups. Overall calibration also does not establish calibration within every relevant subgroup. For a finite collection of report values, let r(Q) = E[Y | Q] denote the true event rate within each report class. Conditional squared-error decomposition and total variance give E[(Q − Y)²] = E[Var(Y | Q)] + E[(Q − r(Q))²] = Var(Y) − Var(r(Q)) + E[(Q − r(Q))²]. The three terms in the second line are uncertainty, resolution and calibration error. Resolution measures how much event rates differ across report classes; higher resolution lowers loss, other things equal. The algebra also holds for a general random report with conditional expectations. To prove it, write Q − Y = Q − r + r − Y; the conditional mean of the cross term is zero. Then apply Var(Y) = E[Var(Y | Q)] + Var(r). For binary Y with overall probability p̄, uncertainty is p̄(1 − p̄). Sharpness concerns concentration of forecast distributions: binary reports nearer 0 or 1 imply less predicted uncertainty. It is a property of the reports alone. A confident forecast can be wrong. Assess sharpness with calibration and proper score rather than treating confidence as an achievement. In a sample, calibration bins estimate r; grouping nearby but distinct reports changes the object and introduces estimation error. An exact population decomposition is not a proof that noisy in-sample bins generalize. Worked example 1: calibrated, informative and overconfident forecasts Half the occasions belong to group L and half to H. Conditional event probabilities are 0.2 and 0.8. Forecaster A always reports 0.5; B reports 0.2 in L and 0.8 in H; C reports 0.05 in L and 0.95 in H. The overall event probability is 0.5, so uncertainty is 0.25. Forecast Calibration error Resolution Expected Brier loss Expected log loss A: constant 0.5 0 0 0.25 log 2 ≈ 0.693147 B: 0.2 / 0.8 0 0.09 0.16 −0.2log 0.2 − 0.8log 0.8 ≈ 0.500402 C: 0.05 / 0.95 0.0225 0.09 0.1825 −0.2log 0.05 − 0.8log 0.95 ≈ 0.640181 A is calibrated because its sole report equals the overall frequency. B is calibrated and resolves the groups: the variance of 0.2 and 0.8 about 0.5 is 0.09. C's report classes still separate the groups, so its resolution is also 0.09, but each report misses its true rate by 0.15; calibration error is 0.15² = 0.0225. Thus 0.25 − 0.09 + 0.0225 = 0.1825. C is sharper than B and less accurate under both proper losses. At classification threshold 0.5, B and C both predict no event in L and an event in H; each is correct 80% of the time. A, with a fixed tie rule, is correct 50%. Classification accuracy cannot distinguish B from C's probability errors. Relative to the fixed population baseline loss 0.25, B's Brier skill index is 1 − 0.16/0.25 = 0.36. This expresses a 36% expected-loss reduction; it is not a probability of success. A skill index usually compares a model's score to a benchmark score. A fixed positive normalization preserves expected-score incentives. A ratio with a random realized benchmark denominator generally does not; its expectation cannot be replaced by the ratio of expected losses. Do not declare a skill-score formula proper merely because its underlying loss is proper. Keep the underlying losses available for inspection. Method source: Gneiting and Raftery's original scoring-rule paper , especially the sign convention, categorical scores, quantile scores and skill-score discussion. The derivations and synthetic comparisons above are written independently. Distributional checks and a credible evaluation design For a continuous conditional CDF F(y | I), the probability integral transform U = F(Y | I) is conditionally uniform on [0, 1] when Y truly has that conditional law. For strictly increasing F, P(U ≤ u | I) = P(Y ≤ F −1 (u | I) | I) = u. A continuous CDF with flat regions gives the same result using a generalized inverse, because those regions have zero probability. Discrete CDFs instead produce jumps; their ordinary transforms are not uniform. Randomizing uniformly within the CDF jump at each observed value gives the appropriate randomized transform, with auxiliary randomness independent of the outcome and information. A marginally uniform transform histogram is weaker than a correct conditional distribution. It does not verify subgroup behavior, serial independence or every joint forecast horizon. Overlapping forecast horizons can produce dependent errors even when individual predictive marginals are correct. Use several diagnostics tied to the particular forecasting task, not a single histogram as universal certification. Evaluate a forecasting procedure using outcomes it did not use for fitting or selection. Freeze the information set at each origin, including which data vintage was available. Fit and tune on earlier data; reserve later observations for confirmation. Compare model and baseline on identical targets, horizons, units and cases, with any missing-outcome rule documented. Future revisions or future labels are not origin-time inputs. A simulation using them estimates a different, infeasible procedure. Record individual score differences as well as their average. Sampling uncertainty depends on their joint law. An iid standard error is not automatically valid with shared shocks or overlapping horizons. Selecting the best of many models on the final test set makes the displayed winner's performance optimistic; that set has become a selection set. Separate tuning from confirmation, and report the number of choices and any repeated inspection. Calibration plots need uncertainty assessment and enough observations in each region. These are design requirements, not a claim that one holdout eliminates drift, selection or misspecification. Expected value of information Let θ be a finite state, d one of finitely many feasible actions, U(d, θ) its utility, and Z a signal with a known joint law with θ. Use the same actions before and after observing Z, allow the recipient to ignore the signal, and assume observing it does not itself change states or payoffs. With finite expected utilities, define V 0 = max d E[U(d, θ)], V Z = E Z [max d E[U(d, θ) | Z]], V PI = E θ [max d U(d, θ)]. The expected value of sample information is EVSI = V Z − V 0 ; that of perfect information is EVPI = V PI − V 0 . They obey 0 ≤ EVSI ≤ EVPI. For the lower bound, use the old maximizing action at every signal; averaging its conditional value gives V 0 . Optimizing can only do better. For the upper bound, U(d, θ) ≤ max a U(a, θ) for every d and state; condition, maximize over d, then average. Signals with zero probability have no effect on the sum. The gain is measured before seeing the signal, integrating over its possible reports. A favorable report's posterior expected payoff is not the ex ante value of buying the report. Information can change beliefs without changing the maximizing action. In that case it has zero gross value for this decision even though it may improve a forecast score. If utility is risk-neutral monetary payoff and a deterministic information fee c is payable regardless of signal and action, subtract c from V Z ; buy when EVSI exceeds c. With nonlinear utility over wealth, put the fee inside U(w + payoff − c) and recompute the best attainable expected utility. Utility units cannot be subtracted from dollars. Delays, capacity constraints or a signal that changes counterparties' behavior may change the decision problem and invalidate a simple nonnegative-gross-value comparison. Worked example 2: a report worth less than perfect information A risk-neutral decision maker may invest or abstain. Investment pays 120 dollars in a good state and −40 in a bad state; abstention pays zero. The good-state prior is 0.3. Investment's prior expected payoff is 0.3(120) − 0.7(40) = 8, so V 0 = 8 dollars. Perfect information leads to investment only when good, giving V PI = 0.3(120) = 36 and EVPI = 28 dollars. A report is positive with probability 0.8 when good and 0.2 when bad. Its positive probability is 0.3(0.8) + 0.7(0.2) = 0.38. The posterior good probability is 0.24/0.38 = 12/19 after a positive report, and 0.06/0.62 = 3/31 after a negative report. Investment is worthwhile when p(120) − (1 − p)40 > 0, or p > 1/4. Invest only after the positive report. Positive-report conditional expected payoff is [12(120) − 7(40)]/19 = 1160/19 ≈ 61.052632 dollars. Negative-report conditional investment payoff is [3(120) − 28(40)]/31 = −760/31 ≈ −24.516129, so choose zero instead. Integrating optimized choices gives V Z = (19/50)(1160/19) + (31/50)(0) = 23.2 dollars. Consequently EVSI = 15.2, below EVPI = 28. A report fee of 10 dollars leaves expected net payoff 13.2, exceeding the no-report payoff 8 by 5.2. The improvement comes entirely from avoiding investment after a negative report: (31/50)(760/31) = 15.2. The positive report confirms the prior action. No comparison of 61.05 with 8 measures the report's pre-purchase value; their conditioning differs. Method source: Duke's decision-analysis notes on information value . The finite-action bounds above are explicitly derived, and the report/payoff numbers are original. Why garbling cannot improve optimal decisions Suppose W is generated by passing Z through a randomized channel K(w | z), with W conditionally independent of θ given Z. This is a precise meaning of a less informative experiment: θ → Z → W. A recipient of Z can simulate W using the channel and then follow the action rule optimal for W. That strategy reproduces the same state/action joint law and therefore the same expected utility V W . Conditional on Z, randomizing among actions averages their conditional utilities; no average exceeds their largest value. Thus the optimal Z-based action has V Z ≥ V W . The argument depends on the stated channel, feasible actions and unchanged payoffs. It does not order arbitrary reports by their names, precision claims, sample sizes or realized outcomes. More informative signals can tie if they do not change the best decision. Whether extra accuracy is economically worthwhile depends on costs, action thresholds, preferences and the state/payoff law. Proper scoring supplies a criterion for honest probability reporting; decision analysis supplies the criterion for using it. Exercises A binary event has probability 0.3. Compute expected Brier and log losses for reports 0.1, 0.3 and 0.6. Prove which minimizes each expected loss. Explain why that forecast need not win on one observed event. Reconstruct the population Brier decomposition for A, B and C in Worked example 1 from the joint group/event law. Compute B's skill against A, and classification accuracy at threshold 0.5 with a fixed tie rule. Explain what calibration and classification accuracy each miss. For weighted Brier loss with w 1 = 9 and w 0 = 1, derive the optimum report at true p = 0.1. Compare its expected loss with truthful reporting. Derive the optimum for general positive weights and explain how to handle an expensive false negative without distorting a probability report. A dollar loss Y takes values 0, 10 and 30 with probabilities 0.2, 0.5 and 0.3. Find squared-error, absolute-error and τ = 0.8 pinball optima. Compute squared loss at the mean and at 10, absolute loss at a median, and pinball loss at 10 and 30. Are the differing forecasts contradictory? An investment pays 100 dollars when good and −20 when bad; the good prior is 0.2, and abstention pays zero. A positive signal has probability 0.75 when good and 0.25 when bad. Find both posteriors, the optimal policy, V 0 , V Z , EVSI and EVPI. Would a risk-neutral recipient pay 8 dollars for this signal? An investment pays 50 dollars when good and −10 when bad; the good prior is 0.9. A positive signal has probability 0.6 when good and 0.4 when bad. Abstention pays zero. Show that the signal changes beliefs but has zero gross value for this decision. Compute the perfect-information value. Garble the signal in Exercise 5 by independently reversing its sign with probability 1/4. Derive the new likelihoods and posteriors, optimal policy and expected payoff. Compare its EVSI to the original signal's and prove the general garbling inequality for finite actions. A forecasting memo selects the best of 100 models on its advertised final test data, uses final revised input series that were unavailable at forecast origins, calls empirical calibration bins proof of exact probabilities, and calls a uniform transform histogram proof of independent errors. It recommends buying a report solely because its classification accuracy is higher. Diagnose the claims and design a usable evaluation and purchase procedure. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria The irreducible Brier component is 0.3(0.7) = 0.21. Squared report errors are 0.04, 0 and 0.09, giving expected losses 0.25, 0.21 and 0.30. Log losses −0.3log q − 0.7log(1 − q) are approximately 0.764528, 0.610864 and 0.794651. The Brier excess is (q − p)²; the log excess is D(p || q), strictly positive for q ≠ p by the proof above. If the realized outcome is 1, q = 0.6 has smaller loss than q = 0.3 under both scores. Expected optimality is a repeated-law criterion, not dominance on every draw. The four joint probabilities for (L,0), (L,1), (H,0), (H,1) are 0.4, 0.1, 0.1 and 0.4. Overall p̄ = 0.5 gives uncertainty 0.25. A has r = 0.5, resolution zero, calibration error zero and Brier 0.25. B has r = 0.2/0.8, resolution (0.3² + 0.3²)/2 = 0.09 and calibration error zero, giving 0.16. C retains the same conditional rates and resolution, but error is (0.15² + 0.15²)/2 = 0.0225, giving 0.1825. B's fixed-baseline skill is 0.36. B/C classify L as 0 and H as 1, correct with probability 0.4 + 0.4 = 0.8. A's constant tie rule gives accuracy 0.5. Calibration alone misses useful resolution; binary classification misses the magnitude of probability error. Expected loss is 0.9(1 − q)² + 0.9q². Its derivative is −1.8 + 3.6q, with positive second derivative, so q* = 0.5 and minimum loss 0.45. Truthful q = 0.1 gives 0.9(0.81) + 0.9(0.01) = 0.738. In general differentiating w 1 p(1 − q)² + w 0 (1 − p)q² gives the displayed transformed optimum. Therefore this particular outcome-weighted loss is not a proper rule for untransformed p. Report p under a proper probability loss, then apply the institution's explicit false-positive/false-negative cost threshold when selecting an action. A transformed output can be inverted if weights are known, but it must not be mislabeled as the original probability. The mean is 0.5(10) + 0.3(30) = 14 dollars and E[Y²] = 0.5(100) + 0.3(900) = 320 dollars squared. Minimum squared loss is 320 − 14² = 124; at 10 it is 124 + 4² = 140. Since F(10−) = 0.2 and F(10) = 0.7, the median is 10; absolute loss there is 0.2(10) + 0.3(20) = 8 dollars. The 0.8 quantile is 30 since F(30−) = 0.7 ≤ 0.8 ≤ 1. At 10, pinball loss is 0.2(0.2·10) + 0.3(0.8·20) = 5.2 dollars. At 30 it is 0.2(0.2·30) + 0.5(0.2·20) = 3.2 dollars. Different losses select different distributional features, so the forecasts answer different tasks. Investment's prior expected payoff is 0.2(100) − 0.8(20) = 4 dollars, so invest without a report. The positive probability is 0.2(0.75) + 0.8(0.25) = 7/20; its good posterior is 3/7. Negative probability is 13/20; its good posterior is 1/13. Investment is preferred when 120p − 20 > 0, namely p > 1/6. Positive conditional investment payoff is (300 − 80)/7 = 220/7 dollars; negative is (100 − 240)/13 = −140/13. Thus invest only after positive, V Z = (7/20)(220/7) = 11, and EVSI = 7. Perfect information gives payoff 0.2(100) = 20 and EVPI = 16. Paying 8 produces expected net payoff 3, below the no-report 4, so decline. Comparing the fee to V Z rather than its improvement would give the wrong decision. Investment is worthwhile above p = 1/6, and V 0 = 0.9(50) − 0.1(10) = 44 dollars. Positive probability is 0.9(0.6) + 0.1(0.4) = 29/50; its good posterior is 27/29. Negative probability is 21/50; its good posterior is 0.36/0.42 = 6/7. Both exceed 1/6, so invest after either signal. Iterated expectation gives V Z = 44, hence EVSI = 0. Perfect information avoids losses in the bad state: V PI = 45 and EVPI = 1 dollar. Belief updates are real but do not change the maximizing action. The garbled positive likelihood is (3/4)(3/4) + (1/4)(1/4) = 5/8 when good; when bad it is (3/4)(1/4) + (1/4)(3/4) = 3/8. Positive probability is (1/5)(5/8) + (4/5)(3/8) = 17/40 and good posterior 5/17. Negative probability is 23/40 and good posterior 3/23. Only 5/17 exceeds 1/6. Optimal expected payoff is the positive joint good mass times 100 minus its bad mass times 20: (1/8)100 − (3/10)20 = 6.5 dollars. Its EVSI is 6.5 − 4 = 2.5, below the original 7. Generally a recipient of Z can generate W using the stipulated state-independent conditional channel and follow W's optimal policy. This matches W's joint state/action distribution. Conditional randomization cannot exceed the largest action utility given Z, establishing V Z ≥ V W . More information does not need to create a strict improvement. A satisfactory critique identifies test-set selection, unavailable origin inputs, finite-sample calibration uncertainty, and the gap between marginal transforms and temporal/joint calibration. Rebuild forecasts from timestamped available vintages; reserve separate earlier training/tuning and later confirmation periods; document targets, horizons, cases and missingness. Compare a declared baseline and model using proper scores and score-difference uncertainty appropriate to dependence. Treat calibration/transform diagnostics as estimates with subgroup and serial checks. For purchase, specify actions, state-contingent consequences, signal likelihoods, prior and preferences, optimize before and after the report, average over possible reports, and include fees/delay. Higher classification accuracy alone supplies neither probability calibration nor an information-value calculation. Alternative designs are acceptable if they address all five mechanisms explicitly. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why a constant base-rate report can be calibrated, why an overconfident report can have high classification accuracy, and why the best probability forecast need not be worth buying. Derive the Brier excess and the information-value bounds without relying on a slogan. Delayed check: after a week, reconstruct the quantile subgradient condition, categorical log-loss inequality, Brier decomposition and signal decision tree. Change the investment payoff and locate the posterior action threshold. Give a garbling argument and a forecast-validation design with dated inputs. The next chapter, measure and stochastic foundations , formalizes information as sigma-algebras, expectation as integration and sequential forecasts as martingales. It supplies conditions for the limit interchanges used throughout statistical reasoning."}, {"title": "03.12 · Measure and stochastic foundations", "url": "v03-c12.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Why formal probability is practical A sequence of forecasts can converge on almost every realized history while its expected loss fails to converge. A conditional mean must use only information actually available. A fair sequential game can appear to produce a certain profit if an invalid stopping argument is used. These are economic problems about information, tails and admissible decisions. Measure-theoretic probability gives the language and proofs needed to distinguish valid arguments from attractive errors. This chapter constructs expectation and conditional expectation, proves the central integration limits, introduces discrete-time martingales, and completes the integrable iid strong law promised in Chapter 6 . It is a bridge to graduate econometrics and stochastic finance. Product-measure construction and general unbounded optional-stopping extensions are identified as further study; continuous-time stochastic integration is taught in later finance material. None is silently treated as already proved. Events, information and measurability A sigma-algebra F on a set Ω contains Ω, is closed under complements in Ω and under countable unions. It therefore contains ∅ and is closed under countable intersections. A probability measure P on F is nonnegative, has P(Ω) = 1 and is countably additive over disjoint events. Together (Ω, F, P) form a probability space. Countability matters: limits of random variables are built from countable unions and intersections of events. The sigma-algebra generated by a collection C, denoted σ(C), is the intersection of all sigma-algebras containing C. This intersection is itself a sigma-algebra; the full power set ensures the family is nonempty. On a finite Ω, a sigma-algebra consists of unions of cells of a partition. Observing a cell identifies which events in that sigma-algebra occurred but need not identify the individual state. Information G ⊆ F therefore means a collection of observable events, rather than a particular observed value. The Borel sigma-algebra on the real line is generated by its open sets, equivalently by intervals (−∞, t] with rational t. A real function X on Ω is F-measurable when X −1 (B) ∈ F for every Borel set B. It suffices to check the generating intervals: the sets B whose preimages lie in F themselves form a sigma-algebra. A random variable is such a measurable function. Its distribution is the measure P X (B) = P(X ∈ B), and σ(X) is the information generated by observing X. Sums and products of measurable real functions are measurable; continuous functions of measurable finite vectors are measurable. Countable suprema and infima are measurable since their level events are countable combinations of the original level events. Consequently liminf, limsup and pointwise limits, wherever the limits exist, have measurable versions. One can set a limit to zero on the measurable event where it fails to exist. A statement holds almost surely if its failure event has probability zero. Countably many almost-sure statements can be used simultaneously after excluding their union of null events. An arbitrary uncountable union of null sets need not be null. Completing a probability space adds subsets of measurable null sets as events. One need not assume the information sigma-algebra is complete to define conditional expectation: its representative is required to be G-measurable and uniqueness is only almost sure. Countable additivity implies continuity from below: if A n ↑ A, disjointify the increments to obtain P(A n ) ↑ P(A). Complementing yields continuity from above for decreasing events on a probability space. These facts will justify limits of event probabilities. Lebesgue measure assigns interval length on the real line and extends it to a sigma-algebra containing Borel sets. Its existence through a measure-extension construction is a standard theorem, not proved here; one must not assume every subset of the real line has a consistent translation-invariant length. A uniform variable on [0, 1] uses the resulting probability measure restricted to that interval. Constructing the integral For a nonnegative simple function s = Σ j=1 m a j 1 A j , with a j finite nonnegative and A j disjoint measurable sets covering Ω, define E[s] = Σ a j P(A j ). Refining two partitions by their intersections proves that this value does not depend on the representation. The same refinement proves linearity and monotonicity for simple functions. For a measurable X taking values in [0, ∞], define E[X] = sup{E[s] : s is nonnegative simple and s ≤ X}. The supremum may be infinite. The definition gives monotonicity immediately. If X is simple, its original value agrees with this definition. One useful sequence of simple approximations is s n = min(n, 2 −n ⌊2 n X⌋), assigning the value n when X = ∞. It increases to X: the finer dyadic grid cannot round a finite value further downward, the cap increases, and the rounding error below the cap is less than 2 −n . For signed X, write X + = max(X, 0) and X − = max(−X, 0). If E[|X|] = E[X + ] + E[X − ] is finite, X is integrable and E[X] = E[X + ] − E[X − ]. Extended expectations can also be defined when only one side is infinite. If both are infinite, the expectation is undefined; ∞ − ∞ is not a number. This is why moment assumptions belong in a theorem, not in a footnote. Changing a measurable variable on a measurable null event leaves its integral unchanged: every simple function supported on that event has expectation zero, and the defining supremum is therefore zero. Split simple approximations into their restrictions to the event and its complement. This also extends integral monotonicity to almost-sure inequalities, so the proofs below may remove a common measurable null event. Monotone convergence, Fatou and domination Monotone convergence theorem. If 0 ≤ X n ↑ X almost surely, then E[X n ] ↑ E[X], allowing infinity. Work off the common null event. Monotonicity gives L = lim E[X n ] ≤ E[X]. For the reverse inequality, fix a nonnegative simple s ≤ X and 0 < c < 1. Set A n = {X n ≥ cs}. These events increase, and their union contains every state where s > 0, since X ≥ s > cs there. Thus E[s1 A n ] → E[s] by the finite simple-function formula and continuity from below. Since X n ≥ cs1 A n , L ≥ cE[s]. Let c increase to 1 and take the supremum over s. This proves L ≥ E[X]. The theorem shows E[s n ] ↑ E[X] for the dyadic approximations. Apply it to simultaneous simple approximations of X and Y to extend nonnegative linearity: E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y], and E[aX] = aE[X] for finite a ≥ 0, with the zero-times-infinity case interpreted by the zero function. Positive/negative decomposition then gives ordinary linearity for integrable signed variables. Since |X + Y| ≤ |X| + |Y|, the sum stays integrable. Integrating inequalities yields |E[X]| ≤ E[|X|]. Fatou's lemma. For nonnegative X n , E[liminf X n ] ≤ liminf E[X n ]. Set Z m = inf n≥m X n . It increases to liminf X n . Also E[Z m ] ≤ inf n≥m E[X n ]. Monotone convergence on the left and the definition of liminf on the right prove the result. Mass can disappear in a pointwise limit; Fatou does not assert equality. Dominated convergence theorem. Suppose X n → X almost surely and |X n | ≤ G for all n almost surely, where G ≥ 0 and E[G] < ∞. Then |X| ≤ G, all variables are integrable, E[X n ] → E[X], and E[|X n − X|] → 0. To prove the expectation claim, apply Fatou to G + X n to get E[X] ≤ liminf E[X n ], and to G − X n to get E[X] ≥ limsup E[X n ]. For the absolute-difference claim, repeat the expectation result with |X n − X| ≤ 2G and limit zero. A uniform finite bound suffices on a probability space because it is integrable. Merely bounding E[|X n |] does not suffice. Let U be uniform on (0, 1), and X n = n1{U ≤ 1/n}. For every U > 0, X n eventually equals zero, yet E[X n ] = 1. There is no common integrable dominator. A useful alternative condition is uniform integrability : lim K→∞ sup n E[|X n |1{|X n | > K}] = 0. The spikes fail this condition; for n > K their tail expectation is 1. For nonnegative Y i , partial sums increase, so monotone convergence gives E[Σ Y i ] = Σ E[Y i ]. This countable interchange requires no independence. If Σ E[|Y i |] < ∞, the sum of absolute values is integrable and finite almost surely: an infinite value on a positive-probability set would force an infinite expectation by monotonicity. Dominated convergence then permits interchange for the signed series too. For integration over a product of sigma-finite measure spaces, Tonelli's theorem permits iterated integrals of a nonnegative measurable function in either order, including infinite results. Fubini's theorem permits signed interchange when the integral of the absolute value is finite; sections are integrable almost everywhere. These product-space theorems include construction and measurability details beyond the countable-series proof above. We state them as standard results and give a source route for their full proofs. A density-based expectation such as ∫h(x)f(x)dx is a special case of Lebesgue integration; absolute integrability is the condition for unrestricted signed iterated expectation calculations. Further method reference: Terence Tao's integration and expectation notes . The construction and limit proofs above are presented explicitly in our notation rather than replacing them with a citation. Conditional expectation as an information-respecting variable For integrable X and G ⊆ F, a conditional expectation m = E[X | G] is an integrable G-measurable variable satisfying E[m1 A ] = E[X1 A ] for every A ∈ G. This says the forecast preserves X's aggregate value on every observable event. It is not a numerical expectation conditional on an event of probability zero. If G comes from a finite partition, m is constant on each positive-probability cell C and equals E[X1 C ]/P(C). Values on zero-probability cells do not change the defining identities. Uniqueness. If m and m′ satisfy the definition, use A k = {m − m′ > 1/k} ∈ G. Its integral difference is zero, but at least P(A k )/k, so P(A k ) = 0. Their union is {m > m′}. Reverse the variables to conclude m = m′ almost surely. This argument also proves order preservation: if X ≥ 0, using the events {m < −1/k} shows m ≥ 0 almost surely. Linearity follows by checking the event identity and invoking uniqueness. Consequently |E[X | G]| ≤ E[|X| | G] and E[|E[X | G]|] ≤ E[|X|], once existence is established. Existence through projection and truncation Here is a constructive existence argument using only the integration tools and completeness. Define L¹ as integrable variables modulo almost-sure equality, with norm ||X|| 1 = E[|X|]. Define L² by E[X²] < ∞, norm ||X|| 2 = √E[X²] and inner product E[XY]. The product is integrable since 2|XY| ≤ X² + Y². Cauchy–Schwarz follows from E[(X − tY)²] ≥ 0 and the discriminant of this quadratic; if E[Y²] = 0, Y = 0 almost surely and the result is immediate. Applying the bound with Y = 1 to |X| gives ||X|| 1 ≤ ||X|| 2 on a probability space. Expanding E[(X + Y)²] and applying Cauchy–Schwarz proves the L² triangle inequality. Completeness. From a Cauchy sequence in L p , p = 1 or 2, choose a subsequence X n j with ||X n j+1 − X n j || p ≤ 2 −j . The sum of the differences' L¹ norms is finite, so their absolute values sum to a finite number almost surely by the preceding countable interchange. The subsequence therefore converges pointwise almost surely to a measurable X. For p = 1, integrate the absolute tail bound; for p = 2, apply Fatou to squared tails and the finite L² triangle inequality. Either gives ||X − X n j || p ≤ Σ i≥j 2 −i . This also shows X has finite norm. The original Cauchy sequence converges to X by its Cauchy property and the triangle inequality. This proves both spaces complete. The subspace H of G-measurable L² variables is closed. Indeed, L² convergence has a subsequence converging almost surely by the same construction; the pointwise limit, set to zero where it fails to exist, is G-measurable. For X ∈ L² let d = inf Y∈H ||X − Y|| 2 , and choose Y j with their distances tending to d. The parallelogram identity gives ||Y j − Y k || 2 ² = 2||X − Y j || 2 ² + 2||X − Y k || 2 ² − 4||X − (Y j + Y k )/2|| 2 ² ≤ 2||X − Y j || 2 ² + 2||X − Y k || 2 ² − 4d² → 0. Thus Y j is Cauchy and converges in the closed space to a minimizer m. For Z ∈ H, the expression ||X − m − tZ||² is minimized at t = 0, so E[(X − m)Z] = 0. In particular Z = 1 A gives the defining conditional-expectation identities. Also m ∈ L² ⊆ L¹, so existence is proved for square-integrable X. The same orthogonality gives the Pythagorean forecast identity for any Y ∈ H: E[(X − Y)²] = E[(X − m)²] + E[(m − Y)²]. Now take merely integrable X, and clip it to X n = max(−n, min(X, n)). These bounded variables are in L². Dominated convergence gives ||X n − X|| 1 → 0. Write m n = E[X n | G]. Positivity and linearity already proved for L² imply ||m n − m k || 1 ≤ ||X n − X k || 1 . Hence m n is L¹-Cauchy and has an integrable G-measurable limit m; choose its measurable representative using an almost-sure subsequence. For any A ∈ G, L¹ convergence permits passing to the limit in E[m n 1 A ] = E[X n 1 A ]. This proves the required identity and general existence. Tower, known factors and conditional probabilities If G ⊆ H ⊆ F, the tower property is E[E[X | H] | G] = E[X | G]. Both sides are integrable and G-measurable, and their integrals against each A ∈ G equal E[X1 A ]. Uniqueness proves the result. Also E[X | G] = X almost surely when X is G-measurable, and E[E[X | G]] = E[X] by choosing A = Ω. For bounded G-measurable Z, E[ZX | G] = Z E[X | G]. First use the definition for Z = 1 B and A ∩ B, then finite linearity for simple Z. Approximate a general bounded Z by uniformly bounded simple functions converging pointwise. Dominated convergence on products with |X| and |m| verifies the event identities, hence proves the formula. Unbounded known factors require their own product-integrability conditions; the bounded proof does not justify arbitrary multiplication. A conditional probability P(B | G) means E[1 B | G], a variable between zero and one almost surely. With G = σ(Z), ordinary finite tables or conditional densities compute versions of this variable when their assumptions hold. Defining a simultaneous regular conditional distribution on a general measurable state space requires extra results; on standard Borel spaces these are available. The event-identity definition suffices for the martingale and projection arguments here. Worked example 1: information, projections and a coarser report Four equally likely states have outcomes X = 0, 2, 6 and 8 dollars. G reports whether the state is in C 1 = {1, 2} or C 2 = {3, 4}. Thus G = {∅, C 1 , C 2 , Ω} and m = E[X | G] takes values 1, 1, 7 and 7. For C 1 , E[m1 C 1 ] = (1 + 1)/4 = 1/2 = (0 + 2)/4; for C 2 both integrals equal 7/2. Unions verify every G event. E[X] = 4, E[X²] = (0 + 4 + 36 + 64)/4 = 26, and Var(X) = 10 dollars squared. Within-cell residuals are −1, 1, −1 and 1, so E[(X − m)²] = 1. The variance of m is 9, giving total variance 10 = 1 + 9. Forecasting with the constant 4 has error 10; observing G reduces optimal squared error to 1. For an arbitrary G-respecting forecast taking values a and b on the cells, its MSE is 1 + [(a − 1)² + (b − 7)²]/2, minimized uniquely by 1 and 7. A still coarser sigma-algebra containing only ∅ and Ω gives conditional expectation 4; taking the expectation of m recovers 4 by the tower. Observing a partition changes the attainable forecast without revealing individual outcomes. Filtrations, martingales and bounded stopping A filtration F 0 ⊆ F 1 ⊆ ··· describes increasing information. A process M n is adapted when M n is F n -measurable. It is a martingale if each M n is integrable and E[M n+1 | F n ] = M n almost surely. This is a conditional fairness statement, not a claim of independence, constant realized paths or negligible risk. If ε n are independent integrable mean-zero shocks and F n records the first n shocks, their partial sums form a martingale. Independence gives E[ε n+1 1 A ] = E[ε n+1 ]P(A) = 0 for A ∈ F n , which is the defining identity for zero conditional mean. For any integrable terminal payoff X, M n = E[X | F n ] is also a martingale by the tower property. Forecasts change as evidence arrives even though their expected next value equals their current value. A predictable strategy h k is F k−1 -measurable: the exposure is selected before the next increment. If h k is bounded for each k, accumulated gains Σ k≤n h k (M k − M k−1 ) form a martingale. Bounded pull-out and the zero conditional increment prove this one step at a time. Unbounded exposures need explicit integrability/admissibility conditions; predictability by itself is insufficient. A nonnegative integer-valued time T, possibly infinite, is a stopping time when {T ≤ n} ∈ F n . It is possible to know at time n whether stopping has occurred without seeing future information. If T ≤ N for a deterministic finite N, an integrable martingale satisfies E[M T ] = E[M 0 ]. Indeed, M T = M 0 + Σ k=1 N 1{T ≥ k}(M k − M k−1 ). The indicator is F k−1 -measurable because {T ≥ k} complements {T ≤ k − 1}. Each summand has zero expectation by bounded pull-out, and the finite sum is integrable. This proves bounded optional stopping. For an unbounded T, a limit of these expectations requires a justified interchange; almost-sure finiteness of T alone does not supply it. Worked example 2: an almost-sure gain with an invalid expectation limit Independent fair coin outcomes ε k are +1 for a win and −1 for a loss. Start at zero dollars. Bet 1, then 2, then 4, and so on until the first win, with bet 2 k−1 at round k if all preceding rounds lost. The rule is predictable. Its stopped wealth process M n is integrable and a martingale at every finite n: each round's bet is bounded by the deterministic amount 2 k−1 and the coin has conditional mean zero. Let T be the first win. P(T > N) = 2 −N → 0, so T is finite almost surely. At T the gain is 2 T−1 − (1 + 2 + ··· + 2 T−2 ) = 1 dollar. At T ∧ N, however, the gain is 1 if a win has occurred and −(2 N − 1) if not. Its expectation is (1 − 2 −N )1 + 2 −N [−(2 N − 1)] = 0. Nevertheless M T∧N → 1 almost surely, and E[M T ] = 1. The rare loss grows fast enough to retain expected magnitude near 1. For any large tail cutoff K, take N with 2 N − 1 > K; the absolute tail expectation is (2 N − 1)2 −N = 1 − 2 −N . Thus the sequence is not uniformly integrable and admits no common integrable dominator. Bounded stopping holds at every N; the unjustified infinite-horizon interchange creates the apparent contradiction. The strategy also requires unbounded borrowing and exposure, so a finite-budget institution cannot implement its purported sure gain. Further method reference: Amir Dembo's author-hosted probability notes . Chapters 1, 4 and 5 provide the surrounding measure, projection and stopping theory, including the product-measure and unbounded-time results explicitly left for further study here. The iid strong law with only a finite absolute mean Let X 1 , X 2 , … be iid real variables with E[|X 1 |] < ∞ and μ = E[X 1 ]. We prove n −1 Σ i≤n X i → μ almost surely without assuming finite variance. The plan is to discard a summable set of extreme observations, prove convergence of an independent weighted series, and convert series convergence into convergence of averages. The first Borel–Cantelli implication needed here is simple. If ΣP(A i ) < ∞, then P(∪ i≥m A i ) ≤ Σ i≥m P(A i ) → 0. The event of infinitely many A i is the intersection of these decreasing unions and has probability zero. No independence is needed for this implication. Step 1: truncate at each observation's index Set Z i = X i 1{|X i | ≤ i}. These variables are independent but need not have identical distributions. Countable nonnegative interchange gives Σ i≥1 P(X i ≠ Z i ) = Σ i≥1 P(|X 1 | > i) = E[Σ i≥1 1{|X 1 | > i}] ≤ E[|X 1 |] < ∞. Thus X i = Z i for all sufficiently large i on almost every path. The earlier differences are a finite sum on that path and vanish after division by n. Also E[Z i ] → μ by dominated convergence: in the common single-variable law, X1{|X| ≤ i} → X and its absolute value is bounded by |X|. Step 2: summable weighted variances Define U i = (Z i − E[Z i ])/i. These are independent, mean zero and square-integrable. Using Var(Z i ) ≤ E[Z i ²] and nonnegative interchange, Σ E[U i ²] ≤ E[X 1 ² Σ i≥1 1{|X 1 | ≤ i}/i²] ≤ 2E[|X 1 |] < ∞. For the last bound, put r = |X 1 |. If 0 ≤ r ≤ 1, Σ i≥1 i −2 ≤ 1 + ∫ 1 ∞ t −2 dt = 2, so r² times that sum is at most 2r. If r > 1, put m = ⌈r⌉ ≥ 2. Then Σ i≥m i −2 ≤ ∫ m−1 ∞ t −2 dt = 1/(m − 1) ≤ 2/r. Again the product with r² is at most 2r. The infinite untruncated second moment never enters this bound. Step 3: independent series converge under summable variances For independent mean-zero square-integrable variables V 1 , …, V N and their partial sums S k , the maximal inequality is P(max k≤N |S k | > ε) ≤ E[S N ²]/ε² = ΣE[V i ²]/ε². To prove it, partition the crossing event into A k , the first crossing at k. On A k , |S k | > ε. The future sum S N − S k is independent of A k and S k , with mean zero. Expanding E[S N ²1 A k ] therefore gives E[S k ²1 A k ] plus a nonnegative future-square term. Sum over the disjoint A k to get E[S N ²] ≥ ε²ΣP(A k ). This proves the inequality. Apply it to U m+1 , …, U N and increase N to infinity. Continuity from below of the crossing events gives P(sup k>m |Σ i=m+1 k U i | > ε) ≤ ε −2 Σ i>m E[U i ²]. Choose increasing m j whose variance tails are at most 2 −3j , and ε j = 2 −j . The crossing probability is at most 2 −j , summable in j. Borel–Cantelli says that eventually every partial tail starting at m j has magnitude at most 2 −j . Any two partial sums beyond m j then differ by at most 2·2 −j . The series ΣU i is Cauchy and therefore converges almost surely. Step 4: Kronecker's averaging lemma and the conclusion If a deterministic series Σ a i /i converges, let s n = Σ i≤n a i /i and s 0 = 0. Since a i = i(s i − s i−1 ), finite summation by parts gives (1/n)Σ i=1 n a i = s n − (1/n)Σ i=1 n−1 s i → 0. Indeed, if s i → s, split the average into a finite prefix and a tail within ε of s. The prefix divided by n vanishes, and the tail error is bounded by ε; the missing final term's weight tends to zero. This proves the Cesàro step and the averaging lemma rather than assuming either. Apply it path by path to a i = Z i − E[Z i ]. Step 3 yields convergence of Σa i /i almost surely, hence the centered Z average tends to zero. Since E[Z i ] → μ, the same finite-prefix/tail argument shows their average tends to μ. Step 1 shows replacing Z i by X i changes the average by a vanishing finite-prefix sum almost surely. Therefore X̄ n → μ almost surely, completing the proof. This theorem permits infinite variance, but does not grant the classical finite-variance CLT, a root-n standard error, or a useful finite-sample convergence rate. It also requires independence and identical distributions; a shared persistent shock can defeat convergence to the unconditional mean as Chapter 6 showed. A finite mean is a substantive tail condition, not an automatic property of an economic payoff. Further method reference: Tao's notes on weak and strong laws . The index truncation, variance summation, independent-series bound and averaging lemma required for our integrable strong-law result are all proved above. Exercises On Ω = {1, 2, 3, 4}, let a report distinguish {1, 3} from {2, 4}. List its generated sigma-algebra. Is the variable X = (0, 1, 0, 1) measurable with respect to it? Is Y = (0, 1, 2, 3)? Explain the information restriction without referring to probability. On [0, 1] with uniform probability, let R be the indicator of rational numbers. Prove that R is measurable and has Lebesgue expectation zero. Explain why its ordinary Riemann integral does not exist. Is expectation allowed to ignore an event that is dense? Let U be uniform on [0, 1]. For s n = 2 −n ⌊2 n U⌋, compute E[s n ] and its limit using a finite partition. For t n = n1{0 < U ≤ 1/n}, compute its almost-sure limit, expectation and tail expectation for n > K. Identify the valid convergence theorem and invalid interchange in these two cases. Prove that if Σ E[|Y i |] < ∞, then ΣY i converges absolutely almost surely and its expectation is ΣE[Y i ]. Identify the common integrable dominator. Then show why E[|X n |] ≤ 1 alone does not imply convergence of expectations when X n → 0 almost surely. Four states have probabilities (1/8, 3/8, 1/4, 1/4) and dollar outcomes (−2, 2, 4, 8). Information G groups states {1, 2} and {3, 4}. Compute E[X | G], E[X], Var(X), residual MSE and Var(E[X | G]). Verify both event identities, total variance and the tower to the trivial information set. Two independent fair signs ε 1 , ε 2 are observed sequentially. A terminal payoff is X = 3 + 2ε 1 + ε 1 ε 2 . Derive M n = E[X | F n ] for n = 0, 1, 2 and verify the martingale property. Stop at 1 if ε 1 = 1 and at 2 otherwise. Enumerate stopped payoffs and verify bounded optional stopping. Why is choosing the better of M 1 and M 2 after seeing both not the same allowed rule? For Worked example 2, compute E[|M T∧N |] and the absolute tail expectation when 1 < K < 2 N − 1. Verify the process is not uniformly integrable, even though T is finite almost surely. State precisely which step of the bounded stopping proof cannot be extended by taking an unqualified limit. An iid positive law has density f(x) = (3/2)x −5/2 for x ≥ 1. Compute its mean, second moment and P(X > i). For Z i = X i 1{X i ≤ i}, compute E[Z i ] and E[Z i ²], verify the two required summability conditions, and explain each step that gives the almost-sure sample-mean limit. Does the proof establish a normal root-n error law? Complete solutions and evaluation criteria The sigma-algebra is {∅, {1, 3}, {2, 4}, Ω}. X is constant on each cell and therefore measurable: each Borel preimage is a union of cells. Y is not, because the preimage of {0} is {1}, which is unavailable in this sigma-algebra. This is a statement about observable distinctions; no probability assignment is needed. Each singleton is Borel and has Lebesgue measure zero. The rationals in [0, 1] are countable, so their union is measurable and has measure zero by countable additivity. Thus R is measurable and E[R] = 0. Every interval of positive length contains both rational and irrational numbers, so every partition's lower Riemann sum is zero and upper sum is one. Their gap cannot vanish, so no Riemann integral exists. Density is topological proximity, not probability mass. An event can be dense and null without contradiction. Except at the probability-zero endpoint U = 1, the 2 n cells each have length 2 −n and s n values j/2 n for j = 0, …, 2 n − 1. Its expectation is [2 n (2 n − 1)/2]/2 2n = (1 − 2 −n )/2, increasing to 1/2 = E[U]. Both monotone convergence and domination by 1 justify the limit. The spikes t n converge to zero for every U, including the excluded endpoint zero, but E[t n ] = n(1/n) = 1. For n > K their tail expectation is also 1. They are not uniformly integrable and have no integrable common dominator. Almost-sure convergence alone does not permit the expectation interchange. By nonnegative countable interchange, E[Σ|Y i |] = ΣE[|Y i |] < ∞. Hence G = Σ|Y i | is finite almost surely, proving absolute convergence. Every signed partial sum has absolute value at most G. Dominated convergence gives E[ΣY i ] = lim n Σ i≤n E[Y i ] = ΣE[Y i ]. No independence was used. The spikes in Exercise 3 have E[|t n |] = 1 and almost-sure limit zero, while their expectations stay one. A bound on each integral does not supply a common dominating variable. Both cells have mass 1/2. Their unnormalized X integrals are (−2)/8 + 3(2)/8 = 1/2 and 4/4 + 8/4 = 3. Therefore m is (1, 1, 6, 6), E[m1 C 1 ] = 1/2 and E[m1 C 2 ] = 3, matching X. E[X] = 7/2 dollars and E[X²] = 4/8 + 3(4)/8 + 16/4 + 64/4 = 22 dollars squared. Var(X) = 22 − 49/4 = 39/4. Residuals are −3, 1, −2, 2, giving MSE 9/8 + 3/8 + 4/4 + 4/4 = 7/2. E[m²] = (1/2)1 + (1/2)36 = 37/2 and Var(m) = 37/2 − 49/4 = 25/4. Thus 39/4 = 7/2 + 25/4. Averaging m gives 7/2, the conditional expectation under the trivial sigma-algebra, verifying the tower. F 0 is trivial, so M 0 = 3. F 1 records ε 1 , hence M 1 = 3 + 2ε 1 because E[ε 2 | F 1 ] = 0. M 2 = X. Conditional averaging of ε 1 gives E[M 1 ] = 3; conditional averaging of ε 2 gives E[M 2 | F 1 ] = M 1 . The stopping event {T ≤ 1} = {ε 1 = 1} is F 1 -measurable. With probability 1/2, stopped payoff is 5; otherwise the terminal outcomes are 0 or 2, each with probability 1/4. Expected stopped payoff is 5/2 + 0 + 2/4 = 3. A hindsight rule stopping at 1 whenever M 1 ≥ M 2 depends on ε 2 , which is unavailable at time 1; its stop event need not lie in F 1 . Indeed E[max(M 1 , M 2 )] = 3.5, a hindsight selection value rather than this bounded stopping policy. The absolute expectation is (1 − 2 −N ) + (2 N − 1)2 −N = 2(1 − 2 −N ). For 1 < K < 2 N − 1, only the all-loss path is in the absolute tail, giving 1 − 2 −N . For every fixed K > 1, taking N large makes this arbitrarily close to 1, so the supremum tail does not tend to zero as K grows. Although M T∧N → M T = 1 almost surely, E[M T∧N ] = 0 does not converge to E[M T ]. The finite sum of zero-mean predictable increments proves each bounded-N identity. Passing from those identities to an infinite stopped horizon needs a limit/interchange hypothesis absent here. Integration gives total mass 1, mean (3/2)∫ 1 ∞ x −3/2 dx = 3, and second moment (3/2)∫ 1 ∞ x −1/2 dx = ∞. For integers i ≥ 1, P(X > i) = i −3/2 , E[Z i ] = 3(1 − i −1/2 ), and E[Z i ²] = 3(√i − 1). The tail-probability sum is finite by comparison with ∫ 1 ∞ t −3/2 dt; specifically it is at most 3. Weighted second moments satisfy ΣE[Z i ²]/i² ≤ 3Σi −3/2 < ∞. Borel–Cantelli gives eventual equality of X i and Z i . The independent centered series Σ(Z i − E[Z i ])/i converges almost surely by the maximal-tail argument. Kronecker's proved identity turns this into vanishing centered averages. The expectations converge to 3, so their averages do too, and finite discarded prefixes vanish after division by n. Thus X̄ n → 3 almost surely. Infinite variance prevents use of the classical finite-variance normal root-n theorem; the strong-law proof supplies no replacement distribution or rate. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain a sigma-algebra as observable distinctions. Construct the expectation of a nonnegative variable from simple functions; prove one integration limit and give a counterexample when its hypothesis is removed. Explain why conditional expectation is a random variable constrained by information. Delayed check: after a week, reconstruct the projection/truncation existence argument, tower identity, bounded-stopping proof and index-truncation strong law. In the last proof, identify exactly where integrability, independence, identical distributions and countable convergence enter. Distinguish an infinite-variance law of large numbers from a finite-variance central limit theorem. The Volume 3 assessment and project package combines these tools in cumulative exams and a reproducible uncertainty/decision laboratory. The next volume begins with reproducible work and data provenance , turning these probability, inference and decision tools into practical analysis; later econometrics and stochastic finance deepen dependence, asymptotic inference, martingale convergence and continuous-time models."}, {"title": "04.01 · Reproducible work and data provenance", "url": "v04-c01.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A result includes its route from evidence to calculation An analyst reports that real spending grew 17.65% between January and March. A colleague repeats the arithmetic and obtains 10%. Neither necessarily made an arithmetic mistake: they may have used different releases of the same months. A third analyst obtains a plausible number from the wrong units. A fourth can reproduce a number exactly from a biased sample. Reliable analysis requires preserving what was observed, what was available when, what each quantity means and how the calculation was performed. In this volume, computational reproduction means obtaining the specified results from the supplied inputs and procedure in a documented environment. Replication with new evidence investigates the claim using additional or independently collected evidence. Robustness analysis changes defensible analytical choices to see which conclusions survive. Terminology varies across fields; state your convention. An exactly reproducible error remains an error. A successful rerun does not establish representativeness, an economic mechanism or causal identification. The immediate objective is a small package another analyst can run without asking which spreadsheet tab or forgotten intermediate file you used. Later chapters teach programming, databases and model evaluation. Here you learn to specify and inspect a workflow before its complexity makes mistakes difficult to see. All spending observations below are original synthetic teaching data, with invented release dates. Define the object before choosing a file format A dataset has an observational unit or grain : one row might represent a firm-year, a household interview, a transaction or a particular release of a monthly series. A key is a field or combination of fields intended to identify a row uniquely. An identifier is not automatically a key. A release history can contain several rows for one month, so month alone is not unique. Its key may be the pair (reference month, availability date). A data dictionary explains fields sufficiently to interpret and validate them. A column headed value with no unit or population is incomplete evidence. Record the quantity, type, permissible values, unit and multiplier, population/geography, time coverage and frequency, adjustment or transformation, missing-value convention and key role. Dataset-level metadata records source identity, version or vintage, retrieval details, access and reuse conditions. Keep these with the files rather than relying on memory. Dictionary for the supplied spending-release history Field Meaning and valid values Interpretive consequence period Reference month, YYYY-MM; January–March 2025 Monthly spending is a flow, not an end-month stock or annualized rate. available_on Date YYYY-MM-DD, interpreted after that day's joint release The month being measured differs from when its value becomes known. nominal_spending Nonnegative integer USD, no thousands multiplier Amounts are at each month's current prices. price_index Positive integer index, common fixed base equal to 100 Deflate each month's spending by its own index/100. Row key Unique ( period , available_on ) Multiple vintages are allowed; duplicate observations at the same release are rejected. The case describes one synthetic economy with no seasonal adjustment and no missing values. These are assumptions of the case, not properties inferred from the numbers. An index's base value is dimensionless; real spending is expressed in the common base-price dollars. A blank, a suppressed value, zero and “not yet released” are different states. Record them separately where relevant. Do not fill a missing price index with zero to satisfy a numeric type: division becomes undefined and the missingness has been concealed. A schema makes some dictionary requirements executable: field names and types, positive indexes, known units, unique keys and required coverage. Range checks identify impossible values under a specification, not every economically implausible value. A spending figure accidentally multiplied by 1,000 can pass positivity and uniqueness checks. Compare scale with the source definition and independent totals. Validation rules themselves must suit the quantity: negative net exports can be meaningful even though negative gross spending is excluded in our case. Keep inputs, transformations and reports distinct A useful workflow is preserved input → validation → dated selection → transformations → tables and figures . Each arrow should correspond to an explicit operation. Treat acquired source files as immutable for the reported analysis. If a source issues a correction, keep a separate version and identify which results use it. Preserve an erroneous original when lawful and useful for the audit; make the correction in a documented transformation rather than silently editing history. study/ README.txt purpose, inputs, environment, commands, expected results raw/ preserved authorized source files src/ programs and declared analysis choices derived/ reproducible cleaned/selected inputs output/ reproducible tables, figures and reports manifest.json release identifiers and input fingerprints This layout is a convention, not a requirement. Our tiny download places its files together. The important properties are ownership, clear direction and enough information to rebuild outputs. Keep intermediate files only when they save material time or preserve an otherwise irretrievable step, and identify how they were created. An analysis that starts from an undocumented “cleaned-final2” file has hidden inputs even if the last regression script runs successfully. Paths locate files. An absolute path such as a researcher's personal desktop identifies one computer; a relative path locates a file from a specified base directory. Document that base. The supplied program resolves its default input beside the program, so changing the terminal's working directory does not silently select another input. Use stable filenames, explicit UTF-8 text encoding and unambiguous ISO dates. In larger datasets, distinguish the timezone of an event from that of its recorded timestamp. A date alone is insufficient for a claim about information available before a same-day market opening. An environment consists of the interpreter/application, relevant versions, dependencies and settings affecting execution. Record the precise runtime used and required external packages, if any. Spreadsheet application/version, calculation mode, date conventions and locale can matter too. A package list does not preserve an unavailable dataset; a frozen dataset does not preserve an unavailable program. Our laboratory uses only Python's standard library, prints the runtime version and has no random sampling. Later simulation work must also specify generator, seed and ordering. A seed alone is not a full environment. Reproduction has levels. Identical output bytes are a strict requirement appropriate for some deterministic artifacts. Equivalent numbers within a declared tolerance may be more appropriate across floating-point platforms. A runtime timestamp may legitimately differ without changing the result. State which outputs must match, their units, rounding rule and tolerance. For the rational calculations below, exact fractions provide an especially clear comparison; decimal display rounding is not part of the underlying computation. Write a README that lets someone finish the job A README is the entry point for a reader who did not participate in the analysis. It should identify the question and claimed outputs, enumerate supplied and separately acquired inputs, explain their origins and dictionaries, state environment requirements, give the ordered commands, map outputs to the reported results, provide expected values or checks, and disclose known limitations. Estimate material runtime or storage requirements when relevant. Write commands a recipient can actually execute, including where to run them and which parameters must be chosen. “Run the scripts in order” is incomplete if the order is not listed. “Download the latest data” specifies a changing analysis, not a fixed historical result. “The result should look sensible” supplies no test. For this case, the README names two explicit cutoffs and gives both exact output totals and growth fractions. Its default command reads the frozen JSON beside the script and prints a report without altering the input. The reader can save output under a new name. As a professional reference point, the AEA Data Editor's FAQ discusses checking submitted code against reported results, software/data accessibility and restricted-data arrangements. It also distinguishes a working repository from an archive. Those publication procedures motivate careful documentation; this chapter's original teaching workflow is not a claim that the library has passed an AEA review. Information has a date as well as a reference period Let x t,v denote an observation for reference period t, released at availability date v. For a cutoff c, the usable version is the row with the greatest v satisfying v ≤ c, among rows for t. If none exists, the observation was unavailable at c. This is an information-set rule. It does not imply that the latest eligible estimate equals the eventual revised quantity or its true economic value. v*(t,c) = max{v : a release for period t exists and v ≤ c}; x t (c) = x t,v*(t,c) . Always define what happens when the set is empty. One task may permit a released-to-date partial series; another requires a complete three-month total and must stop when a month is unavailable. Dropping missing months silently changes the target. The supplied program requires all three months. A cutoff of 1 April therefore fails because March's first release is dated 15 April. Zero is not a substitute for March's unobserved spending. In real work, the availability date of a price index may differ from that of spending. Select each input using its own release history before combining them. A historical forecast also requires the relevant parameter estimates, classifications, constituents and transformations to have been constructible at its origin. Selecting current surviving firms or using a subsequently revised seasonal adjustment can introduce information that an analyst did not possess then. The appropriate vintage depends on the question: evaluating a final estimate may deliberately use revised outcomes, while reconstructing an earlier decision requires its original information set. The St. Louis Fed's real-time-period documentation distinguishes information currently available about the past from information available at a historical date. It describes inclusive real-time interval boundaries and the usual current-date default. A current retrieval alone is therefore not evidence that the retrieved values were known at an earlier forecast origin. Our small joint-release fixture illustrates the distinction without claiming to reproduce a FRED series or its API. Worked example 1 — two valid snapshots, two different answers Monthly nominal spending N and price index P are jointly released as follows. The first three rows form the available history on 20 April; the last three revise those same reference months on 15 May. Original synthetic release history Month Available after N, USD P, base=100 Real spending 100N/P January 15 February 100,000 100 100,000 February 15 March 121,000 110 110,000 March 15 April 132,000 120 110,000 January 15 May 102,000 100 102,000 February 15 May 120,000 108 1,000,000/9 ≈ 111,111.11 March 15 May 138,000 115 120,000 At c = 20 April, sum the three eligible nominal flows: 100,000 + 121,000 + 132,000 = 353,000 USD. Deflate separately, giving 320,000 base-price USD in total. January-to-March real growth is 110,000/100,000 − 1 = 10%; nominal growth is 132,000/100,000 − 1 = 32%. These are endpoint changes, not annualized rates and not the percentage change in a quarterly total. At c = 20 May, select the latest eligible row for each month, not all eligible rows. Nominal total is 360,000 USD; real total is 102,000 + 1,000,000/9 + 120,000 = 2,998,000/9 ≈ 333,111.11 base-price USD. Real endpoint growth is 120,000/102,000 − 1 = 3/17 ≈ 17.6471%; nominal growth is 138,000/102,000 − 1 = 6/17 ≈ 35.2941%. Using May's revisions in an April information set would be a timing error even though the calculations are correct for May. Do not deflate April's total using the unweighted mean index: 353,000/(110/100) ≈ 320,909.09, not 320,000. Generally Σ N t /p t differs from (Σ N t )/p̄. A specially constructed aggregate deflator could reconcile them; the unweighted mean need not. Also, add only flows across compatible nonoverlapping periods. Adding three end-month balance-sheet stocks would answer a different and usually inappropriate question. Version control records changes; provenance explains them Version control preserves named states of selected files and their change history. In Git, the working tree contains editable files, the staging area selects the next recorded content, and a commit records that staged state with metadata and a message. Review both changes not yet staged and the staged changes. A useful message explains an analytical change, such as “select releases available by forecast cutoff,” rather than merely “final.” git init git status git add README.txt src/analyze.py manifest.json git diff --staged git commit -m \"Record documented vintage-selection analysis\" git log --oneline These illustrative commands belong in a learner's own study directory, after the named files exist and Git author identity is configured. They are not necessary to run this chapter's download. Make each commit coherent enough to review. Record the commit used for a reported analysis and whether uncommitted changes existed. A clean tracked tree does not prove that every required file was tracked or that inputs were authorized. A repository on one laptop is also not a backup strategy. The official Git book's recording-changes section explains staging and reviewing changes. In particular, staging captures a file's state at that moment. Editing it afterward does not automatically restage the new content. Ignore patterns help keep generated files out of routine staging; they do not retroactively erase tracked content. Inspect the intended files before recording or sharing them. A manifest associates a release with filenames, source identifiers and fingerprints. A SHA-256 digest is a compact fingerprint calculated from file bytes. Comparing a file with a separately preserved expected digest is a strong practical identity check. Reformatting the same observations changes the bytes and usually the digest; numerical agreement and byte identity are different tests. A malicious or mistaken source can supply internally consistent wrong data and a matching digest. Establish source identity and permissions separately. The laboratory computes a SHA-256 hexadecimal fingerprint using Python's documented hashlib interface . It fingerprints the bytes actually read, before decoding JSON. It does not claim a signed chain of custody or prove the truth of the observations. The separately supplied manifest identifies the intended frozen teaching input. Worked example 2 — audit a package that “runs successfully” A colleague filters the history to all rows available by 20 May and sums spending. The program returns 713,000 USD with no error. That is 353,000 + 360,000: it has counted both releases of each month. The eligible set contains six rows, but the required snapshot contains three distinct reference months. Repair the operation by grouping on month and choosing its greatest eligible availability date. Do not solve the problem by deleting every repeated month before considering the cutoff; that may retain the wrong version. Now duplicate the first January release. A “choose the latest row” routine might hide this duplication depending on its implementation. The dictionary says (period, availability date) is unique, so validate that key before selection and reject the altered input. A validation failure here reveals a data-integrity problem that an apparently sensible total might conceal. Finally, randomly reorder the six valid rows. The correct April and May analytical results must remain unchanged because selection uses explicit dates and output ordering uses reference months. The input fingerprint will change because its bytes changed. This pair of checks distinguishes a harmless storage-order change from a calculation that accidentally treats file order as time. A useful audit asks both “did these exact inputs run?” and “did the operations implement the intended definition?” Access, permissions and a usable release Access to a file, permission to analyze it and permission to redistribute it are distinct. Preserve the applicable license or access agreement reference, its version/date, required citation, permitted uses and redistribution conditions. For public inputs, provide the permitted frozen extract or precise retrieval route. For restricted inputs, document the authorized access procedure and retain the analysis package in an appropriate controlled location. Do not publish protected records or credentials merely to make an example easier to run. A synthetic substitute can demonstrate code behavior, but label that it does not reproduce the confidential empirical estimate. The AEA FAQ explicitly treats licenses as chosen for particular deposits and separates authorized restricted sharing from public release. Its discussion is a useful reminder to inspect the actual dataset's terms. A repository's host name is not a blanket permission for every file it hosts. Prepare a release with the required inputs or honest access route, executable code, documentation, dictionary, manifest and expected outputs. Ask a recipient to start from a clean directory rather than a notebook session containing hidden variables. Have them identify the result they reproduced, their input version, runtime and any deviations. A failure report is evidence to improve the package, not something to remove from the record. A successful clean rerun closes the computational question only; causal and economic arguments need their own review. Run the frozen-input laboratory Download the program , release history and dictionary , input manifest and README into one folder. With Python 3.10 or newer available, run the following from that folder. No packages, credentials or network connection are required. python3 v04-c01-provenance.py --cutoff 2025-04-20 python3 v04-c01-provenance.py --cutoff 2025-05-20 The report shows the selected rows, exact fractions and display decimals, cutoff, input fingerprint and precise runtime. Compare its totals to Worked example 1. The program checks required columns, units, dates, key uniqueness and positivity before selection. In this completed-month case it also rejects a release before the reference month ends; a partial-month estimate or forecast would require a different, labeled contract. A cutoff of 1 April rejects the missing required month. Its finite teaching schema deliberately accepts integer-valued spending/index inputs only; adapting it to actual decimal data requires an explicit new schema, not silently coercing everything to integers. Syntax and program construction are taught in Chapter 3 ; you can audit these results by hand now. Exercises A file has columns country , year and debt . Two rows have the same country/year and values 2.4 and 2,400. Specify the minimum metadata needed before judging a duplicate or an inconsistency. Then state which field combination could be a key if the rows are different releases. Using Worked example 1, select the January–March snapshot at the end of 14 May and at the end of 15 May. Compute each nominal total and real endpoint growth. Explain why a complete-quarter request on 1 April must fail under the supplied specification. An analyst sums all eligible releases on 20 May. Compute the resulting nominal total, its excess above the correct snapshot and the percentage overstatement relative to the correct total. Specify a repair that does not depend on file order. For the original April snapshot, calculate the effective single deflator p* that makes (Σ N)/p* equal the sum of separately deflated spending. Express it both as a price relative and as an index with base 100. Explain why an unweighted mean index fails here. You stage a script, edit its cutoff from 20 April to 20 May, run it, and commit without staging again. Which version does the commit contain? Design a short audit to determine whether a reported May result corresponds to the recorded code and input. A collaborator changes whitespace and row order in the JSON. The SHA-256 digest differs but both dated analytical results agree. Another collaborator changes every nominal spending value by a factor of 1,000 without changing its USD label. Which checks should pass or fail? How do nominal totals, real totals and endpoint growth rates change in the second case? A package says only “Python; download latest spending; run analysis.” It contains a figure but no expected table and reads an absolute desktop path. Rewrite its essential reproduction instructions. You may use this chapter's supplied filenames and outputs; include the information needed to distinguish exact numerical agreement from environment metadata differences. A restricted dataset produces an estimated policy effect, but its terms prohibit public redistribution. A team publishes synthetic rows under the original dataset's name and says the estimate is independently reproduced because the code runs. Diagnose the claim. Specify an honest computational access plan and two additional questions required before calling the effect causal. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria Require the debt concept (gross/net and institutional coverage), stock date versus flow period, currency, multiplier, current/constant prices if relevant, population/geography, source/series identifier, release/vintage, missing conventions and any transformations. The numbers might represent trillions versus billions, different sectors or different releases; the bare file cannot decide. If these are releases of the same concept and units, (country, year, availability date) may be unique. If several series are mixed, include series identity too. A correct response refuses to delete a row until the grain and key are established. Through 14 May, the latest eligible rows are the original January, February and March releases: total 353,000 USD and real growth 10%. At the end of 15 May, all three revisions are eligible: total 360,000 USD and real growth 3/17 ≈ 17.6471%. Boundary inclusion is part of the date convention. On 1 April no March observation has been released, so a three-month total is unavailable. A separately labeled January–February total could be computed under a different request, but zero-filling March or silently dropping it would not answer this request. The incorrect total is 713,000 USD; excess is 353,000 USD. Relative overstatement is 353,000/360,000 = 353/360 ≈ 98.0556%. First validate unique (period, availability date), retain only releases with date ≤ 20 May, and then choose the greatest eligible date separately for each period. Verify the selected periods equal the declared three-month set. This produces 360,000 irrespective of row order. p* = 353,000/320,000 = 353/320 = 1.103125, so its index is 110.3125. The arithmetic mean index is 110 and produces 320,909.09 rather than 320,000 base-price USD. Algebraically, p* = (Σ N)/(Σ N/p), a nominal-spending-weighted harmonic price relative when N > 0. It is constructed for this aggregation, not generally the simple average of price indexes or an official aggregate price statistic. If all N are zero, this ratio is undefined even though both spending totals are zero. The commit records the earlier staged April script; the May edit remains in the working tree unless separately staged. Inspect git status , the unstaged difference and the committed file, then record the actual command/arguments, frozen input digest and selected rows for the reported run. A command-line cutoff may be part of the run specification rather than source code, so preserve it as well. Reproduce the claimed result from the committed package in a clean folder. Before recording a corrected coherent state, review and stage the intended script and documentation; a commit message alone cannot identify the executed content. In the first case, byte identity fails as expected; schema, unique-key and analytical-invariance checks should pass. The new file is not byte-identical to the manifest input, so preserve the original for the frozen release and document any reformatted copy. In the second case positivity and key checks can still pass, but source-unit reconciliation and frozen-input identity fail. April nominal/real totals become 353,000,000 and 320,000,000; May totals become 360,000,000 and 2,998,000,000/9. Both nominal and real endpoint growth rates are unchanged because the common factor cancels in each ratio. Plausible growth alone cannot detect this level error. State the question (dated January–March synthetic spending summary), supplied JSON/dictionary/manifest, original synthetic provenance and permission, Python 3.10+ standard-library requirement and exact runtime used. Keep the companion script and input together and give the two commands printed above with explicit cutoffs. Name selected rows, nominal/real totals and growth fractions as expected outputs; give the supplied input SHA-256 route. Resolve input relative to the script, explain how to save output under a new filename, and document missing-month rejection. Compare exact fractions and selected observations; allow the separately reported runtime field to differ when checking another supported environment. A good alternative includes every element, even with another clear folder layout. Running on synthetic substitutes demonstrates execution and perhaps schema compatibility, not reproduction of the confidential estimate. Label the substitute and its limitations. Provide public code, dictionary, synthetic demonstration and instructions for eligible researchers to obtain authorized access; preserve the confidential snapshot and result audit within permitted controls. An authorized reviewer can record whether the real-data procedure reproduces the claimed number without publishing protected records. Causal review must separately examine a stated identification assumption (for example why treatment selection is unconfounded or an assignment mechanism is credible) and threats such as measurement changes, spillovers or post-treatment controls. Alternative causal questions earn credit if they specify a mechanism capable of changing the conclusion. Computational agreement alone answers none of them. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the difference between reference month, availability date and retrieval date. Use the six-row example to show why “all data available by May” still needs a per-month selection rule. Explain one error a checksum can expose and one it cannot. Delayed check: after a week, reconstruct the April and May calculations from the dictionary, identify the unique release key, and run the package from a clean directory. Change a release date in a separately named copy and predict exactly which cutoff results change before running it. Give an example of reproducible analysis with invalid causal interpretation. The next chapter, spreadsheets and financial models , applies the same discipline to cell formulas, cash-flow dates, reconciliation and sensitivity. Provenance is part of the model's meaning, not paperwork appended after the result."}, {"title": "04.02 · Spreadsheets and financial models", "url": "v04-c02.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A model is an argument that can be inspected A manager receives a spreadsheet recommending a machine purchase. The final cell is positive, but one formula discounts today's payment as though it occurs next year, another drops a year with no receipts, and the cash check compares a calculated total with itself. The output is precise without being dependable. An auditable model lets a second person trace each conclusion through formulas, timing and inputs, and identify which assumptions would change the decision. Capability: build auditable spreadsheet calculations and detect hidden modeling errors. The prerequisite is reproducible work and data provenance : preserve original inputs, distinguish observations from assumptions, and record transformations. Familiarity with arithmetic, percentages and discounted cash flows from the earlier volumes is assumed; the spreadsheet notation is introduced here. All businesses, dollars, transactions and forecasts below are synthetic teaching examples, not observed market data. A workbook contains worksheets. A cell has an address: C5 means column C, row 5. It may hold a number, text, or a formula. A formula begins with = ; =C3*C4 multiplies two cells, while =SUM(C3:C5) adds the inclusive range C3 through C5. The displayed result can differ from the stored number: formatting 0.1 as 10% changes its appearance, not its magnitude. Typing 10 into a cell expected to hold 0.1 creates a different assumption. Separate the model into inputs, calculations, outputs and checks. Give every input a label, unit, timing convention and origin. Record why a forecast is plausible, who supplied it, and when it was frozen. Put an assumption in one authoritative cell and reference it wherever needed. Constants such as zero or the number one in an algebraic identity are legitimate; an unexplained 1.07 hidden in a forecast formula is an undocumented 7% assumption. Formula protection and color may help navigation, but neither verifies the economics, and meaning must remain clear without color. References encode the dependency you intend A copied formula shifts a relative reference with its destination. An absolute reference fixes both coordinates with dollar signs. A mixed reference fixes only the coordinate preceded by a dollar sign. Copying two columns right and three rows down gives this mapping: Original reference Copied reference What remains fixed B5 D8 Neither coordinate $B$5 $B$5 Column and row B$5 D$5 Row 5 $B5 $B8 Column B The copy rules follow Microsoft's reference documentation . The dollar sign controls copying; it does not make a value immutable. Editing the referenced input still changes dependent results. Suppose quantities occupy C13:E13 and a common price is in B3. Entering =C13*$B$3 in C15 and copying across produces =D13*$B$3 and =E13*$B$3 . The quantity follows the year; the price remains common. Fixing everything would repeat the first year's quantity. Fixing nothing would make the price reference drift. Decide the intended economic relationship before choosing the dollar signs. Use parentheses to express the calculation clearly: =Units*(Price-UnitCost)-FixedCost is contribution less fixed cash cost. Compute components on separate rows when that makes inspection easier. A blank, an unknown value and a measured zero have different meanings. Do not silently convert missing assumptions to zero. Check that inputs are numeric and within their defined domain; investigate error values rather than covering every error with a zero-returning fallback. Unintended circular references, where a formula depends on its own result, require redesign. This chapter uses an acyclic sequence of calculations, with no iterative spreadsheet settings. Draw the cash-flow timeline before writing formulas Let C t be incremental net cash received at date t: receipts are positive and payments negative. With equally spaced annual dates, a constant effective annual discount rate r > −1, and t = 0 today, define NPV = C 0 + Σ t=1 T C t /(1 + r) t . Net present value expresses the specified cash stream in today's dollars. This calculation is conditional on the cash forecast, the discount rate and the boundary of the project. A positive number does not verify any of those inputs. Receipts and payments must be incremental to the chosen comparison, with a common currency and price convention. Nominal cash forecasts need a compatible nominal rate; real purchasing-power forecasts need a compatible real rate. Financing and taxes require additional explicit modeling if they are relevant to the decision. Match the time unit in the exponent to the rate. An effective annual rate r a has an equivalent monthly rate r m satisfying (1 + r m ) 12 = 1 + r a ; it is generally not r a /12. At r = 0 the formula becomes the undiscounted sum. Rates at or below −100% are outside this discount-factor model. For irregular dates, write and justify an explicit year-fraction convention and discount using those fractions; an equally spaced annual formula does not become appropriate merely because its columns have dates. Excel's NPV documentation defines its listed values as equally spaced end-of-period cash flows beginning one period after the valuation date. Therefore add the time-zero flow separately. For a range, numeric zero preserves a zero-cash period; a blank or text entry may be ignored, changing the sequence represented by the calculation. Keep a numeric entry for every period and check it. The formulas here use English function names and comma argument separators. Worked example 1: from operating assumptions to an auditable investment model A small workshop buys equipment for 10,000 dollars now and commits 2,000 dollars of working capital now. Here working capital means cash tied up in operating resources, unavailable for distribution while the project runs. Assume it is fully released at the end of year 3. The equipment sells for 1,000 then. Annual sales are 1,000, 1,200 and 1,400 units; price is 12 dollars per unit, variable cash cost 7 per unit, and fixed cash cost 1,500 per year. All operating receipts and payments occur at year end. There are no taxes, financing flows, unpaid sales, later equipment purchases or other terminal claims in this teaching boundary. The discount rate is an assumed 10% effective annual rate. Operating cash is quantity × (price − variable cost) − fixed cost. Thus year 1 gives 1,000(12 − 7) − 1,500 = 3,500 dollars; years 2 and 3 give 4,500 and 5,500. Working-capital release and equipment disposal are separate from operating cash so their timing and possible losses remain visible. Cash item, dollars Now: t = 0 Year 1 end Year 2 end Year 3 end Operating cash 0 3,500 4,500 5,500 Equipment purchase −10,000 0 0 0 Working-capital cash flow −2,000 0 0 2,000 Equipment disposal 0 0 0 1,000 Net cash C t −12,000 3,500 4,500 8,500 Present value −12,000.00 3,181.82 3,719.01 6,386.18 Keeping full precision until display, NPV = −12,000 + 3,500/1.1 + 4,500/1.1² + 8,500/1.1³ = 1,287.002254 dollars , displayed as 1,287.00. Summing the individually rounded present values gives 1,287.01, illustrating why display rounding should not become an accidental intermediate calculation rule. If actual contractual payments are rounded to cents, model that payment rule explicitly instead. Create one worksheet with the following input cells. The quantity multiplier scales all three sales quantities and equals one in the base case; it will support sensitivity analysis. Cell Input label Value Unit B2 Annual discount rate 0.10 Effective rate per year B3 Selling price 12 Dollars per unit B4 Variable cash cost 7 Dollars per unit B5 Fixed cash cost 1,500 Dollars per year B6 Equipment purchase 10,000 Dollars paid at t = 0 B7 Working capital 2,000 Dollars paid at t = 0 and recovered at t = 3 B8 Equipment disposal 1,000 Dollars received at t = 3 B9 Quantity multiplier 1 Dimensionless Put row labels in column A. Enter the following cell map; a stated zero means a numeric zero, not a blank. The two output cells B26 and B27 use different discount implementations on the same declared cash flows. Row / label Column B: time zero Columns C:E: years 1–3 12 / Year index 0 1 , 2 , 3 13 / Base units 0 1000 , 1200 , 1400 14 / Scenario units 0 C14: =C13*$B$9 ; copy across 15 / Revenue 0 C15: =C14*$B$3 ; copy across 16 / Variable cash payments 0 C16: =C14*$B$4 ; copy across 17 / Fixed cash payments 0 C17: =$B$5 ; copy across 18 / Operating cash 0 C18: =C15-C16-C17 ; copy across 19 / Equipment purchase =-$B$6 All three cells: 0 20 / Working-capital cash flow =-$B$7 C20 and D20: 0 ; E20: =$B$7 21 / Equipment disposal 0 C21 and D21: 0 ; E21: =$B$8 22 / Net cash =SUM(B18:B21) Copy B22 across through E22 23 / Discount factor =1/(1+$B$2)^B12 Copy B23 across through E23 24 / Present value =B22*B23 Copy B24 across through E24 26 / NPV from timeline =SUM(B24:E24) Leave unused 27 / NPV from function =B22+NPV($B$2,C22:E22) Leave unused 28 / Discount cross-check =B26-B27 Expect absolute residual below 0.000001 dollar 29 / Working-capital net cash =SUM(B20:E20) Expect zero only under the full-recovery assumption The tempting formula =NPV($B$2,B22:E22) shifts the whole timeline one period later. It returns 1,170.002049, equal to the correct NPV divided by 1.1. Its plausible positive result does not reveal the error. Conversely, a zero in B28 checks two discount implementations but cannot detect a cash flow omitted from both. The zero in B29 describes the assumed undiscounted release of working capital; its present-value contribution is negative because recovery occurs later. Reconciliation needs an independently established target A reconciliation explains how a starting balance and recorded movements lead to an ending balance, then compares that result with another source. If receipts R and payments P are positive magnitudes, predicted closing cash is opening cash + R − P. Define the residual as independently observed closing cash minus predicted closing cash. This sign convention makes a negative residual mean that the model predicts too much cash. The residual locates a discrepancy; it does not, by itself, identify its cause. Worked example 2: a missing payment and a check that can still pass Opening cash is 2,500 dollars. Recorded customer receipts are 8,200, supplier payments 5,400, wages 2,100 and equipment payments 1,800. These categories contain disjoint transactions, all in the same currency and period. The model predicts 2,500 + 8,200 − 5,400 − 2,100 − 1,800 = 1,400 dollars. The separately obtained bank closing balance is 1,175, giving residual 1,175 − 1,400 = −225 dollars . Inspection finds a cleared 225-dollar supplier payment absent from the ledger. Add that identified transaction to supplier payments, bringing them to 5,625 and total payments to 9,525. Revised closing cash is 2,500 + 8,200 − 9,525 = 1,175, so the residual is zero. Preserve the original record and the correction with its transaction identifier. Do not insert an unexplained 225-dollar balancing item merely to turn a check green. Now suppose a separate import wrongly adds 500 to both receipts and supplier payments. The closing-cash residual remains zero because the errors cancel. Receipts become 8,700 and supplier payments 6,125, but 2,500 + 8,700 − 6,125 − 2,100 − 1,800 still equals 1,175. Reconcile gross category totals and underlying transaction identifiers as well as the net balance. A unique identifier can reveal a repeated transaction; an independently sourced transaction listing can reveal an omission. Neither a cash identity nor one total proves completeness and classification. Real bank and ledger balances may differ because their cutoffs or definitions differ. List supported timing items, such as a payment recorded internally but not yet cleared, and specify which balance each adjustment explains. Do not treat every difference as an accounting error. A check built from two copies of the same formula has no independent target: it can only confirm that the copies agree. Sensitivity tables expose assumptions without inventing probabilities A sensitivity analysis changes a specified input while keeping the others fixed. A scenario can change several economically related inputs together. Neither assigns probabilities unless a separate probability model supports them. For the workshop, scale all base quantities by s while keeping price, unit cost, fixed cost and terminal receipts unchanged. The resulting NPV is V(s, r) = −12,000 + (5,000s − 1,500)/(1 + r) + (6,000s − 1,500)/(1 + r)² + (7,000s + 1,500)/(1 + r)³. The final constant 1,500 combines −1,500 fixed cost with 3,000 of terminal receipts. Keep those components separate in the main model even when simplifying the equation for analysis. The table reports dollars, computed at full precision and rounded only for display. Quantity multiplier s r = 5% r = 10% r = 15% 0.8 −492.60 −1,665.66 −2,662.45 1.0 2,757.59 1,287.00 35.01 1.2 6,007.77 4,239.67 2,732.47 To build a transparent grid on the same sheet, put rates 0.05, 0.10 and 0.15 in H2:J2, and multipliers 0.8, 1 and 1.2 in G3:G5. Enter the following formula in H3, then copy across to J3 and down through row 5. The mixed references $G3 and H$2 select the row's quantity assumption and the column's rate. All operating input and year references stay fixed. =-$B$6-$B$7 +($G3*$C$13*($B$3-$B$4)-$B$5)/(1+H$2)^$C$12 +($G3*$D$13*($B$3-$B$4)-$B$5)/(1+H$2)^$D$12 +($G3*$E$13*($B$3-$B$4)-$B$5+$B$7+$B$8)/(1+H$2)^$E$12 The line breaks are for reading; this is one formula. Cell I4 must agree with B26 when B2 = 0.10 and B9 = 1. Formula grids avoid hidden scenario substitution, but they duplicate some model logic: update and recheck them whenever timing or recovery assumptions change. Test a corner case by changing the main model's inputs to that row and column, compare results, and restore the base case. Record that comparison in the audit log. At r = 10%, V(s, 0.10) = 14,763.335838s − 13,476.333584. The coefficient on s is the sum of discounted unit contribution margins, 5q t /1.1 t . The break-even multiplier is 13,476.333584/14,763.335838 ≈ 0.912824 . Under these assumptions, reducing all quantities by about 8.72% removes the positive NPV. This is a conditional threshold, not a confidence interval or a prediction of demand. If lower sales also change price, costs or required working capital, a richer scenario must recompute those relationships. Audit the formulas, the inputs and the economic boundary Begin with a written model contract: purpose, unit of analysis, time zero, cash-flow boundary, signs, units, assumptions and output used for the decision. Inspect formulas as well as displayed values. Look for an isolated hard-coded result in a formula row, a shifted reference, a missing period, an omitted row in a sum, hidden rows affecting totals, a circular dependency, or an external input that cannot be reproduced. Save the input version, calculation settings and freshly recalculated output together. A previously displayed result can be stale after an input changes. Use checks with different failure targets. Input validation rejects impossible or missing values under the stated contract. A cash roll-forward checks movement consistency against a separately established balance. An independent implementation checks the same intended calculation by another route. Perturbation checks ask whether known changes have the expected effect. In this model, increasing the time-zero equipment outlay by 500 must reduce NPV by exactly 500; delaying a positive receipt reduces its present value at a positive rate. Raising the discount rate reduces NPV when all future cash flows are nonnegative. That last direction is not universal when future payments are negative. For floating-point residuals, choose a stated tolerance in meaningful units. A micro-dollar tolerance is convenient for these small, synthetic dollar calculations; it is not a universal audit standard. Larger models may need a scale-aware tolerance and an explicit treatment of contractual rounding. Inspect the numerical residual itself as well as a pass/fail label. A numerically small residual can conceal large offsetting errors, and a perfectly coded model can use an inappropriate forecast or discount rate. The downloadable synthetic inputs and provenance and standard-library Python benchmark reproduce the base timeline, nine sensitivity cells and bank reconciliation. Save them in the same directory and run python3 v04-c02-benchmark.py . No spreadsheet application or network connection is required. The benchmark prints JSON and does not alter the inputs. Its separate terminal-working-capital input permits partial-recovery scenarios. Programming details are taught in Python fundamentals for analysis ; here the benchmark provides a second calculation route and an inspectable output. Practice deliverable: build the cell map, reproduce the grid and reconcile the bank example; then retain a short log of two deliberate defects, the checks that detected them and the repaired outputs. Include an offsetting-error case that passes the net check. Evaluate the work by correct timing and units, transparent dependencies, agreement with the benchmark, meaningful defect detection and an explanation of remaining assumptions. A matching headline NPV alone does not meet the capability objective. This exercise supports the later volume project; it does not replace that package. Exercises Cell D5 contains =B5*$B$2+C$3+$A5 . Write the formula after copying it to F8, explain each change, and identify what can still change when B2 is edited. Why would fixing every reference be wrong for a row-by-row calculation? A project pays 6,000 dollars now, receives zero at year 1 end, 2,500 at year 2 end and 4,500 at year 3 end. The annual rate is 10%. Calculate NPV and give a correct spreadsheet expression. A model omits the zero year and computes =-6000+NPV(0.10,2500,4500) . Calculate its result and explain the discrepancy. In Worked example 1, only half of working capital is recovered, and the equipment sells for 500 dollars. Keep every other assumption unchanged. Derive all four net cash flows and the revised NPV. Specify changes to the cell map and explain why the old zero check in B29 must also change. Opening cash is 600, recorded receipts 1,250, supplier payments 720 and wages 450, all in dollars. The bank reports closing cash of 650. Find the residual. A cleared 30-dollar bank fee was omitted; show the correction. A separate 120-dollar customer receipt and 120-dollar supplier payment are also both absent. Find corrected gross receipts, gross payments and closing cash, and explain why the first corrected residual failed to reveal this pair. At a 10% rate, calculate the workshop's NPV for quantity multipliers 0.9 and 1.1, and derive the break-even multiplier. Does the table establish the probability that NPV is positive? State one economically coherent reason why scaling quantity alone might be inadequate. A single 1,200-dollar receipt arrives in month 12. The effective annual discount rate is 12%. Derive the equivalent monthly rate and present value. Compare with dividing 12% by 12 and discounting for 12 months. Specify the different rate convention under which the latter calculation would be appropriate. A reviewer sees a model with rate input 10 labeled “10%”; a check that subtracts a copied formula from itself; a forecast output wrapped in an error handler that returns zero; and a sensitivity table pasted as values before the latest input revision. Diagnose each problem and design a repair and a check. Would protection of formula cells make this model reliable? Design an independent verification package for the workshop. Compute the effects on NPV of (a) increasing the initial equipment payment by 500, (b) moving the entire year 1 net operating cash flow of 3,500 from year 1 end to year 2 end, and (c) setting the discount rate to zero. Give at least one input-domain check, one reconciliation and one error those checks can miss. State the evidence needed before making a real investment recommendation from these synthetic calculations. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria The destination is two columns right and three rows down, so the formula becomes =D8*$B$2+E$3+$A8 . B5 moves in both coordinates; B2 is fixed in both; C$3 moves only its column; $A5 moves only its row. Editing B2 changes the common multiplier used by both the original and copied formula. Absolute referencing controls address translation, not whether a dependency updates. Fixing every reference would keep referring to the original row's observations, usually repeating its result instead of calculating for the new row. NPV = −6,000 + 0/1.1 + 2,500/1.1² + 4,500/1.1³ = −552.967693 dollars, or −552.97. A correct expression is =-6000+NPV(0.10,0,2500,4500) . Omitting the zero period gives −6,000 + 2,500/1.1 + 4,500/1.1² = −8.264463 , or −8.26. The two positive receipts are now discounted for one year less, overstating present value by 544.703231 dollars. A zero payment is an economic observation at a date, not permission to remove the date. The initial outlay remains −12,000. Years 1 and 2 remain 3,500 and 4,500. Year 3 becomes 5,500 + 1,000 + 500 = 7,000 dollars. NPV = −12,000 + 3,500/1.1 + 4,500/1.1² + 7,000/1.1³ = 160.030053 dollars. It falls by 1,500/1.1³ = 1,126.972201 from the base case. Change B8 to 500 and E20 to =0.5*$B$7 , documenting 0.5 in a labeled recovery-fraction input in a reusable model. B29 becomes −1,000: this is the assumed unrecovered cash, not a formula failure. Its expected target must be −1,000, preferably calculated from the explicit recovery assumption. Update the sensitivity logic, whose original final term assumed full recovery; the benchmark's terminal recovery input becomes 1,000. Predicted closing cash is 600 + 1,250 − 720 − 450 = 680; residual is 650 − 680 = −30 . Recording a separate 30-dollar fee gives total payments 1,200 and predicted closing cash 650. Including both additional missing transactions gives receipts 1,370 and supplier payments 840. Total payments are 840 + 450 + 30 = 1,320 , and closing cash remains 600 + 1,370 − 1,320 = 650 . The pair adds equally to receipts and payments, leaving net movement unchanged. A satisfactory audit compares gross flows and transaction records, with disjoint categories, stable identifiers and common cutoffs. Write A = 5,000/1.1 + 6,000/1.1² + 7,000/1.1³ = 14,763.335838 and B = −12,000 − 1,500/1.1 − 1,500/1.1² + 1,500/1.1³ = −13,476.333584. Then V(s) = As + B. At 0.9 it is −189.331330 ; at 1.1 it is 2,763.335838 dollars. Because A > 0, the zero is s = −B/A = 0.9128244275 , with positive NPV above it under the fixed assumptions. No probabilities have been supplied. A defensible limitation is that raising sales volume might require a lower price, extra capacity or more working capital; a scenario should then vary those linked inputs and explain their relationship. The monthly rate is r m = 1.12 1/12 − 1 ≈ 0.009488793 , or 0.9488793% per month. Present value is 1,200/(1 + r m ) 12 = 1,200/1.12 = 1,071.428571 dollars. Using 0.12/12 = 0.01 instead gives 1,200/1.01 12 = 1,064.939070 . A 1% monthly rate compounds to an effective annual rate of about 12.682503%, exceeding the stipulated 12%. Division by 12 is appropriate if 12% is a nominal annual rate explicitly convertible monthly, rather than an effective annual rate. Rate labels must resolve the convention. The stored 10 is a 1,000% rate under the decimal-rate formula; set the numeric input to 0.10 and check the displayed value and unit. The copied-formula residual lacks independence; compare against a separately established balance or a different implementation with a documented purpose. A blanket zero fallback can turn a broken forecast into a plausible result; expose the error, validate required inputs and handle only specifically understood cases. A pasted sensitivity table is disconnected from current assumptions; rebuild formulas or regenerate from versioned inputs and compare base and corner cells with fresh model runs. Formula protection may prevent some accidental edits but does not repair any of these defects. Full credit requires a targeted repair and a failure-detecting check for each, rather than a general instruction to “review carefully.” (a) The time-zero discount factor is one, so NPV falls exactly 500, to 787.002254 dollars. (b) New cash flows are −12,000, 0, 8,000 and 8,500. The change is 3,500/1.1² − 3,500/1.1 = −289.256198 , giving NPV 997.746056 . (c) At r = 0, NPV is −12,000 + 3,500 + 4,500 + 8,500 = 4,500 . A suitable package retains inputs/provenance, the cell map, a separately programmed cash-flow calculation, exact or high-precision reference values, tolerances and deliberate defect tests. Check that rates are finite and greater than −1, and that every annual quantity is numeric and nonnegative. Reconcile cash movements to an independently obtained closing balance, while recognizing that offsetting errors can pass that check. Independent code can also share an omitted tax or unsupported forecast with the spreadsheet. A real recommendation requires evidence about demand, costs, recovery, opportunity costs, taxes, financing boundary and an appropriate discount rate, with uncertainty and alternatives evaluated. The teaching assumptions supply none of that external evidence. Grade the explicit failure mechanisms and reproducible calculations, not the programming language chosen. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain to a colleague why an absolute reference can still update, why time zero stays outside the NPV function's future-flow range, and why a zero cash-reconciliation residual is insufficient. Trace one sensitivity-table cell to its input assumptions, then name a defect it would fail to reveal. Delayed check: after a week, rebuild the four-date timeline and mixed-reference grid without looking at the formulas. Introduce a missing zero-cash period and an offsetting receipt/payment pair; predict which checks fail before recalculating. Explain the working-capital recovery assumption and the distinction between a break-even threshold and a probability. The next chapter, Python fundamentals for analysis , develops explicit functions, validation and file handling for calculations that outgrow a worksheet. Later accounting and corporate-finance volumes deepen the cash-flow boundary, taxes and valuation assumptions; this chapter establishes how to make a stated model inspectable and testable."}, {"title": "04.03 · Python fundamentals for analysis", "url": "v04-c03.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A program is an explicit calculation contract Capability: write, run and explain a small Python program that calculates an economic quantity, rejects invalid inputs and produces a reproducible result. Prerequisite: spreadsheet and financial-model foundations , including signs, units, cash-flow dates and reconciliation. No programming experience is assumed. The calculations and input files below are original synthetic teaching examples; none are observations of current firms or markets. A spreadsheet formula makes a calculation repeatable across cells. A program makes the sequence of reading, checking, calculating and reporting explicit. Neither decides whether a cost was omitted or a discount rate is defensible. Start with a sentence such as “annual end-of-year cash flows in integer US cents, discounted to today at one annual effective rate.” That sentence becomes a contract against which both code and output can be reviewed. Python is the language; the interpreter is the installed program that executes it. A script is a plain-text file ending in .py . In a terminal, python3 calculation.py asks Python to run that file; on Windows with the Python launcher, use py -3 calculation.py . The terminal command is not a line to paste inside Python. Use an existing Python 3 installation and a plain-text editor. Save this first script as calculation.py : revenue_cents = 12500 cost_cents = 9700 profit_cents = revenue_cents - cost_cents print(profit_cents) # output: 2800 Statements run from top to bottom. = binds a name to a value; the right side is evaluated first. print(...) displays a value. Parentheses after a function name supply its arguments. Text after # is a comment. Names are case sensitive; choose names that carry units. cost could mean dollars, cents or thousands, while cost_cents states the intended unit. A script does not automatically display every expression as an interactive Python session does. Values, types and operations A value's type determines its permitted operations. type(value) reveals that type. These are the types needed here: Type Example Meaning in analysis int 12500 A whole number, suitable for exact counts or integer cents. float 0.05 An approximate real number, useful for rates and discounted quantities. str \"USD\" Text. Quoted \"12500\" is text, not a numeric amount. bool True , False A logical condition, such as whether a balance is negative. NoneType None An explicit absence of a value; it does not mean zero. list [100, -20, 5] An ordered, changeable collection, such as a cash-flow timeline. dict {\"currency\": \"USD\", \"amount_cents\": 100} Values named by unique keys, such as a record's fields. Use + , - , * , / and ** for addition, subtraction, multiplication, division and powers. Thus 2**3 is 8, and 7/2 is the float 3.5. // takes a floor quotient and % a remainder: 7//2 is 3 and 7%2 is 1. Floor means toward negative infinity, so -7//2 is −4. Use parentheses to make a financial formula unambiguous: payment_cents / (1 + rate)**year . Division binds after exponentiation; omitting those parentheses changes the formula. == tests equality, unlike assignment = ; != means unequal, with < , <= , > and >= for ordered comparisons. and , or and not combine conditions. int(\"12\") converts an integer string, but int(\"12.5\") fails; int(12.5) truncates to 12, losing information. Conversion is a transformation to justify, not a universal repair for invalid data. Multiplying text repeats it: \"12\" * 2 produces \"1212\" . Do not infer types from how values look on screen. Language reference for this first step: the official Python introduction , covering assignment, numbers, strings and lists. Our examples use economic units and original values. Collections and unintended changes List positions start at zero: for flows = [-10000, 6000, 6000] , flows[0] is today's payment and len(flows) is 3. flows.append(500) adds a fourth entry; the dot selects a method , a function attached to the value. A dictionary uses names instead of positions: record[\"amount_cents\"] . \"amount_cents\" in record tests whether that key exists. Accessing an absent key raises an error instead of silently supplying zero. Assignment does not copy a list. After scenario = flows , both names refer to the same list; scenario[1] = 0 also changes flows[1] . For a flat list of integers, scenario = flows.copy() creates a separate list. This is a shallow copy: nested lists or dictionaries inside it would still be shared. Prefer calculations that read their arguments and return new results. A function that secretly changes the baseline can make all later scenarios wrong. The official data-structures tutorial explains dictionary keys and collection methods. Duplicate field names are especially dangerous because replacing a dictionary value can erase the earlier value. Decisions and loops A colon introduces an indented block. Use four spaces consistently; indentation is part of Python's syntax. An if block runs when its condition is true; elif tests another condition if the first failed; else handles the remaining case: balance_cents = -250 if balance_cents < 0: print(\"cash deficit\") elif balance_cents == 0: print(\"exactly balanced\") else: print(\"cash surplus\") A for loop takes each item in a collection in order. An accumulator starts at an identity value, such as zero for a sum, then updates once per item. Here total_cents = total_cents + flow_cents uses the old total to calculate its replacement: flows = [120, -30, 10] total_cents = 0 for flow_cents in flows: total_cents = total_cents + flow_cents print(total_cents) # 100 The running totals are 120, 90 and 100 cents. Put initialization outside the loop; resetting to zero inside loses earlier items. sum(flows) is the built-in sum, but tracing the loop reveals the logic. for year in range(1, 4): visits 1, 2, 3: the stop is excluded. enumerate(flows) supplies index/value pairs starting at zero; for year, flow_cents in enumerate(flows): assigns the two members of each pair to two names. Starting at zero is essential when the first entry is today's flow. A while loop repeats as long as a condition stays true. For example, with year = 0 , repeatedly print year and update year = year + 1 inside while year < 3: . Without the update it never ends. A for loop is easier to audit when a finite collection already defines the work. Every additional loop dimension multiplies the number of cases; computation time is part of the design. Functions, contracts and exceptions def defines a function. Its parameters name the inputs, its indented body performs the calculation, and return supplies its result to the caller. A triple-quoted opening string is a docstring , documenting the function. Local variables belong to that call. A function that reaches its end without return returns None ; printing a number does not return it. A contract states permitted input types, units and ranges, output meaning and side effects. A side effect changes something outside the returned result, such as an input list, a file or terminal output. Keep the numerical core free of such changes; isolate file reading and printing in the entry point. That makes the same calculation usable in a scenario loop or a test. An exception interrupts the normal sequence. A SyntaxError means Python cannot parse the program; a NameError may indicate a misspelled or undefined name. Use raise TypeError(...) for the wrong kind of argument and raise ValueError(...) for an inadmissible value. Reject invalid external data explicitly: def contribution_cents(revenue_cents, variable_cost_cents): \"\"\"Return revenue minus variable cost in cents; no side effects.\"\"\" for amount in [revenue_cents, variable_cost_cents]: if type(amount) is not int: raise TypeError(\"amounts must be integer cents\") if amount < 0: raise ValueError(\"revenue and variable cost must be nonnegative\") return revenue_cents - variable_cost_cents answer = contribution_cents(12500, 9700) print(answer) # 2800 is tests identity; here type(amount) is int asks for precisely the integer type. This deliberately rejects True and False , which Python otherwise permits in some integer operations. It also rejects 12500.0 : a reader must deliberately establish the integer-cent representation instead of silently truncating a quantity. The return may be negative even though both inputs must be nonnegative. A caller can use try around the operation and except ValueError as error: to handle that particular failure. as error names the exception object, and str(error) provides its text. Parentheses such as except (ValueError, TypeError) as error: list several handled types. On a matching exception, the remaining statements inside try are skipped. Catch failures where there is a useful response, such as a clear diagnostic and unsuccessful exit. A blanket handler that returns zero turns corrupt input into an apparently legitimate financial result. Leave unexpected programming failures visible for correction. The download uses a few compact checks: abs(amount) is the magnitude; 1 <= n <= 101 requires both bounds; text.strip() removes surrounding whitespace. or stops at a true condition, so type(value) is not str or not value.strip() can reject a non-string without trying to call its string method. Empty text is false in a condition; not value.strip() identifies blank text. The official control-flow tutorial documents loops, function definitions, arguments and return values. The official exception tutorial explains how exceptions propagate and how selected handlers work. Worked example 1: reconcile an order calculation in exact cents A synthetic workshop fills three orders. Quantities are whole units; prices and variable costs are cents per unit. Fixed cost for the batch is 2,500 cents. Assume every listed unit is sold and paid for within the batch; no tax, inventory change or credit timing is modeled. Order Units Price per unit, cents Cost per unit, cents Sales, cents Variable cost, cents A 3 1,250 600 3,750 1,800 B 5 800 300 4,000 1,500 C 2 1,500 700 3,000 1,400 orders = [ {\"units\": 3, \"price_cents\": 1250, \"cost_cents\": 600}, {\"units\": 5, \"price_cents\": 800, \"cost_cents\": 300}, {\"units\": 2, \"price_cents\": 1500, \"cost_cents\": 700}, ] sales_cents = 0 variable_cents = 0 for order in orders: sales_cents = sales_cents + order[\"units\"] * order[\"price_cents\"] variable_cents = variable_cents + order[\"units\"] * order[\"cost_cents\"] contribution = contribution_cents(sales_cents, variable_cents) profit_cents = contribution - 2500 print(sales_cents, variable_cents, contribution, profit_cents) # 10750 4700 6050 3550 The loop multiplies units by cents per unit, obtaining cents; it then sums like units. Sales are 10,750 cents, variable cost 4,700, contribution 6,050 and profit 3,550, or 35.50 USD. Reconcile independently by order margins: 3(1,250 − 600) + 5(800 − 300) + 2(1,500 − 700) − 2,500 = 3,550. The equality checks a different grouping of the same economics. A shared omission, such as delivery cost, could survive both calculations. These literal records are supplied as valid teaching inputs; an external order file would also need checks on every quantity and price. Approximate arithmetic and the standard library The standard library ships with Python. import math makes that module available as math ; math.fsum(values) adds floats with improved summation accuracy. from pathlib import Path brings one named object into the current program. These imports need no third-party package. Prefer a small, explicit dependency list. Binary floating point cannot exactly represent most decimal fractions. On ordinary Python installations, 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is false. This is representation error, not evidence of economic uncertainty. Integer cents retain exact input amounts and integer additions; division in discounting produces approximate floats. Keep units visible throughout. math.fsum reduces accumulation error but cannot undo errors already in inputs or each discounted term. Severe cancellation can make a tiny NPV sensitive even when individual terms are computed accurately. math.isclose(a, b, rel_tol=..., abs_tol=...) checks whether |a − b| is at most the larger of the absolute tolerance and the relative tolerance times max(|a|, |b|). Near zero, choose a positive absolute tolerance in the result's units. In this chapter's small cent-valued examples, abs_tol=1e-8 means one hundred-millionth of a cent, not a claim that the forecast cash flows are known that accurately. 1e-8 is scientific notation for 10 −8 . A tolerance is a declared computational check; economic materiality and model sensitivity are separate judgments. For presentation, f\"{profit_cents / 100:.2f}\" is an f-string : it evaluates the expression in braces and formats it with two digits after the decimal point. The result is text. Formatting changes the display, not the stored result or its precision. Do not repeatedly round every intermediate payment unless an actual accounting rule requires it. For contractual decimal rounding, the standard library's decimal module allows explicit precision and rounding policies; construct amounts from strings, not from already approximated floats. Decimal division can still round. Our NPV demonstration is an analysis tool, not a settlement ledger. See the official floating-point tutorial for representation error and accurate summation. The math reference specifies fsum , isclose and finite-number checks. The decimal reference explains decimal construction, finite precision and explicit rounding control. Files are part of the computation A path identifies a file. A relative filename normally depends on the terminal's current working directory, which may differ from the script's folder. In a script, __file__ identifies the script file. Path(__file__).resolve().parent identifies its resolved containing folder; joining with / \"v04-c03-case.json\" selects a neighboring input file. Here the slash joins paths rather than dividing numbers. This keeps the download usable after moving the folder or launching it from somewhere else. An interactive prompt generally has no __file__ . import json from pathlib import Path path = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"v04-c03-case.json\" with path.open(\"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle: case = json.load(handle) print(case[\"cash_flows_cents\"]) \"r\" opens for reading; encoding=\"utf-8\" specifies how bytes become text. with closes the file when the indented block ends, including on an exception. Writing with mode \"w\" would truncate an existing file, so keep original inputs separate from any outputs. The downloadable program prints its report and does not overwrite its inputs. JSON is a text data format for objects, arrays, strings, numbers, booleans and null. json.load decodes a file to Python values; json.dumps(report, indent=2) encodes a result as readable JSON text. JSON uses double-quoted names and lowercase true , false , null , mapped to Python's True , False , None . Parsing checks the format, not the economic contract. Check required names, types, units, ranges and timing afterward. Never use eval to “read” a data file: it executes Python expressions. This offline exercise needs no passwords or secret keys. The small read-only snippet above uses the ordinary JSON decoder. The supplied complete program additionally passes object_pairs_hook=unique_object : this tells the decoder to call our function on each object's key/value pairs. The function builds a dictionary and raises an exception if it encounters a repeated name, preventing silent replacement. Missing and unexpected fields also fail. These are elementary local-file checks, not a security sandbox for arbitrary large hostile files. The official input/output tutorial covers formatting, file modes, encoding and automatic file closure. The pathlib reference documents path construction, resolution and parents. The JSON reference documents decoding errors and object-pair hooks. Field validation remains the analyst's responsibility. Worked example 2: a complete reproducible cash-flow program Download the Python program , synthetic input data and run instructions and data contract . Keep the first two files together. From their folder, the one-command offline run is: python3 v04-c03-cashflow.py The case pays 120,000 cents today, then receives 45,000, 50,000 and 55,000 cents at the ends of years 1, 2 and 3. Its rate is 1,000 basis points per year. One basis point is 0.0001 as a rate, so 1000 / 10000 is 0.10, or 10%. Rates and cash-flow periods must agree. The program's numerical core is: import math def npv_cents(cash_flows_cents, discount_rate_bps): validate_values(cash_flows_cents, discount_rate_bps) rate = discount_rate_bps / 10000 terms = [] for period, cents in enumerate(cash_flows_cents): terms.append(cents / (1 + rate)**period) return math.fsum(terms) The full download supplies validate_values . The list includes time zero, so NPV in cents is −120,000 + 45,000/1.1 + 50,000/1.1² + 55,000/1.1³. The terms are approximately −120,000, 40,909.090909, 41,322.314050 and 41,322.314050 cents. Their sum is 3,553.719008 cents, or 35.537190 USD. Independently replacing 1.1 with the exact fraction 11/10 gives 430,000/121 cents. The report's dollar display is \"35.54\" ; its undiscounted net is exactly 30,000 cents. Discounting removes most of that nominal surplus. The tool requires 1–101 integer flows, with magnitude at most 10 12 cents each, and an integer rate from −5,000 to 10,000 basis points inclusive. Those documented implementation bounds allow at most 100 future years and rates from −50% to +100%, keeping the demonstration away from floating overflow. They are not general economic restrictions. The mathematical formula requires 1 + r > 0 for our standard discounting interpretation; r = −1 is singular for future flows. At r = 0, NPV equals the undiscounted sum. A single entry is an immediate flow and is undiscounted. Negative permitted rates increase the present value of positive future receipts. The file reader verifies USD, annual periods and t0_then_year_end timing, so it cannot quietly treat monthly data as annual. Quoted numeric strings, fractional cents, booleans, absent values and duplicate names fail. A try / except boundary reports expected file, decoding and validation errors and returns exit status 1; success returns 0. sys.stderr is the diagnostic output stream. The ending if __name__ == \"__main__\": runs main() only when the file is launched directly. raise SystemExit(main()) passes its return code to the operating system. Importing the module for tests defines its functions without reading or printing. A positive NPV is conditional on the supplied timeline and rate, and does not validate demand, costs or risk assumptions. No tax rules, irregular dates, inflation adjustment or cash-flow uncertainty are inferred. Keep the original case and document every scenario change. The README gives expected output and failure experiments, allowing another analyst to reproduce the calculation without a network connection. Assertions test the program; exceptions validate the input An assert states something the developer expects to hold. A false assertion raises AssertionError . It is useful in a test or for an internal invariant. Put test statements after the relevant function definitions and imports in a scratch script. To test the download, place the NPV checks in a working copy immediately before its final if __name__ entry-point block; the contribution check additionally needs the function defined earlier in this chapter. Examples: assert contribution_cents(12500, 9700) == 2800 assert npv_cents([-10000, 6000, 6000], 0) == 2000 assert math.isclose( npv_cents([-10000, 5500, 6050], 1000), 0.0, rel_tol=0.0, abs_tol=1e-8 ) The last exact NPV is −10,000 + 5,000 + 5,000 = 0 cents; a binary calculation can leave a tiny residual. These assertions compare against independently derived expectations. Testing the same mistaken formula against a copy of itself would add little evidence. Include a zero-rate case, a one-period calculation, the time-zero boundary, invalid inputs and checks that input lists remain unchanged. Python's optimization option -O can remove assertions. Therefore assert rate_is_valid is unsuitable as the only external-input guard. Keep validation in explicit if / raise statements, which still run under optimization. Never put an essential update or file operation inside an assertion: its execution could disappear too. Passing tests establishes behavior on those cases; the economic assumptions and the logic still require review. The official assert statement reference specifies assertion removal under optimization. Exercises Give the value and type of 7/2 , 7//2 , 7%2 , \"7\" * 2 and int(\"7\") + 2 . A source records a cost as \"12500\" but gives no unit. Does conversion to an integer solve the analysis problem? Explain. Trace the accumulator for [120, -30, 10] , recording its value after each iteration. What happens if initialization is moved inside the loop? Explain why enumerate must start at zero for a timeline including today's flow and quantify the effect of shifting every flow one period later at a 10% rate. Write a function margin_cents(revenue_cents, cost_cents) that accepts only nonnegative integer cents, returns their difference, and neither prints nor changes its inputs. Give its contract and outputs for (12500, 9700), (0, 0) and (100, 250). Explain how it treats True , \"100\" and −1 as a revenue input. Add an order for four units, each selling for 900 cents and costing 500 cents, to Worked example 1. Calculate sales, variable cost, contribution and profit with the original fixed cost. Reconcile by order margins. Then increase fixed cost by 1,000 cents and explain whether the extra order still covers that additional cost. For the code below, identify the printed value, returned value and final contents of flows after flows = [100, -20, 5] and answer = broken_total(flows) . Correct it without mutating the caller's list. Explain why merely assigning a second name to the list is not a repair. def broken_total(flows): total = 0 for amount in flows: total = amount flows.append(total) print(total) For flows [-60000, 25000, 25000, 25000] in cents at 500 annual basis points, compute NPV in cents and the two-decimal dollar display. Compute the zero-rate NPV as a separate check. Then calculate [-10000, 12100] at 1,000 basis points by hand. Distinguish the formula's singular rate from the demonstration's stricter input bounds. A colleague loads \"case.json\" relative to the terminal's folder, calls eval on the file, validates the rate with assert , and catches every error by reporting NPV = 0. Explain all four faults and write a short replacement reading pattern. State what the supplied complete program should do with a quoted rate, a boolean cash flow, a duplicate field and a monthly-period label. Construct meaningful tests for the NPV program, including zero rate, only time zero, a positive-rate result, mathematical zero with cancellation, invalid input and unchanged input values. State tolerances with units, distinguish computational precision from economic materiality, and explain what should happen when the program is run with -O . Would two matching implementations prove the input assumptions correct? Complete solutions and evaluation criteria The values/types are 3.5 ( float ), 3 ( int ), 1 ( int ), \"77\" ( str ) and 9 ( int ). int(\"12500\") establishes a numeric representation but cannot decide whether the original measure is cents, dollars or thousands of dollars. Inspect the source's definition and retain the declared conversion. A defensible answer separates parsing from unit validation and does not infer units merely from a plausible magnitude. The totals are 120, 90 and 100 cents. Resetting to zero inside each iteration leaves only the last amount, 10 cents, as the final total. A zero-indexed timeline has today's entry divided by (1 + r) 0 = 1. If every index is wrongly increased by one, every term and therefore the NPV is divided by 1 + r. At 10%, the computed value becomes NPV/1.1 = 10NPV/11. For a positive NPV it is about 9.0909% too low; for a negative NPV it becomes less negative. This is a timeline error, not a harmless formatting change. A complete implementation is: def margin_cents(revenue_cents, cost_cents): \"\"\"Nonnegative int-cent inputs; signed int-cent result; no side effects.\"\"\" for value in [revenue_cents, cost_cents]: if type(value) is not int: raise TypeError(\"expected integer cents\") if value < 0: raise ValueError(\"expected nonnegative input cents\") return revenue_cents - cost_cents The outputs are 2,800, 0 and −150 cents. A boolean or quoted string raises TypeError ; −1 raises ValueError . The contract permits a negative margin even though the separate revenue and cost inputs are nonnegative. The function's returned number can be reused without capturing terminal output. The new order contributes sales 4(900) = 3,600 cents and variable cost 4(500) = 2,000 cents. New sales are 14,350, variable cost 6,700, contribution 7,650 and profit 5,150 cents, or 51.50 USD. Independently, original contribution 6,050 plus 4(900 − 500) = 1,600 gives 7,650; subtract 2,500. With fixed cost 3,500, profit is 4,150 cents, or 41.50 USD. The new order's contribution exceeds the additional fixed cost by 600 cents, so profit rises by 600 relative to the original 3,550, under the stated cost and sales assumptions. Each assignment replaces the total, so its last value is 5. The function appends 5, prints 5 and falls through with return value None . The caller's list becomes [100, -20, 5, 5] . Correct the accumulation and return, and remove the append/print: def total_cents(flows): \"\"\"Sum a supplied list of integer cents without changing it.\"\"\" total = 0 for amount in flows: total = total + amount return total On a fresh [100, -20, 5] , this returns 85 and leaves the list intact. The contract here assumes already validated integer entries; external inputs need checks before calling. A second name would still reference the same list. A shallow copy can separate a flat list, but the correct total function needs no copy because it does not modify the collection. The rate is 500/10,000 = 0.05. NPV = −60,000 + 25,000/1.05 + 25,000/1.05² + 25,000/1.05³ = 74,840,000/9,261 ≈ 8,081.200734 cents. Divide by 100 for 80.812007 USD and display \"80.81\" . At zero rate, −60,000 + 75,000 = 15,000 cents. The separate one-period case is −10,000 + 12,100/1.1 = 1,000 cents, or 10 USD. For future flows the denominator vanishes at r = −1, corresponding to −10,000 basis points. The supplied tool rejects even rates below −5,000 or above 10,000 basis points because it has narrower declared computational bounds. A rejected value does not by itself imply an impossible economic rate. A bare relative path depends on where the command was launched; eval executes code instead of decoding data; an assertion can disappear under optimization; returning zero on any failure makes missing or corrupt inputs look like a genuine zero NPV. A minimal decoding pattern is: import json from pathlib import Path path = Path(__file__).resolve().parent / \"v04-c03-case.json\" try: with path.open(\"r\", encoding=\"utf-8\") as handle: case = json.load(handle) except (OSError, UnicodeError, ValueError) as error: raise SystemExit(\"Cannot read case: \" + str(error)) # Continue with explicit schema, field, unit and value validation. This fragment teaches safe decoding and error reporting; it does not supply the complete contract or duplicate-name guard. The downloadable load_case supplies those. It rejects a quoted rate and boolean cash flow by exact-type checks, a duplicate field through its decoding hook, and a monthly label through the timing contract. Each failure yields a diagnostic and unsuccessful exit, not a numeric report. An acceptable alternative preserves all four corrections and labels the remaining validation explicitly. One executable test pattern, after making npv_cents and math available, is: flows = [-10000, 12100] original = flows.copy() assert npv_cents(flows, 0) == 2100 assert npv_cents([12345], 1000) == 12345 assert math.isclose(npv_cents(flows, 1000), 1000, rel_tol=0.0, abs_tol=1e-8) assert flows == original assert math.isclose(npv_cents([-10000, 5500, 6050], 1000), 0, rel_tol=0.0, abs_tol=1e-8) try: npv_cents([True], 1000) except TypeError: pass else: raise AssertionError(\"boolean cash flow was accepted\") pass deliberately does nothing in the expected-error branch; else after try / except runs when no exception occurred. Test empty flows, wrong units in the file, an inadmissible rate and unchanged file inputs similarly. The tolerance is 10 −8 cents for these small examples, not a universal tolerance for much larger or badly conditioned calculations. Under -O these test assertions may vanish, but the program's if / raise validation must still reject invalid data. Run ordinary tests without optimization and separately test optimized invalid-input execution. Numerical agreement cannot prove forecasts, timing choices or discount-rate assumptions correct; two implementations can share the same omitted economic item. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the path from a file's bytes to a reported NPV: decoding, typed values, unit/timing validation, function contract, loop, approximate arithmetic and display. Trace one period by hand. Explain why return differs from print , why an alias can corrupt a scenario, and why external validation cannot rely on assertions. Delayed check: a week later, recreate a pure integer-cent total function and a discounted-sum loop without looking. For [-10000, 9000] and −1,000 basis points, derive exact NPV zero before running the function. Deliberately replace the rate with a string and confirm explicit failure, including under -O . Run the downloaded script from another folder and explain why it still finds its input. Successful performance requires correct units and timing as well as correct syntax. The next chapter, arrays, tables and SQL , extends these small records into tables, joins, aggregation, missing values and duplicate keys. The present chapter supplies a readable executable core; it does not attempt large-data engineering, package ecosystems or advanced software architecture."}, {"title": "04.04 · Arrays, tables and SQL", "url": "v04-c04.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Preserve the meaning while changing the representation Capability: transform structured data without corrupting units, keys or populations. The prerequisite is basic Python for economic computation : variables, lists, dictionaries, loops, functions, exceptions and files. We will use those tools to calculate with aligned arrays, construct tables, query a small database and explain precisely whose observations remain in each answer. A household's income can appear twice after a join even though no household earned more. An average can rise when an unreported value disappears from its denominator. A payment near midnight can move into a different month after its time zone is normalized. Each result can be syntactically valid and economically misleading. Before transforming data, write down the observation unit, measurement unit, identifying key, intended population and missing-value rule. Afterward, reconcile them. All quantities and households below are invented teaching fixtures, with no empirical claim. Money is integer cents of one fictional dollar currency: 100 cents equal one dollar. The code uses Python's standard library and SQLite through sqlite3 ; no numerical package or database server is required. The methods use established functionality within the 6 October 2026 edition boundary; the downloadable program prints the versions actually used on your machine. Arrays: position has meaning only after alignment A numerical vector is an ordered collection of numbers with a declared interpretation for each position. A rectangular array has a shape : a table of two regions by three products has two rows and three columns. In mathematical notation Q ij can mean units of product j sold in region i. A price vector p of length three then gives region revenue r i = Σ j=1 3 Q ij p j . The inner dimension must agree, and each price must belong to the corresponding product. Quantities measured in product units times prices in cents per unit produce cents. A Python list can hold objects of different types, including other lists. A list of lists does not automatically enforce rectangular shape: [[1, 2], [3]] is a valid Python object but is not a two-by-two matrix. The standard-library array module stores a sequence constrained by a type code; array('q', [100, 200]) stores signed integer values and array('d', [1.5, 2.0]) stores floating-point values. Fixed type does not mean fixed length. Neither container attaches economic units or product names to positions. Neither gives list multiplication the meaning of matrix multiplication: [1, 2] * 2 repeats the sequence. For this chapter, nested lists represent dense rectangular quantities and an array represents an aligned price vector. We perform the multiplication explicitly. Always verify the number of columns first; ordinary zip stops at the shorter sequence, which can hide a missing product. Even equal lengths do not establish label alignment. An absent price must raise a question, not silently become zero. API reference: Python's array documentation explains constrained types, type codes and sequence operations. The economic shape and alignment checks here are part of our data contract. Worked example 1: a correctly shaped but misaligned calculation Quantity columns are ordered ['grain', 'tools'] . North sells 20 units of grain and 3 tools; South sells 10 units of grain and 4 tools. A price file happens to list tools first: 5,000 cents per tool and 200 cents per unit of grain. Multiplying the first quantity by the first arriving price gives North 20(5,000) + 3(200) = 100,600 cents. The dimensions agree, but the labels do not. The following compact Python forms abbreviate operations you already know. set(products) collects distinct labels; comparing its length with the list detects repeats. Applied to a dictionary, set(prices) collects its keys. [prices[name] for name in products] is a list comprehension : loop over product names in order, look up each price, and collect the results in a list. sum(expression for j in range(n)) feeds the expression for indices 0 through n − 1 into the sum without first storing another list; this is a generator expression . Neither shorthand performs a hidden alignment check. from array import array products = ['grain', 'tools'] quantities = [[20, 3], [10, 4]] prices = {'tools': 5000, 'grain': 200} if len(set(products)) != len(products): raise ValueError('duplicate product labels') if set(products) != set(prices): raise ValueError('missing or extra price labels') aligned = array('q', [prices[name] for name in products]) revenue_cents = [] for row in quantities: if len(row) != len(products): raise ValueError('quantity row has the wrong length') revenue_cents.append(sum(row[j] * aligned[j] for j in range(len(products)))) The aligned price vector is [200, 5,000]. North revenue is 4,000 + 15,000 = 19,000 cents, or 190 dollars. South revenue is 2,000 + 20,000 = 22,000 cents, or 220 dollars. Total revenue is 41,000 cents. The two-by-two quantity matrix times the length-two price vector produces one value per region. Transposing the matrix would change the interpretation even though this square example retains compatible dimensions; labels remain essential. Our dictionary assumes one price per product. Constructing it from duplicate product records could overwrite an earlier value, so inspect uniqueness before converting source rows into a dictionary. Fixed-width array storage can also overflow for very large input values; these examples are small. Python integer arithmetic in the explicit sum is exact, while fractional prices require a declared representation and rounding rule. Tables: declare grain, keys and admissible values The grain of a table is what one row represents. Our roster has one row per household; the observation table has one row per household-month report. A key identifies a row uniquely and must be present. Household ID alone identifies roster rows, but (household_id, month) identifies observation rows. The separate obs_id is another unique identifier; a fresh observation ID does not make a second record for the same household-month legitimate. Canonical roster: four fictional households Household Region Demonstration weight A North 1 B North 3 C South 2 D South 4 Reports: month denotes the entire named calendar month; amounts are cents obs_id Household month income_cents a1 A 2024-01-01 10,000 a2 A 2024-02-01 12,000 b1 B 2024-01-01 30,000 b2 B 2024-02-01 NULL: unreported c1 C 2024-01-01 0: recorded zero c2 C 2024-02-01 6,000 c3 C 2024-03-01 9,000 The observation table contains seven reports from three households. D belongs to the roster but has no report. B's February report exists and lacks income. C's January report says zero. These are three different states. The weights are positive teaching numbers, not evidence of a real sampling design. Neither a roster nor a weight alone establishes representativeness. SQL is a language for specifying table operations. CREATE TABLE declares columns and constraints; INSERT INTO adds rows. PRIMARY KEY and UNIQUE constrain uniqueness. We explicitly add NOT NULL to every key component. A foreign key connects the observation's household to an existing roster household. The supplied loader enables SQLite foreign-key enforcement, rejects duplicate household-months before insertion, checks integer cents and positive weights, and validates dates in Python. SQLite's ordinary type declarations allow conversions and flexible storage; declaring a column INTEGER alone is not a complete input-validation policy. CREATE TABLE observations ( obs_id TEXT PRIMARY KEY NOT NULL, household_id TEXT NOT NULL REFERENCES households(household_id), month TEXT NOT NULL, income_cents INTEGER, UNIQUE(household_id, month) ); Implementation boundary: SQLite's datatype documentation describes flexible storage and affinity. The downloadable loader adds Python validation and explicit database checks; those checks validate this fixture contract, not all possible meanings of income. Constraint references: CREATE TABLE describes primary, unique and nonmissing constraints; foreign-key support explains explicit enforcement on a connection. A small SQL vocabulary, used from Python SELECT specifies output columns or expressions, FROM names the input, and WHERE keeps qualifying rows. AS gives an output or table a short name. ORDER BY establishes output order; without it, a returned row position is not a reliable identity. GROUP BY partitions rows into groups, aggregate functions summarize each group, and HAVING filters those summaries. Use grouped columns or aggregates in grouped output: an ungrouped household name beside regional totals has no well-defined economic interpretation. SELECT household_id, income_cents / 100.0 AS income_dollars FROM observations WHERE month = '2024-01-01' ORDER BY household_id; This returns A: 100.0, B: 300.0 and C: 0.0 dollars. SQL strings use single quotes. Dividing integer expressions in SQLite can truncate: 5 / 2 gives 2, whereas 5 / 2.0 gives 2.5. The 100.0 above requests a real-valued result. Keep monetary sums in integer cents for this case, converting for display; integer sums have finite storage limits, and real-valued calculations are approximations. To experiment without installing a server, keep the downloaded fixture and lab script in one folder. The lab creates an in-memory database with sqlite3.connect(':memory:') . Python's None corresponds to SQL NULL . A query's ? marks a bound value: pass the values separately rather than composing SQL from input strings. Binding a value does not substitute a table or column name. In the next snippet, runpy.run_path loads the lab's definitions into a dictionary, from which we retrieve its loader functions. The parameter sequence ('2024-01-01',) is a one-element tuple ; its trailing comma distinguishes it from a parenthesized string. try begins the query work and finally closes the connection whether that work succeeds or raises an exception. The output comprehension converts each named SQL result row to a dictionary for readable printing. # Run in the folder containing the two downloaded files. import runpy lab = runpy.run_path('v04-c04-sql-lab.py') con = lab['make_db'](lab['load_case']()) try: result = con.execute( 'SELECT household_id, income_cents FROM observations ' 'WHERE month = ? ORDER BY household_id', ('2024-01-01',) ) print([dict(row) for row in result]) finally: con.close() Python's sqlite3 documentation supplies the connection, bound-parameter and result-row API. SQL examples below use the same tables; enter them as the string passed to con.execute while the connection is open. Joins: count the matches before summing the money An inner join returns matching row pairs. A left join also retains an unmatched left row, filling right-side columns with NULL. The ON expression defines a match. For an equality join on a nonmissing key k, if the left table has m k rows and the right has n k , that key contributes m k n k inner-join rows. For a left join it contributes m k max(1, n k ). This counts row pairs, not distinct economic units. Joining many monthly reports to a unique household roster is many-to-one: every matched report retains one row. Joining a roster household to its many reports is one-to-many and intentionally expands household rows. Duplicates on both sides create many-to-many multiplication. A join on household alone is insufficient if the intended matching unit is household-month. Record expected cardinality and inspect unmatched keys in both directions before accepting the result. SELECT household_id, COUNT(*) AS copies FROM raw_roster GROUP BY household_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 1; The supplied raw_roster deliberately contains A twice; this query returns A, 2. It is a diagnostic input separate from the canonical roster. If these are confirmed duplicate deliveries of the same record, remove the redundant delivery with an audit trail. If they disagree on region or weight, investigate which version is valid. If they represent two legitimate periods or memberships, change the grain and matching rule. DISTINCT removes duplicate selected values; it does not decide any of these questions. SUM(DISTINCT income_cents) is especially unsafe: two different households can legitimately earn the same amount. Sometimes the intended question is whether a match exists. EXISTS evaluates whether its enclosed query returns at least one row, without copying the left row for every match: SELECT h.household_id FROM households AS h WHERE EXISTS ( SELECT 1 FROM observations AS o WHERE o.household_id = h.household_id ) ORDER BY h.household_id; This returns A, B and C once each because the roster is unique. If instead the purpose is to count reports, preserve the report grain and count them. Aggregating after an accidental multiplication merely summarizes the multiplied data. SQLite's SELECT documentation specifies joins, grouping and the placement of filtering. The match-count formulas and reconciliation examples here follow by enumerating row pairs. Missing values, counts and the population behind an average NULL is a missing marker; it does not encode whether the cause is nonresponse, inapplicability or a failed join. Preserve a separate status or source record when that distinction matters. Comparisons such as income_cents = NULL do not test for missingness: use IS NULL or IS NOT NULL . Ordinary equality to NULL yields an unknown result, and WHERE retains only true conditions. Thus income_cents >= 0 excludes missing incomes. An equality join does not match two NULL keys; our valid keys prohibit them. See SQLite's expression rules for NULL comparisons, IS , arithmetic and EXISTS . The NULL behavior notes also document why multiplying a missing value by zero still yields NULL. Aggregate meanings for validated numeric measurements Expression What it counts or measures COUNT(*) Rows, including rows containing NULLs COUNT(x) Rows whose x is not NULL, including recorded zeros SUM(x) Sum of nonmissing x; NULL if none exist AVG(x) Mean over nonmissing x; NULL if none exist COALESCE(x, 0) x when present, otherwise zero: an explicit replacement rule For one row containing a missing income, the two counts are 1 and 0; for no rows they are 0 and 0. Both cases give NULL for the sum and average. An ungrouped aggregate query still returns its one summary row. A missing total is not automatically evidence of zero activity. If an empty transaction set is known to mean no transactions, zero can be appropriate, but that conclusion comes from the recording contract. SQLite's aggregate documentation establishes these behaviors. It also notes that nonnumeric text can be treated as zero by AVG ; this is one reason our loader rejects numeric strings and invalid measurements before querying. Worked example 2: a roster-preserving January summary We want all four roster households represented in a January reconciliation, then the mean among those with observed income. Restrict the matching reports in ON , so a household with no January match survives: SELECT h.household_id, o.obs_id, o.income_cents FROM households AS h LEFT JOIN observations AS o ON h.household_id = o.household_id AND o.month = '2024-01-01' ORDER BY h.household_id; The four resulting income entries are 10,000, 30,000, 0 and NULL cents. D's obs_id is also NULL, identifying the absent report. Replacing the output columns with COUNT(*), COUNT(o.obs_id), COUNT(o.income_cents), SUM(o.income_cents), AVG(o.income_cents) produces 4, 3, 3, 40,000 and 40,000/3 ≈ 13,333.333333 cents. The observed-income mean is therefore about 133.333333 dollars, with coverage 3/4. It is not the identified mean income of all four households because D's income is unknown. If we instead calculate AVG(COALESCE(o.income_cents, 0)) , the answer is 40,000/4 = 10,000 cents, or 100 dollars. That assigns D zero and changes the quantity being reported. If we join January reports to the defective raw_roster , A appears twice: the income rows become 10,000, 10,000, 30,000 and 0. Their sum is 50,000 cents, a 25% inflation over the recorded 40,000. No economic event explains the extra 10,000. There is a second trap. With LEFT JOIN ... ON h.household_id = o.household_id WHERE o.month = '2024-01-01' , D is removed: its right-side month is NULL, so the filter is not true. A household with reports only in other months would also disappear. To retain the roster, put the target-month restriction in ON . Adding OR o.month IS NULL to the faulty WHERE is not a general repair, because an out-of-month matched row is not a NULL-extended row. Aggregation: keep the numerator with its denominator Grouping changes the grain: a region-level result no longer contains one row per household. January's observed North incomes total 40,000 cents across two households, giving a mean of 20,000 cents. South has one observed income of zero, giving a mean of zero. The unweighted average of the two regional means is 10,000 cents. The observed-household mean is instead [2(20,000) + 1(0)]/(2 + 1) = 40,000/3 cents. The first gives each region equal weight; the second gives each observed household equal weight. Preserve group sums and nonmissing counts so that regrouping can reconstruct the intended mean. SELECT h.region, COUNT(o.income_cents) AS n, SUM(o.income_cents) AS total_cents, AVG(o.income_cents) AS mean_cents FROM households AS h LEFT JOIN observations AS o ON h.household_id = o.household_id AND o.month = '2024-01-01' GROUP BY h.region ORDER BY h.region; For positive weights w i and observed-value set S, the weighted mean is Σ i∈S w i x i /Σ i∈S w i . Both sums must use the same set. In January the numerator is 1(10,000) + 3(30,000) + 2(0) = 100,000 weighted cents; the denominator is 1 + 3 + 2 = 6. The result is 16,666.666667 cents, or about 166.666667 dollars. D's weight of 4 cannot enter this complete-case denominator while its unknown income contributes nothing to the numerator. SELECT SUM(h.weight * o.income_cents) AS numerator_cents, SUM(h.weight) AS observed_weight, 1.0 * SUM(h.weight * o.income_cents) / SUM(h.weight) AS mean_cents FROM households AS h JOIN observations AS o ON h.household_id = o.household_id WHERE o.month = '2024-01-01' AND o.income_cents IS NOT NULL; The explicit nonmissing filter aligns both sums. With no observed incomes, both sums and the ratio are NULL. An application should report insufficient data, not relabel the result as zero. The positive-weight rule excludes a zero denominator among nonempty valid cases. These weights produce a descriptive weighted mean; they do not remove nonresponse bias or supply a sampling-variance formula. Repeated household observations introduce another denominator choice. Pooling two months gives equal weight to observed household-months; households with more reports contribute more often. Averaging household-specific means first answers a different question. A complete roster-by-month grid makes the expected cells explicit: CROSS JOIN creates all pairs before a left join attaches reports. A WITH clause can name a temporary query result for one statement, and VALUES can supply its small list of rows: WITH months(month) AS (VALUES ('2024-01-01'), ('2024-02-01')) SELECT h.household_id, m.month, o.obs_id, o.income_cents FROM households AS h CROSS JOIN months AS m LEFT JOIN observations AS o ON h.household_id = o.household_id AND m.month = o.month ORDER BY h.household_id, m.month; The cross join intentionally creates four times two cells. Unlike an accidental many-to-many merge, this expansion defines the target population of household-months before measuring coverage. Dates: normalize first, then define the interval A date is a calendar label; a timestamp identifies an instant only with a time-zone convention. Our monthly labels must be valid, zero-padded YYYY-MM-01 dates. They describe entire months, not payments at midnight. SQLite has no dedicated date/time storage class. For events, the lab accepts timestamps with explicit numeric offsets, converts them in Python to UTC, and stores fixed-format YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS text. It rejects missing offsets, impossible calendar dates and unsupported subsecond inputs rather than guessing or truncating. Define February 2024 in UTC as the half-open interval [2024-02-01 00:00:00, 2024-03-01 00:00:00). Include the lower endpoint and exclude the upper. Adjacent months then do not double-count their shared boundary. Since every stored UTC timestamp has the same format and precision, lexicographic comparison agrees with temporal order. This does not hold for mixed formats, offsets or local time zones. BETWEEN includes both endpoints, so it would include the first instant of March. SELECT event_id, amount_cents FROM events WHERE time_utc >= '2024-02-01 00:00:00' AND time_utc < '2024-03-01 00:00:00' ORDER BY event_id; A local timestamp 2024-01-31T23:30:00-02:00 is 2024-02-01 01:30:00 UTC, inside February. Conversely, 2024-02-01T00:30:00+02:00 is January 31 at 22:30 UTC. Extracting the original text's month before normalizing changes membership. A policy may legitimately use a local business calendar instead; state that calendar and convert accordingly. UTC month is our explicit exercise convention, not a universal reporting standard. SQLite's date/time documentation describes supported representations, UTC offset handling and julianday . For these endpoints, julianday('2024-03-01') - julianday('2024-02-01') is 29 days. A calendar month is not a fixed 30-day duration. The benchmark cross-checks its Python-normalized timestamps with SQLite's datetime on the same offset-bearing inputs. For the loader's parsing and UTC conversion APIs, consult Python's datetime documentation . The loader's narrower input format is deliberate and explicitly validated. Offline lab and acceptance checks Download the JSON fixtures and executable SQL benchmark into the same folder. Run python3 v04-c04-sql-lab.py there, or invoke the script by its path from another directory; it locates its fixture relative to itself. Python 3.9 or later with sqlite3 is required. The program outputs named JSON results for both worked examples and the main computational exercise results, including runtime versions. It creates no persistent database and needs no network. In the source repository, run python3 book/scripts/check_v04_c04.py for separate SQL-versus-Python reconciliation and rejection checks. Before treating a transformed table as an analysis input, inspect unique and nonmissing keys, intended matches and unmatched rows, row-count changes, conserved sums where appropriate, observed counts and weight totals, units and date boundaries. A sum need not stay constant when an intentional filter changes the population; explain and quantify the change. The lab is an introductory small-data workflow. Index design, concurrent transactions, large-scale query plans, panel estimators, survey variance and missing-data identification require further study. Exercises Quantity columns are a, b, c, and region rows are [2, 3, 1] and [1, 0, 4]. Price records arrive as c: 50, a: 100, b: 200 cents per unit. State the shapes, align the prices and compute regional and total revenue. What does a positional use of the arriving price order produce? Explain why a ragged quantity row and a duplicate product record require separate checks. Use all seven observation records. Is household ID a key? Is observation ID alone sufficient to enforce the declared household-month grain? Give both legitimate keys in this fixture, count rows and distinct households, and explain why B's February NULL and D's absent reports differ. What should happen if a new record has obs_id='a2-copy' but repeats A's February household-month? Join January observations to the raw roster with its duplicated A. Give the joined row count, sum and percentage inflation relative to the canonical matched reports. Write the duplicate-key diagnostic and propose a defensible correction procedure for exact duplicates versus conflicting records. Why is SUM(DISTINCT income_cents) not a general repair? Construct the February roster-preserving left join. List household, observation ID and income, then calculate COUNT(*) , COUNT(o.obs_id) , COUNT(o.income_cents) , sum, observed mean and zero-filled mean. Which households survive if the month restriction is placed in WHERE instead? Give the counts, sum and mean for the lone b2 report and for an empty selection. Calculate January's observed regional means, their unweighted average and the observed-household mean. Reconcile the latter using group counts. Compute the complete-case weighted January and February means. In February, what happens if someone divides the observed weighted numerator by the full roster weight 10? Identify the implied missingness assumption. Using all five events in the fixture, normalize each timestamp to UTC and select February's half-open interval. Give included event IDs, total cents and number of calendar days. Compare with selecting February from the original local date strings. Explain the upper-endpoint error with an inclusive interval. A left table has two rows with key x and amounts 100 and 50 cents. A right table has three x rows labeled p, q and r. How many rows does an equality join produce, and what is the sum of the copied left amounts? Derive the general m-by-n count. If the goal is to keep each left record whose key occurs on the right, write an EXISTS solution. Explain why summing distinct amounts fails if two legitimate left records each have 50 cents. A memo says: “Our balanced January–February panel has six households, no missing incomes because SQL averages ignore NULL, and mean monthly income 72.50 dollars after cleaning blanks to zero.” Audit every part using the roster-by-month grid. Compute expected cells, existing reports, observed incomes, missing report cells, reports with missing income, observed-income total and mean, and the zero-filled mean. Give a corrected statement that names the population and does not infer unobserved incomes. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria The quantity array is two by three and prices form a length-three vector. Align prices to [100, 200, 50]. Region revenues are 2(100) + 3(200) + 1(50) = 850 cents and 1(100) + 0(200) + 4(50) = 300 cents; total 1,150 cents, or 11.50 dollars. Using arriving order [50, 100, 200] gives 600 and 850 cents, totaling 1,450. Equal lengths do not prevent that error. A ragged row violates shape; duplicate price labels can overwrite a value in a dictionary even with correct shape. Validate uniqueness on raw records and ensure every quantity column has exactly one admissible price. The lab's exercise_1_cents reports [850, 300]; its array function rejects a ragged row or label mismatch. SELECT COUNT(*), COUNT(DISTINCT household_id) FROM observations returns 7 and 3. Household ID repeats across months, so it is not a key of this table. Both obs_id and the pair (household_id, month) are unique here; both constraints matter because a new arbitrary observation ID could otherwise conceal a duplicate report period. B has an existing February record with unreported income; D has no report rows at all. Neither establishes zero income. The proposed a2-copy must be rejected or quarantined pending an explicit revision/duplicate policy. The lab validator rejects it as a duplicate household-month before loading. Complete reasoning must name the grain and cannot infer household counts from report counts. A's 10,000-cent report matches two rows, B's 30,000 matches one and C's zero matches one. The join has four rows and sums to 50,000 cents versus three matched reports and 40,000 cents canonically. Inflation is (50,000 − 40,000)/40,000 = 1/4 = 25%. The diagnostic is SELECT household_id, COUNT(*) FROM raw_roster GROUP BY household_id HAVING COUNT(*) > 1 ; it returns A, 2. Preserve the raw delivery, investigate why it repeats, document any verified exact-duplicate removal, and recheck unique keys and the 40,000-cent reconciliation. Conflicting region/weight values require provenance or version resolution, not arbitrary retention. Summing distinct incomes deduplicates amounts instead of records; two real 10,000-cent incomes would wrongly contribute only 10,000. With February in ON the rows are (A, a2, 12,000), (B, b2, NULL), (C, c2, 6,000), (D, NULL, NULL). Counts are 4 roster rows, 3 reports and 2 observed incomes. Sum is 18,000 cents, observed mean 9,000 cents = 90 dollars, and zero-filled mean 4,500 cents = 45 dollars. With February in WHERE, A, B and C survive and D disappears; B survives because its month is present despite missing income. For WHERE obs_id='b2' , counts are 1 and 0 and both sum and mean are NULL. For WHERE month='1900-01-01' , counts are 0 and 0 and both sum and mean are NULL. Report coverage and income coverage answer different questions; keeping both observation ID and income makes the distinction visible. January's North mean is 20,000 cents (200 dollars) and South mean is zero. Their equally weighted average is 10,000 cents (100 dollars). Weighting them by observed counts gives [2(20,000) + 1(0)]/3 = 13,333.333333 cents, about 133.333333 dollars. Demonstration-weighted January mean is 100,000/6 cents = 500/3 dollars. February uses only A and C: numerator 1(12,000) + 2(6,000) = 24,000 weighted cents, denominator 1 + 2 = 3, mean 8,000 cents = 80 dollars. Using all roster weight gives 24,000/10 = 2,400 cents = 24 dollars. It leaves the missing B and D incomes contributing zero to the numerator while their weights enter the denominator, equivalent here to zero replacement. Complete-case means remain descriptive of the weighted observed set; no supplied fact identifies the missing incomes. The UTC times are e1: February 1 at 01:30:00; e2: January 31 at 22:30:00; e3: February 29 at 23:59:59; e4: March 1 at 00:00:00; e5: February 1 at 00:00:00. Include e1, e3 and e5, totaling 100 + 300 + 500 = 900 cents (9 dollars). February 2024 contains 29 days. The original local date strings instead select e2, e3 and e5, totaling 1,000 cents. That is a different calendar selection. An interval inclusive of March 1 midnight would incorrectly add e4 and its 400 cents to the UTC result, producing 1,300 cents and potentially counting it again in March. A correct solution states UTC, normalization and both interval boundaries. Each of two left rows matches each of three right rows, giving six joined rows. Each left amount repeats three times, so the sum is 3(100 + 50) = 450 cents instead of 150. Generally, choosing one of m left rows and one of n right rows gives mn pairs. For existing tables, SELECT l.* FROM left_rows AS l WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM right_rows AS r WHERE r.k = l.k) retains each original left row once. If the two amounts are both 50, the correct sum is 100; SUM(DISTINCT amount) returns 50. A grouped total over the multiplied join still equals 450 in the original example. The lab's exercise_7_join uses WITH/VALUES to instantiate the two tiny tables and returns six rows and 450 cents. The roster has four households; six is the number of January–February reports, not households. A balanced target grid has 4 × 2 = 8 household-month cells. Six contain reports; five contain income. Two cells lack reports (D in both months), and one existing report lacks income (B in February). There are three missing-income cells in total. The five recorded incomes sum to 10,000 + 12,000 + 30,000 + 0 + 6,000 = 58,000 cents. Their mean is 58,000/5 = 11,600 cents, or 116 dollars per observed household-month. Filling all three missing cells with zero gives 58,000/8 = 7,250 cents, or 72.50 dollars; the arithmetic is correct for that replacement policy, which is unsupported here. A suitable statement is: “Among eight intended household-month cells for four roster households, six reports contain five measured incomes. Observed incomes total 580 dollars and average 116 dollars across measured cells; the mean over all eight cells is not identified by the supplied records.” A balanced grid of keys does not create balanced measurement coverage. Credit requires all counts, correct units and explicit separation of observed results from the zero assumption. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: draw the rows created by joining two records to three records with the same key. Explain to a colleague why the resulting sum can be wrong without any arithmetic error, why an absent report differs from recorded zero, and why moving a condition from ON to WHERE can change the population. Then demonstrate label alignment on a quantity matrix with three columns. Delayed check: after a week, reconstruct the February join and all three counts without looking at the solution. Change one missing income to a recorded zero and predict which count, numerator and denominator change. Normalize a timestamp crossing a month boundary, state a half-open interval, and rebuild an overall mean from regional sums and counts. The next chapter is visualization and descriptive evidence . Keep this chapter's distinction between records and economic units: a graph cannot repair an incorrectly constructed analysis population."}, {"title": "04.05 · Visualization and descriptive evidence", "url": "v04-c05.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A figure makes a claim through its construction A financial report says that one firm grew faster, a household sample is prosperous and training is associated with higher output. Those statements can be compatible with the first firm's smaller dollar gain, most sampled households earning below the mean, and a negative training–output association within each recorded group. Choosing what to plot is already a choice about the question. This chapter develops the capability to choose and interpret figures that expose evidence. The preceding chapter established units, row grain, keys, joins and denominators. Preserve that contract in a chart. State the observational unit, target population or sample, date/vintage, variable definitions, transformations, exclusions and uncertainty interpretation. All data and statistical models below are original synthetic examples. They are not observations about actual households or firms. Use a distribution plot to investigate a variable's spread, a time plot to study chronological changes, a scatter plot to study paired measurements, and a dot-and-interval plot to compare estimates with their uncertainty. A chart is not a substitute for its underlying table. Supply that table, source and construction method so a reader can recover the numbers and inspect alternative views. Distributions: reveal the observations behind the average A dot plot places observations along a numerical axis. Repeated values can be stacked or counted so that overplotting does not erase frequency. For a large sample, a histogram partitions the numerical axis into bins and summarizes how many observations fall in each. State whether a boundary belongs to its left or right bin. We use left-closed, right-open bins, except that the final right endpoint is included. Every included observation must enter exactly once. If bin j has n j observations, width w j > 0 and total sample size n, its sample share is n j /n. For unequal widths, a density histogram must use height h j = n j /(nw j ). Then rectangle area h j w j equals the share, and total area is one. Heights have inverse measurement units; areas are dimensionless. Density can exceed one when bins use small numerical units. It is not a probability at a single point. Equal-width count histograms are also useful when clearly labeled. With unequal widths, plotting raw counts as heights makes area proportional to count times width and exaggerates broad bins. Bin origin and width can hide clusters or create apparent peaks. Compare reasonable choices and show raw points for small samples. A visually appealing histogram does not prove a population distribution or establish that a flagged observation is erroneous. The NIST histogram reference describes count, relative-frequency and area-normalized forms. Our formulas make the denominator and units explicit. Its display techniques are exploratory tools; selecting a probability law requires evidence beyond a shape that looks familiar. The empirical cumulative distribution function avoids bin choices: F n (x) = n −1 Σ1{X i ≤ x}. It answers the fraction of observed values at or below x. A jump of k/n means k repeated observations there. An ECDF includes no smoothing and says nothing about unobserved tails without further assumptions. With sampling weights, use a stated weighted empirical distribution and its appropriate population interpretation. A box plot compresses a distribution into quantiles and a rule for whiskers or flagged points. Declare the quantile convention; software can interpolate differently. Here the median averages the middle two values when n is even, and Q1/Q3 are medians of the lower and upper halves, omitting a central observation when n is odd. The interquartile range is IQR = Q3 − Q1. A common flagging rule uses fences Q1 − 1.5IQR and Q3 + 1.5IQR, with whiskers at the most extreme observed values inside those fences. This is a descriptive convention, not a 95% confidence interval or an automatic deletion rule. Worked example 1 — one income distribution, several correct summaries Eight synthetic households have annual incomes 10, 12, 15, 18, 20, 22, 25 and 78, in thousand USD. Total income is 200, so the mean is 25. The conventional midpoint median is (18 + 20)/2 = 19. The lower-half median is Q1 = (12 + 15)/2 = 13.5; Q3 = (22 + 25)/2 = 23.5, giving IQR = 10. The fences are −1.5 and 38.5. Thus 78 is flagged; the upper whisker would end at 25. A high genuine income can be economically important and must not be deleted because a rule flags it. Income histogram calculations; the final bin includes its upper endpoint Bin, thousand USD Count Share Width Density height [0, 20) 4 1/2 20 1/40 = 0.025 [20, 40) 3 3/8 20 3/160 = 0.01875 [40, 80] 1 1/8 40 1/320 = 0.003125 Areas represent sample shares. The broad final bin contains one household; its height is reduced to account for its doubled width. All eight original values are listed above. The ECDF at 20 is 5/8 = 62.5%, because equality includes the income of 20. At 19 it is 4/8. The smallest x at which F n (x) ≥ 1/2 is 18; this inverse-ECDF convention chooses a different representative median from the midpoint convention's 19. Both lie in the distribution's median interval [18, 20]. State the convention rather than calling one calculation a software error. The largest observation represents 1/8 of households but 78/200 = 39% of their total income. A frequency plot weights each household equally; a plot of income shares answers a different question. If 78 were removed, the mean would become 122/7 ≈ 17.4286, but that would describe a changed sample. Neither mean 25 nor median 19 identifies the population mean without a sampling argument. In particular, the mean does not describe what every household receives. Time series: levels, rates and bases answer different questions Place time in chronological order with spacing appropriate to elapsed time or explicitly defined periods. A connected line suggests a progression between points; do not draw a confident continuous path across missing dates without labeling the gap or interpolation. Monthly totals, month-end stocks and annualized monthly rates have different aggregation rules. A moving average also changes the object. A centered average uses later observations and cannot be treated as an available real-time forecast feature at its center date. For positive levels y t , an index relative to base period b is I t = 100y t /y b . It removes scale to show relative change from that chosen base. Period growth is g t = y t /y t−1 − 1. Repeated growth compounds: y T /y 0 = ∏(1 + g t ). An index cannot preserve information about absolute size unless the base levels are also reported. Rebasing at a near-zero value creates an unstable comparison; rebasing at zero is undefined. Two synthetic firms: annual nominal revenue in the same currency, no inflation adjustment Year A, USD B, USD A index B index 2021 1,000 10,000 100 100 2022 1,200 11,000 120 110 2023 1,440 12,100 144 121 2024 1,728 13,310 172.8 133.1 The common base compares proportional nominal growth. A gains 72.8%; B gains 33.1%. In dollars, A gains 728 and B gains 3,310. The figure's vertical axis is explicitly cropped and encodes point positions, not bar lengths. A grows 20% each year; B grows 10%. Their three-year changes are 1.2³ − 1 = 72.8% and 1.1³ − 1 = 33.1%. Saying “A grew more” is incomplete unless “more” specifies proportional or absolute change. The plot establishes no change in real quantities: a price index and consistent deflation would be needed for that separate comparison. On a linear scale , equal vertical distances represent equal differences in the measured variable. On a logarithmic scale , equal distances represent equal ratios of positive values: log y 2 − log y 1 = log(y 2 /y 1 ). Constant proportional growth therefore forms a straight line on a semilog time plot. For small g, log(1 + g) is approximately g; it is not exactly g. A 20% rise has natural-log change log 1.2 ≈ 0.182322, or 18.2322 log points when multiplied by 100. A logarithmic axis cannot display zero or negative values. Do not silently drop them or add an arbitrary constant: log(y + c) changes ratios and gives a different interpretation. Keep the original units visible in tick labels and name the transformation. The choice of scale should follow whether the question concerns differences or proportional changes, not which version looks more dramatic. Scales, denominators and visual comparisons Bars encode magnitude through length from a baseline. If values 200 and 220 are drawn from zero, their lengths have ratio 1.1, matching a 10% difference. If drawn from 180, the visible lengths are 20 and 40, with ratio 2. A truncated bar invites the wrong magnitude comparison. Use a zero baseline for magnitude bars, or a clearly labeled point plot when the analytical question concerns small differences near a large level. Negative values can extend in the opposite direction from zero. A line or scatter plot encodes positions rather than filled lengths, so a restricted axis can expose variation legitimately. Label its limits, include the relevant range and use comparable axes for panels meant to be compared. The slope's visual angle depends on the plot's width and height; it is not an economic rate until translated through both axes. Avoid 3D shapes and decorative areas that introduce another scale without another measured quantity. The ONS axes guidance distinguishes zero-based magnitude charts from position-based charts and recommends consistent scales and caution with dual axes. Our scale choices are tied to the statistical questions here; the chapter does not adopt every publication-style preference as a mathematical requirement. Two independently scaled vertical axes can make unrelated series cross, converge or appear equally volatile through choices of offsets and ranges. Prefer separate aligned panels or a justified common index. An intersection between a percentage series on one axis and a dollar series on another has no intrinsic numerical meaning. Even a shared-axis association can be confounded or driven by a common trend. Denominators can reverse a comparison. Suppose one synthetic area reports 40 incidents among 2,000 residents and another 80 among 10,000. The second has twice as many incidents but a lower rate: 8 per 1,000 versus 20 per 1,000. Label whether the chart shows total demand on services or individual exposure risk. Age composition, reporting completeness and repeated incidents per person may require further adjustment; dividing by population does not automatically make areas comparable. Scatter plots: paired observations, not causal arrows A scatter plot places each observed pair (x i , y i ) at its coordinates. The two values must describe the same unit and relevant time. Inspect direction, curvature, clustering, unusual points, changing spread and regions with few observations. Do not connect unrelated workers or firms in row order. For dense data, use transparency, counts or documented aggregation to expose overlap; random jitter is display displacement and must not be mistaken for measured variation. Pearson sample correlation is r = Σ(x i − x̄)(y i − ȳ)/√[Σ(x i − x̄)²Σ(y i − ȳ)²], provided neither variable is constant. It describes standardized linear association. A curved relationship can have correlation zero; one unusual point can change correlation substantially. A group average plot and an individual-observation plot represent different grains. Neither automatically identifies what would happen if one variable were intervened on. The NIST scatter-plot reference describes paired-coordinate displays, nonlinear patterns, changing spread and conditioning plots. We use those diagnostic ideas without treating visual association as causal evidence. An absent overall association also does not rule out causal effects that cancel or are masked by selection. Worked example 2 — a pooled pattern reverses within groups Six synthetic workers have training hours x and output units y. A recorded starting-condition category labels three workers A and three B. All observations; one worker per row Group Training hours x Output units y A 1 8 A 2 7 A 3 6 B 7 18 B 8 17 B 9 16 Shape and color identify the recorded groups. A pooled association mixes within-group and between-group variation; neither association is a training effect without an identification argument. The pooled means are x̄ = 5 and ȳ = 12. Centered sums are S xx = 58, S yy = 154 and S xy = 86. Thus r = 86/√8932 ≈ 0.909964, a strong positive pooled linear association. Within either group, each extra hour corresponds to one fewer output unit in these observations, with centered sums S xx = S yy = 2 and S xy = −2, giving correlation −1. The reversal can be decomposed exactly. Write x̄ g , ȳ g for group means and n g for group counts. Expanding deviations around group means, and noting that centered within-group deviations sum to zero, gives Σ i (x i − x̄)(y i − ȳ) = Σ g Σ i∈g (x i − x̄ g )(y i − ȳ g ) + Σ g n g (x̄ g − x̄)(ȳ g − ȳ). Group means are (2,7) and (8,17). Within-group crossproducts sum to −4; the between-group term is 3(−3)(−5) + 3(3)(5) = 90. Their sum is 86. The plot's apparent positive pattern largely reflects separation between groups. Group-specific displays reveal this structure. They do not by themselves prove that controlling for the label identifies a causal effect; that requires the assumptions introduced in Chapter 8 . Uncertainty: name what the interval describes A distribution of individual outcomes, a standard error of an estimated mean, a confidence interval for a population parameter, a predictive interval for a new observation and a scenario range are distinct objects. A shaded band with no definition is ambiguous. Label the target, interval method and level, whether bands are pointwise or simultaneous, and any assumptions about dependence or modeling. A sample's standard deviation does not equal the standard error of its mean; for iid observations with finite variance, the latter scales as σ/√n. A frequentist 95% confidence procedure covers the fixed target in 95% of repetitions under its assumptions; it is not a posterior probability statement about that target conditional on the observed interval. A pointwise confidence band gives the stated coverage at each preselected location, not necessarily simultaneous coverage of an entire path. Repeatedly inspecting many times, groups or outcomes changes the relevant multiple-comparison question. Refer back to confidence and testing before interpreting a highlighted crossing. Consider two independent normally distributed unbiased mean estimators with known standard errors 4 USD each. Their observed means are A = 100 and B = 112 USD. Using 1.96 as an approximate 95% normal critical value, the marginal intervals are 100 ± 7.84 = [92.16, 107.84] and 112 ± 7.84 = [104.16, 119.84]. These intervals overlap. These are marginal parameter intervals under a stated normal model. To compare means, calculate uncertainty for their difference directly instead of inspecting overlap alone. Under independence, D̂ = B̂ − Â has standard error √(4² + 4²) = √32 ≈ 5.656854. Its interval is 12 ± 1.96√32 ≈ [0.912566, 23.087434], which excludes zero. Overlapping individual 95% intervals therefore do not imply that the difference's corresponding interval includes zero. Conversely, plot styling cannot replace assumptions about the estimators' joint distribution. With covariance c between estimators, Var(B̂ − Â) = Var(B̂) + Var(Â) − 2c. Positive pairing can make the difference more precise even when marginal intervals are unchanged. Negative dependence can make it less precise. For a jointly normal pair with correlation −0.75 here, c = −12 USD² and the difference SE becomes √56 ≈ 7.483315; its interval includes zero. A graph containing only separate marginal intervals omits information needed for that comparison. The ONS uncertainty-display guidance provides examples of interval and band displays with explanatory annotations. Here, the statistical interpretation follows the explicit model and contrast calculation above; visual overlap and shorthand probability language are not used as inferential rules. Publish a chart that another analyst can reconstruct Use a title that names the quantity and population, axis labels with units and transformations, a stated period/base/vintage and a caption explaining exclusions or unusual observations. Use direct labels or a clear legend. Distinguish groups through shape or line style as well as color, maintain sufficient contrast, and supply a text description and underlying values. Do not rely on a hover interaction to disclose essential units. The four figures here are static vector files accompanied by all their observations or sufficient summary inputs. Download the synthetic inputs and metadata , standard-library Python calculation/figure program and README . Run python3 v04-c05-charts.py for the numerical report. To regenerate editable SVG figures in a separate folder, run python3 v04-c05-charts.py --write-figures figures . No external package, server or data feed is needed. The program is deliberately a small fixed-fixture laboratory; it does not validate arbitrary external datasets. Its figure descriptions, base-year labels and axis limits are tailored to these inputs. When changing a bin scheme, base year or dataset, update the figure function, labels, descriptions and limits together, then verify coordinates and coverage again; a changed input file alone does not make every annotation correct. Before release, reconcile plotted counts and sums to the input table, check one coordinate by hand, verify missing values and endpoints, and compare conclusions under another defensible scale or aggregation. Separate a choice made before seeing the result from a choice selected afterward to emphasize it. A visual pattern can motivate a model or investigation; it cannot manufacture a counterfactual or repair a selected sample. Exercises For observed values [0,0,2,4,4,8,8,14], compute mean, midpoint median, Q1/Q3 under this chapter's convention, IQR and ECDF at 4. Use bins [0,4), [4,8), [8,16] to calculate counts, shares and density heights. Show that the histogram's total area is one. What does an observed zero contribute? Using Worked example 1, suppose the unequal-width histogram uses sample shares as heights. Compute each rectangle's area and the total. After normalizing those areas, what apparent mass does the final bin receive? Contrast it with the actual fraction of households there. Explain why deleting 78 solely because it exceeds the upper fence is not justified. Rebase both revenue series to 2022 = 100. Compute their 2021 and 2024 indexes. Compare their 2022–2024 percentage changes and dollar changes. Which facts can an index chart alone recover, and what information does it remove? A report draws bars for 200 and 220 from baseline 180 and labels the result “twice as large.” Compute the actual relative increase and the ratio of visible bar lengths. Separately, compute the two incident rates from 40/2,000 and 80/10,000 per 1,000 people. Propose suitable displays for an absolute resource-allocation question and an exposure-rate question. Reconstruct S xy = −4 + 90 in Worked example 2. Then consider x = [−2,−1,0,1,2] and y = [4,1,0,1,4]. Compute the centered crossproduct and correlation. What would the scatter plot reveal that the correlation conceals? Why would neither example identify a causal effect? Reproduce the marginal intervals and the difference interval in the uncertainty example. Now assume the same observed means and marginal standard errors but a joint normal correlation of −0.75. Calculate covariance, difference variance, standard error and the new interval. Explain why the marginal-interval graphic alone cannot settle the difference question. A monthly series reports January 100, February missing and March 121. A dashboard joins the points with a straight line, labels an arithmetic interpolated February value “observed,” and reports 10% measured growth in each month. Diagnose the claims, compute linear interpolation and the different February value implied by constant proportional growth, and give an honest plotting convention. A chart compares wage means across countries without currency/price conversion, clips its largest observations without disclosure, overlays employment counts on an independently rescaled second axis, and labels an unexplained ribbon “95% of future outcomes.” Write a concrete reconstruction plan covering unit/grain, exclusions, scales, interval target and reproducible delivery. State two conclusions that even a repaired chart cannot establish by itself. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria Total 40 over 8 observations gives mean 5. Middle values 4 and 4 give median 4. Lower half [0,0,2,4] has median 1; upper half [4,8,8,14] has median 8, giving IQR 7. F n (4) = 5/8. Bin counts are 3,2,3, so shares are 3/8,1/4,3/8 and heights are 3/32,1/16,3/64 per measurement unit. Areas are (3/32)4 = 3/8, (1/16)4 = 1/4 and (3/64)8 = 3/8, summing to 1. Each zero is a real observation contributing to n, the first bin and the ECDF; its zero numerical contribution to the total does not make it missing. Using heights 1/2,3/8,1/8 gives areas 10,7.5,5, summing to 22.5. The final rectangle receives 5/22.5 = 2/9 ≈22.2222% of the visible area, versus its actual share 1/8 =12.5%. The error arises because a width 40 bin gets twice the area per unit share of a width 20 bin. The proper density heights restore area-as-share. An outlier flag is a diagnostic; verify units, transcription, population and source. If 78 is genuine, retain it or explicitly define a different target/sample and report the sensitivity rather than silently deleting it. For A, the 2021 index is 100(1000/1200) = 250/3 ≈83.3333; its 2024 index is 100(1728/1200) =144. For B they are 100(10000/11000) =1000/11 ≈90.9091 and 100(13310/11000) =121. Thus 2022–2024 gains are 44% and 21%, but dollar gains are 528 and 2,310. The index preserves relative changes and timing within each series. It loses the absolute base levels, so the graph alone cannot recover dollar gains or compare firm size. Both remain nominal unless price adjustment is supplied. The actual increase is (220−200)/200 =10%; lengths from 180 have ratio 40/20 =2. A zero-based bar chart represents magnitudes correctly; a labeled point plot can emphasize the 20-unit difference without presenting truncated lengths as levels. The incident rates are 20 and 8 per 1,000. A chart of 40 versus 80 counts addresses total recorded events relevant to service demand, while a rate plot addresses events relative to population exposure. State the population, period, repeated-event convention and comparability limits; neither denominator choice answers every policy question. Each group contributes −2, so the within sum is −4. Group mean deviations are (−3,−5) and (3,5), each with n=3; between sum is 45+45 =90. Total 86 yields the positive pooled correlation. In the curved example x̄=0 and ȳ=2. The crossproducts are −4,+1,0,−1,+4, summing to 0; both variables have positive variance, so r=0. Yet y=x² exactly: the plot reveals a U-shaped deterministic association. Zero linear correlation is not independence. Neither observational pattern supplies treatment assignment, counterfactual outcomes or a credible identification assumption; selection or another mechanism can produce the same pattern. Marginal half-width is 1.96(4)=7.84, giving [92.16,107.84] and [104.16,119.84]. Under independence, difference 12 has variance 32, SE√32≈5.656854 and interval [0.912566,23.087434]. Under correlation −0.75, covariance is −0.75(4)(4)=−12 USD²; variance is 16+16−2(−12)=56 USD². SE√56≈7.483315 and half-width≈14.667297, so the interval is [−2.667297,26.667297], including zero. The marginal distributions and their plotted intervals are unchanged. The comparison depends on covariance and the joint model, information absent from two isolated error bars. Arithmetic midpoint interpolation gives (100+121)/2 =110.5, implying changes 10.5% and 121/110.5−1 =21/221≈9.5023%. Constant proportional growth instead requires 100(1+g)² =121, so g=10% and the implied February level is 110. Neither interpolated value was measured. Keep a gap or distinguish the interpolated segment/point with a caption identifying its assumption. The observed two-month change is 21%; its constant monthly equivalent is 10%, which differs from a claim that each month's actual growth was observed. Specify whose wages are measured, the time period and sampling/weighting/missingness rules; identify currency, nominal/real price base and the conversion appropriate to the intended comparison. Preserve the full inputs, audit exceptional values and disclose justified exclusions and sensitivity. Use separate aligned panels or a justified index instead of implying meaning from dual-axis crossings. Define whether the ribbon targets a mean, parameter, future individual outcome or scenario range; recompute it with an appropriate method, dependence assumptions and coverage level. Provide frozen data or authorized access, dictionary, transformations, code, caption and readable text/table alternatives. A satisfactory answer gives concrete repairs for each defect. Even a corrected display cannot alone identify a causal policy effect or prove population representativeness; it also cannot guarantee a future outcome or validate an unsupported exchange-rate/price comparison. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why histogram area needs a width denominator, why a common index hides firm size, why pooled and within-group patterns differ, and why two overlapping 95% intervals do not constitute a test of their difference. Use the actual numbers rather than only slogans. Delayed check: after a week, rebuild one figure from its supplied data, verify a plotted coordinate and a denominator, then change a defensible bin width or base year. State which conclusions change and which remain true. Recreate the covariance calculation for a difference and label an interval's target without looking at the text. The next chapter, simulation and Monte Carlo , distinguishes randomness in an economic model from numerical sampling error when estimating its implications. The same obligation remains: a plotted result must identify its units, population, uncertainty and construction."}, {"title": "04.06 · Simulation and Monte Carlo", "url": "v04-c06.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Specify the question before generating random numbers Capability: estimate a model quantity by simulation, calculate its Monte Carlo uncertainty, and design a reproducible comparison that uses computation efficiently. Prerequisite: visualization and descriptive evidence . We also use the expectation, covariance, laws of large numbers and confidence procedures from moments , large-sample reasoning and confidence and testing , together with the small Python programs of Chapter 3 . All economic numbers here are original synthetic assumptions. A retailer can calculate profit for any known demand, but tomorrow's demand is uncertain. A simulator is an algorithm that generates an artificial outcome according to a specified model. Monte Carlo estimation averages randomly generated outcomes to approximate a quantity such as expected profit or a loss probability. Simulation makes a difficult calculation possible; it does not establish that the modeled demand law describes the retailer. Write the target as μ = E θ [g(X; a)]. Here X is a random scenario, θ specifies its distribution and other model inputs, a is a fixed action, and g converts the scenario and action into the measured outcome. For example, X is a day's demand, a is inventory ordered before demand is known, and g is profit in USD per day. An estimand is this precisely defined target. A replication is one complete independent draw of the scenario needed to calculate one outcome. If a scenario is an entire correlated year of daily demand, the year is the replication; its days are not automatically independent observations. The design must state the decision timing, distribution, dependencies, units, output, simulation budget and comparison rule. Draw the scenario first, then apply a fixed policy to it. A decision rule may respond to information available at its decision time, but choosing inventory after seeing that day's future demand would give the simulator information the retailer does not have. If a small model permits exact summation, calculate it. Exact answers are valuable benchmarks; a complicated simulation is not a virtue by itself. Why the average works, and what its error bar measures Let Yᵢ = g(Xᵢ; a), for i = 1, …, n, be independent, identically distributed (iid) replication outcomes, with finite mean μ and variance σ². The estimator is μ̂ = (1/n)ΣYᵢ. Linearity gives E[μ̂] = (1/n)ΣE[Yᵢ] = μ: the estimate is unbiased across hypothetical repetitions of the sampling procedure. A single run can still be above or below μ. Expanding the variance gives Var(μ̂) = n −2 [ΣVar(Yᵢ) + 2Σ i<j Cov(Yᵢ,Yⱼ)] = σ²/n. The second equality uses independence to remove cross terms. The Monte Carlo standard error (MCSE) is the standard deviation of this estimator, σ/√n; it has the same units as Y. The dispersion of individual profits is σ, not σ/√n. A narrow interval for expected profit can coexist with a large probability of losing money on an individual day. Usually σ² is unknown. For n ≥ 2, calculate s² = Σ(Yᵢ − μ̂)²/(n − 1) and estimated MCSE = s/√n. The identity Σ(Yᵢ − μ̂)² = Σ(Yᵢ − μ)² − n(μ̂ − μ)² explains the denominator: its expectation is nσ² − n(σ²/n) = (n − 1)σ². Thus s²/n is unbiased for Var(μ̂); its square root need not be unbiased for the MCSE. The iid finite-variance assumptions justify these identities, not merely the fact that a program printed n rows. If 0 < σ² < ∞, the iid central limit theorem gives √n(μ̂ − μ)/σ approaching a standard normal distribution. Also s² approaches σ² in probability, because averaging Y and Y² is valid under the finite second moment. Replacing σ by s therefore gives the familiar approximate 95% Monte Carlo interval μ̂ ± 1.96s/√n. Its coverage refers to repeated simulations of this fixed model. It is not an exact finite-sample statement for arbitrary profits, a prediction interval for a future profit, or a confidence interval for an unknown demand model estimated from real data. Rare events and highly skewed outcomes can make the approximation poor at the available n. The law of large numbers needs only an integrable outcome, E|Y| < ∞, but the variance/standard-error formulas and this CLT need finite variance. A heavy-tailed payoff can satisfy the first condition and fail the second. If σ² = 0 is established analytically, every modeled outcome equals μ almost surely, so no averaging is needed. Seeing s = 0 in a finite sample does not establish that condition. For dependent outcomes, retain the covariance terms; dividing a row standard deviation by √n can badly understate uncertainty. For a desired approximate interval half-width h in the outcome's units, a planning calculation is n ≥ (1.96σ/h)², rounded up. A separate pilot can estimate σ for a prespecified final run, but its estimate is uncertain. Halving h requires about four times as many replications. Repeatedly checking an ordinary interval and stopping the first time its lower endpoint becomes positive changes the reporting procedure and does not inherit fixed-n 95% coverage. Precommit to n for this chapter's intervals; sequentially valid procedures require additional theory. Worked example 1: profit dispersion versus numerical uncertainty A synthetic service earns either 10 or 30 USD per day, each with probability 1/2. Directly, μ = 20 USD and σ² = [(10 − 20)² + (30 − 20)²]/2 = 100 USD². A run with n = 100 independent simulated days contains 55 high-profit days and 45 low-profit days. Its average is [55(30) + 45(10)]/100 = 21 USD. The sum of squared deviations from 21 is 55(9²) + 45(−11)² = 9,900 USD². Therefore s² = 9,900/99 = 100 USD², and estimated MCSE = √(100/100) = 1 USD. The approximate 95% Monte Carlo interval is [19.04, 22.96] USD. This particular interval includes the exact model expectation 20. The simulated estimate's error is +1 USD; knowing the exact answer lets us measure that error here. In a large model, the estimated MCSE describes typical error without revealing its realized sign. The future daily outcome remains either 10 or 30. Its standard deviation is 10 USD, ten times the MCSE of the 100-day average. To plan an approximate half-width of 0.50 USD using the known model variance, n ≥ (1.96 × 10/0.50)² = 1,536.64, hence n = 1,537. At n = 10,000 the true MCSE is 0.10 USD. Neither larger run changes the economic risk on one day or validates the assumed 50–50 demand environment. Separate Monte Carlo error from errors in the model Let μ real denote the real quantity one hopes to learn about, μ model the stipulated model's exact expectation, and μ code the expectation under the implemented algorithm. Then a useful accounting identity is μ̂ − μ real = (μ̂ − μ code ) + (μ code − μ model ) + (μ model − μ real ). The terms represent Monte Carlo error, implementation/numerical approximation, and model/input error. More replications address the first term. They do not repair a missing cost, an incorrect correlation, a payoff bug, a coarse time discretization, or a distribution estimated from an unrepresentative sample. A parameter estimated from data creates another uncertainty problem. One may simulate over that uncertainty only after specifying a justified sampling or posterior procedure; adding arbitrary noise to parameters is not a substitute. Validate a simulation in layers: reconcile deterministic payoffs by hand; test extreme and zero cases; compare with exact enumeration in a smaller model; inspect simulated frequencies and outcome distributions; then assess precision. A histogram reveals outcome shape, while a trace of successive running means reveals numerical movement. Neither proves convergence. Report the final prespecified estimate and MCSE; do not select the seed or last portion of a trace that supports the desired decision. For a rare-event probability p, take Yᵢ = 1 when the event occurs and 0 otherwise. Then p̂ is the event count divided by n and Var(p̂) = p(1 − p)/n. With zero observed events, the estimated sample variance is zero, but a rare event can easily have been missed. Under iid Bernoulli sampling, P(zero events) = (1 − p)ⁿ. Inverting that probability at 0.05 gives the exact one-sided 95% upper confidence bound p U = 1 − 0.05 1/n . At n = 1,000 it is about 0.00299125, or 0.2991%, despite p̂ = 0. This bounds a probability in the specified simulator, not an unmodeled real-world disaster rate. See the binomial interval derivation for coverage and the distinct two-sided procedure. Random seeds record a computation, not additional evidence A pseudorandom generator uses a deterministic state update to produce values with designed statistical properties. A seed initializes that state. The iid analysis above describes the ideal sampling experiment that the generator approximates; conditional on a fully specified seed and program there is no remaining randomness in its output. A reproducible run is still only one run, and many copies of it are not independent replications. Use a local generator object, created once before the loop. This isolates its state from unrelated random calls elsewhere in the program. Here random() supplies a number in [0,1); intervals of lengths 0.25, 0.50 and 0.25 generate demands of 60, 90 and 120. This is the inverse-CDF method for a discrete distribution: choose the first cumulative probability strictly greater than the draw. The final return covers the remaining interval. import random def demand_from_uniform(u): if u < 0.25: return 60 if u < 0.75: return 90 return 120 rng = random.Random(20261006) demands = [] for _ in range(5): demands.append(demand_from_uniform(rng.random())) The fragment assumes a valid uniform argument; the downloadable program validates inputs and uses the supplied weights. In a continuous ideal model an exact threshold has probability zero, but computer draws lie on a finite grid, so endpoint conventions still belong in the code. Python's base generator uses 53-bit float precision. Our quarter-probability boundaries align with that grid; arbitrary rational weights may have tiny sampling-grid/rounding discrepancies relative to their exact mathematical probabilities. The official Python random reference documents separate Random instances, generator state, and the narrow reproducibility guarantee for random() with compatible seeding. Other distribution routines can change across Python versions. Record the interpreter/version, generator, seed, inputs, program and random-call order, not just the seed. Inserting one draw before the loop shifts every later draw. Resetting the seed inside each iteration repeats the first scenario. Changing a policy so it consumes extra random numbers can break alignment with the comparison policy. Our benchmark generates all required scenario draws in an explicit order and makes the payoff function deterministic. Its reported runtime and draw order make this design reviewable. Different seed numbers alone are not a mathematical proof of independent streams; parallel generators need an appropriate documented stream design. This chapter uses a single serial generator and no parallel execution. Reduce the variance of the quantity the decision needs Suppose the decision depends on δ = E[B] − E[A], where A and B are payoffs under two policies. One approach simulates independent scenarios for A and B; with n outcomes per policy the variance of their difference of means is (σ² A + σ² B )/n. Common random numbers instead feed the same scenario Xᵢ to both policies, and average Dᵢ = B(Xᵢ) − A(Xᵢ). Each policy retains its correct marginal distribution, so E[Dᵢ] = δ. Across independent scenarios, Var(D̄) = [σ² A + σ² B − 2Cov(A,B)]/n; estimated MCSE = s D /√n. Positive covariance lowers variance because favorable and unfavorable environments move both policies together and cancel in their difference. Negative covariance makes this method worse. Dependence within each pair is intentional; independent pairs are the replication units. Compute the sample variance of the differences, including their covariance implicitly. Adding the two marginal standard-error squares would discard the purpose of pairing. Compare methods at equal total payoff evaluations or measured computational cost, not merely equal labels such as “runs.” Art Owen's author-hosted variance-reduction chapter , sections 8.2 and 8.6, provides further study of antithetic sampling and synchronized common random numbers. The inventory values, proofs by direct expansion, exercises and benchmark below are original to this library. Worked example 2: an inventory decision with an exact benchmark Demand D is 60, 90 or 120 units with probabilities 1/4, 1/2 and 1/4. Stock q is bought before demand is revealed. Each unit sells for 12 USD, costs 6 USD and, if unsold, earns salvage of 2 USD at the day's end. There are no other costs, demand responses, carryover inventory or financing effects in this model. Profit is π(q,D) = 12min(q,D) + 2max(q − D,0) − 6q. Policy A stocks 80 units; policy B stocks 100. Demand units Probability A profit, USD B profit, USD B − A, USD 60 1/4 280 200 −80 90 1/2 480 500 20 120 1/4 480 600 120 At D = 60, A earns 12(60) + 2(20) − 6(80) = 280 USD; B's additional twenty leftover units lose 4 USD each, explaining the −80 difference. At high demand all additional units sell and each contributes 6 USD, explaining +120. Summation gives E[A] = 430, E[B] = 450 and δ = 20 USD per day. B has lower profit than A in one quarter of scenarios, so a positive mean difference does not mean that B dominates in every state. Centered squared outcomes give Var(A) = 7,500 and Var(B) = 22,500 USD². For Dᵢ, the deviations from 20 are −100, 0 and 100, so Var(Dᵢ) = 5,000 USD². Equivalently, Cov(A,B) = 12,500 USD² and 7,500 + 22,500 − 2(12,500) = 5,000. Independent scenario comparison has replication variance 30,000 USD². At n = 10,000 comparisons, true MCSEs are √(5,000/10,000) ≈ 0.707107 USD paired and √(30,000/10,000) ≈ 1.732051 USD independent. Pairing cuts variance by a factor of six at the same two payoff evaluations per comparison; the standard error falls by √6. Download the benchmark program , synthetic inputs and run instructions and project rubric . Keep the program and inputs together and run: python3 v04-c06-inventory.py The input uses integer cents per unit, integer demand/stock quantities and integer probability weights. The program forms exact rational reference moments with fractions.Fraction . Simulation summaries use the sample mean and sample variance. Its central paired calculation is simply: paired.append(profit_cents(shared, case[\"stock_b_units\"], case) - profit_cents(shared, case[\"stock_a_units\"], case)) This line belongs inside the complete program's loop; shared is its freshly drawn demand. Each iteration consumes three uniforms in this fixed order: shared demand for the paired comparison, separate demand for independent A, then separate demand for independent B. Thus the two methods' replication counts and payoff budgets match. Their results may differ in either direction; variance reduction is a repeated-run property, not a promise that the paired realization is always closer. With seed 20261006 and 10,000 replications on the recorded CPython 3.14.6 runtime, shared counts are 2,423, 5,029 and 2,548 for demands 60, 90 and 120. The paired mean is 21.25 USD, estimated MCSE ≈ 0.704978 USD and approximate interval [19.868244, 22.631756] USD. The independent mean is 21.12 USD, estimated MCSE ≈ 1.716149 USD and approximate interval [17.756347, 24.483653] USD. Here the independent realization is closer to the exact 20 despite its larger theoretical variance. Report the full design rather than selecting whichever estimate looks preferable. The program emits cents; divide means, MCSEs and endpoints by 100, and variances by 10,000, to obtain the displayed dollar units. The official statistics reference documents variance 's n − 1 sample calculation and its need for at least two values. The code applies it to replication differences, whose independence is a design assumption rather than something the function can test. The JSON reader rejects booleans, quoted/fractional numeric inputs, nonfinite constants, duplicate fields, incorrect units, unsorted demand support, negative weights and zero total weight. Stock and demand are bounded by one million units, money rates by one million cents per unit, support by twenty states and n by 200,000; these are explicit teaching-tool bounds. It requires salvage ≤ cost ≤ price. It reads relative to the script, does not modify the case and prints an error with unsuccessful exit status rather than a fabricated numeric result. The README specifies how to reproduce the run and vary assumptions. Expected-profit maximization selects B in this two-policy model; cash requirements, risk preferences and demand misspecification could change a real decision. Antithetic pairs: dependence that helps an average For another variance-reduction design, let U be uniform on [0,1] in the ideal continuous model and Y = f(U). Then 1 − U has the same distribution. An antithetic pair evaluates both and averages Z = [f(U) + f(1 − U)]/2. This preserves E[Z] = μ. With finite variance σ² = Var(f(U)) and c = Cov(f(U),f(1 − U)), expanding gives Var(Z) = (σ² + c)/2. From m independent U draws, average the m pair means; its variance is (σ² + c)/(2m). The comparable ordinary estimator uses 2m independent evaluations and has variance σ²/(2m). Negative c helps; positive c hurts. Estimate uncertainty using the m pair means, not by pretending the 2m dependent payoffs are independent. If f is monotone in this one-dimensional uniform input, f(U) and f(1 − U) move in opposite directions. A direct justification uses independent U and V: twice their covariance equals E[(f(U) − f(V))(f(1 − U) − f(1 − V))]. The two factors have opposite signs, so this expectation is nonpositive. The argument also states the limitation: a nonmonotone payoff has no such guarantee. For f(u) = 100 + 40u USD, each pair averages exactly 120 USD, eliminating simulation error for this linear target. For f(u) = (u − 1/2)², reflection gives the identical payoff, so each pair merely duplicates one evaluation and has twice the variance of two independent evaluations. Zero or reduced Monte Carlo error still leaves the economic model unvalidated. For the ideal formula endpoints have probability zero. A finite generator can produce zero, making its reflected partner exactly one; ensure the payoff accepts that endpoint or use a finite-grid pairing that preserves the intended law. Do not silently pass one to a sampler whose contract is [0,1). The inventory download uses common random numbers only. Antithetic sampling here is taught through analytic examples, not hidden inside that code. Exercises A simulation reports the expected present value of a project's cash flows conditional on a fixed demand law and discount rate. Define its estimand and replication if each outcome is a full five-year path. Classify an incorrect discount exponent, an estimated demand parameter and a different estimate after changing the seed. Explain why treating its five correlated annual cash flows as five independent replications can give a wrong MCSE. For iid outcomes of mean μ and finite variance σ², derive unbiasedness of the sample mean and its variance. Show why the sample variance uses n − 1. A run has n = 400, mean 72 USD and sample standard deviation 30 USD. Compute its MCSE and approximate 95% Monte Carlo interval, then plan a run with approximate half-width at most 1 USD using 30 as the planning standard deviation. State the limits of that plan. Redo Worked example 1 for 60 high-profit and 40 low-profit days. Calculate the mean, sum of squared deviations, unbiased sample variance, MCSE and approximate interval. Compare the realized estimation error with the MCSE. Explain why the interval should not be presented as the range containing 95% of daily profits. An iid simulation records zero failures in 2,000 trials. Derive and calculate the one-sided 95% upper probability bound. If the model's actual failure probability were 0.001, what is the probability of seeing zero? Explain why reporting “no failures are possible” or a zero-width normal interval is unjustified. Using Worked example 2, independently compute covariance by E[AB] − E[A]E[B], paired variance and the theoretical MCSE for n = 2,500. How many independent comparisons match that variance? Then suppose an additional daily fee of 25 USD applies only to B, regardless of demand. Calculate the new expected difference, paired variance and expected-profit choice. Write a short loop that draws five demands using one locally seeded generator and demand_from_uniform . Explain three different errors: creating a newly seeded generator inside every iteration; replaying the identical seed/run fifty times and treating those estimates as independent; choosing the best result among twenty seeds. Explain why recording the seed alone does not fix a changed random-call order. Let f(U) = 1{U < 0.2}, with continuous uniform U. Use antithetic pairs Z = [f(U) + f(1 − U)]/2 to estimate the event probability. Find the distribution, mean and variance of Z, and compare the variance of the average of m pairs with 2m independent indicators. State the correct replication unit and estimated MCSE. Explain the result for f(U) = (U − 1/2)². Run the inventory project, preserve the report, and audit its exact and simulated results. Propose a sensitivity case and a design for checking it. Your memo must distinguish model uncertainty from Monte Carlo error, specify the random-call and budget rules, explain a useful exact/edge-case test, and identify a decision claim that the simulation cannot establish. Explain why two matching seeded runs and a confidence interval that contains the exact answer are insufficient to validate all code or the model. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria The target is the expected discounted sum of the five-year path's cash flows under the stipulated joint path law and rate, with today/future timing and money units explicitly stated. A replication is one complete independent five-year path and its scalar present value. A wrong exponent is implementation error; an estimated demand parameter creates input uncertainty relative to the unknown real environment; a changed seed changes Monte Carlo error. Dependence among a path's annual cash flows contributes covariance terms to the variance of its discounted sum. Five rows per path do not create five independent path outcomes. A complete answer also preserves within-path dependence in the simulator and states when investment decisions occur. Linearity gives E[n −1 ΣYᵢ] = μ. Independence and finite variance give Var(n −1 ΣYᵢ) = n −2 nσ² = σ²/n. The sum-of-squares identity in the chapter has expectation (n − 1)σ², so division by n − 1 gives an unbiased variance estimate for n ≥ 2. MCSE = 30/√400 = 1.5 USD; the approximate interval is 72 ± 2.94 = [69.06, 74.94] USD. Planned n ≥ (1.96 × 30/1)² = 3,457.44, so use at least 3,458 independent replications. The pilot standard deviation may misestimate σ, normal coverage is approximate and can be poor for skewed/rare outcomes, and this budget does not handle model uncertainty or arbitrary repeated stopping. The interval estimates a model mean, not an individual outcome. The mean is [60(30) + 40(10)]/100 = 22 USD. Squared deviations total 60(8²) + 40(−12)² = 9,600 USD². Thus s² = 9,600/99 ≈ 96.969697 USD², estimated MCSE = √(96.969697/100) ≈ 0.984732 USD, and the approximate interval is [20.069925, 23.930075] USD. It misses the exact mean 20. The realized error +2 USD is about 2.031010 estimated standard errors. An approximate 95% procedure can miss on some runs; this one miss is not evidence that the true model expectation changed. Daily profits still take only 10 or 30, neither inside this interval. That demonstrates the distinction between an interval for an expectation and a range for daily outcomes. With zero observed successes, P p (K = 0) = (1 − p) 2000 . Solving (1 − p U ) 2000 = 0.05 gives p U = 1 − 0.05 1/2000 ≈ 0.001496745, or 0.1496745%. If p = 0.001, the zero-count probability is 0.999 2000 ≈ 0.135200, about 13.52%. Missing all rare events is therefore plausible. The normal plug-in estimate s = 0 reflects a constant observed sample; it cannot prove constant population outcomes. This is a one-sided bound for the specified iid simulator; a two-sided equal-tail 95% procedure would use 0.025 rather than 0.05 in this endpoint calculation. E[AB] = (1/4)(280)(200) + (1/2)(480)(500) + (1/4)(480)(600) = 206,000 USD². Subtracting 430 × 450 = 193,500 gives covariance 12,500 USD². Paired variance is 7,500 + 22,500 − 25,000 = 5,000 USD². At 2,500 pairs, Var(D̄) = 2 USD² and MCSE = √2 ≈ 1.414214 USD. Independent comparison requires n satisfying 30,000/n = 2, hence 15,000 comparisons, each with two payoff evaluations. The fee shifts every difference by −25, changing its mean to −5 USD but leaving variance 5,000 USD² and paired MCSE unchanged. Expected-profit maximization now prefers A within the two-policy model. This is a modeled incremental fee, not an inference about actual market charges. Using the chapter's sampler, a correct loop is: import random rng = random.Random(20261006) five_demands = [] for _ in range(5): five_demands.append(demand_from_uniform(rng.random())) Creating random.Random(20261006) inside the loop restarts the stream and repeats its first draw, violating the intended independent-scenario design. Identical whole-run replays are perfectly dependent copies, so their average has no reduced simulation error. Selecting the best of twenty seeds reports a selection-biased estimate and invalidates the unadjusted single-run interval as a description of that procedure. One seed fixes an initial state, but an extra earlier call consumes a value and shifts later assignments; record the code, interpreter, inputs, generator, seed and draw order. A complete answer distinguishes reproducible replay from additional statistical evidence. The first indicator is one on U < 0.2; the second is one on U > 0.8. These intervals are disjoint and total length 0.4. Thus Z = 1/2 with probability 0.4 and Z = 0 with probability 0.6. E[Z] = 0.2 and Var(Z) = (1/4)(0.4) − 0.2² = 0.06. The mean of m independent pair means has variance 0.06/m; the ordinary average of 2m independent indicators has variance 0.2(0.8)/(2m) = 0.08/m. Antithetic pairing cuts variance by 25%, a factor of 4/3 improvement at equal evaluation count. Use the m pair means as observations and estimated MCSE s Z /√m for m ≥ 2. For the symmetric quadratic payoff, f(1 − U) = f(U), so Z equals a single original payoff. Its pair-average variance is σ²/m, versus σ²/(2m) for the ordinary method, twice as large. The antithetic label does not guarantee negative payoff covariance. A satisfactory submission reproduces the supplied counts and estimates in the recorded environment, reconciles the exact mean difference of 2,000 cents and paired variance of 50,000,000 cents², and converts units correctly. The seed's paired estimate is farther from the exact answer than its independent estimate, even though pairing has six times lower theoretical variance; recognize both facts. One defensible sensitivity changes demand weights from [1,2,1] to [2,1,1]. The new expected difference is [2(−80) + 20 + 120]/4 = −5 USD, reversing the expected-profit ranking without any code error. Its paired variance is [2(−75)² + 25² + 125²]/4 = 6,875 USD². Keep a prespecified n and documented serial draw order, rerun the exact reference and simulation, and report the sampling interval conditional on each case. Using the same uniforms across sensitivity cases can make their difference more precise if it induces positive covariance; correct marginal weights must still be preserved. A useful edge test sets A = B, for which every paired difference is exactly zero regardless of demand, while independent differences generally need not be zero. Another sets all weight on one state and verifies its deterministic payoff. Two seeded replays test determinism, not economic validity or full code correctness. One interval containing the exact answer is a weak coverage observation, not a proof. Grade the memo on correct target/units, reproducibility, exact checks, uncertainty distinctions, and a qualified decision statement. Real demand validity, financing feasibility, preferences and causal effects of changing inventory require evidence outside this simulation. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: use the inventory table to explain why profit varies much more than the estimate of expected profit, why a seed enables replay without validating the model, and why dependent policies can yield independent paired replications. Derive the covariance term and identify the unit used in the standard-error calculation. Explain to a decision maker exactly what a reported Monte Carlo interval leaves uncertain. Delayed check: after a week, derive Var(μ̂), the sample-variance correction and the rare-event zero-count upper bound without notes. Then change only the inventory demand weights to [2,1,1], derive expected difference −5 USD by enumeration and reproduce it approximately with a prespecified simulation. Restore the original file afterward or retain the scenario under a distinct name. Explain why quadrupling the run size halves the typical MCSE but cannot resolve uncertainty about which demand law is realistic. The next chapter develops regression as projection . Later work extends simulation to estimated models, resampling, dynamic paths and rare-event methods. This chapter establishes the target, independent unit, variance calculation and reproducibility habits those methods require; it does not supply Markov-chain error estimates or a general rare-event importance-sampling implementation."}, {"title": "04.07 · Regression as projection", "url": "v04-c07.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Choose the closest attainable vector Capability: derive ordinary least squares, calculate fitted values and residuals, and explain what the fitted coefficients and fit statistics do—and do not—establish. Prerequisite: Monte Carlo reasoning , together with the linear systems , projection geometry and multivariable derivatives already developed. The optional computation uses standard-library Python . Every dataset in this chapter is an original synthetic teaching example, not an observation of a firm or market. A workshop records output and total cost for several weeks. No single straight line passes through all the observations. Which line should summarize their relationship, and what can the resulting slope tell us? We first make a descriptive choice: select the line with the smallest sum of squared vertical discrepancies. This defines a calculation. A probability model, a forecast claim and a causal interpretation each require additional reasoning. There are n observations. Stack the outcomes in the n -vector y . The design matrix X has n rows and p columns of chosen predictors, also called regressors or features. Write its row i as x i T . The coefficient vector b has p entries; the fitted vector is Xb . A column of ones permits an intercept . In simple regression, the row is (1, x i ), so its fitted value is a + bx i . The generic vector b and the simple-regression scalar slope b will always be distinguished by context. Ordinary least squares (OLS) solves minimize over b ∈ ℝᵖ: S(b) = ‖y − Xb‖² = Σᵢ(yᵢ − xᵢᵀb)². All observations here have equal weight. The squared norm is the ordinary Euclidean norm, so a discrepancy of 4 outcome units contributes 16 squared units. The loss is measured vertically in the outcome, not as a perpendicular distance from a point to a line in the scatterplot. Squaring prevents positive and negative errors from canceling but makes large discrepancies influential. Changing the loss or observation weights changes the problem. Normal equations, existence and the global minimum Holding the observed X and y fixed, differentiating each squared term gives ∂S/∂b j = −2Σ i x ij (y i − x i T b). A minimizing coefficient vector b̂ therefore satisfies the normal equations Xᵀ(y − Xb̂) = 0, or XᵀXb̂ = Xᵀy. “Normal” means perpendicular: the residual e = y − Xb̂ is orthogonal to every column of X . The column space C( X ) consists of all fitted vectors the model can produce. To show both existence and the minimizing property, choose an orthonormal basis Q for that space, as in the earlier projection chapter; if its dimension is zero, the fitted vector is simply zero. Otherwise define ŷ = QQ T y. This vector is in C( X ), and Q T (y − ŷ) = 0 because Q T Q = I. A coefficient vector producing ŷ exists by the definition of column space. More directly, whenever b̂ satisfies the normal equations, expand the loss at any alternative b : ‖y − Xb‖² = ‖e − X(b − b̂)‖² = ‖e‖² + ‖X(b − b̂)‖². The cross term is zero because e T X = 0. The second term is nonnegative, proving a global minimum, not merely a stationary point. Equality holds exactly when X(b − b̂) = 0. Thus all minimizers have the same fitted vector and residual, even if their coefficients differ. Full column rank means Xv = 0 implies v = 0; in particular p ≤ n . In that case v T X T Xv = ‖Xv‖² > 0 for nonzero v, so X T X is invertible and the coefficient vector is unique: b̂ = (XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀy; ŷ = Hy; H = X(XᵀX)⁻¹Xᵀ. The projection matrix , or hat matrix, H is symmetric and satisfies H² = H, by transposing and multiplying the displayed expression. Hence e = (I − H)y, He = 0 and ŷ T e = 0. For deficient rank use H = QQ T with an orthonormal basis of C(X), rather than the undefined inverse. Coefficients then form a family b̂ + v with Xv = 0. A duplicated predictor, a constant predictor alongside the intercept, or a complete set of category indicators alongside the intercept creates such a dependency. More rows alone do not repair an exact dependency. No independence, normal distribution or causal assumption was used in this finite-data proof. Such assumptions enter when making population or uncertainty claims. A residual is the observed outcome minus the fitted value after using the sample to fit the model. It is not automatically the unobserved disturbance u of a proposed population equation y = Xβ + u. In particular X T e = 0 is guaranteed by optimization; it does not demonstrate that an omitted determinant is unrelated to the predictors. For another treatment of the geometry and normal equations, see MIT's projection and least-squares session . The derivation and numerical cases here are self-contained. An intercept and one varying predictor Let x̄ = Σx i /n and ȳ = Σy i /n. The intercept normal equation is Σ(y i − â − b̂x i ) = 0, yielding â = ȳ − b̂x̄. Substitute into the other equation and define S xx = Σ(x i − x̄)² and S xy = Σ(x i − x̄)(y i − ȳ). Because centered x values sum to zero, b̂ = Sₓᵧ/Sₓₓ, â = ȳ − b̂x̄, ŷᵢ = ȳ + b̂(xᵢ − x̄), provided Sₓₓ > 0. The fitted line passes through (x̄, ȳ); residuals sum to zero and satisfy Σx i e i = 0. If all x values are identical, S xx = 0: the best fitted value is still ȳ at every row, but the intercept and slope cannot be distinguished. A negative slope is permitted. The objective concerns the data supplied, not whether a positive relationship was expected. Worked example 1: a cost line and its error budget For four synthetic weeks, x is output in hundreds of finished units and y is total cost in thousands of USD. Assume the units and accounting boundary are consistent across weeks and use equal weights. Week x, hundreds of units y, thousand USD ŷ, thousand USD e = y − ŷ, thousand USD A 0 1 0.7 0.3 B 1 2 1.9 0.1 C 2 2 3.1 −1.1 D 3 5 4.3 0.7 We have x̄ = 1.5, ȳ = 2.5, S xx = 2.25 + 0.25 + 0.25 + 2.25 = 5, and S xy = 2.25 + 0.25 − 0.25 + 3.75 = 6. Thus b̂ = 6/5 = 1.2 thousand USD per hundred units, and â = 2.5 − 1.2(1.5) = 0.7 thousand USD. In ordinary dollar-per-unit notation the slope is 12 USD per unit. It describes the fitted cost difference across these weeks; without an economic identification argument it need not be a causal marginal cost. Check the normal equations directly: 0.3 + 0.1 − 1.1 + 0.7 = 0 and 0(0.3) + 1(0.1) + 2(−1.1) + 3(0.7) = 0. The residual sum of squares is 0.09 + 0.01 + 1.21 + 0.49 = 1.8 (thousand USD)². The total centered sum of squares is (−1.5)² + (−0.5)² + (−0.5)² + 2.5² = 9. The fitted centered sum of squares is (−1.8)² + (−0.6)² + 0.6² + 1.8² = 7.2. We will justify 9 = 7.2 + 1.8 below. At x = 2 the fitted cost is 3.1 thousand USD even though that week's observed cost is 2. A fitted value summarizes the entire sample. At x = 6 the same formula gives 7.9 thousand USD, but this doubles the largest observed output: the calculation is exact conditional on the fitted line, while its use as a future cost forecast is unsupported by observations at that scale. Fit accounting and its conditions When C(X) contains the constant vector 1 , residuals sum to zero and the sample means of y and ŷ agree. Decompose y − ȳ 1 = (ŷ − ȳ 1 ) + e. The first term is in C(X), so it is orthogonal to e. Squaring gives SST = SSR + SSE, SST = Σ(yᵢ − ȳ)², SSR = Σ(ŷᵢ − ȳ)², SSE = Σeᵢ². Here SST denotes total, SSR regression/explained, and SSE error/residual sum of squares. Other texts sometimes use SSR for residuals; read the definition before using a label. All three have squared outcome units. For SST > 0, the in-sample coefficient of determination is R² = 1 − SSE/SST = SSR/SST, between zero and one under the stated intercept condition. In example 1 it is 1 − 1.8/9 = 0.8: the fitted values account for 80% of centered sample variation in this squared-distance sense. It is not the probability that the model is correct or the share of cost caused by output. Root mean squared error, √(SSE/n), retains outcome units: √0.45 ≈ 0.67082 thousand USD here. This descriptive RMSE uses n, not the degrees-of-freedom adjustment needed for a particular disturbance-variance estimator. If y is constant, SST = 0 and this R² is undefined, even if SSE = 0. If the design omits a constant, e remains orthogonal to ŷ but may not sum to zero. The uncentered identity y T y = ŷ T ŷ + e T e still holds, but the centered decomposition can fail. For example, fit y = (1, 2) on x = (1, 0) without an intercept. The minimizing slope is 1, ŷ = (1, 0), SSE = 4 and SST = 1/2. Centered R² = 1 − 4/(1/2) = −7. An uncentered statistic would use Σy² = 5 and report 1/5; it answers a different comparison. Never compare those two denominators as though they were the same measure. Adding columns while retaining the same rows and loss can only decrease SSE: every old fitted vector is still available by giving the new coefficients zero. Thus ordinary training R² cannot fall with additional predictors when its denominator is unchanged. A model with a separate indicator for every observation has C(X) = ℝⁿ, H = I, e = 0 and perfect training fit; this supplies no rule for genuinely new observations. Fit is a property of a model, a loss and a dataset together. Several predictors and what “holding fixed” means In ŷ = â + b̂ 1 x 1 + b̂ 2 x 2 , changing x 1 by one unit at the same x 2 changes the fitted formula by b̂ 1 . This is an algebraic comparison. Data may contain little variation in x 1 at a given x 2 ; the hypothetical combination may not occur at all. Adding controls does not by itself make that comparison causal. The slope can be understood by partialling out the other columns. Write X = [Z, x], with Z full column rank and x outside C(Z). Project onto Z with P Z ; put M Z = I − P Z , r x = M Z x and r y = M Z y. For any fixed slope b on x, the best Z coefficients fit P Z (y − xb), leaving residual M Z (y − xb) = r y − r x b. Minimizing its square over b is a one-variable quadratic. Its derivative gives b̂ = (rₓᵀrᵧ)/(rₓᵀrₓ), provided rₓᵀrₓ > 0. This proves that the multiple-regression slope uses the x variation left after linearly removing Z, matched to the similarly adjusted y. If Z contains an intercept, the adjusted vectors have mean zero. If r x = 0, x is completely explained by the other columns and its separate slope is unidentified by the least-squares criterion. If r x is very small, the denominator exposes sensitivity to small outcome or design changes; exact rank is a weaker requirement than useful information. Worked example 2: a group difference changes the slope Six synthetic workshop records contain batches completed x, a weekend indicator z (0 weekday, 1 weekend), and paid labor time y in hours. Fit an intercept, x and z to these records; the table is constructed for teaching and asserts no effect of changing work schedules. Record x, batches z y, hours ŷ, hours e, hours A 0 0 11 10 1 B 1 0 10 12 −2 C 2 0 15 14 1 D 1 1 15 16 −1 E 2 1 20 18 2 F 3 1 19 20 −1 In column order (1, x, z), X T X has rows (6, 9, 3), (9, 19, 6), (3, 6, 3); X T y = (90, 152, 54) T . Multiplication verifies that (â, b̂, ĉ) = (10, 2, 4) solves all three equations: 60 + 18 + 12 = 90; 90 + 38 + 24 = 152; 30 + 12 + 12 = 54. The columns are independent: if a + bx + cz vanishes in every row, the first two rows force a = b = 0, and a weekend row then forces c = 0. The solution is therefore unique. Independently recover the batch slope by adjusting within groups. Projection on (1, z) replaces a variable by its mean in each group, because a separate constant can be fitted to each. The weekday and weekend x means are 1 and 2; their y means are 12 and 18. Thus r x = (−1, 0, 1, −1, 0, 1) and r y = (−1, −2, 3, −3, 2, 1). Their inner product is 1 + 3 + 3 + 1 = 8, and r x T r x = 4: the slope is 2 hours per batch. The group mean equations give â = 12 − 2(1) = 10 and ĉ = 18 − 10 − 2(2) = 4 hours. Ignoring z instead gives x̄ = 3/2, ȳ = 15, S xx = 11/2 and S xy = 17, so the simple slope is 34/11 ≈ 3.091 hours per batch. Weekend records have both higher average output and higher average labor time; the one-predictor fit mixes within-group and between-group comparisons. In the multiple fit, e sums to zero within each group and Σxe = 0. SSE = 12 hours²; SST = 16 + 25 + 0 + 0 + 25 + 16 = 82 hours². Hence SSR = 70 and R² = 35/41 ≈ 0.85366. Neither slope is a causal effect merely because its residual is orthogonal to included columns. Units, changes of basis and numerical computation The intercept has outcome units; each slope has outcome units divided by its predictor's units. In example 1, recording output as individual units x* = 100x gives slope b̂* = b̂/100 = 0.012 thousand USD per unit. Recording the outcome in USD multiplies every coefficient and residual by 1,000, every sum of squares by 1,000², and leaves R² unchanged. Centering x* = x − c gives a new intercept â* = â + b̂c and unchanged slope. Predictions agree after applying the same transformation to new inputs. A standardized coefficient measures changes in sample standard-deviation units; it does not remove confounding or make unlike economic interventions comparable. In general replacing X by XA for an invertible p × p matrix A leaves its column space unchanged. The new coefficient vector is A −1 b̂, while fitted values and SSE are unchanged. This explains why a coefficient alone, without coding and units, is incomplete. New observations must use the identical column ordering, category definition and transformations. The inverse formula is a derivation, not an instruction to form an inverse on a computer. With full rank, a thin QR factorization X = QR has Q T Q = I and an invertible upper-triangular R. Decompose y into QQ T y and its orthogonal remainder; then minimizing ‖y − QRb‖² is equivalent to solving Rb = Q T y by back substitution. With floating arithmetic, near dependencies and disparate scales require rank diagnostics and appropriate factorization methods. A tiny residual by itself does not certify stable coefficients. Boyd and Vandenberghe's author-hosted Introduction to Applied Linear Algebra , §12.3, develops QR computation; §13.2 discusses checking predictions on data not used to fit the model. These are further-study routes. The laboratory below deliberately uses exact rational elimination for small integer inputs, avoiding floating roundoff while exposing exact rank failure. Residual checks, leverage and prediction beyond the data Residual-versus-fitted and residual-versus-predictor plots can reveal curvature, different spreads, unusual observations or time patterns. The normal equations eliminate linear sample correlation with the included columns, not every pattern. An exact example is x = (−1, 0, 1), y = (1, 0, 1): the line is the constant 2/3 and residuals (1/3, −2/3, 1/3) display curvature despite their zero sum and zero inner product with x. A squared-x feature might improve the descriptive fit, but its predictive value still needs assessment. A large residual can reflect an error, a rare valid observation or an inadequate model; check provenance before deleting it. For full rank, the diagonal element h ii of H is an observation's leverage , reflecting its design position, not its observed y value. If u i is the coordinate vector with a one at position i, then h ii = ‖Hu i ‖². Orthogonal decomposition of u i implies 0 ≤ h ii ≤ 1. With an orthonormal basis Q, Σh ii = Σ i,j Q ij ² = p, so average leverage is p/n. Holding X fixed, changing just y i by δ changes the full fitted vector by δHu i and its own fitted value by δh ii . High leverage therefore allows an observation to move its own fit strongly and potentially have a deceptively small residual. Influence also depends on its outcome discrepancy; leverage alone does not establish an influential error. With an intercept and one predictor, the orthonormal design directions are 1 /√n and (x − x̄ 1 )/√S xx , so h ii = 1/n + (x i − x̄)²/S xx . Example 1 gives (0.7, 0.3, 0.3, 0.7), totaling 2. At a new input x*, the fitted prediction is a weighted sum of training outcomes with weights w i = 1/n + (x* − x̄)(x i − x̄)/S xx . The weights sum to one, but can be negative. Their squared sum is 1/n + (x* − x̄)²/S xx , as expansion and centering show. At x* = 6, the weights are (−1.1, −0.2, 0.7, 1.6) and the squared sum is 4.3, larger than any training diagonal. This new-point quantity is not bounded by one; it measures sensitivity of the predicted value to the training outcomes, without itself being a prediction interval. Extrapolation uses the fitted relationship outside the supported predictor region. In several dimensions, an unobserved combination can be poorly supported even when each coordinate is within its separate observed range. Prediction also depends on population, time and measurement stability. For a forecast, assess errors on later data available only after the training period, with every transformation fitted using the permitted training information. For independent new units from a stable population, an appropriate held-out sample may instead address that target. Repeatedly choosing the model using the same test outcomes makes them part of model selection, weakening their role as a final evaluation. Small test samples have their own uncertainty. To make overfitting concrete, the four cost observations are fitted exactly by the cubic 1 + (17/6)x − (5/2)x² + (2/3)x³. Its training SSE is zero. Now specify two separate synthetic evaluation records, (x, y) = (4, 6) and (5, 7), without refitting. The line predicts 5.5 and 6.7, giving mean squared error (0.5² + 0.3²)/2 = 0.17 (thousand USD)². The cubic predicts 15 and 36, giving (9² + 29²)/2 = 461 in the same units. This constructed counterexample proves that superior training fit need not yield superior evaluation fit; it does not establish that every cubic predicts poorly or estimate a real forecasting advantage. Exact offline regression laboratory Download the Python benchmark , synthetic integer inputs and laboratory instructions into one folder. Run python3 v04-c07-regression.py ; no installation beyond Python 3 is needed. The program reports coefficient and diagnostic values as exact fraction strings, fits the line, group-adjusted model and cubic, and compares the two fixed evaluation records. It reads but does not overwrite inputs. The input contract requires at most 32 rows and 8 columns per case, a leading intercept column, matching integer outcomes and complete named units. Integers are limited in magnitude to 1,000,000; booleans and missing values are rejected. The solver forms the normal equations in Fraction arithmetic, then uses row elimination and back substitution; divisions remain exact. It refuses deficient rank rather than selecting one coefficient vector silently. This is a transparent small-case benchmark, not a scalable algorithm or a remedy for weak empirical information. Exact fractions eliminate arithmetic roundoff, not sensitivity to data changes. To follow its numerical core, Fraction(6, 5) stores 6/5 exactly; Fraction(6) / Fraction(5) does the same; the official Fraction reference documents construction and arithmetic. The matrix routine copies inputs before operations, selects a nonzero pivot in each column, subtracts multiples of the pivot row from lower rows, and then solves the triangular system upward. These are the elimination steps of the earlier linear-systems chapter. The independent checker compares the output with centered sums, within-group residualization and exact projection identities; computing the same inverse twice would add little assurance. from fractions import Fraction as F x = [F(v) for v in [0, 1, 2, 3]] y = [F(v) for v in [1, 2, 2, 5]] x_mean = sum(x) / len(x) y_mean = sum(y) / len(y) sxx = sum((v - x_mean)**2 for v in x) sxy = sum((x[i] - x_mean) * (y[i] - y_mean) for i in range(len(x))) slope = sxy / sxx intercept = y_mean - slope * x_mean print(intercept, slope) # 7/10 6/5 Laboratory deliverable: reconcile both examples, demonstrate rejection of a duplicate column, and change only an evaluation outcome to establish that the training coefficients do not change. Submit a short explanation of the normal equations, one units check, and why the holdout comparison is descriptive evidence about these invented records. A satisfactory report gives exact SSE and R² values, identifies the rank failure rather than disguising it as zero, and keeps evaluation data out of the fit. Exercises Derive and interpret. Starting from S(b) = ‖y − Xb‖², derive the normal equations and prove they are sufficient for a global minimum. State the condition for unique coefficients and explain why normal-equation residual orthogonality is not evidence for causal identification. A new simple regression. For x = (0, 2, 4) in months of experience and y = (2, 3, 7) in tasks completed per hour, fit an intercept and slope. Give all fitted values and residuals, SSE, SST and R². Interpret the slope's units without a causal claim. Coding and units. In example 1, use q = 100x measured in individual units and C = 1,000y measured in USD. Write the fitted equation and its SSE and R². Instead center the original x at 2; give the new intercept and interpret it. Rank and off-support prediction. Append z = 2x to the design in example 1 and fit a + bx + cz. Describe every minimizing (a, b, c), the fitted values and SSE. Are predictions unique at a new (x*, z*) = (4, 8)? At (4, 9)? Explain using the coefficient family. A partialled-out comparison. In example 2, recover the slope on x from r x and r y . Then add exactly 3 hours to every weekend outcome while leaving x and z fixed. Find all new coefficients, residuals, SSE and the new simple-regression slope obtained when z is omitted. R² boundaries. Recompute the no-intercept example x = (1, 0), y = (1, 2), including the centered and uncentered denominators. State what happens to centered R² for a constant outcome fitted with an intercept. Prove that adding columns cannot increase training SSE when the rows and loss remain fixed. Leverage and extrapolation. Using example 1, calculate the change in coefficients, fitted values and residuals when the last training outcome rises by 1 thousand USD with X fixed. Also find the change in the prediction at x* = 6. Reconcile it with the prediction weights and explain why high leverage alone does not prove a recording error. Choose a claim the evidence supports. Verify the cubic's training predictions and the two evaluation MSEs. A manager writes, “The cubic's R² of 1 proves that extra output causes its fitted cost increase, and the line's worse fit makes it unsuitable for future budgeting.” Critique both inferences. Design a brief evaluation appropriate for next month's workshop budgeting, naming the timing, input support and at least one residual check. State what extra reasoning would be needed for a causal decision. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria Expand S = y T y − 2b T X T y + b T X T Xb. Its gradient is −2X T y + 2X T Xb, so stationarity is X T (y − Xb̂) = 0. With e = y − Xb̂, S(b) = S(b̂) + ‖X(b − b̂)‖² because the cross term is −2e T X(b − b̂) = 0. This proves global minimization and shows that equality requires X(b − b̂) = 0. Coefficients are unique exactly when X has full column rank; fitted values are unique regardless. Orthogonality is imposed by the fit even for selected, confounded or misspecified data. Identifying an intervention's effect requires a design and assumptions connecting observed comparisons to that intervention; no such connection follows from this algebra. x̄ = 2, ȳ = 4, S xx = 8 and S xy = (−2)(−2) + 0(−1) + 2(3) = 10. Therefore b̂ = 5/4 and â = 3/2. Fitted values are (3/2, 4, 13/2); residuals are (1/2, −1, 1/2), whose sum and inner product with x vanish. SSE = 3/2, SST = 4 + 1 + 9 = 14, SSR = 25/2 and R² = 1 − 3/28 = 25/28. The fitted association is 1.25 additional tasks per hour per additional month of experience; it is not proof of an experience intervention's effect. Since x = q/100 and C = 1,000y, Ĉ = 700 + 12q USD. Residuals become (300, 100, −1,100, 700) USD, so SSE = 1,800,000 USD². SST = 9,000,000 USD² and R² remains 4/5. For t = x − 2, ŷ = 0.7 + 1.2(t + 2) = 3.1 + 1.2t; the new intercept is the fitted thousand-USD cost at output of 200 units, not a newly estimated fixed cost. On the observed rows bx + cz = (b + 2c)x. Thus a = 7/10 and b + 2c = 6/5: every solution is (7/10, 6/5 − 2t, t), with arbitrary real t. Fitted values remain (7/10, 19/10, 31/10, 43/10) and SSE = 9/5. At (4, 8), the prediction is 7/10 + 4(6/5 − 2t) + 8t = 11/2, uniquely. At (4, 9), it is 11/2 + t, arbitrary. Even unique training fitted values do not supply a unique off-support forecast under a redundant coding relation. The adjusted inner product is 8 and the adjusted x squared norm is 4, giving b̂ = 2. Adding 3z to y adds the already included design vector 3z to fitted values. The new coefficients are (10, 2, 7), residuals remain (1, −2, 1, −1, 2, −1) and SSE remains 12 hours². For the unadjusted fit, the extra centered cross-product is 3Σ(x − x̄)z = 3[(1 − 1.5) + (2 − 1.5) + (3 − 1.5)] = 9/2. Hence S xy becomes 17 + 9/2 = 43/2 while S xx stays 11/2; the new simple slope is 43/11 hours per batch. The unchanged within-group slope and changed aggregate slope demonstrate how coding and comparisons matter. Without an intercept, S(b) = (1 − b)² + 4, minimized at b = 1. Fitted values are (1, 0); residuals (0, 2) do not sum to zero. SSE = 4 and centered SST = (−1/2)² + (1/2)² = 1/2, so centered R² = −7. Uncentered total Σy² = 5 yields 1 − 4/5 = 1/5. The centered fitted sum is (1 − 3/2)² + (0 − 3/2)² = 5/2; adding SSE gives 13/2, not 1/2, confirming that the centered orthogonal decomposition failed. Constant y with an intercept gives SSE = SST = 0; 0/0 has no defined value. For nested designs every old coefficient vector extends with zero new coefficients to reproduce its loss, so minimizing over the enlarged feasible set cannot produce a larger minimum. The last x deviation is 3/2 and S xx = 5. Raising its y by 1 raises S xy by 3/2, so Δb̂ = 3/10. The outcome mean rises by 1/4; Δâ = 1/4 − (3/10)(3/2) = −1/5. The coefficient pair becomes (1/2, 3/2). Fitted values change by (−1/5, 1/10, 2/5, 7/10), while residuals change by (1/5, −1/10, −2/5, 3/10) because the last actual outcome also rises. New fitted values are (1/2, 2, 7/2, 5); new residuals are (1/2, 0, −3/2, 1), and SSE = 7/2. The last fitted change 7/10 matches h 44 . At x* = 6 the prediction rises by −1/5 + 6(3/10) = 8/5 thousand USD, exactly the last prediction weight 1.6. Leverage describes design position; a valid extreme observation can have high leverage, and outcome influence depends on the discrepancy as well. At x = 0, 1, 2, 3 the cubic yields 1, 2, 2, 5, respectively; substitution at 4 and 5 yields 15 and 36. Its evaluation MSE is [(6 − 15)² + (7 − 36)²]/2 = 461. The line predicts 11/2 and 67/10; its errors are 1/2 and 3/10, yielding 17/100. Perfect training R² demonstrates interpolation of these four observations, not causal identification or forecast validity. These two invented evaluation outcomes favor the line on their stated loss, but cannot certify a real budgeting model. A satisfactory design freezes candidate formulas using records available before the forecast date, defines the next-month accounting boundary and output scenarios, uses later outcomes for evaluation without refitting to them, and compares error in USD at supported and extrapolated output levels. Inspect residual curvature or time patterns and document uncertainty from few evaluation records and possible input-price changes. For a causal output decision, state an intervention and justify how the comparison isolates it from costs, demand, scheduling and other determinants; the next chapter develops this identification question. Equivalent designs with explicit timing, support, a diagnostic and a credible causal distinction receive full credit. Teach-back and delayed check Teach-back: in three minutes, draw the column space, fitted vector and orthogonal residual. Explain why a duplicate predictor leaves fitted values unchanged while making coefficients nonunique; then explain why orthogonality says nothing by itself about an intervention. Use units to interpret a slope and identify the intercept condition behind centered R². Delayed check, after 48 hours without notes: for x = (0, 1, 2) and y = (1, 3, 2), recover the line, residuals and R²; give one reason it might forecast poorly outside this range. Then state the necessary and sufficient rank condition for unique least-squares coefficients. Delayed-check answer x̄ = 1, ȳ = 2, S xx = 2 and S xy = 1. The slope is 1/2 and intercept 3/2. Fitted values (3/2, 2, 5/2) give residuals (−1/2, 1, −1/2), SSE = 3/2 and SST = 2, so R² = 1/4. Unmodeled curvature, a changed population or future operating conditions can defeat extrapolation. Unique coefficients require that the design columns be linearly independent; no distributional assumption can repair an exact column dependency. Next: causal reasoning, identification and confounding examines when comparisons can support intervention claims. This chapter establishes finite-sample least-squares geometry; sampling distributions, standard errors under dependence, regularization and full econometric specification tests are developed later. They must not be inferred from a fitted coefficient or a training R² alone."}, {"title": "04.08 · Identification and confounding", "url": "v04-c08.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "A difference between people is not yet an effect on people Capability: distinguish descriptive associations from identified causal effects, state the assumptions that connect them, and explain when controlling for a variable helps or harms that connection. Prerequisite: regression as projection , together with conditional expectation from Volume 3. All populations, earnings and mechanisms below are original synthetic teaching constructions, not observations about an actual program. A training provider reports that participants earn less than nonparticipants a year later. Is training harmful? Participants might have enrolled precisely because their employment prospects were worse. A regression can summarize the observed gap accurately while answering the wrong policy question. The causal question compares a person's outcome under two interventions; the descriptive question compares outcomes of different people who received different treatments. Specify the intervention, unit and target Our unit is an eligible worker at a stated enrollment date. Treatment D = 1 means completing a specified six-week training course beginning then; D = 0 means receiving no such course during that period. Outcome Y is earnings over the following twelve months, measured in thousands of USD at one fixed price basis. A study would also need eligibility rules, course content, follow-up dates and a rule for measuring zero earnings. An offer, attendance and completion are different treatments. A lottery of offers identifies an effect of the offer under its design; it does not automatically identify an effect of completion. Write Y i (1) for worker i 's earnings under training and Y i (0) for that same worker's earnings over the same future period without training. Parentheses label interventions, not multiplication or earlier/later dates. The individual effect is τ i = Y i (1) − Y i (0). Last year's earnings are an observed baseline variable, not automatically this year's untreated potential outcome. This notation requires a sufficiently well-defined treatment: materially different courses must be distinguished or a policy for assigning course versions specified. We assume no interference : one worker's treatment does not change another's outcome. Job displacement, peer learning or economy-wide wage changes can violate that assumption. Such settings need potential outcomes indexed by others' assignments or by a specified policy. They cannot be settled by silently applying the two-outcome model. Consistency connects the intervention to measurement: if worker i receives treatment d as defined, observed earnings equal Y i ( d ). Hence the observed-outcome identity is Y i = D i Y i (1) + (1 − D i )Y i (0). Only one potential outcome is observed for a worker in this intervention period. The other is the missing counterfactual. Recording more columns does not reveal it. Define the average treatment effect , ATE = E[Y(1) − Y(0)], for the declared target population, and the average treatment effect on the treated , ATT = E[Y(1) − Y(0) | D = 1]. In a finite population of N workers, ATE means N −1 Στ i . ATT averages over the actually treated workers. These targets can differ when effects differ and treatment selection changes who is averaged. Effects retain earnings units; an effect of 4 means 4,000 USD over the outcome year. For the formal potential-outcome definitions, observed-outcome identity and treatment-stability assumptions, see Scott Cunningham's author-hosted Potential Outcomes and Randomization , sections 4.2–4.5. The numerical constructions and proofs used here are developed explicitly below. Selection creates a gap between association and effect Assume the expectations exist and both treatment groups occur. Let Δ = E[Y | D = 1] − E[Y | D = 0] be the observed mean difference. Consistency and adding and subtracting E[Y(0) | D = 1] give Δ = E[Y(1) | D = 1] − E[Y(0) | D = 0] = E[Y(1) − Y(0) | D = 1] + E[Y(0) | D = 1] − E[Y(0) | D = 0] = ATT + baseline selection difference. The selection term compares the groups' earnings if neither group were treated. It is not generally observable. The identity holds whether the program helps or harms and whether effects are constant. If the target is ATE instead, Δ − ATE = (ATT − ATE) + the selection term. Eliminating the latter alone need not eliminate the target mismatch. “Selection” here concerns assignment into treatment; selecting who enters a dataset can create an additional problem, considered below. Worked example 1: a helpful course with a negative observed gap There are 100 eligible workers. Let X be a measured baseline employment-prospect category: 0 denotes lower prospects and 1 higher prospects. The teaching model makes potential outcomes identical within each category. Administrators place 40 of the 50 lower-prospect workers and 10 of the 50 higher-prospect workers into the course. The table exposes the normally hidden potential outcomes so that we can audit a causal analysis. Synthetic finite population; outcomes in thousands of USD over twelve months X Workers Treated Untreated Y(0) Y(1) Effect 0 50 40 10 20 26 6 1 50 10 40 40 42 2 The treated mean is (40 × 26 + 10 × 42)/50 = 29.2. The untreated mean is (10 × 20 + 40 × 40)/50 = 36. Thus Δ = −6.8, although every worker benefits. The ATE is (50 × 6 + 50 × 2)/100 = 4. The ATT is (40 × 6 + 10 × 2)/50 = 5.2 because participants disproportionately belong to the group with the larger effect. Had participants remained untreated, their mean would have been (40 × 20 + 10 × 40)/50 = 24. The selection term is 24 − 36 = −12. Our decomposition checks: 5.2 − 12 = −6.8. Relative to the ATE, the gap is 1.2 − 12 = −10.8. No arithmetic error or poor regression fit is needed to produce a misleading causal conclusion. Within X = 0, the observed contrast is 26 − 20 = 6; within X = 1, it is 42 − 40 = 2. Weighting those contrasts by the target population's halves produces 4. Weighting by the participants' shares, 0.8 and 0.2, produces 5.2. Why these comparisons recover causal effects here, and when they would fail in real data, requires assumptions. Identification is a bridge justified by assumptions A causal quantity is identified under stated assumptions if every causal model satisfying those assumptions and producing the same observable distribution gives that quantity the same value. Identification asks what could be learned if the observable distribution were known exactly. Estimation uses a finite sample to approximate that distribution or its relevant features; inference quantifies uncertainty under a sampling or assignment model. More observations can reduce sampling error while leaving an unidentified effect unresolved. For a discrete, finite-valued vector of baseline variables X , a sufficient set of assumptions for adjustment is: Consistency and the well-defined-treatment/no-interference scope already stated. Conditional exchangeability: (Y(0), Y(1)) ⟂ D | X. The symbol ⟂ means independence. Within each X stratum, assignment does not select people with different potential-outcome distributions. Conditional equality of the relevant means would suffice for the mean effects below; the displayed independence is a convenient stronger assumption. Positivity: 0 < P(D = 1 | X = x) < 1 for every x with positive probability in the target population. Each required stratum can produce both treatment states. For now the observed distribution and the target population coincide, and variables are measured correctly with complete follow-up. Transporting to another population or correcting selective missing outcomes requires further reasoning. The existence of a measured variable named “risk score” does not establish exchangeability. Derive standardization rather than invoking “control for X” Define μ d (x) = E[Y | D = d, X = x]. For d = 0 or 1: E[Y(d)] = Σ x E[Y(d) | X = x]P(X = x) [total expectation] = Σ x E[Y(d) | D = d, X = x]P(X = x) [exchangeability] = Σ x μ d (x)P(X = x) [consistency]. Positivity ensures that each conditional observed mean on the last line exists on the target support. Subtract the d = 0 equation from the d = 1 equation: ATE = Σ x [μ 1 (x) − μ 0 (x)]P(X = x). This is standardization : compare within strata, then average using a single target distribution. It does not mean subtracting a mean and dividing by a standard deviation. Under the same sufficient assumptions, conditioning total expectation on D = 1 similarly gives ATT = Σ x [μ 1 (x) − μ 0 (x)]P(X = x | D = 1). In fact, ATT needs only an appropriate untreated-counterfactual exchangeability condition and untreated comparisons over the treated group's support. Its weights and requirements should not be silently replaced by those of ATE. In example 1, the within-stratum potential outcomes are constants, so exchangeability holds in this constructed finite distribution; treatment probabilities are 0.8 and 0.2, so positivity holds. Standardized treated earnings are (26 + 42)/2 = 34, and standardized untreated earnings are (20 + 40)/2 = 30. Their difference is 4. A real analyst observes only the relevant outcome in each row. Assuming no remaining within-category selection would then be a substantive claim about admissions, motivation, constraints and measurement, not a fact established by the table. Cunningham's Unconfoundedness , section 5.3, develops conditional independence, common support and target-specific weighting. The derivation here explicitly distinguishes population support from finite-sample cell counts. Where the bridge can break Exchangeability refers partly to unobserved potential outcomes and cannot generally be verified from the joint distribution of observed D, X and Y alone. Institutional assignment rules, genuine randomization, rich prior measurements and sensitivity analyses can inform its credibility. A high R², small p-value, balanced measured means or an insignificant extra control does not prove it. Support can be investigated in observed data, but finite data do not prove population positivity. An empty cell may reflect either an impossible assignment or a small sample; tiny nonempty cells may still be unreliable. If all workers of one category are treated, the untreated mean for that category is unavailable. A fitted line can supply a number there only by adding an extrapolation assumption. Dropping unsupported categories changes the target population and must be reported. Refining X into many categories can improve comparability while leaving too little data for useful estimation. Regression can estimate the conditional means, followed by averaging predicted differences over the target population. But the causal assumptions and an adequate outcome specification are still necessary. With different effects across X, a common treatment coefficient in Y ~ 1 + D + X need not equal the desired ATE or ATT. Interactions or separate outcome models followed by explicit standardization expose the target weights. Residual orthogonality is an algebraic property of the fit; it cannot establish independence between assignment and counterfactual outcomes. Worked example 2: randomization is an expectation, not exact balance Four workers A, B, C and D have untreated outcomes (10, 20, 30, 40) and treated outcomes (12, 24, 36, 48). Outcomes use the same earnings units. A complete lottery assigns exactly two workers to treatment, uniformly among the six possible pairs. Treatment is actually delivered as assigned; every outcome is observed; interference is absent. The finite-population ATE is (2 + 4 + 6 + 8)/4 = 5. All six equally likely assignments Treated pair Treated observed mean Untreated observed mean Observed difference A, B 18 35 −17 A, C 24 30 −6 A, D 30 25 5 B, C 30 25 5 B, D 36 20 16 C, D 42 15 27 Averaging the last column gives 30/6 = 5. The A, B assignment has a realized ATT of 3 and a baseline selection difference of 15 − 35 = −20, yielding −17. That chance imbalance does not make the lottery a nonrandom design. It illustrates why one experiment needs uncertainty assessment. More generally, with fixed potential outcomes and exactly n 1 of N units treated uniformly, E assignment [D i ] = n 1 /N. Therefore E[(1/n 1 )ΣD i Y i (1)] = (1/N)ΣY i (1). Apply the same argument to the fixed n 0 = N − n 1 controls and subtract: the observed mean difference is unbiased for the finite-population ATE. Require 0 < n 1 < N. The treatment indicators across workers are dependent under this lottery, but linearity of expectation does not require their independence. Random assignment is distinct from random sampling. This result targets these four workers; an effect for a wider workforce needs a population argument. Selective attrition, treatment noncompliance or interference can also break the stated analysis. A seed and random-number function alone do not establish that a real program followed the protocol. Choose controls from causal structure and the estimand A control is a variable on which an analysis conditions, through stratification, matching, regression or sample restriction. Conditioning compares cases with equal observed values. It is not the same operation as intervening to set that variable to a value. Three different roles; arrows express hypothesized causal directions Role Simple structure Consequence for a total-effect question Confounder D ← X → Y A common cause can generate baseline selection. Adjustment may restore comparability if the complete adjustment set meets the assumptions. Mediator D → M → Y Conditioning on M can remove a channel included in the total effect. Collider A → C ← B C is a common effect on this path. Conditioning on it can create dependence between otherwise independent causes. The role depends on the specified causal question, the full structure and the other controls. A correlation table does not supply the arrows. In example 1, baseline prospects affect both assignment and earnings, and X suffices by construction. This does not imply that every measured predictor should be added, or that any single common-cause variable removes all confounding. A mediator changes what is held fixed Consider a separate toy economy with randomized binary D and an independent baseline trait U uniformly distributed over {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}. Let skill points after training be M = 2D + U, and earnings be Y = 10 + 3D + 4M. These structural equations specify how variables respond to interventions; each coefficient carries the units needed to produce thousands of USD. Substituting for M gives Y(d) = 10 + 11d + 4U. Thus every worker's total effect is 11: a direct increment of 3 plus 2 extra skill points × 4 per point = 8. Since E[U] = 2, observed randomized group means are 29 and 18, a difference of 11. A regression including D and M instead has an exact D coefficient of 3, with zero residuals. The columns 1, D and M have full rank because U, and therefore M, varies within each D group. It fits perfectly while omitting the mediated 8 from that coefficient. Define a different intervention that sets both treatment and skill: Y(d, m) = 10 + 3d + 4m. The controlled direct effect at fixed m is Y(1, m) − Y(0, m) = 3. It is a coherent different estimand in this model. At m = 2, untreated cases have U = 2 and earnings 18; treated cases have U = 0 and earnings 21. Their difference is 3 because U has no additional route to Y here. Both treatment states occur at m = 2, 3 and 4. Ordinary mediator adjustment does not generally identify a direct effect. It needs a meaningful joint intervention, consistency, sufficient treatment and mediator exchangeability, and support for the chosen joint intervention; mediator–outcome common causes, including ones affected by treatment, require further methods. Randomizing D alone does not randomize M. In this deliberately simple model the structural equations settle those questions. In an actual study, a coefficient labeled “direct” cannot settle them. Worked example 3: a pre-treatment control creates bias despite overlap Let baseline binary traits A, B and E be independent fair draws. All eight triples are equally likely. A screening flag C is recorded before treatment: C = 1 if A + B + E ≥ 2, and 0 otherwise. Later treatment is D = A. Earnings are Y = 10 + 4B regardless of treatment: Y(1) = Y(0) = 10 + 4B. This is an original finite model, not a claim about an actual screening system. Without conditioning, D depends only on A and Y only on the independent B. Both treatment groups have mean earnings 12; the observed contrast and true ATE are zero. But C is a common effect of A and B. Among people with a given screening flag, learning that A is high changes the distribution of B. Enumeration of the eight triples, grouped by observed D and baseline C C D Number of triples Mean Y 0 0 3 34/3 0 1 1 10 1 0 1 14 1 1 3 38/3 For C = 0, the contrast is 10 − 34/3 = −4/3. For C = 1, it is 38/3 − 14 = −4/3. Both strata have probability 1/2, so standardization on C returns −4/3, not zero. Both treatment states occur within each C: their probabilities are 1/4 and 3/4. Positivity holds; exchangeability given C fails. Every person's causal effect is still zero. The relevant path is D ← A → C ← B → Y. Conditioning on C connects the otherwise independent A and B. It can happen through adding C to a model or restricting the sample to C = 1. The latter restriction also has a true zero ATE here because every individual effect is zero. C was recorded before D, so “use only pre-treatment controls” is not a sufficient rule for safety. Cinelli, Forney and Pearl's original university-hosted note A Crash Course in Good and Bad Control (14 August 2019), especially models 7 and 11–16, explains why timing alone does not decide control validity and why mediators and colliders require different reasoning. Our numerical models make these failures directly calculable without requiring a full graphical-calculus course. The same observations can support incompatible effects Suppose the entire observed distribution is half the population at (D, Y) = (0, 20) and half at (1, 30). Let U be a hidden fair binary trait and D = U. Each of these three complete causal models produces exactly that observable distribution: Observational equivalence; outcomes in thousands of USD Model Y(0) Y(1) ATE A 20 30 10 B 20 + 10U 20 + 10U 0 C 20 + 20U 10 + 20U −10 Check U = 0: D = 0 reveals Y(0) = 20 in every model. Check U = 1: D = 1 reveals Y(1) = 30 in every model. Yet the intervention effects differ even in sign. An infinite sample of these observed variables would still show the same positive gap of 10. Unconditional exchangeability rules out B and C here, but the observed table alone cannot justify choosing that assumption. Even measuring U would not create overlap: D = U deterministically. New design information or additional restrictions would be necessary. Reproduce the reasoning offline Download the synthetic case file , enumerative Python benchmark and README into one directory. Run python3 v04-c08-identification.py (or py -3 on Windows). No network request, random seed or third-party package is required: the program enumerates every lottery assignment and every combination in the small structural examples. It reads its neighboring JSON file regardless of the terminal's working directory and leaves inputs unchanged. The report separates synthetic truth, observable contrasts and adjusted comparisons. All reported fractions are exact strings, so \"-34/5\" means −6.8. The training routine first expands the grouped counts into workers, then constructs an observed-only table containing X, D and Y. Standardization consumes only that table; the separately reported ATE and ATT use the deliberately supplied potential outcomes. Real data generally cannot supply those truth columns. Altering a synthetic mechanism illustrates consequences of an assumption; it does not test whether a real mechanism is correct. The file contract bounds the training population and lottery size so enumeration remains small. Invalid types, duplicate JSON fields and unsupported units fail explicitly. If a training stratum has no treated or no untreated worker, the program reports standardization as unavailable instead of filling the missing mean from the hidden potential outcomes. The README specifies the schema and modification experiments. Exercises Define the target. A city randomizes offers of the six-week course but only 60% of those offered attend. Specify a unit, binary treatment and outcome period for an offer effect. Explain why last year's earnings and the earnings of a nonattender are not automatically an attendee's missing Y(0). Give one interference mechanism. Audit selection. Keep example 1's populations and potential outcomes but treat 30 lower-prospect workers and 20 higher-prospect workers. Find the treated and untreated observed means, Δ, ATT, ATE and the baseline selection term. Reconcile the decomposition with units. Change the weights. A population has X proportions (1/4, 3/4), treated conditional outcome means (18, 28) and untreated conditional means (10, 24). The treated group's X proportions are (1/2, 1/2). Assuming the stated adjustment conditions, compute ATE and ATT. Explain why their difference is not a calculation error. Derive and diagnose. Reproduce the standardization proof for E[Y(0)], naming each assumption at the step where it enters. Now suppose P(D = 1 | X = 1) = 1 and P(X = 1) = 1/3. Which observed mean is missing? Is a fitted regression prediction enough to identify the full ATE under the original assumptions? State two honest ways to proceed. Enumerate a lottery. In example 2, derive the mean difference if C and D are treated. Average all six differences and calculate their exact assignment variance, defined as the mean squared deviation from that average. Explain why this variance uses denominator six and why a large realized baseline imbalance need not contradict random assignment. Separate total and direct effects. Replace the mediator model by M = 3D + U and Y = 5 + 2D + 2M, keeping independent randomized D and uniform U on {0, 1, 2, 3, 4}. Derive the total effect, group means and controlled direct effect at m = 3. Check that both groups can attain m = 3. Explain why putting M in a regression would not estimate the total effect. Enumerate a collider. List the four baseline triples (A, B, E) with C = 1 in example 3. Derive their within-C treatment contrast and the true ATE in that restricted population. Which adjustment assumption fails? Explain why both pre-treatment timing and observed overlap fail to rescue the adjusted comparison. Identification memo. An analyst sees the final two-point observed distribution, obtains a perfectly fitted regression with treatment coefficient 10 and concludes that mandatory training would raise everyone's earnings by 10. Verify the observed values and effects in models A, B and C, then write a short correction. Include what the fit establishes, what further information would be needed, and one reason a population-wide mandate might exceed this chapter's no-interference model. Complete solutions The unit can be a worker eligible at the announced lottery date. Define D = 1 as receiving the specified offer at that date and D = 0 as receiving no offer, with Y equal to earnings over the following twelve months on one price basis. The potential outcomes now concern offers, including their induced attendance patterns. Comparing actual attendees with nonattendees generally reintroduces selection; the lottery did not randomize attendance. Last year's earnings occurred at a different time under different conditions. A nonattender is a different worker whose baseline prospects can differ. Neither value equals the attendee's untreated future outcome without additional assumptions. Job competition or peer skill transfer can make one worker's outcome depend on others' offers. An acceptable answer specifies eligibility, timing, units and the offer/receipt distinction; alternative clearly defined outcome windows are valid. The treated mean is (30 × 26 + 20 × 42)/50 = 32.4. The untreated mean is (20 × 20 + 30 × 40)/50 = 32, so Δ = 0.4. ATT = (30 × 6 + 20 × 2)/50 = 4.4; ATE remains 4 because the potential outcomes and population did not change. Treated workers' untreated counterfactual mean is (30 × 20 + 20 × 40)/50 = 28. The selection term is 28 − 32 = −4. Consequently 4.4 − 4 = 0.4. Each quantity is in thousands of USD over the outcome year. A positive raw association still understates the effect on participants. The stratum effects are 18 − 10 = 8 and 28 − 24 = 4. ATE = (1/4)8 + (3/4)4 = 5. ATT = (1/2)8 + (1/2)4 = 6. For ATE, standardized treated and untreated levels are 25.5 and 20.5. For ATT, the levels are 23 and 17. The larger-effect group receives greater weight among participants, so distinct estimands legitimately differ. We assumed earnings units consistent with the chapter, correct measurement, consistency, exchangeability and positivity. The supplied weights alone are not an identification argument. First, E[Y(0)] = Σ x E[Y(0) | X = x]P(X = x) by total expectation. Exchangeability replaces the inner mean with E[Y(0) | D = 0, X = x]; consistency turns it into E[Y | D = 0, X = x]. Positivity supplies an observed comparison group for every needed stratum. If everyone with X = 1 is treated, μ 0 (1) is unobserved; its weight is 1/3, not zero. The original positivity condition fails. A regression prediction adds an outcome-model extrapolation assumption rather than restoring identification under the original set. One can explicitly narrow the target to supported strata, reporting who is excluded; alternatively obtain suitable untreated comparisons through a justified design. Explicitly defend an additional structural restriction or report bounds under outcome restrictions as other acceptable routes. Merely hiding the missing cell is unacceptable. C and D have treated outcomes 36 and 48, mean 42. A and B have untreated outcomes 10 and 20, mean 15, so the difference is 27. The six differences (−17, −6, 5, 5, 16, 27) average to 5. Their deviations are (−22, −11, 0, 0, 11, 22). The variance is (484 + 121 + 0 + 0 + 121 + 484)/6 = 605/3, in squared thousands of USD. This is the entire equally likely assignment distribution, not a sample variance estimated from six sampled experiments; no degrees-of-freedom correction is called for. For the C, D realization, untreated potential-outcome means are 35 versus 15, a chance difference of 20. A design ensures expectations over assignments, not exact equality in each draw. Substitute M: Y(d) = 5 + 2d + 2(3d + U) = 5 + 8d + 2U. Total effect is 8. E[U] = 2, so the untreated mean is 9 and treated mean 17. Under the joint intervention, Y(d, m) = 5 + 2d + 2m, giving controlled direct effect 2. At m = 3, D = 0 requires U = 3 and gives Y = 11; D = 1 requires U = 0 and gives Y = 13. Both combinations have positive probability. The exact regression on 1, D and M has coefficients 5, 2 and 2 and therefore removes the mediated increment 3 × 2 = 6 from the D coefficient. Its fit does not change the total-effect definition. The direct interpretation is warranted here by the specified equations and support; an actual mediator analysis needs additional causal assumptions. The triples are (0, 1, 1), (1, 0, 1), (1, 1, 0) and (1, 1, 1). The sole untreated case has Y = 14. The three treated cases have outcomes 10, 14 and 14, mean 38/3; the difference is −4/3. Every individual's Y(1) − Y(0) is zero, including everyone in the restricted C = 1 population, whose ATE is consequently zero. Conditional exchangeability fails: among these cases, B = 1 occurs with probability 1 for D = 0 but 2/3 for D = 1, so their potential-outcome distributions differ. Positivity holds because both treatment states appear, but it is a different assumption. Pre-treatment C is still a common effect of baseline traits that separately determine treatment and outcomes. When U = 0, D = 0 and every model reveals Y(0) = 20. When U = 1, D = 1 and every model reveals Y(1) = 30. A has effect 30 − 20 = 10. B has effect zero at both U values. C has effect (10 + 20U) − (20 + 20U) = −10 at both values. Thus the observed gap and perfect fit agree across incompatible causal effects. An acceptable memo says the regression describes E[Y | D] on the observed support; it does not establish what earnings would be after changing D. A credible assignment design or explicitly defended counterfactual restrictions are required; recording more observations from the same mechanism is insufficient. A mandate could change hiring competition, wages, course quality or peer effects, so the no-interference treatment definition may fail at scale. Grade the distinctions among fit, identification, estimand and policy scale, not a preferred policy recommendation. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain why the −6.8 observed training gap, 5.2 ATT and 4 ATE can all be correct. Derive the selection decomposition and standardization formula on paper. Then use the screening example to explain why an extra baseline control can worsen a causal analysis even when every treatment–control cell is populated. Delayed check: one week later, reconstruct the six lottery assignments without the table, recover their mean of 5, and identify which expectation makes the estimator unbiased. Name the missing counterfactual for a participant, distinguish conditioning on skill from setting skill, and reproduce two observationally equivalent models with different effects. A successful explanation names the target, units, timing and assumptions before interpreting a coefficient. The next chapter on prediction and model validation asks whether a model forecasts well using information actually available at the prediction date. That is a different question from identification. Planned Volume 12 develops richer causal designs, weighting, graphical criteria, sensitivity analysis and related econometric inference. This chapter establishes a defensible starting point; it does not claim that generic controls solve every observational study."}, {"title": "04.09 · Prediction and model validation", "url": "v04-c09.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Evaluate the procedure that will actually be used Capability: evaluate predictions using data splits and information sets that match the economic decision. Prerequisite: identification and confounding , the regression chapter and forecast losses and information . A forecast can be useful without identifying a causal effect; a causally credible estimate can still predict individual outcomes poorly. This chapter evaluates prediction, with original synthetic examples throughout. A firm selects a demand model because its historical errors are small. Six months later, its budget misses badly. The discrepancy can arise even without a programming error: historical outcomes influenced the model choice, inputs may have been unavailable at forecast time, the validation period may differ from the operating environment, or the reported loss may measure the wrong consequence. A credible evaluation specifies the entire decision procedure before scoring its final performance. Write a forecast as ŷ t+h|t : a prediction of outcome Y at target date t+h, formed at origin t using information then available. The horizon h is elapsed time from origin to target. A record's reference period, release time and later revision time need not coincide. A December statistic released in February cannot be an input to a January forecast merely because its row is labeled December. For future outcomes measured over an interval, a training label may not become known until the interval and reporting delay have ended. Declare the unit, population, forecast origin, horizon, available features, outcome definition, loss and updating rule. Forecasting next month's demand for existing stores differs from forecasting sales for a new store in a new region. A split that estimates one task may misrepresent the other. Record data vintages and availability as in the provenance chapter . What an honest evaluation estimates For an independent-observation setting, let T denote the training data and f T the complete fitted prediction rule, including its learned transformations. With a specified deployment distribution P for a new (X,Y), conditional prediction risk is R(f T ) = E P [L(Y, f T (X)) | T]. The rule is fixed conditional on T. If m test observations are drawn independently from that same distribution and are independent of everything used to construct the rule, then their average loss R̂ test = m −1 ΣL(Y i ,f T (X i )) satisfies E[R̂ test | T] = R(f T ) by linearity and the common conditional distribution. Independence among the test observations is not needed for this expectation identity if their marginals remain correct, but it matters for the usual variance and standard-error calculations. A distribution shift, selected test sample or adaptive reuse of test outcomes changes the argument. This evaluates the particular trained rule. Averaging also over repeated training sets evaluates a learning procedure at that training size. Cross-validation generally trains on smaller subsets than the final fit, so its target is related but not identical. A chronological evaluation instead describes losses across its stated dates and forecasting protocol. It does not become an unbiased estimate of every future regime merely by being chronological. Training loss uses observations that helped fit the rule. In nested least-squares models it can only improve when more columns are added, as already proved; future loss need not. Under squared loss, another useful identity separates the sources of error. At fixed x let μ(x) = E[Y | X=x], let a(x) = E T [f T (x)], and assume finite second moments and a new outcome independent of training conditional on x. Expand Y − f T (x) = [Y − μ(x)] + [μ(x) − a(x)] + [a(x) − f T (x)]. The first and third terms have mean zero, and conditional independence makes their cross-product expectation zero. The other cross terms vanish for the same zero-mean reason. Consequently E T,Y|x [(Y − f T (x))²] = Var(Y|x) + [μ(x) − a(x)]² + Var T (f T (x)). The terms are conditional outcome noise, squared prediction bias and variability across training sets. A more flexible procedure may reduce approximation bias while becoming more sensitive to training data. This identity does not prove a universal tradeoff or that a particular flexible model is worse. The noise term is relative to the information X: adding useful available information can reduce it. The decomposition does not apply unchanged to a dependent future outcome or to absolute-error loss. Choose a loss and a baseline before choosing a winner For errors e i = y i − ŷ i , mean absolute error is MAE = Σ|e i |/m, mean squared error is MSE = Σe i ²/m, and root mean squared error is RMSE = √MSE. MAE and RMSE have outcome units; MSE has squared units. On the same cases and weights, MSE and RMSE rank methods identically because the square root is increasing. MAE need not agree. A signed mean error diagnoses average direction, but positive and negative misses can cancel. Expected squared loss targets a conditional mean, while expected absolute loss targets a conditional median; the earlier forecast chapter gives these decision-theoretic derivations. For positive finite costs c u and c o , and finite conditional E[|Y| | X=x], let underprediction cost c u per unit and overprediction cost c o . Then loss is c u max(y−a,0) + c o max(a−y,0). Where the conditional distribution is continuous, differentiating expected loss gives (c u +c o )F(a)−c u , so an optimal action is the c u /(c u +c o ) quantile. At atoms use the corresponding quantile inequality. A stock planner with unequal shortage and excess costs should not automatically optimize symmetric squared error. Percentage measures need scrutiny. MAPE averages 100|e i /y i | and is undefined at y=0; near-zero denominators can dominate. A constant arbitrary offset changes the target rather than repairing the measure. For probabilities, use an appropriate proper score such as Brier or log loss, and assess calibration over relevant groups; accuracy after a threshold discards probability information. State weights explicitly: equal weight per firm, transaction or dollar of exposure defines different performance. Hyndman and Athanasopoulos's author-hosted point-forecast accuracy chapter provides further discussion of forecast errors, unit-sensitive losses and percentage denominators. Our examples below use their own synthetic data, fixed comparison rules and explicit three-way selection/evaluation roles. A baseline is a simple, genuinely available rule that the proposed method must justify improving upon. Options include a training-only mean, the latest available value, a seasonal value available at the same point in the cycle, or the current business rule. A baseline must receive the same information, score dates, loss and weights as its competitor. Comparing a complex method at one horizon with a baseline at another is not a valid performance comparison. A seasonal baseline needs a defined calendar and enough released history. Worked example 1: two losses choose different methods Four synthetic monthly demand outcomes are (10,20,20,20) units. Method A predicts (10,20,20,12); B predicts (7,17,17,17). Treat these as forecasts frozen before their respective outcomes, not fitted values chosen afterward. Identical evaluation cases and equal weights Method Errors, units MAE, units MSE, units² RMSE, units A 0, 0, 0, 8 2 16 4 B 3, 3, 3, 3 3 9 3 A has smaller total absolute error, 8 versus 12. B avoids the large miss and has smaller total squared error, 36 versus 64. Neither statement is an arithmetic contradiction. Under equal per-unit shortage costs with no overprediction here, A has the smaller realized cost. Under the specified quadratic penalty, B wins. Those four observations cannot establish which rule has lower population risk. For a separate probability example, four binary outcomes are (1,0,1,0). A forecasts probability 0.5 every time; B forecasts (0.9,0.9,0.9,0.9). Their mean Brier scores are 0.25 and [0.01+0.81+0.01+0.81]/4 = 0.41. A threshold-only score would conceal how overconfident B is on the failures. Calibration and proper-score assessment require more than this tiny illustrative sample, and a good probability forecast is not automatically a profitable trading strategy after costs and constraints. Separate fitting, selection and final evaluation Training estimates coefficients and learns transformations. Validation compares candidate procedures, features, tuning parameters and other design choices. Test data evaluate the resulting locked procedure. These are information roles, not magic proportions: no universal 60/20/20 split can ensure enough data, credible timing or representative coverage. Reserve enough final cases to address the intended horizon and operating conditions. A tuning parameter, or hyperparameter, controls a fitting procedure rather than being estimated by its ordinary fit; examples include model degree, a regularization strength or a rolling-window length. Once validation chooses it, a final model may be refitted on training plus validation observations before the test period, if that is the declared protocol. The test assesses that refitting procedure. Any preprocessing must be refitted consistently on the same permitted data. Why does using the validation winner's score as its final assessment overstate performance? Suppose each fixed candidate j has an unbiased score R̂ j for risk R j . Pointwise, min j R̂ j ≤ R̂ k for every fixed k. Taking expectations and then the smallest R k gives E[min j R̂ j ] ≤ min j R j . The selected candidate's true risk is at least that minimum, so its selected score is optimistic in this expectation comparison. Unbiased scores for fixed choices do not guarantee an unbiased score after choosing the smallest one. For an exact original example, two candidates each have true risk 10. Each validation score independently equals 8 or 12 with probability 1/2. Each score is unbiased. The minimum is 12 only when both are 12, probability 1/4; otherwise it is 8. Thus its expected value is 9, while whichever method is selected still has true risk 10. The missing one unit is selection optimism. Real candidate errors are often dependent, and the numerical size of optimism differs; the inequality did not require independence. Cawley and Talbot's original 2010 study of model-selection overfitting examines how optimizing a noisy selection criterion compromises evaluation. The two-candidate construction and inequality here expose the mechanism without relying on a particular learning algorithm. Repeatedly examining a test result, revising the method and reporting the best revision turns that test into additional validation. Renaming it “untouched” afterward does not restore its role. Keep a new final sample, or explicitly report that evaluation is exploratory and seek later prospective evidence. Merely rerunning the same split with a new seed is not a fresh untouched sample. Splits must respect time, groups and all learned steps With exchangeable independent new units from a stable population, random disjoint partitions can approximate the intended deployment comparison. If many rows belong to the same household, firm or patient and the target is a new entity, put whole entities into separate folds. Otherwise a method may exploit entity-specific information that will be absent for a new entity. If the target instead concerns future records of known firms, a chronological within-firm design can be appropriate. Group separation alone does not enforce forecasting time; chronological separation alone does not test transfer to unseen firms. In K-fold cross-validation , partition the development sample into K folds. For each fold fit the complete candidate procedure on the other folds and score the held-out observations; aggregate those losses. Every learned imputation rule, scaling constant, feature choice and tuning step must respect that fold's training data. When fold sizes differ, the pooled per-observation loss weights each fold by its observation count, rather than averaging fold means equally by accident. Folds with overlapping training samples do not supply independent estimates for a naive standard error across folds. If cross-validation is itself choosing tuning parameters and also intended to evaluate the whole selection procedure, use an outer evaluation split and perform selection only within each outer training sample using inner splits. Refit the selected procedure on that outer training sample and score the untouched outer cases. This nested construction protects the outer evaluation role; it does not cure a wrong grouping rule, temporal leakage or distribution shift. A distinct final holdout can serve that role instead when the sample permits. Leakage means information enters the procedure that its declared prediction task would not permit. Examples include a future outcome used as a feature, final revised values substituted for original releases, learning imputation constants from held-out data, choosing features against all labels before splitting, or putting duplicate records into both partitions. The violated contract matters even when a particular numerical result happens not to improve. For a concrete preprocessing example, training feature values are 2 and 4; a training-only mean imputer fills a later missing value with 3. If a future test feature of 100 is also used to learn the imputation constant, the result becomes (2+4+100)/3 = 106/3. Under a fixed illustrative prediction formula 10+2x, the missing record's prediction changes from 16 to 242/3 ≈ 80.6667. This calculation proves unauthorized future information changed the prediction, not that it always improves the test score. If a task truly permits all unlabelled future inputs to be known together, that is a different, explicitly defined transductive task and needs a matching evaluation. The developers' data-leakage guidance distinguishes fitting transformations on permitted training data from applying those learned transformations afterward. No scikit-learn installation is used here; the same information rule applies to spreadsheets and our standard-library programs. Rolling origins and the availability of labels A rolling-origin evaluation repeats the intended forecast in historical time. At each origin, reconstruct available inputs and labels, fit or update as declared, freeze the forecast, then score it after the target outcome becomes available. An expanding window retains all eligible past observations; a fixed-length rolling window retains a stated recent span. Window length is a model choice to select in development data. Neither window is guaranteed to handle a structural change well. A gap between training and evaluation can be needed when labels span overlapping periods, information is released late, or adjacent observations share information forbidden by the deployment task. Determine the necessary exclusion from label end times, release dates and information flow. A fixed gap of one row is not a universal leakage remedy. Do not remove legitimate past information merely to make a dataset appear independent; specify which risk and operational information set the evaluation is meant to approximate. Distinguish a forecast of several horizons issued once from successive forecasts that can learn newly released outcomes. At origin t, a four-month budget must use the information available at t for all four target months. A system that issues a new one-month forecast each month can update after each outcome arrives. Evaluating the second system and claiming the result for the first overstates the information available to the budget. The author-hosted time-series cross-validation discussion introduces repeated origins and horizon-specific evaluation. The example below adds explicit release eligibility, development selection and a separate final period; its dates and numerical results are original. Worked example 2: the validation winner loses later Twelve synthetic monthly demand totals are (100,102,101,105,108,111,115,118,117,116,118,119), in units per month. For this model each total is known at the end of its own month, with no revisions. An origin just after month t closes can use months 1 through t. Initial training ends at month 4; targets 5–8 are validation, and targets 9–12 form the final evaluation. These boundaries, methods and loss are fixed in advance. Compare three rules. Naive predicts the latest known value. Mean predicts the mean of the entire released history. Drift adds the average endpoint change per elapsed month to the latest value. With complete history through t, its one-step forecast is y t +(y t −y 1 )/(t−1). For h months ahead from the same origin, multiply that drift by h. This is an endpoint rule, not OLS on a time trend. It assumes no causal mechanism. Validation: forecasts rebuilt at each preceding month-end Target Actual Naive Mean Drift Drift error 5 108 105 102 320/3 4/3 6 111 108 516/5 110 1 7 115 111 209/2 566/5 9/5 8 118 115 106 235/2 1/2 At origin 4 the endpoint drift is (105−100)/3 = 5/3, giving 105+5/3 = 320/3. Validation MSEs are 43/4 = 10.75 for naive, 35109/400 = 87.7725 for mean, and [(4/3)²+1²+(9/5)²+(1/2)²]/4 = 5641/3600 ≈ 1.566944 for drift. At the end of month 8 select drift. Ties, if any, would use the prespecified order naive, mean, drift. The selected procedure will continue updating each month with then-available labels. Final one-step evaluation: method choice frozen after month 8; refitting rule unchanged Origin → target Actual Naive forecast Drift forecast Drift error 8 → 9 117 118 844/7 −25/7 9 → 10 116 117 953/8 −25/8 10 → 11 118 116 1060/9 2/9 11 → 12 119 118 599/5 −4/5 The selected drift rule has final MSE 147393481/25401600 ≈ 5.802527 and RMSE ≈ 2.408844 units per month. Naive errors are (−1,−1,2,1), with MSE 7/4 = 1.75 and RMSE ≈ 1.322876. The expanding mean's final MSE is about 75.182913. The later data favor naive under the stated loss. Report that unfavorable comparison; do not silently replace the chosen rule and call its newly selected test score an untouched evaluation. Using month 9's released outcome to form the month 10 forecast is allowed by this prespecified updating protocol. Using month 9 to revise the family of methods, loss or window length would be a new selection decision. The distinction is what is learned and when, not a prohibition on every future update. Four final months remain weak evidence about long-run relative performance, especially across changes in demand patterns. If instead all four forecasts were issued once at origin 8, drift is 18/7 per month and predictions are 844/7, 862/7, 880/7 and 898/7. Against the same outcomes the errors are −25/7, −50/7, −54/7 and −65/7. Their MSE is 5133/98 ≈ 52.377551. It answers the fixed-budget question, not the repeatedly updated one-step question. The fixed-origin naive forecast stays 118 at every target and has MSE 3/2. Even the baseline's evaluation changes with the protocol. Compare errors without inventing precision Use the same target cases to compare two methods. A paired loss difference d i = L A,i −L B,i measures which method did better on each case; its average is the difference in average loss. Under iid cases with finite variance, the standard error is s d /√m. Adding two separate mean-loss variance estimates ignores their covariance. Under serial, clustered or overlapping-horizon dependence, retain dependence in the uncertainty method; four chronological losses do not justify an iid t-test. Later econometrics develops appropriate dependence-aware inference. Report baseline performance, loss units, horizon, number of scored cases, eligible and excluded populations, and conditions under which the result may transfer. Inspect performance across economically relevant groups without selecting only favorable slices. Separate explorations suggested by test failures from prespecified comparisons. A deployable financial strategy also requires feasible timing, transaction costs, constraints and risk assessment; a low prediction loss alone does not settle those questions. Reproduce and challenge the information contract Download the synthetic release-indexed inputs , exact Python benchmark and README . Run python3 v04-c09-validation.py . Fractions in the report give both exact and decimal representations. The code has no network dependency and does not modify inputs. It validates complete monthly keys, integer units, nonnegative demand and release dates, and refuses to select a method at month 8 if a validation outcome has not yet been released. The computation separates history_as_of , which filters permitted observations, from forecast , which receives only that history and the target index. Actual target outcomes enter the later scoring function. If a label is delayed, drift uses the elapsed time from the latest released month to the target, not merely one row step. The complete rule needs at least two released observations. Its declared small-data bounds keep the example inspectable; it is not a production forecasting service. Audit the information boundary by changing future labels. Changing only month 12's value must leave the validation winner and all forecasts through month 12 unchanged; only its realized loss changes. Changing month 9 can affect the allowed updates for months 10–12, but cannot alter the month 9 forecast or the method selected at month 8. Delaying month 8's release to month 9 must prevent selection at month 8. These checks expose look-ahead bugs more directly than simply comparing two runs of the same implementation. Exercises Specify the forecast. On 1 March a lender predicts each existing borrower's default over March–May. A training loan's outcome window ends on 31 March and its label arrives on 15 April. State the unit, origin, horizon/outcome window, permitted training labels and a suitable chronological evaluation. Would random row splitting test performance on entirely new borrowers if borrowers have repeated records? Loss and asymmetric cost. Recompute example 1's MAE/MSE/RMSE. Then let shortage cost be 9 USD per unit and excess-stock cost be 1 USD per unit. For a predictive demand distribution putting probability 0.8 on 10 units and 0.2 on 20, compare actions 10, 12 and 20 by expected cost and identify an optimal action. Explain why a conditional-mean forecast need not be optimal for this decision. Selection optimism. Enumerate the four equally likely two-candidate validation-score pairs in the 8/12 example. Find the mean selected score and compare it with the selected method's true risk. Generalize the pointwise inequality to any finite number of unbiased candidate scores. Does it require their independence? Reconstruct the chronological comparison. Derive the month 7 drift forecast from its actual available history, then calculate all validation drift errors and MSE. Calculate naive final MSE and the difference between selected drift and naive final MSE. State what can and cannot be inferred from that difference. Frozen or updating? Derive origin-8 drift forecasts for all four final targets and their MSE. Recompute the fixed-origin naive MSE. Explain why using month 9's outcome in month 10's one-step forecast can be legitimate while using it for a forecast originally issued at origin 8 is not. How does a delayed month-9 release affect the information set at origin 9? Preprocessing and leakage audit. Reconstruct the 2,4,100 imputation example. Identify the leaked information and why the changed prediction does not prove a lower test loss. A researcher selects the ten predictors most correlated with all observed outcomes, then cross-validates a regression on those ten predictors. Specify the repair, including the final evaluation role if the feature count is also selected. Weighting and paired uncertainty. Two evaluation folds have sizes 2 and 8 and MSEs 1 and 9. Compute their pooled per-observation MSE and their unweighted mean fold MSE. For a separate iid four-case comparison, A's squared losses are (0,0,0,64) and B's are (9,9,9,9). Compute the paired mean difference, its sample variance and standard error. Explain why substituting the four chronological targets from example 2 would not automatically justify the iid standard-error interpretation. Challenge a performance claim. Run the offline laboratory and one of its future-label/release mutations. A manager wants to switch to naive after seeing the final losses and advertise 1.75 as independently validated performance of the entire search. Write a corrected report and a plan for the next evaluation. Include target, loss, information set, updating rule, candidate selection, baseline and a meaningful remaining uncertainty. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria The unit is an existing borrower or a precisely defined loan–borrower exposure, with duplicate exposures and aggregation declared. The origin is 1 March; the target is default during the three-month March–May window, not just the May-end status without a definition. Only labels whose outcome windows have ended and whose releases have arrived by the origin may train the rule. The label released 15 April is unavailable on 1 March even if the loan record starts earlier. An evaluation reconstructs successive past 1-March-like origins, fits on then-available data, freezes predictions and scores after follow-up matures. Respect overlapping outcome windows and release delays when defining development/evaluation separation. Repeated borrower rows split randomly can place the same entity on both sides; that does not assess generalization to wholly new borrowers. A new-borrower target requires borrower-level separation plus the appropriate timing. A complete answer states feature availability and does not equate row date with label availability. A's errors (0,0,0,8) give MAE 2, MSE 16, RMSE 4; B's (3,3,3,3) give 3, 9, 3. The asymmetric cost targets a 9/(9+1)=0.9 quantile. Action 10 costs 0.8(0)+0.2(9×10)=18 USD. Action 12 costs 0.8(1×2)+0.2(9×8)=16 USD. Action 20 costs 0.8(1×10)+0.2(0)=8 USD. For any a between 10 and 20, expected cost is 0.8(a−10)+1.8(20−a)=28−a, decreasing toward 20; below 10 it decreases as a rises, and above 20 it increases. Thus 20 is optimal. The mean is 12, optimal for squared prediction loss but not for this shortage/excess decision. The score pairs are (8,8), (8,12), (12,8), (12,12), with selected scores 8,8,8,12. Their average is 9. Each candidate's true risk is 10, so selection does not change that true risk but lowers the expected reported score by 1. For any fixed k, min j R̂ j ≤R̂ k ; taking expectations gives E[min R̂]≤R k when each score is unbiased. Minimizing the right-hand side gives E[min R̂]≤min R. Also R selected ≥min R for every selection, so the expected selected score cannot exceed the expected selected true risk under these premises. Independence is needed for the four equally likely pairs in the numerical construction, not for the general inequality. Finite expectations and fixed candidate-risk definitions matter. At origin 6, history ends at y 6 =111 and begins at y 1 =100; five elapsed months give drift 11/5. Month 7 forecast is 111+11/5=566/5=113.2, hence error 115−113.2=9/5. The complete validation errors are 4/3,1,9/5,1/2, giving MSE 5641/3600≈1.566944. Naive final errors −1,−1,2,1 give MSE 7/4. The selected drift's final MSE minus naive's is 102940681/25401600≈4.052527 in squared monthly-demand units. These later constructed cases favor naive under this loss/protocol. They neither invalidate the recorded validation choice nor prove a universal future advantage; the comparison has only four outcomes and may reflect changed patterns. The final score cannot be reused as an untouched estimate of a newly chosen winner. Origin-8 drift is (118−100)/(8−1)=18/7. At horizons 1–4, predictions 118+h(18/7) equal 844/7,862/7,880/7,898/7. Errors against 117,116,118,119 are −25/7,−50/7,−54/7,−65/7, with MSE(625+2500+2916+4225)/(49×4)=5133/98≈52.377551. Fixed-origin naive predicts 118 four times; errors −1,−2,0,1 give MSE 6/4=3/2. In an updating task, y9 is permitted after release and before forecasting target10. It was unavailable at origin8 and cannot enter forecasts issued then. If month9 is released only at end10, origin9 must omit it. With all other original releases unchanged, the latest available value is118 from month8, and the drift target10 forecast is118+2(18/7)=862/7. At origin10, newly released months9 and10 can both enter the declared updating rule. The permitted imputation constant is (2+4)/2=3, giving10+2×3=16. Including the future100 yields106/3 and forecast10+2(106/3)=242/3. A held-out feature distribution influenced a fitted transformation despite the declared sequential task. The actual missing record's outcome is unspecified, so no claim about the direction of its loss change follows. For feature selection, each fold must select predictors using only its own training labels and then fit/apply the resulting model to that fold's held-out cases. If feature count is also tuned, select it with inner development splits and evaluate the full selection process on untouched outer folds or a separate final test. Splitting after selecting against all labels leaks outcome information even if subsequent regression code is correct. Fold sums of squared errors are2×1=2 and8×9=72; pooled MSE 74/10=7.4. The unweighted fold average is(1+9)/2=5 and gives each fold equal weight despite their unequal case counts. For the separate paired comparison d=(−9,−9,−9,55); its mean is 7. Deviations are−16,−16,−16,48, whose squares sum 3072. Sample variance is 3072/3=1024, and iid standard error is√(1024/4)=16. A's mean loss16 minus B's9 also gives 7. Pairing retains covariance induced by scoring the same cases. Time-ordered losses may be serially dependent, share estimation histories or span overlapping horizons; dividing by√4 alone does not address these dependencies. Even in the iid illustration, four cases provide a fragile basis for distributional inference. A satisfactory report preserves the exact original selection rule, its validation score and the drift final score ≈ 5.802527 versus naive 1.75. Switching after examining the final period is a new development decision. Describe 1.75 as naive's observed loss on the four reported synthetic months, not an untouched estimate of the whole adaptive search. Demonstrate that a future-label edit leaves earlier forecasts/selection unchanged, or that a release delay removes an unavailable input or prevents premature selection. Predeclare the new method and any updating/window rules, collect a subsequent evaluation period with mature labels, retain the same economic loss and baseline, and state the target population/horizon and information-time rule. Report uncertainty from limited periods and potential demand changes. Equivalent plans receive full credit if they preserve evaluation independence appropriate to their task and avoid causal or guaranteed-profit claims. Teach-back and delayed check Teach-back: explain why training, validation and test are different information roles; derive the two-candidate optimism example; then explain exactly which past test labels an updating forecast may use. Use the origin-8 and origin-9 calculations to distinguish legitimate refitting from retrospective selection. Delayed check: after one week, reconstruct the month-5 and month-9 drift forecasts without the table. Explain how a late release changes eligible history. Give one task requiring group separation, one requiring chronological separation, and one requiring both. Recalculate the asymmetric-loss optimal action and explain why it differs from the mean. Delayed-check answer The forecasts are 105+(105−100)/3=320/3 and 118+(118−100)/7=844/7. Include a record only when its reference period and release are no later than the origin. New entities require group separation; later outcomes for known entities require temporal separation; future outcomes for new entities require both. With probabilities 0.8/0.2 and shortage/excess costs 9/1, the 0.9 quantile is 20, whereas the mean is 12. The former minimizes the stated operational loss. Next: time-series and panel data basics makes the calendar, dependence, aggregation and population questions more explicit. Advanced model classes, regularization and formal forecast-comparison inference are developed later; this chapter establishes the evaluation contract they must satisfy."}, {"title": "04.10 · Time-series and panel data basics", "url": "v04-c10.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "The calendar and the population are part of the variable Capability: prepare longitudinal data with correct entity–time keys and lags, and identify dependence, selection and aggregation pitfalls. Prerequisite: prediction and validation with economic timing , together with the table/key methods of Chapter 4 and covariance calculations from Volume 3 . All tables and stochastic laws here are original synthetic teaching constructions, not observations of actual firms or current economic conditions. A firm appears in January and March but not February. The previous row is January; the previous month is February. Treating the two as interchangeable creates a false monthly change. A second problem arises when the March sample contains new firms and excludes closed ones: its mean can fall even if every retained complete-case firm's sales rose. Neither error is repaired by fitting a more elaborate regression. A time series follows a defined variable across ordered times. A panel follows identified entities across times. In a monthly firm panel, write y it for firm i's value in calendar month t. The key is (firm identifier, month), with exactly one row at the declared grain. Repeated cross-sections survey different units at each date; they do not become a panel because they contain the same variables. Stable identifiers require care around mergers, reorganizations or reassignment: an unchanged label need not imply an unchanged economic unit. Record what each date means: observation instant, interval covered, publication date or extraction vintage. Monthly sales cover an interval; month-end inventory describes an instant. A release published in April may refer to March, and later revisions may change it. The calendar lag below concerns the reference period. Whether that lagged value was available at a forecast origin remains the separate information-time check from Chapter 9. Calendar-correct lags, differences and gaps For regular monthly periods, the first lag is Ly it = y i,t−1 . A first difference is Δy it = y it − y i,t−1 . A proportional growth rate is g it = y it /y i,t−1 − 1 when the denominator is nonzero; its usual positive-level interpretation requires positive levels. If a needed value is absent, the lag, difference and growth calculation are unavailable. Do not turn an absent value into zero or substitute a different date. If the denominator is a genuine zero, growth is undefined, even though a level difference may be meaningful. Represent a month by an integer m = 12 × year + (month number − 1). December 2023 then precedes January 2024 by exactly one. For each row, look up the key (i, m − 1) in a unique-key table. Sorting is useful for display, but a row shift is a monthly lag only when the entity is unchanged and the reference months are consecutive. A 12-month lag means the same month of the prior year, not twelve earlier observed rows. Daily trading lags require a specified trading calendar; the previous trading session and previous calendar day differ. A missing row, an explicitly missing value, a structural absence and a measured zero carry different information. A firm not yet operating has no active firm-month observation; an active firm failing to report has an unknown value. A confirmed zero-sales month remains an observed value. A filled calendar grid exposes these states but does not supply missing economics. Preserve a reason or status field. After a join, check that no duplicate key multiplied a record; dropping duplicates arbitrarily may discard genuinely different establishments or vintages that should instead change the grain. The official Stata panel-declaration reference explains within-panel time ordering, gaps and a specified time step. Its concepts are useful even though this chapter implements the lookup directly in standard-library Python and requires no Stata installation. Deflate with a price measure matched to the question Let N it be a current-dollar amount and P t a suitable price index with base period b equal to 100. The corresponding constant-base-dollar amount is R it = 100N it /P t . To see the meaning without an index-number theorem, suppose all firms sell one homogeneous good whose base price is 1 USD and current price is P t /100 USD per unit. Then N it = (P t /100)q it , so R it = q it × 1 USD per unit. In our examples this deliberately simple assumption makes deflation exact. In applications, a household consumption index, an output-price index and an investment-goods index target different baskets. Currency conversion is another operation, not a substitute for choosing prices and quantities consistently. Use the index's level, not its inflation percentage, and match period, geography, coverage and vintage. An index merely rebased to 100 changes the numerical constant-dollar unit; it does not by itself supply new price information. Rₜ/Rₛ = (Nₜ/Nₛ)/(Pₜ/Pₛ), so 1 + g real = (1 + g nominal )/(1 + inflation). Subtracting inflation from nominal growth is an approximation, not this exact identity. Negative or zero index levels make the proposed deflator invalid; negative nominal amounts may be legitimate signed flows but require a separate interpretation of percentage growth. With multiple products and changing relative prices, official real aggregates can use chain-weighted index methods; their published components need not add like our single-good constant-base amounts. Follow the series' stated construction. BEA's explanation of PCE prices and quantities describes appropriate detailed deflators and chain-weighted aggregation. It supports the need to identify the price measure; our one-good teaching index is not a model of the full PCE system. Worked example 1: missing reports, changing membership and real sales The window is January–March 2024. All firms sell the one good just defined; the synthetic output-price indexes are 100, 110 and 120. A and B operate throughout. C begins in February. D ceases before March, with the roster confirming no March operation. B's February sales report is missing while it remains active. These facts come from a separate roster; they cannot be deduced from the observed sales rows alone. Monthly sales in USD; real amounts use January 2024 prices Firm Month Nominal sales Price index Real sales Previous calendar month's real sales A January 100 100 100 Outside window A February 121 110 110 100 A March 144 120 120 110 B January 200 100 200 Outside window B February Unreported 110 Unknown 200 B March 216 120 180 Unknown C January Not operating 100 Not an active observation Outside window C February 330 110 300 Not operating C March 360 120 300 300 D January 400 100 400 Outside window D February 330 110 300 400 D March Not operating 120 Not an active observation 300 For A, February real growth is 110/100 − 1 = 10%, while March growth is 120/110 − 1 = 1/11 ≈ 9.0909%. For B, the previous observed real sale before March is 200 in January; 180/200 − 1 = −10% is a two-month change. Calling it a monthly change would invent the February information. C's March growth is zero; its February growth from a nonexistent active observation is unavailable. The means across observed reports are 700/3 in January, 710/3 in February and 600/3 = 200 in March, in constant-January USD. February uses A, C and D: (110 + 300 + 300)/3 = 710/3 ≈ 236.667 . Each mean uses three reports, but the reporting firms differ across months. January-to-March observed-mean growth is 200/(700/3) − 1 = −1/7 ≈ −14.2857%. Two different fixed cohorts tell different stories. Firms observed at both endpoints are A and B: mean real sales are 150 at each endpoint, so their change is zero. A is the only firm with all three active monthly reports: the complete-window mean rises from 100 to 120, or 20%. Requiring a balanced complete panel therefore changes the target and selects against missing reports as well as entry and exit. Neither selected cohort measures the whole evolving population automatically. For the total sales of this explicitly closed roster, nonoperating firm-months can contribute zero because no sales occur outside their documented operating intervals. Endpoint totals are 700 and 600. The change decomposes as continuing firms' change (120 − 100) + (180 − 200) = 0, plus entrant C's 300, minus exiting D's 400: −100. February's total for all active firms remains unknown because B is unreported. The observed subtotal 710 must not be labeled a complete total. Using zero for a documented nonoperating sales flow is a target-specific accounting decision, not a license to zero-fill unknown records or stock variables. Panel balance, survivorship and dependence A panel balanced on a chosen set of dates contains the same entities with the required observations at every date. An unbalanced panel does not. “Balanced” describes a data structure; it does not certify random sampling or comparability. Keep the roster and inclusion flow: eligible entities, observed entities, missing outcomes, entries, exits and exclusions by period. Report the count behind every mean. A larger observed average may reflect a different population rather than improvement for a typical continuing entity. Survivorship selection occurs when analysis uses future survival or continued reporting to choose earlier records. A fund-performance table containing only currently operating funds can omit earlier failed funds; a worker panel restricted to those still employed can omit departures. The selected average is valid for its stated selected group, but does not identify the original group's performance without assumptions about missing members. Requiring “complete data” can itself use future information. A weighting or imputation correction requires a defensible model for observation and the target; balance alone supplies none. Panel rows are often dependent within an entity and across entities exposed to the same period shock. In Y it = α i + C t + u it , fixed α i describes persistent firm differences, C t is a shared time shock and u it is an individual disturbance. If C t and the individual disturbances have mean zero, C t is independent of those disturbances, and different firms' u it are uncorrelated, two different firms in the same month share covariance Var(C t ). Counting N × T rows as that many independent observations ignores this structure. Appropriate inference depends on the sampling and shock model; this chapter diagnoses the issue rather than prescribing one universal standard error. Changing frequency requires changing the operation Choose aggregation by economic meaning, not by a default spreadsheet function Object Example Possible quarter construction Required qualification Flow over disjoint intervals Monthly sales or units delivered Sum the three complete monthly amounts No overlapping intervals; consistent coverage and units Stock at an instant Month-end inventory Use quarter-end stock for a quarter-end target An average stock requires observations/assumptions covering time, not a sum Ratio of accumulated flows Defective units / delivered units Sum numerators and denominators separately, then divide This weights monthly rates by their denominator exposures Compounding return Monthly total investment return Multiply gross returns: ∏(1+r t ) − 1 Consistent reinvestment and cash-flow convention Published annualized level or rate Quarterly output at an annual rate; annualized quarterly growth Undo the specified annualization before the needed calculation A rate label and a period total are different units A quarter-end stock is not necessarily the most recent available stock if the quarter-end reading is missing. A monthly flow is not the average of a few sampled days unless a measurement design justifies that conversion. An interval crossing a month boundary requires dated underlying events or an explicit allocation assumption; assigning the whole interval to its ending month changes the object. Seasonal adjustment also changes a series' construction and can be revised; record whether the input is adjusted before comparing frequencies or reconstructing real-time data. For nonnegative denominator exposures d t with positive total, a pooled rate is Σn t /Σd t = Σ[d t /Σd s ](n t /d t ) over positive denominators. A zero denominator does not define a monthly rate; zero numerator and denominator contribute zero exposure to the pooled ratio. A positive numerator with zero exposure signals an inconsistent definition. An unweighted average of monthly percentages instead gives every month equal weight, which can be a legitimate different target if stated. A quarterly flow reported as an annualized level of 480 USD under the convention “four times this quarter's flow” corresponds to 120 USD for that quarter. By contrast, an annualized quarterly growth rate a satisfies 1 + a = (1 + g) 4 when compounding is the convention. For positive gross growth, g = (1 + a) 1/4 − 1; dividing a by four is only an approximation. These operations must not be mixed. BEA's quarterly growth annualization explanation specifies compounding a quarterly rate for four quarters. The chapter's amounts and percentages are original examples; no current GDP release is used. Worked example 2: one quarter, four different calculations A separate synthetic workshop has the following monthly records. Sales concern the same single good with January price 1 USD. Deliveries and defective deliveries are disjoint monthly counts; each defective delivery is included in its month's delivered count. Investment returns describe a separate fully reinvested account with no external deposits or withdrawals. Month Nominal sales, USD Price index Real sales, January USD End inventory, units Defects / deliveries Account return January 120 100 120 10 2 / 10 10% February 220 110 200 16 3 / 30 −10% March 360 120 300 9 6 / 20 20% Quarter nominal sales are 700 USD. Deflate each monthly flow at its matched monthly price and then sum: 120 + 200 + 300 = 620 January USD . Deflating the 700 total by the unweighted mean index 110 instead gives 7000/11 ≈ 636.364, which is different. The aggregate deflator consistent with this particular real total is 100(700/620) = 3500/31 ≈ 112.9032. It reflects the sales pattern; it is not simply the arithmetic mean of the three monthly indexes. This one-good result does not assert that official multi-good chain indexes can always be added. Quarter-end inventory is 9 units, not 10 + 16 + 9 = 35. The average of the three observed endpoint stocks is 35/3, but a quarter's time-average stock is not identified by those three endpoints alone: different within-month inventory paths can share them. Quarterly defective-delivery share is (2 + 3 + 6)/(10 + 30 + 20) = 11/60 ≈ 18.3333%. Averaging the monthly shares (20% + 10% + 30%)/3 gives 20%, an equally weighted monthly-rate summary rather than the fraction of this quarter's delivered units that were defective. The account's quarterly gross return is (11/10)(9/10)(6/5) = 297/250, hence return 47/250 = 18.8%. Adding the monthly returns gives 20% and ignores compounding. A 100 USD initial account balance becomes 110, then 99, then 118.80 USD. If there were external cash flows, these balances alone would not identify the same reinvested return measure. Stationarity describes a law, not a flat-looking line A realized time plot is one path of random variables {Y t }. A process is weakly stationary , also called covariance stationary, if it has finite second moments, E[Y t ] = μ independent of t, and Cov(Y t , Y t−h ) = γ(h) depending only on the lag h, not on the calendar date t. Variance γ(0) is consequently constant. If γ(0) > 0, the autocorrelation is ρ(h) = γ(h)/γ(0); for a constant zero-variance process this ratio is undefined. Weak stationarity constrains first and second moments, not every feature of the joint distribution. It does not require successive observations to be equal or independent. Consider independent identically distributed shocks ε t with values −2 and 2, each with probability 1/2, indexed over all integer times. Thus Eε t = 0 and Var(ε t ) = 4. Define Yₜ = 10 + εₜ + (1/2)εₜ₋₁. The mean is 10. Independence makes the two shocks' covariance zero, so Var(Y t ) = 4 + (1/4)4 = 5. Adjacent values share exactly one shock: Cov(Y t ,Y t−1 ) = Cov((1/2)ε t−1 ,ε t−1 ) = 2. At lag |h| ≥ 2 they share no shocks, and their covariance is zero. Thus γ(0) = 5, γ(±1) = 2 and γ(h) = 0 elsewhere, with ρ(1) = 2/5. Every quantity is independent of t: this is stationary dependent data. The construction is a moving-average process of order one, or MA(1); it is not the same operation as averaging observed monthly values for presentation. For n consecutive observations of any weakly stationary process, summing all variances and covariances gives Var(Ȳₙ) = [nγ(0) + 2Σ h=1 n−1 (n−h)γ(h)]/n². There are n diagonal variance terms and n − h pairs at each positive lag h, counted twice in the full double sum. For the MA(1) above, this becomes [5n + 4(n − 1)]/n² = (9n − 4)/n². For n = 4 it is 2, whereas treating the observations as independent gives 5/4. The additional observations provide information, but not at the independent-observation rate assumed by that naive calculation. Mikusheva's MIT notes on stationarity, lag operators and covariance provide a route to fuller time-series theory. The finite-shock construction and covariance derivation above establish the particular law used here without appealing to an unexplained large-sample theorem. Trends, random walks and transformations Using the same iid shocks, the process T t = 3t + ε t has constant variance 4 but mean 3t, so it is not weakly stationary. Subtracting its known deterministic trend leaves ε t , which is stationary. A fixed calendar seasonal mean, such as a different expected sales level each December, also violates a constant unconditional mean. Estimating and removing a trend or seasonal pattern is a modeling choice; doing so with future data can additionally violate a forecasting information boundary. For a different law, take W 0 = 0 and W t = Σ s=1 t ε s for t ≥ 1. This random walk has E[W t ] = 0 but Var(W t ) = 4t. For s ≤ t, W t = W s plus independent later shocks, so Cov(W s ,W t ) = 4s. Its changing variance violates stationarity despite its constant mean. Differencing gives ΔW t = ε t , a stationary sequence. Adding deterministic drift d per period gives mean dt in levels and mean d in the stationary increments. Detrending and differencing answer different questions. For T t = 3t + ε t , differences are 3 + ε t − ε t−1 , with variance 8 and adjacent covariance −4. Differencing a deterministic-trend-plus-independent-noise model has introduced negative serial dependence; subtracting the known trend leaves independent shocks. Conversely, subtracting a deterministic line from the random walk does not eliminate its growing variance. Differencing, logging or seasonal adjustment is not a universal stationarity repair. Stationarity alone also does not guarantee that one long sample average learns the population mean. Let Y t = C + u t , where the common C has mean zero and variance τ² > 0, and iid u t have mean zero and variance σ², independently of C. This process is weakly stationary: γ(0) = τ² + σ² and γ(h) = τ² for nonzero h. Yet Var(Ȳ n ) = τ² + σ²/n does not approach zero. More directly, the averaged u term has mean-square size σ²/n and converges to zero in mean square, so Ȳ n converges in mean square to C. A single path retains its nondegenerate common component instead of converging to the population mean zero. Assumptions permitting time averages to recover population quantities must be established in addition to stationary moments. Hyndman and Athanasopoulos's stationarity and differencing discussion connects trend, seasonality and differencing to forecasting. Our examples distinguish laws by their proved moments; a plotted path alone cannot establish those moments for all dates. Diagnose dependence without pretending to prove stationarity For a complete equally spaced sequence y 1 ,…,y n , one common sample autocorrelation convention is r̂(h) = [Σ t=h+1 n (yₜ−ȳ)(yₜ₋ₕ−ȳ)] / [Σ t=1 n (yₜ−ȳ)²], 0 ≤ h < n. The same full-sample mean and denominator are used at every lag. Software using a different normalization may give different finite-sample values. A constant sample makes the denominator zero. With gaps, pairing must follow dates; silently compressing the records changes the meaning of h. Sample autocorrelations are diagnostics estimated from one path, not known population γ(h) or a proof of independence. Inspect time plots, period coverage, within-entity paths, subgroup composition, rolling summaries and lagged plots. Investigate abrupt changes in definitions, prices or coverage before interpreting a break economically. Persistent autocorrelation can reflect a stationary dependent law, a deterministic trend, an accumulating shock, a shared component or aggregation. A short stable-looking segment can occur under a changing law. Tests and richer models require explicit alternatives, sampling assumptions and finite-sample caution; full time-series and panel inference belongs to the later econometrics volume. Offline longitudinal-data laboratory Download the standard-library Python program , synthetic roster, monthly records and price inputs , and README into one folder. Run python3 v04-c10-panel.py . The report shows a complete entity–month status grid, exact deflated values and calendar lags, cohort and observed-sample summaries, the quarter calculations and exact MA/random-walk moment targets. It does not write or repair the original file. The input fixes a short monthly window and explicit operating intervals. Its supported units remain nominal USD per month and constant-January-2024 USD; the window must include January 2024 with index 100. The program rejects different unit labels or base normalization rather than silently reinterpreting them. Observed rows must lie inside an entity's operating interval; every month has one positive price-index value. Duplicate keys, unknown entities, bad dates, missing index months and inconsistent counts are rejected. Missing active sales rows remain unknown. The program distinguishes them from nonoperating periods and counts the denominator of each summary. As a small local teaching tool, it does not infer a merger history, validate an externally supplied roster's truth, or estimate stationary laws from the synthetic panel. def month_number(text): year, month = map(int, text.split(\"-\")) if not 1 <= month <= 12: raise ValueError(\"month must be 01 through 12\") return 12 * year + month - 1 sales = {(\"B\", month_number(\"2024-01\")): 200, (\"B\", month_number(\"2024-03\")): 216} key = (\"B\", month_number(\"2024-03\") - 1) print(sales.get(key)) # None: February is absent, not zero This small illustration assumes strings already have the intended year-month form; the download adds strict text and range validation. Dictionary get returns None for an absent key by default. The computation retains the calendar distinction even when input rows are shuffled. Real sales use exact fractions from integer inputs, so output such as \"710/3\" means an exact rational result, not a decimal estimate of statistical precision. Laboratory deliverable: reconcile both examples, shuffle the observations to verify unchanged results, remove B's March report to expose the changed coverage, and deliberately insert a duplicate key to verify refusal. Submit the active/observed counts, exact endpoint and complete-window comparisons, and one sentence explaining each aggregation operation. A satisfactory report preserves unknown values, states the chosen population and makes no claim that these constructed records prove stationarity or causality. Exercises Calendar gaps. Firm K reports 80 in December 2023 and 100 in February 2024; firm J reports 7 in January 2024 and 8 in February. All are positive same-unit monthly values. Give each firm's February monthly lag, difference and growth when defined. Give K's observed two-month growth. Explain why an ordinary shift of rows, even after sorting by firm and month, needs an additional calendar check. Choose the panel target. Using example 1, compute January and March means for observed reports, for firms reported at both endpoints, and for firms reported in every month. Give all three percentage changes. Decompose the whole-roster total change into continuing, entering and exiting contributions. Explain why B's February omission cannot be filled like C's January nonoperation for the stated total-sales target. Deflation and rebasing. For A in example 1, compute January-to-March nominal growth, price inflation and real growth, and verify the exact gross-growth identity. Rebase the index so February equals 100; calculate the three new index levels and A's new constant-base sales. Explain which growth conclusions remain unchanged. Reconcile the correctly deflated quarter sales and implicit aggregate deflator in example 2. Aggregation transfer. A different quarter has monthly defects/deliveries 1/5, 0/15 and 4/20; endpoint inventories 8, 12 and 7 units; and fully reinvested account returns 5%, 0% and −5%. Find the pooled defect share, equally weighted monthly defect rate, quarter-end inventory and quarter return. A separate quarterly flow is published at 480 USD per year under four-times-quarter annualization: find the quarter amount. Can the time-average inventory or exact monthly allocation of a weekly flow spanning two months be recovered from these summaries alone? Stationary dependence. For Y t = 10 + ε t + ε t−1 /2 with iid shocks ±2, derive its mean, variance, lag-one and lag-two covariance. Compute the exact variance and standard error of the average of four consecutive observations; compare with treating them as independent. State why positive autocorrelation does not refute weak stationarity. Trend versus random walk. With the same iid shocks, compare T t = 3t + ε t and W t = Σ s=1 t ε s . Give each mean and variance, Var(W 5 ) and Cov(W 2 ,W 5 ). Derive the variance and adjacent covariance of each first-difference process. Does subtracting a known linear trend make both level processes stationary? A short path is limited evidence. For equally spaced observations (0, 1, 0, −1, 0), compute the stated sample autocorrelations at lags 1 and 2. A report says “lag-one correlation is zero, so observations are independent and stationary.” Assess that claim. State how missing dates and a constant sample would alter the computation. Shared shocks in panels. At one month, Y i = α i + C + u i for ten firms with fixed α i . C has mean zero and variance 9; independent u i have mean zero and variance 4, and are independent of C. Compute each outcome variance, covariance across two firms and the variance of the ten-firm average around its fixed mean. Compare with an independence calculation. Explain what a longitudinal analyst should document before selecting a variance estimator or using a complete-case panel. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria K's February monthly lag is unknown because January is absent; monthly difference and growth are therefore unavailable. Its observed December-to-February growth is 100/80 − 1 = 1/4 = 25%, covering two months. J's February lag is 7, difference is 1 and growth is 1/7 ≈ 14.2857%. Within-firm sorting does not insert absent calendar months. A shift must check both entity equality and a one-month gap; an ungrouped shift can additionally import another firm's observation. Month ordinals make December-to-January a one-month transition without confusing month numbers with dates. Observed-report means are 700/3 and 200, giving −1/7 ≈ −14.2857%. Endpoint-reported firms A and B have means (100+200)/2 = 150 and (120+180)/2 = 150, giving zero. The all-month complete group contains only A, with means 100 and 120, giving 20%. Whole-roster endpoint totals fall by 100: continuing firms contribute +20−20 = 0, C's entry adds 300 and D's exit removes 400. C is documented as nonoperating in January, and this specific sales-flow total assigns no sales outside operation. B is active in February with an unknown report; zero would assert an unobserved economic outcome and bias a claimed complete total. February observed subtotal 710 over three reports is therefore incomplete for four active firms. A's nominal growth is 144/100−1 = 44%; inflation is 120/100−1 = 20%; real growth is (144/120)/(100/100)−1 = 20%. Thus 1.44/1.20 = 1.20, whereas subtracting percentages gives the inaccurate 24%. The February-base price index is P′ t = 100P t /110: (1000/11,100,1200/11). New real amounts are 100N/P′ = (11/10)(100N/P), so A's values become 110,121,132 February-price USD. Every same-firm real growth ratio stays unchanged. Example 2 gives 100(120/100 + 220/110 + 360/120) = 620 January USD; its aggregate deflator is 100(700)/620 = 3500/31. The unweighted index average produces 7000/11, not 620. Pooled defects are 5 out of 40 deliveries: 1/8 = 12.5%. Monthly rates are 1/5,0,1/5, whose equally weighted mean is 2/15 ≈ 13.3333%. Quarter-end inventory is 7 units. The return is (21/20)(1)(19/20)−1 = −1/400 = −0.25%; a 100 USD starting balance ends at 99.75 USD, despite the monthly percentage sum being zero. The annualized flow level 480 corresponds to 120 USD in the quarter. Three endpoint stocks do not identify the time-average path. A weekly flow straddling months needs dated underlying transactions or a disclosed within-week allocation assumption; a total and endpoint date alone do not identify its monthly split. Shock mean is zero and variance is 4. Hence mean(Y) = 10, variance = 4 + (1/4)4 = 5, lag-one covariance = (1/2)4 = 2 and lag-two covariance = 0. Expanding the four-term average gives variance [4(5)+2(3)(2)]/16 = 32/16 = 2, so standard error is √2 ≈ 1.414214 outcome units. An independence calculation gives variance 5/4 and SE √5/2 ≈ 1.118034. Lag-one autocorrelation 2/5 is constant over time; weak stationarity permits it. Independence would instead require, among other things, zero covariance when second moments exist. T has mean 3t and variance 4; W has mean zero and variance 4t. Neither has stationary moments. Var(W 5 ) = 20 and Cov(W 2 ,W 5 ) = Var(ε 1 +ε 2 ) = 8. ΔT t = 3+ε t −ε t−1 has variance 8 and adjacent covariance −4 because the shared shock has opposite signs. ΔW t = ε t has variance 4 and adjacent covariance zero. Both difference processes have constant mean and lag-dependent covariance. Subtracting the known 3t trend from T leaves stationary iid shocks. Subtracting any deterministic line from W changes its mean but leaves variance 4t; it cannot make the random-walk levels weakly stationary. The sample mean is zero and denominator is 1+1 = 2. Lag-one products are all zero, so r̂(1) = 0. At lag two the products are 0, −1 and 0, giving r̂(2) = −1/2. A sample estimate of zero at one lag proves neither all-lag zero covariance nor independence. Even population zero covariance at every nonzero lag would not imply independence without stronger assumptions. These five realized values cannot establish invariance of a generating law's moments at all dates. With missing dates, match observations h calendar periods apart and disclose the chosen missing-data estimator; do not compress time silently. A constant sample has zero denominator, so the stated sample correlation is undefined. Each firm has variance 9+4 = 13; two different firms share covariance 9. The average minus its fixed mean is C + (1/10)Σu i , with variance 9+4/10 = 47/5 = 9.4. Treating ten outcomes as independent yields 13/10 = 1.3, substantially too small under this stipulated law. More firms do not average away the common month shock. A satisfactory documentation answer identifies entity and time keys, the sampling/observation mechanism, time and cross-entity shock dependence, the inference target, availability/vintage and the reasons for missing reports and entry/exit. Selecting only complete cases changes the population and can condition on future survival or reporting. Appropriate uncertainty and missingness methods require assumptions for those mechanisms; no particular estimator is justified by row count alone. Teach-back and delayed check Teach-back: explain to an analyst why the previous row can be the wrong lag, why a balanced panel can answer the wrong population question, and why quarter sales, quarter-end inventory, delivery ratios and returns require different aggregation. Then construct a stationary process with dependent observations and a nonstationary process with constant mean. Delayed check, after 48 hours: a firm reports nominal sales 90 at price index 100 in December and 121 at index 110 in February, with January unreported. Calculate both real amounts and the observed change; state whether monthly growth is known. For iid mean-zero variance-1 shocks, give the variance and lag-one covariance of Z t = ε t − ε t−1 . Delayed-check answer Real sales are 90 and 110 base-price USD, a level increase of 20 and an observed two-month growth of 110/90−1 = 2/9 ≈ 22.2222%. February monthly growth is unknown because January is unreported. Z has variance 2 and lag-one covariance −1; nonoverlapping shocks give zero covariance for |h| ≥ 2. Its mean and lag covariances do not depend on calendar time, so it is weakly stationary despite negative serial dependence. Next: computational reliability and numerical audits develops systematic ways to detect wrong units, broken identities and fragile numerical results. Later econometrics adds estimation and inference for time and panel dependence, structural breaks, missingness and richer price-index construction."}, {"title": "04.11 · Computational reliability and numerical audits", "url": "v04-c11.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "An answer needs a chain of checks Capability: verify a computational claim using explicit contracts, independent references, invariants, edge cases and numerical error analysis, then make the audit reproducible by another analyst. Prerequisite: time-series and panel data basics . We build on the error bounds of numerical methods , the types and validation of Python fundamentals , and the distinction between numerical and model uncertainty in Monte Carlo . The examples are original synthetic stress tests, not measurements of an actual institution. A treasury analyst reports a positive net cash balance and almost no variation in operating margins. Both results can be wrong even when every formula looks familiar. A net sum can lose a small receipt between large offsetting entries; a variance formula can subtract two large rounded numbers and erase all dispersion. More decimal places in the report will not repair either calculation. A computational claim includes the target quantity, input interpretation, algorithm and accuracy requirement. An audit tests that chain. First establish whether the selected rows, dates, units and economic definitions answer the question. Then check arithmetic and numerical accuracy. Integer cents can preserve an amount exactly while a missing account, wrong sign or duplicate transaction makes the economic total wrong. A test suite checks specified behaviors; it does not certify every possible program or data change. Turn the claim into a contract and independent evidence For a same-day ledger, our contract is: signed integer US cents, receipts positive, payments negative, all entries belonging to the declared day, no duplicate or omitted transactions, and no change to the caller's data. The numerical target is S = Σxᵢ in cents. “No duplicates” cannot be established from amounts alone because two real transactions can have the same amount; an actual ledger needs transaction identifiers and reconciliation to its source. The synthetic lab supplies amounts and tests arithmetic, not source completeness. For a supplied list of n margins, the target is the population variance of that list , V = (1/n)Σ(xᵢ − μ)², where μ = (1/n)Σxᵢ. It describes all entries in the list, with divisor n. Estimating a wider population variance from an iid sample is a different target whose usual unbiased estimator divides by n − 1. Neither is the standard error of the mean. State which target the code must compute before writing a test. Complementary forms of audit evidence Check Example What can survive it Known small case The population variance of [1,2,3] is 2/3. A bug triggered only by large levels. Independent reference Exact pairwise squared differences versus centered floating deviations. A shared mistake in which economic rows belong in the calculation. Invariant A variance cannot be negative; a sum reconciles across partitions. Returning zero for every variance. Transformation test Adding a constant leaves variance unchanged; multiplying amounts by k multiplies it by k². A consistently wrong denominator. Boundary and failure case One observation has population variance zero; an empty list or wrong unit fails explicitly. A mistake away from the selected boundary. Peer reproduction Another analyst runs the archived inputs and independently checks the definition and reference. A false source assumption accepted by both analysts. An invariant is a property that must hold for the specified calculation. A transformation test changes inputs in a way whose output relationship is known without calculating a new answer from scratch. It is also called a metamorphic test. “Independent” refers to a different route to the answer: copying the production formula into a second file adds little protection against a common sign, timing or denominator error. Combine a small known answer with structure-preserving transformations and a distinct reference calculation. Assertions, validation and tests serve different purposes An assertion states a programmer's expectation, such as assert exact_total([3, -1, 2]) == 4 after importing the downloaded function. Assertions are useful when running ordinary tests, but external inputs require explicit checks that remain active in every supported launch mode. A purported monetary field can decode to a boolean or float; silently converting it to integer cents can lose information. def validate_cents(values): if type(values) is not list or not values: raise ValueError(\"supply a nonempty list\") for amount in values: if type(amount) is not int: raise TypeError(\"integer cents required\") if abs(amount) > 10**18: raise ValueError(\"amount outside teaching-tool bounds\") This short validator illustrates the distinction; the complete program also bounds list length, checks the full file schema and verifies units. An invalid file should produce a diagnostic and unsuccessful exit, not an invented balance of zero. Never put a required update or validation side effect inside an assertion. An audit gate that must survive optimization can itself use if not condition: raise ValueError(...) , as the download does. The official Python assert reference states that optimized execution with -O omits assertion code. The lab is checked under both ordinary and optimized execution; its required validation and audit gates use explicit conditional exceptions. Tests should be able to detect a plausible mistake. A deliberately broken variant changes an implementation in a known harmful way, such as converting integer money to float too early or changing a variance divisor. If the intended test still passes, that test has not demonstrated sensitivity to the fault. A detected variant is evidence about that fault and fixture, not proof of complete test coverage. Tests also check contracts that do not change the scalar answer, such as input nonmutation. Precision, tolerance and units A floating-point value uses a finite set of representable numbers. We assume ordinary binary64 arithmetic, round to nearest, with 53 significant binary bits. Define the unit roundoff u = 2 −53 , approximately 1.110223 × 10 −16 . For a normal-range arithmetic operation with no overflow or problematic underflow, its rounded result can be modeled as fl(a ◦ b) = (a ◦ b)(1 + δ), with |δ| ≤ u. An exact zero needs no relative-error interpretation. These are numerical assumptions, not a model for uncertain economic measurements. The official floating-point tutorial explains binary64 representation and the distinction between displayed decimals and stored values. Our lab uses an explicit left-to-right loop for its faulty sum, because the implementation of Python's built-in sum may use a more accurate strategy. Absolute error is |computed − reference| in the output's units. Relative error divides that by |reference| when the reference is nonzero. Near zero, a relative criterion alone is unsuitable. math.isclose(a,b,rel_tol=r,abs_tol=h) accepts when |a − b| ≤ max(h, r max(|a|,|b|)). Here h has output units and r is dimensionless. With a = 0 and b = 10 −10 cents, a tolerance h = 10 −9 cents accepts the difference, while relative tolerance 10 −9 alone does not. A comparison budget must follow the calculation and decision. Setting a huge tolerance after seeing a failure hides the evidence. Reject nonfinite results before comparison: an infinite result is not an acceptable estimate merely because it compares equal to another infinity. Also distinguish computational tolerance from economic materiality. A verified numerical error below a millionth of a cent says nothing about whether the input forecast is accurate to a thousand dollars. The official math reference specifies isclose , isfinite and accurate summation through fsum . Its accuracy discussion includes platform-dependent rounding qualifications. We compare observed results with exact references instead of treating a library routine's name as an accuracy certificate. If x is in cents, x/100 is in USD and V(x/100) = V(x)/10,000 is in USD². Dividing a variance by 100 is a factor-of-100 unit error. Tests of squared quantities need squared units. Likewise a residual in an equation, an error in a root and a loss in a decision have different units; the bounds developed in Volume 2 supply conversions, not a license to compare any two small-looking decimals. Conditioning belongs to the problem; stability belongs to the algorithm Conditioning describes how the exact answer changes when the specified inputs change. For a nonzero sum S = Σxᵢ, suppose componentwise input perturbations obey |eᵢ| ≤ ε|xᵢ|. Then |Σ(xᵢ + eᵢ) − S| ≤ Σε|xᵢ|, and |ΔS|/|S| ≤ κ sum ε, where κ sum = Σ|xᵢ|/|Σxᵢ|. This bound follows from the triangle inequality and is attainable by choosing perturbations with a common sign and allowed magnitudes. Large offsetting entries can make κ sum large. If S = 0, the relative condition number is undefined, but the absolute bound remains valid. Under a different error model, |eᵢ| ≤ η cents for every entry, the absolute sum error is at most nη cents. Always name the perturbation model: an exactly recorded integer ledger is different from a list of forecasts known only approximately. Forward error compares an algorithm's output with the exact answer for the specified inputs. A backward-error interpretation asks whether the output is the exact answer for slightly perturbed inputs. An algorithm is backward stable in a stated error model when those perturbations remain appropriately small relative to arithmetic precision across its supported problems. A backward-stable method can still have a large forward error on an ill-conditioned problem. Cancellation signals possible sensitivity or loss of prior digits; subtracting two nearby exactly represented values is not by itself proof that the subtraction operation is unstable. Nicholas Higham's author-hosted What Is Numerical Stability? distinguishes forward, backward and mixed error from problem conditioning. The original sum and variance audits below turn those distinctions into calculations with explicit units. A usable bound for a sequential sum Let aᵢ be the already represented floating inputs. Start with a₁ and add the remaining n − 1 terms sequentially. Under the stated arithmetic assumptions, each input's contribution to the final answer is multiplied by at most k = n − 1 factors of the form (1 + δ). For ku < 1, the deviation of any such product from one is bounded by (1 + u) k − 1 ≤ Σ j≥1 (ku) j = ku/(1 − ku) ≡ γ k . To justify the inequality, expand the finite product, bound each product of j rounding errors by u j , and bound the number of j-factor terms by k j ; extending the resulting positive sum to infinity gives the geometric series. Thus the computed sum is Σaᵢ(1 + θᵢ), with |θᵢ| ≤ γ n−1 , and |computed − Σaᵢ| ≤ γ n−1 Σ|aᵢ|. This is a conservative absolute arithmetic bound and a componentwise backward-error interpretation of ordinary summation. It is not a bound on mistakes in choosing the entries. If original inputs xᵢ were rounded to aᵢ before summing, triangle inequality adds the representation term |Σaᵢ − Σxᵢ|. That term can be bounded by Σ|aᵢ − xᵢ| or computed exactly in a small reference problem. Our lab converts each stored float back to its exact rational value using Fraction.from_float , so it can separate representation and accumulation error. The γ bound excludes overflow and unhandled underflow; the bounded integer-valued sum cases avoid those regimes. Worked example 1: a cent disappears, then disappears earlier A synthetic ledger stress case contains receipts/payments [10 16 , 1, −10 16 ] cents. Its exact integer total is one cent. All three inputs are exactly representable as binary64 values, but spacing near 10 16 is two cents. With round-to-nearest, ties-to-even, adding one to 10 16 rounds back to 10 16 ; the final subtraction returns zero. The error is one cent. Reordering to [10 16 , −10 16 , 1] gives one. A mathematically order-invariant sum therefore exposes an arithmetic implementation issue. The relative condition number is 20,000,000,000,000,001, so a modest relative backward error can permit a large error relative to the tiny net. With n = 3, γ₂Σ|aᵢ| = [2u/(1 − 2u)](2 × 10 16 + 1) ≈ 4.440892 cents. The actual one-cent error satisfies this conservative bound. A computed zero with that bound does not certify a positive sign. The exact integer reference does establish a positive one-cent net for these specified entries; it still cannot establish source completeness or meaningful economic surplus. Now change the inputs to [2 53 + 1, −2 53 ] cents. Their exact total is again one. Conversion of the first integer to binary64 loses that cent: its stored value becomes 2 53 . Even math.fsum then returns zero, correctly summing the represented inputs. There is one cent of net representation error and zero further accumulation error. In the first case fsum returns one; in the second it cannot restore information already discarded. Use exact integer addition for an integer-cent ledger when feasible. Fractions can preserve rational arithmetic; decimal arithmetic can support a specified decimal rounding policy when constructed from decimal strings. None makes a missing fee, wrong sign or incompatible unit correct. These very large values deliberately expose arithmetic boundaries; they are not claims about real transaction scale or measured precision. Audit variance with a different identity The identity V = (1/n)Σxᵢ² − μ² is correct in exact arithmetic, but its two large terms can nearly cancel. A better computation for nearby values chooses an anchor a, forms dᵢ = xᵢ − a, computes d̄ and averages (dᵢ − d̄)². Since xᵢ − μ = dᵢ − d̄, this is the same population variance. With integer-cent inputs, subtract the anchor before conversion to float so the small offsets are retained exactly. The final mean a + d̄ can still have its own rounding; accuracy of the dispersion does not guarantee every low digit of a large displayed level. import math def centered_population_variance(values): # Contract: a validated, nonempty list of bounded integer cents. anchor = values[0] offsets = [value - anchor for value in values] mean_offset = math.fsum(offsets) / len(offsets) return math.fsum((delta - mean_offset)**2 for delta in offsets) / len(offsets) For an independent exact reference, expand every unordered pair: Σ i<j (xᵢ − xⱼ)² = nΣxᵢ² − (Σxᵢ)² = nΣ(xᵢ − μ)². Therefore V = n −2 Σ i<j (xᵢ − xⱼ)². The first equality follows because each square appears n − 1 times and the cross terms complete the square of the total. The second substitutes μ = Σxᵢ/n. Integer differences, integer squaring and a final Fraction yield an exact answer. This pairwise algorithm costs order n² operations, so it is a bounded reference for small cases, not an automatic large-data replacement. The centered implementation and the reference use different algebra and arithmetic paths. Input sensitivity of dispersion Suppose the actual inputs are xᵢ + eᵢ with |eᵢ| ≤ η in cents. Write ē for their mean error. Expanding the new centered variance gives ΔV = 2n −1 Σ(xᵢ − μ)(eᵢ − ē) + n −1 Σ(eᵢ − ē)². Since Σ(xᵢ − μ) = 0, the cross term can use eᵢ alone. Also n −1 Σ(eᵢ − ē)² = n −1 Σeᵢ² − ē² ≤ η². Cauchy–Schwarz bounds the mean absolute centered x by √V. Consequently |ΔV| ≤ 2η√V + η². The units are cents². This bound depends on dispersion and absolute input uncertainty, not on a common monetary level. Relative perturbations to a very large level can, however, imply a large η. Thus centering can remove unnecessary numerical cancellation while a poorly measured dispersion remains economically uncertain. If V = 0, the new variance is bounded by η²; a relative variance error is then undefined. Worked example 2: equal-looking margins do not have zero dispersion Three synthetic daily margin observations are [100,000,001, 100,000,002, 100,000,003] cents. Their mean is 100,000,002 cents and centered deviations are −1, 0 and 1 cents. The population variance is 2/3 cents². Pair differences are −1, −2 and −1, so the independent identity gives (1 + 4 + 1)/3² = 2/3. In dollar units it is (2/3)/10,000 = 1/15,000 USD². The raw second-moment implementation in the lab returns 0.0 on the recorded binary64 runtime: rounding large squared values and subtracting the squared mean erases the small difference. The centered implementation returns 0.6666666666666666 cents², with exact absolute error 1/27,021,597,764,222,976 cents², about 3.70 × 10 −17 . Translating the list down by 100,000,000 cents gives [1,2,3], for which the same exact variance holds; that shift exposes why agreement on small inputs alone is insufficient. If these were instead measured margins uncertain by at most η = 0.1 cent each, the derived input bound is 0.2√(2/3) + 0.01 ≈ 0.173299 cents². That economic-input bound is vastly larger than the computational error. If the objective were an unbiased estimate of a wider iid population variance, the same three deviations would produce 2/(3 − 1) = 1 cent². That is a different target, not a more accurate computation of 2/3. A nonnegative result alone would not catch the faulty zero. A tolerance loose enough to accept zero versus 2/3 would conceal the failure. The lab therefore uses the exact reference plus translation and scaling checks. Those checks also cannot suffice alone: an algorithm returning zero for every input is nonnegative, translation invariant and scales as k² times zero. The known nonzero reference is essential. Run, challenge and reproduce the audit Download the audit program , synthetic inputs and README and project rubric into one directory. Run: python3 v04-c11-audit.py python3 -O v04-c11-audit.py The program reads its neighboring input regardless of the terminal's working directory and uses only the standard library. It reports exact rational references, actual floating outputs, separated conversion/accumulation errors, a rational γ bound, transformation checks, and six detected broken variants. These faults are premature floating summation, information loss during conversion, raw second-moment cancellation, the wrong variance denominator, the wrong conversion of squared units and mutation of the input list. Its fixed challenge cases remain separate from any modified user case. The reported mean uses the exact integer total followed by one division; the bounds keep that total below 2 53 in magnitude. Its observed error is checked against u|μ|, including exact zero. The input contract allows 1–200 entries per list, ledger/probe amounts up to 10 18 cents in magnitude and margin entries up to 10 12 cents. It requires exact integer types, specified USD/cent/day labels and precisely the documented fields; duplicate fields, nonfinite constants and unsupported inputs fail. These bounds keep integer references, pair enumeration and the roundoff model manageable. The report's centered-variance comparison budget is max(10 −9 cents², 10 −12 times the exact variance), an explicit engineering test budget rather than a theorem about every execution or measurement error. The exact observed error is separately reported. Both launch modes must retain the same calculations and audit gates. Only the reported assertion-mode metadata changes between ordinary and -O execution. The baseline reports exact net one cent in both ledger cases, variance 2/3 cents² and six detected variants. “Passed” means the listed checks passed for these inputs and fixtures; it does not claim every conceivable bug was removed. A reproduction package preserves inputs, program, units, run command, output, runtime/version and the expected comparison criteria. The report includes SHA-256 fingerprints of the input bytes and program bytes: matching fingerprints are a practical way to identify the same files, not a guarantee that their contents are economically correct. A colleague should first run the saved package in a fresh working directory, then calculate one result independently and inspect the definition, not merely copy the output. Exact integer/fraction results should agree exactly. Floating fields should use the declared tolerances and recorded environment; differing runtime metadata is not a failed economic calculation. Record each discrepancy with the smallest input that reproduces it, the expected behavior, actual output and causal explanation of the fault. Preserve the failing case as a regression test, meaning a test that protects against reintroducing a repaired error. Correct the cause before changing expected results. A reviewer who changes both the program and its supposed reference to the same new formula can accidentally erase the only independent check. Exercises Specify the contract. A file contains daily cash amounts but no currency, unit, sign convention or transaction identifier. The values can be parsed as integers. List the unresolved questions before claiming an exact daily net. Explain what integer arithmetic can and cannot establish, and why two equal amounts are not necessarily duplicate transactions. Condition the sum. For x = [1,000,000, −999,990, 5] cents, compute the exact net and κ sum . Bound the net error under componentwise relative uncertainty ε = 10 −6 and, separately, absolute uncertainty of one cent per entry. Does either bound allow the net to become nonpositive? Explain what changes for [100, −100]. Audit arithmetic error. Derive the sequential-sum γ bound under its stated assumptions. For Worked example 1's first list, calculate γ₂Σ|xᵢ| approximately and compare it with the actual error. For its conversion probe, separate representation and accumulation error. Would replacing the loop with fsum repair both cases? Explain why a loose but valid error bound does not certify the sign. Use an independent variance identity. For [100000002, 100000004, 100000006] cents, compute the mean and population variance using centered deviations and pairwise differences. Translate the entries down by 100000000, then multiply them by −3: state the new mean and variance after each transformation. Convert the original variance to USD². Explain what divisor n − 1 would calculate instead. Separate sensitivity from rounding. Derive |ΔV| ≤ 2η√V + η² for entrywise absolute errors bounded by η. Apply it to [100000002,100000004,100000006] with η = 1/4 cent. If a numerical routine's actual error is 10 −12 cents², which source dominates this bound? Explain the boundary V = 0 and why a shared exact translation leaves variance unchanged. Tests with power to detect faults. A variance routine returns zero for every input. Another always uses divisor n − 1. Which of nonnegativity, permutation, translation and scaling tests can fail to catch them? Give a concrete reference fixture that catches both. A summation routine sorts the caller's list before returning an otherwise correct total. Write a check that catches the contract violation. Explain why one caught variant does not prove general correctness. Validate under optimization. Explain what is wrong with assert type(value) is int as the only input check. Write an explicit replacement that rejects booleans, floats, strings and magnitudes above 10 18 . For an approximate result near zero, choose an absolute tolerance of 10 −8 cents and relative tolerance of 10 −12 ; state whether comparisons of 0 with 10 −9 and with 10 −7 cents pass. Explain the additional nonfinite-result check. Peer reproduction memo. Run the package in ordinary and optimized modes and report the exact baseline targets, all six detected faults and one modified valid case. State what should match exactly, what needs tolerance, and what metadata can differ. Propose a minimal failing case if the modified program reports zero variance for a nonconstant list. Include a reason why matching file hashes, passing all tests and exact arithmetic still do not validate the economic claim. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria Establish the currency and whether amounts are cents, dollars or scaled units; the sign convention; the day/date/time-zone or accounting cutoff; whether entries are stocks or transactions; the population of accounts; and a source-based way to identify records, duplicates, omissions and reversals. Parsing only establishes a representation. Integer addition computes the exact sum of the supplied integer entries; it cannot infer their meanings or recover omitted transactions. Two distinct receipts can have the same amount, so deduplication by amount alone can delete real activity. A satisfactory answer separates arithmetic integrity, source reconciliation and economic scope without claiming missing metadata can be inferred from plausible magnitudes. S = 15 cents and Σ|xᵢ| = 1,999,995 cents, so κ sum = 1,999,995/15 = 133,333. Under relative input uncertainty, |ΔS| ≤ 1.999995 cents, about 13.3333% of the original net. The compatible interval [13.000005,16.999995] remains positive. Under one-cent absolute errors, |ΔS| ≤ 3 cents, giving [12,18], also positive. For [100,−100], S = 0 and relative error/relative conditioning at the output are undefined. The absolute bounds remain 200ε cents under the relative input model and 2η cents under the absolute model; no positive sign is certified. These are input-error bounds, separate from computation at fixed inputs. Expansion of n − 1 rounded additions expresses the answer as Σaᵢ times products of at most n − 1 factors (1 + δ). Bounding product deviations by the geometric majorant gives γ n−1 = (n − 1)u/[1 − (n − 1)u], and triangle inequality gives γ n−1 Σ|aᵢ|. This assumes represented finite inputs, round-to-nearest normal-range operations, no overflow/problematic underflow and (n − 1)u < 1. In the first case the bound is about 4.440892 cents, versus actual error 1 cent and zero input-conversion error. In the second, the first input rounds down by a cent; represented inputs sum to zero and subsequent summation is exact, so representation error is 1 and accumulation error 0. fsum returns one in the first case and zero in the second. A bound centered on computed zero that includes both signs cannot establish positive net cash, even though an exact integer reference can establish the sign for those entries. The mean is 100,000,004 cents. Deviations are −2,0,2; V = (4 + 0 + 4)/3 = 8/3 cents². Pairwise squared differences total 4 + 16 + 4 = 24, and 24/9 = 8/3. After subtracting 100,000,000, the list is [2,4,6], mean 4 and variance 8/3. Multiplying that translated list by −3 gives [−6,−12,−18], mean −12 and variance 9(8/3) = 24 cents². Original dollar variance is (8/3)/10,000 = 1/3,750 USD². Dividing the original squared deviations by n − 1 gives 8/2 = 4 cents², the usual unbiased sample-variance estimator under an iid wider-population interpretation; it is not the specified list's population variance. Write the centered perturbed values as (xᵢ − μ) + (eᵢ − ē) and expand their mean square. The cross term loses ē because centered x sums to zero. Its absolute value is at most 2η times the mean absolute deviation, at most 2η√V by Cauchy–Schwarz. The variance of the eᵢ is at most their second moment, at most η². For V = 8/3 and η = 1/4, the bound is (1/2)√(8/3) + 1/16 ≈ 0.878997 cents². A 10 −12 cents² numerical error is negligible beside this worst-case input bound; the bound is not a claim that the actual input error attains it. At V = 0 the perturbed variance is at most η², and a relative variance error is undefined. A shared exact translation has identical eᵢ, so eᵢ − ē = 0 and variance changes by exactly zero, a sharper statement than the generic bound. Both algorithms can pass nonnegativity, permutation, translation and quadratic scaling tests for lists where their denominators are defined. The constant-zero routine has all those algebraic properties. A fixed wrong denominator also preserves them at fixed n. The known list [1,2,3] has population variance 2/3; zero and the n − 1 result 1 both fail that reference. A one-entry population case is another useful boundary because its variance is zero while the n − 1 formula is undefined. For the mutation fault, use: values = [3, -1, 2] before = values.copy() answer = candidate_total(values) if answer != 4 or values != before: raise AssertionError(\"wrong total or input mutation\") Here candidate_total is the routine being tested; the original non-sorted order makes its sorting observable. An implementation can be correct on this fixture and wrong elsewhere, so combine independent references, meaningful transformations, boundaries and code review. A fault-specific detection result is not a universal correctness proof. Under -O an assertion can disappear, accepting an invalid value. Use an exact-type check and a separate range guard: def validate_amount(value): if type(value) is not int: raise TypeError(\"integer cents required\") if abs(value) > 10**18: raise ValueError(\"amount outside supported bounds\") This rejects True , 1.0 and \"1\" ; it accepts either sign within the bounds. The 10 −9 -cent difference passes because it is below the 10 −8 -cent absolute tolerance. The 10 −7 -cent difference fails: its relative allowance is only 10 −19 cents and its absolute allowance remains 10 −8 . Check that actual and reference values are finite before a numerical comparison; an infinity cannot be treated as a verified financial amount, and NaN indicates a failed computation rather than a valid zero. Required validation and test gates must remain active in every supported execution mode. The baseline exact ledger and conversion-probe nets are both 1 cent; the margin mean is 100,000,002 cents and variance 2/3 cents² = 1/15,000 USD². The six faults are left-to-right floating ledger addition, early float conversion, raw-moment variance, the sample divisor substituted for the population divisor, dividing a variance by 100 instead of 10,000, and mutating inputs. All six fixed detectors should report true. A valid modification changes the margin list to [7,7,7], giving mean 7 and variance 0; or use the preceding exercise's nonconstant case for an exact 8/3 variance. A minimal nonconstant failure probe is [0,2], whose population variance is 1, though a cancellation-specific failure may require a large common translation such as [100000000,100000002]. Preserve the smallest case that actually fails. Exact references and recorded input/program hashes should match when the files match. Floating values use declared tolerances; runtime/version and assertion-mode metadata can differ as documented. Both modes must preserve explicit input rejection and audit gates. Grade the memo on target/units, reproducibility record, independent expectations, detected faults, minimal-case reasoning and limitations. Matching hashes identify files; tests verify selected implications; exact arithmetic eliminates one error source. None establishes source completeness, truthful measurements or a justified decision model. Teach-back and retention Teach-back: explain the difference between a difficult numerical problem and a poor implementation of it. Use the two one-cent ledgers to distinguish input conversion from accumulation error. Derive one sum-error bound and the pairwise variance identity, then explain why exact references, invariants and peer reproduction supply different evidence. Delayed check: after a week, audit [10 16 ,3,−10 16 ] cents by exact addition and by a left-to-right float loop. Explain the difference before changing the code. Reconstruct the variance of [8,10,12] by two identities, convert its units correctly, and create a test that detects a mutation without relying on a changed scalar answer. A complete explanation states assumptions and tolerances and distinguishes numerical accuracy from input credibility. Delayed-check answers The exact ledger net is 3 cents. Under the declared binary64 ties-to-even arithmetic, the intermediate 10 16 + 3 rounds to 10 16 + 4; the explicit left-to-right loop therefore returns 4 cents. All original entries are representable, so this is accumulation error. For [8,10,12], the mean is 10 cents and population variance is (4 + 0 + 4)/3 = 8/3 cents²; the pairwise identity gives (4 + 16 + 4)/9 = 8/3. In USD² this is 1/3,750. The copy-and-compare pattern in Exercise 6 detects an input mutation even when the returned scalar sum remains correct. The next chapter, empirical integration and replication , applies this discipline to an entire analysis with provenance, transformations and an interpretable result. This chapter does not provide a universal proof of floating-point correctness, automatic economic validation or a substitute for a knowledgeable independent reviewer."}, {"title": "04.12 · Empirical integration and replication", "url": "v04-c12.html", "written": true, "status": "Author-reviewed", "text": "Make an empirical claim reproducible at its actual scope Capability: reconstruct a small published empirical result from a documented historical source, explain numerical departures, and write a replication report that separates what was reproduced from what remains unverified. Prerequisite: computational reliability and numerical audits . Earlier chapters supply data contracts, descriptive analysis, causal identification and timing-aware prediction. A report says that the United States unemployment rate rose to 4.3% in July 2024. Repeating that sentence is citation. Recovering its numerator and denominator from the appropriate archived table, calculating the rate, matching the published precision and preserving every input is a small empirical reconstruction. Rebuilding the survey weights and seasonal adjustment from person-level records would be a much larger replication. Success at the first task does not establish success at the second. This chapter performs the first task with actual historical survey estimates. The six source records below are not synthetic observations . All teaching calculations, exercises and code are original. The narrow empirical target is useful because its departures reveal the consequences of units, vintages, rounding, definitions and research discretion without hiding those choices inside a large statistical package. Freeze a claim before calculating A replicable claim has a target, a source version, a transformation and a comparison rule. Our baseline is the household-survey TOTAL unemployment statistic in the Bureau of Labor Statistics (BLS) Employment Situation — July 2024 , release USDL-24-1571, published 2 August 2024 at 08:30 Eastern Daylight Time. Use its Table A-1, seasonally adjusted (SA) columns, July and June 2024. Reconstruct July's rate, its monthly change, and the change in the unemployed level. The published targets are 4.3%, +0.2 percentage point and +352 thousand persons. The targets come from the archived BLS release , its Household Survey Data narrative and complete Table A-1 header and TOTAL rows. Observation month, publication time and retrieval date are different: July 2024 describes the observation period; 2 August 2024 identifies the release vintage; 7 October 2026 records our source inspection. Nothing here is an observation of October 2026 unemployment. The baseline transformation is 100 times unemployed persons divided by the civilian labor force. Our comparison rule is agreement at the source's one decimal place for the rate and monthly rate change, together with an exact integer-thousand comparison for the level change. Retain the unrounded rational result. A rate discrepancy of 0.047 percentage point is acceptable only if explained by the declared display rule; it is not a universal tolerance for every calculation. We also declare a four-cell comparison grid: SA and not seasonally adjusted (NSA), each with July 2024 compared to June 2024 and to July 2023. Report every cell. Compare subtracting source-displayed rates with subtracting reconstructed ratios and rounding afterward. These are fixed teaching specifications chosen with the historical answer known, not a claim of prior research registration . In a new investigation, date and preserve the protocol before inspecting the relevant outcomes; mark later additions as exploratory and retain their results. Recover the data and the meaning of each number The Current Population Survey household statistics concern the civilian noninstitutional population age 16 and over in the 50 states and District of Columbia. The labor force contains employed and unemployed persons. The unemployed generally have no job, are available for work and have actively searched during the preceding four weeks; people on temporary layoff expecting recall have a search exception. Receiving unemployment insurance is not the classification rule. BLS's CPS definitions explain these concepts. The archived release's technical note gives the corresponding definitions and the reference week, generally the calendar week containing the 12th. Let P , L , E , U and N denote the published population, labor force, employed, unemployed and not-in-labor-force levels, respectively, all measured in thousands of persons . The unemployment rate is r = 100U/L percent. The participation rate, 100L/P, answers a different question. These levels are survey estimates of people, not the number of survey respondents and not establishment-survey payroll jobs. Frozen Table A-1 TOTAL extract, 2 August 2024 vintage; all levels in thousands of persons Period Adjustment P L E U N Published r (%) July 2023 NSA 267,002 168,354 161,982 6,372 98,649 3.8 June 2024 NSA 268,438 169,007 161,774 7,233 99,431 4.3 July 2024 NSA 268,644 169,723 162,038 7,685 98,921 4.5 July 2023 SA 267,002 167,113 161,209 5,904 99,889 3.5 June 2024 SA 268,438 168,009 161,199 6,811 100,429 4.1 July 2024 SA 268,644 168,429 161,266 7,163 100,215 4.3 The full source has nine time/adjustment columns: three NSA columns followed by six SA columns. Our extract uses original positions 1, 2, 3, 4, 8 and 9. Reading a row without its full header could silently mix columns. Table A-1 footnote 1 says the population itself is not seasonally adjusted; the same population estimates appear on both sides. The table also notes annual January population-control updates. The release's technical note describes seasonal adjustment and revisions. A current database may contain later revised values: that is another vintage, not a replacement for this baseline. A small but auditable retrieval chain The archived HTML was retrieved without an account or payment through the available web retrieval service. A separate direct download attempt returned HTTP 403. The local source extract is therefore explicitly a manual transcription from the successfully retrieved complete table, not a raw HTML download. It records the original URL, full column order, row labels, units, release time, selected values and access limitation. No unavailable webpage bytes are claimed to have been preserved. The analysis JSON is a structured copy of that extract. The manifest records SHA-256 hashes for both local files. Comparing those copies catches a transformation error; comparing the printed values back to the official table addresses transcription. Agreement between two copies produced by one transcription is not independent empirical corroboration. A hash detects changed bytes relative to a trusted manifest, but does not prove that the bytes came from BLS. Someone can change a file and its manifest together; provenance still needs documented source inspection. The BLS public-use statement places its publications in the public domain except previously copyrighted photographs and illustrations and requests attribution. This package includes only a small attributed numeric extract, with no emblem or images. Retrieval access and permission to reuse are separate fields in the record; a failed automated download does not make fabricated replacement data acceptable. Worked example 1: reconstruct the monthly published result Use July SA unemployment U = 7,163 and labor force L = 168,429, both in thousands. Because their units cancel, multiplying numerator and denominator by 1,000 would leave the ratio unchanged: r July = 100(7,163/168,429) = 716,300/168,429 ≈ 4.252830570% → 4.3%. June's corresponding ratio is 100(6,811/168,009) ≈ 4.053949491% → 4.1%. Subtraction before display gives 4.252830570 − 4.053949491 = 0.198881079 percentage point, which rounds to +0.2. Subtracting the two published one-decimal rates also gives 4.3 − 4.1 = 0.2 percentage point. Both operations agree for this pair, but they need not always agree. The unemployed level change is 7,163 − 6,811 = 352 thousand persons, or 352,000 persons. July's level is 7.163 million, which rounds to the narrative's 7.2 million. All three primary targets match at their specified precision. The published 4.3 exceeds our unrounded ratio by about 0.047169430 percentage point. That is a display difference, not an estimated standard error. The calculation does not reveal the exact rate computed from undisplayed full-precision estimates. It establishes that the supplied published aggregates reproduce the published display. Nor does it reconstruct survey weighting or seasonal-adjustment estimation. Those steps generated our inputs. Our result is conditional on their release-vintage values and definitions. Explain discrepancies before changing code or inputs A useful discrepancy log states the expected output, observed output, size and unit of the departure, proposed explanation, evidence and unresolved work. Keep the original record. A genuine data error should be corrected in a new version with a change note, never silently repaired to force agreement. Three classes matter here: a harmless display difference, a different statistical target, and a calculation or transcription failure. A match at one headline number cannot distinguish them alone. Worked example 2: a disagreement caused by rounding order July 2023 SA gives r 2023 = 100(5,904/167,113) ≈ 3.532938790%, which rounds to 3.5%. The year comparison produces two legitimate descriptions: Difference of displayed rates: 4.3 − 3.5 = 0.8 percentage point. Difference of reconstructed ratios: 4.252830570 − 3.532938790 ≈ 0.719891780 percentage point → 0.7. Rounding is nonlinear: R(a) − R(b) need not equal R(a − b). Here the July 2024 display error is about +0.047169430, while the July 2023 display error is about −0.032938790. Their difference, about +0.080108220 percentage point, explains why 0.8 exceeds the reconstructed unrounded change. Neither the archive nor the analyst needs to be wrong. Report the operation alongside the number. We have not recovered the unpublished full-precision annual change. How much can rounding of the published levels alone move July's ratio? If each level is within half a thousand of its displayed integer, positivity of the denominator and monotonicity give the conservative envelope 100(7,163 − 0.5)/(168,429 + 0.5) ≤ r ≤ 100(7,163 + 0.5)/(168,429 − 0.5), 4.252521084% ≤ r ≤ 4.253140056%. Every number in that interval still rounds to 4.3%. This is a bound under a stated input-rounding assumption, not a sampling confidence interval. Because the numerator and denominator are related, not every corner of that rectangle must be jointly attainable; it nevertheless bounds all values inside. It cannot account for survey error or later revisions. There is also an accounting residual: June SA has L − E − U = 168,009 − 161,199 − 6,811 = −1 thousand. If underlying values satisfy L* = E* + U* and each displayed value has rounding error of magnitude at most 0.5, the displayed residual is e L − e E − e U , with magnitude at most 1.5 thousand. An integer-valued residual can therefore have magnitude at most 1. This observation makes the −1 compatible with rounding; it does not establish the unknown underlying values. July 2023 NSA similarly has P − L − N = −1 thousand. Keep both residuals visible. The identity check is deliberately narrow: these two aggregate identities on this six-record extract. Do not generalize it into a demand that every seasonally adjusted subgroup table must add exactly. BLS explains that seasonal adjustment may be performed on component series. A reconciliation rule must follow the actual construction and display precision of the statistic being checked. Baseline discrepancy log Record Finding Resolution or limit D01 July ratio 4.252830570%; published 4.3% Nearest-tenth displays agree for all six rates; preserve raw ratios. D02 Annual SA displayed difference 0.8 pp; ratio difference 0.719891780 pp Label rounding order; neither is an exact full-precision source rate change. D03 Two identities have −1 thousand residuals Compatible with component rounding; retain published integers. D04 Survey estimation and sampling variance are unavailable in this extract No claim to reconstruct weights, seasonal factors or sampling inference. Robustness has to answer a declared question With more time periods or adjustment choices, an analyst can select the comparison that best suits a story. A visible complete grid reduces that discretion. Our grid asks whether the direction of these descriptive changes differs across two adjustment definitions and two horizons. It does not test the same numerical estimand four times: replacing SA with NSA retains recurring seasonal variation; replacing a month with a year changes the comparison interval. All declared secondary comparisons; pp means percentage points Adjustment Comparison Ratio difference (pp) Round last (pp) Subtract displayed rates (pp) SA July 2024 − June 2024 0.198881079 0.2 0.2 SA July 2024 − July 2023 0.719891780 0.7 0.8 NSA July 2024 − June 2024 0.248261766 0.2 0.2 NSA July 2024 − July 2023 0.743084301 0.7 0.7 Every reconstructed change is positive. That limited conclusion survives these four choices. It does not establish a persistent trend from six records, and the two adjustment versions are not independent replications on new respondents. A year comparison also crosses a January population-control update; our extract does not isolate that contribution. A sensitivity check can disclose a changed definition without deciding which definition is appropriate for a new question. Robustness is a declared check of how conclusions respond to defensible choices. Fishing uses the observed results to choose and present a favorable subset while hiding the search. Exploratory analysis is useful when identified as exploratory. Preserve a specification ledger with the choice, reason, timing and result, including results that weaken the preferred claim. Do not widen a tolerance after seeing a failure and still call the original specification successful. A new tolerance requires a justified new analysis and a retained record of the old one. What the reconstruction cannot establish Agreement demonstrates arithmetic and source-definition compatibility for a specified release. Sampling uncertainty remains. The labor-force estimate 168,429 thousand is a population-level estimate, not 168,429,000 independent Bernoulli trials. Putting that number into √[p(1 − p)/n] would invent a sampling design and grossly confuse estimated population size with respondent count. The CPS design, weights, estimation procedures and dependence between monthly estimates matter for uncertainty in a change. This package supplies no survey-standard-error calculation and no significance declaration. The archived technical note discusses sampling and nonsampling errors, seasonal adjustment and revisions. These limitations are not removed by exact rational arithmetic. Our rounding envelope addresses a much smaller, different issue. It is inappropriate to relabel it “95% confidence,” or to treat an illustrative uncertainty number elsewhere in the note as an estimated interval for this specific result. The descriptive increase also lacks a treatment definition and counterfactual. Changes in a policy, interest rates or job search may be plausible research topics; this reconstruction identifies none of their causal effects. Nor does it forecast August or establish the performance of a prediction model: there is no fitted forecasting rule, information-time design or held-out evaluation. Use identification and confounding and prediction and validation to formulate those additional tasks. Run and report the offline reproduction Download the source extract , analysis data , provenance and hash manifest , standard-library Python benchmark and instructions and rubric into one directory. The preserved benchmark report provides a comparison output. No network call or third-party package is needed: python3 v04-c12-replication.py # Or give the directory containing the three required input files: python3 v04-c12-replication.py /path/to/package The program validates types, units, keys, release metadata and local file hashes; parses the source extract independently of the analysis JSON; then calculates exact fractions and display values. It reports all six rates, the full comparison grid, component residuals and a discrepancy log. Invalid input produces a diagnostic and a nonzero exit status. A valid but numerically mismatching reconstruction also returns a nonzero status. It never rewrites the frozen inputs. Floating decimals in the output are conveniences; the exact fraction fields preserve the underlying ratio calculations. A complete report includes: the target and scope; source and access route; observation and release dates; file identifiers and hashes; definitions and units; processing steps and rounding rules; original versus reconstructed results; every declared sensitivity result; discrepancies and unresolved issues; and a bounded conclusion. Record the Python version as execution metadata. An independent reviewer should recompute a few quantities by a different route and inspect the source header, not merely run the same function twice. Project deliverables: run the package unchanged, preserve its report, independently calculate the baseline and one annual comparison, and submit a one-page replication memo plus a specification ledger. In a separate temporary copy, change June SA labor force to 168,109 thousand, explicitly label it an invented error, and explain why both byte-integrity and identity checks are useful. Preserve the original files. The README supplies scoring criteria and the benchmark numbers; a successful assignment requires both faithful calculation and an honest scope statement. Exercises Write the baseline target so another researcher cannot accidentally use a current revised series, an NSA column or a payroll denominator. State the observation month, release date, source-inspection date, population and units. Explain why these dates differ. Using only the frozen NSA levels, reconstruct July 2024's rate and its change from June. Report the ratio to six decimals, the monthly difference in percentage points to six decimals, their one-decimal displays and the change in unemployed persons. Does the NSA rate reproduce the SA headline? Reconstruct the SA July-to-July rate change in two ways: difference of published rates and difference of level ratios. Explain their discrepancy numerically using the two display errors. Does either operation recover BLS's undisplayed full-precision estimates? A memo defines July's unemployment rate as 100U/P, then describes 4.3 − 4.1 as “a 0.2% increase.” Calculate 100U/P and 100U/E from July SA levels, identify the proper denominator, and calculate the relative percent increase implied by the two published rates. Explain why treating the labor-force level as the survey sample size would be another error. Derive the maximum possible integer residual in L − E − U if an exact underlying identity is preserved and each displayed integer is rounded within half a thousand. Check June SA. In a deliberately corrupted copy, replace its L by 168,109 while keeping E and U fixed. Show that a check of the rounded unemployment rate alone misses this error while the identity check detects it. Reconstruct the complete four-cell SA/NSA by month/year grid to six decimal places. A researcher presents only the larger NSA monthly change as evidence that a policy caused unemployment. Identify both the reporting problem and the identification problem. State one conclusion supported by the full grid and one it does not support. An analyst changes whitespace in the JSON without changing any values; another changes a value in both the JSON and text extract and regenerates both hashes. Explain what the benchmark's integrity checks can and cannot establish in these cases. Give a defensible workflow for a newly revised official series. Write a replication memo of at most 200 words. Include the original targets, reconstructed outputs, release vintage, important discrepancies, scope of success and two remaining inference limits. Describe an independent check and distinguish a rounding envelope from a sampling interval. Complete solutions and evaluation criteria The target is the July 2024 U.S. civilian noninstitutional population age 16 and over household-survey TOTAL, SA unemployment rate and June-to-July changes from Table A-1 and the narrative in the BLS release published 2 August 2024 at 08:30 EDT. Source inspection occurred 7 October 2026. Rates are percent; rate changes are percentage points; levels and their tabular changes are thousands of persons. Use U/L, with L the civilian labor force, not payroll jobs or population. July identifies the observation period, August identifies information availability/vintage, and October 2026 identifies this package's retrieval. Full credit requires all three dates and an explicit SA/household/labor-force specification. July NSA: 100(7,685/169,723) = 4.527966% to six decimals, displayed 4.5%. June NSA: 100(7,233/169,007) ≈ 4.279704391%. Their difference is 0.248262 percentage point to six decimals, displayed 0.2. The unemployed count rises by 7,685 − 7,233 = 452 thousand = 452,000 persons. These values reconstruct the NSA columns. July's 4.5% does not match the 4.3% SA target because the adjustment definition changed; that is not evidence of an arithmetic failure in the SA reconstruction. Displayed rates give 4.3 − 3.5 = 0.8 percentage point. Reconstructed ratios give 100(7,163/168,429 − 5,904/167,113) ≈ 0.719891780, displayed 0.7. The display errors are 4.3 − 4.252830570 ≈ +0.047169430 and 3.5 − 3.532938790 ≈ −0.032938790. Subtracting those errors yields +0.080108220; add that to the ratio difference to obtain 0.8. The disagreement is rounding order. Both calculations use already published rounded inputs; neither recovers exact undisplayed estimates. 100U/P = 100(7,163/268,644) ≈ 2.666354%; 100U/E = 100(7,163/161,266) ≈ 4.441730%. Neither is the unemployment rate, which uses L = E + U conceptually and here gives about 4.252831%. The displayed rate increases by 0.2 percentage point . Its relative increase is 100(4.3/4.1 − 1) ≈ 4.878049%. A relative change formed from the unrounded reconstructed ratios would be another calculation. The estimated number of people in the labor force is not the number of independently sampled respondents; an iid binomial standard error based on it has no justified sampling interpretation here. Write each displayed count as its underlying count plus e with |e| ≤ 0.5. The exact identity cancels, leaving e L − e E − e U , with magnitude at most 1.5. Because displayed residuals are integers, their magnitude is at most 1. June gives −1 and is compatible. The invented L = 168,109 produces 168,109 − 161,199 − 6,811 = 99 thousand, outside that bound. Yet 100(6,811/168,109) ≈ 4.051538% still rounds to 4.1%. Thus one displayed-rate match misses a serious inconsistency. The untouched manifest also detects the altered file bytes. Do not replace June's actual source value with the invented value. The grid is SA month 0.198881, SA year 0.719892, NSA month 0.248262 and NSA year 0.743084 percentage point. Their rounding-after-subtraction displays are 0.2, 0.7, 0.2 and 0.7. Subtracting displayed rates instead gives 0.2, 0.8, 0.2 and 0.7. Omitting the other declared specifications selectively reports the larger monthly contrast. Even complete reporting would not identify a policy effect: there is no specified intervention/comparison design establishing the missing counterfactual. Supported: these four descriptive contrasts are positive. Unsupported: a policy caused the change, the increase will persist, or the four cells are independent statistical replications. Alternative unsupported claims receive credit if their missing assumptions are explained. Whitespace changes the bytes and therefore generally the SHA-256 hash, even when parsed values are unchanged. The unchanged manifest correctly flags departure from the frozen byte artifact. If someone changes both copies and recomputes hashes, local consistency and hashing can pass without truth to the original source; numerical target/identity checks might catch some changes but cannot certify origin. Keep the original package immutable, retrieve the new official vintage into distinctly named files, document its release/retrieval dates and changes, independently inspect the new source, and run a separate analysis. Do not overwrite the old vintage and claim that its reproduction succeeded. Full credit requires both the integrity/provenance distinction and retained original data. Illustrative memo: “We reconstructed the BLS July 2024 household-survey TOTAL SA unemployment statistics in the 2 August 2024 release. Published targets were 4.3%, a monthly rise of 0.2 percentage point and 352,000 additional unemployed persons. Frozen Table A-1 levels give 100(7,163/168,429) = 4.252830570%, a monthly difference of 0.198881079 percentage point and a 352-thousand count change, matching the target displays. Independent division checked the rate and a separately parsed source extract checked the transcription. Annual SA changes differ by rounding order: displayed rates subtract to 0.8 point; reconstructed ratios differ by 0.719891780 point. Two component identities have −1-thousand residuals compatible with rounding. The success concerns published aggregates, not reconstruction of survey weights or seasonal factors. The July input-rounding envelope, 4.252521084–4.253140056%, is not a sampling interval. No sampling significance, policy effect or forecast has been established.” Evaluate accuracy, units/vintage, evidence for discrepancies, an actual independent calculation, preservation of limits and the 200-word bound; equivalent clear memos are acceptable. Teach-back and delayed check Teach-back: explain to a colleague why 0.8 and 0.7 percentage point can both be correctly calculated from one release, why one thousand of nonaddition need not be a bad transcription, and why a matching hash cannot prove official origin. Then state exactly what the replication accomplished in one sentence. Delayed check, after several days: without the worked examples, reconstruct July's SA rate from U = 7,163 and L = 168,429 thousand; identify the unit of 4.3 − 4.1; describe one failure a rounded-rate check misses; and name one missing ingredient for sampling inference and one for causal inference. Check against 4.252830570% → 4.3%, percentage points, the corrupted-denominator identity example, a justified survey variance calculation and a counterfactual identification design. Revisit the relevant section for each missed distinction. Next: complete the Volume 4 assessment and integrative project . The later empirical-research work in Volume 12 and Volume 16 extends this bounded reconstruction to econometric designs and research replications. Preserve this chapter's source, target, discrepancy and inference disciplines as the statistical machinery grows."}, {"title": "05.01 · Preferences and consumer choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive demands and identify corner solutions under a budget constraint. Preferences utility indifference curves budget sets Marshallian demand revealed preference"}, {"title": "05.02 · Demand, duality and welfare", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Decompose price responses and measure welfare with correct income concepts. Hicksian demand expenditure function Slutsky indirect utility compensating variation"}, {"title": "05.03 · Production, costs and firm choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive cost functions and output/input choices from technology. Production sets marginal products cost minimization fixed and sunk cost supply"}, {"title": "05.04 · Competitive equilibrium and surplus", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve partial equilibrium and distinguish efficiency from distribution. Market clearing consumer and producer surplus elasticity entry tax incidence"}, {"title": "05.05 · General equilibrium and welfare", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze exchange and production interactions across markets. Edgeworth box Walras law Pareto efficiency welfare theorems missing markets"}, {"title": "05.06 · Monopoly, oligopoly and pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve market-power models and compare price and quantity competition. Monopoly markup price discrimination Cournot Bertrand entry barriers"}, {"title": "05.07 · Game theory and strategic behavior", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify equilibria and credible strategies in static and dynamic games. Dominance Nash mixed strategies extensive form backward induction commitment"}, {"title": "05.08 · Information, signaling and selection", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain how asymmetric information changes trade and incentives. Adverse selection signaling screening pooling separating equilibria lemons"}, {"title": "05.09 · Contracts, incentives and organizations", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve simple principal-agent problems and evaluate incentive trade-offs. Moral hazard participation incentive compatibility monitoring multitasking firm boundaries"}, {"title": "05.10 · Externalities and public goods", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare corrective instruments under informational and institutional constraints. External costs Pigouvian taxes public goods free riding Coase conditions congestion"}, {"title": "05.11 · Auctions, matching and market design", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze allocation rules and incentive properties in designed markets. Auction formats revenue equivalence conditions matching stability strategy-proofness"}, {"title": "05.12 · Behavioral economics and microeconomic evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v05-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate departures from standard choice and the evidence needed to support interventions. Biases reference dependence time inconsistency experiments welfare interpretation replication"}, {"title": "06.01 · National accounts and macro measurement", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reconcile output, expenditure and income measures and explain what GDP omits. GDP value added expenditure identity deflators real income stocks versus flows"}, {"title": "06.02 · Growth and the Solow model", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive steady states and distinguish level effects from sustained growth. Capital accumulation depreciation population technology convergence growth accounting"}, {"title": "06.03 · Innovation, human capital and institutions", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare mechanisms of long-run development with appropriate evidence. Endogenous growth human capital diffusion institutions geography identification"}, {"title": "06.04 · Consumption, saving and investment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive intertemporal decisions and connect household and firm behavior to aggregates. Permanent income life cycle liquidity constraints investment adjustment costs expectations"}, {"title": "06.05 · Labor markets and unemployment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain employment dynamics using search, incentives and institutional mechanisms. Participation matching unemployment flows wage setting efficiency wages hysteresis"}, {"title": "06.06 · Money, banking and monetary transmission", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace monetary-policy mechanisms without relying on a mechanical deposit multiplier. Money demand policy rates reserves bank balance sheets credit transmission"}, {"title": "06.07 · Inflation, expectations and nominal rigidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain inflation dynamics and distinguish supply from demand disturbances. Quantity theory conditions Phillips curves expectations price stickiness shocks"}, {"title": "06.08 · Business cycles and stabilization models", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare short-run models and identify their assumptions and predictions. IS-LM aggregate demand-supply RBC introduction New Keynesian introduction stabilization"}, {"title": "06.09 · Fiscal policy and public debt", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive debt dynamics and evaluate fiscal trade-offs with explicit assumptions. Budget constraint debt-to-GDP multipliers automatic stabilizers fiscal space distribution"}, {"title": "06.10 · Trade and comparative advantage", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive gains and distributional effects from trade models. Ricardian factor endowments scale firms tariffs terms of trade"}, {"title": "06.11 · Exchange rates and external balances", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Connect international accounts, exchange-rate mechanisms and policy regimes. Balance of payments net foreign assets exchange rates PPP interest parity capital flows"}, {"title": "06.12 · Crises, policy regimes and macroeconomic judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v06-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare crisis mechanisms and evaluate a country case without claiming certainty from one model. Financial crises sudden stops sovereign distress historical comparison scenario analysis"}, {"title": "07.01 · Transactions and double-entry accounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Record transactions and preserve the accounting equation. Debits credits journals accruals recognition transaction analysis"}, {"title": "07.02 · Balance sheet, income statement and cash flow", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain and reconcile the three principal statements. Assets liabilities equity revenues expenses operating-investing-financing cash flows"}, {"title": "07.03 · Revenue, working capital and operating cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Model operating cash needs and distinguish reported sales from cash collection. Receivables inventory payables revenue timing cash conversion cycle allowances"}, {"title": "07.04 · Long-lived assets and investment accounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Track asset investment and distinguish accounting expense from economic depreciation. Capitalization depreciation amortization impairment disposals investment assets"}, {"title": "07.05 · Debt, leases and financing obligations", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze financing obligations and their effects on performance and cash flow. Borrowing interest leases covenants commitments effective interest disclosure"}, {"title": "07.06 · Equity, consolidation and ownership", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace equity claims and distinguish ownership, control and reported group results. Share capital retained earnings dilution minority interests consolidation concepts buybacks"}, {"title": "07.07 · Taxes, accruals and earnings quality", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify timing differences and assess sustainability of reported earnings. Current and deferred tax concepts accruals provisions one-offs manipulation incentives"}, {"title": "07.08 · Managerial accounting and cost decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use contribution margins and relevant costs for operational decisions. Cost behavior break-even allocation activity-based costing make-or-buy capacity"}, {"title": "07.09 · Financial ratios and business diagnosis", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Interpret ratios in context and explain accounting comparability limitations. Liquidity leverage margins turnover ROIC DuPont peer selection"}, {"title": "07.10 · Three-statement forecasting", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build linked forecasts with a transparent financing policy. Revenue drivers operating expenses working capital capex financing cash balancing"}, {"title": "07.11 · Accounting standards, disclosure and comparability", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Identify where accounting-policy differences require adjustment and dated source verification. IFRS and US GAAP concepts disclosure estimates restatements segment reporting jurisdiction"}, {"title": "07.12 · Forensic analysis and accounting integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v07-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Diagnose a business using reconciled accounts and explicit alternative explanations. Cash-versus-profit gaps covenant risk red flags normalization business model economics"}, {"title": "08.01 · Value creation and capital budgeting", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate incremental project cash flows and reconcile NPV with decision criteria. NPV IRR opportunity costs sunk costs cash flow mutually exclusive projects"}, {"title": "08.02 · Free cash flow and discounting", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive cash flows to the firm and equity and choose consistent discount rates. FCFF FCFE reinvestment terminal value nominal-real consistency currency consistency"}, {"title": "08.03 · Valuation models and terminal assumptions", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value an operating business using explicit growth and reinvestment economics. DCF perpetuities value drivers ROIC growth fade terminal constraints"}, {"title": "08.04 · Risk, cost of capital and WACC", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate financing costs and explain when WACC is inappropriate. Cost of equity beta introduction debt costs market weights leverage project risk"}, {"title": "08.05 · Capital structure and financing frictions", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare financing policies under taxes, distress and incentive problems. Modigliani-Miller assumptions trade-off pecking order agency debt overhang"}, {"title": "08.06 · Payout, liquidity and financial policy", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Choose payout and liquidity policies consistent with investment and constraints. Dividends buybacks retained cash signaling working capital financial flexibility"}, {"title": "08.07 · Relative valuation and market evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use comparable valuations with economically meaningful denominators and peer choices. Enterprise-equity bridge multiples normalization comparability cycle effects triangulation"}, {"title": "08.08 · Real options and investment timing", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value flexibility and identify where static NPV understates contingent choices. Delay expand abandon staged investment decision trees information value"}, {"title": "08.09 · Mergers, acquisitions and control", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate acquisition economics and distinguish synergy from wealth transfers. Purchase price synergy integration control premiums financing dilution versus value"}, {"title": "08.10 · Private equity, venture capital and entrepreneurship", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze illiquid ownership structures and financing incentives. LBO venture rounds cap tables preferred claims exits fund economics selection"}, {"title": "08.11 · Distress, restructuring and governance", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare restructuring alternatives and their incentive and valuation consequences. Insolvency concepts priority recovery refinancing governance stakeholder conflicts"}, {"title": "08.12 · Corporate finance integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v08-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a financing and investment plan under alternative operating and market conditions. Integrated valuation capital allocation downside covenants scenario consistency board memo"}, {"title": "09.01 · Returns, payoffs and investment opportunity sets", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Measure investment outcomes with correct timing, distributions and benchmark conventions. Simple-log returns total return compounding cash flows gross-net returns opportunity sets"}, {"title": "09.02 · Portfolio mathematics and diversification", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compute portfolio risk and explain the role of covariance and concentration. Mean covariance matrix correlations diversification estimation error leverage"}, {"title": "09.03 · Mean-variance optimization and allocation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive efficient portfolios and assess sensitivity to estimated inputs. Efficient frontier minimum variance tangency constraints optimization shrinkage"}, {"title": "09.04 · CAPM and equilibrium risk pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive the CAPM mechanism and distinguish its theoretical assumptions from empirical evidence. Market portfolio beta security market line alpha equilibrium testing limitations"}, {"title": "09.05 · Arbitrage, state prices and discount factors", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Price contingent payoffs using no-arbitrage and stochastic discount-factor reasoning. Replication state prices pricing kernel completeness fundamental pricing equation"}, {"title": "09.06 · Factor models and empirical asset pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate factor exposures and distinguish explanatory fits from investable premiums. APT multifactor models cross-sectional pricing factor construction omitted factors"}, {"title": "09.07 · Market efficiency, anomalies and behavior", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate apparent predictability with information, risk and implementation costs in view. Efficiency forms joint hypothesis anomalies behavioral mechanisms limits to arbitrage"}, {"title": "09.08 · Active management and performance evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess whether performance reflects skill, risk, selection or luck. Benchmark choice alpha attribution information ratio persistence fees survivorship"}, {"title": "09.09 · Strategic and dynamic asset allocation", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design allocation policies across horizons and investor constraints. Lifecycle liabilities hedging demand rebalancing tactical decisions estimation risk"}, {"title": "09.10 · Alternative assets and illiquidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare nontraditional investments with transparent valuation and liquidity assumptions. Real estate private markets commodities hedge funds appraisal smoothing lockups"}, {"title": "09.11 · Execution, trading costs and market microstructure", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate execution and liquidity effects on feasible investment strategies. Bid-ask spread impact order types information execution benchmarks capacity"}, {"title": "09.12 · Investment policy and portfolio integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v09-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a mandate and portfolio using economic objectives and robust evidence. IPS governance benchmarks risk budget costs stress scenarios reporting"}, {"title": "10.01 · Bond cash flows and yield curves", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Price cash flows with spot discount factors and distinguish yield conventions. Coupon schedules clean-dirty price discount curves YTM spot-forward rates day-count assumptions"}, {"title": "10.02 · Duration, convexity and immunization", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive rate sensitivity and construct hedges with explicit residual risks. Macaulay-modified duration convexity key rates immunization nonparallel shifts"}, {"title": "10.03 · Term-structure models and rate risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare curve models and distinguish physical from risk-neutral dynamics. Expectations term premiums short-rate models no-arbitrage calibration curve scenarios"}, {"title": "10.04 · Credit risk and recovery", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Value defaultable claims and separate hazard, recovery and liquidity assumptions. Credit spreads structural models intensity recovery transition correlation"}, {"title": "10.05 · Forwards, futures and swaps", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive contract values and explain financing, collateral and basis effects. Cost of carry forward-futures distinction swaps collateral basis margin"}, {"title": "10.06 · Options and binomial replication", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Construct replicating portfolios and price options through state-contingent payoffs. Payoffs put-call parity binomial tree risk-neutral probability American exercise"}, {"title": "10.07 · Brownian motion and Ito calculus", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive stochastic differentials and explain quadratic variation. Filtrations Brownian motion stochastic integral Ito lemma SDE drift-diffusion"}, {"title": "10.08 · Black-Scholes and risk-neutral valuation", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive the pricing equation and distinguish a model's assumptions from market conventions. Replication PDE risk-neutral measure Black-Scholes formula dividends boundaries"}, {"title": "10.09 · Greeks, volatility and hedging", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze hedge sensitivities and the implications of changing volatility assumptions. Delta gamma vega theta implied volatility smiles discrete hedging"}, {"title": "10.10 · Numerical pricing and Monte Carlo", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Implement pricing methods with convergence and error diagnostics. Trees finite differences simulation variance reduction early exercise numerical checks"}, {"title": "10.11 · Incomplete markets and advanced derivatives", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain why some contingent claims cannot be uniquely priced by replication. Incompleteness stochastic volatility jumps exotic payoffs credit derivatives bounds"}, {"title": "10.12 · Quantitative model governance", "url": "curriculum.html#v10-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Validate pricing and risk models against independent benchmarks and failure scenarios. Calibration model risk implementation errors limitations validation controlled use"}, {"title": "11.01 · Financial intermediation and bank economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain the economic functions and fragilities of financial intermediaries. Screening monitoring maturity transformation deposits bank income capital"}, {"title": "11.02 · Bank balance sheets, capital and liquidity", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reconcile bank accounts and distinguish liquidity shortages from insolvency. Assets funding equity capital ratios concepts liquidity buffers accounting-economic value"}, {"title": "11.03 · Credit analysis and lending decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate creditworthiness using cash flow, incentives and downside protection. Debt service leverage covenants collateral repayment sources credit underwriting"}, {"title": "11.04 · Market risk and portfolio loss", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare risk measures and state their information and distributional limitations. VaR expected shortfall scenarios tails dependence horizon backtesting"}, {"title": "11.05 · Liquidity, funding and asset-liability management", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze funding gaps and contingent cash needs across scenarios. Liquidity ladders ALM rollover collateral margin maturity mismatch"}, {"title": "11.06 · Operational, legal and model risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design controls around human, operational and modeling failure mechanisms. Process controls cyber concepts fraud legal uncertainty model validation incident learning"}, {"title": "11.07 · Stress testing and risk aggregation", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build internally consistent stress scenarios without double counting exposures. Scenario design nonlinear losses contagion concentration reverse stress aggregation"}, {"title": "11.08 · Insurance, pensions and household finance", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate long-horizon liabilities and intermediary incentives. Insurance pooling adverse selection pensions longevity guarantees household credit"}, {"title": "11.09 · Currency, sovereign and cross-border risk", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze international claims with explicit currency and jurisdiction assumptions. FX exposure hedging sovereign debt capital flows country risk convertibility"}, {"title": "11.10 · Financial regulation and incentives", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate regulatory purposes and trade-offs using dated jurisdiction-specific sources. Prudential regulation resolution conduct systemic externalities arbitrage regulatory capture"}, {"title": "11.11 · Financial crises and systemic mechanisms", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Trace balance-sheet feedback, runs and contagion in a crisis model. Runs fire sales leverage cycles networks lender of last resort resolution"}, {"title": "11.12 · Risk governance and institutional integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v11-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an institution's risk appetite and response to a combined stress scenario. Risk limits board oversight escalation incentives decision logs contingency plans"}, {"title": "12.01 · Identification, estimands and asymptotic tools", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Separate what data identify from what an estimator computes and justify limiting arguments. Estimands identification consistency asymptotic normality delta method stochastic orders"}, {"title": "12.02 · Linear models and robust inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive linear estimators and choose inference appropriate to dependence and heterogeneity. OLS GLS heteroskedasticity clustering serial correlation finite-sample concerns"}, {"title": "12.03 · Instrumental variables and GMM", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive moment-based estimators and evaluate instrument relevance and validity. IV 2SLS weak instruments exclusion GMM overidentification local effects"}, {"title": "12.04 · Likelihood and nonlinear models", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Estimate nonlinear models and distinguish functional-form identification from causal identification. MLE score information binary response count selection marginal effects"}, {"title": "12.05 · Panel data and longitudinal identification", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate within-unit identification and dynamic-panel assumptions. Fixed-random effects correlated heterogeneity dynamic panels attrition dependence"}, {"title": "12.06 · Experiments and causal inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design and evaluate interventions with appropriate estimands and assignment mechanisms. Potential outcomes randomization compliance interference attrition power randomization inference"}, {"title": "12.07 · Difference-in-differences and event studies", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend counterfactual trends and handle treatment timing and heterogeneous effects. Parallel trends anticipation staggered adoption event studies weighting sensitivity"}, {"title": "12.08 · Regression discontinuity and synthetic controls", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate local and comparative designs with transparent support and validity conditions. Continuity manipulation bandwidth fuzzy RD donor pools synthetic control placebo analysis"}, {"title": "12.09 · Time series, forecasting and financial econometrics", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze persistent and dependent economic series with model-specific inference. ARMA VAR unit roots cointegration volatility GARCH forecast evaluation"}, {"title": "12.10 · Structural estimation and economic mechanisms", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Connect economic models to data and distinguish calibration from identified estimation. Structural parameters moments simulation equilibrium indirect inference sensitivity"}, {"title": "12.11 · Bayesian, high-dimensional and machine-learning methods", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Apply modern methods while preserving valid inference and honest model evaluation. Bayesian computation regularization cross-fitting double robustness causal ML multiple testing"}, {"title": "12.12 · Econometric research audit", "url": "curriculum.html#v12-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Independently audit a complete empirical paper and defend methodological choices. Identification robustness inference specification search reproducibility external validity"}, {"title": "13.01 · Preference representation and choice theory", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Establish representation and choice results under precise axioms. Preferences continuity utility representation revealed preference rationalizability"}, {"title": "13.02 · Consumer and producer duality", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive duality identities and characterize economically admissible demand and cost functions. Expenditure profit integrability Slutsky symmetry homogeneity envelope identities"}, {"title": "13.03 · Uncertainty and information", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze risk attitudes and the economic value of information formally. Expected utility stochastic dominance risk aversion Bayesian information Blackwell ordering"}, {"title": "13.04 · General equilibrium and existence", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Explain and apply equilibrium existence arguments and their assumptions. Exchange-production economies fixed points excess demand Walras law existence uniqueness limits"}, {"title": "13.05 · Welfare, aggregation and social choice", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Prove welfare results and assess aggregation and distributional restrictions. Welfare theorems aggregation social welfare impossibility collective choice"}, {"title": "13.06 · Static games and refinements", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Establish strategic equilibria and analyze equilibrium selection. Mixed-strategy existence Bayesian games best responses refinements correlated equilibrium"}, {"title": "13.07 · Dynamic and repeated games", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze sequential incentives and intertemporal cooperation with credible strategies. Subgame perfection sequential equilibrium repeated games reputation folk-theorem conditions"}, {"title": "13.08 · Information economics and screening", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve screening and signaling models with participation and incentive constraints. Single crossing menus adverse selection signaling equilibrium selection"}, {"title": "13.09 · Contracts and dynamic incentives", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive optimal contracts under hidden action and limited commitment. Principal-agent sufficient statistics limited liability dynamic contracts relational incentives"}, {"title": "13.10 · Mechanism design and auctions", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive incentive-compatible allocation and payment rules with explicit informational assumptions. Revelation principle implementation optimal auctions incentive compatibility transfers"}, {"title": "13.11 · Matching, networks and market design", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze stable allocations and strategic interactions on markets and networks. Matching stability assignment network games externalities design constraints"}, {"title": "13.12 · Theory synthesis and research critique", "url": "curriculum.html#v13-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate a theoretical contribution through proofs, counterexamples and economic relevance. Model choice theorem scope proof checking robustness interpretation theoretical referee report"}, {"title": "14.01 · Dynamic optimization and recursive methods", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive Bellman and Euler conditions and establish valid numerical solutions. Bellman principle contraction Euler equations transversality policy iteration value iteration"}, {"title": "14.02 · Stochastic growth and real business cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve a stochastic growth model and evaluate its empirical mechanisms. Planner competitive equilibrium shocks labor perturbation calibration moments"}, {"title": "14.03 · Incomplete markets and heterogeneous agents", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze distributional state variables and precautionary decisions in incomplete markets. Aiyagari Huggett borrowing constraints distributions aggregation numerical methods"}, {"title": "14.04 · Search, matching and labor dynamics", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive dynamic labor-market equilibria and evaluate their implications. Matching functions bargaining vacancy creation unemployment dynamics shocks"}, {"title": "14.05 · New Keynesian models and monetary policy", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive a nominal-rigidity model and compare policy rules under explicit approximations. Household-firm problems Calvo NK Phillips curve dynamic IS Taylor rules determinacy"}, {"title": "14.06 · Fiscal policy and sovereign debt", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze dynamic public finance and sovereign default mechanisms. Government constraints fiscal-monetary interaction default sustainability redistribution"}, {"title": "14.07 · Consumption-based asset pricing", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive stochastic discount factors and evaluate major pricing puzzles. Euler equation consumption risk equity premium risk-free rate habits long-run risks"}, {"title": "14.08 · Investment, debt and financial frictions", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Derive how financing constraints alter investment and aggregate dynamics. q theory agency collateral debt overhang financial accelerator balance sheets"}, {"title": "14.09 · Intermediaries, liquidity and leverage cycles", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze financial-sector amplification and endogenous risk. Intermediary capital leverage liquidity runs risk-bearing capacity feedback"}, {"title": "14.10 · International macro-finance", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Solve open-economy dynamic models with financial and exchange-rate mechanisms. Risk sharing sudden stops sovereign risk currencies global financial conditions"}, {"title": "14.11 · Quantitative solution and model evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess macroeconomic models through transparent estimation and numerical diagnostics. Perturbation global methods likelihood-moment methods identification validation sensitivity"}, {"title": "14.12 · Macro-finance research frontier", "url": "curriculum.html#v14-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Critically compare frontier contributions and develop a tractable research question. Heterogeneity financial networks monetary transmission intermediary pricing current literature protocol"}, {"title": "15.01 · Public economics and taxation", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze tax and spending decisions with efficiency and distribution in view. Tax incidence optimal taxation public goods social insurance welfare weights"}, {"title": "15.02 · Labor economics and human capital", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate wage, employment and education mechanisms using credible designs. Human capital returns to education labor supply-demand discrimination mobility"}, {"title": "15.03 · Industrial organization and competition", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess market conduct and competitive policy using models and evidence. Demand estimation entry pricing merger analysis platforms market definition"}, {"title": "15.04 · Development, poverty and structural change", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Compare poverty-reduction mechanisms and their institutional constraints. Poverty measurement credit health education structural transformation field experiments"}, {"title": "15.05 · International trade and global production", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze firm and worker responses to trade and production networks. Heterogeneous firms trade costs global value chains tariffs distribution adjustment"}, {"title": "15.06 · Political economy and institutions", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze collective decisions and institutional incentives with defensible identification. Voting accountability lobbying bureaucracy state capacity capture"}, {"title": "15.07 · Economic history and historical inference", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Use historical evidence to distinguish mechanisms and avoid anachronistic causal claims. Industrialization institutions crises historical data natural experiments comparative inference"}, {"title": "15.08 · Behavioral and experimental economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design and critique evidence about nonstandard preferences and beliefs. Behavioral models experiments replication external validity welfare intervention"}, {"title": "15.09 · Environmental, energy and climate economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate externalities and long-horizon choices under uncertainty. Carbon pricing discounting resource economics damages energy markets distribution"}, {"title": "15.10 · Health, education and demographic economics", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess service provision and demographic change through incentives and evidence. Insurance health markets education production fertility aging public policy"}, {"title": "15.11 · Urban economics, housing and inequality", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Analyze spatial decisions and distribution with equilibrium feedback. Location housing supply amenities sorting migration inequality mobility"}, {"title": "15.12 · Policy synthesis and institutional feasibility", "url": "curriculum.html#v15-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an implementable policy with evidence, uncertainty and distributional consequences. Cost-benefit political constraints administrative capacity counterfactuals monitoring evaluation"}, {"title": "16.01 · Choosing a field and a research agenda", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Select a tractable specialization and define an economically significant question. Comparative advantage scope feasibility contribution field boundaries macro-finance default"}, {"title": "16.02 · Literature mapping and critical reading", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Map a field by questions, methods and disagreements rather than accumulating citations. Search strategy seminal papers frontier updates evidence tables citation chains"}, {"title": "16.03 · Replication and reproduction", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Reproduce a research result and document discrepancies without overstating their meaning. Data provenance code reconstruction measurement versions replication limits reporting"}, {"title": "16.04 · Referee reports and research judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Write a fair report that distinguishes fatal identification issues from improvements. Contribution correctness evidence novelty major-minor concerns constructive criticism"}, {"title": "16.05 · Question design and identification strategy", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Formulate a research question with an explicit path from data or assumptions to conclusions. Estimand mechanism identification counterfactual feasibility alternative explanations"}, {"title": "16.06 · Theory building and model discipline", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Develop a parsimonious model that produces testable or interpretable implications. Baseline assumptions equilibrium comparative statics falsifiable implications extensions"}, {"title": "16.07 · Data construction, measurement and ethics", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build a defensible research dataset with documented measurement and access constraints. Collection linkage proxies privacy consent concepts missingness research integrity"}, {"title": "16.08 · Research design, power and preregistration", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Plan an empirical study that separates design from outcome-driven specification choices. Power assignment analysis plan preregistration multiple outcomes contingency decisions"}, {"title": "16.09 · Robustness, uncertainty and external validity", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate whether a conclusion survives plausible alternative assumptions and populations. Sensitivity robustness specification curves transportability structural stability limits"}, {"title": "16.10 · Writing a scholarly paper", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Organize a paper around its question, contribution, evidence and limitations. Abstract introduction methods results tables appendices transparent claims"}, {"title": "16.11 · Seminars, revision and oral defense", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend research under probing questions and revise based on justified criticism. Presentation questions response memo revision defense disagreement"}, {"title": "16.12 · Independent research portfolio", "url": "curriculum.html#v16-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assemble original work and an evaluation protocol for independent expert judgment. Original contribution reproducibility ethics audit external review research agenda next papers"}, {"title": "17.01 · Learning objectives and prerequisite design", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate disciplinary knowledge into assessable learning capabilities. Objectives prerequisite maps cognitive load course alignment transfer"}, {"title": "17.02 · Explaining intuition, mathematics and evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Teach a concept across levels without changing its meaning or hiding assumptions. First principles representations notation derivation empirical interpretation analogies"}, {"title": "17.03 · Designing an undergraduate course", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build a coherent course from prerequisites to cumulative assessments. Syllabus sequencing pacing assignments readings integration"}, {"title": "17.04 · Designing a graduate seminar", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Organize a research seminar around questions, methods and scholarly disagreement. Paper selection presentations discussion critique replication research proposals"}, {"title": "17.05 · Problem design and meaningful assessment", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Write assessments that diagnose reasoning and transfer rather than template recognition. Difficulty validity original problems unseen transfer rubrics exam security"}, {"title": "17.06 · Grading, feedback and remediation", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Grade consistently and give feedback that identifies the next learning action. Calibration fairness partial credit misconceptions remediation reassessment"}, {"title": "17.07 · Teaching cases and leading discussion", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Lead analytical discussion while making assumptions and evidence visible. Cases questioning participation alternative decisions synthesis facilitation"}, {"title": "17.08 · Misconceptions and learning evidence", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Diagnose persistent misunderstandings and evaluate whether instruction changed them. Diagnostic questions retrieval formative feedback retention learning measurement"}, {"title": "17.09 · Research supervision and mentoring", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Guide research toward independence with explicit responsibilities and ethical boundaries. Scope milestones feedback authorship conflict mentorship independence"}, {"title": "17.10 · Communication to policymakers and boards", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate technical findings into decisions without overstating certainty. Executive memos uncertainty graphs trade-offs audience actionable recommendations"}, {"title": "17.11 · Observed teaching and reflective practice", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Evaluate teaching using observation and evidence of learner understanding. Observation rubric peer feedback student work revision accessibility reflection"}, {"title": "17.12 · Teaching and supervision portfolio", "url": "curriculum.html#v17-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend a complete teaching program and its assessment evidence. Syllabi materials teaching demonstration exam design supervision plan external evaluation"}, {"title": "18.01 · Institutional purpose and strategy", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c01", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Translate an institution's mission into coherent choices and resource priorities. Mission stakeholders advantage trade-offs strategy success measures"}, {"title": "18.02 · Resource allocation and institutional budgets", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c02", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build budgets and allocate resources under opportunity cost and uncertainty. Operating budgets capital budgets endowments research funding P&L scenario planning"}, {"title": "18.03 · Organizations, incentives and culture", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c03", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design organizational responsibilities and incentives that support desired behavior. Structure principal-agent delegation culture coordination measurement unintended incentives"}, {"title": "18.04 · Hiring, evaluation and talent development", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c04", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Build defensible hiring and development processes for expert organizations. Selection evidence evaluation promotion feedback succession bias controls"}, {"title": "18.05 · Negotiation, conflict and stakeholder decisions", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c05", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Negotiate institutional decisions using incentives, alternatives and principled process. BATNA interests bargaining coalition conflict resolution contracts"}, {"title": "18.06 · Governance, fiduciary duties and integrity", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c06", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Map decision rights and accountability using jurisdiction-aware legal and ethical analysis. Boards duties conflicts disclosure academic freedom research integrity escalation"}, {"title": "18.07 · Academic institution leadership", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c07", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Design academic strategy while protecting scholarship and educational standards. Faculty curriculum research funding admissions governance donors endowment autonomy"}, {"title": "18.08 · Financial institution leadership", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c08", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Integrate business strategy, capital, liquidity, risk and operating accountability. Business model capital allocation risk appetite controls compensation regulatory relations"}, {"title": "18.09 · Operations, innovation and change", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c09", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Implement organizational improvements with measurable feedback and adaptation. Process bottlenecks change experimentation technology AI evaluation execution"}, {"title": "18.10 · Crisis leadership and high-stakes judgment", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c10", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Make and revise accountable crisis decisions with incomplete information. Triage uncertainty scenarios communication decision logs contingency after-action review"}, {"title": "18.11 · Real responsibility and leadership evaluation", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c11", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Assess leadership through observed decisions and outcomes rather than self-description. Project ownership people budgets delivery external feedback retrospective attribution"}, {"title": "18.12 · Institutional leadership portfolio and final integration", "url": "curriculum.html#v18-c12", "written": false, "status": "Planned", "text": "Defend an integrated strategy and demonstrate the limits and strengths of one's preparation. Academic-financial cases governance research teaching capital risk external evaluation continuing development"}];
